window.PK_DATA=[{"id":"blp","title":"Business Law and Practice","stop":1,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><rect x=\"-70\" y=\"-108\" width=\"132\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\"/><rect x=\"-66\" y=\"-100\" width=\"124\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#FFD6EA\"/><path d=\"M-72,-80 H64 V-64 Q52.66666666666667,-53 41.33333333333334,-64 Q30,-53 18.66666666666667,-64 Q7.333333333333343,-53 -4,-64 Q-15.333333333333329,-53 -26.666666666666664,-64 Q-38,-53 -49.33333333333333,-64 Q-60.666666666666664,-53 -72,-64 Z\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"-72\" y=\"-78\" width=\"22.666666666666668\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke=\"none\"/><rect x=\"-26.666666666666664\" y=\"-78\" width=\"22.666666666666668\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke=\"none\"/><rect x=\"18.66666666666667\" y=\"-78\" width=\"22.666666666666668\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M-72,-80 H64 V-64 Q52.66666666666667,-53 41.33333333333334,-64 Q30,-53 18.66666666666667,-64 Q7.333333333333343,-53 -4,-64 Q-15.333333333333329,-53 -26.666666666666664,-64 Q-38,-53 -49.33333333333333,-64 Q-60.666666666666664,-53 -72,-64 Z\" fill=\"none\"/><rect x=\"-56\" y=\"-52\" width=\"72\" height=\"44\" fill=\"#E0F7FF\"/><rect x=\"-50\" y=\"-38\" width=\"17\" height=\"27\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-51\" y=\"-43\" width=\"19\" height=\"6\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-45\" cy=\"-30\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-39\" cy=\"-22\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-45\" cy=\"-16\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-38\" cy=\"-33\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-29\" y=\"-38\" width=\"17\" height=\"27\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-30\" y=\"-43\" width=\"19\" height=\"6\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-24\" cy=\"-30\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-18\" cy=\"-22\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-24\" cy=\"-16\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-17\" cy=\"-33\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-8\" y=\"-38\" width=\"17\" height=\"27\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-9\" y=\"-43\" width=\"19\" height=\"6\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-3\" cy=\"-30\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"3\" cy=\"-22\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-3\" cy=\"-16\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"4\" cy=\"-33\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"24\" y=\"-48\" width=\"26\" height=\"48\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\"/><circle cx=\"44\" cy=\"-24\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"70\" y=\"-74\" width=\"6\" height=\"74\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"73\" cy=\"-86\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#FF6FB5\"/><path d=\"M73,-86 m-8,0 a8,8 0 1 1 8,8 a4,4 0 1 1 -4,-4\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"business-structures","title":"Business structures and legal personality","q":[[0,"Which of these is a separate legal person?",["An LLP","A general partnership","A sole trader"],"In England and Wales, <b>LLPs</b> and <b>companies</b> are legal persons in their own right. Sole traders and general partnerships are not."],[1,"A sole trader's business can't pay its debts. Can creditors go after the owner's house?",["Yes","No, only the business assets","Only if the owner signed a guarantee"],"A sole trader has <b>unlimited liability</b>: the business debts are the owner's own debts."],[2,"What is the minimum share capital for a private limited company?",["There is no minimum","£50,000","£1,000"],"No minimum: a private company can start with <b>one share of any value (e.g. 1p)</b>. Only a <b>plc</b> needs at least <b>£50,000</b> allotted share capital."],[3,"Who runs a private limited company day to day?",["The directors","The shareholders","Companies House"],"<b>Directors</b> manage the company. Shareholders own it and vote on key matters."],[4,"Which case says a company is a separate person, even if one person owns almost all of it?",["Salomon v Salomon","Prest v Petrodel","Gilford Motor v Horne"],"<b>Salomon</b> (1897): once properly registered, the company is a different person from Mr Salomon, so he was not liable for its debts."],[5,"An owner insures his company's timber in his own name. It burns down. Can he claim?",["No, the company owns the timber","Yes, he owns all the shares","Yes, if he is a director"],"<b>Macaura v Northern Assurance</b>: a shareholder doesn't own the company's property, so he had no <b>insurable interest</b>."],[6,"Can a company's sole director also be its employee?",["Yes","No","Only in a plc"],"<b>Lee v Lee's Air Farming</b>: the company is a separate person, so it can employ its own director. His widow could claim as an employee's widow."],[7,"Can a man dodge his own non-compete promise by trading through a new company?",["No, the court can stop him and the company","Yes, the company is a separate person","Only if his wife runs it"],"<b>Gilford Motor v Horne</b>: using a company to <b>evade an existing obligation</b> lets the court pierce the veil. An injunction was granted against him and the company."],[8,"Will a court pierce the corporate veil just because the company is insolvent?",["No","Yes","Only for a one-person company"],"Insolvency, control by one person, being in a group or fairness are <b>not enough</b>. The veil is pierced only to stop <b>evasion of an existing obligation</b> (Prest)."],[9,"How many directors must a plc have?",["At least 2","At least 1","At least 3"],"A <b>plc</b> needs at least <b>2 directors</b> and a qualified company secretary. A private company needs just 1 director."],[10,"What tax does a company pay on its trading profits?",["Corporation tax","Income tax","None until profits are paid out"],"A company pays <b>corporation tax</b>. Shareholders pay income tax only on dividends they receive."],[11,"A company's only shareholder dies. What happens to the company?",["It carries on","It is dissolved","It becomes a partnership"],"<b>Perpetual succession</b>: a company continues whatever happens to its shareholders or directors."],[12,"Which can grant a floating charge?",["A company","A general partnership","A sole trader"],"<b>Companies and LLPs</b> can grant a <b>floating charge</b> (security over changing assets like stock). Sole traders and ordinary partnerships effectively can't, because the <b>Bills of Sale Acts</b> get in the way."],[13,"A limited partnership must have at least one partner with what?",["Unlimited liability","A trading certificate","A members' agreement"],"A <b>limited partnership (LP)</b> needs at least one <b>general partner with unlimited liability</b>. Don't confuse it with an <b>LLP</b>, where all members have limited liability."],[14,"A director signed a personal guarantee and the company defaulted. Why must the director pay?",["Because of the guarantee","Because the veil is pierced","Small companies have no limited liability"],"The director pays under the <b>guarantee</b>, a separate contract. The company is still a separate person: this is <b>not</b> veil piercing."]],"c":[[0,"Which business structures have separate legal personality?","<b>Companies</b> (Ltd and plc) and <b>LLPs</b> do. <b>Sole traders</b> and <b>general partnerships</b> do not."],[1,"Who has unlimited liability for business debts?","<b>Sole traders</b> and <b>general partners</b>. Creditors can take their personal assets. Company shareholders and LLP members have <b>limited liability</b>."],[4,"Two people start running a business together hoping to make a profit, but sign and register nothing. The business makes a loss in year 1. Is there a partnership?","<b>Yes.</b> A general partnership arises <b>automatically</b> when people carry on a business <b>in common with a view of profit</b>. No agreement, registration or intention to be partners is needed. A <b>loss</b> doesn't matter: the <b>aim</b> of profit is enough. Both have <b>unlimited liability</b>."],[10,"How is an LLP created and what is its legal status?","Only by <b>registering at Companies House</b> under <b>LLPA 2000</b>. It is a <b>body corporate</b> and a separate legal person. Each member is an <b>agent of the LLP</b>."],[11,"What are the minimum membership rules for an LLP?","At least <b>2 members</b>. At least <b>2 designated members</b>, who sign and file the accounts and confirmation statement."],[14,"Mia wants to set up a private limited company on her own with £1 of share capital and no company secretary. Can she?","<b>Yes.</b> A Ltd needs only <b>1 shareholder</b> and <b>1 director</b> (the same person can be both). At least one director must be a <b>natural person</b>. A <b>secretary is optional</b>. There is <b>no minimum share capital</b> (only a plc needs <b>£50,000</b>)."],[16,"What extra requirements apply to a plc?","At least <b>2 directors</b>. A <b>qualified company secretary</b>. Allotted share capital of at least <b>£50,000</b>. Each share paid up to at least <b>one quarter</b> of nominal value plus the <b>whole premium</b>."],[32,"When will the court pierce the corporate veil?","Only under the <b>evasion principle</b>. A person under an <b>existing legal obligation</b> deliberately evades it by <b>interposing a company</b> they control. Even then, only as a <b>last resort</b> (Prest)."],[40,"Does a company or LLP pay income tax on its trading profits?","A <b>company</b> pays <b>corporation tax</b>, never income tax. An <b>LLP</b> and a <b>partnership</b> pay <b>no tax themselves</b>: the members or partners are taxed."]]},{"id":"formation","title":"Forming a company, partnership or LLP (incl. ECCTA 2023)","q":[[0,"What is filed to register a new company?",["Application, articles, memorandum and statements","Articles only","Application and a business plan"],"A company registers by filing an <b>application (IN01)</b> with the <b>articles</b>, <b>memorandum</b>, statement of capital and <b>PSC</b> statement."],[1,"What does the memorandum of association of a new company state?",["The subscribers agree to form it and take shares","The company's objects","How the directors are paid"],"The <b>memorandum</b> is just a short statement by the <b>subscribers</b> that they wish to form the company and will take at least one share each."],[2,"A company registers no articles of its own. Which articles apply?",["The Model Articles","None, it cannot be formed","The Companies Act itself"],"The <b>Model Articles</b> apply by default to a company that registers none."],[3,"The articles are a contract between whom (s.33)?",["The company and its members, and members with each other","The company and its directors only","The company and Companies House"],"<b>s.33</b>: the articles bind the <b>company and its members</b> as if signed by each of them."],[4,"Which resolution is needed to change the articles?",["Special resolution","Ordinary resolution","Board resolution only"],"Articles are amended by <b>special resolution</b> (at least 75%, <b>s.21</b>), then filed at Companies House."],[5,"Within how long must a special resolution be filed at Companies House?",["15 days","7 days","28 days"],"A copy of a <b>special resolution</b> must be filed within <b>15 days</b> (<b>s.30</b>); the amended articles also within 15 days."],[6,"Which person must be registered as having significant control (PSC)?",["Anyone holding more than 25% of shares or votes","Anyone holding any shares","Every director"],"A <b>PSC</b> is, broadly, someone with <b>more than 25%</b> of the shares or voting rights, or the right to appoint most of the board."],[7,"How can a company's name be challenged after registration if it is too like another?",["The Secretary of State can direct a change within 12 months","It cannot be challenged","Only by the court within 5 years"],"Under <b>s.67</b> the Secretary of State may direct a change of a name that is <b>too like</b> an existing one, within <b>12 months</b> of registration."],[8,"What is a 'shelf' company?",["One already formed and bought ready-made","A company that has gone dormant","A company with no shareholders"],"A <b>shelf company</b> is formed in advance and sold. Its name, articles and directors usually need changing."],[9,"A new company's directors resolve to allot shares. What goes in the register of members?",["The shareholders' names, within 2 months","Only directors' names","Nothing, Companies House keeps it"],"The <b>register of members</b> must be updated within <b>2 months</b> of allotment, and <b>share certificates</b> issued within 2 months too."],[10,"A private company's objects are not restricted in its articles. What can it do?",["Any lawful activity","Only what its first business plan says","Only trading activities"],"Under <b>CA 2006 s.31</b> the objects are <b>unrestricted</b> unless the articles say otherwise."],[11,"When does a company come into existence?",["On the date on its certificate of incorporation","When the application is posted","When the first meeting is held"],"The company exists from the <b>date of incorporation</b> shown on the <b>certificate of incorporation</b>, which is conclusive evidence of registration."]],"c":[[44,"Who is a person with significant control (PSC)?","Someone who holds <b>more than 25%</b> of shares or votes. Or can appoint or remove a <b>majority of the board</b>. Or has <b>significant influence or control</b>."],[46,"What is the effect of the certificate of incorporation?","It is <b>conclusive evidence</b> that the company is validly formed. The company exists <b>from the date on the certificate</b> (<b>ss.15(4) and 16</b>)."],[47,"When does a company have the Model Articles?","By default (<b>s.20</b>) if it files <b>no articles</b>, or articles that do not exclude or modify them. Older companies keep their old articles (often <b>Table A</b>)."],[50,"What objects does a CA 2006 company have?","<b>Unrestricted objects</b>, unless the articles restrict them (<b>s.31</b>). Even if restricted, an act's validity <b>cannot be challenged</b> for lack of capacity (<b>s.39</b>). Directors who exceed them breach <b>s.171</b>."],[51,"What name endings are required?","Private: <b>\"Limited\"</b> or <b>\"Ltd\"</b>. Public: <b>\"public limited company\"</b> or <b>\"plc\"</b>. LLP: <b>\"limited liability partnership\"</b> or <b>\"LLP\"</b>."],[58,"How are the articles changed and what must be filed?","By <b>special resolution</b> (<b>75%</b>) (<b>s.21</b>). File the resolution within <b>15 days</b> (<b>s.30</b>). File the <b>amended articles</b> within <b>15 days</b> (<b>s.26</b>)."],[60,"What limits apply to changing the articles?","A member is not bound to take more shares or increase liability without <b>written consent</b> (<b>s.25</b>). Class rights need <b>class consent</b>. The change must be <b>bona fide for the benefit of the company as a whole</b>."],[75,"Who is liable on a pre-incorporation contract?","The <b>person who made it</b> (<b>s.51</b>). They are personally liable and can enforce it, <b>subject to any agreement to the contrary</b>."],[76,"Does signing \"for and on behalf of\" the company exclude personal liability under s.51?","<b>No</b>. An agreement to the contrary needs <b>clear words</b> excluding personal liability (Phonogram v Lane)."]]},{"id":"directors","title":"Directors: appointment, removal and duties","q":[[0,"Which duty requires a director to act within the company's constitution?",["s.171, act within powers","s.172, promote success","s.173, independent judgement"],"<b>s.171</b> requires a director to act in accordance with the <b>constitution</b> and use powers only for the <b>purposes</b> they were given."],[1,"A director must promote the company's success. For whose benefit?",["The members as a whole","The directors","The largest creditor"],"<b>s.172</b>: the director acts in the way he thinks most likely to promote the success of the company <b>for the benefit of its members as a whole</b>, having regard to stakeholders."],[2,"Which test applies to a director's duty of care, skill and diligence (s.174)?",["Objective and subjective, whichever is higher","Subjective only","Objective only"],"<b>s.174</b> sets a minimum <b>objective</b> standard (reasonably diligent person) raised by the director's <b>actual</b> skill and knowledge."],[3,"A director takes a business opportunity he learned of through the company. Which case applies?",["Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver","Salomon v Salomon","Foss v Harbottle"],"<b>Regal (Hastings)</b>: a director who profits from an opportunity gained through his office must <b>account for the profit</b>, even if the company could not have taken it."],[4,"A director has an interest in a proposed transaction with the company. What must he do (s.177)?",["Declare it to the other directors","Get shareholder approval","Resign from the board"],"<b>s.177</b>: declare the nature and extent of the interest to the <b>other directors</b> before the company enters the transaction."],[5,"Which duty is breached by accepting a gift from a supplier because of his position?",["s.176, third-party benefits","s.171, proper purposes","s.174, reasonable care"],"<b>s.176</b> forbids accepting a <b>benefit</b> from a third party conferred because of the director's position or his doing (or not doing) something as a director."],[6,"Who can enforce directors' duties on the company's behalf?",["A shareholder bringing a derivative claim","Any customer","Companies House"],"Duties are owed to the <b>company</b>. A member can bring a <b>derivative claim</b> (Part 11) with the court's permission."],[7,"Which resolution removes a director from office under s.168?",["Ordinary resolution with special notice","Special resolution","Written resolution"],"<b>s.168</b>: removal by <b>ordinary resolution</b> at a meeting on <b>special notice</b> (28 days). A written resolution cannot be used."],[8,"A director's service contract guarantees employment for 3 years. What is needed?",["Prior approval by ordinary resolution","Nothing, the board decides","A special resolution"],"<b>s.188</b>: a guaranteed term of <b>more than 2 years</b> needs prior approval by <b>ordinary resolution</b> of the members."],[9,"A company lends its director £20,000 for personal use. What is needed (s.197)?",["Prior approval by ordinary resolution","Nothing, it is below the limit","Court permission"],"Loans to a director over <b>£10,000</b> need <b>member approval</b> (ordinary resolution) unless an exception applies. Business loans have a <b>£50,000</b> limit."],[10,"Under the Model Articles, who can the board delegate day-to-day management to?",["A managing director or committee","Only the company secretary","Only the shareholders"],"The directors may <b>delegate</b> powers to a person or committee (art.5) and are generally responsible for management (art.3)."],[11,"The shareholders want to give the board a binding instruction. How?",["Special resolution","Ordinary resolution","They cannot"],"Under Model Articles <b>art.4</b>, members can direct the directors by <b>special resolution</b>. It is not retrospective."]],"c":[[85,"To whom are directors' general duties owed?","To the <b>company</b> (<b>s.170(1)</b>). Not to individual shareholders or creditors."],[97,"What does s.40 CA 2006 provide?","For a person dealing <b>in good faith</b>, the directors' power to bind the company is <b>free of any constitutional limitation</b>. Good faith is <b>presumed</b>. Knowing the act is beyond the directors' powers is <b>not bad faith</b>."],[101,"How can shareholders remove a director?","By <b>ordinary resolution at a general meeting</b> (<b>s.168</b>). This applies <b>whatever the articles or any agreement say</b>."],[102,"What notice is required for a s.168 removal resolution?","<b>Special notice</b> to the company at least <b>28 days</b> before the meeting (<b>s.312</b>). Members then get notice, usually at least <b>14 days</b> before."],[114,"What does the s.171 CA 2006 duty require of a director?","Act in accordance with the company's <b>constitution</b>. Use powers only for the <b>purposes for which they are conferred</b> (proper purposes)."],[116,"What is the s.172 duty?","Act in the way they consider, <b>in good faith</b>, most likely to promote the company's success <b>for the benefit of its members as a whole</b>."],[119,"When is the creditor duty triggered?","When the company is <b>insolvent or bordering on insolvency</b>, or insolvent liquidation or administration is <b>probable</b> (Sequana). A mere <b>real risk</b> is not enough."],[122,"What is the s.174 standard of care?","A <b>reasonably diligent person</b> with the knowledge, skill and experience <b>reasonably expected</b> of someone in that role (<b>objective minimum</b>). Plus the director's <b>actual</b> knowledge and skill, which can only <b>raise</b> the standard."],[125,"Is it a defence to s.175 that the company could not have taken the opportunity?","<b>No</b> (<b>s.175(2)</b>). The director must still <b>account for profits</b> (Cooley)."],[126,"When is the s.175 duty to avoid conflicts of interest not breached?","If the situation <b>cannot reasonably be regarded as likely</b> to give rise to a conflict. Or it is <b>authorised by the independent directors</b> (<b>s.175(4)</b>): in a <b>private</b> company unless the articles prevent it; in a <b>plc</b> only if the articles allow (<b>s.175(5)</b>)."],[129,"What does s.177 require?","Declare the <b>nature and extent</b> of an interest in a <b>proposed transaction with the company</b> to the other directors <b>before</b> it is entered into."],[136,"How can members ratify a director's breach?","By <b>ordinary resolution</b> (<b>s.239</b>). Votes of the <b>director</b> and <b>connected</b> members are <b>disregarded</b>. Unlawful acts and fraud on the minority cannot be ratified."]]},{"id":"decision-making","title":"Decision-making: board, general meetings and resolutions","q":[[0,"How many clear days' notice is needed for a general meeting of a private company?",["14","7","21"],"A private company needs at least <b>14 clear days'</b> notice (<b>s.307</b>), excluding the day of sending and the day of the meeting."],[1,"Notice of a private company's meeting is emailed on Monday. When is it deemed received?",["Wednesday, 48 hours later","Monday, at once","Tuesday"],"Under <b>s.1147</b> notice is deemed received <b>48 hours</b> after sending (ignoring non-working days)."],[2,"When can a private company hold a meeting on short notice?",["With majority by number holding 90% of votes","With majority by number holding 75%","If the chair agrees"],"<b>s.307</b>: short notice needs a <b>majority in number</b> of members holding at least <b>90%</b> of the voting rights."],[3,"What majority passes an ordinary resolution at a meeting?",["More than 50% of votes cast","At least 75% of votes cast","At least 51% of all shares"],"<b>s.282</b>: an ordinary resolution needs a <b>simple majority</b>: more than <b>50%</b> of the votes cast."],[4,"What majority passes a special resolution at a meeting?",["At least 75% of votes cast","More than 50%","At least 90%"],"<b>s.283</b>: a special resolution needs <b>at least 75%</b> of the votes cast."],[5,"What majority is needed for a written resolution that is special?",["75% of total voting rights","75% of votes cast","More than 50% of total voting rights"],"A <b>written resolution</b> is measured against <b>total</b> eligible voting rights: <b>75%</b> for special, over 50% for ordinary."],[6,"Which resolution cannot be passed by a written resolution?",["Removing a director","Changing the articles","Allotting shares"],"<b>s.288</b>: written resolutions cannot remove a <b>director</b> (s.168) or an <b>auditor</b>. Those need a meeting."],[7,"What is the minimum holding to require directors to call a general meeting?",["5% of voting rights","10%","25%"],"Members with <b>5%</b> of the paid-up voting capital can <b>require</b> the directors to call a general meeting (<b>s.303</b>)."],[8,"A shareholder holds 26%. What can she do?",["Block a special resolution","Pass an ordinary resolution","Pass a special resolution"],"More than <b>25%</b> blocks a special resolution, because the remaining 74% cannot reach <b>75%</b>."],[9,"A company buys a £150,000 asset from a director. What approval is needed?",["Ordinary resolution of members","None, the board can approve","Special resolution"],"A <b>substantial property transaction</b> (<b>s.190</b>) over <b>£100,000</b> needs prior <b>ordinary resolution</b>, else it is voidable."],[10,"What is the filing deadline for a copy of a special resolution?",["15 days","14 days","21 days"],"File a copy at Companies House within <b>15 days</b> (<b>s.30</b>)."],[11,"Is a company's AGM compulsory for a private company?",["No","Yes, every year","Yes, if over 50 members"],"Only a <b>plc</b> must hold an <b>AGM</b> (within 6 months of year end). A private company need not."]],"c":[[143,"What is the quorum for a board meeting under the Model Articles?","<b>2</b>, unless the directors fix a higher number (<b>art.11</b>). A <b>sole director</b> can decide alone."],[149,"What are the exceptions allowing an interested director to vote?","Shareholders <b>disapply art.14</b> by <b>ordinary resolution</b>. Interest <b>unlikely to cause a conflict</b>. A <b>permitted cause</b>: a <b>guarantee</b> for company obligations, <b>subscribing</b> for shares, or general <b>employee benefits</b>."],[156,"What notice is required for a general meeting?","At least <b>14 clear days</b> for a private company and a plc non-AGM (<b>s.307</b>). <b>21 clear days</b> for a plc AGM."],[161,"What consent is needed for short notice of a private company GM?","A <b>majority in number</b> of members entitled to vote, holding at least <b>90%</b> of voting shares (articles may raise to 95%). Public: <b>95%</b>. Plc AGM: <b>all</b> members."],[169,"What majorities are needed for written resolutions?","Ordinary: <b>more than 50%</b> of <b>total eligible votes</b>. Special: at least <b>75%</b> of <b>total eligible votes</b>. Not just those who reply."],[172,"What majorities pass ordinary and special resolutions at a meeting?","Ordinary: <b>more than 50%</b> of votes cast (<b>s.282</b>). Special: <b>at least 75%</b> of votes cast (<b>s.283</b>)."],[173,"Which company decisions need a special resolution of the shareholders?","<b>Changing the articles</b>. <b>Changing the name</b>. <b>Disapplying pre-emption</b>. <b>Buyback out of capital</b>. <b>Reduction of capital</b>. <b>Re-registration</b>. <b>Voluntary winding up</b>. Directing the board (<b>art.4</b>)."],[174,"Which company decisions need an ordinary resolution of the shareholders?","<b>Removing</b> or appointing a director. <b>s.551</b> authority. Approving <b>s.188, s.190, s.197, s.217</b>. <b>Buyback contract</b>. <b>Ratification</b>. <b>Final dividend</b>. <b>Disapplying art.14</b>."],[177,"When is an asset substantial for s.190?","Value <b>over £100,000</b>, or over <b>10% of net assets</b> and <b>over £5,000</b> (<b>s.191</b>). £5,000 or less is never substantial."],[181,"When does a loan to a director need approval?","<b>Ordinary resolution</b> for any loan, guarantee or security (<b>s.197</b>). Exceptions: business expenditure up to <b>£50,000</b> (<b>s.204</b>); minor loans up to <b>£10,000</b> (<b>s.207</b>); money-lending companies."]]},{"id":"shareholders-minority","title":"Shareholders' rights and minority protection","q":[[0,"What does s.994 let a member petition for?",["Relief for unfair prejudice","Removal of auditors","A refund of share capital"],"<b>s.994</b>: a member may petition where the company's affairs are conducted in a way that is <b>unfairly prejudicial</b> to members. The usual remedy is a <b>buy-out</b>."],[1,"Which case limits s.994 'unfairness' to breaches of agreed terms or equitable duties?",["O'Neill v Phillips","Salomon v Salomon","Regal v Gulliver"],"<b>O'Neill v Phillips</b>: unfairness means a breach of the <b>agreed terms</b> or use of rules contrary to <b>good faith and equity</b>. No right to leave just because trust is lost."],[2,"What is a quasi-partnership company?",["One run on mutual trust, like a partnership","A company with two shareholders","A company owned by a partnership"],"In a <b>quasi-partnership</b> (Ebrahimi), members have personal trust and expectations of involvement in management, so equitable duties arise."],[3,"Which case allowed a just and equitable winding up of a quasi-partnership?",["Ebrahimi v Westbourne Galleries","Foss v Harbottle","Prest v Petrodel"],"<b>Ebrahimi</b>: exclusion from management of a quasi-partnership was a ground for winding up under <b>IA 1986 s.122(1)(g)</b>."],[4,"Under the rule in Foss v Harbottle, who is the proper claimant for a wrong to the company?",["The company","Any shareholder","Any director"],"The <b>company</b> is the proper claimant for wrongs done to it. Exceptions include the statutory <b>derivative claim</b>."],[5,"Who is the claimant in a derivative claim?",["A member suing on the company's behalf","The company's liquidator","The director"],"A <b>derivative claim</b> (Part 11) is brought by a <b>member</b>, for the <b>company's</b> benefit, and needs the court's <b>permission</b>."],[6,"What stamp duty applies to a share transfer for £10,000?",["0.5%, paid by the buyer","1%, paid by the seller","None"],"<b>Stamp duty</b> is <b>0.5%</b> of the price, paid by the <b>buyer</b>, on transfers over £1,000."],[7,"A buyer has a signed share transfer but it is not registered. Can she vote?",["No, not until registered as a member","Yes, she owns the shares","Yes, by proxy"],"A buyer becomes a <b>member</b> only on <b>registration</b> in the register of members. Until then the seller remains the registered holder."],[8,"Within what period must a company register a share transfer or refuse it?",["2 months","14 days","28 days"],"The company must register or send <b>notice of refusal with reasons</b> within <b>2 months</b> of the transfer being lodged (<b>s.771</b>)."],[9,"What is a key benefit of a shareholders' agreement over the articles?",["It is private and needs all parties to change","It is filed at Companies House","It binds future shareholders automatically"],"A <b>shareholders' agreement</b> is <b>confidential</b> and can only be varied with the consent of <b>all parties</b>, unlike the articles (75%)."],[10,"Under s.321, which holding can always demand a poll?",["10% of the voting rights","1% of the voting rights","25% of the voting rights"],"The articles cannot stop a poll demanded by members with <b>10%</b> of the voting rights or by <b>5 members</b> (<b>s.321</b>)."],[11,"A shareholder holds 51%. What can she pass alone?",["An ordinary resolution","A special resolution","A written special resolution"],"Over <b>50%</b> carries any <b>ordinary resolution</b>, such as removing a director. A special resolution needs <b>75%</b>."]],"c":[[186,"What is the proper claimant rule?","If a wrong is done to the <b>company</b>, the <b>company itself</b> is the proper claimant (Foss v Harbottle). Courts will not interfere with honest majority decisions."],[191,"What can a 5% shareholder do?","<b>Require</b> a general meeting (<b>s.303</b>). Require circulation of a <b>written resolution</b> (<b>s.292</b>) or <b>members' statement</b> (<b>s.314</b>)."],[192,"What can a 10% shareholder do?","Demand a <b>poll</b>. Require an <b>audit</b> of a small company's accounts (<b>s.476</b>)."],[194,"What does a holder of more than 50% control?","<b>Ordinary resolutions</b>, including appointing and <b>removing directors</b>, so control of the board."],[206,"When must the court refuse permission for a derivative claim?","A person acting under <b>s.172</b> would <b>not continue</b> the claim. Or the act is <b>authorised</b> (future) or <b>authorised or ratified</b> (past) (<b>s.263(2)</b>)."],[208,"Who receives damages from a successful derivative claim?","<b>The company</b>, not the claimant. The court can order the company to <b>indemnify the claimant's costs</b>."],[210,"What is the test for unfair prejudice under s.994?","The company's affairs are being or have been conducted in a manner <b>unfairly prejudicial</b> to members' interests, including the petitioner's. Judged <b>objectively</b>."],[212,"Does a breakdown of trust alone give a right to exit under s.994?","<b>No</b>. There is <b>no right to exit at will</b>. Unfairness usually needs <b>breach of agreed terms</b> or conduct contrary to <b>good faith</b> (O'Neill v Phillips)."],[215,"What is the most common s.994 remedy?","An order for the <b>purchase of the petitioner's shares</b> at <b>fair value</b> by the other members or the company (<b>s.996</b>)."],[218,"What are recognised grounds for just and equitable winding up?","<b>Failure of substratum</b>. <b>Deadlock</b>. <b>Breakdown of trust</b> in a <b>quasi-partnership</b>, including <b>exclusion from management</b>. Fraud or lack of probity."]]},{"id":"partnerships","title":"Partnerships and LLPs: authority, agreements and liability","q":[[0,"Without an agreement, how are partnership profits shared?",["Equally","By capital contributed","By time worked"],"<b>PA 1890 s.24</b>: default is <b>equal</b> shares in profits and losses, whatever the capital contributed."],[1,"Without an agreement, do partners get a salary?",["No","Yes, if full time","Yes, at the market rate"],"Under <b>s.24</b> there is <b>no salary</b> unless agreed. Partners share profits."],[2,"Without an agreement, what interest is paid on a partner's loan to the firm beyond capital?",["5% a year","No interest","Bank base rate"],"<b>s.24(3)</b>: a partner who advances money beyond capital is entitled to <b>5%</b> simple interest. There is no interest on capital."],[3,"Is a general partnership a separate legal person?",["No","Yes","Yes, if it has a written agreement"],"A <b>general partnership</b> has no separate legal personality, so the partners own assets and owe debts together."],[4,"A partner makes a contract within the firm's usual business. Is the firm bound?",["Yes, as agent of the firm","No, unless all partners sign","Only if it is in writing"],"<b>s.5</b>: each partner is an <b>agent</b> of the firm for its usual business, unless the other party knew he had no authority."],[5,"How are partners liable for the firm's contract debts?",["Jointly, with no limit","Only up to their capital","Not personally liable"],"Under <b>s.9</b> partners are liable <b>jointly</b> for the firm's debts, with <b>unlimited</b> personal liability."],[6,"Is a new partner liable for debts incurred before she joined?",["No","Yes, in full","Only for contract debts"],"<b>s.17</b>: a new partner is <b>not liable</b> for debts from before she joined, unless she agrees to take them on."],[7,"A partner retires. Is he still liable for debts from before he left?",["Yes","No","Only if notified"],"A <b>retiring partner</b> stays liable for <b>pre-retirement debts</b> unless the creditor agrees to release him (novation)."],[8,"Which vote changes the nature of the partnership business?",["Unanimous","Simple majority","75%"],"<b>s.24(8)</b>: ordinary matters by <b>majority</b>, but changing the <b>nature of the business</b> (or admitting a new partner) needs <b>unanimity</b>."],[9,"Without an agreement, can a majority expel a partner?",["No","Yes, by simple majority","Yes, by 75%"],"Under the <b>PA 1890</b> there is <b>no power to expel</b> unless the agreement says so."],[10,"Which of these is a legal person?",["An LLP","A general partnership","A sole trader"],"An <b>LLP</b> is a <b>body corporate</b> with separate legal personality, formed by registration at Companies House."],[11,"How many members must an LLP have?",["At least 2","At least 1","At least 3"],"An LLP needs <b>at least 2 members</b>."]],"c":[[232,"What is the default rule on capital, profits and losses?","Shared <b>equally</b>, whatever each partner contributed (<b>s.24(1)</b>)."],[235,"Which partnership decisions need unanimity by default?","<b>Changing the nature of the business</b>. Admitting a <b>new partner</b> (<b>s.24(7)</b>). <b>Changing the partnership terms</b> (<b>s.19</b>). Ordinary matters: <b>majority</b> (<b>s.24(8)</b>)."],[236,"Can a majority expel a partner?","<b>No</b>, unless an <b>express power</b> has been agreed (<b>s.25</b>)."],[238,"What duties of good faith do partners owe under ss.28-30?","<b>s.28</b>: true accounts and full information. <b>s.29</b>: account for <b>secret profits</b> from firm transactions, property, name or connection. <b>s.30</b>: account for <b>all profits</b> of a <b>competing business</b>."],[239,"When does a partner's act bind the firm under s.5?","When done for carrying on <b>in the usual way</b> business <b>of the kind carried on by the firm</b>. Each partner is agent of the <b>firm and the other partners</b>."],[247,"When is the firm liable for a partner's wrong?","Where the partner acts in the <b>ordinary course of the firm's business</b> or with authority. Every partner is <b>jointly and severally liable</b> (<b>s.12</b>). Includes misapplied client money (<b>s.11</b>)."],[253,"Does a retiring partner remain liable for debts from their time?","<b>Yes</b> (<b>s.17(2)</b>). Discharged only by a <b>novation</b> with the <b>creditor</b> (<b>s.17(3)</b>). An <b>indemnity</b> from the continuing partners does not bind the creditor."],[254,"How does a retiring partner avoid liability for debts incurred after leaving?","<b>Actual notice</b> to everyone who <b>dealt with the firm before</b>. A <b>London Gazette</b> advertisement for those who had not (<b>s.36</b>)."],[256,"What events dissolve a partnership by default?","<b>Expiry</b> of term or end of venture. <b>Notice</b> (at will). <b>Death or bankruptcy</b> of a partner. Option of others if a share is <b>charged</b>. <b>Illegality</b>. <b>Court order</b>."],[270,"When is an LLP member personally liable?","For their own negligence only if they <b>assumed personal responsibility</b>. On insolvency: <b>wrongful/fraudulent trading</b> and <b>s.214A</b> clawback of withdrawals in the <b>2 years</b> before winding up."]]},{"id":"equity-finance","title":"Equity finance: shares, allotment, buyback and redemption","q":[[0,"A £1 share is issued for £3. What is the £2?",["Share premium","Nominal value","Share capital"],"<b>Share premium</b> is any amount paid above <b>nominal value</b>. It goes to the share premium account and cannot be paid out as a dividend."],[1,"Can a company issue a £1 share for 80p?",["No","Yes, if the shareholders agree","Yes, if a plc"],"<b>s.580</b>: shares cannot be issued at a <b>discount</b> to nominal value."],[2,"Which shares get a fixed dividend before ordinary shareholders?",["Preference shares","Ordinary shares","Deferred shares"],"<b>Preference shares</b> usually carry a <b>fixed dividend</b> paid first, and often priority on return of capital."],[3,"A private company has one class of shares. Do directors need s.551 authority to allot?",["No, unless the articles restrict them","Yes, always","Yes, by special resolution"],"A private company with <b>one class</b> can allot without <b>s.551</b> authority (<b>s.550</b>) unless its articles prevent it."],[4,"When must directors get authority under s.551 to allot shares?",["Public company, or a private one creating a new class","Never","Only when issuing for non-cash"],"Authority is needed for a <b>plc</b>, or a private company with <b>more than one class</b> or creating a <b>new class</b>, by ordinary resolution."],[5,"What do pre-emption rights (s.561) give existing shareholders?",["First chance to buy new shares for cash","A right to veto the issue","A right to a free share"],"<b>s.561</b>: new <b>equity shares issued for cash</b> must first be offered to existing shareholders, <b>pro rata</b>."],[6,"How can pre-emption rights be disapplied in a private company?",["By special resolution or in the articles","By ordinary resolution","By the directors"],"<b>ss.569-571</b>: disapplied by <b>special resolution</b> (or the articles of a private company)."],[7,"How long must a pre-emption offer remain open?",["14 days","7 days","28 days"],"Under <b>s.562</b> the offer must be in writing and stay open for at least <b>14 days</b>."],[8,"Which approval lets a private company buy back shares off-market?",["Ordinary resolution approving the contract","Board decision only","Unanimous consent of all members"],"An <b>off-market</b> buyback contract must be approved in advance by <b>ordinary resolution</b> (<b>s.694</b>). The seller's votes do not count."],[9,"A private company buys back shares. What can it pay from, normally?",["Distributable profits or a fresh issue","Share premium account","Any reserves"],"Buybacks are normally paid for from <b>distributable profits</b> or the <b>proceeds of a fresh issue</b> of shares."],[10,"What majority approves a variation of class rights under s.630?",["75% in nominal value of the class, in writing","Simple majority of the class","75% of all shareholders"],"<b>s.630</b>: consent of <b>75% in nominal value</b> of the class (written), or a <b>special resolution</b> at a separate class meeting."],[11,"Within how long must share certificates be issued after allotment?",["2 months","14 days","28 days"],"<b>s.769</b>: certificates must be issued within <b>2 months</b> of allotment (or of a transfer being lodged)."]],"c":[[281,"How are class rights varied?","<b>Written consent</b> of holders of <b>75%</b> of the class, or a <b>special resolution</b> at a separate <b>class meeting</b> (<b>s.630</b>). <b>15%</b> non-consenting holders may apply to court within <b>21 days</b> (<b>s.633</b>)."],[284,"When do directors have authority to allot under s.550?","In a <b>private company with one class</b> of shares, to allot more of <b>that class</b>, <b>unless the articles prohibit</b> it. No resolution needed."],[285,"When is s.551 authority needed and what must it state?","Public companies, private companies with <b>more than one class</b>, or creating a <b>new class</b>. By <b>articles</b> or <b>ordinary resolution</b>. Must state the <b>maximum number</b> and an <b>expiry date</b> within <b>5 years</b>."],[288,"What are statutory pre-emption rights?","Before allotting <b>equity securities</b> (ordinary shares) <b>wholly for cash</b>, offer them to existing <b>ordinary shareholders</b> <b>in proportion</b> to their holdings (<b>s.561</b>)."],[290,"When do pre-emption rights not apply?","<b>Non-cash consideration</b>. <b>Bonus shares</b>. <b>Employees' share schemes</b>. Preference shares with limited participation rights."],[291,"How can a private company disapply pre-emption?","<b>Exclude</b> it in the articles (<b>s.567</b>). One class: <b>articles or special resolution</b> (<b>s.569</b>). General s.551 authority: <b>articles or special resolution</b> (<b>s.570</b>). Specific allotment: <b>special resolution</b> with directors' statement (<b>s.571</b>). Or members <b>waive</b>."],[295,"How may a buyback be funded?","<b>Distributable profits</b> and/or the <b>proceeds of a fresh issue</b> (<b>s.692</b>). A private company may use <b>capital</b>, after using profits and fresh issue proceeds."],[297,"What approval does an off-market buyback need?","<b>Ordinary resolution</b> approving the <b>contract terms</b> <b>before</b> it is entered into, or the contract made conditional on approval (<b>s.694</b>)."],[301,"What must the directors' statement on a buyback out of capital say?","<b>Immediately after</b> payment there are <b>no grounds</b> for finding the company unable to pay its debts. For the <b>next year</b> it can carry on as a <b>going concern</b> and pay debts as they fall due. False statement without reasonable grounds is an <b>offence</b>."],[314,"What conditions give capital treatment on a buyback?","<b>Unquoted trading company</b>. <b>Wholly or mainly for the benefit of the trade</b>. Seller <b>UK resident</b>. Owned <b>5 years</b> (3 if inherited). <b>Substantial reduction</b> (no more than <b>75%</b> of previous %). Afterwards <b>not connected</b> (no more than <b>30%</b>)."]]},{"id":"debt-security","title":"Debt finance and security","q":[[0,"How many days does a company have to register a charge at Companies House?",["21 days","14 days","28 days"],"<b>s.859A</b>: file the charge (form <b>MR01</b>) within <b>21 days</b> beginning with the day after creation."],[1,"A charge is not registered in time. What is the result?",["Void against liquidator and creditors","Void against the company","Still fully valid"],"The charge is <b>void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors</b>. The <b>debt</b> is still owed, just unsecured."],[2,"Which charge lets the company sell the charged stock in the ordinary course?",["Floating charge","Fixed charge","Legal mortgage"],"A <b>floating charge</b> hovers over a class of assets that change. The company can deal with them until the charge <b>crystallises</b>."],[3,"Which charge gives the lender control over the asset?",["Fixed charge","Floating charge","Negative pledge"],"A <b>fixed charge</b> attaches to a specific asset, which the company cannot dispose of without the lender's consent (<b>Spectrum Plus</b>)."],[4,"Which case held that a charge over book debts without control of proceeds is floating?",["Re Spectrum Plus","Re MC Bacon","Salomon v Salomon"],"<b>Spectrum Plus</b>: a fixed charge needs <b>control</b> over the asset and its proceeds. Book debts the company can freely use are <b>floating</b>."],[5,"Which of these events crystallises a floating charge?",["The company goes into liquidation","A new director is appointed","A dividend is paid"],"A floating charge <b>crystallises</b> automatically on <b>liquidation</b>, appointment of a receiver, or the company ceasing business."],[6,"Which ranks first on insolvency, a fixed charge or a floating charge over the same asset?",["The fixed charge","The floating charge","They share equally"],"A <b>fixed charge</b> generally ranks ahead of a <b>floating charge</b>, which ranks behind <b>preferential creditors</b> and the prescribed part."],[7,"Two registered fixed charges over the same asset: who ranks first?",["The one created first","The one registered last","The larger loan"],"Charges of the same type rank by <b>date of creation</b>, if each is <b>registered</b> in time."],[8,"A bank takes a floating charge. Which term stops the company giving later competing charges?",["A negative pledge","A force majeure clause","A retention of title clause"],"A <b>negative pledge</b> stops the company granting other charges ranking ahead or equal."],[9,"Who can give a floating charge?",["A company or LLP","A sole trader","A partnership"],"Individuals cannot give floating charges (Bills of Sale Act); <b>companies and LLPs</b> can."],[10,"Which is true of debt finance compared with equity?",["Interest is tax deductible; dividends are not","Dividends are tax deductible","Both are tax deductible"],"A company can deduct <b>interest</b> from profits. <b>Dividends</b> are paid after tax."],[11,"A guarantee of a company's loan must be in what form?",["In writing, signed by the guarantor","Oral is enough","A witnessed will"],"A <b>guarantee</b> must be <b>in writing and signed</b> by the guarantor (Statute of Frauds 1677)."]],"c":[[331,"What is a fixed charge?","A charge over <b>specific, identifiable assets</b>. The company <b>cannot deal</b> with them <b>without the lender's consent</b>. That <b>control</b> makes it fixed."],[332,"What is a floating charge?","A charge over a <b>changing class of assets</b>, present and future, e.g. stock, book debts, cash. The company is <b>free to deal</b> with them in the ordinary course of business until <b>crystallisation</b>."],[334,"What are the weaknesses of a floating charge?","Ranks behind <b>expenses</b>, <b>preferential creditors</b> and the <b>prescribed part</b>. Vulnerable under <b>s.245</b>. A <b>later fixed charge</b> can take priority. Assets may be dissipated."],[337,"When is a charge over book debts fixed?","Only if proceeds go into a <b>blocked account</b> the company cannot draw on without consent. If the company can draw freely, it is <b>floating</b> (Spectrum Plus)."],[339,"When does a floating charge crystallise automatically by law?","On <b>winding up</b>. On appointment of a <b>receiver</b> (and of an <b>administrator</b> under most charge documents). When the company <b>ceases business</b>."],[345,"What is the time limit for registering a charge?","<b>21 days</b> beginning with the <b>day after</b> the date of creation (<b>s.859A(4)</b>)."],[347,"What happens if a charge is not registered in time?","The security is <b>void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors</b> (<b>s.859H</b>). The <b>debt remains valid</b> but becomes <b>immediately repayable</b>."],[351,"How do two fixed charges over the same asset rank?","In <b>order of creation</b>, provided each is <b>registered within 21 days</b>."],[352,"Does a later fixed charge beat an earlier floating charge?","<b>Yes</b>, generally. <b>Exception</b>: the floating charge has a <b>negative pledge</b> and the fixed chargee had <b>actual notice</b> of it."]]},{"id":"distributions-accounts","title":"Distributions, dividends and accounting requirements","q":[[0,"From what can a company lawfully pay a dividend?",["Distributable profits","Share capital","Share premium account"],"<b>s.830</b>: only out of <b>distributable profits</b> (accumulated, realised profits less accumulated realised losses)."],[1,"A company made £40,000 profit this year but has £55,000 past losses. Can it pay a dividend?",["No","Yes, £40,000","Yes, £15,000"],"Profits are <b>accumulated</b>: past realised losses must be set off first, so there are <b>no distributable profits</b>."],[2,"A member takes a dividend knowing it is unlawful. What follows (s.847)?",["He must repay it","Nothing, it is his","The company must repay him"],"A member who knew, or had <b>reasonable grounds to believe</b>, the distribution was unlawful must <b>repay</b> it."],[3,"Who declares a final dividend under the Model Articles?",["Shareholders by ordinary resolution","Directors alone","Companies House"],"<b>Final</b> dividends are declared by <b>ordinary resolution</b>, but cannot exceed the amount the <b>directors recommend</b>."],[4,"Who can decide an interim dividend?",["The directors","The auditor","The shareholders only"],"<b>Interim dividends</b> can be paid by the directors if profits justify it, without a shareholder vote."],[5,"What extra test applies to a public company's dividend (s.831)?",["A net assets test","A turnover test","A solvency statement"],"A <b>plc</b> must also keep <b>net assets</b> at least equal to called-up share capital and undistributable reserves after the payment."],[6,"By when must a private company file its annual accounts?",["9 months after year end","6 months","12 months"],"A <b>private</b> company has <b>9 months</b> after its year end. A <b>public</b> company has <b>6 months</b>."],[7,"How long must a private company keep its accounting records?",["3 years","6 years","10 years"],"<b>s.388</b>: <b>3 years</b> for a private company, <b>6 years</b> for a public company."],[8,"Which standard must a company's accounts meet?",["A true and fair view","Tax authority approval","Shareholder approval"],"Directors must not approve accounts unless they give a <b>true and fair view</b> (<b>s.393</b>)."],[9,"How many size conditions must a company meet to qualify as small?",["2 of 3","All 3","Any 1"],"A company is <b>small</b> (or micro, medium) if it meets <b>2 of 3</b> limits: turnover, balance sheet total, employees."],[10,"Are small companies normally exempt from audit?",["Yes","No","Only if dormant"],"<b>s.477</b>: <b>small companies</b> are generally exempt, but members with <b>10%</b> can require an audit."],[11,"How often must a company file a confirmation statement?",["At least every 12 months","Every 6 months","Every 3 years"],"A <b>confirmation statement</b> (CS01) must be delivered at least <b>every 12 months</b>."]],"c":[[359,"How are profits available for distribution calculated?","<b>Accumulated, realised profits</b> not previously distributed or capitalised <b>less</b> <b>accumulated, realised losses</b> not previously written off (<b>s.830(2)</b>)."],[361,"Is a revaluation gain distributable?","<b>No</b>. It is <b>unrealised</b>. Only profits actually made count."],[362,"What extra test applies to public companies?","The <b>net assets test</b> (<b>s.831</b>): after the distribution, net assets must be <b>not less than called-up share capital plus undistributable reserves</b>. So a plc must also cover <b>unrealised losses</b>."],[363,"What are undistributable reserves?","The <b>share premium account</b>, the <b>capital redemption reserve</b>, and the excess of unrealised profits over unrealised losses (e.g. a <b>revaluation reserve</b>)."],[365,"How is a final dividend declared under the Model Articles?","<b>Recommended by the directors</b>, then <b>declared by ordinary resolution</b> (<b>art.30</b>). Shareholders <b>cannot declare more</b> than recommended."],[366,"Who decides an interim dividend?","The <b>directors alone</b> (<b>art.30(1)</b>), if justified by available profits. No shareholder approval."],[371,"When must a shareholder repay an unlawful distribution?","If at the time they <b>knew or had reasonable grounds for believing</b> it contravened the rules (<b>s.847</b>)."],[373,"What is directors' liability for an unlawful dividend?","<b>Breach of duty</b> (<b>s.171</b>, possibly <b>s.172</b>, <b>s.174</b>). <b>Jointly and severally liable</b> to repay the full amount with interest, even if not received personally."],[385,"What are the small company thresholds?","Turnover <b>£15m</b>. Balance sheet total <b>£7.5m</b>. <b>50</b> employees. Meet <b>at least 2 of 3</b>."],[394,"What are the accounts filing deadlines?","<b>Private</b>: <b>9 months</b>. <b>Public</b>: <b>6 months</b>. First accounts over 12 months: <b>21 months</b> from incorporation (public <b>18</b>)."]]},{"id":"corporate-insolvency","title":"Corporate insolvency procedures","q":[[0,"Which test asks if a company can pay debts as they fall due?",["Cash flow test","Balance sheet test","Turnover test"],"<b>IA 1986 s.123(1)(e)/(2)</b>: the <b>cash flow test</b> (debts as they fall due) and the <b>balance sheet test</b> (liabilities exceed assets)."],[1,"A creditor owed £2,000 serves a statutory demand. How long does the company have?",["3 weeks","7 days","28 days"],"A <b>statutory demand</b> gives <b>21 days</b> (3 weeks) to pay. Failure is deemed inability to pay debts (debt over <b>£750</b>)."],[2,"Which winding up needs a declaration of solvency?",["Members' voluntary liquidation","Compulsory liquidation","Creditors' voluntary liquidation"],"An <b>MVL</b> requires a <b>declaration of solvency</b> that debts will be paid in full within 12 months."],[3,"Who petitions for compulsory liquidation?",["Usually a creditor","The Official Receiver only","Companies House"],"Most petitions are by an <b>unpaid creditor</b>. The company, directors or members can also petition."],[4,"Who becomes liquidator first after a compulsory winding-up order?",["The Official Receiver","The creditor who petitioned","A shareholder"],"The <b>Official Receiver</b> is the first liquidator. An insolvency practitioner may later replace them."],[5,"What is the main aim of administration?",["Rescue the company as a going concern","Pay shareholders first","Close the company quickly"],"The administrator's first objective is to <b>rescue the company as a going concern</b>, then a better result for creditors than liquidation."],[6,"Who can appoint an administrator out of court?",["A qualifying floating charge holder","A shareholder","An employee"],"A <b>QFCH</b> can appoint out of court, as can the company or its directors. Creditors need a court order."],[7,"What does administration give the company?",["A moratorium","Immunity from tax","A fixed charge"],"A <b>moratorium</b> stops creditors enforcing security or starting legal action without consent or court permission."],[8,"What majority approves a CVA?",["75% in value of creditors voting","50% in value","100% of creditors"],"A <b>CVA</b> needs <b>75% by value</b> of creditors voting, and fails if more than 50% of unconnected creditors vote against."],[9,"Does a CVA bind secured creditors without consent?",["No","Yes","Only if over 75%"],"A <b>CVA</b> cannot bind <b>secured</b> or <b>preferential</b> creditors without their agreement."],[10,"How does a creditors' voluntary liquidation begin?",["Members pass a special resolution","The court makes an order","The board decides alone"],"A <b>CVL</b> starts with a members' <b>special resolution</b> to wind up (<b>s.84</b>); the <b>creditors</b> then choose the liquidator."],[11,"Which company can be struck off by the directors applying on form DS01?",["A solvent, non-trading one","Any insolvent one","One in administration"],"<b>Striking off</b> is for companies with <b>no debts</b> that have not traded recently. It is cheap, unlike an MVL."]],"c":[[401,"What is the cash flow test of insolvency?","The company is <b>unable to pay its debts as they fall due</b> (<b>s.123(1)(e)</b>)."],[402,"What is the balance sheet test of insolvency?","The <b>value of assets is less than liabilities</b>, including <b>contingent and prospective</b> liabilities (<b>s.123(2)</b>). Not mechanical: looking at all the facts, are its assets not enough to meet all its liabilities (<b>Eurosail</b>)?"],[403,"When is a company deemed unable to pay its debts?","A creditor owed <b>more than £750</b> serves a <b>statutory demand</b> and it is unpaid, unsecured or uncompounded for <b>3 weeks</b>. Or <b>execution</b> is returned <b>unsatisfied</b>."],[412,"What are the CVA approval thresholds?","Creditors: at least <b>75% in value</b> of those voting, but fails if more than <b>50% of unconnected creditors</b> voting oppose it. Members: more than <b>50% in value</b>. If they disagree, the <b>creditors' decision prevails</b>."],[414,"What can a CVA not do without the creditor's consent?","Affect a <b>secured creditor's</b> right to enforce, or alter the priority of <b>preferential debts</b> (<b>s.4(3)-(4)</b>)."],[417,"What is the statutory purpose of administration?","In strict order: <b>(a)</b> rescue the company <b>as a going concern</b>. <b>(b)</b> a <b>better result for creditors as a whole</b> than winding up. <b>(c)</b> realise property for <b>secured or preferential creditors</b>."],[420,"When can a QFCH appoint an administrator out of court?","When its charge over the <b>whole or substantially the whole</b> of the property is <b>enforceable</b>. Insolvency need not be shown. <b>2 business days'</b> notice to any prior QFCH."],[421,"What are the conditions for an out-of-court appointment by the company or directors?","The company <b>is or is likely to become unable to pay its debts</b>. Give <b>5 business days' notice</b> of intention to any <b>QFCH</b>, who may appoint its own choice. Not available if a <b>winding-up petition</b> or administration application is pending, an administrative receiver is in office, or within <b>12 months</b> after an earlier company/directors' administration ended (para.23)."],[434,"What is a members' voluntary liquidation?","A winding up of a <b>solvent</b> company. Needs a <b>declaration of solvency</b> and a <b>special resolution</b>. <b>Members</b> appoint the liquidator."],[437,"How does a creditors' voluntary liquidation start?","<b>Special resolution</b> of the members. Directors prepare a <b>statement of affairs</b>. <b>Creditors</b> choose the liquidator within <b>14 days</b>; their choice <b>prevails</b>. Winding up starts on the <b>resolution</b>."],[441,"What is the effect of dispositions after a winding-up petition?","Any disposition of company property or share transfer after <b>presentation</b> is <b>void</b> unless the court makes a <b>validation order</b> (<b>s.127</b>)."]]},{"id":"insolvency-clawback-priority","title":"Clawback, directors' liability and order of priority","q":[[0,"What is the lookback period for a transaction at an undervalue (s.238)?",["2 years","6 months","12 months"],"<b>s.238</b>: a gift or sale for much less than value within <b>2 years</b> before the onset of insolvency, if the company was insolvent then."],[1,"Which defence protects a transaction at an undervalue?",["Good faith, for business, reasonable grounds to benefit","The buyer is an associate","The company was solvent later"],"The court will not set aside if done in <b>good faith</b>, to carry on <b>business</b>, with <b>reasonable grounds</b> to believe it would <b>benefit the company</b>."],[2,"The company repays a connected creditor 18 months before liquidation. Which section applies?",["s.239, preference","s.238, undervalue","s.245, floating charge"],"A <b>preference</b> to a <b>connected</b> person falls within <b>2 years</b> (6 months if unconnected), with desire to prefer presumed."],[3,"What must be shown for a preference (s.239)?",["A desire to prefer the creditor","An intention to defraud","A gift"],"A <b>preference</b> needs a <b>desire to prefer</b> (<b>Re MC Bacon</b>). It is presumed for <b>connected</b> persons."],[4,"A floating charge for past debts is granted 8 months before liquidation to an unconnected bank. Valid?",["Invalid, if the company was then insolvent","Valid, as over 6 months ago","Valid, as the bank is unconnected"],"<b>s.245</b>: floating charges within <b>12 months</b> (2 years if connected) are invalid if the company was <b>insolvent</b>, except for <b>new value</b>."],[5,"Who is liable for wrongful trading (s.214)?",["Directors who ignored no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvency","Any creditor","Only the shareholders"],"<b>s.214</b>: a director who knew, or should have concluded, there was <b>no reasonable prospect</b> of avoiding insolvent liquidation, and failed to minimise loss."],[6,"Which wrongful trading defence is available?",["Taking every step to minimise losses","Being unpaid","Following a shareholder's order"],"The director avoids liability by showing he took <b>every step</b> to <b>minimise potential loss to creditors</b>."],[7,"What is needed for fraudulent trading (s.213)?",["Intent to defraud creditors","Mere negligence","Shareholder approval"],"<b>s.213</b> needs <b>dishonest intent</b>, and can catch <b>any person</b> knowingly party to it, not just directors."],[8,"Which is paid first on liquidation?",["Fixed charge holder from fixed charge assets","Unsecured creditors","Shareholders"],"The <b>fixed charge</b> holder is paid from the charged asset first. Then expenses, preferential creditors, prescribed part, floating charge, unsecured, shareholders."],[9,"Which preferential claim is capped at £800 per employee?",["Unpaid wages for the last 4 months","HMRC VAT","Directors' fees"],"<b>Ordinary preferential</b> claims include wages for 4 months up to <b>£800</b> each, plus holiday pay. HMRC is secondary preferential."],[10,"What is the 'prescribed part'?",["A share of floating charge assets set aside for unsecured creditors","A bank's fee","Shareholders' share"],"A part of <b>floating charge</b> realisations (<b>50% of first £10,000, 20% after</b>, cap £800,000) set aside for <b>unsecured</b> creditors."],[11,"For how long is re-use of a failed company's name restricted (s.216)?",["5 years","2 years","10 years"],"<b>s.216</b>: a director in the <b>12 months</b> before insolvent liquidation cannot use the name (or similar) for <b>5 years</b> without leave."]],"c":[[454,"What is a transaction at an undervalue?","A <b>gift</b>, a transaction for <b>no consideration</b>, or for consideration <b>significantly less</b> in value than what the company gives (<b>s.238(4)</b>)."],[455,"What is the time limit for a transaction at an undervalue?","<b>2 years</b> before the onset of insolvency, whether or not the other party is connected."],[456,"When is insolvency presumed for a transaction at an undervalue?","When the other party is <b>connected</b>. They must prove the company was solvent."],[457,"What is the defence to a transaction at an undervalue?","The company acted <b>in good faith</b>, <b>for the purpose of carrying on its business</b>, with <b>reasonable grounds</b> for believing it would <b>benefit the company</b> (<b>s.238(5)</b>)."],[464,"What are the time limits for a preference?","<b>6 months</b> if <b>unconnected</b>. <b>2 years</b> if <b>connected</b>."],[466,"When is a floating charge invalid under s.245?","If created within the relevant period, <b>except</b> to the extent of <b>new consideration</b> given <b>at the same time as or after</b> creation."],[468,"What are the relevant periods for s.245?","<b>Unconnected</b>: <b>12 months</b>, and only if the company was <b>unable to pay its debts</b> at the time or as a result. <b>Connected</b>: <b>2 years</b>, solvency <b>irrelevant</b>."],[473,"What are the elements of wrongful trading?","Company in <b>insolvent liquidation or administration</b>. Before that, a director <b>knew or ought to have concluded</b> there was <b>no reasonable prospect</b> of avoiding it (<b>s.214</b>)."],[476,"What is the defence to wrongful trading?","The director took <b>every step</b> to <b>minimise potential loss to creditors</b> (<b>s.214(3)</b>), e.g. IP advice, board meetings, stopping credit. <b>Resigning</b> is rarely enough."],[486,"What is the order of priority on insolvency?","<b>Fixed charges</b>. <b>Expenses</b>. <b>Ordinary preferential</b>. <b>Secondary preferential</b>. <b>Prescribed part</b>. <b>Floating charges</b>. <b>Unsecured</b>. Statutory interest and deferred debts. <b>Shareholders</b>."],[492,"How is the prescribed part calculated?","<b>50% of the first £10,000</b> plus <b>20% of the rest</b> of net floating charge realisations. Capped at <b>£800,000</b> (charges from <b>6 April 2020</b>; <b>£600,000</b> earlier)."],[710,"What must a liquidator show for a preference, and when is the desire to prefer presumed?","The company put a <b>creditor or guarantor</b> in a <b>better position</b> on insolvent liquidation (<b>s.239(4)</b>), and was <b>influenced by a desire</b> to do so (<b>s.239(5)</b>). Desire is <b>presumed</b> if the person is <b>connected</b> (not merely as an employee). Acting under <b>commercial pressure</b> is not a desire to prefer."]]},{"id":"personal-insolvency","title":"Personal insolvency: bankruptcy and IVAs","q":[[0,"What minimum debt is needed to petition for someone's bankruptcy?",["£5,000","£750","£1,000"],"The <b>bankruptcy level</b> is <b>£5,000</b>. A creditor typically serves a <b>statutory demand</b> first."],[1,"How long does a debtor have to comply with a statutory demand?",["21 days","14 days","28 days"],"The debtor has <b>3 weeks (21 days)</b> to pay, secure or compound the debt. Setting aside must be applied for within <b>18 days</b>."],[2,"Who makes a bankruptcy order on an online debtor application?",["The Adjudicator","A High Court judge","The Official Receiver"],"An online application is decided by the <b>Adjudicator</b>, normally within <b>28 days</b>, without a hearing."],[3,"What majority approves an IVA?",["75% in value of creditors voting","50% in value","100% in number"],"An <b>IVA</b> needs <b>75% by value</b> of voting creditors, and fails if over 50% of unconnected creditors vote against."],[4,"Who is bound by an approved IVA?",["Unsecured creditors, even if they voted against","Secured creditors","Preferential creditors"],"An <b>IVA</b> binds all <b>unsecured</b> creditors entitled to vote, but not <b>secured</b> or <b>preferential</b> creditors without consent."],[5,"How long does a bankrupt normally wait for automatic discharge?",["1 year","3 years","5 years"],"Discharge is normally after <b>1 year</b>. An <b>income payments order</b> can run up to 3 years."],[6,"What happens to the bankrupt's property on the bankruptcy order?",["It vests in the trustee in bankruptcy","It stays with the bankrupt","It goes to the court"],"The bankrupt's estate <b>vests in the trustee</b>, who realises it for creditors."],[7,"Can a trustee claim an inheritance received after the order but before discharge?",["Yes","No","Only if over £10,000"],"<b>s.307</b>: <b>after-acquired property</b> can be claimed by the trustee until discharge."],[8,"After how long does the trustee's interest in the bankrupt's home revert to the bankrupt?",["3 years","1 year","5 years"],"<b>s.283A</b>: if the trustee has not dealt with the home within <b>3 years</b>, the interest <b>re-vests</b> in the bankrupt."],[9,"Above what sum must a bankrupt disclose his bankruptcy when obtaining credit?",["£500","£100","£1,000"],"<b>s.360</b>: obtaining credit above <b>£500</b> without disclosing the bankruptcy is an offence."],[10,"What is the lookback for a transaction at an undervalue in bankruptcy (s.341)?",["5 years","2 years","6 months"],"The lookback is <b>5 years</b>. Within <b>2 years</b> insolvency need not be proved; between 2 and 5 it must."],[11,"Who is paid first from the bankrupt's estate after fixed security?",["Costs and expenses","Unsecured creditors","The bankrupt"],"The order: secured creditors, <b>costs and expenses</b>, <b>preferential</b> creditors, then <b>unsecured</b>, then interest, then the bankrupt."]],"c":[[498,"What is an IVA?","A <b>legally binding agreement</b> with creditors to pay <b>all or part</b> of the debts, usually from <b>income over a period</b> (often 5 years). The balance is <b>written off</b>. Supervised by an IP <b>supervisor</b>."],[502,"What are the IVA approval thresholds?","At least <b>75% in value</b> of creditors <b>voting</b>. Not approved if more than <b>50% in value</b> of <b>unconnected creditors</b> voting oppose it."],[507,"What are the conditions for a creditor's bankruptcy petition?","Debt at least <b>£5,000</b>. A <b>liquidated sum</b>. <b>Unsecured</b>. Debtor <b>appears unable to pay</b>. No pending application to set aside a statutory demand."],[512,"What happens to the bankrupt's estate?","It <b>vests automatically</b> in the <b>trustee</b> on appointment (<b>s.306</b>). The <b>Official Receiver</b> initially takes control."],[513,"What property is excluded from the bankrupt's estate?","<b>Tools of the trade</b> necessary <b>personally</b> for work. <b>Basic domestic items</b> for the family. Property held <b>on trust</b>. Most <b>pension</b> rights."],[519,"How does the court decide an order for sale after one year?","It <b>assumes</b> the <b>creditors' interests outweigh all other considerations</b>, <b>unless the circumstances are exceptional</b>."],[528,"When is a bankrupt discharged?","<b>Automatically one year</b> after the order (<b>s.279(1)</b>). Can be <b>suspended</b> for non-cooperation."],[533,"What is the time limit and insolvency rule for a bankruptcy undervalue?","Within <b>5 years</b> before the petition. Within <b>2 years</b>: solvency <b>irrelevant</b>. <b>2 to 5 years</b>: insolvency required, <b>presumed</b> if the other party is an <b>associate</b>."],[535,"What are the time limits for a preference in bankruptcy?","<b>6 months</b> before the petition, or <b>2 years</b> for an <b>associate</b>. Insolvency required. Desire <b>presumed</b> for an associate (not just an employee) (<b>s.340</b>)."]]},{"id":"income-tax","title":"Income tax: employees, sole traders, partners, shareholders and lenders","q":[[0,"What is the personal allowance for 2026/27?",["£12,570","£10,000","£15,000"],"The standard <b>personal allowance</b> is <b>£12,570</b>, reduced by £1 for every £2 of adjusted net income over <b>£100,000</b>."],[1,"At what income is the personal allowance lost completely?",["£125,140","£100,000","£150,000"],"The allowance reduces by <b>£1 for each £2</b> above £100,000, so it is nil at <b>£125,140</b>."],[2,"What is the basic rate band for income tax?",["First £37,700 at 20%","First £50,270 at 20%","First £12,570 at 20%"],"The <b>basic rate</b> band is <b>£37,700</b> (20%) after the personal allowance. Higher rate 40% to £125,140, then 45%."],[3,"What is the dividend allowance?",["£500","£1,000","£2,000"],"The <b>dividend allowance</b> is <b>£500</b>, taxed at 0%. It still uses up part of the rate band."],[4,"Which NICs does a sole trader pay on profits?",["Class 4","Class 1 primary","Class 1A"],"A sole trader pays <b>Class 4</b> NICs: <b>6%</b> on profits between £12,570 and £50,270, 2% above."],[5,"Which NICs does an employer pay on benefits provided to employees?",["Class 1A","Class 4","Class 2"],"<b>Class 1A</b> (15%) is paid by the <b>employer</b> on most taxable benefits in kind."],[6,"Which relief lets a loss in a new trade's first 4 years be set against income of earlier years?",["s.72 early years relief","s.64 general relief","s.89 terminal relief"],"<b>s.72</b>: a loss in the <b>first 4 tax years</b> may be set against total income of the <b>3 previous years</b>, earliest first."],[7,"Which loss relief covers the final 12 months of trading?",["s.89 terminal loss relief","s.72","s.64"],"<b>s.89</b>: a <b>terminal loss</b> is set against the trade's profits of the final year and <b>3 previous years</b>, latest first."],[8,"A sole trader has a loss. Which relief sets it against the same year's other income?",["s.64 sideways relief","s.89","Group relief"],"<b>s.64</b> allows a trading loss to be set against <b>total income</b> of the loss year and/or the <b>previous year</b>."],[9,"What is the annual investment allowance for plant and machinery?",["100% on the first £1 million","50% on any amount","18% a year"],"The <b>AIA</b> gives <b>100%</b> relief on the first <b>£1 million</b> of qualifying spend each year."],[10,"When are payments on account due?",["31 January and 31 July","31 March and 30 September","1 April and 1 October"],"Two payments on account each <b>50%</b> of last year's liability, due <b>31 January</b> and <b>31 July</b>."],[11,"Partners are taxed on what?",["Their individual share of the firm's profits","The firm's profit as a whole","Only drawings"],"A partnership is <b>transparent</b>: each partner is <b>taxed on his own share</b> of profits, whether drawn or not."]],"c":[[543,"What are the steps of the income tax calculation?","<b>Total income</b>. Deduct <b>reliefs</b> = net income. Deduct <b>personal allowance</b> = taxable income. Apply <b>rates</b> in order. Add up and deduct <b>tax already paid</b>."],[546,"What is the personal allowance and how is it reduced?","<b>£12,570</b>. Reduced <b>£1 for every £2</b> of adjusted net income over <b>£100,000</b>. Lost entirely at <b>£125,140</b>."],[548,"What are the basic, higher and additional rates for non-savings and savings income?","<b>20%</b> on the first <b>£37,700</b> of taxable income. <b>40%</b> up to <b>£125,140</b>. <b>45%</b> above."],[549,"What are the dividend rates for 2026/27?","<b>10.75%</b> basic. <b>35.75%</b> higher. <b>39.35%</b> additional. The basic and higher rates rose by <b>2 points</b> from 6 April 2026."],[550,"What is the personal savings allowance?","<b>£1,000</b> at 0% for a <b>basic rate</b> taxpayer. <b>£500</b> for <b>higher rate</b>. <b>£0</b> for <b>additional rate</b>."],[551,"What is the dividend allowance?","The first <b>£500</b> of dividends taxed at <b>0%</b>, for everyone."],[563,"When is a sole trader's expense deductible?","If <b>revenue</b>, not capital, and incurred <b>wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade</b> (<b>s.34</b>). An identifiable business part of a mixed cost can be deducted."],[571,"How does s.64 general loss relief work?","Set against <b>total income</b> of the <b>loss year</b> and/or the <b>previous year</b>. Subject to the cap. Unused part can go against <b>capital gains</b> (<b>s.71</b>)."],[572,"How does s.72 early years relief work?","A loss in the <b>first 4 tax years</b> of a trade. Set against <b>total income</b> of the <b>3 previous tax years</b>, <b>earliest first</b>."],[576,"How are partners taxed?","The firm is <b>tax transparent</b>. Each partner is taxed <b>as a sole trader</b> on their <b>share</b> of the firm's profits, with income tax and <b>Class 4 NICs</b> via <b>self-assessment</b>."]]},{"id":"cgt","title":"Capital gains tax and business reliefs","q":[[0,"What is the CGT annual exempt amount for 2026/27?",["£3,000","£6,000","£12,300"],"The <b>annual exempt amount</b> is <b>£3,000</b>. It cannot be carried forward."],[1,"What are the main CGT rates for individuals?",["18% and 24%","10% and 20%","20% and 40%"],"Gains are taxed at <b>18%</b> within the unused basic rate band and <b>24%</b> above."],[2,"What is the BADR rate from 6 April 2026?",["18%","10%","14%"],"<b>Business asset disposal relief</b> is <b>18%</b> on up to a <b>£1m</b> lifetime limit of gains."],[3,"For BADR on shares, how long must the 5% holding be held?",["2 years","12 months","5 years"],"The conditions must be met for <b>2 years</b> before disposal: <b>5%</b> of shares and votes, <b>trading</b> company, <b>officer or employee</b>."],[4,"A director holds 4% of a trading company for 3 years. Does BADR apply on sale?",["No","Yes","Yes, if she works full time"],"BADR needs at least <b>5%</b> of ordinary shares and votes (a personal company). <b>4%</b> does not qualify."],[5,"What is the CGT rule on a sale between spouses living together?",["No gain, no loss","Taxed at market value","Taxed at 24%"],"<b>s.58</b>: disposals between <b>spouses</b> are on a <b>no gain / no loss</b> basis. The recipient takes the transferor's cost."],[6,"An owner dies holding shares. Is there CGT on death?",["No, heirs take probate value as cost","Yes, at 24% on the estate","Yes, at 18% on the estate"],"Death is <b>not</b> a disposal for CGT: assets are <b>uplifted</b> to <b>market value</b> on death."],[7,"Which shares are matched first on a disposal?",["Same day acquisitions","The share pool","Oldest shares"],"<b>Share matching</b>: same-day first, then the next <b>30 days</b>, then the <b>s.104 pool</b>."],[8,"Rollover relief (s.152) applies if the new asset is bought when?",["1 year before to 3 years after","3 months before to 1 year after","Within 5 years after"],"Reinvestment must be made from <b>1 year before</b> to <b>3 years after</b> the disposal of the old asset."],[9,"Which assets qualify for rollover relief?",["Land, buildings and fixed plant","Shares","Cars"],"Rollover applies to <b>qualifying business assets</b>: <b>land and buildings</b> and <b>fixed plant</b> used in a trade."],[10,"A chattel is sold for £5,000. What is the CGT position?",["Exempt","Taxable at 24%","Taxable at 18%"],"Chattels sold for <b>£6,000 or less</b> are exempt."],[11,"By when is CGT on shares paid?",["31 January after the tax year","30 April","31 July"],"CGT is reported and paid through self-assessment by <b>31 January</b> following the tax year of disposal."]],"c":[[594,"Is death a disposal for CGT?","<b>No</b>. No CGT on death. The heir takes the asset at <b>probate value</b> (the <b>tax-free uplift</b>)."],[595,"How are disposals between spouses or civil partners treated?","<b>No gain / no loss</b> (<b>s.58</b>). The recipient takes over the transferor's <b>original base cost</b>."],[599,"What can be deducted in calculating a gain?","<b>Incidental costs of disposal</b>. <b>Acquisition cost</b> and <b>incidental acquisition costs</b>. <b>Enhancement expenditure</b>. No <b>indexation</b> for individuals."],[602,"What is an individual's capital gains tax annual exempt amount for 2026/27?","<b>£3,000</b> of net gains. It is deducted <b>after losses</b> and is applied first to gains taxed at the <b>highest rate</b> (non-BADR gains)."],[603,"What are the CGT rates from 30 October 2024?","Gains go <b>on top of taxable income</b>. <b>18%</b> within any <b>unused basic rate band</b>. <b>24%</b> above."],[604,"What is the BADR rate, lifetime limit and interaction with the basic rate band?","<b>18%</b> from <b>6 April 2026</b>, on up to <b>£1 million</b> of lifetime gains (Budget 2026: falling to <b>£500,000</b> for disposals from <b>6 April 2027</b>). BADR gains use up the basic rate band <b>first</b>, so other gains are more likely taxed at <b>24%</b>."],[610,"What conditions apply to BADR on shares?","Throughout <b>2 years</b>: a <b>trading company</b>. Personal company: at least <b>5%</b> of shares <b>and</b> votes, plus 5% economic interest. The individual is an <b>officer or employee</b>."],[613,"How does rollover relief work?","A gain on a <b>qualifying business asset</b> is <b>deducted from the base cost</b> of a <b>replacement</b> qualifying asset. Tax is deferred until the new asset is sold."],[619,"How does gift relief work?","The gain on a gift of business assets is <b>held over</b>. The donor pays nothing now. The <b>recipient's base cost is reduced</b> by the held-over gain."],[625,"How does incorporation relief operate?","For transfers on or after <b>6 April 2026</b> the relief must be <b>claimed</b> (by the first anniversary of the 31 January after the tax year of transfer; s.162 as amended by FA 2026). Before then it was automatic unless the taxpayer elected out. The gain is <b>deducted from the base cost of the shares</b>."]]},{"id":"corporation-tax","title":"Corporation tax","q":[[0,"What is the main rate of corporation tax?",["25%","19%","20%"],"The <b>main rate</b> is <b>25%</b> on profits over <b>£250,000</b>. The <b>small profits rate</b> is <b>19%</b> up to <b>£50,000</b>."],[1,"Between £50,000 and £250,000 profits, what applies?",["25% less marginal relief","19% only","20% only"],"Between the limits tax is at <b>25% less marginal relief</b>, effective rate 26.5% on the band."],[2,"How do associated companies affect the limits?",["They are divided equally between the companies","They double","They have no effect"],"The <b>£50,000/£250,000</b> limits are <b>divided</b> by the number of <b>associated companies</b>."],[3,"When is corporation tax due for a normal company?",["9 months and 1 day after the accounting period","12 months after","31 January"],"Non-large companies pay <b>9 months and 1 day</b> after the end of the accounting period."],[4,"How long does a company have to file its CT600?",["12 months after the end of the period","9 months","6 months"],"The <b>CT600</b> return is due within <b>12 months</b> of the end of the accounting period."],[5,"Is interest paid by a company deductible?",["Yes","No","Only if secured"],"<b>Interest</b> on trading loans is deductible. <b>Dividends</b> are not deductible."],[6,"Does a company claim the CGT annual exempt amount?",["No","Yes","Only if small"],"<b>Companies</b> pay <b>corporation tax</b> on gains and have <b>no annual exempt amount</b>."],[7,"Which relief lets a company's trading loss be set against profits of the previous 12 months?",["s.37 carry back","Group relief","s.72"],"<b>s.37</b>: current period first, then <b>carry back 12 months</b>. A 3-year carry back applies only to terminal losses."],[8,"What group share holding allows group relief?",["75%","51%","90%"],"<b>Group relief</b> applies between companies in a <b>75% group</b>."],[9,"A close company lends its director £50,000. What tax result follows?",["A s.455 charge on the company","No tax","CGT on the director"],"<b>s.455</b>: a close company that lends to a participator pays a tax charge, <b>refunded</b> when the loan is repaid."],[10,"How is a director's salary treated by the company?",["Deductible","Not deductible","Taxable as profit"],"<b>Salary</b> is a deductible trading expense. <b>Dividends</b> are paid out of profits after tax."],[11,"How soon must a new company notify HMRC it is within charge to corporation tax?",["3 months of starting to trade","12 months","6 months"],"Notify HMRC within <b>3 months</b> of starting to trade."]],"c":[[641,"How do company chargeable gains differ from individuals' gains?","Companies get <b>indexation</b>, <b>frozen at December 2017</b>, which cannot create or increase a loss. <b>No annual exempt amount</b>. Taxed at the <b>corporation tax rate</b>."],[645,"How does s.37 current period and carry-back loss relief work?","Set the loss against <b>total profits of the same AP</b> first. Then carry back against total profits of the <b>previous 12 months</b>."],[646,"How are post-April 2017 company losses carried forward?","Against <b>total profits</b> of later APs, by claim (<b>s.45A</b>). Above a <b>£5 million</b> allowance, only <b>50%</b> of profits can be relieved."],[650,"What are the corporation tax rates for FY2026?","<b>19%</b> up to <b>£50,000</b>. <b>25%</b> over <b>£250,000</b>. In between: <b>25% less marginal relief</b>."],[654,"When is corporation tax payable by a company that is not large?","<b>9 months and 1 day</b> after the end of the AP."],[655,"When do large companies pay corporation tax?","Profits over <b>£1.5 million</b>: <b>quarterly instalments</b> in months <b>7, 10, 13 and 16</b>. Over <b>£20 million</b>: months 3, 6, 9 and 12."],[658,"Which profit extraction methods are deductible for the company?","<b>Salary</b>, <b>interest</b>, employer <b>pension contributions</b> and <b>rent</b>. <b>Dividends are not</b>."],[660,"What is a close company?","A UK company <b>controlled</b> by <b>five or fewer participators</b>, or by any number of participators who are <b>directors</b> (<b>s.439</b>)."],[661,"What is the s.455 charge?","A close company's loan to a <b>participator</b> not repaid within <b>9 months</b> of the end of the AP. Temporary charge of <b>35.75%</b> (loans from 6 April 2026). <b>Refunded</b> when repaid."]]},{"id":"vat-iht-bpr","title":"VAT and inheritance tax business property relief","q":[[0,"What is the standard rate of VAT?",["20%","5%","17.5%"],"The <b>standard rate</b> is <b>20%</b>. Reduced <b>5%</b>, zero-rated <b>0%</b>."],[1,"What is the VAT registration threshold?",["£90,000","£85,000","£100,000"],"A business must register when taxable turnover exceeds <b>£90,000</b> in any rolling 12 months (look-back test)."],[2,"Within what time must a business notify HMRC after the historic test is met?",["30 days from the end of the month","7 days","3 months"],"Notify within <b>30 days</b> of the end of the month in which the threshold was exceeded."],[3,"Do zero-rated supplies count towards the registration threshold?",["Yes","No","Only if exports"],"<b>Zero-rated</b> supplies are <b>taxable</b> at 0%, so they count. <b>Exempt</b> supplies do not."],[4,"Can an exempt supplier recover input VAT?",["No","Yes","Only half"],"An <b>exempt</b> supplier charges no VAT and <b>cannot recover</b> input tax. A zero-rated supplier can."],[5,"A price is £1,200 including VAT at 20%. How much is VAT?",["£200","£240","£120"],"The <b>VAT fraction</b> is <b>1/6</b> of the VAT-inclusive price: £1,200 / 6 = <b>£200</b>."],[6,"How soon after the VAT period are the return and payment due?",["1 month and 7 days","7 days","28 days"],"Electronic returns and payments are due <b>1 month and 7 days</b> after the period ends."],[7,"What is the IHT nil-rate band?",["£325,000","£175,000","£500,000"],"The <b>nil-rate band</b> is <b>£325,000</b>. The <b>residence</b> nil-rate band is up to <b>£175,000</b> extra. Death rate is <b>40%</b>."],[8,"A gift to an individual is made. When does it become chargeable?",["If the donor dies within 7 years","If the donor dies within 14 years","At once"],"A <b>PET</b> becomes chargeable only if the donor dies <b>within 7 years</b>. <b>Taper relief</b> applies after 3 years."],[9,"A man gives £500,000 to his wife. Both are long-term UK residents. IHT?",["Exempt without limit","Exempt up to £325,000","Taxed at 40%"],"Transfers between <b>spouses or civil partners</b> are <b>exempt</b> without limit (a cap applies only if the recipient is not a <b>long-term UK resident</b>)."],[10,"What BPR applies to unquoted trading company shares?",["100%","50%","25%"],"<b>Unquoted trading company</b> shares of any size get <b>100%</b> BPR, subject to the allowance. AIM shares get 50%."],[11,"How long must business property be owned for BPR?",["2 years","1 year","5 years"],"<b>s.106</b>: the transferor must have owned the property for <b>2 years</b> before the transfer."]],"c":[[672,"How does zero-rated differ from exempt?","<b>Zero-rated</b> supplies are <b>taxable</b> at 0%: they count for registration and input tax is <b>recoverable</b>. <b>Exempt</b> supplies do <b>not</b> count and related input tax is <b>not recoverable</b>."],[681,"What is taxable turnover and the registration threshold?","The value of <b>taxable supplies</b> (including <b>zero-rated</b>), excluding VAT, <b>exempt</b> supplies and capital asset sales. Threshold <b>£90,000</b>."],[682,"What is the historic registration test?","At the <b>end of any month</b>, taxable supplies in the <b>previous 12 months</b> (rolling) <b>exceed £90,000</b>. Notify within <b>30 days</b>. Registered from the <b>first day of the second month</b> after."],[684,"What is the future registration test?","Reasonable grounds to believe taxable supplies in the <b>next 30 days alone</b> will <b>exceed £90,000</b>. Notify <b>by the end</b> of the 30 days. Registered from the <b>start</b> of the period."],[690,"What is the 14-day rule?","If a VAT invoice is issued <b>within 14 days after</b> the basic tax point, the <b>invoice date</b> becomes the tax point."],[699,"Which property gets 100% BPR?","A <b>business</b> or <b>interest in a business</b> (including a partner's share). <b>Unquoted shares</b> in a trading company, <b>any size</b> of holding. Subject to the <b>£2.5m allowance</b>."],[700,"Which property gets 50% BPR?","<b>AIM</b> shares (from 6 April 2026). <b>Controlling</b> holdings of <b>listed</b> shares. Personally owned <b>land, buildings or plant</b> used by a <b>controlled company</b> or the transferor's <b>partnership</b>."],[702,"What is the £2.5 million BPR/APR allowance?","From <b>6 April 2026</b>, <b>100%</b> relief applies only to the <b>first £2.5m</b> of qualifying property. Above that, <b>50%</b> relief."],[705,"What is the BPR ownership condition?","The transferor must have <b>owned</b> the property for <b>2 years</b> before the transfer. A <b>spouse's</b> ownership counts if inherited from them, and <b>replacement property</b> rules apply."],[706,"Which businesses are excluded from BPR?","Those <b>wholly or mainly</b> dealing in securities, land or buildings, or <b>making or holding investments</b>, e.g. property letting. \"Mainly\" means more than 50%."]]}]},{"id":"dr","title":"Dispute Resolution","stop":2,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M-62,0 L-30,-62 L2,0\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"-30\" cy=\"-62\" r=\"46\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\"/><path d=\"M-30,-62 L16,-62 M-30,-62 L2.526911934581186,-29.473088065418814 M-30,-62 L-29.999999999999996,-16 M-30,-62 L-62.526911934581186,-29.473088065418814 M-30,-62 L-76,-61.99999999999999 M-30,-62 L-62.52691193458119,-94.5269119345812 M-30,-62 L-30.000000000000007,-108 M-30,-62 L2.526911934581179,-94.5269119345812 \" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-30\" cy=\"-62\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#E63946\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"-65\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-3.473088065418814\" y=\"-32.473088065418814\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-36\" y=\"-19\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-68.5269119345812\" y=\"-32.473088065418814\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-82\" y=\"-65\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-68.5269119345812\" y=\"-97.5269119345812\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-36.00000000000001\" y=\"-111\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-3.473088065418821\" y=\"-97.5269119345812\" width=\"12\" height=\"11\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><polygon points=\"34,0 70,0 62,-96 42,-96\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M34,-6 L69,-26 M37,-40 L65,-58 M40,-72 L63,-88\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"12\"/><path d=\"M34,-6 L69,-26 M37,-40 L65,-58 M40,-72 L63,-88\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><polygon points=\"36,-96 52,-126 68,-96\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\"/><path d=\"M52,-126 V-138\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"52,-138 64,-134 52,-130\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"dr-options","title":"Options: arbitration, mediation and litigation","q":[[0,"A party starts court proceedings despite an arbitration clause. Is a stay mandatory?",["Yes, unless the agreement is void or inoperative","No, it is in the judge's discretion","Only if the claim is over £100,000"],"Under <b>s.9 AA 1996</b> the court must grant a <b>stay</b> unless the arbitration agreement is null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed."],[1,"A defendant wants a s.9 stay. What must it avoid doing first?",["Filing a defence","Filing an acknowledgment of service","Telling the claimant about the clause"],"The stay application must come <b>before any step to answer the substantive claim</b>, such as a defence. An acknowledgment of service is fine."],[2,"How long to challenge an award under ss.67-69 of the Arbitration Act 1996?",["28 days","14 days","3 months"],"Any challenge must be brought within <b>28 days</b> of the award (s.70(3))."],[3,"A tribunal ignores a party's right to put its case, causing substantial injustice. Which section?",["s.68 serious irregularity","s.67 jurisdiction","s.69 point of law"],"<b>s.68</b> covers <b>serious irregularity</b> causing substantial injustice, such as breach of the tribunal's s.33 duty to act fairly."],[4,"On a s.69 appeal, can the court reopen the arbitrator's findings of fact?",["No","Yes, under s.69","Yes, if the award is large"],"A challenge cannot reopen <b>findings of fact</b>. A s.69 appeal is only on a <b>point of law</b>."],[5,"Which Convention helps enforce arbitration awards in other countries?",["New York Convention 1958","Hague Convention 1965","Rome Convention 1980"],"The <b>New York Convention 1958</b> (170+ countries) makes awards easier to enforce abroad than most court judgments."],[6,"English-law contract, arbitration seated in Paris, no law chosen for the clause. Which law governs it?",["French law, the law of the seat","English law, the main contract","The law of the claimant's home"],"Under <b>s.6A</b> (Arbitration Act 2025), absent an express choice for the agreement itself, the <b>law of the seat</b> applies."],[7,"What does a mediator do?",["Helps the parties reach their own settlement","Decides who is right","Gives a binding ruling on law"],"A <b>mediator</b> facilitates only. Nothing is binding until a written <b>settlement agreement</b> is signed."],[8,"The claim has started and settles with confidential terms. Which order suits?",["A Tomlin order","A default judgment","A summary judgment"],"A <b>Tomlin order</b> stays the claim on terms in a <b>confidential schedule</b>, with liberty to apply to enforce them."],[9,"A winning claimant ignored two invitations to mediate. What is the risk?",["A reduction in its costs","Losing the case","Having its claim struck out"],"Silence is unreasonable (<b>PGF II</b>). The sanction is a <b>costs</b> penalty, not losing the case."],[10,"Which case held a court can lawfully order the parties into ADR?",["Churchill v Merthyr Tydfil","Halsey v Milton Keynes","Salomon v Salomon"],"<b>Churchill</b> (2023): the court can order ADR or stay proceedings for it, if proportionate and the right to a fair trial is not impaired."],[11,"A client needs an urgent injunction against a former supplier. Best route?",["Litigation","Mediation","Arbitration"],"<b>Litigation</b> gives full powers to grant <b>interim relief</b> fast. Mediation and arbitration are weaker for urgency."]],"c":[[0,"What are the three main routes for resolving a civil dispute?","<b>Litigation</b>: a judge decides under the <b>CPR</b>. <b>Arbitration</b>: a private tribunal makes a binding <b>award</b>. <b>Mediation</b>: a neutral helps the parties reach their own settlement."],[9,"When must the court stay proceedings in favour of arbitration?","On application under <b>s.9</b>, the stay is <b>mandatory</b> unless the arbitration agreement is <b>null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed</b>."],[10,"What must a party do to obtain a s.9 stay?","File an <b>acknowledgment of service</b> and apply <b>before taking any step to answer the substantive claim</b>. Filing a <b>defence</b> first loses the right."],[11,"What is the time limit for challenging an arbitration award?","<b>28 days</b> from the award (<b>s.70(3)</b>), after exhausting any arbitral appeal or review."],[12,"On what grounds can an award be challenged?","<b>s.67</b>: lack of <b>substantive jurisdiction</b>. <b>s.68</b>: <b>serious irregularity</b> causing <b>substantial injustice</b>. <b>s.69</b>: appeal on a <b>point of law</b>."],[20,"What is mediation?","A <b>voluntary</b>, <b>confidential</b> process where a neutral mediator helps the parties <b>negotiate</b> a settlement. The mediator <b>does not decide</b> and <b>cannot impose</b> an outcome."],[24,"How is a settlement recorded after proceedings have started?","A <b>consent order</b>, enforceable as a judgment. Or a <b>Tomlin order</b>: claim <b>stayed</b> on terms in a <b>confidential schedule</b>, with <b>liberty to apply</b>."],[30,"Can a winning party be penalised for refusing ADR?","<b>Yes</b>, in <b>costs</b>, if the refusal was <b>unreasonable</b>."],[31,"What factors decide whether refusing ADR was unreasonable?","<b>Nature of the dispute</b>. <b>Merits</b>. <b>Other settlement methods</b> tried. <b>Costs</b> of ADR. Prejudicial <b>delay</b>. <b>Reasonable prospect of success</b> (Halsey)."],[34,"What CPR changes from 1 October 2024 concern ADR?","The <b>overriding objective</b> includes <b>promoting or using ADR</b>. The court can <b>order</b> ADR (<b>CPR 3.1</b>). Costs (<b>CPR 44.2</b>) consider failure to comply with an ADR order or <b>unreasonable failure to engage</b>."]]},{"id":"dr-limitation","title":"Limitation periods","q":[[0,"What is the limitation period for a simple contract claim?",["6 years from breach","6 years from discovery","3 years from breach"],"<b>6 years from breach</b> (s.5 LA 1980), even if the claimant has not yet discovered the breach."],[1,"A contract made by deed is breached. How long to sue?",["12 years","6 years","15 years"],"A <b>specialty</b> (deed) has <b>12 years</b> from breach under s.8 LA 1980."],[2,"When does time start for a negligence claim needing damage?",["When the damage occurs","When the negligent act happens","When the claimant discovers it"],"Negligence is not complete without damage, so time runs from when the <b>damage</b> occurs (6 years, s.2)."],[3,"What is the basic limitation period for a personal injury claim?",["3 years","6 years","1 year"],"<b>3 years</b> from the later of the injury and the claimant's <b>date of knowledge</b> (ss.11 and 14)."],[4,"For personal injury, does the claimant need to know the act was legally negligent for time to start?",["No, ignorance of the law is irrelevant","Yes, they must know it was negligent","Only if they have a solicitor"],"<b>Date of knowledge</b> concerns facts (significant, attributable, who the defendant is). Knowing it was legally negligent is <b>not</b> needed."],[5,"Which section lets the court allow an out-of-time personal injury claim?",["s.33","s.32","s.14B"],"<b>s.33 LA 1980</b> gives a discretion if it is <b>equitable</b>, weighing prejudice. It applies only to PI and fatal accident claims."],[6,"What is the longstop for latent damage negligence claims?",["15 years from the negligent act","15 years from the damage","10 years from discovery"],"<b>s.14B</b>: an absolute <b>15-year</b> bar from the negligent act or omission. The court cannot extend it."],[7,"A child is injured aged 10 and has a PI claim. When does the 3 years end?",["Their 21st birthday","Their 18th birthday","Their 13th birthday"],"Time does not run during minority (s.28). It starts at 18, so a PI claim expires on the <b>21st birthday</b>."],[8,"A builder deliberately hides defective work. Which section may postpone time?",["s.32 deliberate concealment","s.28 disability","s.29 acknowledgment"],"<b>s.32</b>: where a relevant fact is deliberately concealed, time runs from when the claimant discovered it or could with reasonable diligence have done so."],[9,"A debtor signs a written acknowledgment of the debt before expiry. Effect?",["The period restarts","Nothing changes","The debt is extinguished"],"A signed, written <b>acknowledgment</b> (or part payment) of a liquidated debt before expiry <b>restarts</b> the period (ss.29-30)."],[10,"Breach of contract on 12 May 2020. Last day to issue the claim form?",["12 May 2026","11 May 2026","13 May 2026"],"The day of accrual is excluded (<b>Marren v Dawson Bentley</b>), so the last day to issue is the <b>anniversary</b>, 12 May 2026."],[11,"Limitation is close and pre-action steps are not finished. What should the claimant do?",["Issue protectively and seek a stay","Wait for the protocol to finish","Ask the defendant to ignore the deadline"],"<b>Protocols do not stop time running.</b> Issue protectively, then stay the claim, or agree a <b>standstill agreement</b>."]],"c":[[37,"What is the effect of a limitation period expiring?","The claim is <b>time-barred</b>. The defendant has a complete <b>defence</b>, however good the claim."],[42,"What is the limitation period for a simple contract?","<b>6 years</b> from the date of <b>breach</b> (<b>s.5</b>), even if the breach is not discovered and loss occurs later."],[43,"What is the limitation period for a contract by deed?","<b>12 years</b> from breach (<b>s.8</b>)."],[45,"When does time run for a negligence claim (not personal injury)?","<b>6 years</b> from when the <b>damage</b> occurs (<b>s.2</b>), not from the negligent act."],[47,"What is the limitation period for personal injury?","<b>3 years</b> from the <b>later</b> of the date of injury and the claimant's <b>date of knowledge</b> (<b>ss.11, 14</b>)."],[48,"What must the claimant know for the PI date of knowledge?","The injury was <b>significant</b>. It was <b>attributable</b> to the act or omission. The <b>identity of the defendant</b>. If someone else is responsible, their identity and supporting facts."],[52,"When can the court disapply the PI limitation period?","Under <b>s.33</b> if it would be <b>equitable</b>, weighing the prejudice to each side."],[55,"What is the latent damage period in negligence?","The <b>later</b> of <b>6 years</b> from the damage, or <b>3 years</b> from the <b>starting date</b> (knowledge and right to sue) (<b>s.14A</b>)."],[56,"What is the latent damage longstop?","<b>15 years</b> from the <b>negligent act or omission</b> (<b>s.14B</b>). Absolute: applies even if undiscovered and cannot be extended."],[62,"By when must a child bring a PI claim, and a contract claim?","PI: by their <b>21st birthday</b>. Contract or ordinary tort: by their <b>24th birthday</b>."],[64,"How do fraud, concealment or mistake affect limitation?","Time does not start until the claimant <b>discovered</b> it or <b>could with reasonable diligence</b> have done so (<b>s.32</b>). A deliberate breach unlikely to be discovered counts as concealment."],[65,"How do acknowledgment and part payment affect limitation?","For a <b>debt or liquidated sum</b>, a <b>written signed acknowledgment</b> or <b>part payment</b> restarts the period, if made <b>before</b> it expires (<b>ss.29-30</b>)."]]},{"id":"dr-pre-action","title":"Pre-action conduct and protocols","q":[[0,"Two companies dispute a supply contract. Which pre-action rules apply?",["The Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct","The Debt Claims protocol","The Professional Negligence protocol"],"With <b>no specific protocol</b>, the <b>PD-PAC</b> applies. The Debt Claims PAP covers businesses claiming from individuals."],[1,"What is the usual response time in a straightforward PD-PAC case?",["14 days","30 days","3 months"],"A <b>reasonable time</b>: <b>14 days</b> in a simple case, no more than <b>3 months</b> in a very complex one."],[2,"A business claims a debt from an individual customer. How long does the debtor have to respond?",["30 days","14 days","21 days"],"Under the <b>Debt Claims PAP</b> the debtor has <b>30 days</b> to reply using the reply form."],[3,"Which party must the Debt Claims PAP letter of claim go to?",["An individual, including a sole trader","Any limited company","A public authority only"],"The Debt Claims PAP covers a business (or public body) claiming from an <b>individual</b>, including a sole trader."],[4,"A defendant's insurer in a PI claim has how long to say whether liability is admitted?",["3 months from acknowledgment","21 days from the letter","28 days from the letter"],"The PI PAP: acknowledge within <b>21 days</b>, then the insurer has <b>3 months</b> to investigate and respond on <b>liability</b>."],[5,"How long does a defendant have to respond under the Clinical Disputes protocol?",["4 months from the letter of claim","3 months from the letter","28 days from the letter"],"Acknowledge within <b>14 days</b>; the <b>letter of response</b> is due within <b>4 months</b> of the letter of claim."],[6,"A claimant ignores the protocol but the defendant would have paid anyway. Likely sanction?",["A costs penalty for the claimant","The claim is struck out","The claimant cannot issue"],"The usual sanctions are <b>costs</b>, <b>interest</b> or a <b>stay</b>. Striking out for pre-action failings alone is very rare."],[7,"What interest sanction can a defendant face for ignoring the protocol?",["Enhanced interest on damages","Loss of interest on damages","Dismissal of its defence"],"A defendant at fault may pay interest up to <b>10% above base rate</b>. A claimant at fault may lose <b>interest</b>."],[8,"Do pre-action protocols stop the limitation clock?",["No","Yes, from the letter of claim","Yes, for up to 3 months"],"Protocols <b>do not stop time running</b>. Issue protectively and seek a <b>stay</b>, or agree a <b>standstill</b>."],[9,"Which rule allows a court to order documents before proceedings start?",["CPR 31.16","CPR 36","CPR 24"],"<b>CPR 31.16</b> allows <b>pre-action disclosure</b> where both are likely parties and early disclosure is desirable."],[10,"Which is not a PD-PAC objective?",["Making the claimant prove liability first","Settling without proceedings","Reducing the costs of the dispute"],"The aims include <b>understanding positions</b>, <b>settling</b>, considering <b>ADR</b> and <b>saving costs</b>. There is no duty to prove liability first."],[11,"A defendant fails to respond to a letter before claim by a few days. Will the court sanction?",["Unlikely, minor slips are ignored","Yes, automatically","Yes, with a stay"],"The court looks at <b>substantial</b> non-compliance and real prejudice, not minor or technical slips."]],"c":[[71,"What are the objectives of pre-action conduct?","<b>Understand each other's position</b>. Try to <b>settle</b>. Consider <b>ADR</b>. Support <b>efficient management</b>. <b>Reduce costs</b>. Proceedings are a <b>last resort</b>."],[74,"When does the PD-PAC itself apply?","Where <b>no specific protocol</b> applies, e.g. a business-to-business contract dispute."],[76,"What must a PD-PAC letter before claim contain?","Concise <b>details of the claim</b>. <b>What the claimant wants</b>. How any sum is <b>calculated</b>. The <b>key documents</b> relied on."],[77,"When must the defendant respond under the PD-PAC?","Within a <b>reasonable time</b>: <b>14 days</b> in a straightforward case, <b>no more than 3 months</b> in a very complex one."],[84,"What are the Personal Injury protocol time limits?","Defendant acknowledges within <b>21 days</b>, identifying its insurer. Insurer has <b>3 months</b> from acknowledgment to respond on <b>liability</b>."],[86,"What are the Professional Negligence protocol time limits?","Acknowledge the letter of claim within <b>21 days</b>. <b>Letter of response</b> within <b>3 months</b> of acknowledgment."],[90,"What sanctions can the court impose for pre-action non-compliance?","A <b>stay</b> until steps are taken. Paying <b>all or part of the other side's costs</b>, possibly on the <b>indemnity basis</b>. Interest sanctions. Paying money <b>into court</b>."],[92,"What interest sanction applies to a defendant at fault?","Interest at a <b>higher rate</b>, up to <b>10% above base rate</b>, for a period."]]},{"id":"dr-parties-law","title":"Parties, causes of action, applicable law and Welsh language","q":[[0,"A German company sells machinery to an English company. No governing law clause. Which law applies?",["German law, the seller's","English law, the buyer's","The law of the court hearing it"],"Rome I Art 4(1)(a): <b>sale of goods</b> is governed by the law of the <b>seller's</b> habitual residence."],[1,"No law chosen for a services contract. Whose habitual residence decides the law?",["The service provider's","The customer's","The court's"],"Rome I Art 4(1)(b): <b>provision of services</b> follows the <b>service provider's</b> habitual residence."],[2,"Two friends from Leeds are in a car crash in Spain. Which law governs the negligence claim?",["English law","Spanish law","The law of the court's choice"],"Rome II Art 4(2): <b>common habitual residence</b> beats the place of damage, so <b>English law</b> applies."],[3,"Which Rome II claims are excluded?",["Defamation and privacy","Negligence","Product liability"],"<b>Defamation and privacy</b> are excluded from Rome II. Common law rules apply."],[4,"A child wants to sue. How does the claim proceed?",["Through a litigation friend","The child sues alone","Through the child's school"],"A <b>child</b> (under 18) or <b>protected party</b> acts through a <b>litigation friend</b> (CPR 21)."],[5,"A settlement for a child is agreed. Is it binding on its own?",["No, it needs court approval","Yes, once signed","Yes, if a parent agrees"],"Any settlement for a <b>child or protected party</b> is <b>not binding unless approved by the court</b>."],[6,"The defendant company is in administration. Can the claimant just issue?",["No, it needs consent or permission","Yes, as normal","Yes, but only against directors"],"The administration <b>moratorium</b> needs the <b>administrator's consent</b> or the <b>court's permission</b>."],[7,"How does a partnership get sued?",["In the firm name","Only in each partner's name","In the managing partner's name only"],"A <b>partnership</b> can sue and be sued in the <b>firm name</b>, and partners can be asked to disclose names."],[8,"A solicitor takes 30% of damages if the client wins and nothing if they lose. What is it?",["A damages-based agreement","A conditional fee agreement","After-the-event insurance"],"A <b>DBA</b> takes a percentage of damages. A <b>CFA</b> instead uses a success fee on normal fees."],[9,"Is a CFA success fee recoverable from the losing opponent?",["No, not since April 2013","Yes, in full","Yes, up to 25%"],"Since April 2013 the <b>success fee</b> is <b>not recoverable</b> from the opponent and comes from the client's damages."],[10,"A defendant is served with the claim form while visiting England. Does the court have jurisdiction?",["Yes, under common law rules","No, he is not resident","Only with the court's permission"],"Under common law the court has jurisdiction if the defendant is <b>served in England and Wales</b>, even briefly."],[11,"A claimant in Swansea will give evidence in Welsh. What must the solicitor do?",["Tell the court early","Apply for permission","Ask the other side to agree"],"No permission is needed. The parties must <b>inform the court</b> (at the latest in the directions questionnaire)."]],"c":[[105,"What are the bases for claiming interest?","A <b>contractual</b> rate. The <b>Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998</b>. The court's <b>discretion</b> (<b>s.35A SCA 1981</b> or <b>s.69 CCA 1984</b>)."],[106,"What does the Late Payment of Commercial Debts Act provide?","For <b>business-to-business</b> contracts for goods or services: interest at <b>8% above base rate</b>, plus fixed compensation of <b>£40, £70 or £100</b>."],[115,"How do children and protected parties conduct proceedings?","Through a <b>litigation friend</b> (<b>CPR 21</b>)."],[117,"Is a settlement by a child or protected party binding without approval?","<b>No</b>. It is <b>not binding unless approved by the court</b>."],[121,"What is joint and several liability?","Each defendant is liable for the <b>whole</b> loss. The claimant can sue <b>any or all</b> and recover in full from one, who seeks <b>contribution</b> from the others."],[123,"When does the English court have jurisdiction at common law?","The defendant is <b>served while present</b> in England and Wales. Or <b>submits</b> to the jurisdiction. Or is <b>served out</b> of the jurisdiction with or without permission under <b>CPR 6</b>."],[125,"What does the 2005 Hague Choice of Court Convention require?","Contracting states, including <b>EU states</b>, must respect an <b>exclusive</b> choice of court clause and enforce the resulting judgment."],[130,"What law governs a contract with no choice under Rome I Art 4(1)?","<b>Sale of goods</b>: <b>seller's</b> habitual residence. <b>Services</b>: <b>provider's</b>. <b>Land</b>: where the <b>land</b> is. <b>Franchise</b>: <b>franchisee's</b>. <b>Distribution</b>: <b>distributor's</b>."],[131,"What law governs other contracts with no choice?","The habitual residence of the party performing the <b>characteristic performance</b> (<b>Art 4(2)</b>). Escape clause: <b>manifestly more closely connected</b> country (<b>Art 4(3)</b>)."],[134,"What is the general rule under Rome II?","The law where the <b>damage occurs</b> (<b>Art 4(1)</b>), regardless of where the event happened."]]},{"id":"dr-where-to-start","title":"Where to start: High Court or county court","q":[[0,"A company is owed £85,000 under a contract. Where must it issue?",["County Court","High Court","Either, its choice"],"Money claims of <b>£100,000 or less</b> must start in the <b>County Court</b>."],[1,"What is the High Court threshold for a personal injury claim?",["£50,000","£100,000","£25,000"],"PI claims worth <b>less than £50,000</b> must start in the County Court. Do not confuse with the £100,000 figure."],[2,"A £98,000 debt plus £6,000 interest. Where to issue?",["County Court","High Court","The Commercial Court"],"<b>Interest and costs are ignored</b> when valuing. The claim is worth £98,000, so the <b>County Court</b>."],[3,"Which claim must start in the High Court unless the parties agree otherwise?",["Defamation","Debt of £5,000","Possession of a home"],"<b>Defamation</b> claims start in the <b>High Court</b> (Media and Communications List)."],[4,"Which court hears building and engineering disputes?",["Technology and Construction Court","Admiralty Court","Administrative Court"],"The <b>TCC</b> deals with technically complex building, engineering and IT disputes (CPR 60)."],[5,"Which is NOT ignored when valuing a claim for court thresholds?",["The damages the claimant expects to recover","Interest","Possible contributory negligence"],"The value is the amount the claimant <b>reasonably expects to recover</b>, ignoring interest, costs, contributory negligence and counterclaims."],[6,"A £60,000 contract claim: which court and track?",["County Court, intermediate track","High Court, multi-track","County Court, fast track"],"Court and track are separate questions. £60,000 starts in the <b>County Court</b> and is likely <b>intermediate track</b>."],[7,"A claim wrongly started in the High Court. Costs risk?",["Recoverable costs cut by up to 25%","The claim is void","Claim is struck out"],"The claim is not invalid. It can be <b>transferred</b>, and costs may be <b>reduced by up to 25%</b> (SCA 1981 s.51)."],[8,"Which High Court Division deals with probate and trusts?",["Chancery Division","King's Bench Division","Family Division"],"The <b>Chancery Division</b> handles business, property, trusts, probate, insolvency and companies."],[9,"For a PI claim, what PSLA figure usually keeps it a small claim?",["Not more than £1,500","Not more than £10,000","Not more than £25,000"],"In most PI claims the small claims limit is <b>£1,500</b> for pain, suffering and loss of amenity (£5,000 for road traffic cases)."],[10,"A £2 million claim with complex issues. What factors justify the High Court?",["Value, complexity or public importance","Value only","The defendant's preference"],"PD 7A para 2.4: <b>financial value</b>, <b>complexity</b> and/or <b>public importance</b> justify issuing in the High Court."],[11,"A judgment of £5,000 or more is enforced by taking control of goods. Where?",["In the High Court","Only in the County Court","By the Court of Appeal"],"Judgments of <b>£5,000 or more</b> are enforced by a <b>writ of control</b> in the <b>High Court</b> (unless regulated consumer credit)."]],"c":[[150,"Where must a non-PI money claim of £100,000 or less start?","<b>Must</b> start in the <b>County Court</b>."],[151,"Where may a non-PI money claim over £100,000 start?","In the County Court, or the <b>High Court</b> if the case justifies it."],[152,"What is the High Court threshold for personal injury claims?","Under <b>£50,000</b>: <b>must</b> start in the <b>County Court</b>. <b>£50,000 or more</b>: may start in the High Court if justified."],[153,"Where must defamation and judicial review claims start?","In the <b>High Court</b>."],[154,"When should a claim above the threshold go to the High Court?","If it ought to be dealt with by a <b>High Court judge</b> because of its <b>financial value</b>, <b>complexity</b>, or <b>importance to the public</b> (<b>PD 7A para 2.4</b>)."],[157,"What is disregarded when valuing a claim?","<b>Interest</b> and <b>costs</b>. <b>Contributory negligence</b>. Any <b>counterclaim</b> or <b>set-off</b>. CRU benefits in PI (<b>CPR 16.3(6)</b>)."],[158,"What statement of value must a claim form for money contain under CPR 16.3?","The <b>amount claimed</b>, or whether the claimant expects to recover <b>not more than £10,000</b>, <b>£10,000 to £25,000</b>, <b>£25,000 to £100,000</b>, <b>more than £100,000</b>, or <b>cannot say</b> (<b>CPR 16.3(2)</b>)."],[166,"What is the costs penalty for starting in the High Court a claim that should be in the County Court?","Recoverable costs may be <b>reduced by up to 25%</b> (<b>SCA 1981 s.51(8)-(9)</b>)."]]},{"id":"dr-issue-service","title":"Issuing and serving proceedings; changing parties","q":[[0,"How long does a claimant have to take the step to serve a claim form within England and Wales?",["Midnight, 4 months after issue","14 days after issue","6 months after issue"],"<b>CPR 7.5</b>: the step required must be taken before midnight on the calendar day <b>4 months</b> after issue. Abroad it is 6 months."],[1,"The claim form is posted on the last day of the 4 months. In time?",["Yes, posting is the step required","No, it must be deemed served","No, it must be received"],"For first class post the <b>step required</b> is <b>posting</b>. Deemed service falling later does not matter."],[2,"When is a claim form deemed served, whatever the method?",["The second business day after the step","The day of the step","The next day if sent before 4.30pm"],"<b>CPR 6.14</b>: a claim form is deemed served on the <b>second business day</b> after the step required."],[3,"A defendant's solicitors confirmed in writing they accept service. Where must the claim form go?",["To the solicitors","To the defendant personally","To the defendant's employer"],"Under <b>CPR 6.7</b> the claim form <b>must</b> be served on the nominated solicitor. Personal service is invalid."],[4,"When can a claim form be served by email?",["If the other side previously agreed in writing","If the email is on the letterhead","Always, after issue"],"<b>PD 6A</b>: electronic service needs a <b>prior written indication</b> that it will be accepted. A letterhead address is not enough."],[5,"A litigant in person emailed the claim form without consent, then time expired. Result in Barton?",["Service was not validated","Service was validated retrospectively","The court served it for him"],"<b>Barton</b> (2018): the defendant's knowledge was not enough, and litigants in person get no special leniency."],[6,"Which rule allows the court to approve service by a non-standard method?",["CPR 6.15 alternative service","CPR 6.14","CPR 7.6"],"<b>CPR 6.15</b> allows alternative service for a <b>good reason</b>, even retrospectively. Knowledge of the claim is critical but not enough alone."],[7,"Time to serve the claim form has expired. What is needed for a late extension?",["Reasonable steps taken but unable to serve, and prompt action","Any good reason","The defendant's consent"],"<b>CPR 7.6(3)</b>: only if the court failed to serve, or the claimant took <b>all reasonable steps</b> but could not serve, and acted <b>promptly</b>."],[8,"The contract has an English jurisdiction clause. Does the claimant need permission to serve a foreign defendant?",["No","Yes, always","Yes, from the Senior Master"],"Service out without permission is allowed where a contract gives the courts of England and Wales <b>jurisdiction</b> (CPR 6.33)."],[9,"Permission to serve out needs a serious issue and a gateway case. What else?",["England is the proper place","The defendant consents","A claim above £100,000"],"The claimant must also show England and Wales is the <b>proper place</b> (<b>forum conveniens</b>)."],[10,"After service of the claim form, how can a new party be added?",["With the court's permission","Without permission","Only by the defendant"],"<b>CPR 19.4</b>: permission is needed after service. Before service no permission is needed."],[11,"Limitation has expired. The claimant sued the wrong company by mistake. Can the right one be substituted?",["Yes, if the claim was in time when issued","No, never","Yes, if the defendant agrees"],"<b>CPR 19.6 and LA 1980 s.35</b>: substitution is allowed where a party was named by <b>mistake</b> and the limitation period was current at issue."]],"c":[[175,"What is the time limit for serving a claim form within the jurisdiction?","The <b>step required</b> must be taken <b>before midnight</b> on the calendar day <b>4 months after issue</b> (<b>CPR 7.5</b>)."],[178,"When must separate particulars of claim be served?","Within <b>14 days</b> after service of the claim form, and <b>no later than</b> the last day for serving the claim form (<b>CPR 7.4</b>)."],[180,"When can time for serving a claim form be extended on an application made late?","Only if the <b>court</b> failed to serve, or the claimant <b>took all reasonable steps</b> but could not serve. <b>And</b> the claimant acted <b>promptly</b>."],[181,"What happens if a claim form is not served in time?","It <b>expires</b>. A new claim is possible only if still within <b>limitation</b>."],[183,"When may a claim form be served by email or fax?","Only if the party or its solicitor has <b>previously indicated in writing</b> it will accept service that way and given the address (<b>PD 6A para 4.1</b>). An email on letterhead is <b>not enough</b>."],[186,"When must the claim form be served on the defendant's solicitor?","If the solicitor has notified <b>in writing</b> it is <b>authorised to accept service</b> (<b>CPR 6.7</b>). Serving the defendant personally is then <b>invalid</b>."],[190,"When is a claim form deemed served?","On the <b>second business day</b> after the step required, <b>whatever the method</b> (<b>CPR 6.14</b>)."],[191,"When are other documents served by post or DX deemed served?","The <b>second day</b> after posting if a business day; otherwise the <b>next business day</b> after that (<b>CPR 6.26</b>)."],[192,"When are other documents served personally, by delivery, fax or email deemed served?","That day if done <b>before 4.30pm</b> on a business day; otherwise the <b>next business day</b>."],[199,"What must be shown for permission to serve out of the jurisdiction?","A <b>good arguable case</b> within a <b>PD 6B gateway</b>. A <b>serious issue to be tried</b>. England and Wales is <b>the proper place</b> (forum conveniens)."]]},{"id":"dr-responding","title":"Responding to a claim, default judgment, discontinuance and settlement","q":[[0,"A defendant files an acknowledgment of service. How long does it have for its defence?",["28 days from service of particulars","14 days from service of particulars","28 days from the claim form"],"With an <b>AoS</b>, the defence is due <b>28 days</b> after service of the particulars. Without one, 14 days."],[1,"By how long can parties agree to extend the time for a defence without a court order?",["Up to 28 days","Up to 14 days","Up to 3 months"],"<b>CPR 15.5</b>: up to <b>28 days</b>, with the court notified in writing. Anything longer needs a court order."],[2,"Particulars served 1 March. AoS filed. Earliest default judgment request?",["After 28 days have expired","After 14 days have expired","Straight after the AoS"],"With an AoS, default judgment is available only after the <b>28 days</b> (plus any agreed extension) have passed."],[3,"A defendant wants to dispute jurisdiction. What must it do after the AoS?",["Apply within 14 days","Apply within 28 days","File a defence first"],"<b>CPR 11</b>: apply within <b>14 days after filing the AoS</b>, otherwise it is treated as <b>accepting jurisdiction</b>."],[4,"Default judgment was entered before the defence deadline had expired. What must the court do?",["Set it aside","Leave it unless there is a defence","Vary the amount"],"<b>CPR 13.2</b>: a wrongly entered judgment <b>must</b> be set aside, however weak the defence."],[5,"On which grounds may the court set aside a correctly entered default judgment?",["Real prospect of defending, or other good reason","The defendant is unrepresented","The claim is large"],"<b>CPR 13.3</b>: a <b>real prospect</b> of successfully defending or <b>some other good reason</b>, with prompt application."],[6,"When can default judgment be obtained only by application, not request?",["Against a child or protected party","For a specified sum","For a debt claim"],"An <b>application</b> is needed for children, protected parties, non-money remedies, and some service-out cases."],[7,"A defendant admits part of a specified sum. What must the claimant tell the court?",["Whether it accepts that in full satisfaction","Whether it wants a trial date","Nothing, judgment is automatic"],"The claimant either <b>accepts</b> the part admission in full satisfaction, or <b>rejects</b> it and the claim continues."],[8,"Who must pay costs if a claimant discontinues?",["The claimant, normally","The defendant","Each side bears its own"],"<b>CPR 38.6</b>: unless the court orders otherwise, the claimant pays the <b>defendant's costs</b> up to the notice."],[9,"When does a claimant need the court's permission to discontinue?",["After an interim injunction was granted","After a defence was filed","After 14 days"],"Permission is needed where an <b>interim injunction</b> was granted or the claimant has had an <b>interim payment</b> (without consent)."],[10,"What is special about a Tomlin order?",["Claim stayed on confidential schedule terms","It is a judgment for money","It ends all rights to return to court"],"A <b>Tomlin order</b> stays the claim on terms in a <b>confidential schedule</b>, with <b>liberty to apply</b> to enforce."],[11,"No defence is filed and the claimant seeks no judgment for 6 months after the deadline. Effect?",["The claim is stayed","The claim is struck out","Judgment is entered"],"<b>CPR 15.11</b>: if no defence or admission is filed and the claimant does not seek judgment within <b>6 months</b> of the defence deadline, the claim is <b>stayed</b>."]],"c":[[205,"What is the time limit for an admission or acknowledgment of service?","<b>14 days</b> after service of the particulars."],[206,"When is a defence due?","<b>14 days</b> after service of the particulars if no AoS. <b>28 days</b> if an <b>AoS</b> was filed."],[209,"How can time for the defence be extended by agreement?","By up to <b>28 days</b> (max <b>56 days</b> with an AoS) (<b>CPR 15.5</b>). The defendant must <b>notify the court in writing</b>. Longer needs a <b>court order</b>."],[210,"Can time for an acknowledgment of service be extended by agreement?","<b>No</b>."],[214,"What are the claimant's options on a part admission of a specified sum?","<b>Accept</b> the lesser sum <b>in full satisfaction</b> and enter judgment, or <b>reject</b> it and the claim continues as defended."],[216,"How does a defendant dispute the court's jurisdiction?","File an <b>AoS</b>, then <b>apply</b> within <b>14 days after filing it</b>, with evidence (<b>Part 11</b>)."],[220,"When is default judgment available?","No AoS and no defence after <b>14 days</b>. Or an AoS but no defence after <b>28 days</b> plus any agreed extension. Also on an undefended <b>counterclaim</b>."],[224,"When must the court set aside default judgment?","If <b>wrongly entered</b>: conditions <b>not satisfied</b> (e.g. entered too early) or the claim was <b>satisfied</b> before judgment (<b>CPR 13.2</b>)."],[225,"When may the court set aside or vary default judgment?","If the defendant has a <b>real prospect of successfully defending</b>, or <b>some other good reason</b> (<b>CPR 13.3</b>). The court considers <b>promptness</b>."],[230,"What are the costs consequences of discontinuance?","Unless ordered otherwise, the claimant pays the <b>defendant's costs</b> up to service of the notice (<b>CPR 38.6</b>)."]]},{"id":"dr-statements-of-case","title":"Statements of case","q":[[0,"The defendant cannot know if the claimant lost £30,000 profit. How should it plead?",["Not admitted","Denied","Admitted"],"Plead <b>not admitted</b> where the defendant does not know, so the claimant must prove it. <b>Denied</b> needs reasons and a different version."],[1,"A defence says nothing about an allegation in the particulars. Effect?",["It is taken to be admitted","It is taken as denied","It is struck out"],"<b>CPR 16.5</b>: an allegation not dealt with is <b>taken to be admitted</b>, unless the defence sets out its case on that issue."],[2,"Is the claimant's reply to a defence compulsory?",["No, and silence is not an admission","Yes, within 14 days","Yes, or the defence is deemed true"],"A <b>reply</b> is optional (CPR 15.8). Not filing one is <b>not an admission</b> (CPR 16.7)."],[3,"Which must be filed within 14 days of the counterclaim being served?",["The claimant's defence to counterclaim","The claimant's reply","The acknowledgment of service"],"A <b>defence to counterclaim</b> is due within <b>14 days</b>. If missing, the defendant can get <b>default judgment</b> on the counterclaim."],[4,"A builder blames his subcontractor. What claim should he bring?",["A Part 20 additional claim","A Part 8 claim","A reply"],"A <b>Part 20 claim</b> for contribution or indemnity, ideally issued <b>with the defence</b> so no permission is needed."],[5,"Amending a statement of case after service needs what?",["Written consent of all parties or permission","Nothing, it can be amended freely","Only the claimant's solicitor's approval"],"<b>CPR 17</b>: after service, amendment needs <b>written consent</b> of all other parties or the <b>court's permission</b>."],[6,"After limitation expires, which amendment may be allowed?",["A new claim from the same facts","A claim from entirely different facts","Adding a wholly new claimant"],"<b>CPR 17.4</b>: a new claim arising out of the <b>same or substantially the same facts</b> as one already pleaded."],[7,"Who signs the statement of truth on a witness statement?",["The witness","The solicitor","The judge"],"A <b>witness statement</b> is signed by the <b>witness</b>. A statement of case can be signed by the party or its solicitor."],[8,"What is the risk of a false statement of truth made without honest belief?",["Contempt of court","A costs order only","Dismissal of the claim"],"Verifying a document without an honest belief in its truth may be <b>contempt of court</b>, with a fine or prison."],[9,"A statement of case is not verified by a statement of truth. Effect?",["It stands but is not evidence","It is void","It is automatically struck out"],"It remains effective unless <b>struck out</b>, but the party <b>cannot rely on it as evidence</b>."],[10,"Which must be specifically pleaded under PD 16?",["An allegation of fraud","The claimant's address","The claimant's solicitor's name"],"PD 16 para 8.2: <b>fraud</b>, illegality, misrepresentation, breach of trust and similar matters must be specifically set out."],[11,"Which document sets out what the claimant claims and ends with a prayer?",["Particulars of claim","The defence","A Part 18 request"],"The <b>particulars of claim</b> state the facts, breach, loss and interest, and close with a <b>prayer</b> of what is claimed."]],"c":[[241,"What must particulars of claim contain?","A <b>concise statement of the facts</b>, plus details of any <b>interest</b> and any claim for <b>aggravated, exemplary or provisional</b> damages (<b>CPR 16.4</b>)."],[249,"What must a defendant say about each allegation?","<b>Denied</b>, with <b>reasons</b> and any <b>different version</b>. <b>Not admitted</b>: unable to admit or deny, claimant to <b>prove</b> it. Or <b>admitted</b> (<b>CPR 16.5</b>)."],[251,"What is the effect of failing to deal with an allegation in the defence?","It is <b>taken to be admitted</b>, unless the defence sets out the <b>nature of the defendant's case</b> on that issue, when it <b>requires proof</b>."],[257,"What is a reply and is it compulsory?","The claimant's answer to <b>new matters</b> in the defence. <b>Optional</b>. Filed with the <b>directions questionnaire</b> (<b>CPR 15.8</b>)."],[258,"Is failing to file a reply an admission?","<b>No</b> (<b>CPR 16.7</b>). A reply that fails to deal with something <b>requires proof</b> of it."],[260,"When is permission needed for a counterclaim?","Not if filed <b>with the defence</b>; otherwise yes. A counterclaim adding a <b>new party</b> <b>always</b> needs a court order."],[261,"When is permission needed for a contribution or indemnity claim against a co-defendant?","Not if filed and served <b>with the defence</b> (or within 28 days after the co-defendant's defence). Otherwise yes."],[268,"When can a statement of case be amended?","<b>Before service</b>: without permission. <b>After service</b>: with <b>written consent of all parties</b> or <b>court permission</b>."],[270,"What amendments are allowed after limitation has expired?","A new claim from the <b>same or substantially the same facts</b>. Correcting a genuine <b>mistake as to name</b>. Altering <b>capacity</b> (<b>CPR 17.4</b>)."]]},{"id":"dr-interim","title":"Interim applications, summary judgment, interim payments and injunctions","q":[[0,"What is the test for summary judgment under CPR 24?",["No real prospect and no other compelling reason for trial","No prospect at all of succeeding","The claim is unlikely to succeed"],"The test is <b>no real prospect</b> (realistic, not fanciful) <b>and</b> no other <b>compelling reason</b> for a trial."],[1,"Can a defendant apply for summary judgment?",["Yes, against a hopeless claim","No, only claimants can","Only after trial directions"],"Either party can apply: a defendant against a hopeless <b>claim</b>, a claimant against a hopeless <b>defence</b>."],[2,"How much notice must the respondent get of a summary judgment hearing?",["14 days","7 days","3 days"],"At least <b>14 days</b> notice; the respondent's evidence is due 7 days before and the reply 3 days before."],[3,"A claim is possible but improbable to succeed. What order might the court make?",["A conditional order, e.g. pay money into court","Judgment for the defendant at once","An interim injunction"],"The court can make a <b>conditional order</b> requiring a payment into court or a step, failing which the claim or defence is struck out."],[4,"Strike out under CPR 3.4 looks at what?",["The statement of case itself","The trial evidence","The witness's credibility"],"<b>Strike out</b> tests defects in the <b>statement of case</b> (no reasonable grounds, abuse, non-compliance). Summary judgment looks at the <b>evidence</b>."],[5,"Which is a condition for an interim payment?",["The defendant has admitted liability","The claimant needs the money","The trial date is set"],"Conditions: <b>admission of liability</b>, <b>judgment</b> for damages to be assessed, or the claimant <b>would obtain judgment for a substantial sum</b>."],[6,"How much interim payment can be ordered?",["A reasonable proportion of the likely judgment","The full likely judgment","Up to £10,000"],"No more than a <b>reasonable proportion</b> of the likely final judgment, allowing for contributory negligence and any counterclaim."],[7,"Is the trial judge told an interim payment was made?",["No, not until liability and quantum are decided","Yes, at once","Only if the claimant wins"],"The fact of an interim payment is <b>not disclosed to the trial judge</b> until all questions of liability and quantum are decided."],[8,"First step of the American Cyanamid test?",["Is there a serious question to be tried?","Where does the balance of convenience lie?","Is there a good arguable case?"],"Step 1 is <b>serious question to be tried</b>. Step 2 asks if <b>damages</b> are an adequate remedy."],[9,"Damages would adequately compensate the claimant and the defendant can pay. Result?",["The injunction is normally refused","The injunction is granted","The injunction is granted without notice"],"If <b>damages are adequate</b> to the claimant and the defendant can pay, the interim injunction should normally be <b>refused</b>."],[10,"What must a party seeking an interim injunction usually give?",["A cross-undertaking in damages","A deposit equal to the claim","A guarantee from a bank"],"The applicant gives a <b>cross-undertaking in damages</b> to compensate the respondent if the injunction should not have been granted."],[11,"Which is not required for a freezing injunction?",["A real possibility evidence will be destroyed","A good arguable case","A real risk of dissipation"],"A freezing injunction needs a <b>good arguable case</b>, <b>assets</b>, and a <b>real risk of dissipation</b>. Destruction of evidence is for search orders."]],"c":[[277,"How long before the hearing must an application notice be served?","As soon as practicable and at least <b>3 clear days</b> before, unless another period applies."],[278,"When can an application be made without notice?","<b>Exceptional urgency</b>. Notice would <b>defeat the purpose</b> (e.g. a freezing injunction). Or the rules permit it or all parties consent."],[279,"What duty applies on a without notice application?","<b>Full and frank disclosure</b>, including points <b>against</b> the applicant. Breach can lead to the order being <b>discharged</b>."],[282,"What is the test for summary judgment?","The party has <b>no real prospect of succeeding</b> on the claim, defence or issue, <b>and</b> there is <b>no other compelling reason</b> for a trial (<b>CPR 24.3</b>)."],[283,"What does \"real prospect\" mean?","<b>Realistic, not fanciful</b>. No <b>mini-trial</b>; the court considers the evidence available and reasonably expected at trial."],[287,"What are the summary judgment time limits?","Notice of hearing at least <b>14 days</b> before. Respondent's evidence <b>7 days</b> before. Applicant's reply <b>3 days</b> before."],[289,"When is a conditional order made?","Where success is <b>possible but improbable</b>. A party must <b>pay a sum into court</b> or take a step, failing which its case is struck out."],[293,"What are the conditions for an interim payment?","Defendant <b>admitted liability</b>. Claimant has <b>judgment</b> for damages to be assessed. Claimant <b>would obtain judgment for a substantial sum</b> at trial. Or, with several defendants, against at least one, all <b>insured</b> or public bodies."],[294,"How much can an interim payment be?","<b>No more than a reasonable proportion</b> of the likely final judgment, allowing for <b>contributory negligence</b> and any <b>set-off or counterclaim</b>."],[298,"What are the steps of the American Cyanamid test?","<b>Serious question to be tried</b>. Are <b>damages adequate</b> for the claimant? Does the <b>cross-undertaking</b> protect the defendant? <b>Balance of convenience</b>. <b>Special factors</b>."],[305,"What must be shown for a freezing injunction?","A <b>good arguable case</b>. The respondent has <b>assets</b>. A <b>real risk of dissipation</b>."],[307,"What must be shown for a search order?","An <b>extremely strong prima facie case</b>. Very <b>serious potential damage</b>. Clear evidence of incriminating material and a <b>real possibility it will be destroyed</b>."]]},{"id":"dr-case-management","title":"Case management, tracks and sanctions","q":[[0,"A £18,000 contract claim is defended. Which track?",["Fast track","Small claims track","Intermediate track"],"<b>Fast track</b> covers £10,000 to £25,000 (trial no more than 1 day)."],[1,"A £60,000 claim needs a 2-day trial. Which track?",["Intermediate track","Fast track","Multi-track"],"<b>Intermediate track</b>: £25,000 to £100,000, trial no more than <b>3 days</b>. A longer trial pushes it to the multi-track."],[2,"Which track covers a £150,000 claim?",["Multi-track","Intermediate track","Fast track"],"Claims over <b>£100,000</b> go to the <b>multi-track</b>."],[3,"A PI claim has PSLA of £1,200 and total damages under £10,000. Which track?",["Small claims track","Fast track","Intermediate track"],"PI is small claims if total up to £10,000 and <b>PSLA up to £1,500</b> (£5,000 for road traffic claims in the whiplash regime)."],[4,"How is value counted for allocation?",["Ignoring interest, costs and contributory negligence","Including interest and costs","Adding claim and counterclaim together"],"Value <b>ignores interest, costs</b> and possible <b>contributory negligence</b>. With a counterclaim, the largest claim usually counts."],[5,"Which form do the parties file after the notice of proposed allocation?",["Directions questionnaire","Part 36 offer","Application notice N244"],"The <b>directions questionnaire</b> (N180 small claims, N181 others) is filed after the notice of proposed allocation."],[6,"Which track uses costs budgets?",["Multi-track","Fast track","Intermediate track"],"<b>Costs budgets</b> (Precedent H) apply on the <b>multi-track</b> (claims under £10 million). Fast and intermediate use fixed costs."],[7,"A party files its costs budget late. What is it treated as having filed?",["Only court fees","A budget of zero total costs","Its full budget"],"<b>CPR 3.14</b>: treated as filing a budget of <b>applicable court fees only</b>, unless relief from sanctions is granted."],[8,"Which case set the three-stage approach to relief from sanctions?",["Denton v TH White","Mitchell v News Group","Halsey v Milton Keynes"],"<b>Denton</b>: (1) seriousness and significance, (2) reason for default, (3) all the circumstances."],[9,"A solicitor is two days late because of a diary error and no hearing is affected. Likely outcome?",["Relief granted","Relief refused","Claim struck out"],"At stage 1 the breach is <b>not serious or significant</b>, so relief is usually granted. Opponents should not take opportunistic points."],[10,"By how long may parties agree in writing to extend a time limit without the court?",["Up to 28 days, if no hearing is at risk","Up to 7 days","Up to 3 months"],"<b>CPR 3.8(4)</b>: up to <b>28 days</b>, provided no hearing date is put at risk."],[11,"Which sanction means a claim is struck out automatically if a step is missed?",["An unless order","A costs order","A stay"],"An <b>unless order</b> states that unless a party acts by a set time, its claim or defence is struck out."]],"c":[[309,"What is the overriding objective?","To enable the court to deal with cases <b>justly and at proportionate cost</b> (<b>CPR 1.1</b>)."],[316,"What factors govern allocation?","<b>Value</b>. <b>Remedy</b>. <b>Complexity</b>. <b>Number of parties</b>. Value of any <b>counterclaim</b>. Amount of <b>oral evidence</b>. <b>Importance</b> to non-parties. Parties' views and circumstances (<b>CPR 26.13</b>); any <b>vulnerability</b> is also considered (PD 1A)."],[318,"What is the normal limit for the small claims track?","Claims up to <b>£10,000</b>."],[319,"When is a personal injury claim on the small claims track?","Total up to <b>£10,000</b> <b>and</b> PSLA up to <b>£1,500</b>. <b>£5,000</b> for whiplash-reform road traffic claims; <b>£1,000</b> for some excluded road traffic claims."],[322,"What are the fast track criteria?","Value <b>£10,000 to £25,000</b>. Trial <b>no more than 1 day</b>. Oral expert evidence: <b>1 expert per party per field</b>, <b>no more than 2 fields</b>."],[324,"What are the intermediate track criteria?","Value <b>£25,000 to £100,000</b>. Trial <b>no more than 3 days</b>. <b>No more than 2 experts per party</b> giving oral evidence. One claimant v one or two defendants, or two claimants v one defendant."],[327,"Which claims go to the multi-track?","Claims over <b>£100,000</b>, claims unsuitable for other tracks (complex or longer trials), and the <b>excluded</b> types."],[332,"When must a costs budget be filed?","Form <b>Precedent H</b>. <b>With the DQ</b> if value <b>under £50,000</b>. Otherwise at least <b>21 days before the first CMC</b>."],[335,"What is the sanction for failing to file a costs budget on time?","The party is treated as having filed a budget of <b>court fees only</b> (<b>CPR 3.14</b>). It must seek <b>relief from sanctions</b>."],[338,"When can a statement of case be struck out?","<b>No reasonable grounds</b>. <b>Abuse of process</b> or likely to obstruct just disposal. <b>Failure to comply</b> with a rule, PD or order (<b>CPR 3.4(2)</b>)."],[340,"Can parties extend time limits by agreement without the court?","<b>Yes</b>, in writing, by <b>up to 28 days</b>, if it does not put any <b>hearing date</b> at risk (<b>CPR 3.8(4)</b>)."],[343,"What are the three Denton stages?","<b>1.</b> <b>Seriousness and significance</b> of the breach. <b>2.</b> <b>Why</b> it occurred. <b>3.</b> <b>All the circumstances</b>."]]},{"id":"dr-evidence","title":"Evidence: witnesses, hearsay and experts","q":[[0,"What is the standard of proof in a civil claim, even for fraud?",["Balance of probabilities","Beyond reasonable doubt","Clear and convincing evidence"],"There is <b>one</b> civil standard: the <b>balance of probabilities</b> (more likely than not), even for serious allegations (Re B)."],[1,"A driver was convicted of careless driving. Effect in the injured claimant's civil claim?",["The driver must prove he was not careless","The claimant need prove nothing","The conviction is inadmissible"],"<b>Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11</b>: the conviction is admissible, and he is taken to have committed the offence unless he proves otherwise."],[2,"Is hearsay admissible in civil proceedings?",["Yes, subject to notice rules","No, never","Only with the judge's consent"],"<b>Civil Evidence Act 1995 s.1</b>: hearsay is <b>admissible</b> in civil cases. Notice rules affect weight and costs."],[3,"A hearsay notice was not served. Is the evidence excluded?",["No, it may affect weight and costs","Yes, automatically","Yes, unless the court allows"],"<b>s.2(4)</b>: failure to give notice does <b>not</b> make hearsay inadmissible. It may affect <b>weight</b> and <b>costs</b>."],[4,"How long to apply to call the maker of a hearsay statement for cross-examination?",["14 days from the hearsay notice","7 days from the hearsay notice","28 days from the hearsay notice"],"<b>CPR 33.4</b>: within <b>14 days</b> of service of the hearsay notice. The same period applies to attacking credibility."],[5,"A witness statement is served late with no permission. What is the automatic sanction?",["The witness cannot give oral evidence","The claim is struck out","The statement is hearsay"],"<b>CPR 32.10</b>: the witness may not give oral evidence without the court's <b>permission</b>. Relief is sought under Denton."],[6,"Who signs the statement of truth on a witness statement?",["The witness","The instructing solicitor","The judge"],"The <b>witness</b> signs. It confirms honest belief, with <b>contempt of court</b> the risk for a false statement."],[7,"A party cannot get a statement from a reluctant witness. What may it serve?",["A witness summary","An affidavit","A hearsay notice only"],"<b>CPR 32.9</b>: with permission, a <b>witness summary</b> of the evidence expected or matters to be asked."],[8,"When is an affidavit normally required?",["For a freezing injunction","For a witness statement at trial","For every application"],"Affidavits (sworn) are needed for <b>freezing injunctions</b>, <b>search orders</b> and <b>contempt</b> applications. Otherwise use a witness statement."],[9,"Can a party call an expert without the court's permission?",["No","Yes, if the expert is qualified","Yes, in any claim over £10,000"],"<b>CPR 35.4</b>: no expert evidence without <b>permission</b>. The application names the field and costs estimate."],[10,"To whom does an expert owe an overriding duty?",["The court","The party who pays","The instructing solicitor"],"<b>CPR 35.3</b>: the duty to the <b>court</b> overrides any duty to the person instructing or paying."],[11,"A party may put written questions to the other side's expert. Within what time?",["28 days of service of the report","14 days of service of the report","Any time before trial"],"<b>CPR 35.6</b>: once only, within <b>28 days</b>, to <b>clarify</b> the report. Answers form part of the report."]],"c":[[351,"What is the civil standard of proof?","The <b>balance of probabilities</b>: more likely than not. There is <b>one</b> standard, even for <b>fraud</b>; seriousness is just a factor."],[352,"What is the effect of a previous conviction in civil proceedings?","It is <b>admissible</b> to prove the offence, and the person is <b>taken to have committed it unless they prove otherwise</b> (<b>CEA 1968 s.11</b>)."],[360,"How is a witness statement used at trial?","It <b>stands as evidence in chief</b> (<b>CPR 32.5</b>). The witness may <b>amplify</b> only with <b>permission</b> and for <b>good reason</b>, then is cross-examined."],[362,"What is the sanction for not serving a witness statement in time?","The witness <b>may not give oral evidence</b> without the court's <b>permission</b> (<b>CPR 32.10</b>)."],[366,"What is hearsay?","A statement made <b>otherwise than by a person giving oral evidence</b>, used to <b>prove the truth</b> of what it states (<b>CEA 1995 s.1</b>)."],[367,"Is hearsay admissible in civil proceedings?","<b>Yes</b>. It is not excluded just because it is hearsay."],[370,"How can a party test hearsay from an absent maker?","Apply to <b>call the maker</b> for cross-examination, or give notice to <b>attack credibility</b>, each within <b>14 days</b> after the hearsay notice (<b>ss.3, 5</b>)."],[372,"When is expert evidence allowed?","Only what is <b>reasonably required</b>. <b>No expert without the court's permission</b> (<b>CPR 35.1, 35.4</b>)."],[374,"To whom does an expert owe their overriding duty?","To the <b>court</b>, overriding any obligation to the instructing party (<b>CPR 35.3</b>). Evidence must be <b>independent</b>, <b>objective</b> and <b>unbiased</b>."],[379,"What are the rules on written questions to experts?","<b>Once only</b>, within <b>28 days</b> of service of the report, only to <b>clarify</b> it, unless permitted or agreed. Answers form <b>part of the report</b> (<b>CPR 35.6</b>)."]]},{"id":"dr-disclosure","title":"Disclosure, privilege and without prejudice","q":[[0,"Under standard disclosure must a party disclose a document that harms its own case?",["Yes","No, only helpful documents","Only if asked specifically"],"<b>CPR 31.6</b>: disclose documents relied on, that <b>adversely affect its own case</b>, adversely affect another's case, or support another's case."],[1,"What is meant by a document under CPR 31?",["Anything in which information is recorded","Paper records only","Only signed records"],"<b>CPR 31.4</b>: anything in which information of any description is recorded, including <b>emails</b>, texts and <b>metadata</b>."],[2,"Who signs the disclosure statement on the list of documents?",["The party","The judge","The expert"],"The <b>party</b> signs, certifying it understands the duty and has carried it out. A false statement can be <b>contempt of court</b>."],[3,"How long must a party allow for inspection after written notice?",["Within 7 days","Within 14 days","Within 28 days"],"<b>CPR 31.15</b>: the other party must permit inspection <b>within 7 days</b> of written notice."],[4,"Before suing, a patient wants records from the hospital she plans to sue. Which rule?",["CPR 31.16 pre-action disclosure","CPR 31.17 non-party disclosure","CPR 31.12 specific disclosure"],"<b>Pre-action disclosure</b> (CPR 31.16): both likely to be parties, documents within standard disclosure, and early disclosure desirable."],[5,"Which privilege can protect a report from a third party such as an expert?",["Litigation privilege","Legal advice privilege","Without prejudice privilege"],"Only <b>litigation privilege</b> covers third-party communications, and only when litigation is reasonably in contemplation."],[6,"A report is prepared equally for safety and for expected litigation. Privileged?",["No, litigation was not the dominant purpose","Yes, because litigation was contemplated","Yes, if marked confidential"],"<b>Waugh v British Railways Board</b>: litigation must be the <b>dominant purpose</b>."],[7,"Does legal advice privilege need litigation to be contemplated?",["No","Yes, reasonably in contemplation","Yes, already issued"],"<b>Legal advice privilege</b> covers confidential client-lawyer communications for legal advice, whether or not litigation is in prospect."],[8,"Can an accountant giving tax advice attract legal advice privilege?",["No, only lawyers' advice counts","Yes, always","Yes, if on headed paper"],"Legal advice privilege does not extend to non-lawyers such as accountants (<b>Prudential</b>)."],[9,"A genuine settlement offer is made without prejudice. Can the judge be told on liability?",["No","Yes, if the offer was generous","Yes, with permission only on the claimant's request"],"<b>Without prejudice</b> communications in a genuine attempt to settle cannot be used as evidence of liability (<b>Rush &amp; Tompkins</b>)."],[10,"What is special about a 'without prejudice save as to costs' offer?",["It can be shown to the judge on costs","It is binding on acceptance","It carries Part 36 consequences automatically"],"A <b>Calderbank</b> offer is hidden on liability but may be shown on <b>costs</b> after judgment. It lacks Part 36's automatic consequences."],[11,"A privileged document is obviously sent by mistake. What should the receiving solicitor do?",["Return it unread and tell the other side","Use it at trial","Keep it and say nothing"],"Use of an <b>obviously mistaken</b> disclosure needs permission (<b>CPR 31.20</b>, <b>Al Fayed</b>). The receiver should return it unread and tell the other side."]],"c":[[385,"What is the difference between disclosure and inspection?","<b>Disclosure</b>: stating that a document <b>exists or has existed</b> (<b>CPR 31.2</b>). <b>Inspection</b>: the other party's right to <b>see</b> (usually copy) it."],[386,"What is a \"document\" for disclosure?","<b>Anything in which information of any description is recorded</b> (<b>CPR 31.4</b>), including emails, texts, voicemails, <b>metadata</b> and recoverable deleted files."],[393,"What orders are on the multi-track disclosure menu?","<b>No disclosure</b>. Documents <b>relied on</b> plus specific requests. <b>Issue by issue</b>. <b>Standard disclosure</b>. <b>Train of inquiry</b>. Any other order (<b>CPR 31.5</b>)."],[397,"What does standard disclosure require?","Documents the party <b>relies on</b>, which <b>adversely affect its own case</b>, <b>adversely affect another party's case</b>, or <b>support another party's case</b> (<b>CPR 31.6</b>)."],[404,"What is the test for pre-action disclosure?","Both are <b>likely to be parties</b>. Documents would fall within <b>standard disclosure</b>. Early disclosure is <b>desirable</b> to dispose fairly, <b>avoid proceedings</b> or <b>save costs</b> (<b>CPR 31.16</b>)."],[405,"What is the test for non-party disclosure?","Documents <b>likely to support the applicant's case or adversely affect another party's</b>, and disclosure is <b>necessary</b> to dispose fairly or save costs (<b>CPR 31.17</b>)."],[409,"On what grounds can inspection be withheld?","<b>Privilege</b>. Document <b>no longer in control</b>. Inspection <b>disproportionate</b>. <b>Public interest immunity</b> (needs a court order)."],[411,"What does legal advice privilege protect?","<b>Confidential</b> communications between <b>client</b> and <b>lawyer</b> for the <b>dominant purpose</b> of giving or receiving <b>legal advice</b>. Litigation <b>need not be contemplated</b>."],[415,"What does litigation privilege protect?","<b>Confidential</b> communications between lawyer or client and a <b>third party</b> (or lawyer and client), when litigation is <b>reasonably in contemplation</b>, for the <b>dominant purpose</b> of that litigation."],[418,"What are without prejudice communications?","Communications made in a <b>genuine attempt to settle an existing dispute</b>. They <b>cannot be used as evidence</b> of liability and the judge is not told."],[420,"When can without prejudice material be admitted?","To decide if a <b>settlement was reached</b>. To set it aside for <b>misrepresentation or fraud</b>. <b>Unambiguous impropriety</b>. To explain <b>delay</b>. <b>Estoppel</b>. <b>WP save as to costs</b> offers on costs."]]},{"id":"dr-trial","title":"Trial preparation, procedure and judgment","q":[[0,"Who prepares the trial bundle?",["The claimant","The defendant","The court"],"The <b>claimant</b> prepares and files the paginated, indexed bundle, normally <b>3 to 7 days</b> before trial."],[1,"How long before attendance must a witness summons be served to be binding?",["At least 7 days","At least 14 days","At least 48 hours"],"<b>CPR 34</b>: served at least <b>7 days</b> before the date, with <b>conduct money</b> offered for travel and loss of time."],[2,"Which question is proper when examining your own witness?",["What colour was the light?","The light was red, wasn't it?","You saw it was red, didn't you?"],"No <b>leading questions</b> in examination in chief. Use <b>open</b> questions (who, what, where, when, how)."],[3,"When are leading questions allowed?",["In cross-examination","In examination in chief","In re-examination"],"Leading questions are normal in <b>cross-examination</b>. In chief and re-examination they are barred except on undisputed matters."],[4,"What is re-examination limited to?",["Matters arising from cross-examination","New topics not yet covered","Credibility of the other side"],"<b>Re-examination</b> may only deal with matters arising from cross-examination, with no new topics."],[5,"Who gives the closing speech last in a civil trial?",["The claimant","The defendant","The judge"],"The <b>defendant</b> closes first, the <b>claimant</b> has the <b>last word</b>. The claimant also opens."],[6,"How is a High Court Judge addressed in court?",["My Lord or My Lady","Your Honour","Judge"],"High Court Judges are <b>My Lord or My Lady</b>. Circuit Judges and Recorders are <b>Your Honour</b>; district judges are <b>Judge</b>."],[7,"How is a Circuit Judge addressed?",["Your Honour","My Lord","Master"],"<b>Circuit Judges</b> and Recorders: <b>Your Honour</b>. A Master is <b>Master</b>."],[8,"A defendant fails to attend trial. What can the court do?",["Strike out the defence and give judgment","Adjourn automatically","Dismiss the claim"],"<b>CPR 39.3</b>: if the defendant does not attend, the court may strike out the defence and give judgment for the claimant."],[9,"What must be shown to set aside a judgment given in a party's absence?",["Promptness, good reason and reasonable prospect of success","Only a good defence","Only a good reason"],"<b>All three</b> are needed: acted <b>promptly</b>, <b>good reason</b> for non-attendance, and a <b>reasonable prospect of success</b>."],[10,"By when must a money judgment normally be paid?",["14 days","28 days","7 days"],"<b>CPR 40.11</b>: within <b>14 days</b> of judgment unless the court orders otherwise."],[11,"A solicitor knows of a relevant authority against the client's case. What must they do?",["Draw it to the court's attention","Stay silent","Cite only helpful cases"],"The SRA Code (para 2.7) requires drawing the court's attention to relevant authorities, <b>including those against the client</b>."]],"c":[[429,"What happens if no party files a pre-trial checklist?","The court usually orders that unless one is filed within <b>7 days</b>, the claim, defence and counterclaim will be <b>struck out</b>."],[434,"When is a witness summons binding?","If served at least <b>7 days</b> before the witness must attend, unless the court directs otherwise."],[438,"Who prepares the trial bundle and when is it filed?","The <b>claimant</b>, normally <b>not more than 7 and not less than 3 days</b> before trial (<b>PD 39A</b>)."],[447,"Who closes first at trial?","Normally the <b>defendant</b>, with the <b>claimant</b> having the <b>last word</b>."],[452,"When are leading questions allowed?","In <b>cross-examination</b>. Not in <b>examination in chief</b> or <b>re-examination</b>, except on <b>introductory or undisputed</b> matters."],[457,"How are High Court, Court of Appeal and Supreme Court judges addressed?","\"<b>My Lord</b>\" or \"<b>My Lady</b>\"."],[460,"What duties does a solicitor owe the court at trial?","Do not <b>mislead</b>, including by omission. Draw attention to relevant law <b>including against the client</b>. Only <b>properly arguable</b> submissions. Do not <b>influence evidence</b>. The duty to the court <b>overrides</b> the duty to the client."],[466,"What must a party show to set aside a judgment given in its absence?","It <b>acted promptly</b>, had a <b>good reason</b> for not attending, <b>and</b> has a <b>reasonable prospect of success</b>. All three."]]},{"id":"dr-costs","title":"Costs, Part 36 offers and security for costs","q":[[0,"What is the general rule on costs?",["The loser pays the winner's costs","Each side pays its own","The winner pays half"],"<b>CPR 44.2</b>: the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs, but the court has a wide <b>discretion</b>."],[1,"Which basis resolves doubt in favour of the paying party?",["Standard basis","Indemnity basis","Fixed costs basis"],"On the <b>standard basis</b>, doubt goes to the <b>paying party</b> and costs must be <b>proportionate</b>. On the <b>indemnity basis</b>, doubt favours the receiving party."],[2,"Which track uses fixed recoverable costs for claims issued after October 2023?",["Fast and intermediate tracks","Multi-track","Small claims only"],"<b>Fixed recoverable costs</b> apply on the <b>fast</b> and <b>intermediate</b> tracks. The multi-track uses budgets and assessment."],[3,"What does 'costs in any event' mean?",["That party gets them whatever the final outcome","They follow the final result","Each side bears its own"],"<b>Costs in any event</b>: the party gets them regardless of outcome. <b>Costs in the case</b> follows the final result."],[4,"What does 'costs thrown away' cover?",["Costs wasted by a step that is undone","Costs of the whole trial","Costs of the winning party only"],"<b>Costs thrown away</b> are costs wasted because a step must be undone, e.g. a set-aside default judgment."],[5,"A personal injury claimant loses at trial. Can the defendant usually enforce its costs order?",["No, under QOCS","Yes, in full","Yes, up to £10,000"],"<b>QOCS</b>: enforcement is limited to damages and costs the claimant receives, so a claimant who loses outright usually pays nothing."],[6,"When is QOCS protection lost without permission?",["The claim is struck out as an abuse","The claimant loses at trial","The claim is settled"],"Protection is lost on <b>strike out</b> (no reasonable grounds, abuse) without permission, or on <b>fundamental dishonesty</b> with permission."],[7,"What is the minimum relevant period in a Part 36 offer?",["21 days","14 days","28 days"],"<b>CPR 36.5</b>: a relevant period of <b>not less than 21 days</b>, in writing, stating it is made pursuant to Part 36."],[8,"A defendant's Part 36 money offer must be to pay within what time of acceptance?",["14 days","7 days","28 days"],"A defendant's offer must be a <b>single sum</b>, payable within <b>14 days</b> of acceptance."],[9,"A claimant accepts a defendant's Part 36 offer late. Who pays costs after the relevant period?",["The claimant pays the defendant's costs","The defendant pays the claimant's costs","Each bears its own"],"Usually the claimant gets costs to the <b>end of the relevant period</b> and pays the defendant's costs from then until acceptance."],[10,"The claimant's Part 36 offer is matched at trial. What does the defendant face?",["Indemnity costs and enhanced interest","Only standard costs","Loss of its defence"],"<b>CPR 36.17</b>: enhanced interest, <b>indemnity costs</b>, interest on costs and an additional amount (10% of damages up to £500,000)."],[11,"Against whom can security for costs be ordered?",["A claimant","A defendant defending a claim","A non-party witness"],"Security for costs is sought by a defendant against a <b>claimant</b> (or a counterclaimant), never against a defendant simply defending."]],"c":[[467,"What is the general rule on costs?","The <b>unsuccessful party</b> pays the <b>successful party's</b> costs (<b>CPR 44.2</b>). The court has a wide <b>discretion</b> to order otherwise."],[472,"When are costs assessed on the indemnity basis?","Where a party's conduct is <b>\"out of the norm\"</b>, and under <b>Part 36</b> when a claimant beats its own offer."],[473,"How does the standard basis differ from the indemnity basis?","<b>Standard</b>: costs must be <b>proportionate</b>; doubt goes to the <b>paying party</b>. <b>Indemnity</b>: no proportionality test; doubt goes to the <b>receiving party</b>."],[479,"What is summary assessment?","The judge fixes costs <b>at the end of the hearing</b>, usually for hearings of <b>one day or less</b>. <b>Statements of costs</b> (<b>N260</b>) are filed <b>24 hours</b> before."],[481,"What is the detailed assessment timetable?","<b>Notice of commencement</b> and bill within <b>3 months</b>. <b>Points of dispute</b> within <b>21 days</b>. Optional <b>reply</b> within 21 days. Bills of <b>£75,000 or less</b>: <b>provisional assessment</b> on paper."],[482,"What does \"costs in any event\" mean?","The party gets the costs of the application <b>whatever the final outcome</b>."],[483,"What does \"costs in the case\" mean?","Whoever pays costs at the <b>end of the case</b> also pays the costs of the application."],[489,"What is QOCS?","<b>Qualified one-way costs shifting</b> protects <b>personal injury claimants</b>. Costs orders against them are enforceable only up to the total of <b>damages, interest and costs</b> awarded to them."],[494,"What are the formal requirements of a Part 36 offer?","In <b>writing</b>. States it is made <b>pursuant to Part 36</b>. A <b>relevant period</b> of <b>at least 21 days</b>. Whether it relates to the <b>whole claim</b>, part or an issue. Whether it takes a <b>counterclaim</b> into account (<b>CPR 36.5</b>)."],[500,"What happens if a claimant fails to beat a defendant's Part 36 offer at trial?","The claimant pays the <b>defendant's costs</b> from the <b>end of the relevant period</b>, plus <b>interest</b> on those costs."],[501,"What are the consequences if a claimant's judgment is at least as good as its own Part 36 offer?","Defendant pays <b>interest on damages</b> up to <b>10% above base</b>. <b>Indemnity costs</b> from the end of the relevant period. <b>Interest on costs</b> up to 10% above base. An <b>additional amount</b> (<b>10%</b> to £500,000, <b>5%</b> above, cap <b>£75,000</b>)."],[508,"What are the conditions for security for costs?","Claimant <b>resident out of the jurisdiction</b> (anywhere abroad since April 2025). A <b>company</b> with reason to believe it <b>cannot pay</b>. <b>Changed address</b> to evade. <b>Failed to give or gave wrong address</b>. <b>Nominal claimant</b> unable to pay. Steps with <b>assets</b> hindering enforcement."]]},{"id":"dr-appeals","title":"Appeals","q":[[0,"A District Judge in the County Court decides an interim application. Where does the appeal go?",["A Circuit Judge","A High Court Judge","The Court of Appeal"],"Appeals go to the <b>next level of judge up</b>: District Judge to <b>Circuit Judge</b>."],[1,"A Circuit Judge decides a £150,000 County Court trial. Appeal goes where?",["A High Court Judge","The Court of Appeal","Another Circuit Judge"],"Circuit Judge or Recorder decisions go to a <b>High Court Judge</b>, whatever the track."],[2,"Where does an appeal from a High Court Judge go?",["The Court of Appeal","The Supreme Court","A Divisional Court"],"A <b>High Court Judge</b> decision is appealed to the <b>Court of Appeal</b>."],[3,"Where does an appeal from a High Court Master go?",["A High Court Judge","The Court of Appeal","A Circuit Judge"],"<b>Masters</b> and High Court district judges: appeal to a <b>High Court Judge</b>."],[4,"Who should the loser ask first for permission to appeal?",["The trial judge, at the hearing","The appeal court by letter","The Court of Appeal"],"Ask the <b>lower court</b> at the hearing where the decision is given. If refused, ask the appeal court in the appellant's notice."],[5,"What is the test for permission on a first appeal?",["Real prospect of success or other compelling reason","Balance of probabilities of success","Certainty that the judge erred"],"<b>CPR 52.6</b>: a <b>real prospect of success</b> or some other <b>compelling reason</b>."],[6,"How long to file the appellant's notice?",["21 days from the decision","14 days from the decision","28 days from the order"],"<b>CPR 52.12</b>: <b>21 days</b> after the date of the decision, unless the lower court directs otherwise."],[7,"Can the parties agree to extend the time for filing the appellant's notice?",["No, the court must extend","Yes, in writing","Yes, up to 7 days"],"Parties <b>cannot agree</b> an extension. The court must grant it, and late applications are judged like relief from sanctions."],[8,"Does filing an appeal stop the judgment being enforced?",["No, a stay must be obtained","Yes, automatically","Yes, for 21 days"],"<b>CPR 52.16</b>: no automatic stay. The appellant must apply for one."],[9,"Is a civil appeal usually a rehearing or a review?",["A review","A full rehearing","A new trial"],"<b>CPR 52.21</b>: appeals are normally a <b>review</b> of the decision, not a rehearing."],[10,"On a second appeal, who gives permission?",["Only the Court of Appeal","The first appeal judge","Either court"],"<b>Second appeals</b> need permission from the <b>Court of Appeal</b>, with a real prospect and an important point of principle or practice."],[11,"A claimant says the trial judge should have believed her over the defendant's witness. Likely outcome?",["Permission unlikely, findings of fact rarely disturbed","Permission readily given","The appeal court rehears the evidence"],"Appeal courts are very reluctant to interfere with <b>findings of fact</b>, especially on credibility, unless plainly wrong."]],"c":[[517,"What if the lower court refuses permission to appeal?","Ask the <b>appeal court</b> in the <b>appellant's notice</b>. A paper refusal can usually be <b>reconsidered orally</b>, unless <b>totally without merit</b>."],[518,"What is the test for permission for a first appeal?","A <b>real prospect of success</b>, <b>or</b> <b>some other compelling reason</b> for the appeal to be heard (<b>CPR 52.6</b>)."],[520,"Who gives permission for a second appeal and on what test?","Only the <b>Court of Appeal</b>. A <b>real prospect of success</b> <b>and</b> an <b>important point of principle or practice</b>, or another <b>compelling reason</b> (<b>CPR 52.7</b>)."],[521,"What is the time limit for filing an appellant's notice?","<b>21 days after the date of the decision</b>, unless the <b>lower court</b> directs otherwise (<b>CPR 52.12(2)</b>)."],[526,"Does an appeal stay the judgment?","<b>No</b>. The order takes effect unless a <b>stay</b> is ordered (<b>CPR 52.16</b>). The appellant must <b>apply for a stay</b>."],[527,"Where does an appeal from a County Court District Judge go?","To a <b>Circuit Judge</b>."],[528,"Where does an appeal from a Circuit Judge or Recorder go?","To a <b>High Court Judge</b>."],[536,"When will an appeal be allowed?","The decision was <b>wrong</b> (law, fact or discretion), or <b>unjust</b> because of a <b>serious procedural or other irregularity</b>."]]},{"id":"dr-enforcement","title":"Enforcement of money judgments","q":[[0,"A creditor has no idea what the debtor owns. First step?",["An order to obtain information","A charging order","A writ of control"],"<b>CPR 71</b> orders the debtor (or company officer) to attend and answer questions on <b>means and assets</b> before choosing a method."],[1,"The debtor has a large balance in a bank account. Best method?",["Third party debt order","Charging order","Attachment of earnings"],"A <b>third party debt order</b> freezes and captures money owed to the debtor by the bank."],[2,"A third party debt order: which money does it catch?",["Funds in the account when the interim order is served","Future deposits too","Overdrawn balances"],"It catches only money in the account <b>when the interim order is served</b>. It does not reach future deposits or overdrafts."],[3,"The debtor owns a house with large equity but little cash. Best method?",["Charging order","Attachment of earnings","Statutory demand only"],"A <b>charging order</b> secures the debt on the <b>land</b>; an <b>order for sale</b> may follow."],[4,"The debtor is employed on a steady salary. Which method?",["Attachment of earnings","Third party debt order","Charging order over shares"],"<b>Attachment of earnings</b> is a <b>County Court</b> remedy for <b>employed</b> debtors. It is not available against the self-employed."],[5,"The debtor is a self-employed plumber. Which method is unavailable?",["Attachment of earnings","Charging order","Writ of control"],"<b>Attachment of earnings</b> needs an employer, so it cannot be used against the <b>self-employed</b>."],[6,"A judgment of £8,000 is to be enforced by taking control of goods. Which court?",["The High Court","The County Court only","Either court"],"Judgments of <b>£5,000 or more</b> go to the <b>High Court</b> by writ of control, unless regulated consumer credit."],[7,"Who executes a County Court warrant of control?",["County Court bailiffs","High Court Enforcement Officers","The judgment creditor"],"<b>Bailiffs</b> execute <b>warrants of control</b>. HCEOs execute High Court <b>writs of control</b>."],[8,"Which goods cannot be taken by an enforcement agent?",["Tools of the debtor's trade, up to a value limit","The debtor's jewellery","Any goods in the debtor's house"],"Items needed for the debtor's work (up to <b>£1,350</b>) and <b>basic domestic needs</b> are exempt, along with goods owned by third parties."],[9,"What minimum debt is needed to petition for an individual's bankruptcy?",["£5,000","£750","£1,000"],"Bankruptcy needs a debt of at least <b>£5,000</b>. A company winding-up petition needs <b>£750</b>."],[10,"How long does a debtor have to respond to a statutory demand before a petition?",["21 days","14 days","28 days"],"If not paid, secured or compounded within <b>21 days</b>, the creditor can petition."],[11,"At what rate does interest run on High Court judgment debts?",["8% a year","5% a year","The Bank of England base rate"],"Judgment interest is <b>8% a year</b> on High Court judgments, and on County Court judgments of <b>£5,000 or more</b>."]],"c":[[541,"When must a money judgment normally be paid?","Within <b>14 days</b>, unless the court orders otherwise, e.g. <b>instalments</b> (<b>CPR 40.11</b>). No enforcement while instalments are kept up."],[542,"What interest runs on judgment debts?","<b>8%</b> a year on all High Court judgments and County Court judgments of <b>£5,000 or more</b>."],[544,"Which court enforces a judgment by taking control of goods?","<b>£5,000 or more</b>: <b>High Court</b>, unless a regulated <b>consumer credit</b> debt. <b>£600 to £4,999.99</b>: either court. <b>Under £600</b>: <b>County Court</b> only."],[546,"What is the purpose of an order to obtain information?","To find out the debtor's <b>means and assets</b> so the creditor can choose a method (<b>CPR 71</b>). It is fact-finding, not a way of getting paid."],[553,"Which goods are exempt from taking control?","Items <b>necessary for personal use</b> in work or study up to <b>£1,350</b> in total. Items for <b>basic domestic needs</b> of the household."],[555,"What is a third party debt order?","An order capturing money <b>owed to the debtor by a third party</b>, usually a <b>bank account in credit</b>, paid directly to the creditor (<b>CPR 72</b>)."],[557,"When is the final hearing for a third party debt order?","At least <b>28 days</b> after the interim order. The court decides whether to make it <b>final</b>."],[560,"What is a charging order?","An order <b>securing</b> the judgment debt on the debtor's <b>land</b> or securities. It makes the creditor a <b>secured</b> creditor (<b>CPR 73</b>)."],[566,"Against whom is attachment of earnings available?","<b>Employed</b> debtors, including occupational pensioners. <b>Not</b> the <b>self-employed</b>."],[572,"Which method suits each type of asset?","<b>Unknown</b>: order to obtain information. <b>Bank account</b>: third party debt order. <b>Land</b>: charging order. <b>Salary</b>: attachment of earnings. <b>Goods</b>: writ or warrant of control."]]}]},{"id":"ls","title":"Legal Services","stop":3,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><polygon points=\"-84,-84 0,-114 84,-84\" fill=\"#6D597A\"/><rect x=\"-76\" y=\"-84\" width=\"152\" height=\"84\" fill=\"#A0C4FF\"/><path d=\"M-64,-14 V-52 A20,20 0 0 1 -24,-52 V-14 Z\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\"/><path d=\"M-64,-44 H-24 M-64,-28 H-24\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-61\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-61\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-55\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-55\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-49\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-49\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-43\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-43\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-37\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-37\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-31\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-31\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M24,-14 V-52 A20,20 0 0 1 64,-52 V-14 Z\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\"/><path d=\"M24,-44 H64 M24,-28 H64\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"33\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"33\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"39\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"39\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"45\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"45\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"51\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"51\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"57\" y=\"-54\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"57\" y=\"-38\" width=\"5\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-14,0 V-34 A14,14 0 0 1 14,-34 V0 Z\" fill=\"#E76F51\"/><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-96\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\"/><rect x=\"-86\" y=\"-9\" width=\"30\" height=\"9\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-84\" y=\"-18\" width=\"26\" height=\"9\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-87\" y=\"-27\" width=\"28\" height=\"9\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"sra-regulatory-role","title":"The SRA, the Legal Services Act 2007 and reserved activities","q":[[0,"A client is annoyed by slow replies from her solicitor. Who handles a service complaint?",["Legal Ombudsman","The SDT","The Legal Services Board"],"The <b>Legal Ombudsman</b> deals with consumer complaints about poor service, after the firm's own complaints process."],[1,"By when must a client go to the Legal Ombudsman after the firm's final response?",["Within 6 months","Within 28 days","Within 3 years"],"The client must go to LeO within <b>6 months</b> of the firm's final response (and within 1 year of the problem or of finding out about it)."],[2,"Which of these is a regulatory objective under the Legal Services Act 2007?",["Promoting competition","Keeping affairs confidential","Duty to the court"],"<b>Promoting competition</b> is an aim for the regulators. Confidentiality and duty to the court are professional principles for lawyers."],[3,"Which of these is a professional principle in the Legal Services Act 2007?",["Keeping clients' affairs confidential","Improving access to justice","Promoting competition"],"<b>Confidentiality</b> is a professional principle that binds authorised persons. Access to justice is a regulatory objective."],[4,"How many Principles are in the SRA Principles?",["Seven","Five","Ten"],"The SRA Standards and Regulations (2019) have <b>seven Principles</b>, such as the rule of law, honesty and integrity."],[5,"Two SRA Principles pull in different directions. Which wins?",["The one protecting the public interest","The one serving the client","The one serving the firm"],"Where Principles conflict, those protecting the <b>public interest</b> (rule of law, public trust) come before the client's interests."],[6,"A solicitor is accused of stealing client money. Who alone can strike her off?",["The SDT","The SRA","The Legal Ombudsman"],"Only the <b>Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal</b> (or the High Court) can strike off or suspend. The SRA can fine, rebuke and set conditions."],[7,"Which body is the approved regulator for solicitors but has delegated its regulation to the SRA?",["The Law Society","The Legal Services Board","The Bar Standards Board"],"The <b>Law Society</b> is the approved regulator and the representative body. Day-to-day regulation is done by the independent SRA."],[8,"An unregulated company charges a fee to prepare papers for a grant of probate. Lawful?",["No, it is a reserved activity","Yes, probate is not reserved","Yes, if the estate is small"],"Preparing the papers for a grant, for a fee, is a reserved <b>probate activity</b>. Only authorised or exempt people may do it."],[9,"Which of these is NOT a reserved legal activity?",["Writing a will","Issuing court proceedings","Taking an affidavit"],"<b>Will writing</b> is not reserved. Conduct of litigation and administration of oaths (affidavits) are."],[10,"A non-lawyer carries on a reserved legal activity without authority. What is the legal position?",["A criminal offence","Only a regulatory breach","Only a civil wrong"],"Under <b>s.14 LSA 2007</b> it is a criminal offence for an unentitled person to carry on a reserved activity."],[11,"A solicitor works for an unregulated company. What must clients be told about protection?",["No Compensation Fund or compulsory PII","Nothing, the solicitor's PII applies","The SRA will pay any losses"],"Code paras 8.10-8.11: clients must understand they do <b>not</b> get the <b>Compensation Fund</b> or compulsory <b>PII</b> protection."]],"c":[[1,"What is the role of the Legal Services Board (LSB)?","It is the <b>oversight regulator</b>. It supervises the <b>approved regulators</b>. It does not regulate individual lawyers."],[5,"When can a client take a complaint to the Legal Ombudsman?","After <b>8 weeks</b> without resolution, or once the firm gives its <b>final response</b>. Within <b>6 months</b> of the final response. Within <b>1 year</b> of the problem (or of when they should have known)."],[6,"What can the Legal Ombudsman order?","An <b>apology</b>, a <b>fee reduction</b> or <b>compensation</b>. Compensation is capped at <b>£50,000</b>."],[7,"What are the LSA regulatory objectives?","Public interest; rule of law; <b>access to justice</b>; consumer interests; <b>competition</b>. An independent, strong, diverse and effective profession; public understanding of legal rights. Adherence to the professional principles; <b>prevention and detection of economic offences</b> (added by ECCTA 2023)."],[8,"What are the LSA professional principles?","<b>Independence and integrity</b>. <b>Proper standards</b> of work. Act in clients' <b>best interests</b>. <b>Duty to the court</b>. Keep clients' affairs <b>confidential</b>."],[11,"Which SRA Principles win if they conflict?","Those protecting the <b>public interest</b> (including the rule of law and public confidence). They take precedence over the <b>client's interests</b>."],[16,"What is the maximum fine the SRA can impose on a solicitor or traditional firm?","<b>£25,000</b>. It is <b>unlimited</b> for conduct relating to <b>economic crime</b> (under ECCTA, since 2024)."],[19,"Who can strike off or suspend a solicitor?","Only the <b>SDT</b> (or the High Court). The <b>SRA cannot</b>. The SDT can also impose an <b>unlimited fine</b> in any case."],[21,"What are the six reserved legal activities?","<b>Rights of audience</b>; <b>conduct of litigation</b>; <b>reserved instrument activities</b>. <b>Probate activities</b>; <b>notarial activities</b>; <b>administration of oaths</b>."],[24,"Which common legal services are not reserved legal activities under the Legal Services Act 2007?","<b>Will writing</b>; general legal <b>advice</b>; most <b>contract</b> drafting. <b>Employment tribunal</b> representation; most <b>tax</b> advice; <b>immigration</b> advice (regulated separately, not reserved). Mediation; estate administration <b>after</b> the grant."]]},{"id":"pii-other-providers","title":"Professional indemnity insurance and other regulated providers","q":[[0,"Lee &amp; Co LLP negligently causes a £3.5m loss. It has minimum PII. What will the insurer pay?",["Up to £3m plus defence costs","Up to £2m plus defence costs","The full £3.5m"],"The minimum for an <b>LLP or company</b> is <b>£3m per claim</b>, with defence costs on top. The client must pursue the LLP for the rest."],[1,"A firm's PII is on a claims-made basis. Which policy responds to a claim?",["The one in force when the claim is made","The one in force when the mistake happened","The one in force at the client's first instruction"],"<b>Claims-made</b> means the policy running when the claim is <b>first made</b> against the firm pays out."],[2,"The insurer finds the firm lied on its proposal form. Must it still pay the client's claim?",["Yes, then recover from the firm","No, it can avoid the policy","Only half of the claim"],"Under the Minimum Terms the insurer cannot avoid the policy for <b>misrepresentation</b>. It pays the client and <b>sues the firm</b> afterwards."],[3,"The firm's PII has a £20,000 excess. A client's loss is covered. Who bears the excess?",["The firm, after the insurer pays the client","The client, deducted from the payout","The Compensation Fund"],"The insurer pays the client in full and <b>recovers the excess from the firm</b>. It cannot be taken from the client."],[4,"A firm fails to renew its PII. How long is the extended policy period?",["30 days","60 days","6 years"],"The old policy continues for <b>30 days</b> (extended period), then a <b>60-day</b> cessation period. The firm cannot take new work in the cessation period."],[5,"A firm closes with no successor practice. How long does run-off cover last?",["6 years","2 years","12 months"],"The Minimum Terms require <b>6 years' run-off</b> cover for claims made after closure about earlier work."],[6,"A solicitor misses a limitation date. A client loses her claim. Which protection responds first?",["PII","The Compensation Fund","The Legal Ombudsman only"],"Missing a limitation date is <b>negligence</b>, so <b>PII</b> is the first port of call. The Compensation Fund is for dishonesty."],[7,"A solicitor steals the completion money and has no cover. What may help the client as a last resort?",["The SRA Compensation Fund","Compulsory PII","The Legal Services Board"],"The <b>Compensation Fund</b> is a discretionary last-resort fund for <b>dishonesty</b> or <b>failure to account</b>."],[8,"Does a client have a legal right to a Compensation Fund payment?",["No, it is discretionary","Yes, if the loss is proved","Yes, up to £2m"],"The Fund is <b>discretionary</b>: the SRA decides whether to pay and how much. It is also a <b>last resort</b>."],[9,"A solicitor employed by an unregulated will-writing company gives negligent advice. Is the Compensation Fund available?",["No","Yes, because a solicitor was involved","Yes, if the loss is under £2m"],"The Fund and compulsory PII do <b>not</b> cover clients of <b>unregulated</b> businesses, even if a solicitor works there."],[10,"An accountancy firm wants to offer conveyancing with two non-lawyer owners. What must it become?",["An ABS","A recognised sole practice","A freelance solicitor firm"],"A firm with non-lawyer owners doing reserved work needs a licence as an <b>alternative business structure (ABS)</b>."],[11,"A person holds a 10% stake in an ABS. What follows?",["They must be approved by the regulator","Nothing, it is under 25%","They must become a solicitor"],"A <b>material interest</b> (generally <b>10%</b> or more) must be approved by the licensing authority as fit and proper."]],"c":[[30,"What two financial safety nets do clients of SRA-authorised firms get?","<b>Professional indemnity insurance (PII)</b> for negligence. The <b>SRA Compensation Fund</b> for dishonesty or failure to account."],[32,"What is the minimum PII cover for a firm?","<b>£3 million</b> per claim for <b>LLPs and companies</b>. <b>£2 million</b> for sole practitioners and partnerships. <b>Defence costs</b> are paid on top."],[33,"What does claims-made basis mean for PII?","The policy in force <b>when the claim is first made</b> pays. Not the policy in force when the mistake happened."],[36,"Can a PII insurer refuse to pay because the firm lied when buying cover?","<b>No</b>. It cannot avoid or reduce payment for <b>non-disclosure</b>, <b>misrepresentation</b> or breach of terms. It can <b>recover</b> from the firm afterwards."],[37,"What happens if a firm fails to renew its PII?","<b>Extended policy period</b> of <b>30 days</b>. Then a <b>cessation period</b> of <b>60 days</b> (90 in total). No new work in the cessation period. No cover by the end means the firm must <b>close</b>."],[38,"What run-off cover applies when a firm closes?","<b>6 years' run-off cover</b> if it closes <b>without a successor practice</b>. It covers claims made after closure about earlier work."],[41,"What losses does the Compensation Fund cover?","Loss caused by <b>dishonesty</b> of a solicitor, firm or employee. <b>Failure to account</b> for money received. Grants where a firm lacked required <b>PII</b>."],[43,"What are the Compensation Fund limits?","Maximum grant normally <b>£2 million</b> per claim. Claim normally within <b>12 months</b> of becoming aware of the loss."],[49,"What compliance officers must an SRA firm have?","A <b>COLP</b> (compliance with the rules generally). A <b>COFA</b> (Accounts Rules). Both <b>approved</b> by the regulator. All SRA firms need them, not only ABSs."]]},{"id":"equality-act","title":"Equality Act 2010 and law firms","q":[[0,"Which of these is a protected characteristic in the provision of services?",["Pregnancy and maternity","Marriage and civil partnership","Being single"],"<b>Marriage and civil partnership</b> is protected at work only. <b>Pregnancy and maternity</b> is protected in work and services."],[1,"A law firm refuses to act for a woman because she is a Christian. What type of discrimination?",["Direct discrimination","Indirect discrimination","Harassment"],"Treating someone worse <b>because of</b> a characteristic (religion or belief) is <b>direct</b> discrimination."],[2,"Which protected characteristic allows direct discrimination to be justified?",["Age","Race","Sex"],"Direct discrimination cannot be justified, except for <b>age</b>: a <b>proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim</b>."],[3,"A firm requires all associates to work full-time in the office. This disadvantages women. What is it?",["Possible indirect discrimination","Direct discrimination","Victimisation"],"A neutral <b>PCP</b> that hits a group harder is <b>indirect</b> discrimination, unless it is a proportionate means of a legitimate aim."],[4,"A firm refuses to hire a woman because she cares for her disabled child. What is this?",["Discrimination by association","Indirect discrimination","Harassment"],"Direct discrimination covers treatment because of a person's <b>association</b> with someone who has a protected characteristic."],[5,"How long must an impairment last to be 'long-term' for disability?",["12 months or likely to last that long","6 months","3 months"],"Disability needs a substantial and <b>long-term</b> effect: lasted or likely to last <b>12 months</b>. Cancer, HIV and MS count from diagnosis."],[6,"A trainee complains in good faith about racist comments, then loses interesting work. What is this?",["Victimisation","Harassment","Indirect discrimination"],"Detriment because someone did a <b>protected act</b>, such as complaining, is <b>victimisation</b>, even if the complaint fails."],[7,"A disabled solicitor is disciplined for sick days caused by her condition. Which claim fits?",["Discrimination arising from disability","Indirect discrimination","Victimisation"],"<b>Section 15</b>: unfavourable treatment because of something arising from disability, unless justified or the firm did not know of the disability."],[8,"A firm has stepped entrance only. Must it wait for a wheelchair-using client to ask for help?",["No, the duty is anticipatory","Yes, only once she asks","Yes, unless it is a small firm"],"For <b>service providers</b> the reasonable adjustments duty is <b>anticipatory</b>: think ahead about disabled clients' needs."],[9,"A deaf client needs a BSL interpreter. Who pays?",["The firm","The client","The client, then reclaimed from the firm"],"The firm <b>cannot pass the cost</b> of a reasonable adjustment to the disabled person."],[10,"An employee harasses a colleague at work. What defence can the firm run to avoid liability?",["It took all reasonable steps to prevent it","It did not know about it","The employee acted against its policy"],"The employer is vicariously liable (<b>s.109</b>) unless it took <b>all reasonable steps</b> to prevent the conduct."],[11,"Two candidates are equally qualified. May a firm pick the one from an under-represented group?",["Yes, as lawful positive action","No, it is always unlawful","Only if the firm has quotas"],"<b>Positive action</b> allows a tie-breaker between equally qualified candidates. Quotas or choosing regardless of merit are unlawful."]],"c":[[59,"What are the nine protected characteristics?","Age; disability; gender reassignment; marriage and civil partnership; pregnancy and maternity. Race; religion or belief; sex; sexual orientation."],[61,"What is a disability under s.6 Equality Act?","A <b>physical or mental impairment</b>. With a <b>substantial</b> and <b>long-term</b> (<b>12 months</b>) adverse effect on normal day-to-day activities. <b>Cancer, HIV and MS</b> count from diagnosis."],[65,"What is direct discrimination (s.13)?","Treating a person <b>less favourably</b> than others <b>because of</b> a protected characteristic. It includes discrimination by <b>association</b> and by <b>perception</b>."],[66,"Can direct discrimination be justified?","<b>No</b>, except for <b>age</b>. Age discrimination can be justified as a <b>proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim</b>."],[67,"What are the elements of indirect discrimination (s.19)?","A <b>PCP</b> applied to everyone. It puts a group sharing a characteristic at a <b>particular disadvantage</b>. It disadvantages the individual. The firm <b>cannot show</b> it is a <b>proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim</b>."],[70,"What is harassment (s.26)?","<b>Unwanted conduct related to</b> a protected characteristic. With the <b>purpose or effect</b> of violating <b>dignity</b> or creating an <b>intimidating, hostile, degrading, humiliating or offensive</b> environment."],[73,"What is victimisation (s.27)?","A <b>detriment</b> because the person did a <b>protected act</b>. For example bringing a claim, giving evidence or alleging a breach. A <b>false</b> allegation made in <b>bad faith</b> is not protected."],[76,"What three things can trigger the duty to make reasonable adjustments?","A <b>PCP</b>. A <b>physical feature</b>. Lack of an <b>auxiliary aid or service</b>."],[77,"How is the reasonable adjustments duty different for service providers?","It is <b>anticipatory</b>. The firm must think in advance about what disabled clients in general may need. For employers it is owed to a <b>known</b> disabled person."],[78,"Can a firm charge a disabled client for a reasonable adjustment?","<b>No</b>. The firm <b>cannot pass on the cost</b>, e.g. of a BSL interpreter."],[81,"When is a firm vicariously liable for discrimination by employees (s.109)?","For acts done <b>in the course of employment</b>, whether or not it knew or approved. Its only defence is taking <b>all reasonable steps</b> to prevent them. The employee can also be personally liable (s.110)."]]},{"id":"aml-framework-cdd","title":"Money laundering: purpose, scope and the MLR 2017 (due diligence)","q":[[0,"A client pays money in, cancels the deal and wants a refund to a third party. Which stage?",["Layering","Placement","Integration"],"<b>Layering</b> hides the origin of money by moving it around. This aborted-deal refund is a classic red flag."],[1,"Does the MLR 2017 apply to a firm that only does employment advice?",["No","Yes, to all law firms","Yes, if it holds client money"],"The MLR 2017 apply to <b>transactional</b> work such as property, companies and trusts. Employment work is usually out of scope, though <b>POCA</b> still applies."],[2,"Which work is within the MLR 2017?",["Buying a company for a client","Defending a criminal trial","Acting in an employment tribunal"],"The MLR cover participation in <b>buying and selling businesses</b>, property, and managing trusts or client money."],[3,"For a company client, who is a beneficial owner?",["An individual owning over 25% of the shares","Any shareholder","Only the directors"],"A <b>beneficial owner</b> is an individual who owns or controls <b>more than 25%</b> of the shares or voting rights, or otherwise controls it."],[4,"Which of these always triggers CDD, whatever the sums involved?",["Suspicion of money laundering","Payment by debit card","A client that is a charity"],"CDD is required whenever the firm <b>suspects money laundering</b>, whatever the value of the transaction."],[5,"When should the firm normally verify the client's identity?",["Before the relationship starts","Within 30 days","At completion"],"Verification is normally completed <b>before</b> the business relationship is established or the transaction is carried out."],[6,"A client buying a flat through her company will not say who owns it. What must the firm do?",["Not act, and consider a SAR","Act, and finish checks later","Act, and tell the NCA after"],"If CDD cannot be completed the firm <b>must not act</b>, must terminate any relationship, and must <b>consider</b> making a <b>SAR</b>."],[7,"What does simplified due diligence (SDD) mean?",["Reduced checks for low risk clients","No checks at all","Checks only on the beneficial owner"],"<b>SDD</b> is lower-level CDD for low-risk cases. It is <b>not</b> no CDD: identity is still established and monitoring continues."],[8,"The firm acts for the spouse of a foreign government minister. What is required?",["Enhanced due diligence","Simplified due diligence","No extra checks"],"A <b>family member of a PEP</b> needs <b>EDD</b>: senior management approval, source of wealth and funds, and enhanced monitoring."],[9,"What is a client's 'source of wealth'?",["How the person built up their overall wealth","Where this deal's money comes from","Which bank holds the money"],"<b>Source of wealth</b> is how the person built up their overall wealth. <b>Source of funds</b> is where the money for this transaction comes from."],[10,"A firm relies on another firm's CDD checks. Who is liable if those checks fail?",["The relying firm","The other firm only","Nobody"],"Under <b>reg 39</b> the firm that <b>relies</b> on another's CDD remains liable for any failure."],[11,"How long must CDD and transaction records be kept?",["5 years","12 months","2 years"],"Records are kept for <b>5 years</b> after the business relationship ends or the one-off transaction is completed."]],"c":[[87,"What are the three stages of money laundering?","<b>Placement</b>: cash enters the system. <b>Layering</b>: moved to hide its origin. <b>Integration</b>: returns looking clean."],[94,"When do the MLR 2017 apply to solicitors?","When they <b>participate in financial or real property transactions</b>. E.g. buying or selling <b>land</b> or <b>businesses</b>; managing <b>client money</b> or assets; managing accounts. Organising contributions to companies; creating or managing <b>trusts or companies</b>."],[99,"What is the role of the MLRO?","The <b>nominated officer</b>. Receives internal suspicion reports. Decides whether to make a <b>SAR</b> to the <b>NCA</b>."],[100,"How long must CDD records be kept?","<b>5 years</b> after the end of the business relationship or completion of the occasional transaction."],[102,"When must CDD be carried out?","Establishing a <b>business relationship</b>. An <b>occasional transaction</b> of <b>£12,000 or more</b>. Any <b>suspicion</b> of money laundering. <b>Doubts</b> about earlier ID. Existing clients on a <b>risk-sensitive</b> basis."],[103,"What does CDD involve?","<b>Identify and verify</b> the client from a reliable, independent source. <b>Identify</b> the <b>beneficial owner</b> and take reasonable measures to verify. Understand the <b>purpose</b> of the transaction. <b>Ongoing monitoring</b>."],[104,"Who is a beneficial owner of a company?","Any individual owning or controlling <b>more than 25%</b> of shares or votes. Or who otherwise exercises <b>control</b>."],[108,"What must a firm do if it cannot complete CDD (reg 31)?","<b>Not</b> carry out the transaction or start the relationship. <b>Terminate</b> any existing relationship. <b>Consider</b> making a SAR."],[109,"What is the lawyers' exception to reg 31?","Where the lawyer is <b>ascertaining the legal position</b> for the client. Or <b>defending or representing</b> the client in proceedings."],[112,"When is EDD mandatory?","A party established in a <b>FATF call for action country</b> (the FATF 'black list', not the grey list). A <b>PEP</b> or their family or close associate. <b>Unusually complex or unusually large</b> transactions, unusual patterns, or no apparent purpose. <b>False or stolen</b> ID. Any other <b>high-risk</b> case."],[113,"Who is a politically exposed person (PEP)?","Someone with a <b>prominent public function</b>, UK or abroad. E.g. heads of state, ministers, <b>MPs</b>, senior judges, ambassadors. <b>Not</b> middle-ranking or junior officials."],[115,"What must a firm do when acting for a PEP?","Obtain <b>senior management approval</b>. Establish <b>source of wealth</b> and <b>source of funds</b>. Conduct <b>enhanced ongoing monitoring</b>."]]},{"id":"poca-offences-reporting","title":"Money laundering: POCA offences, defences and reporting","q":[[0,"In Da Silva, what does 'suspecting' money laundering mean?",["A possibility, more than fanciful","A firm, clear belief","Proof on the balance of probabilities"],"<b>R v Da Silva</b>: suspicion is a <b>low threshold</b>, a possibility that is more than fanciful. It need not be clear or firmly grounded."],[1,"A client says his flat deposit is from untaxed cash jobs. Is it criminal property?",["Yes, it represents the benefit of tax evasion","No, tax matters are civil","No, the work was lawful"],"<b>Any</b> crime counts, including <b>tax evasion</b>, with no minimum value. The unpaid tax is a benefit of crime."],[2,"Does s.328 POCA cover the ordinary conduct of genuine litigation?",["No (Bowman v Fels)","Yes, always","Yes, but only settlements"],"<b>Bowman v Fels</b>: s.328 does not cover the ordinary conduct of legal proceedings or a consensual settlement. Sham litigation is still caught."],[3,"A conveyancer proceeds knowing part of the price is the buyer's brother's drug money. Which offence?",["Section 328, an arrangement","Section 333A, tipping off","Section 342, prejudicing an investigation"],"Becoming concerned in an <b>arrangement</b> facilitating another's use of criminal property is <b>s.328</b>, a risk for transactional lawyers."],[4,"Who can commit the principal money laundering offences in ss.327-329?",["Anyone","Only the regulated sector","Only solicitors"],"The <b>principal offences</b> apply to <b>everyone</b>. Failing to disclose (s.330) and tipping off are for the regulated sector."],[5,"What is the maximum sentence for a principal money laundering offence?",["14 years","5 years","2 years"],"Ss.327-329 carry up to <b>14 years'</b> imprisonment and/or an unlimited fine."],[6,"What is the main defence to the principal offences?",["Authorised disclosure and consent","Telling the client first","Reporting after completion"],"Disclose <b>before</b> the act to the MLRO or NCA and get <b>consent</b> (a <b>DAML</b>), and the principal offences are defended."],[7,"After a SAR asking for consent, how long is the notice period?",["7 working days","31 days","28 days"],"If the NCA does not refuse within <b>7 working days</b>, consent is deemed. If it refuses, a <b>31-day</b> moratorium follows."],[8,"What is the moratorium period after the NCA refuses consent?",["31 days","7 working days","6 months"],"The moratorium is <b>31 days</b> from refusal. The firm must not proceed. A court can extend it by up to <b>186 days</b> more in total."],[9,"A solicitor never actually suspected laundering, but a reasonable person would have. Offence?",["Yes, under s.330","No, suspicion must be actual","Only if she was a partner"],"<b>Section 330</b> (regulated sector) includes an <b>objective</b> limb: reasonable grounds for suspicion is enough."],[10,"A client tells her solicitor about a fraud in order to get help carrying it out. Is it privileged?",["No, privilege is lost to further a crime","Yes, all legal advice is privileged","Yes, unless a SAR is made"],"<b>LPP</b> and the s.330 privileged circumstances defence are lost where the communication is made to <b>further a crime or fraud</b>."],[11,"A client asks why completion is delayed after the MLRO reported. What may the solicitor say?",["There is a delay, with no reason given","A SAR has been made","The NCA is investigating"],"Telling the client a SAR was made risks <b>tipping off</b> (s.333A). Delay them without explaining why, but do not lie."]],"c":[[122,"What does suspicion mean for POCA?","A <b>possibility that is more than fanciful</b> that the facts exist. A vague unease is not enough. It need not be clear or firmly grounded (Da Silva)."],[124,"What is the maximum sentence for the principal offences?","<b>14 years</b> and/or an unlimited fine."],[125,"What is the s.327 offence?","<b>Concealing</b>, disguising, converting or transferring criminal property. Or <b>removing</b> it from the UK jurisdictions."],[126,"What is the s.328 offence?","Entering into or becoming <b>concerned in an arrangement</b>. Which you know or suspect <b>facilitates</b> acquisition, retention, use or control of criminal property <b>by or for another</b>. The main risk for <b>transactional</b> lawyers."],[127,"What is the s.329 offence and its special defence?","<b>Acquiring, using or possessing</b> criminal property. Defence: <b>adequate consideration</b> (e.g. normal fees for genuine work). Not if you know or suspect the services help the criminal."],[128,"Does conducting litigation fall within s.328?","<b>No</b>. Ordinary <b>conduct of legal proceedings</b> or a consensual <b>settlement</b> is not an arrangement (Bowman v Fels). <b>Sham litigation</b> is still caught."],[129,"What is the main defence to the principal offences?","An <b>authorised disclosure</b> (s.338) to the <b>MLRO</b> or NCA <b>before</b> the act. Then <b>appropriate consent</b> (s.335), known as a <b>DAML</b>."],[132,"What is the NCA notice period?","<b>7 working days</b>, from the first working day after the disclosure. If the NCA does not refuse, consent is <b>deemed</b> and you can proceed."],[133,"What is the moratorium period?","<b>31 days</b> from the NCA's refusal. You <b>must not</b> proceed. If nothing more happens, you may go ahead at the end."],[135,"What are the elements of s.330 failure to disclose?","You <b>know or suspect</b>, or have <b>reasonable grounds</b> to. The information came <b>in the course of business in the regulated sector</b>. You can identify the launderer or property, or it may assist. You do not disclose to the <b>MLRO</b> or NCA <b>as soon as practicable</b>."],[137,"What are the defences to s.330?","<b>Reasonable excuse</b>. <b>Privileged circumstances</b>. <b>Lack of training</b> (for an employee who did not actually suspect)."],[140,"What is tipping off (s.333A)?","Disclosing that a <b>SAR</b> has been made or an <b>investigation</b> is contemplated, where <b>likely to prejudice</b> it. <b>Regulated sector</b> only. Maximum <b>2 years</b>."]]},{"id":"financial-services","title":"Financial services: FSMA 2000 and the exempt regime","q":[[0,"A firm carries on a regulated activity without authorisation or exemption. What is the legal position?",["A criminal offence under s.19 FSMA","Only a regulatory breach","Lawful if no commission is taken"],"The <b>general prohibition</b> (s.19 FSMA) bans unauthorised, non-exempt regulated activity. Breach is a criminal offence (up to 2 years)."],[1,"Which of these is a specified investment?",["Shares in a company","A house","Bank notes"],"<b>Shares</b> are specified investments. <b>Land</b> and <b>cash</b> are not, so selling a house is not regulated under FSMA."],[2,"A solicitor sells a client's house. Is this a regulated activity under FSMA?",["No, land is not a specified investment","Yes, it is dealing as agent","Yes, if for a fee"],"<b>Land and buildings</b> are not specified investments, so selling a house is outside FSMA. Arranging a mortgage might be caught."],[3,"Which statement is regulated advice on investments?",["'You should sell your shares in X plc'","'Shares are riskier than gilts'","'This share transfer form must be signed'"],"Advice on the merits of a <b>particular</b> investment is regulated. <b>Generic</b> or legal-effect advice is not."],[4,"A solicitor refers a client to an IFA for investment advice. What is the position?",["Excluded as an introduction","Regulated advice","Regulated arranging"],"<b>Introducing</b> a client to an authorised person such as an IFA is an excluded activity (art 33), so no authorisation is needed."],[5,"A solicitor advises on the sale of shares carrying 60% of a company's votes. Is this excluded?",["Yes, sale of a body corporate","No, shares are always regulated","No, it needs FCA authorisation"],"The <b>sale of a body corporate</b> exclusion (art 70) covers dealing, arranging or advising on a sale of <b>50% or more</b> of the voting shares."],[6,"Under the Part 20 exemption, the investment activity must be what to the legal service?",["Incidental","The main part of the practice","Separately charged"],"The Part 20 exemption needs the activity to be <b>incidental</b> to legal services, with no unaccounted-for reward and compliance with the SRA Scope Rules."],[7,"A firm earns commission on a policy it arranged for a client. What must it do to stay within Part 20?",["Account to the client for it","Keep it as extra income","Pay it to the SRA"],"The firm must not receive any reward from anyone but the client <b>unless it accounts to the client</b> for it (s.327)."],[8,"Can an SRA firm recommend a client buy units in a unit trust?",["No, the Scope Rules prohibit it","Yes, if incidental","Yes, with a written warning"],"The SRA Scope Rules <b>prohibit</b> recommending <b>retail investment products</b>. The firm should pass the client to an <b>authorised person</b>."],[9,"What must an SRA firm tell clients under the Financial Services Conduct of Business Rules?",["It is not authorised by the FCA","It is authorised by the FCA","It is regulated by the PRA"],"<b>Status disclosure</b>: the firm must tell clients in writing it is <b>not FCA authorised</b>, that it is regulated by the SRA, and how to complain."],[10,"A client pays a £3,000 bill in six interest-free monthly instalments. Is credit authorisation needed?",["No, it is an exempt agreement","Yes, always","Yes, if over £1,000"],"Credit repayable in <b>12 or fewer instalments within 12 months</b>, with <b>no interest or charges</b>, is exempt. Interest would remove the exemption."],[11,"A contract is made in breach of the general prohibition. What is the effect for the other party?",["Unenforceable against them, and they can recover money","Fully enforceable against them","Void only if it was in writing"],"Under <b>s.26 FSMA</b> the agreement is <b>unenforceable</b> against the other party, who can recover money paid plus compensation."]],"c":[[147,"What does s.19 FSMA (the general prohibition) say?","No one may carry on a <b>regulated activity</b> in the UK, or <b>purport</b> to, unless <b>authorised</b> or <b>exempt</b>."],[148,"What are the consequences of breaching the general prohibition?","<b>Criminal offence</b> (s.23, up to <b>2 years</b>). Agreements are <b>unenforceable</b> against the other party, who can recover money and <b>compensation</b> (s.26). A serious <b>regulatory</b> matter for a solicitor."],[151,"What are the four questions for whether an activity is regulated?","Is there a <b>specified investment</b>? A <b>specified activity</b>? Is it <b>by way of business</b>? Does an <b>exclusion</b> apply, and if not, can <b>Part 20</b> be used?"],[152,"Which investments are specified investments under FSMA 2000?","<b>Shares</b>; <b>debentures, bonds, gilts</b>; units in <b>collective investment schemes</b>. Options, futures, CFDs; <b>contracts of insurance</b> (incl. <b>ATE</b>). <b>Deposits</b>; regulated <b>mortgage</b> contracts; funeral plans; credit agreements."],[156,"What counts as advising on investments?","Advice to an <b>investor</b> on the <b>merits</b> of buying or selling a <b>particular</b> investment. <b>Generic</b> advice (e.g. shares are riskier than gilts) is not."],[158,"What is the introducing exclusion (art 33)?","Introducing a client to an <b>authorised person</b> (e.g. an <b>IFA</b>) for independent advice or discretionary management."],[160,"What is the necessary part of professional services exclusion (art 67)?","Arranging or advising that is a <b>necessary</b> part of other services. And <b>not remunerated separately</b>."],[163,"What are the four conditions of the Part 20 exemption (s.327)?","<b>No unaccounted-for reward</b>: commission passed to the client. <b>Incidental</b> to legal services. Only activities <b>permitted</b> by the SRA Scope Rules. <b>Compliance</b> with the Scope and Conduct of Business Rules."],[165,"What do the SRA Scope Rules prohibit?","<b>Market making</b> and dealing as principal for profit. <b>Recommending</b> a <b>retail investment product</b> or <b>personal pension</b>, or advising on pension transfers. Discretionary management for private clients (limited exceptions). Acting as a <b>lender</b> under regulated credit or mortgages."],[168,"What should a solicitor do if a client wants a recommendation of a particular pension or unit trust?","<b>Introduce</b> them to an <b>IFA</b> (excluded under art 33). Recommending a retail investment product is <b>prohibited</b> under Part 20."]]},{"id":"funding-private-cfa-dba","title":"Funding: private retainers, CFAs and DBAs","q":[[0,"When must a solicitor give the client price and likely cost information?",["At engagement and as the matter progresses","Only at engagement","Only when the client asks"],"Code para <b>8.7</b>: give the <b>best possible information</b> on pricing and likely overall cost, at the outset and as the matter goes on."],[1,"A firm gives a client an estimate of fees. Is it binding?",["No, but it must be realistic","Yes, as a cap","Yes, as a fixed fee"],"An <b>estimate</b> is not binding but must be realistic and updated. A <b>quotation</b> or <b>cap</b> is binding."],[2,"A client pays money on account of costs before work starts. How is it held?",["As client money in the client account","In the firm's office account","As the firm's income"],"Money on account is <b>client money</b> until a bill is delivered or the money is spent on a disbursement. It goes in the <b>client account</b>."],[3,"A CFA success fee is calculated as a percentage of what?",["The base costs","The damages","The opponent's costs"],"A <b>CFA</b> success fee is an uplift on <b>costs</b>. A <b>DBA</b> payment is a percentage of <b>damages</b>."],[4,"What is the general cap on a CFA success fee?",["100% of base costs","50% of base costs","25% of damages"],"The general cap on a CFA success fee is <b>100% of base costs</b>. Personal injury has a further cap on damages."],[5,"In a personal injury CFA, the success fee is capped at 25% of which damages?",["PSLA and past financial loss","All damages","PSLA and future loss"],"The cap is <b>25%</b> of damages for <b>PSLA</b> and <b>past pecuniary loss</b>. Future loss is excluded to protect future care money."],[6,"A claimant wins under a CFA. Who pays the success fee?",["The client, usually from damages","The losing defendant","The Legal Aid Agency"],"Since <b>April 2013</b> (LASPO) the success fee is <b>not recoverable</b> from the loser. The client usually pays it from damages."],[7,"A CFA is proposed for a divorce case. Is this allowed?",["No, not in family proceedings","Yes, with a 100% uplift","Yes, if in writing"],"CFAs cannot be used in <b>criminal</b> or <b>family</b> proceedings. A CFA must also be in writing."],[8,"A client loses under a pure CFA. What might the client still have to pay?",["The opponent's costs and disbursements","The success fee","The solicitor's base costs"],"No fee is due to the solicitor, but the client may owe <b>disbursements</b> and the <b>opponent's costs</b>, hence <b>ATE</b> insurance."],[9,"A DBA payment is a percentage of what?",["The damages recovered","The base costs","The hourly rate"],"A <b>DBA</b> (contingency fee) gives the lawyer a <b>percentage of damages</b>, and only if the client wins."],[10,"What is the maximum DBA payment in an employment tribunal claim?",["35%","25%","50%"],"DBA caps: <b>25%</b> personal injury (of PSLA and past loss), <b>35%</b> employment tribunal, <b>50%</b> all other civil claims."],[11,"A DBA for a commercial claim takes 55% of damages. What is the position?",["Unenforceable, over the 50% cap","Enforceable if in writing","Enforceable, with VAT extra"],"The cap for <b>other civil claims</b> is <b>50%</b>, including VAT and counsel's fees. A non-compliant DBA is unenforceable."]],"c":[[172,"What costs information must a solicitor give under Code para 8.7?","The <b>best possible information</b> about how the matter will be <b>priced</b>. And the <b>likely overall cost</b>, at the outset and as the matter progresses."],[177,"Is a quotation or fixed fee binding?","<b>Yes</b>. The firm cannot charge more for the work described. Extra work outside the scope can be charged if agreed."],[181,"How can a client challenge a solicitor's bill?","Ask the court to <b>assess</b> it under the <b>Solicitors Act 1974</b>. Apply within <b>one month</b> of delivery for assessment as of right. Or complain to the firm and then the <b>Legal Ombudsman</b>."],[184,"How does a CFA work?","Fees are payable <b>only if the client wins</b>. On a win: <b>base costs</b> plus any <b>success fee</b>. The success fee is a <b>percentage uplift on base costs</b>."],[187,"What is the general cap on a CFA success fee?","<b>100%</b> of base costs."],[188,"What is the personal injury cap on a CFA success fee?","<b>25%</b> of damages for <b>PSLA</b> and <b>past pecuniary loss</b>, net of CRU. <b>Future loss</b> is excluded."],[189,"Who pays a CFA success fee?","The <b>client</b>, usually from damages (since <b>1 April 2013</b>, LASPO s.44). It is <b>not recoverable</b> from the opponent. The opponent still pays <b>base costs</b>."],[192,"What are the DBA caps?","<b>Personal injury</b>: <b>25%</b> of PSLA and past loss. <b>Employment tribunal</b>: <b>35%</b>. <b>Other civil claims</b>: <b>50%</b>. Caps <b>include VAT</b> and counsel's fees."],[193,"What happens if a DBA does not comply with the Regulations?","It is <b>unenforceable</b>: the lawyer gets nothing under it. It must be in writing and specify the claim, when payment is due and why that percentage."]]},{"id":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","title":"Funding: legal aid, third-party funding and legal expenses insurance","q":[[0,"Which body administers civil legal aid?",["The Legal Aid Agency","The Legal Ombudsman","The Law Society"],"The <b>Legal Aid Agency (LAA)</b>, part of the Ministry of Justice, runs legal aid. Providers need an LAA contract."],[1,"A poor client wants legal aid for a road accident injury claim. What is the position?",["Out of scope","In scope, if means are met","In scope, if prospects are good"],"Most <b>personal injury</b> claims are <b>out of scope</b> of civil legal aid, however poor the client. Check scope first."],[2,"Which case is generally within civil legal aid scope?",["Defending possession of the client's home","A contract dispute","A divorce about finances with no abuse"],"<b>Housing</b> cases involving loss of the home are in scope. Most contract and private family disputes are not."],[3,"A private child arrangements dispute has evidence of domestic abuse. Is legal aid possible?",["Yes, it is in scope","No, private family law is always out","Only with exceptional funding"],"Private family cases are in scope <b>where there is evidence of domestic abuse</b> or child abuse."],[4,"When can an out-of-scope case get exceptional case funding?",["If refusal would breach ECHR rights","If the client is on benefits","If damages exceed £10,000"],"<b>ECF</b> (LASPO s.10) is available if refusing legal aid would breach, or risk breaching, the client's <b>Convention rights</b>."],[5,"A client on Universal Credit applies for legal aid. What does passporting mean?",["Income test is met automatically","Capital test is met automatically","Merits test is met automatically"],"<b>Passporting</b> satisfies the <b>income</b> test. The client's <b>capital</b> is still assessed, and merits must still be met."],[6,"A legally aided client wins a house in a dispute. What can the LAA do?",["Take a statutory charge over it","Nothing, it is protected","Take 25% of the damages only"],"The <b>statutory charge</b> (LASPO s.25) gives the LAA a first charge on money or property recovered or preserved. Warn the client at the outset."],[7,"A legally aided client loses. How much of the opponent's costs may the court order?",["Only a reasonable amount","All of them","None, ever"],"<b>Costs protection</b> (s.26): liability is limited to what is <b>reasonable</b>, considering both parties' resources. It is often nil."],[8,"A commercial funder pays the costs of a claim. What does it receive if the claim fails?",["Nothing","Its money back from the client","Its money back from the solicitor"],"Third-party funding is <b>non-recourse</b>: the funder takes a return only if the claim wins. It can still face an adverse costs order."],[9,"What should a solicitor check first when advising a client on funding?",["Whether the client has BTE insurance","Whether to use a DBA","Whether a funder will back it"],"Check for <b>BTE</b> cover or <b>trade union</b> funding first, then legal aid, then CFA or DBA with ATE, then third-party funding."],[10,"Can a winning claimant recover the ATE insurance premium from the loser?",["No, since April 2013","Yes, in full","Yes, if over £1,000"],"Since <b>LASPO (April 2013)</b> the ATE premium is not recoverable, so it comes out of the client's damages. Clinical negligence expert reports are an exception."],[11,"A client's BTE insurer wants its own panel solicitor. When can the client choose a different lawyer?",["Once proceedings start","Never","Only after the claim ends"],"Under the 1990 Regulations the insured can choose their own lawyer once <b>proceedings</b> begin or where a <b>conflict of interest</b> arises."]],"c":[[201,"Which family cases are in scope for legal aid?","Cases with evidence of <b>domestic abuse</b> or <b>child abuse</b> (for the victim). <b>Care proceedings</b>."],[206,"What does the legal aid means test look at?","<b>Gross income</b>. <b>Disposable income</b>. <b>Disposable capital</b>."],[207,"What is passporting?","Clients on certain means-tested benefits (e.g. <b>Universal Credit</b>) <b>automatically pass the income test</b>. Their <b>capital</b> is still assessed."],[209,"What prospects of success are needed for full representation?","Generally <b>moderate</b> (<b>50% or more</b>). <b>Marginal</b> may be enough in some categories. <b>Poor</b> (below 45%) fails."],[212,"What is the statutory charge?","The LAA's <b>first charge</b> on money or property <b>recovered or preserved</b> (LASPO <b>s.25</b>). It recovers unrecovered legal aid costs. The client must be <b>warned</b> at the outset."],[213,"What costs protection does a legally aided loser have?","They pay the opponent's costs only up to a <b>reasonable</b> amount (LASPO <b>s.26</b>). Considering both parties' <b>resources</b> and conduct. Often <b>nil</b>."],[218,"What is the effect of PACCAR?","A funding agreement based on a <b>percentage of damages</b> is a <b>DBA</b>. It is <b>unenforceable</b> unless DBA-compliant. It cannot be used in opt-out CAT collective proceedings."],[223,"When can a BTE insured choose their own lawyer?","Once <b>proceedings or an inquiry</b> start. Or where a <b>conflict of interest</b> arises."],[225,"Is the ATE premium recoverable from the opponent?","<b>No</b> (since April 2013). Exception: in <b>clinical negligence</b>, the part covering <b>expert reports on liability and causation</b>."]]},{"id":"transparency-client-care","title":"Transparency Rules, client care and costs information","q":[[0,"Where must a firm publish its Transparency Rules information?",["Prominently on its website","In the client care letter only","In a local newspaper"],"Information goes in a <b>prominent place on the firm's website</b>. A firm with no website gives it on request."],[1,"For which service must a firm publish price information for individuals?",["Residential conveyancing","Commercial property","Contested probate"],"<b>Residential conveyancing</b> is a listed service. Commercial property, family, personal injury and contested probate are not."],[2,"Which information must every SRA firm publish, whatever work it does?",["Its complaints procedure","Its prices","Its fee earners' salaries"],"The <b>complaints</b> and <b>regulatory</b> information applies to every firm. Price information applies only to listed services."],[3,"Which item must be in a firm's published price information?",["Likely disbursements and VAT","The firm's annual turnover","The partners' profit shares"],"Price information must cover the <b>total cost</b>, <b>disbursements</b>, VAT, services included, staff experience and <b>timescales</b>."],[4,"When must a client be told in writing about the right to complain?",["At the time of engagement","When a complaint is made","At the end of the matter"],"Code para <b>8.3</b>: tell clients in writing <b>at engagement</b> about the right to complain and any right to go to the Legal Ombudsman."],[5,"A complaint is unresolved after 8 weeks. What must the firm do?",["Tell the client in writing about LeO","Wait for the client to chase","Report it to the SRA"],"Para <b>8.4</b>: tell the client in writing of the right to complain to <b>LeO</b>, the time frame and LeO's contact details."],[6,"A solicitor cold calls members of the public to advertise her services. Allowed?",["No, unless they are current or former clients","Yes, if the call is polite","Yes, in office hours"],"Para <b>8.9</b> bans <b>unsolicited approaches</b> to the public, except to current or former clients."],[7,"A firm's brochure overstates its success rate. Which duty is breached?",["Publicity must not be misleading","Duty to report to the NCA","Duty to keep client money safe"],"Para <b>8.8</b>: publicity must be <b>accurate and not misleading</b>, including about charges and interest."],[8,"A client complains about a bill. Can the firm charge for dealing with the complaint?",["No, it must be free","Yes, at hourly rates","Yes, if the complaint fails"],"Para <b>8.5</b>: complaints must be dealt with <b>promptly, fairly and free of charge</b>."],[9,"Which body deals with a client's complaint about misconduct, such as taking client money?",["The SRA","The Legal Ombudsman","The Legal Services Board"],"<b>LeO</b> deals with <b>service</b> complaints. <b>Conduct</b> matters can be reported to the <b>SRA</b> at any time."],[10,"A consumer instructs a solicitor by phone only. What right does she have?",["To cancel within 14 days","To cancel within 7 days","No cancellation right"],"Under the <b>Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013</b> a consumer contracting at a distance or off-premises has <b>14 days</b> to cancel."],[11,"A phone client wants work to start within the 14 days. What is needed?",["An express request from the client","Nothing, work can just start","A deposit of 50%"],"The client must <b>expressly request</b> early start. If they then cancel, they pay for work done up to cancellation."]],"c":[[231,"For which individual services must price information be published?","<b>Residential conveyancing</b>. <b>Uncontested probate</b>. <b>Motoring offences</b>. <b>Bringing</b> ET claims for unfair or wrongful dismissal. <b>Immigration</b> (not asylum)."],[232,"For which business services must price information be published?","<b>Debt recovery</b> up to <b>£100,000</b>. <b>Defending</b> ET claims for unfair or wrongful dismissal. <b>Licensing</b> applications for business premises."],[234,"What must every firm publish, whatever services it offers?","Its <b>complaints procedure</b>, including how to complain to <b>LeO</b> and the <b>SRA</b>. That it is <b>regulated by the SRA</b>, with its number (and the <b>SRA clickable logo</b>)."],[237,"What must clients be told under Code para 8.3?","<b>In writing at engagement</b>: their right to <b>complain</b> about services and charges. How and to whom. Any right to complain to <b>LeO</b> and <b>when</b>."],[238,"What must clients be told under Code para 8.4?","If unresolved after <b>8 weeks</b>: in writing, their right to go to <b>LeO</b>, the time frame and full contact details. If the complaints procedure is <b>exhausted</b>: that the firm cannot settle it, the name and website of an approved <b>ADR</b> body and whether the firm will use it."],[239,"How must complaints be dealt with (para 8.5)?","<b>Promptly</b>, <b>fairly</b> and <b>free of charge</b>."],[247,"When do the Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013 give a client of a law firm a right to cancel?","When a <b>consumer</b> contract is made <b>off-premises</b> or <b>at a distance</b> (e.g. online, phone, at the client's home). The firm must give <b>pre-contract information</b> and the right to <b>cancel within 14 days</b>."],[248,"What if the firm fails to give cancellation information?","The cancellation period is <b>extended</b> by up to <b>12 months</b>."]]}]},{"id":"contract","title":"Contract Law","stop":4,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><rect x=\"-24\" y=\"-36\" width=\"74\" height=\"36\" fill=\"#FFE8A3\"/><rect x=\"-18\" y=\"-28\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#7BDFF2\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"54\" y=\"-92\" width=\"16\" height=\"92\" fill=\"#DDE3EA\"/><polygon points=\"42,-92 82,-92 76,-114 48,-114\" fill=\"#7BDFF2\"/><rect x=\"44\" y=\"-120\" width=\"36\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#10294A\"/><path d=\"M62,-120 V-132\" fill=\"none\"/><g transform=\"translate(-30,-92) rotate(-10)\"><path d=\"M-6,0 L-22,22 L-10,22 L12,0 Z\" fill=\"#4361EE\"/><path d=\"M-34,-8 H26 Q42,-8 42,0 Q42,8 26,8 H-28 Z\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M-34,-8 L-42,-26 L-30,-26 L-18,-8 Z\" fill=\"#E63946\"/><path d=\"M-6,-2 L-16,-18 L-6,-18 L8,-2 Z\" fill=\"#4361EE\"/><circle cx=\"-12\" cy=\"-1\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#7BDFF2\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"-2\" cy=\"-1\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#7BDFF2\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"8\" cy=\"-1\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#7BDFF2\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"-1\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#7BDFF2\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g><path d=\"M-80,-4 H-30 M-20,-4 H0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"offer-acceptance","title":"Agreement: offer and acceptance","q":[[0,"A company offers £100 to anyone who catches flu after using its product, and deposits the money. The advert is?",["A unilateral offer to the world","An invitation to treat","A mere sales puff"],"<b>Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball</b>: the deposit showed intent; acceptance was by performing the conditions."],[1,"At an auction, who makes the offer?",["The bidder","The auctioneer","The seller"],"The auctioneer only invites bids (an <b>invitation to treat</b>). The bid is the offer; the hammer falling is acceptance."],[2,"A seller offers goods at £1,000. The buyer asks, 'Can I pay in instalments?' Is the offer still open?",["Yes, it was only an enquiry","No, it is a counter-offer","No, the offer has lapsed"],"<b>Stevenson v McLean</b>: a mere request for information is not a counter-offer, so the offer survives."],[3,"A buyer asks for the 'lowest price' for a property. The seller replies with a figure. Is that reply an offer?",["No, only information","Yes, it is an offer","Yes, if the buyer pays a deposit"],"<b>Harvey v Facey</b>: stating the lowest acceptable price is just information, not an offer to sell."],[4,"The seller offers land, then a reliable third party tells the buyer it has been sold elsewhere. Can the buyer still accept?",["No, the offer is revoked","Yes, until the seller tells them","Yes, for a reasonable time"],"<b>Dickinson v Dodds</b>: revocation can be communicated by any reliable source."],[5,"Can an offeror revoke a unilateral offer once the offeree has started to perform?",["No, not while performance continues","Yes, at any time","Yes, if notice is posted"],"<b>Errington v Errington</b>: the offeror cannot withdraw once the offeree has begun the performance."],[6,"A buyer accepts an offer by email. When is acceptance effective?",["When the seller receives it","When the buyer sends it","When the seller reads it"],"The postal rule is limited to post. For instantaneous communication, acceptance takes effect on <b>receipt</b> (Entores v Miles Far East)."],[7,"Each side sends its own standard terms. The buyer's form is signed last and the goods delivered. Whose terms usually govern?",["The party who fired the last shot","The party who made the first offer","Neither, there is no contract"],"<b>Butler Machine Tool v Ex-Cell-O</b>: the last shot wins where the other party accepts it by conduct."],[8,"An offer is made by letter. The buyer does not reply for several months. Can the buyer still accept?",["Probably not, it has lapsed","Yes, offers last for ever","Yes, if the seller is silent"],"<b>Ramsgate Victoria Hotel v Montefiore</b>: an offer lapses after a reasonable time."],[9,"The acceptance letter is posted on Monday and arrives on Wednesday. When is the contract formed?",["Monday, on posting","Wednesday, on arrival","Tuesday, when it is collected"],"<b>Adams v Lindsell</b>: under the <b>postal rule</b>, acceptance is complete when properly posted."],[10,"A claimant returns a lost wallet without knowing a reward was offered. Can the claimant claim it?",["No, acceptance needs knowledge of the offer","Yes, performance is enough","Yes, if the reward was in writing"],"<b>R v Clarke</b>: the offeree must know of the offer when performing, or there is no acceptance."],[11,"An auction is advertised 'without reserve'. The auctioneer refuses the highest bid. What is the position?",["Breach of a collateral contract","No breach, it is only an invitation","The bid is void"],"<b>Barry v Heathcote Ball</b>: the auctioneer is bound by a collateral contract to sell to the highest bidder."]],"c":[[3,"What is an offer?","A statement of terms on which the offeror is willing to be bound. With the <b>intention to be bound as soon as it is accepted</b>. Nothing more to negotiate."],[5,"What is the difference between a bilateral and a unilateral contract?","<b>Bilateral</b>: a promise for a promise; both bound on acceptance. <b>Unilateral</b>: a promise for an <b>act</b>; accepted by <b>performing</b> the act."],[8,"What is an invitation to treat?","An invitation to <b>make offers</b> or negotiate. It <b>cannot be accepted</b>. The responder makes the offer."],[9,"What is the legal status of goods displayed in a shop?","An <b>invitation to treat</b>. The <b>customer offers</b> at the till. The shop <b>accepts</b> or refuses."],[19,"What is the effect of a counter-offer?","It is <b>not</b> an acceptance. It <b>rejects and destroys</b> the original offer, which cannot later be accepted."],[21,"What is the battle of the forms and the usual rule?","Each business sends its own <b>standard terms</b>. Each form is a <b>counter-offer</b>. The <b>last shot</b> before performance is accepted by conduct."],[30,"When does an email acceptance take effect?","On <b>receipt</b>, judged by reference to <b>business hours</b>. The postal rule does not apply."],[31,"What is the postal rule?","A letter of acceptance takes effect when <b>properly posted</b>. Even if delayed or <b>lost</b>."],[34,"Can an offeror revoke after promising to keep the offer open?","<b>Yes</b>, at any time before acceptance. Unless the offeree <b>paid</b> for the promise (an <b>option</b>)."],[35,"When does revocation take effect?","When it is <b>communicated</b> (actually received). Posting a revocation is not enough."],[38,"Can a unilateral offer be revoked once performance has started?","<b>No</b>. The offeree must have a reasonable chance to <b>finish</b>. The offeror must not <b>prevent</b> completion."]]},{"id":"consideration","title":"Consideration and promissory estoppel","q":[[0,"A friend fixes a car as a favour. Afterwards the owner promises £200 for it. Enforceable?",["No, past consideration","Yes, the work was valuable","Yes, if the promise is oral"],"<b>Re McArdle</b>: past consideration is no consideration. The work was done before the promise."],[1,"A claimant does a task at the defendant's request, both expecting payment, then the defendant promises a price. Enforceable?",["Yes, a Lampleigh exception","No, it is past consideration","Only if made by deed"],"<b>Lampleigh v Brathwait / Pao On</b>: past work on request with understood payment can support a later promise."],[2,"A witness under a subpoena is promised payment for attending court. Is there good consideration?",["No, it is an existing public duty","Yes, attending is valuable","Yes, if the sum is reasonable"],"<b>Collins v Godefroy</b>: performing an existing public duty imposed by law is not good consideration."],[3,"Police provide far more protection than required, on request, for a promised payment. Can they claim it?",["Yes, they went beyond duty","No, it is a public duty","Only a reasonable sum"],"<b>Glasbrook Bros v Glamorgan CC</b>: exceeding the public duty is good consideration."],[4,"Two sailors desert, and the captain promises the rest extra pay to sail home. Under Stilk v Myrick?",["Not enforceable, existing duty","Enforceable as a gift","Enforceable if written"],"<b>Stilk v Myrick</b>: doing only what you already owed is no consideration."],[5,"A contractor promises a subcontractor extra pay to finish work already owed, gaining a practical benefit. Binding?",["Binding if no duress","Never binding","Binding only by deed"],"<b>Williams v Roffey</b>: a practical benefit is good consideration if there is no duress or fraud."],[6,"A debtor pays £500 of £1,000 on the due date; the creditor agrees it is enough. Can she sue for the rest?",["Yes, no consideration","No, the debt is discharged","Only after six years"],"<b>Foakes v Beer</b> (affirming Pinnel's Case): part payment is no consideration for a promise to forgo the rest."],[7,"Which part payment can discharge a larger debt?",["A smaller sum paid earlier than due","A smaller sum paid on the due date","A smaller sum paid by bank transfer"],"<b>Pinnel's Case</b>: paying earlier, at a different place, or with a chattel can be fresh consideration."],[8,"A son promises to stop complaining to his father in return for a debt being released. Consideration?",["No, nothing of real value","Yes, any promise counts","Yes, if the father agreed"],"<b>White v Bluett</b>: a promise to stop complaining gives nothing of value, so it is not good consideration."],[9,"Which case established the modern doctrine of promissory estoppel?",["Central London Property v High Trees","Foakes v Beer","Stilk v Myrick"],"<b>High Trees</b> (1947): a landlord who promised reduced rent was estopped from claiming the full rent for that period."],[10,"A landlord accepts reduced rent during a crisis. When the crisis ends, can he return to full rent?",["Yes, on reasonable notice","No, never","Only with a court order"],"Promissory estoppel is normally <b>suspensory</b>: rights can be resumed on reasonable notice (Tool Metal v Tungsten)."],[11,"A debtor pressures a struggling creditor into accepting part payment. Can the debtor rely on estoppel?",["No, the debtor acted inequitably","Yes, the promise was clear","Yes, the debtor relied on it"],"<b>D &amp; C Builders v Rees</b>: promissory estoppel is equitable, so a party who used pressure cannot rely on it."]],"c":[[44,"What is consideration?","The <b>price paid for the other party's promise</b>. A <b>promise</b>, an <b>act</b> or a <b>forbearance</b>."],[48,"What does sufficient but need not be adequate mean?","Consideration must have <b>some value</b> in law. The court does <b>not</b> ask if it was a <b>fair price</b> (e.g. £1 a year rent; chocolate wrappers)."],[52,"When is a past act good consideration?","Done at the <b>promisor's request</b>. Both <b>understood it would be paid</b> for. A promise to pay in advance would have been <b>enforceable</b> (Lampleigh; Pao On)."],[57,"What is the practical benefit test?","A contract for goods or services. <b>Before completion</b>, B doubts A will complete. B <b>promises extra</b> payment. B obtains a <b>practical benefit</b>. No <b>economic duress or fraud</b> by A."],[59,"Is part payment of a debt consideration for forgiving the rest?","<b>No</b> (Pinnel; Foakes v Beer). The creditor can sue for the <b>balance</b>."],[60,"Can practical benefit make a promise to accept less binding?","<b>No</b> (Re Selectmove). It applies to promises to <b>pay more</b>, not to accept less."],[62,"When does part payment discharge the whole debt?","Payment at the <b>creditor's request</b> <b>earlier</b>, at a <b>different place</b>, or with <b>something different</b>. Part payment by a <b>third party</b>. A <b>composition</b> with creditors. A <b>disputed</b> or unliquidated claim. <b>Promissory estoppel</b>."],[68,"What are the elements of promissory estoppel?","An <b>existing legal relationship</b>. A <b>clear and unequivocal promise</b> not to enforce strict rights. <b>Reliance</b> by the promisee. <b>Inequitable</b> to go back on it."],[71,"Can promissory estoppel found a claim?","<b>No</b>. It is a <b>shield, not a sword</b>. It cannot create a new cause of action."],[72,"Does promissory estoppel end the promisor's rights permanently?","Usually it only <b>suspends</b> them. Full rights can resume for the <b>future</b> on <b>reasonable notice</b>, or when the circumstances end."]]},{"id":"intention-certainty-capacity","title":"Intention, certainty and capacity","q":[[0,"A husband working abroad promises his wife £30 a month. Is it legally enforceable?",["No, presumed no intention","Yes, it is a contract","Yes, if he signed it"],"<b>Balfour v Balfour</b>: domestic arrangements are presumed not to be intended as legally binding."],[1,"A separated couple sign a written agreement about house payments. Is the presumption of no intention applied?",["No, it is rebutted","Yes, it still applies","Only if there are children"],"<b>Merritt v Merritt</b>: where the parties are separated and the deal is written, intention is found."],[2,"A mother promises to support her daughter while she trains as a barrister, but they fall out. Enforceable?",["No, family arrangement","Yes, it was a business deal","Yes, because it was in writing"],"<b>Jones v Padavatton</b>: family arrangements usually lack intention to create legal relations."],[3,"Which clause can rebut the presumption of intention in a commercial agreement?",["An honourable pledge clause","A governing law clause","An entire agreement clause"],"<b>Rose &amp; Frank v Crompton</b>: stating the deal is binding in honour only, not law, rebuts the presumption."],[4,"A deal for a van is made 'on hire-purchase terms', with no terms agreed. Enforceable?",["No, too uncertain","Yes, terms will be implied","Yes, if the van was delivered"],"<b>Scammell v Ouston</b>: a vague, incomplete term makes the agreement too uncertain to enforce."],[5,"When can a court rescue a vague term in a commercial deal that has been partly performed?",["Using trade custom or past dealings","Never","Only if a price was fixed"],"<b>Hillas v Arcos</b>: courts try to give effect to commercial agreements by reference to custom and previous dealings."],[6,"The parties agree to negotiate in good faith about future terms. Enforceable?",["No, an agreement to negotiate","Yes, as a contract","Yes, if signed"],"<b>Walford v Miles</b>: an agreement to negotiate or an agreement to agree is too uncertain to enforce."],[7,"A contract for goods says nothing about price. What is payable under the Sale of Goods Act 1979?",["A reasonable price","Nothing","The seller's list price"],"<b>s.8 SGA 1979</b>: where the price is not fixed, the buyer pays a reasonable price."],[8,"A 16-year-old buys a non-necessary car on credit. Can the seller enforce it against her?",["Generally no","Yes, she is over 16","Yes, if she signed"],"Contracts for non-necessaries are generally <b>not enforceable against a minor</b> (under 18)."],[9,"An adult guarantees a minor's loan, which the minor cannot be made to repay. Is the guarantee enforceable?",["Yes, under the 1987 Act","No, it falls with the loan","Only if the minor ratifies"],"<b>Minors' Contracts Act 1987 s.2</b>: a guarantee is not unenforceable merely because the minor's obligation is."],[10,"When can a person lacking mental capacity avoid a contract?",["If the other party knew of the incapacity","Always","Only after a court declaration"],"Such a contract is <b>voidable</b> at the option of the person lacking capacity if the other party knew or ought to have known."],[11,"A buyer is so drunk they cannot understand the deal, and the seller knows. What is the contract?",["Voidable by the buyer","Void from the start","Fully binding"],"<b>Gore v Gibson</b>: drunkenness avoids the contract only if the other party knew of it."]],"c":[[75,"What is the presumption for domestic or social agreements?","<b>No</b> intention to be legally bound. The party saying it is binding must prove otherwise."],[77,"What is the presumption for commercial agreements?","Intention <b>is</b> present. The burden of proving otherwise is <b>heavy</b>."],[79,"How is the commercial presumption rebutted?","A clear <b>honour clause</b>. <b>Subject to contract</b>. A <b>letter of comfort</b> stating only policy."],[87,"Is an agreement to agree the price binding?","<b>No</b>, an agreement to agree an <b>essential term</b> is not a contract."],[93,"What is the general rule for contracts with minors?","<b>Binding on the adult</b>. <b>Not enforceable against the minor</b>, who can enforce it."],[94,"What must a minor pay for necessaries?","A <b>reasonable price</b>, not necessarily the contract price."],[96,"Are beneficial contracts of service binding on a minor?","<b>Yes</b>, if on the whole for the minor's <b>benefit</b> (employment, apprenticeship, training). <b>Not</b> if on the whole <b>oppressive</b>."],[101,"When can a contract be avoided for mental incapacity or drunkenness?","It is <b>voidable</b> if the person could not understand. <b>Only if</b> the other party <b>knew or ought to have known</b>."]]},{"id":"privity-third-parties","title":"Privity and third party rights","q":[[0,"Two fathers agree with each other to pay the groom money. One fails to pay. Can the groom sue at common law?",["No, he is not a party","Yes, he benefits","Only the daughter can"],"<b>Tweddle v Atkinson</b>: a person who is not a party to the contract cannot enforce it. This is <b>privity</b>."],[1,"Under the 1999 Act, when can a third party enforce a term?",["If it expressly says so or purports to benefit them","Always","Only if they paid consideration"],"<b>s.1(1)</b> Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999: express provision, or the term purports to confer a benefit."],[2,"Under s.1(2) of the 1999 Act, what if a term benefits a third party but the parties did not intend it to be enforceable?",["The third party cannot enforce it","The third party can still enforce it","The contract is void"],"<b>s.1(2)</b> disapplies s.1(1)(b) if, on a proper construction, the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party."],[3,"Why could the claimant in Dunlop v Selfridge not enforce a contract made by others?",["Consideration must move from the claimant","The contract was oral","The price was too low"],"Dunlop v Selfridge: <b>consideration must move from the promisee</b>. The claimant gave none."],[4,"A third party who can enforce a term under the 1999 Act can claim which remedies?",["Those available to a contracting party","Damages only","Nominal damages only"],"<b>s.1(5)</b>: the third party has all the remedies that would be available had the third party been a party."],[5,"Under the 1999 Act, when can the parties no longer vary the term without the third party's consent?",["Once the third party has assented","As soon as the contract is signed","After one month"],"<b>s.2</b>: variation needs consent once the third party has assented, or has relied and the promisor knew or could foresee it."],[6,"A third party sues on a term. Can the promisor raise defences it could raise against the other party?",["Yes, arising from the contract","No, never","Only a set-off"],"<b>s.3</b>: the promisor may rely on defences and set-offs that arise from or in connection with the contract."],[7,"Can a third party rely on an exclusion clause in a contract between others under the 1999 Act?",["Yes, via s.1(6)","No, never","Only if a party agrees later"],"<b>s.1(6)</b>: enforcing a term includes availing oneself of an exclusion or limitation clause."],[8,"A family's holiday is spoilt and the father alone booked it. Who can recover for the family's disappointment?",["The father, for the whole group","Each child separately","Nobody"],"<b>Jackson v Horizon Holidays</b>: the contracting party may recover damages for the family's loss."],[9,"A creditor assigns only half of a debt to a friend. Can it be a legal assignment?",["No, only equitable","Yes, if in writing","Yes, if notice is oral"],"A <b>legal</b> assignment under s.136 LPA 1925 must be of the whole (absolute) debt. A part assignment is equitable."],[10,"A debtor owes £500 to the assignor, but has a valid set-off. The assignee sues. Result?",["The debtor can raise it against the assignee","The debtor loses the set-off","The assignee wins automatically"],"An assignee takes <b>subject to equities</b>: defences and set-offs available against the assignor."],[11,"Which method transfers the burden of a contract to a new person?",["Novation","Assignment","Notice to the debtor"],"The burden cannot be assigned. <b>Novation</b> replaces the contract with a new one, with all parties' consent."]],"c":[[105,"What is privity of contract?","A contract creates rights and obligations <b>only between the parties</b>. A third party cannot <b>enforce</b> it or be <b>bound</b> by it."],[108,"What are the two routes to enforcement under s.1?","<b>s.1(1)(a)</b>: the contract <b>expressly provides</b> the third party may enforce. <b>s.1(1)(b)</b>: the term <b>purports to confer a benefit</b> on them."],[109,"How is the s.1(1)(b) presumption rebutted?","Only if the contract, properly construed, shows the parties <b>did not intend</b> the third party to enforce (s.1(2)). <b>Silence is not enough</b>."],[111,"How must the third party be identified (s.1(3))?","<b>Expressly in the contract</b>. By <b>name</b>, <b>class</b> or <b>description</b>. They <b>need not exist</b> yet."],[115,"Can a third party rely on an exclusion clause under the Act?","<b>Yes</b> (s.1(6)). Enforcing includes taking advantage of an <b>exclusion or limitation</b> clause."],[116,"When can the parties not vary or cancel without the third party's consent (s.2)?","The third party has <b>communicated assent</b> to the promisor. The promisor <b>knows</b> of reliance. The promisor could <b>reasonably foresee</b> reliance <b>and</b> the third party has relied."],[120,"What defences can the promisor raise against the third party (s.3)?","Any defence or <b>set-off</b> arising from the contract that it would have had against the <b>promisee</b>. Any defence it would have against the <b>third party</b> personally."],[127,"When can a promisee recover damages for a third party's loss?","Exceptionally: e.g. <b>family holidays</b> or group meals. In building contracts where the property passes to a <b>later owner</b>, unless the owner has a <b>direct right</b> to sue."],[134,"How can the burden of a contract be transferred?","Only by <b>novation</b>: a new contract agreed by <b>all parties</b>. The burden cannot be assigned."]]},{"id":"express-terms","title":"Express terms: incorporation, interpretation, classification and variation","q":[[0,"A buyer signs a contract without reading it. Is she bound by its terms?",["Yes, by her signature","No, she did not read it","Only the terms she knew"],"<b>L'Estrange v Graucob</b>: a signature binds, absent fraud or misrepresentation."],[1,"For terms printed on a ticket, what must the party relying on them have done?",["Taken reasonable steps to give notice","Obtained a signature","Read them to the customer"],"<b>Parker v South Eastern Railway</b>: reasonable notice of the terms must be given before the contract is made."],[2,"A very onerous term is buried in small print. What extra is needed for incorporation?",["Special notice of the term","A signature","Nothing"],"<b>Interfoto v Stiletto</b>: a particularly onerous or unusual term must be fairly and fully brought to attention."],[3,"What does the parol evidence rule say about a written contract?",["Oral evidence cannot add to or vary it","Oral terms always override","Only emails can vary it"],"The rule limits <b>extrinsic evidence</b> that adds to or contradicts a written contract (with exceptions such as collateral contracts)."],[4,"A car dealer with expert knowledge states the mileage and the buyer relies on it. Likely status?",["A term of the contract","A mere representation","A puff"],"<b>Dick Bentley v Harold Smith</b>: a statement by a party with special knowledge is likely to be a term."],[5,"Breach of a warranty gives the innocent party what remedy?",["Damages only","Termination and damages","Rescission"],"A <b>warranty</b> is a minor term: breach gives damages but no right to terminate."],[6,"Breach of a condition gives the innocent party what?",["The right to terminate and damages","Damages only","Specific performance only"],"A <b>condition</b> goes to the root of the contract: breach allows termination as well as damages."],[7,"Under Hong Kong Fir, what decides whether breach of an innominate term allows termination?",["How serious the breach's consequences are","The label of the term","Who drafted it"],"An <b>innominate term</b>: termination only if the breach deprives the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit."],[8,"How do courts interpret a contract, following Arnold v Britton?",["By its natural meaning in context","By what each party hoped","To protect the weaker party"],"The court looks at the natural meaning of the words. <b>Commercial common sense</b> does not rescue a party from a bad bargain."],[9,"An ambiguous exclusion clause is read against which party, under contra proferentem?",["The party relying on it","The party signing it","The court's choice"],"<b>Contra proferentem</b>: ambiguity is resolved against the party who drafted or seeks to rely on the clause."],[10,"A contract says it can only be varied in writing. The parties vary it orally. Effective?",["Generally no","Yes, always","Yes, if one party agrees"],"<b>MWB v Rock Advertising</b> (2018): a no oral modification clause is valid, subject to estoppel."],[11,"What does an entire agreement clause do?",["Excludes pre-contract statements as terms","Makes the contract void","Stops all variations"],"It keeps pre-contract statements out of the terms. Excluding misrepresentation liability must still be <b>reasonable</b> (s.3 MA 1967)."]],"c":[[138,"What factors point towards a statement being a term?","The recipient made clear it was <b>vital</b>. The maker has <b>special knowledge</b>. The maker said <b>not to check</b>. Made just <b>before</b> contracting. Included in the <b>written</b> contract."],[143,"What does an entire agreement clause do?","Prevents a pre-contract statement being a <b>term</b> or <b>collateral warranty</b>. It does <b>not</b> by itself exclude <b>misrepresentation</b> liability."],[144,"What are the three ways a written term is incorporated?","<b>Signature</b>. <b>Reasonable notice</b>. <b>Course of dealing</b>."],[147,"When must notice of an unsigned term be given?","<b>Before or at the time</b> the contract is made. Terms introduced afterwards are <b>too late</b>."],[150,"How much notice is needed for an onerous or unusual term?","The more <b>onerous or unusual</b>, the more clearly it must be <b>drawn to attention</b> (red hand rule). A general reference to conditions is not enough."],[154,"What is the aim of contract interpretation?","What a <b>reasonable person</b> with the parties' <b>background knowledge</b> would understand the words to mean."],[168,"What is the remedy for breach of a condition?","<b>Terminate and/or damages</b>, however minor the actual consequences."],[169,"What is the remedy for breach of a warranty?","<b>Damages only</b>. No right to terminate."],[170,"When can an innocent party terminate for breach of an innominate term?","Only if the breach <b>deprives them of substantially the whole benefit</b> of the contract. Otherwise damages only."],[180,"Is a no oral modification clause effective?","<b>Yes</b> (Rock Advertising). An oral variation that does not comply is <b>ineffective</b>."]]},{"id":"implied-terms","title":"Implied terms: common law and statute","q":[[0,"In The Moorcock, a term was implied to make the contract work. What test is that?",["Business efficacy","Fairness","Public interest"],"<b>The Moorcock</b>: a term is implied if needed to give the contract business efficacy."],[1,"What did Marks and Spencer v BNP Paribas say about implying a term for reasonableness?",["Reasonableness alone is not enough","Reasonableness is enough","Courts should imply fair terms"],"A term is implied only if <b>necessary</b> for business efficacy or so obvious it goes without saying."],[2,"A landlord's duty to keep common parts in repair was implied in Liverpool CC v Irwin. What kind of implication?",["A term implied in law","A term implied in fact","A statutory term"],"<b>Terms implied in law</b>: implied into a class of contract where necessary, regardless of the parties' intentions."],[3,"Goods are sold by a seller who has no right to sell them. Which SGA term is breached?",["s.12, the right to sell","s.13, description","s.15, sample"],"<b>s.12 SGA</b>: an implied condition that the seller has the right to sell. Rowland v Divall: the buyer recovered the full price."],[4,"Tinned fruit is described as packed 30 per case but arrives 24 per case. Otherwise fine. Breach?",["Yes, of s.13","No, same value","Only of s.14"],"<b>Re Moore &amp; Landauer</b>: the description must be matched exactly, even if the goods are equally good."],[5,"A neighbour privately sells a used mower, which breaks down. Is there an implied term of satisfactory quality?",["No, not a business sale","Yes, always","Yes, if over £100"],"<b>s.14(2) SGA</b> applies only where the seller sells <b>in the course of a business</b>."],[6,"A buyer with unusually sensitive skin, who did not tell the seller, gets dermatitis from a coat. Seller in breach of fitness for purpose?",["No, the sensitivity was not disclosed","Yes, the coat was unfit","Yes, liability is strict"],"<b>Griffiths v Peter Conway</b>: under <b>s.14(3) SGA</b> the particular purpose must be made known; the unusual sensitivity was not."],[7,"A seller points out a scratch before sale. Can the buyer later claim unsatisfactory quality for that scratch?",["No, it was drawn to attention","Yes, always","Yes, if it was serious"],"<b>s.14(2C)</b>: no breach of satisfactory quality for defects specifically drawn to the buyer's attention before contracting."],[8,"Goods are sold by sample and the bulk differs from the sample. Which section is breached?",["s.15","s.12","s.14(3)"],"<b>s.15 SGA</b>: an implied condition that the bulk will correspond with the sample in quality."],[9,"Which statute implies reasonable care and skill into a B2B services contract?",["Supply of Goods and Services Act 1982","Sale of Goods Act 1979","Misrepresentation Act 1967"],"<b>SGSA 1982 s.13</b>. For consumers, <b>CRA 2015 s.49</b> applies."],[10,"A services contract fixes no time for performance. What is implied?",["A reasonable time","Within 7 days","No time limit"],"<b>s.14 SGSA 1982</b>: the service must be carried out within a reasonable time."],[11,"A consumer's service was done without reasonable care. What is the first remedy under the CRA 2015?",["Repeat performance","Price reduction","Termination"],"<b>CRA s.55</b>: the consumer may require repeat performance. Price reduction (s.56) follows if that fails."]],"c":[[186,"What is the modern test for implying a term in fact?","<b>Necessary for business efficacy</b> <b>or</b> so <b>obvious</b> it goes without saying (alternatives). Capable of <b>clear expression</b>. Must not <b>contradict an express term</b> (M&amp;S v BNP Paribas)."],[193,"What does SGA s.12 imply?","The seller has the <b>right to sell</b> (good title): a <b>condition</b>. Quiet possession and freedom from undisclosed charges are <b>warranties</b> (s.12(2)). Applies to private sellers."],[194,"What does SGA s.13 imply?","Goods correspond with their <b>description</b>. A <b>condition</b>; applies to private sellers. Even a small departure can be a breach."],[195,"What does SGA s.14(2) imply and who is bound?","Goods are of <b>satisfactory quality</b>. Only where sold <b>in the course of a business</b>."],[200,"What does SGA s.14(3) imply?","Goods are <b>reasonably fit</b> for a <b>particular purpose</b> the buyer makes known. Unless the buyer <b>did not rely</b>, or it was <b>unreasonable</b> to rely, on the seller."],[202,"Which implied terms does a private seller give?","Only <b>title</b> (s.12), <b>description</b> (s.13) and <b>sample</b> (s.15). <b>No</b> quality or fitness terms."],[205,"What terms does SGSA Part II imply into B2B services?","<b>s.13</b>: <b>reasonable care and skill</b>. <b>s.14</b>: <b>reasonable time</b>. <b>s.15</b>: <b>reasonable charge</b>."],[208,"Who is a consumer under the CRA?","An <b>individual</b> acting wholly or mainly <b>outside</b> their trade, business, craft or profession. A <b>company</b> is never a consumer."],[214,"What are the three tiers of consumer remedies for goods?","<b>Short-term right to reject</b>. <b>Repair or replacement</b>. <b>Price reduction</b> or <b>final right to reject</b>."],[215,"How long is the short-term right to reject?","<b>30 days</b> from the latest of transfer, <b>delivery</b> and <b>installation</b>. Shorter for <b>perishable</b> goods."],[218,"When can a consumer claim price reduction or final rejection?","After <b>one</b> failed repair <b>or</b> replacement. Or if repair or replacement is <b>impossible</b> or not done properly in time. One or the other, not both."],[220,"What is the 6-month presumption under the CRA?","Goods failing within <b>6 months</b> of delivery are <b>presumed</b> faulty at delivery unless the trader proves otherwise. It applies to tiers 2 and 3, <b>not</b> the short-term right to reject."]]},{"id":"exemption-clauses","title":"Exemption clauses and unfair terms (UCTA 1977 and CRA 2015 Part 2)","q":[[0,"A B2B clause excludes liability for negligently damaged goods. What applies?",["The reasonableness test","Automatically void","Automatically valid"],"<b>UCTA 1977 s.2(2)</b>: other negligence loss can be excluded only if the clause satisfies reasonableness."],[1,"UCTA s.3 applies to a party's written standard terms. What must a clause excluding liability for breach satisfy?",["Reasonableness","Nothing","Plain English only"],"<b>s.3</b>: where one party deals on the other's written standard terms, exclusions must be reasonable."],[2,"Does a fundamental breach automatically defeat an exclusion clause?",["No, it is a question of construction","Yes, always","Yes, if serious"],"<b>Photo Production v Securicor</b>: no rule of law. The clause's wording decides, subject to UCTA."],[3,"At what time is reasonableness under UCTA assessed?",["When the contract was made","When the breach occurred","At trial"],"<b>s.11(1)</b> UCTA: the circumstances known or contemplated when the contract was made."],[4,"Which is a Schedule 2 UCTA reasonableness guideline?",["Relative bargaining strength","Size of the loss in the end","Brand reputation"],"Sch 2 includes bargaining strength, inducement, knowledge of the term and whether goods were made to special order."],[5,"A B2B seller tries to exclude the s.12 SGA implied term as to title. Effect?",["Void","Valid if reasonable","Valid if signed"],"<b>UCTA s.6(1)</b>: liability for breach of s.12 cannot be excluded."],[6,"A clause in a consumer contract excludes liability for death by negligence. Effect?",["Not binding on the consumer","Valid if reasonable","Valid if prominent"],"<b>CRA 2015 s.65</b>: a consumer contract term cannot exclude liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence."],[7,"When is a consumer contract term unfair under the CRA 2015?",["Significant imbalance, contrary to good faith","Any term the consumer dislikes","Any term not negotiated"],"<b>s.62(4)</b>: a term is unfair if, contrary to good faith, it causes a significant imbalance to the consumer's detriment."],[8,"A consumer contract term is ambiguous. How is it read?",["In the consumer's favour","In the trader's favour","As void"],"<b>CRA s.69(1)</b>: where a term could have more than one meaning, the meaning most favourable to the consumer prevails."],[9,"How does the CRA treat terms on the Schedule 2 grey list?",["They may be unfair","They are automatically unfair","They are always fair"],"The list gives <b>examples</b> of terms that may be regarded as unfair. Each is assessed individually."],[10,"Who is a consumer under the CRA 2015?",["An individual acting outside their trade","Any business buyer","Any buyer of goods"],"<b>s.2(3)</b>: an individual acting for purposes wholly or mainly outside their trade, business, craft or profession."],[11,"Exclusion terms appear only on a car park ticket issued by a machine as the driver enters. Incorporated?",["No, notice came too late","Yes, it was printed","Yes, if the driver kept it"],"<b>Thornton v Shoe Lane Parking</b>: the contract was made at the machine, so terms on the ticket came too late."]],"c":[[230,"How are exemption clauses construed?","<b>Strictly</b>: clear words are needed. A genuinely <b>ambiguous</b> clause is read <b>against</b> the party relying on it (contra proferentem)."],[232,"How do you decide whether a clause covers negligence?","Does it <b>expressly</b> mention <b>negligence</b> (or a synonym)? If not, are the words <b>wide enough</b>? If so, could it realistically cover <b>other liability</b> instead? These are <b>guidelines</b> (Canada Steamship)."],[237,"Can a business exclude liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence?","<b>No</b>. It is <b>void</b> (UCTA <b>s.2(1)</b>)."],[238,"How does UCTA treat exclusion of other loss caused by negligence?","Valid only if <b>reasonable</b> (<b>s.2(2)</b>)."],[239,"When does UCTA s.3 apply?","Where one party deals on the <b>other's written standard terms of business</b>. Clauses excluding liability for breach, or allowing <b>substantially different</b> or <b>no</b> performance, must be <b>reasonable</b>."],[241,"How does UCTA treat exclusion of the SGA implied terms?","<b>Title</b> (s.12): <b>void</b>. <b>Description, quality, fitness, sample</b> (ss.13-15): <b>reasonableness</b> (s.6(1A))."],[243,"How is exclusion of liability for misrepresentation controlled?","Valid only if <b>reasonable</b> (Misrepresentation Act 1967 <b>s.3</b>, applying the UCTA test)."],[245,"What is the UCTA reasonableness test (s.11)?","Was it <b>fair and reasonable to include</b> the term. Considering what was known or contemplated <b>when the contract was made</b>."],[253,"What terms are blacklisted under the CRA for goods and digital content?","Excluding or restricting liability for the <b>statutory rights</b> for goods (<b>s.31</b>) or digital content (<b>s.47</b>)."],[255,"What does CRA s.65 say?","Liability for <b>death or personal injury</b> caused by <b>negligence</b> cannot be excluded or restricted."],[256,"What is the CRA fairness test (s.62)?","A term is <b>unfair</b> if, <b>contrary to good faith</b>, it causes a <b>significant imbalance</b> in rights and obligations <b>to the consumer's detriment</b>."],[262,"What is the core terms exemption (s.64)?","Terms on the <b>main subject matter</b> or the <b>appropriateness of the price</b> cannot be assessed for fairness. Only if <b>transparent</b> and <b>prominent</b>."]]},{"id":"misrepresentation","title":"Misrepresentation","q":[[0,"A seller says, 'I intend to farm this land' but never meant to. Can this be a misrepresentation?",["Yes, as a false statement of fact","No, it is only an intention","No, it is an opinion"],"<b>Edgington v Fitzmaurice</b>: a statement of present intention is a statement of fact about the maker's state of mind."],[1,"A seller says nothing about a defect in the house. Is that a misrepresentation?",["Generally not, silence is not enough","Yes, always","Yes, if the defect was serious"],"There is <b>no general duty of disclosure</b>, so silence alone is not usually a misrepresentation."],[2,"The seller says the farms are fully let, but omits that the tenants have given notice to quit. Actionable?",["Yes, a misleading half-truth","No, the statement was true","No, silence"],"<b>Dimmock v Hallett</b>: a true statement that gives a false impression can be a misrepresentation."],[3,"A statement is true when made but false by the time of the contract. What must the maker do?",["Correct it","Nothing","Wait for the buyer to ask"],"<b>With v O'Flanagan</b>: a representor must correct a statement that has become false before the contract."],[4,"A buyer could easily have checked the statement but did not. Can the buyer still claim?",["Yes, no duty to check","No, the buyer was careless","Only if the loss was large"],"<b>Redgrave v Hurd</b>: the representee need not check, even if they could have."],[5,"A buyer inspects the accounts and relies on their own checks. Can they claim misrepresentation?",["No, not induced","Yes, if the statement was false","Yes, if it was fraud"],"<b>Attwood v Small</b>: if the buyer actually relied on their own investigation, the statement did not induce the contract."],[6,"What is fraud under Derry v Peek?",["A statement made knowing it is false or reckless","Any careless statement","Any false opinion"],"<b>Derry v Peek</b>: knowingly false, without belief in its truth, or reckless as to whether it is true."],[7,"Under s.2(1) Misrepresentation Act 1967, who must prove reasonable grounds for belief?",["The representor","The claimant","The court"],"<b>s.2(1)</b>: the representor is liable unless they prove they had reasonable grounds to believe, up to the time of contract."],[8,"A court refuses rescission for innocent misrepresentation and awards money instead. Under which section?",["s.2(2) Misrepresentation Act 1967","s.2(1)","s.3"],"<b>s.2(2)</b>: the court may award damages in lieu of rescission where that would be equitable."],[9,"A buyer finds out five years later that a painting's attribution was an innocent misrepresentation. Can they rescind?",["No, lapse of time","Yes, any time","Yes, if the painting is unsold"],"<b>Leaf v International Galleries</b>: for innocent misrepresentation, delay can bar rescission."],[10,"A buyer is misled into buying goods and sells them on to an innocent third party. Effect on rescission?",["Barred, third party rights","Not affected","Allowed with notice"],"Rescission is barred where a third party has acquired rights for value without notice."],[11,"In deceit, which losses can the claimant recover?",["All direct losses, even unforeseeable","Only foreseeable losses","Only wasted expenditure"],"<b>Smith New Court v Scrimgeour Vickers</b>: for fraud, all losses directly flowing from the transaction are recoverable."]],"c":[[268,"What is a misrepresentation?","An <b>untrue statement of fact or law</b>. Made by one party to the other <b>before or at</b> contracting. Which <b>induces</b> the contract."],[269,"What is the effect of a misrepresentation on the contract?","It makes the contract <b>voidable</b>. Valid until the innocent party <b>rescinds</b>. Damages may also be available."],[273,"When is a statement of opinion a misrepresentation?","When the maker has <b>special knowledge</b>, so it <b>implies facts</b> justifying it. Or the opinion was <b>not honestly held</b>."],[277,"Is silence a misrepresentation?","<b>No</b>: there is <b>no general duty to disclose</b> (caveat emptor). Subject to exceptions."],[278,"What are the exceptions to the silence rule?","<b>Half-truths</b>. A <b>change of circumstances</b> before the contract. <b>Fiduciary</b> relationships. <b>Insurance</b> contracts (now modified by statute)."],[287,"What is fraudulent misrepresentation?","A statement made <b>knowingly</b>, <b>without belief</b> in its truth, or <b>recklessly</b> careless whether true or false."],[290,"What is the test for liability under s.2(1) Misrepresentation Act 1967?","Liable as if fraudulent, <b>unless</b> the representor proves <b>reasonable grounds to believe</b> and <b>did believe</b> the statement up to the contract."],[291,"Why is s.2(1) usually the best claim for non-fraudulent misrepresentation?","<b>Burden</b> is on the representor. <b>No duty of care</b> needed. <b>Fraud measure</b> of damages: all direct loss, even unforeseeable (Royscot)."],[295,"When can the court award damages in lieu of rescission (s.2(2))?","For <b>non-fraudulent</b> misrepresentation where rescission is available. If <b>equitable</b>, considering the misrepresentation and the losses either way."],[301,"What are the bars to rescission?","<b>Affirmation</b>. <b>Lapse of time</b>. <b>Restitutio in integrum impossible</b>. <b>Third party rights</b>. The court's discretion under <b>s.2(2)</b>."]]},{"id":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","title":"Mistake, duress, undue influence and illegality","q":[[0,"A contract is void for mistake. Can an innocent buyer from the rogue keep the goods?",["No, the original owner recovers them","Yes, if bought in good faith","Yes, if they paid full value"],"A void contract means no title passes, so the rogue had nothing to sell."],[1,"Specific goods have already perished when the contract is made, unknown to the seller. Under SGA s.6?",["The contract is void","The buyer must still pay","The price is halved"],"<b>SGA 1979 s.6</b>: a sale of specific goods that have perished without the seller's knowledge is void."],[2,"A mistake about the quality of the subject matter makes a contract void only when?",["It is essentially different","One party regrets it","The price is high"],"<b>Bell v Lever Brothers</b>: only a fundamental mistake qualifies. It is very rare."],[3,"After Great Peace, can the court rescind for common mistake in equity?",["No, no separate equitable power","Yes, always","Yes, if just"],"<b>Great Peace</b> (2002) disapproved Solle v Butcher. Common mistake operates at common law only, making the contract void."],[4,"A seller sells salvage rights over a wreck that does not exist, implying that it did. Result?",["The seller is liable in damages","The contract is void","Neither party is liable"],"<b>McRae v Commonwealth Disposals</b>: the seller took the risk of the wreck's existence, so there is no common mistake."],[5,"A buyer knows the seller has quoted a price by obvious mistake, and 'snaps it up'. Binding?",["No, the buyer cannot take advantage","Yes, it is an offer","Yes, if the buyer pays"],"<b>Hartog v Colin &amp; Shields</b>: where the other party knows of an obvious error, there is no contract."],[6,"A rogue buys a car face to face, posing as someone else, then sells it to an innocent buyer. Who wins?",["The innocent buyer","The original owner","Whoever is first to sue"],"<b>Lewis v Averay</b>: face-to-face dealing means the contract is voidable, so title passes before rescission."],[7,"A rogue writes a forged order imitating a reputable firm. A seller delivers goods. Contract with the rogue?",["Void for mistake","Voidable","Valid"],"<b>Cundy v Lindsay</b>: the seller intended to deal only with the reputable firm. The contract was void."],[8,"A haulier threatens to breach unless the customer pays more and no alternative exists. Possible remedy?",["Avoid for economic duress","Enforce as agreed","Specific performance"],"<b>Atlas Express v Kafco</b>: illegitimate pressure with no practical alternative makes the contract voidable."],[9,"Is there a presumption of undue influence between husband and wife?",["No, it must be proved","Yes, irrebuttable","Yes, rebuttable"],"<b>Etridge</b>: marriage is not a relationship where influence is presumed. It must be proved."],[10,"A wife guarantees her husband's business loan. How can the bank protect itself against undue influence?",["Insist on independent legal advice","Get her signature in front of the husband","Require nothing"],"<b>Etridge</b>: a bank put on inquiry should insist she receives independent legal advice."],[11,"Which approach to illegality does Patel v Mirza take?",["A range of factors, proportionality","A strict rule against claimants","Always enforce"],"<b>Patel v Mirza</b>: the court weighs the underlying purpose, public interest and proportionality."]],"c":[[309,"What is the effect of an operative mistake?","The contract is <b>void</b>: it never existed and no ownership passes. An innocent buyer from a rogue gets <b>nothing</b>."],[314,"What is the test for common mistake?","A <b>common assumption</b>. <b>Neither party warranted</b> it. Not either party's <b>fault</b>. Performance <b>impossible</b> or <b>essentially different</b>. About the existence or a <b>vital attribute</b> of the subject matter."],[318,"When does a unilateral mistake about the terms prevent a contract?","When the other party <b>knows or ought to know</b> of the mistake (e.g. price per pound instead of per piece)."],[320,"What is the presumption in a face-to-face dealing with a rogue?","The seller intends to deal with <b>the person in front of them</b>. The contract is <b>voidable</b> for fraud, not void."],[327,"What is the test for economic duress?","<b>Pressure</b> leaving <b>no practical choice</b>. The pressure was <b>illegitimate</b>. It was a <b>significant cause</b>, at least a <b>but for</b> cause."],[335,"What must be proved to raise presumed undue influence?","A relationship of <b>trust and confidence</b>. And a transaction that <b>calls for explanation</b>."],[336,"What is the effect of the presumption of undue influence?","The <b>burden shifts</b> to the wrongdoer to show the victim acted freely. Usually by proving <b>independent advice</b>."],[340,"When is a bank put on inquiry?","When a wife, or anyone in a <b>non-commercial relationship</b> with the debtor, offers to <b>stand surety</b> for the debtor's or <b>their company's</b> debts."],[348,"What is the trio of considerations for illegality?","The <b>purpose</b> of the prohibition broken. Any <b>other public policy</b>. Whether denying the claim is <b>proportionate</b>."],[351,"When is a restraint of trade clause valid?","Only if it protects a <b>legitimate interest</b> and is <b>reasonable</b> in area, time and activity. Otherwise <b>void</b>."]]},{"id":"discharge-frustration","title":"Termination: performance, breach and frustration","q":[[0,"A sailor is paid only if he completes the voyage, but dies partway. Can his widow claim?",["No, entire obligation","Yes, a proportion","Yes, on quantum meruit"],"<b>Cutter v Powell</b>: under an <b>entire obligation</b>, no payment is due until full performance."],[1,"A decorator finishes the job apart from minor defects costing £55 to fix. What can he claim?",["Price less cost of fixing","Nothing","Cost of materials only"],"<b>Hoenig v Isaacs</b>: substantial performance allows the contract price minus the cost of making good."],[2,"A builder abandons a half-built house on the owner's land. Can he claim for the work?",["No, the owner had no choice","Yes, quantum meruit","Yes, a proportion of the price"],"<b>Sumpter v Hedges</b>: the owner did not freely accept partial performance, so no quantum meruit."],[3,"A party commits a repudiatory breach and the innocent party carries on without reserving rights. What happens?",["The right to terminate is lost","Nothing changes","The contract ends"],"The innocent party has <b>affirmed</b> the contract, so loses the right to terminate for that breach."],[4,"A seller announces before the due date that it will not deliver. When can the buyer sue?",["Immediately","Only after the due date","After one month"],"<b>Hochster v De La Tour</b>: an anticipatory breach lets the innocent party terminate and sue at once."],[5,"A contract says 'time is of the essence' and delivery is late. What is the effect?",["The buyer can terminate","Only damages","No effect"],"The term is a <b>condition</b>, so any late performance allows termination."],[6,"A music hall hired for concerts burns down before the date. What is the result?",["The contract is frustrated","The hirer pays anyway","The hirer sues for damages"],"<b>Taylor v Caldwell</b>: destruction of the subject matter without fault frustrates the contract."],[7,"Rooms are hired to watch a coronation procession which is then cancelled. Result?",["Frustrated, the purpose has gone","Not frustrated, rooms still exist","Voidable"],"<b>Krell v Henry</b>: the common purpose was removed, so the contract is frustrated."],[8,"A party's own deliberate choice makes performance impossible. Can it rely on frustration?",["No, self-induced","Yes, always","Yes, if reasonable"],"<b>Maritime National Fish v Ocean Trawlers</b>: frustration cannot be self-induced."],[9,"Under s.1(3) of the 1943 Act, what can a party who gave a valuable benefit before the frustrating event claim?",["A just sum for the benefit","Nothing","The full price"],"<b>s.1(3) Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943</b>: the court may award a just sum for any valuable benefit obtained."],[10,"When does frustration end the contract?",["Automatically at the event","When a party elects","When a court declares"],"Frustration <b>automatically</b> discharges the contract from the frustrating event, whether or not the parties know."],[11,"A contract becomes much more expensive to perform. Is it frustrated?",["No, not frustration","Yes, frustrated","Only if the price doubles"],"Increased cost does not frustrate a contract (Davis Contractors v Fareham UDC)."]],"c":[[356,"What are the exceptions to the entire obligations rule?","<b>Substantial performance</b>. <b>Severable</b> obligations. <b>Free acceptance</b> of partial performance. <b>Prevention</b> by the other party. <b>Tender</b> of performance."],[369,"When is a breach repudiatory?","Breach of a <b>condition</b>. A serious breach of an <b>innominate</b> term depriving the innocent party of <b>substantially the whole benefit</b>. <b>Renunciation</b>."],[370,"Does a repudiatory breach end the contract automatically?","<b>No</b>. The innocent party must <b>elect</b> to <b>affirm</b> or <b>terminate</b>."],[372,"What is the effect of accepting a repudiation?","Both are released from <b>future</b> obligations. <b>Accrued rights</b> stay enforceable. The innocent party claims damages including <b>loss of the bargain</b>."],[377,"What can the innocent party do on an anticipatory breach?","<b>Accept</b> and sue <b>immediately</b>, but must <b>mitigate</b>. Or <b>affirm</b> and wait, with no duty to mitigate before acceptance."],[380,"What is the test for frustration?","An event <b>after</b> contracting, <b>not the fault</b> of either party, <b>not provided for</b>. Making performance <b>impossible</b>, <b>illegal</b> or <b>radically different</b>."],[381,"Does increased cost or difficulty frustrate a contract?","<b>No</b>. E.g. labour shortages, a longer route when a canal closes, or market changes."],[385,"What is self-induced frustration?","An event the party <b>caused or chose</b>, e.g. allocating licences or vessels elsewhere. It does <b>not</b> frustrate the contract."],[389,"What does s.1(2) of the 1943 Act say about money paid or payable?","Money <b>paid</b> before the event is <b>recoverable</b>. Money <b>payable</b> before the event <b>ceases to be payable</b>."],[391,"What does s.1(3) of the 1943 Act provide?","A party that obtained a <b>valuable benefit</b> (not money) must pay a <b>just sum</b>, up to the <b>value of the benefit</b>."]]},{"id":"damages","title":"Remedies: damages, remoteness, mitigation and penalties","q":[[0,"What do expectation damages aim to do?",["Put the claimant as if the contract had been performed","Punish the defendant","Return both to the start"],"Damages compensate for loss of the bargain. They are <b>not punitive</b>."],[1,"A pool is built slightly shallower than agreed, remains safe, and rebuilding would cost £21,000. Cost of cure?",["Not awarded, out of proportion","Awarded in full","Awarded in half"],"<b>Ruxley v Forsyth</b>: cost of cure is refused if wholly disproportionate. The claimant got a modest sum for loss of amenity."],[2,"A seller fails to deliver goods and a market exists. How are the buyer's damages normally measured?",["Market price less contract price","Contract price only","Lost profit on resale"],"<b>s.51 SGA 1979</b>: the difference between the contract price and market price at the due delivery date."],[3,"When might a claimant choose reliance damages instead?",["When lost profit is too speculative","When the defendant is rich","When the contract was oral"],"<b>Anglia Television v Reed</b>: wasted expenditure can be recovered where profits cannot be proved."],[4,"For which contracts can damages for distress be claimed?",["A holiday contract","A contract for sale of goods","A guarantee"],"<b>Jarvis v Swans Tours</b>: where an important object of the contract is enjoyment or peace of mind."],[5,"A mill's crankshaft is sent late, and the carrier did not know the mill had no spare. Are the lost profits recoverable?",["No, too remote","Yes, they were foreseeable","Yes, in full"],"<b>Hadley v Baxendale</b>: the carrier lacked knowledge of the special circumstances, so the loss was too remote."],[6,"A boiler is delivered late. A laundry lost ordinary profits and a special lucrative contract. Recoverable?",["Ordinary profits only","Both","Neither"],"<b>Victoria Laundry v Newman</b>: ordinary profits were foreseeable. The exceptional contract was not."],[7,"A buyer could easily have bought replacement goods but did not. Can it recover the avoidable loss?",["No, duty to mitigate","Yes, in full","Yes, a proportion"],"<b>British Westinghouse</b>: the claimant cannot recover loss it could reasonably have avoided."],[8,"A claimant takes reasonable but unsuccessful steps to mitigate, and incurs costs. Can it recover them?",["Yes, if reasonable","No, they failed","Only if the claimant won"],"Reasonable mitigation costs are recoverable even where the steps do not reduce the loss."],[9,"The penalty rule applies to which kind of obligation?",["One triggered by breach","One triggered by any event","Any price term"],"<b>Makdessi</b>: the rule applies only to <b>secondary obligations</b> that arise on breach."],[10,"A clause sets a daily sum for late completion that protects a legitimate interest and is not out of proportion. Enforceable?",["Yes, liquidated damages","No, a penalty","Only if exact"],"<b>Cavendish v Makdessi</b>: detriment must not be out of all proportion to a legitimate interest."],[11,"An actress loses a real chance to be selected at an interview due to breach. Can she recover?",["Yes, for the loss of a chance","No, it is too uncertain","Only if she would have won"],"<b>Chaplin v Hicks</b>: damages are assessed by the value of the lost chance, discounted for probability."]],"c":[[402,"What are the two ways of calculating expectation loss?","<b>Difference in value</b>. <b>Cost of cure</b>."],[403,"When is cost of cure not awarded?","When it is <b>wholly unreasonable</b> or <b>out of all proportion</b> to the benefit of curing. Damages for <b>loss of amenity</b> may be awarded instead (Ruxley pool)."],[410,"What do reliance damages do?","Compensate <b>wasted expenditure</b>, as if the contract had <b>never been made</b>. Used where lost profits are <b>too speculative</b>."],[414,"When can damages for distress or disappointment be recovered?","Where a <b>major or important object</b> is <b>pleasure, relaxation or peace of mind</b> (holidays, weddings). Distress from <b>physical inconvenience</b>. <b>Loss of amenity</b>."],[418,"What are the two limbs of the remoteness rule?","<b>Limb 1</b>: loss arising <b>naturally</b> in the <b>usual course of things</b>. <b>Limb 2</b>: loss in both parties' <b>contemplation</b> at contracting because of <b>special circumstances</b> known to the defendant."],[420,"What degree of likelihood does the contract remoteness test require?","Loss <b>not unlikely</b> or a <b>serious possibility</b>. A <b>higher</b> degree than tort's reasonably foreseeable test."],[421,"Is the type or the extent of loss what must be contemplated?","The <b>type</b>. If the type was contemplated, the full <b>extent</b> is recoverable (Parsons pigs)."],[425,"What is the duty to mitigate?","The claimant cannot recover loss it could have <b>avoided by reasonable steps</b>. It is not a true duty; failure just <b>reduces</b> damages."],[434,"What is the effect of a penalty clause?","It is <b>unenforceable</b>. The innocent party recovers only its <b>actual loss</b>."],[435,"What is the modern penalty test?","A <b>secondary obligation</b> imposing a <b>detriment out of all proportion</b> to the innocent party's <b>legitimate interest</b> in enforcing the primary obligation (Makdessi; ParkingEye)."]]},{"id":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","title":"Specific performance, injunctions, indemnities and guarantees","q":[[0,"Why does a court readily order specific performance of a contract to sell land?",["Land is regarded as unique","Land is expensive","Land is registered"],"Damages are inadequate as each piece of land is <b>unique</b>."],[1,"Can a court order specific performance of an employment contract?",["No, personal service","Yes, always","Yes, for senior staff"],"The court will not force people to work for each other (and TULRCA 1992 <b>s.236</b> prevents compelling employees)."],[2,"A shop closes in breach of a covenant to keep trading. Will specific performance be ordered?",["No, it needs constant supervision","Yes, it is clear","Yes, if the landlord asks"],"<b>Co-op Insurance v Argyll Stores</b>: courts will not order a business to carry on trading."],[3,"Which remedy is available as of right for breach of contract?",["Damages","Specific performance","An injunction"],"Damages are available as of right. Equitable remedies are <b>discretionary</b>."],[4,"Before completion, the seller becomes gravely ill and disabled and relies on nearby family. Effect?",["Hardship may bar specific performance","No effect","Contract void"],"<b>Patel v Ali</b>: severe hardship to the defendant, arising after the contract, can bar specific performance."],[5,"An order not to do something is called what?",["A prohibitory injunction","A mandatory injunction","Specific performance"],"A <b>prohibitory</b> injunction restrains a wrong. A <b>mandatory</b> one orders a positive step."],[6,"A singer under an exclusive contract sings for a rival. Which order is possible?",["Injunction against singing for rivals","An order to sing for the claimant","No remedy at all"],"<b>Warner Brothers v Nelson</b>: a negative covenant can be enforced if it does not effectively force performance."],[7,"What is the first test for an interim injunction under American Cyanamid?",["A serious question to be tried","A strong case won already","Damages are inadequate"],"Then the court considers whether damages are adequate and the <b>balance of convenience</b>."],[8,"A buyer has taken the goods but not paid the price. What can the seller claim?",["An action for the price","Specific performance","An injunction"],"<b>s.49 SGA 1979</b>: where property has passed, the seller may sue for the price."],[9,"Which creates a primary liability to the creditor?",["An indemnity","A guarantee","Neither"],"An <b>indemnity</b> is a primary obligation. A <b>guarantee</b> is secondary, dependent on the debtor's default."],[10,"The creditor varies the main contract in a way that prejudices the guarantor without consent. Effect?",["The guarantor may be discharged","The guarantee is unaffected","The guarantee doubles"],"<b>Holme v Brunskill</b>: a material variation can discharge a guarantor who has not consented."],[11,"A nephew breaks a promise to his uncle to pay the uncle's widow an annuity. The estate's loss is nominal. Remedy?",["Specific performance","Nominal damages only","No remedy"],"<b>Beswick v Beswick</b>: damages were inadequate, so the widow, as administratrix, got specific performance."]],"c":[[444,"What is the basic requirement for specific performance?","<b>Damages must be inadequate</b>, typically because the subject matter is <b>unique</b> or unobtainable."],[445,"When are damages typically inadequate?","<b>Land</b>. <b>Unique goods</b> (SGA s.52: specific or ascertained goods). Goods <b>unobtainable</b> elsewhere. <b>Private company shares</b>. Where damages would be <b>nominal</b>. CRA s.58 repair or replacement."],[447,"What are the bars to specific performance?","<b>Personal service</b>. <b>Constant supervision</b>. Lack of <b>mutuality</b>. <b>Unclean hands</b>. <b>Delay</b>. <b>Severe hardship</b>. <b>Volunteer</b> (no consideration). <b>Uncertainty</b> or impossibility."],[454,"When will a prohibitory injunction be granted to enforce a negative promise?","<b>Usually</b>, where there is an <b>express negative promise</b> (exclusivity, non-compete, confidentiality). Inadequacy of damages need not be proved."],[458,"What is the test for an interim injunction?","<b>Serious question to be tried</b>. Would <b>damages</b> adequately compensate the claimant? Would the claimant's <b>undertaking in damages</b> protect the defendant? <b>Balance of convenience</b>; if even, preserve the <b>status quo</b>."],[465,"What formality does a guarantee need?","<b>In writing</b> or evidenced by a memorandum, <b>signed</b> by the guarantor (Statute of Frauds 1677 <b>s.4</b>)."],[473,"What is an indemnity?","A promise to <b>make good a loss</b>, whether or not anyone else is liable. Liability is <b>primary</b> and <b>independent</b>."],[476,"How do you tell a guarantee from an indemnity?","By the <b>substance</b>, not the label. <b>If he does not pay, I will</b> is a guarantee. <b>I will see you paid</b> or I will make good your loss is an indemnity."]]}]},{"id":"tort","title":"Tort","stop":5,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M-44,-40 L-36,-112\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"13\"/><path d=\"M-44,-40 L-36,-112\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"-30\" cy=\"-118\" rx=\"13\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#FFC300\"/><path d=\"M-38,-124 V-132 M-30,-125 V-133\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"-27\" cy=\"-120\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"-41\" cy=\"-70\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#B5651D\" stroke=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"-38\" cy=\"-92\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#B5651D\" stroke=\"none\"/><ellipse cx=\"-36\" cy=\"-44\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#FFC300\"/><circle cx=\"-44\" cy=\"-46\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#B5651D\" stroke=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"-28\" cy=\"-40\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#B5651D\" stroke=\"none\"/><rect x=\"-82\" y=\"-84\" width=\"12\" height=\"84\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><rect x=\"6\" y=\"-84\" width=\"12\" height=\"84\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><path d=\"M-86,-84 Q-32,-108 22,-84 L22,-74 Q-32,-96 -86,-74 Z\" fill=\"#52B788\"/><path d=\"M-70,0 V-60 M-58,0 V-60 M-6,0 V-60 M6,0\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><ellipse cx=\"58\" cy=\"-26\" rx=\"27\" ry=\"21\" fill=\"#ADB5BD\"/><rect x=\"40\" y=\"-12\" width=\"10\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#ADB5BD\"/><rect x=\"64\" y=\"-12\" width=\"10\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#ADB5BD\"/><path d=\"M24,-36 C14,-28 18,-12 10,-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"10\"/><path d=\"M24,-36 C14,-28 18,-12 10,-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ADB5BD\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"32\" cy=\"-38\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#ADB5BD\"/><ellipse cx=\"42\" cy=\"-36\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#CED4DA\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"-42\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"negligence","title":"Negligence: duty of care and breach","q":[[0,"Who must prove negligence, and to what standard?",["The claimant, on the balance of probabilities","The defendant, on the balance of probabilities","The claimant, beyond reasonable doubt"],"The <b>claimant</b> proves every element of negligence, to the <b>civil standard</b>: more likely than not."],[1,"Which case gave the three-stage test for a duty in a novel situation?",["Caparo v Dickman","Donoghue v Stevenson","Bolam v Friern"],"<b>Caparo</b>: foreseeability, proximity, and whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty."],[2,"Which case produced the 'neighbour principle'?",["Donoghue v Stevenson","Hedley Byrne v Heller","Bolton v Stone"],"<b>Donoghue v Stevenson</b> (1932): the snail in the ginger beer bottle."],[3,"A stranger is drowning and the defendant could easily save them but does not. Is the defendant liable?",["No, there is no general duty to rescue","Yes, because the rescue was easy","Yes, because the harm was foreseeable"],"The general rule is <b>no duty for omissions</b> unless an exception applies, such as assumption of responsibility or control."],[4,"A ten-year-old child's carelessness injures a playmate. What standard of care applies?",["A reasonable child of that age","A reasonable adult","A reasonable teenager"],"Children are judged against a <b>reasonable child of the same age</b> (Mullin v Richards)."],[5,"How is a doctor's treatment judged for breach?",["By a responsible body of medical opinion","By the best specialist's practice","By what the patient expected"],"The <b>Bolam</b> test: no breach if a responsible body of doctors would have acted the same way."],[6,"A practice is backed by a body of doctors but cannot withstand logical analysis. Is the doctor safe?",["No, the court may find breach","Yes, Bolam protects every practice","Yes, if the practice is common"],"<b>Bolitho</b>: the opinion must be <b>logical and defensible</b>, or the court can still find breach."],[7,"What does res ipsa loquitur let the court do?",["Infer negligence from the accident itself","Presume the defendant owed a duty","Make the defendant strictly liable"],"If the thing was under the defendant's control and the accident would not normally happen without negligence, the court may <b>infer breach</b>."],[8,"Which statute says a court may consider whether finding breach would deter a desirable activity?",["Compensation Act 2006 s.1","Occupiers' Liability Act 1957","Limitation Act 1980 s.14"],"<b>Compensation Act 2006 s.1</b>: the court can weigh the deterrent effect on desirable activities."],[9,"Which is an exception to the rule of no duty to prevent a third party's harm?",["The defendant controlled the third party","The defendant owned nearby land","The defendant was an adult"],"A duty can arise where the defendant has <b>control</b> of the third party (Dorset Yacht)."],[10,"Do the police generally owe a duty to individuals to prevent crime or catch criminals?",["No, not generally","Yes, under the Caparo test","Yes, but only for violent crime"],"The police are generally <b>not liable for omissions</b> in investigating or preventing crime (Hill; Michael)."],[11,"A rescuer is hurt saving someone put in danger by the defendant's negligence. Is a duty owed?",["Yes, rescuers are foreseeable claimants","No, rescuers are volunteers","Only if the rescuer is a professional"],"A negligent defendant owes a duty to <b>rescuers</b>, as rescue is foreseeable (Haynes v Harwood)."]],"c":[[0,"What four elements must a claimant prove in negligence?","<b>Duty of care</b>. <b>Breach</b>. <b>Causation</b>. <b>Remoteness</b>. Each on the <b>balance of probabilities</b>."],[3,"How does a court decide whether a duty of care exists?","Step 1: is it an <b>established category</b>? If so, a duty <b>exists</b>. Step 2: only in a <b>novel</b> case, develop the law <b>incrementally</b> by <b>analogy</b>, using the Caparo factors (Robinson)."],[7,"What are the Caparo factors?","<b>Reasonable foreseeability</b> of damage. <b>Proximity</b>. Whether it is <b>fair, just and reasonable</b> to impose a duty."],[11,"Is there a general duty to act or to rescue?","<b>No</b>. There is no duty to act to prevent harm, to rescue, or to stop a <b>third party</b> causing harm."],[12,"What are the exceptions where there is a duty to act?","<b>Assumption of responsibility</b>. <b>Control</b> over the third party. <b>Creating the danger</b>. <b>Control of land</b> or a known danger. A <b>special relationship</b>. <b>Interfering</b> with someone else's rescue."],[25,"What is the test for breach?","Did the defendant fall below the standard of a <b>reasonable person</b> in their position? It is <b>objective</b>, not about their personal best."],[27,"What standard applies to a learner driver?","A <b>reasonably competent qualified driver</b>. <b>No allowance</b> for inexperience, even towards an instructor."],[29,"What is the Bolam test?","A professional is <b>not negligent</b> if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted by a <b>responsible body</b> of professional opinion. Even if others would differ."],[30,"What limit does Bolitho add to Bolam?","The professional opinion must be <b>capable of withstanding logical analysis</b>. Risks and benefits must have been weighed to a <b>defensible conclusion</b>."],[32,"What is the test for advising a patient of risks?","The doctor must take reasonable care to disclose <b>material risks</b> and <b>reasonable alternatives</b> (Montgomery). Bolam does <b>not</b> apply."],[39,"What factors does the court balance in deciding breach?","<b>Likelihood</b> of harm. <b>Seriousness</b> of harm. <b>Cost and practicality</b> of precautions. <b>Social value</b>. <b>Common practice</b>. <b>Knowledge</b> at the time."],[49,"What are the conditions for res ipsa loquitur?","The thing was under the defendant's <b>control</b>. The accident would <b>not ordinarily happen</b> without negligence. <b>No explanation</b> of how it happened."]]},{"id":"causation-remoteness","title":"Causation and remoteness","q":[[0,"Which test decides factual causation?",["The but-for test","The foresight test","The neighbour test"],"Would the damage have happened anyway without the breach? If yes, there is <b>no factual causation</b>."],[1,"Which case set the remoteness test of foreseeable type of damage?",["The Wagon Mound (No 1)","Hughes v Lord Advocate","Smith v Leech Brain"],"<b>Wagon Mound (No 1)</b>: the type of damage must be reasonably foreseeable, not just direct."],[2,"The claimant has an unusually thin skull, so a minor blow causes severe injury. Is the defendant liable for all of it?",["Yes, take the victim as found","No, only for a normal skull's injury","Only if the defendant knew"],"The <b>thin skull rule</b>: the defendant takes the victim as they find them (Smith v Leech Brain)."],[3,"Must the defendant foresee the exact way the damage happens?",["No, only the type of harm","Yes, the exact sequence of events","Yes, the exact extent of harm"],"Only the <b>type</b> of damage must be foreseeable (Hughes v Lord Advocate)."],[4,"In Fairchild, what was enough to prove causation for mesothelioma?",["A material increase in the risk","Proof on the but-for test","Proof the breach doubled the risk"],"<b>Fairchild</b>: where the but-for test cannot be met, a <b>material increase in risk</b> can suffice."],[5,"A claimant's unreasonable act after the injury causes further harm. What is the legal effect?",["It may break the chain of causation","It never affects liability","It always bars the whole claim"],"A wholly unreasonable act by the claimant can be a <b>novus actus interveniens</b> (McKew v Holland)."],[6,"Negligence cost the claimant a 40% chance of a better outcome. What is the personal injury award?",["Nothing","40% of the loss","60% of the loss"],"In personal injury, <b>loss of a chance</b> is not recoverable: the claimant must prove on balance that the breach caused the outcome (Hotson; Gregg v Scott)."],[7,"After a negligent injury the claimant develops an unrelated natural illness. What happens to damages?",["They are reduced","They are unchanged","The claim is barred"],"Later natural illness <b>reduces</b> damages, as it would have affected the claimant anyway (Jobling)."],[8,"Two negligent drivers independently cause one indivisible injury. From whom can the claimant recover?",["Either driver, for the whole","Each driver, for half only","The first driver only"],"Concurrent tortfeasors are <b>jointly and severally liable</b>; contribution is sorted out between them."],[9,"What is the time limit to claim contribution from another tortfeasor?",["2 years from judgment or settlement","3 years from the injury","6 years from the breach"],"Under <b>Limitation Act 1980 s.10</b>, a contribution claim must be brought within <b>2 years</b> of judgment or settlement."],[10,"In Bonnington Castings, what had to be shown about the guilty dust source?",["It made a material contribution","It was the sole cause","It doubled the risk"],"A <b>material contribution</b> (more than minimal) to the cumulative disease is enough."],[11,"Children play on an abandoned boat, prop it up and it falls on one. Is the injury too remote?",["No, the type of harm was foreseeable","Yes, the children's act was unforeseeable","Yes, the exact accident was unusual"],"<b>Jolley v Sutton</b>: injury from children playing on the boat was a foreseeable type, however it happened."]],"c":[[53,"What is the but-for test?","But for the breach, would the claimant have suffered the damage? If it would have happened <b>anyway</b>, the claim fails."],[56,"Can a personal injury claimant recover for loss of a chance below 50%?","<b>No</b>. It is <b>all or nothing</b> on the balance of probabilities."],[58,"What is the material contribution test?","For a disease from a <b>build-up</b> of causes, a defendant whose breach made a <b>more than negligible</b> contribution is liable. It also applies to medical negligence."],[62,"How much can a mesothelioma claimant recover from one defendant?","The <b>whole</b> of the damage: liability is <b>joint and several</b> (Compensation Act 2006 s.3). That defendant claims <b>contributions</b> from others."],[64,"Does a later tort reduce the first wrongdoer's liability?","<b>No</b>. The first wrongdoer still pays for the original disability (Baker v Willoughby).<br>Contrast a later <b>natural illness</b>, which <b>does</b> reduce it (Jobling v Associated Dairies)."],[66,"What is a novus actus interveniens?","A later event so <b>independent and significant</b> that it <b>breaks the chain</b>. The defendant is liable only for damage before it. The more <b>foreseeable</b> the act, the less likely a break."],[67,"When does a third party's act break the chain?","If it is <b>free, deliberate and informed</b>. Or so <b>unreasonable</b> or reckless that it eclipses the original breach."],[68,"Does negligent medical treatment break the chain?","<b>Usually not</b>. Only if <b>grossly</b> negligent or palpably wrong."],[73,"What is the test for remoteness in negligence?","The damage must be of a <b>reasonably foreseeable type</b> (Wagon Mound No 1). Not liability for all direct consequences."],[77,"What is the eggshell skull rule?","<b>Take your victim as you find them</b>. If the <b>type</b> of injury is foreseeable, the defendant is liable for its <b>full extent</b>, even if a pre-existing weakness makes it worse."]]},{"id":"economic-loss","title":"Pure economic loss and negligent misstatement","q":[[0,"What is the general rule on pure economic loss in negligence?",["It is not recoverable","It is recoverable if foreseeable","It is recoverable if the loss is large"],"Pure economic loss is <b>not recoverable</b> unless an exception applies, such as negligent misstatement."],[1,"A contractor cuts a cable, ruining a furnace melt and halting production. Which loss is pure economic?",["Profit on later melts","The ruined metal","The cable repair"],"Loss <b>consequent on physical damage</b> is recoverable; later lost profit is pure economic loss (Spartan Steel)."],[2,"Which case allows recovery for negligent misstatement?",["Hedley Byrne v Heller","Murphy v Brentwood","Caparo v Dickman"],"<b>Hedley Byrne</b>: a duty arises where the defendant assumed responsibility and the claimant reasonably relied."],[3,"A council negligently approves foundations. The owner sues for the cost of repairing the house. Result?",["Not recoverable: pure economic loss","Recoverable as property damage","Recoverable by the first owner only"],"<b>Murphy v Brentwood</b>: the cost of repairing a defective building is <b>pure economic loss</b>."],[4,"An investor relies on audited accounts to buy shares. Do the auditors owe a duty?",["No, not to individual investors","Yes, to any investor","Yes, to any bidder"],"<b>Caparo</b>: auditors owe no duty to investors buying shares in reliance on the accounts."],[5,"What was the purpose of the accounts in Caparo?",["Letting shareholders oversee the company","Helping investors buy shares","Helping lenders decide on loans"],"Statutory accounts exist for <b>shareholders collectively</b>, so reliance for another purpose gave no duty."],[6,"A solicitor's delay means an intended beneficiary loses a legacy. Does he owe her a duty?",["Yes, to the intended beneficiary","No, only to the testator","Only if she paid him"],"<b>White v Jones</b>: the solicitor owed a duty to the disappointed <b>beneficiary</b>."],[7,"A negligent driver blocks a road. A nearby shop loses customers. Can the shop recover its lost profit?",["No, it is pure economic loss","Yes, the loss was foreseeable","Yes, if the shop is on that road"],"The shop suffered no damage to its own person or property, so its lost profit is <b>pure economic loss</b>."],[8,"A valuer for a lender is relied on by the buyer of a modest house. Is a duty owed to the buyer?",["Yes, to the buyer","No, only to the lender","Only if the buyer paid him"],"<b>Smith v Eric Bush</b>: the valuer owed the buyer a duty, as reliance on the valuation was foreseeable."],[9,"Can a client sue a professional in both contract and tort for the same negligence?",["Yes, concurrent liability","No, contract only","No, tort only"],"<b>Henderson v Merrett</b>: concurrent duties can exist in contract and tort."],[10,"Between businesses, a disclaimer of liability for negligent advice is valid only if...",["Reasonable under UCTA","Signed by the claimant","Printed in bold"],"A business disclaimer of negligence liability for financial loss must pass the <b>UCTA 1977 reasonableness</b> test (s.2(2))."]],"c":[[81,"What is the general rule for pure economic loss caused by a negligent act?","<b>No duty</b> of care."],[83,"When a power cable is cut, which losses can a factory recover?","<b>Physical damage</b> to its property. Profit lost <b>because of</b> that damage. <b>Not</b> profit lost only through the interruption."],[85,"Is the cost of repairing a defective product or building that damaged only itself recoverable in negligence?","<b>No</b>: pure economic loss. Claim in <b>contract</b> or under the <b>Defective Premises Act 1972</b> for dwellings."],[87,"What is the main exception allowing recovery of pure economic loss?","<b>Hedley Byrne</b>: an <b>assumption of responsibility</b> in a <b>special relationship</b>, usually through advice, information or services."],[88,"What must a claimant show for a Hedley Byrne special relationship?","<b>Assumption of responsibility</b> (judged objectively). <b>Special skill</b>. <b>Reasonable reliance</b>. The defendant <b>knew or ought to have known</b> of the reliance."],[90,"When is a duty owed for a statement to a wider audience such as accounts?","The maker <b>knew</b> it would go to the claimant (or an <b>identifiable class</b>). For a <b>particular transaction</b>. And the claimant would very likely rely on it <b>without independent inquiry</b> (Caparo)."],[96,"Does a solicitor owe a duty to intended beneficiaries of a will?","<b>Yes</b>, even though they are not clients and did not rely. Otherwise no one could sue (White v Jones)."],[99,"What is the scope of duty principle?","An adviser is liable only for loss within the <b>purpose</b> of the duty. Ask <b>what risk</b> the duty guarded against and whether the loss resulted from it."]]},{"id":"psychiatric-harm","title":"Psychiatric harm","q":[[0,"Which describes a primary victim?",["Within the zone of physical danger","Told of the accident later","A close relative of the injured"],"A <b>primary victim</b> was within the zone of danger or reasonably feared physical injury."],[1,"A driver is in a crash with no physical injury but suffers a psychiatric relapse. Can he claim?",["Yes, as a primary victim","No, physical injury is needed","No, the harm was unforeseeable"],"<b>Page v Smith</b>: a primary victim need only foresee physical injury, not psychiatric harm."],[2,"Which relationship is presumed to be a close tie of love and affection?",["Parent and child","Brother and sister","Close friends"],"<b>Alcock</b>: spouses and parent and child are presumed; other relationships must be proved."],[3,"A woman watches a live TV broadcast of a disaster in which her brother dies. Is this enough for proximity?",["No, a broadcast is not direct perception","Yes, because it was live","Yes, because he was her brother"],"<b>Alcock</b>: the claimant must perceive the event with their <b>own unaided senses</b>."],[4,"A mother reaches the hospital two hours after her child's accident and sees the injuries. Immediate aftermath?",["Yes","No","Only if she saw the crash"],"<b>McLoughlin v O'Brian</b>: seeing the victim's state in hospital shortly after was within the <b>immediate aftermath</b>."],[5,"Which counts as a recognised psychiatric illness?",["PTSD","Grief","Distress"],"Grief, sorrow and distress alone are not enough; there must be a <b>recognised psychiatric illness</b>."],[6,"A police officer helped at Hillsborough but was never in danger. How is he treated?",["As a secondary victim","As a primary victim","As a special rescuer category"],"<b>White v CC South Yorkshire</b>: rescuers not in danger must satisfy the <b>secondary victim</b> rules."],[7,"A secondary victim must show that psychiatric illness was foreseeable in a person of...",["Normal fortitude","Unusual sensitivity","Any character"],"The test uses a person of <b>ordinary fortitude</b>; Alcock also requires a close tie."],[8,"A father watches his son slowly decline over 14 days after negligent treatment. Is there a sudden shock?",["No, it was gradual","Yes, if he was present","Yes, the death was sudden"],"<b>Sion v Hampstead HA</b>: a gradual process lacks the required <b>sudden shock</b>."],[9,"A stranger sees a fatal accident and develops PTSD. Does a secondary victim claim succeed?",["No, there is no close tie","Yes, the shock was foreseeable","Yes, if he was nearby"],"A secondary victim must have a <b>close tie of love and affection</b> with the person injured."],[10,"A claimant exposed to asbestos has no disease but fears getting one. Does anxiety alone give a claim?",["No (Rothwell)","Yes, as a primary victim","Yes, if it was serious"],"<b>Rothwell</b>: pleural plaques and anxiety about future illness are not actionable damage."]],"c":[[104,"What is the threshold for a psychiatric harm claim?","A <b>recognised psychiatric illness</b> on medical evidence (e.g. <b>PTSD</b>, clinical <b>depression</b>, pathological grief). Ordinary <b>grief, fear or distress</b> is not enough."],[106,"Who is a primary victim?","Someone within the <b>zone of physical danger</b>, or who <b>reasonably believed</b> they were in danger."],[107,"What is the test for a primary victim?","Was <b>personal injury</b> (physical <b>or</b> psychiatric) foreseeable? Psychiatric injury itself need not be (Page v Smith)."],[110,"Who is a secondary victim?","Someone <b>not</b> in danger who suffers illness from <b>witnessing</b> harm or risk of harm to <b>someone else</b>."],[111,"What are the Alcock control mechanisms?","<b>Close tie of love and affection</b>. <b>Proximity in time and space</b>. Perception by <b>own unaided senses</b>. Foreseeability for a person of <b>reasonable fortitude</b>."],[112,"For whom is a close tie of love and affection presumed?","<b>Parents and children</b>, <b>spouses</b> and <b>engaged couples</b>. Siblings, grandparents and friends must <b>prove</b> it."],[113,"What does proximity in time and space require?","Presence at the <b>accident</b> or its <b>immediate aftermath</b> (e.g. at hospital within about two hours before clean-up). Identifying a body after <b>eight hours</b> is too late."],[116,"What else must a secondary victim witness, following Paul?","An <b>accident</b>: an <b>external, unexpected event</b> causing injury or risk of injury to the primary victim."],[123,"Do rescuers have a special category for psychiatric harm?","<b>No</b>. They recover only as a <b>primary victim</b> (in physical danger) or under the secondary victim rules. Being the defendant's employee does not help."],[125,"What is the key question in a work stress claim?","Was this kind of harm to <b>this employee</b> <b>reasonably foreseeable</b>? Look for signs: vulnerability, earlier illness, abnormal workload, complaints."]]},{"id":"employers-liability","title":"Employers' primary liability","q":[[0,"Is an employer's primary duty to employees personal or delegable?",["Personal and non-delegable","Delegable to the manager","Delegable to contractors"],"The employer's duty is <b>personal</b>, so appointing a competent person does not discharge it."],[1,"How many heads make up the employer's primary duty (Wilsons and Clyde)?",["Four","Three","Five"],"Competent staff, safe plant and equipment, safe place of work and a safe system of work: <b>four</b>."],[2,"An employer knew of one worker's dangerous pranks and did nothing. Which duty was breached?",["Providing competent staff","Providing safe plant","Providing a safe place"],"<b>Hudson v Ridge Manufacturing</b>: the employer failed to provide <b>competent fellow employees</b>."],[3,"An employee is hurt by a tool defective through its manufacturer's fault. Is the employer liable?",["Yes, under the 1969 Act","No, only the manufacturer","Only if the employer was negligent"],"The <b>Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969</b> makes the employer liable for the manufacturer's fault."],[4,"An employer knows an employee has only one eye. Does that affect the precautions needed?",["Yes, individual vulnerability counts","No, one standard for all","Only if the employee asks"],"<b>Paris v Stepney BC</b>: the duty depends on the known circumstances of the particular employee."],[5,"How are precautions against risk judged?",["Risk weighed against cost and practicality","The employer must remove all risk","By industry custom only"],"The employer need only take <b>reasonable precautions</b>, balancing risk against cost (Latimer v AEC)."],[6,"An employee shows no signs of stress and later has a breakdown. Is the employer liable?",["Generally not, harm not foreseeable","Yes, stress is always foreseeable","Yes, for heavy workloads"],"<b>Hatton v Sutherland</b>: employers may assume employees cope unless there are signs of impending harm."],[7,"What is the minimum compulsory insurance cover for employers' liability?",["£5 million","£1 million","£10 million"],"The <b>Employers' Liability (Compulsory Insurance) Act 1969</b> requires cover of at least <b>£5 million</b>."],[8,"What is the usual limitation period for an employee's personal injury claim?",["3 years from injury or knowledge","6 years from the injury","1 year from the injury"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.11</b>: 3 years from the injury or, if later, the <b>date of knowledge</b>."],[9,"An employee knew of a risk at work. Will volenti usually succeed against an employer?",["No, rarely","Yes, if the employee knew","Yes, if experienced"],"<b>Smith v Baker</b>: knowledge of a danger is not consent to run it; volenti rarely defeats employees."],[10,"An employee works at a client's premises. Does the employer's duty continue?",["Yes, reasonable care still applies","No, the client is responsible","Only for the first visit"],"<b>Wilson v Tyneside Window Cleaning</b>: the duty continues, though the steps needed depend on the circumstances."],[11,"Employers' primary liability arises from...",["The employer's own breach of duty","An employee's tort","The employer's criminal liability"],"Primary liability is for the employer's <b>own failure</b>; vicarious liability is for another's tort."]],"c":[[131,"What duty does an employer owe each employee?","A <b>personal duty</b> to take <b>reasonable care</b> for their safety at work."],[132,"What does non-delegable mean for an employer's duty?","The employer can delegate the <b>task</b> but <b>not the responsibility</b>. If the delegate fails, the employer is in breach, even if it chose carefully."],[134,"Is the duty owed to employees collectively or individually?","To <b>each employee individually</b>. Known vulnerabilities must be taken into account."],[135,"What are the four aspects of the employer's duty?","<b>Competent fellow employees</b>. <b>Adequate plant and equipment</b>. A <b>safe place of work</b>. A <b>safe system of work</b>."],[137,"What does the Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969 provide?","If an employee is injured by a <b>defect in equipment</b> provided by the employer, due to a <b>third party's fault</b>, the employer is <b>deemed negligent</b>."],[143,"What does a safe system of work include?","Organisation and order of tasks; <b>training</b>; <b>supervision</b>; <b>warnings</b>. Protective equipment; enough staff. It must be <b>enforced</b>."],[149,"Is breach of health and safety regulations actionable in a civil claim?","<b>No</b>, for breaches on or after <b>1 October 2013</b> (ERRA 2013 s.69), save narrow exceptions. The employee must prove <b>negligence</b>."],[151,"What is the usual defence to an employee's claim?","<b>Contributory negligence</b>. Courts are slower to blame <b>momentary inattention</b> in repetitive work."]]},{"id":"vicarious-liability","title":"Vicarious liability","q":[[0,"Does vicarious liability require fault on the employer's part?",["No, it is strict","Yes, negligent supervision","Yes, negligent selection"],"The employer is liable for the employee's tort <b>without personal fault</b>."],[1,"Can vicarious liability apply to someone who is not formally an employee?",["Yes, if akin to employment","No, employees only","Only if paid a salary"],"<b>Cox; Barclays</b>: a relationship <b>akin to employment</b> can suffice."],[2,"Is an employer generally liable for the torts of an independent contractor?",["No, unless a non-delegable duty","Yes, always","Yes, if hired by contract"],"Independent contractors run their <b>own business</b>; liability needs a non-delegable duty or personal fault."],[3,"After serving a customer, an attendant follows him out and assaults him. Close connection?",["Yes, employer liable (Mohamud)","No, it was personal","No, it was a crime"],"<b>Mohamud v Morrisons</b>: the attack was a continuous episode linked to the job he was given."],[4,"An employee leaks colleagues' data to harm his employer out of a grudge. Is the employer liable?",["No (Morrisons)","Yes, he had access through work","Yes, data law applies"],"<b>Morrisons v Various Claimants</b> (2020): acting for <b>personal reasons</b> broke the close connection."],[5,"A boarding-house warden sexually abuses pupils. Is the school vicariously liable?",["Yes, close connection to his job","No, it was a crime","Only if the school knew"],"<b>Lister v Hesley Hall</b>: abuse was closely connected with the care entrusted to him."],[6,"A bus driver races a rival bus against orders and crashes. Is the employer liable?",["Yes, unauthorised way of doing the job","No, he disobeyed orders","Only if told to hurry"],"<b>Limpus</b>: a wrongful manner of doing an authorised task is still in the course of employment."],[7,"An employee drives home after her shift and crashes. Is the employer liable?",["Generally not, commuting","Yes, as she was an employee","Yes, if driving the employer's car"],"Travelling to and from work is <b>normally outside</b> the course of employment."],[8,"A delivery driver goes miles off route to visit a friend and crashes. Is the employer liable?",["Probably not, a frolic","Yes, he was on duty","Yes, always"],"A substantial deviation for his own purposes is a <b>frolic</b> (Joel v Morison)."],[9,"A claimant is hurt by an employee in the course of employment. Who can the claimant sue?",["Employer, employee, or both","Employer only","Employee only"],"They are <b>joint tortfeasors</b>, so the claimant can sue either or both."],[10,"Can an employer who pays damages recover the sum from the employee?",["In theory, yes (Lister v Romford)","No, never","Only if a crime"],"The employee breaches an <b>implied term</b>, but insurers rarely pursue it."],[11,"A pupil is hurt in a swimming lesson run by an independent contractor. Is the school liable?",["Yes, non-delegable duty","No, the contractor only","Only if the school was careless"],"<b>Woodland</b>: the school assumed a <b>non-delegable</b> duty that it cannot hand to a contractor."]],"c":[[155,"What is vicarious liability?","Liability of one person (usually an <b>employer</b>) for a tort committed by another (usually an <b>employee</b>). A form of <b>strict liability</b>."],[158,"What is the two-stage test for vicarious liability?","<b>Relationship</b>: employment or <b>akin to employment</b>. <b>Close connection</b>: the tort so closely connected to that relationship that it is <b>fair and just</b> to hold the defendant liable."],[159,"How do courts decide whether someone is an employee?","The whole relationship: <b>control</b>, <b>integration</b> and <b>economic reality</b>. The <b>label</b> used is not conclusive."],[161,"When is a relationship akin to employment?","When the person's work is <b>integral</b> to the defendant's activities, for its benefit, and the defendant <b>created the risk</b> by assigning them those activities. A full wage or profit is not needed."],[164,"Is a defendant vicariously liable for an independent contractor?","<b>Generally no</b>. Not where the tortfeasor carries on their <b>own independent business</b> (e.g. a self-employed doctor with other clients)."],[165,"What is the close connection test?","Was the wrong so closely connected with what the employee was <b>authorised</b> to do that it can fairly be regarded as done <b>in the course of employment</b>?"],[172,"Is a prohibited act within the course of employment?","<b>Yes</b> if done <b>for the employer's purposes</b> (milkman's boy helper). <b>No</b> if done for the <b>employee's own purposes</b> (giving a hitchhiker a lift)."],[176,"What is a non-delegable duty in this context?","A <b>personal</b> duty to ensure care is taken, making the defendant liable for an <b>independent contractor's</b> negligence. It is <b>not</b> vicarious liability."]]},{"id":"defences","title":"Defences: consent, contributory negligence, illegality","q":[[0,"What is the effect of a successful volenti defence?",["The claim fails completely","Damages are reduced","Damages are halved"],"<b>Volenti</b> is a complete defence."],[1,"What must the claimant know for volenti to apply?",["The nature and extent of the risk","That a risk may exist","That the defendant is careless"],"The claimant must have <b>full knowledge</b> of the risk and freely accept it."],[2,"A passenger accepts a lift from a driver he knows is drunk. Can volenti defeat his claim?",["No, barred by RTA 1988 s.149","Yes, he knew the risk","Yes, if he was sober"],"<b>RTA 1988 s.149</b> bars volenti for passengers; contributory negligence may still apply."],[3,"What is the effect of contributory negligence?",["Damages are reduced","The claim fails","The claim is unaffected"],"The <b>1945 Act</b> reduces damages to what is just and equitable."],[4,"No seatbelt, but injuries would have been less severe if worn. What is the usual reduction?",["15%","25%","Nil"],"<b>Froom v Butcher</b>: 15% where the belt would have lessened the injuries."],[5,"No seatbelt, and the injury would have been entirely prevented. What is the usual reduction?",["25%","15%","50%"],"<b>Froom v Butcher</b>: 25% where wearing a seatbelt would have prevented the injury."],[6,"A motorcyclist is not wearing a helmet. What is the usual reduction?",["About 15%","About 25%","About 50%"],"<b>O'Connell v Jackson</b>: roughly 15% for failing to wear a helmet."],[7,"Can contributory negligence reach 100%?",["No, it cannot","Yes, if wholly at fault","Yes, if reckless"],"The Act reduces damages for <b>partial</b> fault, so 100% is not available."],[8,"How is a child's contributory negligence judged?",["By a reasonable child of that age","By a reasonable adult","Children can never be at fault"],"Children are judged by the standard of a <b>reasonable child of the same age</b>."],[9,"A rescuer is hurt saving someone put in danger by the defendant. Does volenti apply?",["Rarely, as rescue is not voluntary","Yes, he chose to act","Yes, if trained"],"<b>Haynes v Harwood</b>: a rescuer is not usually treated as consenting to the risk."],[10,"Which approach now governs the illegality defence?",["A range of factors (Patel v Mirza)","An absolute bar for any crime","Only if convicted"],"<b>Patel v Mirza</b>: the court weighs policy factors and proportionality, not a rigid rule."],[11,"How do courts apportion contributory negligence?",["By blameworthiness and causative potency","By who was injured more","Equally between the parties"],"<b>Jackson v Murray</b>: courts look at each party's fault and how much each caused the damage."]],"c":[[180,"What are the three negligence defences and their effect?","<b>Volenti</b>: complete. <b>Contributory negligence</b>: partial, damages <b>reduced</b>. <b>Illegality</b>: complete."],[182,"What must be shown for volenti?","<b>Full knowledge</b> of the nature and extent of the risk. <b>Free and voluntary agreement</b> to accept it, including the <b>legal</b> risk of the defendant's negligence."],[183,"Is knowing about a danger the same as consenting to it?","<b>No</b>. Carelessly running a risk usually means <b>contributory negligence</b> instead."],[185,"Can volenti be used against a car passenger who knew the driver was drunk?","<b>No</b>. <b>RTA 1988 s.149</b> bars it for vehicles needing compulsory insurance. It does not apply to aircraft."],[186,"Does s.149 RTA 1988 stop contributory negligence or illegality?","<b>No</b>."],[194,"What does the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 s.1 provide?","Where damage is partly the claimant's <b>fault</b>, the claim is <b>not defeated</b>. Damages are <b>reduced</b> as is <b>just and equitable</b> for the claimant's share of responsibility."],[195,"What must the defendant prove for contributory negligence?","The claimant <b>failed to take reasonable care</b> for their own safety. This <b>contributed to the damage</b>, not necessarily the accident."],[198,"What are the seatbelt reductions?","<b>25%</b> if the belt would have <b>prevented</b> the injury. <b>15%</b> if injuries <b>less severe</b>. <b>Nil</b> if no difference."],[202,"Can contributory negligence be 100%?","<b>No</b>. If the claimant was entirely to blame, the defendant's breach did not <b>cause</b> the damage."],[204,"What is the modern approach to illegality?","The <b>Patel v Mirza</b> trio: the <b>purpose</b> of the prohibition; <b>other public policies</b>; whether denial is <b>proportionate</b>. It applies in tort."]]},{"id":"remedies","title":"Remedies for personal injury and death; limitation","q":[[0,"In personal injury, which damages cover financial losses before trial?",["Special damages","General damages","Aggravated damages"],"<b>Special damages</b> are losses up to trial; <b>general</b> covers future loss and pain."],[1,"What uplift applies to general damages for pain and suffering since 2013?",["10%","5%","25%"],"<b>Simmons v Castle</b> added <b>10%</b> to general damages in most cases."],[2,"Why is the multiplier less than the number of years of loss?",["Early lump sum, invested, and life is uncertain","Courts prefer low awards","Claimants always get better"],"The claimant gets the money <b>early and can invest it</b>, and the future is uncertain."],[3,"A claimant's injury shortens his life. Can he claim earnings for the lost years?",["Yes, less his living expenses","No, only to death","Yes, in full"],"<b>Pickett v British Rail</b>: a living claimant can recover <b>lost years</b> earnings, less personal spending."],[4,"Which claim covers a deceased's pain before death?",["The estate under the 1934 Act","Dependants under the 1976 Act","Neither"],"The <b>Law Reform (Misc Provisions) Act 1934</b> estate claim covers pain and loss up to death."],[5,"A cohabitee has lived with the deceased for 2 years. Can she claim as a dependant?",["Yes","No","Only after 5 years"],"<b>Fatal Accidents Act 1976</b>: a cohabitant of at least <b>2 years</b> can claim loss of dependency."],[6,"Can an adult child claim bereavement damages for a parent's death?",["No","Yes, a fixed sum","Yes, if a dependant"],"Bereavement damages are limited to a spouse, civil partner, cohabitant and a minor's parents."],[7,"What is the standard limitation period for personal injury?",["3 years","6 years","1 year"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.11</b>: 3 years from the injury or date of knowledge."],[8,"What is the limitation period for negligence causing property damage?",["6 years","3 years","12 years"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.2</b>: 6 years from the date of damage."],[9,"A child is injured at age 10. When does the personal injury limitation period start?",["On the 18th birthday","On the injury","On the 21st birthday"],"Time does not run while the claimant is a <b>minor</b>; the 3 years start at 18 (s.28)."],[10,"Which section lets the court disapply the personal injury time limit?",["Section 33","Section 2","Section 11A"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.33</b> gives a discretion where it is equitable to allow the claim."],[11,"The date of knowledge requires the claimant to know that the injury is...",["Significant and attributable to the act","Permanent and disabling","Confirmed by a doctor"],"The claimant must know the injury is <b>significant</b> and attributable to the defendant's act or omission (s.14)."],[12,"What is the limitation period for latent damage negligence, other than injury?",["6 years, or 3 from knowledge; 15-year longstop","6 years from the breach, no longstop","3 years from the breach only"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.14A</b>: 6 years from damage or 3 years from knowledge if later, with a <b>15-year</b> longstop (s.14B)."],[13,"Can a relative recover tort damages for grief at a negligently caused death?",["No, only statutory bereavement damages","Yes, damages for grief","Yes, if close family"],"Grief alone is not actionable; the only award is the fixed statutory <b>bereavement damages</b> (Fatal Accidents Act 1976 s.1A)."]],"c":[[209,"What is the aim of compensatory damages in tort?","To put the claimant in the position as if the tort <b>had not been committed</b> (restitutio in integrum)."],[218,"How are future losses calculated?","<b>Multiplicand</b> (net annual loss) × <b>multiplier</b> from the <b>Ogden Tables</b> using the discount rate."],[220,"What is the personal injury discount rate?","Set by the <b>Lord Chancellor</b>. <b>+0.5%</b> since 11 January 2025. A lower rate means a higher award."],[226,"How are state benefits treated?","<b>Deducted</b> via the <b>CRU</b> from the matching heads only. <b>Never from PSLA</b>."],[231,"What can a deceased claimant's estate recover under the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1934?","Losses <b>between injury and death</b>: PSLA (if meaningful suffering), earnings to death, medical costs, <b>funeral</b> expenses.<br><b>Not</b> lost years (income after death), bereavement or exemplary damages."],[232,"Who are dependants under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976?","<b>Spouse or civil partner</b> (or former). <b>Cohabitant</b> of <b>two years</b>. <b>Parents</b>; <b>children</b>. <b>Siblings</b>, uncles, aunts and their issue."],[235,"How much are bereavement damages and who gets them?","<b>£15,120</b>. <b>Spouse or civil partner</b>. <b>Cohabitant</b> of two years. <b>Parents</b> of an <b>unmarried minor</b>. Shared equally if more than one."],[240,"What is the limitation period for tort causing property damage or economic loss?","<b>6 years</b> from the date of <b>damage</b>."],[241,"What is the limitation period for latent damage (not personal injury)?","6 years, <b>or</b> 3 years from <b>date of knowledge</b> if later. <b>15-year longstop</b> from the negligent act."],[242,"What is the limitation period for personal injury?","<b>3 years</b> from injury or <b>date of knowledge</b> if later. The court can <b>disapply</b> it (s.33)."],[243,"What is the date of knowledge?","When the claimant first knew the injury was <b>significant</b>, <b>attributable</b> to the act, and the <b>identity</b> of the defendant. Knowing it is negligence in law is <b>irrelevant</b>."]]},{"id":"occupiers-liability","title":"Occupiers' liability (1957 and 1984 Acts)","q":[[0,"Which Act covers lawful visitors to premises?",["Occupiers' Liability Act 1957","Occupiers' Liability Act 1984","Defective Premises Act 1972"],"The <b>1957 Act</b> covers visitors; the <b>1984 Act</b> covers trespassers and others."],[1,"Who is an occupier?",["Whoever has control of the premises","Only the freehold owner","Only a tenant"],"Occupation depends on <b>control</b> (Wheat v Lacon), so there can be more than one occupier."],[2,"What is the common duty of care to visitors?",["Reasonable care so they are reasonably safe","To make premises completely safe","To avoid deliberate harm"],"<b>1957 Act s.2(2)</b>: take reasonable care to see the visitor is reasonably safe for the permitted purpose."],[3,"A visitor wanders into a restricted area beyond his permission. Which Act covers him?",["The 1984 Act, as a trespasser","The 1957 Act","Neither"],"A visitor exceeding permission is a <b>trespasser</b> for that area."],[4,"When does a warning discharge the duty to a visitor?",["Only if it makes the visitor reasonably safe","Whenever it is displayed","Never"],"<b>s.2(4)(a)</b>: a warning is enough only if it allows the visitor to be reasonably safe."],[5,"An occupier hires a contractor whose work injures a visitor. When is the occupier not liable?",["Reasonable care in choosing and checking","If the contractor was insured","If a warning was posted"],"<b>s.2(4)(b)</b>: the occupier must reasonably entrust the work to a competent contractor and check it."],[6,"Under the 1984 Act, when is a duty owed to a trespasser?",["If he knows of a danger and that people may come","Only if the trespasser is a child","Whenever any danger exists"],"The occupier must know or have reasonable grounds to believe in a danger and a trespasser's presence."],[7,"Does the 1984 Act cover damage to a trespasser's property?",["No, personal injury only","Yes, all losses","Yes, if over £275"],"<b>1984 Act s.1(8)</b>: no duty for loss of or damage to property."],[8,"A swimmer dives into shallow water and is injured. Why was the occupier not liable in Tomlinson?",["The danger came from his own choice","He had signed a waiver","The water was private"],"The risk arose from what the claimant chose to do, not the <b>state of the premises</b>."],[9,"A business notice tries to exclude liability for a visitor's death by negligence. Effect?",["Void under UCTA 1977 s.2(1)","Valid if prominent","Valid if reasonable"],"<b>UCTA 1977 s.2(1)</b>: business liability for death or personal injury through negligence cannot be excluded."],[10,"How should an occupier treat child visitors?",["Expect them to be less careful","Treat them as adults","Expect parents to supervise"],"<b>s.2(3)(a)</b>: children are less careful, so premises must be reasonably safe for them."],[11,"A visitor knew of the danger and accepted the risk. What defence applies under the 1957 Act?",["Volenti non fit injuria","Contributory negligence only","Illegality"],"<b>s.2(5)</b>: the occupier owes no duty for risks willingly accepted by the visitor."]],"c":[[249,"Which statutes govern occupiers' liability?","<b>OLA 1957</b>: duties to <b>visitors</b>. <b>OLA 1984</b>: duties to <b>non-visitors</b>, mainly trespassers."],[251,"Who is an occupier?","A person with sufficient <b>control</b> over the premises. Ownership or physical presence is <b>not</b> required."],[255,"Who is a visitor under the 1957 Act?","People with <b>express</b> or <b>implied</b> permission. People entering under a <b>contract</b>. People entering under a <b>right conferred by law</b> (e.g. police with a warrant)."],[256,"Who is a non-visitor under the 1984 Act?","<b>Trespassers</b>. Users of <b>private rights of way</b>. People exercising the <b>right to roam</b>."],[259,"What is the common duty of care (s.2(2) OLA 1957)?","To take reasonable care to see that the <b>visitor</b> will be <b>reasonably safe</b> using the premises for the permitted <b>purposes</b>. Not to make the premises completely safe."],[262,"How must occupiers treat child visitors?","Be prepared for children to be <b>less careful</b> than adults (s.2(3)(a)). Guard against <b>allurements</b> and anticipate children's ingenuity."],[264,"What can an occupier expect of skilled visitors?","That they will <b>appreciate and guard against</b> special risks <b>ordinarily incident to their calling</b> (s.2(3)(b))."],[266,"When does a warning discharge the 1957 duty?","Only if, in all the circumstances, it was <b>enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe</b> (s.2(4)(a)). It must be clear and specific; sometimes a barrier is needed."],[268,"When is an occupier not liable for an independent contractor's faulty work?","It <b>acted reasonably</b> in entrusting the work. Took reasonable steps to check the contractor was <b>competent</b>. Took reasonable steps to check the work was <b>properly done</b>, if the nature of the work allowed (s.2(4)(b))."],[275,"When does an occupier owe a duty to a trespasser (s.1(3) OLA 1984)?","It is <b>aware of the danger</b> or has reasonable grounds to believe it exists. It knows or has reasonable grounds to believe the trespasser may be <b>in the vicinity</b>. The risk is one it may <b>reasonably be expected to offer some protection</b> against."],[280,"Does the 1984 Act cover property damage?","<b>No</b>: <b>personal injury only</b> (s.1(8))."]]},{"id":"product-liability","title":"Product liability","q":[[0,"Under the CPA 1987, must the claimant prove the producer was negligent?",["No, it is strict liability","Yes, negligence is needed","Only for personal injury"],"The <b>Consumer Protection Act 1987</b> imposes strict liability for defective products."],[1,"When is a retailer liable under the CPA?",["If it fails to identify the producer","Always, as seller","Never"],"<b>s.2(3)</b>: a supplier who cannot identify the producer or supplier on request is treated as the producer."],[2,"Under the CPA, a product is defective if its safety is...",["Lower than persons generally expect","Lower than the buyer expects","Lower than the price suggests"],"<b>s.3</b>: the test is what persons <b>generally</b> are entitled to expect."],[3,"A defective heater destroys a sofa worth £900 and the heater. What does the CPA cover?",["The sofa only","The heater and sofa","Neither"],"Under the CPA, damage to the product itself is excluded; other private property over £275 is covered."],[4,"A defect destroys a sofa worth £200. Can it be recovered under the CPA?",["No, below the £275 threshold","Yes, in full","Yes, minus £275"],"Property damage claims under the CPA must <b>exceed £275</b>."],[5,"Can a business owner recover for damaged commercial property under the CPA?",["No, private use only","Yes, if foreseeable","Yes, if over £275"],"CPA property damage must be to property intended for <b>private use</b>."],[6,"Which CPA defence applies if the defect could not be found given scientific knowledge?",["Development risks defence","Volenti","Contributory negligence"],"<b>s.4(1)(e)</b>: the state of scientific knowledge at the time did not allow the defect to be discovered."],[7,"What is the CPA absolute longstop for claims?",["10 years from supply by the producer","6 years from damage","15 years from manufacture"],"The right is extinguished <b>10 years</b> after the product was put into circulation."],[8,"A gift recipient is hurt by a faulty toaster. Can she sue the retailer in contract?",["No, not a party to the contract","Yes, as the user","Yes, if she is a consumer"],"<b>Privity</b>: only the buyer can sue in contract; she can use negligence or the CPA."],[9,"Which case founded the manufacturer's duty to the ultimate consumer?",["Donoghue v Stevenson","Hedley Byrne v Heller","Caparo v Dickman"],"<b>Donoghue v Stevenson</b>: a manufacturer owes a duty to the consumer where there is no intermediate examination."],[10,"Can a producer exclude CPA liability by notice?",["No","Yes, if prominent","Yes, for business customers"],"<b>CPA s.7</b>: liability cannot be limited or excluded by notice or contract term."],[11,"How does contributory negligence affect a CPA claim?",["It can reduce damages","It is irrelevant","It bars the claim"],"<b>CPA s.6(4)</b> applies the 1945 Act, so damages can be reduced."]],"c":[[283,"What three claims may someone injured by a defective product have?","<b>Contract</b> against the seller (strict, but only for a party). <b>Negligence</b> against the manufacturer (fault). <b>Strict liability</b> under <b>CPA 1987 Part 1</b> (defect, not fault)."],[284,"What duty does a manufacturer owe in negligence?","Reasonable care to the <b>ultimate consumer</b>, where the product reaches them in the form it left, with <b>no reasonable possibility of intermediate examination</b>."],[291,"What damage is recoverable in a product negligence claim?","<b>Personal injury</b> and damage to <b>other property</b>. <b>Not</b> the product itself: that is <b>pure economic loss</b>."],[294,"Who can be liable under the CPA?","The <b>producer</b> (manufacturer, abstractor or processor). An <b>own-brander</b>. The <b>importer</b> into the UK. A <b>supplier</b>, only in limited circumstances."],[299,"When is a product defective (s.3)?","When its <b>safety is not such as persons generally are entitled to expect</b>."],[306,"What must the CPA claimant prove?","That the product was <b>defective</b> and the defect <b>caused</b> the damage. Not how the defect arose."],[307,"What damage does the CPA cover?","<b>Death</b> or <b>personal injury</b>. Damage to <b>private</b> property, not the product itself, if the claim exceeds <b>£275</b>."],[311,"What are the CPA s.4 defences?","<b>Compliance</b> with a legal requirement. <b>No supply</b>. <b>Non-commercial</b> supply. <b>Defect not present</b> at supply. <b>Development risks</b>. <b>Component</b> defect due to the finished product's design."],[312,"What is the development risks defence?","The state of <b>scientific and technical knowledge</b> at the time did not allow the defect to be <b>discovered</b>. It fails once the risk is <b>known</b> anywhere, even if no test exists."],[314,"What is the CPA limitation period?","<b>3 years</b> from damage or <b>date of knowledge</b>. Absolute <b>10-year longstop</b> from when the producer put the product into <b>circulation</b>; s.33 cannot extend it."]]},{"id":"nuisance-rylands","title":"Nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher","q":[[0,"What is private nuisance?",["Unreasonable interference with use of land","Any harm caused by a neighbour","A breach of planning law"],"It is an <b>unreasonable interference</b> with enjoyment of land, judged by balancing the parties' interests."],[1,"Who can generally sue in private nuisance?",["Someone with a right in the land","Any user nearby","Any resident, including guests"],"<b>Hunter v Canary Wharf</b>: the claimant needs an interest in land, such as owner or tenant."],[2,"Which factor explains why noise is tolerated in an industrial area?",["Locality","Malice","Duration"],"<b>Sturges v Bridgman</b>: the character of the neighbourhood affects what is reasonable."],[3,"The claimant's unusually sensitive stock is damaged by ordinary heat. Is it nuisance?",["No, abnormal sensitivity","Yes, harm occurred","Yes, if foreseeable"],"<b>Robinson v Kilvert</b>: there is no nuisance if only an abnormally sensitive use is affected."],[4,"A neighbour bangs on the wall out of spite. Does malice matter?",["Yes, it can make it a nuisance","No, only effects matter","Only for public nuisance"],"<b>Christie v Davey</b>: malicious use can be unreasonable even if otherwise reasonable."],[5,"Claimant moves next to a noisy factory. Is 'coming to the nuisance' a defence?",["No","Yes","Only after 20 years"],"<b>Sturges v Bridgman</b>: the claimant's later arrival is not a defence."],[6,"How long must a nuisance continue to acquire a right by prescription?",["20 years","6 years","12 years"],"<b>Prescription</b> requires 20 years' continuous actionable nuisance."],[7,"Does planning permission automatically give a defence to nuisance?",["No, usually not","Yes, always","Yes, for businesses"],"Planning permission may change the locality but does not itself authorise a <b>nuisance</b> (Coventry v Lawrence)."],[8,"Does Rylands v Fletcher allow recovery for personal injury?",["No (Transco)","Yes, strict liability","Yes, if serious"],"<b>Transco</b>: the rule protects rights in land and does not cover personal injury."],[9,"Small solvent spills seep into groundwater over years. Can the claimant use Rylands?",["No, the damage was unforeseeable","Yes, strict liability","Yes, if the spills were deliberate"],"<b>Cambridge Water</b>: the damage must be of a type foreseeable if the thing escaped."],[10,"What must a private individual show to sue in public nuisance?",["Special damage beyond the public's","Ownership of land","A criminal conviction"],"A claimant must suffer <b>special damage</b> over and above that of the affected class."],[11,"An occupier knows a trespasser's drain is blocked and does nothing. Is the occupier liable?",["Yes, for continuing the nuisance","No, he did not create it","No, a third party caused it"],"<b>Sedleigh-Denfield</b>: an occupier who knows of and fails to abate a hazard <b>continues</b> the nuisance."]],"c":[[315,"What is private nuisance?","An <b>unreasonable interference</b> with a neighbour's <b>use or enjoyment of their land</b>. It protects <b>rights in land</b>."],[321,"Who can sue in private nuisance?","Only a person with a <b>proprietary interest</b> (owner or tenant) or <b>exclusive possession</b>. Not family members, guests or lodgers."],[322,"Who can be sued in private nuisance?","The <b>creator</b>, whether or not still in occupation. The <b>occupier</b>, for nuisances it creates, or that trespassers or nature create if it <b>adopts or continues</b> them."],[326,"What is the core test for private nuisance?","Is the defendant's use <b>unreasonable</b> in its effect on the claimant, judged by the <b>ordinary person</b> and <b>give and take</b>? Reasonable care is no defence."],[327,"How does locality affect private nuisance?","What is acceptable depends on the <b>character of the neighbourhood</b>. Locality is relevant to <b>amenity</b> nuisance but <b>not</b> to <b>physical damage</b>."],[330,"What is the rule on abnormal sensitivity?","No claim if only an <b>unusually sensitive</b> use is affected. The modern test is whether the damage was <b>reasonably foreseeable</b>. Once a nuisance to ordinary use exists, sensitive uses are protected too."],[335,"What is the prescription defence?","A right to commit a nuisance after <b>20 years'</b> continuous actionable nuisance against the claimant. Time runs only from when it became a nuisance <b>to the claimant</b>."],[337,"Which arguments are not defences to nuisance?","<b>Coming to the nuisance</b>. <b>Public benefit</b>. <b>Planning permission</b> as such. Many contributing together. Taking <b>all reasonable care</b>."],[340,"What damages are recoverable in private nuisance?","<b>Physical damage</b>, <b>diminution in value</b>, <b>loss of amenity</b> and consequential loss. <b>Not personal injury</b>."],[344,"Who can sue in public nuisance?","The <b>Attorney General</b> or a local authority. An <b>individual</b> who suffered <b>special damage</b> over and above the public."],[347,"What must a claimant prove for liability for an escape of a dangerous thing?","The defendant <b>brought and kept</b> something on its land. <b>Exceptionally high risk</b> of mischief if it escapes. <b>Non-natural use</b>. <b>Escape</b>. Damage of a <b>foreseeable type</b>."],[349,"What is non-natural use?","An <b>extraordinary and unusual</b> use by current standards. Bulk industrial chemicals: yes. Water supply to flats or stored tyres: no. Community benefit does not make a use natural."]]}]},{"id":"legalsys","title":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law","stop":6,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M30,-70 C62,-80 70,-108 64,-130\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"14\"/><path d=\"M30,-70 C62,-80 70,-108 64,-130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFFDF5\" stroke-width=\"8\"/><path d=\"M48,-82 l8,-6 M58,-96 l8,-2 M62,-112 l8,0\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M56,-136 Q66,-146 82,-138 Q84,-130 72,-128 Z\" fill=\"#FFFDF5\"/><circle cx=\"66\" cy=\"-137\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M74,-131 l2,3 M78,-132 l2,3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"-78\" y=\"-14\" width=\"132\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#E9DCC3\"/><rect x=\"-70\" y=\"-26\" width=\"116\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#F6EFE2\"/><rect x=\"-62\" y=\"-80\" width=\"100\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#B59F84\"/><rect x=\"-56\" y=\"-80\" width=\"12\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#FFFDF5\"/><rect x=\"-32\" y=\"-80\" width=\"12\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#FFFDF5\"/><rect x=\"-8\" y=\"-80\" width=\"12\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#FFFDF5\"/><rect x=\"16\" y=\"-80\" width=\"12\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#FFFDF5\"/><rect x=\"-72\" y=\"-92\" width=\"120\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#F6EFE2\"/><polygon points=\"-78,-92 -12,-120 54,-92\" fill=\"#E9DCC3\"/><circle cx=\"-12\" cy=\"-104\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#FFD23F\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"courts-judiciary","title":"Courts, the judicial function and rights of audience","q":[[0,"Who is the head of the judiciary of England and Wales?",["The Lord Chief Justice","The Lord Chancellor","The Attorney General"],"Since the <b>Constitutional Reform Act 2005</b> the <b>Lord Chief Justice</b> heads the judiciary. The Lord Chancellor is a minister, not a judge."],[1,"A minister phones a judge to share the government's view on a pending case. Lawful?",["No, it breaches s.3 CRA 2005","Yes, ministers may advise judges","Yes, if the call is recorded"],"Ministers must <b>uphold judicial independence</b> and must not seek to influence particular decisions through special access to judges (<b>s.3 CRA 2005</b>)."],[2,"Who selects judges for appointment in England and Wales?",["The Judicial Appointments Commission","The Prime Minister","The Lord Chancellor alone"],"The independent <b>Judicial Appointments Commission</b> selects judges on merit. It was created by the CRA 2005."],[3,"How can a High Court judge be removed from office?",["Address by both Houses of Parliament","Order of the Lord Chancellor","Vote of the Cabinet"],"Senior judges hold office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the monarch on an <b>address by both Houses</b> (s.11(3) Senior Courts Act 1981)."],[4,"A judge wrongly orders a man's detention in good faith. Can he sue the judge?",["No, the judge has immunity","Yes, for false imprisonment","Yes, in negligence"],"<b>Judicial immunity</b> protects acts in a judicial capacity (<b>Sirros v Moore</b>), so the judge cannot be sued."],[5,"Which court is the final appeal court for civil cases from England and Wales?",["The Supreme Court","The Court of Appeal","The Privy Council"],"The <b>UK Supreme Court</b> replaced the House of Lords appeal committee in 2009."],[6,"In which court is a judicial review claim usually heard?",["The Administrative Court","The County Court","The Court of Appeal"],"Judicial review is heard in the <b>Administrative Court</b>, part of the <b>King's Bench Division</b> of the High Court."],[7,"Which offences are sent straight to the Crown Court for trial?",["Indictable-only offences","Summary-only offences","Either-way offences"],"<b>Indictable-only</b> offences (e.g. murder, robbery) go to the Crown Court. Every case still starts in the magistrates' court."],[8,"A new solicitor has no higher rights. Which hearing can she NOT do as advocate?",["A Crown Court jury trial","A magistrates' court plea","A County Court hearing"],"Solicitors need the <b>higher rights of audience</b> qualification to act as advocate at trials in the <b>Crown Court</b> and other higher courts."],[9,"Which is a reserved legal activity under the Legal Services Act 2007?",["Exercising a right of audience","Giving general legal advice","Drafting a will for oneself"],"<b>Rights of audience</b> are reserved (s.12 LSA 2007). Doing one without authority is a criminal offence (s.14)."],[10,"Does a McKenzie friend have a right of audience?",["No, they only assist quietly","Yes, in the County Court","Yes, if the other side agrees"],"A <b>McKenzie friend</b> can sit with a litigant in person and advise, but has no right of audience unless the court gives permission."],[11,"Which tribunal hears appeals on points of law from the First-tier Tribunal?",["The Upper Tribunal","The County Court","The Administrative Court"],"Under the <b>TCEA 2007</b>, the <b>Upper Tribunal</b> hears these appeals. It is a superior court of record."]],"c":[[1,"Can a court strike down an Act of Parliament?","<b>No.</b> Parliament is sovereign. The most a higher court can do is make a <b>declaration of incompatibility</b> under <b>s.4 HRA</b>."],[4,"How can a senior judge (High Court or Court of Appeal) be removed?","They hold office <b>during good behaviour</b>. They can be removed only by the monarch on an <b>address presented by both Houses of Parliament</b> (s.11(3) Senior Courts Act 1981; s.33 CRA 2005 for Supreme Court Justices)."],[6,"Who selects judges in England and Wales?","The independent <b>Judicial Appointments Commission (JAC)</b>, solely on <b>merit</b> (CRA 2005 s.63). The <b>Lord Chancellor</b> (or the <b>Lord Chief Justice</b> for most lower judicial posts) can only <b>accept</b>, <b>reject</b> or ask it to <b>reconsider</b> a selection. Supreme Court Justices are chosen by an <b>ad hoc selection commission</b>."],[10,"What is the UK Supreme Court's jurisdiction?","Final appeals in <b>civil</b> cases from the <b>whole UK</b>. Final appeals in <b>criminal</b> cases from England, Wales and Northern Ireland, <b>not Scotland</b>. Also <b>devolution</b> issues."],[17,"Which courts are the Senior Courts, and can they be judicially reviewed?","The <b>Court of Appeal, High Court and Crown Court</b>. They are <b>superior courts of record</b> and are <b>not</b> subject to judicial review. Exception: the <b>Crown Court</b> in matters <b>not relating to trial on indictment</b>."],[18,"Which courts are inferior courts?","The <b>County Court</b>, <b>magistrates' courts</b> and <b>coroners' courts</b>. They are <b>subject to judicial review</b> by the High Court."],[23,"How are criminal offences classified for trial?","<b>Summary-only</b>: magistrates' court only. <b>Either-way</b>: magistrates' court or Crown Court, depending on allocation and the defendant's choice. <b>Indictable-only</b>: sent straight to the <b>Crown Court</b>."],[24,"When can a civil claim be started in the High Court?","If it is worth <b>more than £100,000</b>, or <b>£50,000 or more</b> for <b>personal injury</b> (PD 7A para 2). Or a statute or rule requires it, or it is complex or specialist. Otherwise claims start in the <b>County Court</b>."],[26,"What are the six reserved legal activities?","Exercising a <b>right of audience</b>. <b>Conducting litigation</b>. <b>Reserved instrument activities</b>. <b>Probate activities</b>. <b>Notarial activities</b>. <b>Administering oaths</b> (s.12 LSA 2007)."],[29,"What rights of audience does a newly admitted solicitor have?","On paper, rights in <b>all courts</b>. But for <b>trials and substantive hearings</b> in the <b>higher courts</b> they need the <b>Higher Rights of Audience</b> qualification (criminal, civil or both)."]]},{"id":"precedent","title":"The doctrine of precedent","q":[[0,"Which part of a judgment binds later courts?",["The ratio decidendi","Obiter dicta","The dissenting speech"],"Only the <b>ratio decidendi</b> (the legal reason for the decision on the material facts) binds. Obiter is persuasive only."],[1,"A judge's passing comment on facts not before the court is called what?",["Obiter dictum","Ratio decidendi","A reversal"],"<b>Obiter dicta</b> are things said by the way. They may persuade but never bind."],[2,"Which court is not bound by its own past decisions?",["The Supreme Court","The Court of Appeal","The Divisional Court"],"The <b>Supreme Court</b> may depart from its earlier decisions under the <b>1966 Practice Statement</b> when it appears right to do so."],[3,"The Court of Appeal thinks its own earlier decision was badly reasoned. Must it follow it?",["Yes, unless a Young exception applies","No, it may always depart","Only if the Supreme Court agrees"],"The Court of Appeal is bound by itself, subject to the three <b>Young v Bristol Aeroplane</b> exceptions."],[4,"Which is a Young v Bristol Aeroplane exception?",["Earlier decision made per incuriam","Earlier decision is very old","Earlier decision was unpopular"],"A decision made <b>per incuriam</b> (ignoring binding law) need not be followed. The other two: conflicting decisions, or a later Supreme Court ruling."],[5,"Which courts can distinguish a Supreme Court decision on its material facts?",["Any court","Only the Court of Appeal","Only the High Court"],"<b>Distinguishing</b> is open to any court, because the earlier ratio simply does not apply to different material facts."],[6,"Do Crown Court decisions create binding precedent?",["No","Yes, for magistrates","Yes, for the County Court"],"The Crown Court, County Court and magistrates' courts create <b>no binding precedent</b>."],[7,"A higher court changes the outcome of the same case on appeal. What is this called?",["Reversing","Overruling","Distinguishing"],"<b>Reversing</b> changes the decision in the same case. <b>Overruling</b> corrects a rule from a different, earlier case."],[8,"Are Privy Council decisions binding on English courts?",["No, only persuasive","Yes, always","Yes, in civil cases"],"The <b>Privy Council</b> is outside the English hierarchy. <b>Willers v Joyce</b> gives only a narrow exception."],[9,"Who is bound by pre-2021 assimilated EU case law?",["The High Court and below","The Supreme Court","The Court of Appeal"],"Lower courts remain bound. The <b>Supreme Court</b> and <b>Court of Appeal</b> may depart using the Practice Statement test."],[10,"Does a CJEU decision made in 2023 bind UK courts?",["No, but may be considered","Yes, in all courts","Yes, but only the High Court"],"CJEU decisions after <b>31 December 2020</b> do not bind; courts may have regard to them (s.6(2) EUWA)."],[11,"A court's overruling of an old case operates how?",["Retrospectively","Only for future cases","Only from the judgment date"],"Under the <b>declaratory theory</b>, the corrected rule is treated as always having been the law (<b>Kleinwort Benson</b>)."]],"c":[[34,"What is the ratio decidendi?","The <b>legal principle</b> the judge relied on to decide the case on its <b>material facts</b>. It is the <b>only</b> part that <b>binds</b> later courts."],[36,"What is obiter dicta, and is it binding?","Everything else said about the law: hypotheticals, wider comments and <b>dissenting</b> reasons. It is <b>persuasive only</b>. It can later be adopted as law (Howe obiter adopted in Gotts)."],[38,"What is distinguishing, and who can do it?","The later court finds the <b>material facts are different</b>, so the earlier ratio does not apply. <b>Any</b> court can do it, even a magistrates' court facing a Supreme Court decision."],[39,"Overruling v reversing: what is the difference?","<b>Overruling</b>: a higher court holds the <b>rule</b> in an earlier, <b>different</b> case was wrong. <b>Reversing</b>: an appeal court changes the decision in the <b>same case</b>."],[43,"Is the Supreme Court bound by its own decisions?","<b>No.</b> Under the <b>1966 Practice Statement</b> it may depart <b>\"when it appears right to do so\"</b>. It is used <b>rarely and cautiously</b>."],[46,"When can the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) depart from its own decision?","1: <b>Two conflicting</b> CA decisions: it <b>must choose</b> one, and the other is overruled. 2: Its decision <b>cannot stand</b> with a later <b>Supreme Court/HL</b> decision: it <b>must not</b> follow it. 3: Its decision was <b>per incuriam</b>."],[47,"What does per incuriam mean?","A decision made in <b>ignorance of a binding statute or binding authority</b> that would have changed the result. It is applied <b>narrowly</b>."],[49,"What extra flexibility does the Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) have?","It may also depart where the law was <b>misapplied or misunderstood</b> and following it would cause <b>injustice</b> to the defendant, because <b>liberty</b> is at stake."],[51,"Who binds a Divisional Court and a single High Court judge?","<b>Divisional Court</b>: bound by the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal, and <b>generally by itself</b> (Young exceptions apply). <b>Single judge</b>: bound by those and Divisional Courts, but <b>not</b> by other single judges (though normally follows them)."],[55,"Which courts can depart from assimilated EU case law?","Only the <b>Supreme Court</b> and <b>Court of Appeal</b> (and equivalents in Scotland and NI) (s.6(4) EUWA and SI 2020/1525). The <b>High Court and below remain bound</b>."]]},{"id":"legislation-structure","title":"Structure of legislation and where it applies (England and Wales)","q":[[0,"Which part of an Act is NOT part of the Act itself?",["The Explanatory Notes","A Schedule","The long title"],"<b>Explanatory Notes</b> are written by the government department and are not enacted. A court may use them only as an external aid."],[1,"Which part of an Act describes its purpose, starting 'An Act to...'?",["The long title","The short title","The enacting formula"],"The <b>long title</b> states what the Act does, and is a legitimate aid to finding its purpose."],[2,"A Westminster Act says nothing about commencement. When does it come into force?",["Start of the day of Royal Assent","Day after Royal Assent","Two months after Royal Assent"],"<b>s.4 Interpretation Act 1978</b>: it takes effect at the beginning of the day it receives Royal Assent."],[3,"An Act of Senedd Cymru says nothing about commencement. When does it come into force?",["Start of the day after Royal Assent","Start of the day of Royal Assent","On Welsh Government order"],"<b>s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019</b> sets the default as the beginning of the day after Royal Assent."],[4,"How is an Act of Senedd Cymru from 2024 cited?",["2024 asc 6","2024 c. 6","2024 anaw 6"],"<b>asc</b> means Act of Senedd Cymru. <b>anaw</b> was used for earlier Assembly Acts. <b>c.</b> is for Westminster."],[5,"Which statement about Senedd Acts is correct?",["English and Welsh texts have equal standing","The English text prevails","The Welsh text is a translation"],"<b>s.156 Government of Wales Act 2006</b>: both texts are equally authoritative, and neither is a translation."],[6,"What does 'extent' of a provision mean?",["Which legal system it forms part of","Which people it governs","Which courts enforce it"],"<b>Extent</b> is about the legal jurisdiction. <b>Application</b> is about who or what the provision actually governs."],[7,"A section extends to England and Wales but says 'a local authority in England'. Does it bind a Welsh council?",["No","Yes","Only if the Senedd consents"],"Extent is not application. The wording limits the section to <b>English</b> local authorities."],[8,"What must a Senedd Act apply to, to be within competence?",["Wales only","England and Wales","The whole UK"],"<b>s.108A GoWA 2006</b>: it must extend to England and Wales only and apply only in relation to <b>Wales</b>."],[9,"Is a Schedule to an Act as much part of the law as a section?",["Yes","No, it is only a guide","Only if it has a heading"],"<b>Schedules</b> are part of the Act with full legal force, and are cited as e.g. Sch.2 para 3."],[10,"What is delegated legislation usually made as?",["A statutory instrument","A Private Act","A Royal proclamation"],"Ministers make secondary legislation under a parent Act, usually as a <b>statutory instrument</b>."],[11,"Which type of Act is the law for everyone, rather than one place or person?",["A public general Act","A private Act","A local Act"],"<b>Public general Acts</b> apply generally. Private and local Acts affect only particular people or places."]],"c":[[62,"What is secondary legislation?","Law made by ministers or other bodies under a power in a <b>parent (enabling) Act</b>, usually as a <b>statutory instrument</b>. Welsh Ministers make <b>Welsh SIs</b>, numbered with a <b>\"W.\"</b>."],[65,"Are Schedules part of the Act?","<b>Yes.</b> They are <b>as much part of the Act as the sections</b>. Each is introduced by a section in the body and cited as e.g. <b>Sch.2 para 3(1)</b>."],[66,"Are Explanatory Notes part of the Act?","<b>No.</b> They are written by the government department and have <b>no legal force</b>. Courts may use them as an <b>external aid</b> to context."],[73,"When does a Westminster Act with no commencement provision come into force?","At the <b>beginning of the day</b> on which it receives <b>Royal Assent</b> (<b>s.4 Interpretation Act 1978</b>)."],[74,"What commencement methods do Acts usually use?","<b>On Royal Assent</b>. A <b>fixed date</b> or after a set <b>period</b>. A day appointed by <b>commencement regulations</b>, which can differ between provisions."],[80,"When does a Senedd Act with no commencement provision come into force?","At the <b>beginning of the day after</b> Royal Assent (<b>s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019</b>)."],[81,"What is the difference between extent and application?","<b>Extent</b>: which <b>legal system</b> a law forms part of. <b>Application</b>: <b>who or what</b> it actually governs within that territory."],[85,"What extent and application must an Act of Senedd Cymru have?","It must <b>extend</b> only to <b>England and Wales</b> and <b>apply</b> only <b>in relation to Wales</b> (<b>s.108A(2) GoWA 2006</b>). Otherwise it is outside competence and <b>not law</b>."]]},{"id":"statutory-interpretation","title":"Statutory interpretation (including Welsh legislation)","q":[[0,"Which approach gives words their plain ordinary meaning, even if the result is harsh?",["The literal rule","The golden rule","The mischief rule"],"The <b>literal rule</b> applies the ordinary meaning (e.g. <b>Whiteley v Chappell</b>). Fixing harsh results is for Parliament."],[1,"A shop displays a flick knife with a price. Is it 'offering for sale'?",["No, it is an invitation to treat","Yes, the price makes it an offer","Yes, the display is an offer"],"<b>Fisher v Bell</b>: the literal rule plus contract law means a display is only an <b>invitation to treat</b>."],[2,"A son murders his mother, who left no will. Which rule stops him inheriting?",["The golden rule (wide)","The literal rule","Ejusdem generis"],"<b>Re Sigsworth</b>: the <b>golden rule</b> was used to avoid a repugnant result, even though the words were clear."],[3,"What are the four questions in Heydon's Case about?",["The mischief rule","The literal rule","Pepper v Hart"],"<b>Heydon's Case</b> asks about the old law, the mischief, the remedy and its reason. This is the <b>mischief rule</b>."],[4,"Women solicit from balconies. The Act bans soliciting 'in a street'. Guilty?",["Yes, mischief rule applied","No, literal rule applied","No, golden rule applied"],"<b>Smith v Hughes</b>: the mischief was people being solicited in the street, so the <b>mischief rule</b> caught them."],[5,"Which approach is now the dominant one in the courts?",["Purposive approach","Literal rule only","Mischief rule only"],"Courts read words in context to give effect to the Act's <b>purpose</b> (e.g. <b>Quintavalle</b>)."],[6,"'Dogs, cats, rabbits and other animals' banned from a park. Is a lion covered?",["Probably not, by ejusdem generis","Yes, it is an animal","Yes, by the golden rule"],"<b>Ejusdem generis</b>: general words after a list are limited to things of the same kind (pets)."],[7,"Which rule of language says a word takes meaning from the words around it?",["Noscitur a sociis","Expressio unius","Ejusdem generis"],"<b>Noscitur a sociis</b>: a word is known by the company it keeps (<b>IRC v Frere</b>)."],[8,"Which presumption applies to a new criminal offence silent on intent?",["Mens rea is required","Strict liability applies","Intent is presumed"],"<b>Sweet v Parsley</b> and <b>B v DPP</b>: unless clearly excluded, a guilty mind is presumed to be needed."],[9,"Which is NOT a condition for using Hansard under Pepper v Hart?",["The statement was by a backbench MP","The wording is ambiguous or absurd","The statement is clear"],"The statement must come from a <b>minister or promoter</b> of the Bill, not an ordinary MP."],[10,"The English and Welsh texts of a Senedd Act seem to differ. What applies?",["They have equal standing","The English text prevails","The Welsh text prevails"],"<b>s.156 GoWA 2006</b>: both texts are equally authoritative, and the court reconciles them."],[11,"Which Act sets the general interpretation rules for Senedd Acts made after 1 January 2020?",["Legislation (Wales) Act 2019","Interpretation Act 1978","Government of Wales Act 2006"],"<b>Part 2 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019</b> applies instead of the Interpretation Act 1978 for this Welsh legislation."]],"c":[[88,"What is the modern approach to interpretation?","<b>Contextual and purposive</b>. The <b>words</b>, read in context, are the <b>primary source</b>. <b>External aids</b> are secondary and cannot displace a meaning clear from the words (R (O) v SSHD)."],[90,"What is the literal rule?","Words get their <b>ordinary, plain</b> meaning, and technical words their technical meaning, <b>even if the result is harsh</b>. It is for Parliament to fix bad results."],[92,"Narrow v wide golden rule: what is the difference?","<b>Narrow</b>: where a word has <b>more than one meaning</b>, choose the one that avoids absurdity. <b>Wide</b>: even where there is <b>only one meaning</b>, modify it to avoid a <b>repugnant</b> result (murderer cannot inherit: Re Sigsworth)."],[93,"What four questions does the mischief rule ask?","What was the <b>common law</b> before the Act? What <b>mischief</b> did it not cover? What <b>remedy</b> did Parliament provide? What is the <b>true reason</b> for the remedy?"],[96,"When can a court read words into an Act to correct a drafting error?","Only if sure of: 1, the <b>intended purpose</b>; 2, that by inadvertence Parliament <b>failed to give effect</b> to it; 3, the <b>substance</b> of the provision Parliament would have made (Inco Europe)."],[98,"What is ejusdem generis?","General words after a list of <b>specific</b> words are limited to things of the <b>same kind</b> as the list (\"other place\" meant indoor places)."],[104,"What is the principle of legality?","Parliament is presumed <b>not to override fundamental rights</b> by <b>general or ambiguous words</b>. It must \"<b>squarely confront what it is doing</b>\" (ex p Simms)."],[108,"What are intrinsic aids?","Aids <b>inside</b> the Act: <b>long title</b>, short title, <b>preamble</b>, <b>headings</b>, <b>definition sections</b>, <b>Schedules</b>, punctuation and the Act <b>as a whole</b>."],[109,"What are extrinsic aids?","Aids <b>outside</b> the Act: <b>Interpretation Act 1978</b>, dictionaries, earlier statutes, <b>Explanatory Notes</b>, <b>Law Commission</b> reports, treaties and, in limited cases, <b>Hansard</b>."],[112,"When can a court refer to Hansard?","Only if <b>all three</b> apply: 1, the legislation is <b>ambiguous, obscure</b> or literally <b>absurd</b>; 2, the statements are by a <b>minister or other promoter</b> of the Bill; 3, the statements are <b>clear</b> (Pepper v Hart)."]]},{"id":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","title":"Parliamentary sovereignty, separation of powers, rule of law and conventions","q":[[0,"Which is one of Dicey's three elements of parliamentary sovereignty?",["No Parliament can bind its successors","Courts may strike down Acts","Acts need the Lords' consent"],"Parliament can make or unmake any law, <b>no Parliament can bind its successors</b>, and no body can set an Act aside."],[1,"Can a court strike down an Act of Parliament for breaching international law?",["No","Yes, in the High Court","Yes, in the Supreme Court"],"<b>Mortensen v Peters</b>: Acts are valid even if they breach international law. Courts cannot strike down primary legislation."],[2,"What can a court do about an Act that cannot be read compatibly with Convention rights?",["Make a declaration of incompatibility","Quash the Act","Refuse to apply the Act"],"<b>s.4 HRA</b>: a declaration of incompatibility does not affect the Act's validity or continuing operation."],[3,"Why did the court refuse to examine how the Act was passed in Pickin?",["The enrolled Act rule","Implied repeal","The Ponsonby rule"],"Under the <b>enrolled Act rule</b>, courts do not investigate Parliament's internal procedures."],[4,"A later Act conflicts with an earlier ordinary Act. Which prevails?",["The later Act","The earlier Act","The one passed by the Lords"],"<b>Implied repeal</b> (Ellen Street Estates): the later Act prevails to the extent of the conflict."],[5,"Which case held that constitutional statutes cannot be impliedly repealed?",["Thoburn v Sunderland CC","Entick v Carrington","Madzimbamuto v Lardner-Burke"],"The <b>'metric martyrs'</b> case (<b>Thoburn</b>) recognised constitutional statutes, such as the HRA and devolution Acts."],[6,"Which case found the Hunting Act 2004 valid despite the Parliament Act 1949 route?",["Jackson v Attorney General","Burmah Oil","Factortame"],"In <b>Jackson</b> (2005) the House of Lords held the Parliament Act 1949 and the Hunting Act were valid."],[7,"Which case shows the state needs legal authority to enter and seize papers?",["Entick v Carrington","M v Home Office","Cheney v Conn"],"In <b>Entick v Carrington</b> (1765) no law authorised the warrant, so the entry was a trespass."],[8,"Which Act made the Lord Chancellor no longer head of the judiciary?",["Constitutional Reform Act 2005","Human Rights Act 1998","Bill of Rights 1689"],"The <b>CRA 2005</b> strengthened the separation of powers and created the Supreme Court and the JAC."],[9,"Will a court enforce a constitutional convention?",["No, it may recognise but not enforce","Yes, if written into statute","Yes, if the ministers agree"],"<b>Miller 1</b>: even the Sewel convention, stated in statute, is political and not legally enforceable."],[10,"A minister overrides a court ruling simply because he disagrees. Which case says this is unlawful?",["R (Evans) v Attorney General","R v Jogee","Pepper v Hart"],"In <b>Evans</b> (2015) the Supreme Court held the executive cannot override a judicial decision just because it disagrees."],[11,"Which principle came from Lord Bingham's rule of law, not Dicey's?",["Law must be accessible and clear","Equality before the law","No punishment without a breach of law"],"<b>Bingham's eight principles</b> include accessible, intelligible, clear and predictable law. Dicey had three."]],"c":[[121,"What are Dicey's three parts of parliamentary sovereignty?","Parliament can <b>make or unmake any law</b>. <b>No Parliament can bind its successors</b>. <b>No person or body</b>, including the courts, can <b>override or set aside</b> an Act."],[125,"What is implied repeal?","If a later Act conflicts with an earlier one, the <b>later Act prevails</b> to the extent of the inconsistency. This applies even if the earlier Act tried to prevent it, as Parliament cannot bind successors on <b>content</b>."],[126,"Can a constitutional statute be impliedly repealed?","<b>No.</b> It can be repealed only <b>expressly</b> or by <b>unmistakably clear words</b> (Thoburn)."],[133,"Does the HRA 1998 limit sovereignty?","<b>No.</b> Courts may only make a <b>declaration of incompatibility</b>, which does not affect the Act's <b>validity</b>."],[138,"Does the UK have a full separation of powers?","<b>No</b>, only a <b>partial</b> one with <b>checks and balances</b>. Ministers sit in Parliament, the executive makes <b>secondary legislation</b> and judges <b>develop</b> the common law."],[144,"What are Lord Bingham's eight principles of the rule of law?","Law <b>accessible, clear and predictable</b>. Rights decided by <b>law not discretion</b>. <b>Equality</b>. Powers used <b>in good faith, fairly, for their purpose, within limits</b>. Protection of <b>human rights</b>. Civil disputes resolved <b>without prohibitive cost or delay</b>. <b>Fair</b> procedures. Compliance with <b>international law</b>."],[146,"Does the state need legal authority to interfere with a person's property?","<b>Yes.</b> Without statutory or common law authority, entry is a <b>trespass</b>, and <b>\"state necessity\" is no defence</b> (Entick v Carrington)."],[149,"What is a constitutional convention?","A <b>non-legal rule</b> of constitutional behaviour that those it applies to regard as <b>binding</b>."],[153,"Can conventions become law?","<b>Yes.</b> The <b>Ponsonby rule</b> on treaties became statute in <b>Part 2 CRaG 2010</b>. The <b>Sewel convention</b> is stated in statute but is still <b>not legally enforceable</b> (Miller 1)."],[154,"Will a court enforce a convention?","<b>No.</b> Courts are not the guardians of political conventions. Breach brings <b>political</b> consequences, not legal ones."]]},{"id":"crown-executive-prerogative","title":"The Crown, central government, accountability and the royal prerogative","q":[[0,"Who is the head of state of the UK?",["The monarch","The Prime Minister","The Speaker"],"The <b>monarch</b> is head of state. Power is in practice exercised by ministers on the monarch's advice."],[1,"Whom does the monarch appoint as Prime Minister, by convention?",["Whoever commands the Commons' confidence","The leader of the Lords","The largest donor"],"By <b>convention</b> the PM is the person best able to command the <b>confidence of the House of Commons</b>."],[2,"A minister publicly attacks a Cabinet decision and will not resign. Which convention is breached?",["Collective responsibility","Individual responsibility","The Salisbury convention"],"<b>Collective responsibility</b> requires ministers to support government decisions publicly or resign. It is political, not legal."],[3,"What does the Carltona principle allow?",["Officials to act in the minister's name","Ministers to ignore statutes","The Crown to create new prerogatives"],"<b>Carltona</b>: civil servants can exercise a minister's powers in the minister's name, as the minister stays accountable."],[4,"Which body investigates maladministration by government departments?",["Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman","The Cabinet Office","The Joint Committee on Statutory Instruments"],"The <b>Ombudsman</b> investigates complaints (referred by an MP). Recommendations are not legally binding."],[5,"Can the Crown create a new prerogative power today?",["No","Yes, by proclamation","Yes, with ministerial advice"],"<b>BBC v Johns</b> and the <b>Case of Proclamations</b>: prerogative can only shrink, never grow."],[6,"A statute covers the same ground as a prerogative power. What happens to the prerogative?",["It is put in abeyance","It is strengthened","It is repealed by the courts"],"<b>De Keyser's Royal Hotel</b>: the minister must use the statute, and the prerogative is suspended."],[7,"Which case held that notice to leave the EU needed an Act of Parliament?",["Miller 1","Miller 2","Fire Brigades Union"],"<b>Miller 1</b> (2017): the prerogative cannot change domestic law or remove rights given by statute."],[8,"Which case held prorogation advice was unlawful and void?",["Miller 2","GCHQ","Entick v Carrington"],"In <b>Miller 2</b> (2019), five weeks' prorogation frustrated Parliament without reasonable justification."],[9,"Which case held that exercise of a prerogative power can be judicially reviewed?",["CCSU v Minister for the Civil Service","Case of Proclamations","Bradlaugh v Gossett"],"The <b>GCHQ case</b> said reviewability depends on <b>subject matter</b>, not on the source of the power."],[10,"Which prerogative matter is generally non-justiciable?",["Making treaties","Issuing passports","Criminal injuries compensation"],"<b>Treaties</b>, defence, honours, dissolution and appointing ministers are high policy. Passports (<b>Everett</b>) and compensation schemes (<b>Lain</b>) are reviewable."],[11,"Under CRaG 2010, how long must a treaty be laid before Parliament before ratification?",["21 sitting days","14 days","28 working days"],"<b>Part 2 CRaG 2010</b> puts the Ponsonby rule on a statutory footing: <b>21 sitting days</b> without a Commons objection."]],"c":[[162,"What is the Carltona principle?","<b>Officials</b> can exercise a minister's powers <b>in the minister's name</b>, and the minister stays <b>accountable</b> to Parliament. It does <b>not</b> apply where statute requires the minister <b>personally</b> to decide."],[165,"What does individual ministerial responsibility require?","Each minister <b>answers to Parliament</b> for their <b>department</b> and <b>personal conduct</b>. They must <b>not knowingly mislead</b> Parliament and should offer to <b>resign</b> if they do."],[169,"What is the royal prerogative?","The <b>residue</b> of discretionary authority legally left with the Crown (Dicey). It is <b>common law</b> power, not statute. It can <b>only shrink, never grow</b>."],[172,"When is Parliament automatically dissolved?","At the beginning of the day that is the <b>fifth anniversary</b> of the day it <b>first met</b> (s.4 DCPA 2022)."],[173,"Can a court review a dissolution decision?","<b>No.</b> <b>s.3 DCPA 2022</b> is an <b>ouster clause</b> covering the exercise of the power, decisions about it, and its <b>limits or extent</b>."],[174,"Is prorogation covered by the DCPA ouster?","<b>No.</b> <b>Prorogation</b> is still an ordinary prerogative power and is <b>reviewable</b>."],[179,"What happens where a statute covers the same ground as the prerogative?","The prerogative is put into <b>abeyance</b>. The minister <b>must use the statute</b> (and, e.g., pay statutory compensation) (De Keyser)."],[181,"Can the prerogative be used to change domestic law or remove statutory rights?","<b>No.</b> An <b>Act of Parliament</b> is needed (Miller 1: Article 50 notice needed an Act)."],[182,"When is prorogation unlawful?","When it <b>frustrates or prevents, without reasonable justification</b>, Parliament's ability to act as a <b>legislature</b> and to <b>supervise the executive</b>. The prorogation is then <b>void</b> (Miller 2)."],[183,"Is the exercise of a prerogative power judicially reviewable?","<b>Yes</b>, depending on its <b>subject matter</b> (justiciability), not the <b>source</b> of the power (GCHQ)."],[185,"Which prerogative powers are non-justiciable (excluded categories)?","<b>Treaties</b>, <b>defence</b> and deploying the armed forces, <b>honours</b>, <b>dissolution</b> and <b>appointing ministers</b>. They involve high policy unsuited to courts."]]},{"id":"parliament-legislation-privilege","title":"Parliament: making and repealing legislation, and parliamentary privilege","q":[[0,"What happens at the second reading of a Bill?",["Debate on its general principles","Line-by-line amendment","Final vote on amendments"],"<b>Second reading</b> debates the general principles. Line-by-line scrutiny is at committee stage."],[1,"Which statement must a minister make before second reading of a government Bill?",["A s.19 HRA compatibility statement","A Sewel statement","A prorogation statement"],"<b>s.19 Human Rights Act 1998</b> requires a written statement of compatibility, or that the government wishes to proceed anyway."],[2,"How long can the Lords delay a Money Bill under the Parliament Acts?",["One month","One year","Three months"],"A certified <b>Money Bill</b> can get Royal Assent if the Lords do not pass it within <b>one month</b>."],[3,"Which Bill can NOT use the Parliament Acts?",["One that began in the Lords","A Government Bill from the Commons","A Private Members' Bill from the Commons"],"The Parliament Acts cannot be used for Bills starting in the Lords, private Bills, or ones extending Parliament beyond five years."],[4,"Which case held an Act passed under the Parliament Acts was valid?",["R (Jackson) v Attorney General","Pepper v Hart","Prebble v TVNZ"],"<b>Jackson</b> (2005): the Hunting Act 2004 and the Parliament Act 1949 were valid primary legislation."],[5,"When does a negative-procedure SI stop being law?",["If either House annuls it","If neither House approves it","If the Lords delay it"],"<b>Negative procedure</b>: the SI stays law unless either House annuls it within 40 days."],[6,"Which SI procedure needs a resolution of both Houses to approve?",["Affirmative procedure","Negative procedure","No procedure"],"The <b>affirmative procedure</b> needs approval by resolution (Commons only for financial matters)."],[7,"What is a Henry VIII clause?",["Power to amend primary law by SI","Power to dissolve Parliament","Power to suspend the Lords"],"A <b>Henry VIII clause</b> lets ministers amend or repeal Acts by statutory instrument. Courts read them narrowly."],[8,"Can a court quash a statutory instrument that goes beyond its parent Act?",["Yes, as ultra vires","No, only Parliament can","Only if it breaches an Act"],"Unlike Acts, <b>secondary legislation</b> can be quashed for being ultra vires (substantively or procedurally)."],[9,"An MP repeats in a debate a damaging claim. Can she be sued for defamation?",["No, Article 9 protects her","Yes, if it was false","Yes, if she acted with malice"],"<b>Article 9 Bill of Rights 1689</b> gives absolute immunity for proceedings in Parliament."],[10,"The same MP repeats the claim on social media. Is she protected?",["No, it is outside proceedings","Yes, by Article 9","Yes, by qualified privilege"],"Article 9 covers only <b>proceedings in Parliament</b>. Posts, press releases and interviews are not covered."],[11,"MPs charged with false accounting over expenses claims plead privilege. Result?",["They can be prosecuted","They are immune","Only the Commons may try them"],"<b>R v Chaytor</b> (2010): expenses claims are not proceedings in Parliament, so there is no immunity."]],"c":[[196,"What does s.19 HRA require before second reading of a government Bill?","The minister in charge must make a written <b>statement of compatibility</b>. Or a statement that they <b>cannot</b> make one but the government wishes the House to proceed anyway."],[197,"How do the Parliament Acts treat Money Bills?","A <b>Money Bill</b> is certified by the <b>Speaker</b> as dealing only with national <b>taxation</b> or <b>public spending</b>. If the Lords do not pass it within <b>one month</b>, it can receive Royal Assent without them."],[198,"What are the conditions for passing other public Bills without the Lords?","The Commons passes it in <b>two successive sessions</b>. <b>One year</b> passes between the Commons <b>second reading</b> in session 1 and <b>passing</b> in session 2. It goes to the Lords at least <b>one month</b> before each session ends. It is the <b>same Bill</b>."],[199,"When can the Parliament Acts not be used?","For a Bill to <b>extend Parliament beyond five years</b>. For <b>private Bills</b>. For Bills that <b>start in the Lords</b>. For <b>delegated legislation</b>."],[204,"How does the negative procedure work?","The SI becomes or stays law <b>unless either House annuls it</b> within <b>40 days</b>. This excludes dissolution, prorogation or adjournments of both Houses over four days. It is the <b>most common</b> procedure."],[205,"How does the affirmative procedure work?","The SI must be <b>approved by both Houses</b> (Commons only for financial matters). <b>Draft affirmative</b>: approved before it is made. <b>Made affirmative</b>: effective at once but <b>lapses</b> unless approved in time."],[207,"On what grounds can a court quash secondary legislation?","<b>Substantive ultra vires</b>: beyond the parent Act's powers, improper purpose or irrational. <b>Procedural ultra vires</b>: a <b>mandatory procedure</b> (e.g. consultation) was not followed. <b>Incompatible with Convention rights</b> unless the parent Act requires it. Breach of the <b>principle of legality</b>."],[210,"What does Article 9 Bill of Rights 1689 protect?","Freedom of speech and debates or <b>proceedings in Parliament</b> must not be <b>impeached or questioned in any court</b> outside Parliament. Members have <b>absolute immunity</b>, e.g. from defamation, for what they say in proceedings."],[212,"Is an MP protected for repeating a parliamentary statement outside Parliament?","<b>No.</b> Interviews, social media and press releases are not protected. <b>Effectively repeating</b> it (\"I stand by what I said\") can also lead to liability."],[213,"Are expenses claims or constituency letters protected by privilege?","<b>No.</b> Submitting <b>expenses claims</b> is not a proceeding in Parliament, so MPs can be prosecuted for false accounting (Chaytor). Ordinary <b>correspondence</b> is generally not covered."]]},{"id":"devolution-wales","title":"Devolution, the Senedd and the Sewel convention","q":[[0,"Which statute is the main source of Welsh devolution?",["Government of Wales Act 2006","Wales Act 1978","Scotland Act 1998"],"The <b>Government of Wales Act 2006</b> (as amended by the Wales Acts 2014 and 2017) sets up the Senedd and Welsh Government."],[1,"Which model of devolution applies to Wales since the Wales Act 2017?",["Reserved powers","Conferred powers","Federal"],"The Senedd can legislate on anything <b>not reserved</b> (Sch 7A) or restricted (Sch 7B)."],[2,"A provision of a Senedd Act is outside legislative competence. What is its status?",["It is not law","It is valid until challenged","It is law but voidable"],"<b>s.108A GoWA 2006</b>: so far as a provision is outside competence, it is <b>not law</b>."],[3,"Which of these is a reserved matter for Wales?",["Immigration","Housing","Local government"],"<b>Immigration</b> is reserved (Sch 7A). Housing and local government are devolved."],[4,"How is a Senedd provision tested for relating to a reserved matter?",["By its purpose, having regard to effect","By its title only","By the Presiding Officer's view"],"<b>s.108A(6)</b>: whether a provision relates to a reserved matter is decided by its <b>purpose</b>, with regard to its effect."],[5,"Who can refer a passed Senedd Bill to the Supreme Court within four weeks?",["Counsel General or Attorney General","The First Minister alone","The Presiding Officer"],"Under <b>s.112 GoWA 2006</b>, either law officer may refer the competence question. The Secretary of State uses s.114 instead."],[6,"Who can make a s.114 order prohibiting a Senedd Bill from going for Royal Assent?",["The Secretary of State for Wales","The Attorney General","The Counsel General"],"Only the <b>Secretary of State for Wales</b> may do so, on specified grounds such as adverse effect on a reserved matter."],[7,"What is the Sewel convention in its Welsh form?",["No UK legislation without consent, normally","The Senedd can veto UK Acts","Wales may leave the UK"],"The convention is political. It is mentioned in <b>s.107(6) GoWA 2006</b> but is not legally enforceable."],[8,"Which case held the Sewel convention cannot be enforced by courts?",["Miller 1","Jackson","Pepper v Hart"],"In <b>Miller 1</b> (2017) the Supreme Court said statutory recognition did not make the convention justiciable."],[9,"Westminster legislates on a devolved matter without Senedd consent. Is the Act valid?",["Yes","No, it is void in Wales","Only if the Lords approve"],"Parliament stays sovereign (<b>s.107(5) GoWA 2006</b>), so the Act is fully valid."],[10,"What is the legal adviser of the Welsh Government called?",["The Counsel General","The Presiding Officer","The Secretary of State"],"The <b>Counsel General for Wales</b> is the Welsh Government's chief legal adviser."],[11,"Who can quash Welsh Ministers' regulations that exceed their powers?",["The High Court on judicial review","The Presiding Officer","The Secretary of State"],"Welsh subordinate legislation outside the parent Act or <b>Convention rights</b> (s.81 GoWA) can be quashed by judicial review."]],"c":[[221,"What does s.A1 GoWA 2006 say about permanence?","The Senedd and Welsh Government are a <b>permanent part</b> of the constitution. They will not be abolished except after a <b>referendum</b> of the people of Wales. Whether this binds a future Parliament legally is <b>doubtful</b>."],[223,"How is the Senedd elected from May 2026?","<b>96 Members</b> from <b>16 constituencies</b> of <b>6 Members</b> each. By <b>closed list proportional representation</b>, with seats allocated by <b>D'Hondt</b>. <b>Four-year</b> terms, so the next ordinary election is in <b>2030</b>."],[226,"What model of devolution does Wales now have?","The <b>reserved powers</b> model, since the <b>Wales Act 2017</b> (from 1 April 2018). The Senedd can legislate on <b>anything not reserved or restricted</b>. Before, it had <b>conferred powers</b>."],[228,"What are the five tests of competence in s.108A(2)?","It must <b>extend</b> only to <b>England and Wales</b>. It must <b>apply</b> only <b>in relation to Wales</b>. It must not <b>relate to a reserved matter</b> (Sch 7A). It must not breach a <b>restriction</b> (Sch 7B). It must be <b>compatible with Convention rights</b>."],[229,"What are examples of matters reserved to the UK Parliament under Schedule 7A of the Government of Wales Act 2006?","General: <b>constitution</b>, <b>civil service</b>, the <b>single legal jurisdiction</b>, <b>courts</b>, <b>foreign affairs</b>, <b>defence</b>. Specific: most <b>justice and policing</b>, <b>immigration</b>, <b>macroeconomic policy</b>, <b>employment rights</b>, <b>social security</b>."],[231,"How is it decided whether a provision \"relates to\" a reserved matter?","By its <b>purpose</b>, having regard to its <b>effect</b> in all the circumstances (s.108A(6)). Merely <b>touching</b> a reserved matter incidentally may still be within competence."],[235,"Who can refer a Senedd Bill to the Supreme Court, and when?","The <b>Counsel General</b> or the <b>Attorney General</b>, within <b>four weeks</b> of the Bill being passed, on whether it is <b>within competence</b> (<b>s.112 GoWA 2006</b>)."],[237,"What can the Secretary of State for Wales do under s.114?","Within the <b>four weeks</b>, make an order <b>prohibiting</b> the Bill from being submitted for Royal Assent if there are reasonable grounds to believe it would adversely affect a <b>reserved matter</b> or <b>the law as it applies in England</b>, or be incompatible with an <b>international obligation</b> or <b>defence or national security</b> (s.114(1)). The old <b>water</b> ground was removed by the Wales Act 2017."],[239,"What is the Sewel convention (Welsh form)?","Westminster <b>will not normally legislate</b> on <b>devolved matters</b> without the <b>Senedd's consent</b>, given by a <b>legislative consent motion</b>."],[240,"Is the Sewel convention legally enforceable?","<b>No.</b> It is stated in <b>s.107(6) GoWA 2006</b>, but it remains a <b>political convention</b>. Courts cannot enforce it or rule on its scope (Miller 1)."]]},{"id":"public-order","title":"Public order: processions, assemblies and breach of the peace","q":[[0,"How much advance notice does a public procession need under s.11 POA 1986?",["Six clear days","Two working days","Fourteen days"],"<b>s.11</b>: written notice to the police at least <b>six clear days</b> before, unless not reasonably practicable."],[1,"Which event needs no advance notice under the POA 1986?",["A static assembly","A procession","A banned march"],"Only <b>processions</b> need notice. <b>Assemblies</b> need none, but may have conditions imposed."],[2,"Which is exempt from the s.11 notice requirement?",["A customary procession","A first-time march","A march with a set route"],"Processions <b>commonly or customarily held</b> (e.g. <b>Kay</b>, Critical Mass), spontaneous ones and funerals are exempt."],[3,"Who may impose conditions on a procession under s.12?",["The senior police officer","Only the Home Secretary","The local council"],"The <b>senior police officer</b> (the chief officer in advance, or the senior officer at the scene) may impose conditions."],[4,"What must the officer reasonably believe to impose conditions on a procession?",["It may cause serious disorder or disruption","It may cause any inconvenience","It is politically unpopular"],"Triggers include <b>serious public disorder</b>, serious damage, serious disruption, or intimidation."],[5,"On what ground can a procession be banned under s.13?",["Serious public disorder","Noise","Serious disruption only"],"A ban needs a belief that conditions will <b>not be sufficient</b> to prevent <b>serious public disorder</b>."],[6,"Outside London, who can prohibit processions in an area?",["The council, with Home Secretary's consent","The chief officer alone","A magistrate"],"The chief officer applies to the <b>council</b>, which needs the <b>Home Secretary's consent</b>. In London the Commissioner makes the order."],[7,"A static rally of 200 people is planned. Can the police ban it under s.13?",["No, only impose conditions","Yes, if disorder is feared","Yes, with the council's consent"],"Assemblies can have <b>s.14 conditions</b>. Only <b>trespassory</b> assemblies can be banned under s.14A."],[8,"In Ziegler, how was obstruction of a highway by protesters assessed?",["By fact-specific proportionality","By strict liability","By the police's view"],"<b>DPP v Ziegler</b> (2021): lawful excuse for obstruction needs proportionality under Articles 10 and 11."],[9,"Is breach of the peace a criminal offence?",["No","Yes, summary only","Yes, either way"],"<b>Breach of the peace</b> is not an offence, but anyone may arrest to stop one that is happening or imminent (<b>R v Howell</b>)."],[10,"Police stop coaches several miles from a demo and send them back. Lawful?",["No, no breach was imminent","Yes, any risk justifies it","Yes, if the coaches are searched"],"<b>Laporte</b> (2006): no imminent breach, and the action was disproportionate. Contrast <b>Moss v McLachlan</b>."],[11,"Preachers attract a hostile crowd, so the officer arrests the preachers. Lawful?",["No, they were not the threat","Yes, they caused the crowd","Yes, it is always lawful"],"<b>Redmond-Bate v DPP</b>: action should normally be against those threatening violence, not those acting lawfully."]],"c":[[244,"Which Convention rights protect protest, and what kind of rights are they?","<b>Article 10</b> (expression) and <b>Article 11</b> (peaceful assembly). Both are <b>qualified</b>: restrictions must be <b>prescribed by law</b>, for a <b>legitimate aim</b> and <b>necessary in a democratic society</b> (proportionate)."],[249,"What is the s.11 notice deadline?","<b>Written notice</b> at least <b>six clear days</b> before the procession. If that is not reasonably practicable, <b>as soon as reasonably practicable</b>."],[254,"What are the triggers for conditions on a procession (s.12)?","A <b>reasonable belief</b> that it may result in <b>serious public disorder</b>, <b>serious damage to property</b> or <b>serious disruption to the life of the community</b>. Or <b>noise</b> causing serious disruption or significant impact. Or organisers' purpose to <b>intimidate</b>. Or (since 2026) intimidating people accessing a <b>place of worship</b>."],[259,"What is the only trigger for banning processions (s.13)?","The <b>chief officer</b> reasonably believes s.12 <b>conditions will not be sufficient</b> to prevent <b>serious public disorder</b>."],[260,"How is a procession ban made outside London?","The chief officer <b>applies to the council</b>, which may prohibit all processions (or a <b>class</b>) for up to <b>three months</b>, with the <b>Home Secretary's consent</b>."],[265,"What conditions can be imposed on an assembly (s.14)?","On the <b>same grounds</b> as processions. Since the PCSCA 2022, <b>any conditions</b> that appear necessary. Before 2022 they were limited to <b>place</b>, <b>duration</b> and <b>numbers</b>."],[268,"What are the rules for banning trespassory assemblies?","An order for up to <b>four days</b> within up to <b>five miles</b>, on the same council and Home Secretary route. Trigger: <b>20 or more</b> people on land with <b>no or limited public access</b>, likely <b>without permission</b>, risking <b>serious disruption</b> or <b>significant damage</b> to important sites."],[273,"What is a breach of the peace?","Harm is <b>done or likely to be done to a person</b>, or to his property <b>in his presence</b>. Or a person is in <b>fear</b> of such harm through an assault, affray, riot or other disturbance."],[275,"When can action be taken to prevent a breach of the peace?","When it is committed <b>in the person's presence</b>, or <b>reasonably believed</b> to be <b>imminent</b>, or to prevent a <b>renewal</b>. <b>Imminence is essential</b>."],[278,"When is kettling (containment) lawful?","When it is <b>necessary</b> to prevent an <b>imminent</b> breach and <b>proportionate</b>. Done in <b>good faith</b> and lasting no longer than necessary, it is <b>not a deprivation of liberty</b> under Article 5."]]},{"id":"judicial-review-procedure","title":"Judicial review: nature, amenability, standing, time limits and procedure","q":[[0,"Which court hears most judicial review claims?",["The Administrative Court","The County Court","The Court of Appeal"],"JR is heard in the <b>Administrative Court</b> (King's Bench Division), under <b>s.31 Senior Courts Act 1981</b> and CPR Part 54."],[1,"What does judicial review examine?",["The lawfulness of the decision","Whether the decision was right","The merits of the policy"],"JR is <b>supervisory</b>, not an appeal. If it succeeds, the body usually decides again lawfully."],[2,"A racing body disqualifies a horse using powers from contract. Is it open to JR?",["No, its power is contractual","Yes, it regulates a national sport","Yes, it is a public body"],"<b>Aga Khan</b>: power from <b>contract</b> or consent is not amenable. <b>Datafin</b> covers bodies with a public regulatory function."],[3,"Why was the Takeover Panel reviewable in Datafin?",["It performed a public function","It was set up by statute","It was set up by prerogative"],"<b>Datafin</b>: the court looks at the <b>nature of the function</b>, not only the source of the power."],[4,"What must a claimant have to bring ordinary JR?",["A sufficient interest","Personal financial loss","The Attorney General's consent"],"<b>s.31(3) SCA 1981</b> requires a <b>sufficient interest</b>. Pressure groups often qualify on matters of public importance."],[5,"Which test applies to a pressure group relying on Convention rights?",["It must be a victim","Sufficient interest only","It needs 100 members"],"<b>s.7 HRA 1998</b> limits claims to <b>victims</b>, who must be directly affected."],[6,"What is the general time limit for a JR claim?",["Promptly, and within 3 months","Within 6 weeks","Within 28 days"],"<b>CPR 54.5</b>: promptly and at the latest <b>three months</b> from when the grounds first arose."],[7,"What is the JR time limit for a planning decision?",["Six weeks","Three months","30 days"],"Planning challenges must be brought within <b>six weeks</b> (CPR 54.5(5)). Procurement is 30 days."],[8,"Can the parties agree to extend the JR time limit?",["No, only the court can","Yes, in writing","Yes, via a letter before claim"],"<b>CPR 54.5(2)</b>: time cannot be extended by agreement, and pre-action letters do not stop the clock."],[9,"What must a claimant show to get permission for JR?",["An arguable ground with realistic prospect","A certain win on the merits","That no alternative remedy exists at all"],"The <b>permission</b> test is an arguable ground with a realistic prospect of success, and no bar such as delay."],[10,"Within how many days must a defendant file an acknowledgment of service?",["21 days","7 days","35 days"],"<b>CPR 54.8</b>: 21 days from service of the claim form. Detailed grounds follow within 35 days of permission."],[11,"Which case said private law claims in disguise may be struck out as abuse of process?",["O'Reilly v Mackman","Roy v Kensington FPC","Ridge v Baldwin"],"<b>O'Reilly v Mackman</b> (1983) set procedural exclusivity, now applied flexibly (<b>Roy</b>)."]],"c":[[282,"What if an adequate alternative remedy exists?","JR is a <b>last resort</b>. The claimant should normally use the <b>alternative remedy</b> (e.g. statutory appeal) first, or permission or relief may be refused."],[285,"What does amenability ask?","Whether the body is <b>public</b> and the decision has a sufficient <b>public law</b> element, i.e. it relates to a <b>public function</b> (CPR 54.1)."],[291,"What is the test for standing in JR?","A <b>sufficient interest in the matter</b> (<b>s.31(3) SCA 1981</b>)."],[295,"What is the standing test for an HRA claim?","The claimant must be a <b>victim</b>, i.e. <b>directly affected</b> (<b>s.7 HRA</b>). A pressure group that is not a victim cannot bring an HRA claim, but may bring ordinary JR."],[296,"What is the general JR time limit?","<b>Promptly</b> and in any event <b>not later than three months</b> after the grounds <b>first arose</b> (<b>CPR 54.5(1)</b>)."],[297,"What is the time limit for a planning JR?","<b>Six weeks</b> after the grounds arose (<b>CPR 54.5(5)</b>)."],[298,"What is the time limit for a public procurement JR?","<b>30 days</b> from when the claimant <b>knew or ought to have known</b> of the grounds."],[302,"What is the undue delay rule in s.31(6) SCA 1981?","Even within time, permission or relief may be refused if it would cause <b>substantial hardship</b>, <b>prejudice</b> to anyone's rights, or be <b>detrimental to good administration</b>."],[305,"What are the service and acknowledgment deadlines in JR?","Serve the defendant and <b>interested parties</b> within <b>7 days</b> of issue. Acknowledgment of service with <b>summary grounds</b> within <b>21 days</b> of service."],[309,"What is the permission test?","An <b>arguable ground</b> with a <b>realistic prospect of success</b>, not subject to a discretionary bar such as <b>delay</b> or an <b>alternative remedy</b>."],[310,"What is the \"no difference\" rule at permission?","Permission <b>must be refused</b> if it is <b>highly likely</b> the outcome would <b>not have been substantially different</b> without the conduct complained of. Exception: <b>exceptional public interest</b> (s.31(3C)-(3F) SCA 1981)."]]},{"id":"judicial-review-grounds","title":"Judicial review: grounds, remedies and ouster clauses","q":[[0,"Which three heads of review did Lord Diplock set out in GCHQ?",["Illegality, irrationality, procedural impropriety","Illegality, bias, proportionality","Ultra vires, estoppel, mistake"],"<b>GCHQ</b> (1985) grouped grounds as <b>illegality, irrationality and procedural impropriety</b>. Legitimate expectation is a fourth."],[1,"A council bans stag hunting because it finds it morally repugnant. Which ground?",["Illegality: irrelevant consideration","Irrationality","Bias"],"<b>Fewings</b>: the power was for the area's benefit, and moral views were irrelevant, so the ban was quashed."],[2,"A minister refuses to refer a complaint to avoid embarrassment. Which ground?",["Illegality: improper purpose","Irrationality","Legitimate expectation"],"<b>Padfield</b>: using a power to frustrate the Act's purpose is an <b>improper purpose</b>."],[3,"A policy refuses all grants under £25 and the body never considers exceptions. Which ground?",["Fettering discretion","Apparent bias","Unlawful delegation"],"A rigid policy with no exceptions <b>fetters discretion</b>. In <b>British Oxygen</b> the Board did listen, so it was lawful."],[4,"What is the Wednesbury test of irrationality?",["So unreasonable no reasonable authority would decide it","Disproportionate to the aim","Different from the court's own view"],"<b>Wednesbury</b>: the decision must be so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could have reached it. It is a high bar."],[5,"A planning committee member's brother is the applicant. Which test applies?",["Real possibility of bias to a fair-minded observer","Proof of actual bias","Proof of a financial interest"],"<b>Porter v Magill</b>: <b>apparent bias</b> applies. Actual bias need not be proved."],[6,"A judge owns shares in a party to the case. What is the effect?",["Automatic disqualification","Apparent bias only","No effect unless he votes"],"<b>Dimes v Grand Junction Canal</b>: a direct financial interest disqualifies automatically."],[7,"Which is NOT one of the Gunning consultation principles?",["Consultation must be oral","Proposals must be at a formative stage","Adequate time must be given"],"The <b>Gunning principles</b> (approved in <b>Moseley</b>) cover formative stage, sufficient reasons, adequate time and conscientious consideration."],[8,"A council promises to consult traders before changing fees, then does not. What arises?",["Procedural legitimate expectation","Substantive legitimate expectation","Automatic disqualification"],"A clear promise to consult creates a <b>procedural</b> expectation of consultation."],[9,"Which remedy sets aside an unlawful decision?",["Quashing order","Mandatory order","Prohibiting order"],"A <b>quashing order</b> (formerly certiorari) sets aside the decision. A <b>mandatory order</b> compels a duty."],[10,"Can damages be claimed in JR alone?",["Only with a private law or HRA claim","Yes, for any unlawful decision","Yes, up to a statutory cap"],"<b>s.31(4) SCA 1981</b>: damages need a private law cause of action or an HRA claim."],[11,"An Act says decisions 'shall not be called in question'. The body made an error of law. Result?",["Court can still review","Court cannot review","Only the Lords can review"],"<b>Anisminic</b>: an error of law means a purported determination, so a total ouster clause does not protect it."]],"c":[[313,"What are the three GCHQ grounds of judicial review?","<b>Illegality</b>, <b>irrationality</b> and <b>procedural impropriety</b>. <b>Legitimate expectation</b> is tested as a fourth ground."],[318,"What is the rule on relevant and irrelevant considerations?","The body must consider what the statute <b>expressly or impliedly requires</b>. It must <b>not</b> take <b>irrelevant</b> considerations into account (e.g. a ban based purely on <b>moral repugnance</b>, ignoring the power's purpose)."],[320,"What is improper purpose?","Using a power given for one purpose for <b>another purpose</b>, e.g. to avoid <b>embarrassment</b>, to <b>raise money</b> or for <b>electoral advantage</b>. Even an apparently unlimited discretion must promote the Act's purpose."],[321,"When does a policy unlawfully fetter discretion?","When it is applied so <b>rigidly</b> that the body refuses to consider whether an <b>exception</b> should be made. A policy is <b>lawful</b> if the body is willing to <b>listen</b> to anyone with something new to say."],[325,"What is Wednesbury unreasonableness?","A decision <b>so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could ever have come to it</b>. Lord Diplock: <b>so outrageous</b> in its defiance of <b>logic</b> or accepted <b>moral standards</b> that no sensible person could have reached it."],[329,"What are the Gunning principles for consultation?","At a <b>formative stage</b>. <b>Sufficient reasons</b> for intelligent response. <b>Adequate time</b>. The responses are <b>conscientiously taken into account</b>."],[331,"When is a decision-maker automatically disqualified for bias?","Where they have a <b>direct financial or proprietary interest</b> in the outcome, or are in effect a <b>party</b> or closely linked to a party's cause. No inquiry into actual bias is needed."],[332,"What is the test for apparent bias?","Would a <b>fair-minded and informed observer</b>, having considered the facts, conclude there was a <b>real possibility</b> of bias? (Porter v Magill)"],[337,"What creates a legitimate expectation?","A promise that is <b>clear, unambiguous and devoid of relevant qualification</b>, or a settled, regular <b>practice</b>. The claimant must usually have <b>disclosed all relevant facts</b>, and the promise must be made with <b>authority</b>."],[339,"When is frustrating a substantive legitimate expectation unlawful?","When it is <b>so unfair as to amount to an abuse of power</b>, unless an <b>overriding public interest</b> justifies the change (\"home for life\" promise: Coughlan)."],[341,"Are JR remedies automatic?","<b>No.</b> All are <b>discretionary</b>. Relief can be refused for <b>delay</b>, the claimant's <b>conduct</b> or because it serves no purpose."],[342,"What do the prerogative orders do?","<b>Quashing order</b>: sets aside the decision, which is usually <b>remade</b>. <b>Prohibiting order</b>: stops an unlawful act <b>about to happen</b>. <b>Mandatory order</b>: requires performance of a <b>public duty</b>."]]},{"id":"human-rights-act","title":"The Human Rights Act 1998 and Convention rights","q":[[0,"What must courts do with Strasbourg case law under s.2 HRA?",["Take it into account","Follow it strictly","Ignore it"],"<b>s.2</b>: courts must <b>take into account</b> ECtHR case law. They are not bound, but normally follow a clear and constant line."],[1,"What does s.3 HRA require?",["Read legislation compatibly so far as possible","Strike down incompatible Acts","Ministers to repeal the Act"],"<b>s.3</b> is a strong interpretive duty (<b>Ghaidan v Godin-Mendoza</b>), but cannot go against a fundamental feature of the Act."],[2,"Which courts can make a declaration of incompatibility?",["High Court and above","The Crown Court","Any court or tribunal"],"<b>s.4(5)</b>: only the higher courts. Magistrates, the Crown Court and the County Court cannot."],[3,"What is the effect of a declaration of incompatibility?",["The Act remains valid","The Act is struck down","It binds the parties"],"<b>s.4(6)</b>: it does not affect validity or continuing operation, and does not bind the parties. Parliament decides next."],[4,"Who is a core public authority under s.6 HRA?",["A local council","A private prison contractor","A parish church council"],"<b>Core</b> authorities (councils, police, ministers) are bound in everything they do. Private firms are hybrid only for public functions."],[5,"A private company runs a prison. When is it bound by s.6?",["Only when exercising public functions","Always","Never"],"A <b>hybrid</b> authority is bound only for acts of a public nature (s.6(5)), not private ones such as ordinary employment."],[6,"Is Parliament a public authority under s.6?",["No, it is excluded","Yes, the Commons only","Yes, both Houses"],"<b>s.6(3)</b>: Parliament is excluded, so primary legislation itself cannot be unlawful under s.6."],[7,"Who can bring an HRA claim under s.7?",["A victim of the act","Any pressure group","Anyone with sufficient interest"],"<b>s.7(1)</b>: the claimant must be a <b>victim</b>, i.e. directly affected, a narrower test than for ordinary JR."],[8,"What is the time limit for an HRA claim under s.7(5)?",["One year, or longer if equitable","Six months","Three months"],"<b>s.7(5)</b>: one year, subject to any stricter rule (such as three months for JR) and an equitable extension."],[9,"When are damages awarded under s.8 HRA?",["If necessary for just satisfaction","Always, if there is a breach","Never"],"<b>s.8(3)</b>: damages only if necessary to afford <b>just satisfaction</b>. Awards are usually modest (<b>Greenfield</b>)."],[10,"Which Convention right is absolute, with no proportionality balancing?",["Article 3","Article 8","Article 10"],"<b>Article 3</b> (torture and inhuman or degrading treatment) is <b>absolute</b>. Articles 8 to 11 are qualified."],[11,"Which is NOT part of justifying interference with a qualified right?",["It must be authorised by a court order","Prescribed by law","Legitimate aim"],"Interference must be <b>prescribed by law</b>, pursue a <b>legitimate aim</b>, and be <b>necessary and proportionate</b> (Bank Mellat)."]],"c":[[355,"What does s.3(1) HRA require?","<b>So far as it is possible</b>, primary and subordinate legislation must be <b>read and given effect</b> compatibly with Convention rights."],[356,"Does s.3 apply to Acts passed before the HRA, and only where words are ambiguous?","It applies to legislation <b>whenever passed</b>. It is a <b>strong</b> duty: courts can <b>read words in or down</b> even with <b>no ambiguity</b>."],[359,"When can a court make a declaration of incompatibility?","When a provision of <b>primary legislation</b> is incompatible and <b>cannot be read compatibly</b> under s.3 (s.4(2)). Also for subordinate legislation where the parent Act prevents removal of the incompatibility."],[360,"Which courts can make a declaration of incompatibility?","Only <b>higher courts</b>: Supreme Court, Privy Council, <b>Court of Appeal</b>, <b>High Court</b>, Court Martial Appeal Court and certain senior Court of Protection judges. <b>Not</b> the Crown Court, County Court, magistrates or most tribunals."],[364,"What does s.6(1) HRA provide?","It is <b>unlawful</b> for a <b>public authority</b> to act, or fail to act, <b>incompatibly with a Convention right</b>."],[366,"What is a hybrid public authority, and when is it bound?","A body certain of whose functions are <b>of a public nature</b> (s.6(3)(b)). It is bound only when exercising <b>public functions</b>, not when the act is <b>private</b> in nature (s.6(5))."],[372,"Who can bring an HRA claim or rely on a Convention right?","Only a <b>victim</b> of the unlawful act (s.7(1)). They may <b>bring proceedings</b> or <b>rely on</b> the right in any proceedings, e.g. as a defence."],[374,"What is the HRA time limit?","<b>One year</b> from the act complained of, or a <b>longer period</b> the court considers <b>equitable</b>. This is subject to any <b>stricter</b> limit for the procedure, e.g. <b>three months</b> for JR (s.7(5))."],[380,"Which Convention rights are absolute?","<b>Article 3</b> (torture, inhuman or degrading treatment). <b>Article 4(1)</b> (slavery, servitude). <b>Article 7</b> (no punishment without law). <b>Protocol 13</b> (death penalty). Freedom of <b>belief</b> under Article 9."],[386,"When is interference with a qualified right justified?","It must be <b>prescribed by law</b> (accessible and foreseeable). It must pursue a <b>legitimate aim</b> listed in the Article. It must be <b>necessary in a democratic society</b>: a <b>pressing social need</b> and <b>proportionate</b>."],[387,"What are the four proportionality questions (Bank Mellat)?","1: Is the <b>objective sufficiently important</b>? 2: Is the measure <b>rationally connected</b>? 3: Could a <b>less intrusive</b> measure be used? 4: Does it strike a <b>fair balance</b> between the individual and the community?"]]},{"id":"eu-law-brexit","title":"EU law in the UK after Brexit","q":[[0,"Which Act brought EU law into UK law between 1973 and 2020?",["European Communities Act 1972","European Union Act 2011","Human Rights Act 1998"],"The <b>ECA 1972</b> gave EU law effect, including supremacy (s.2(4)). It was repealed by the <b>EUWA 2018</b>."],[1,"Which case disapplied an Act of Parliament in favour of directly effective EU law?",["Factortame (No 2)","Thoburn","Miller 1"],"In <b>Factortame (No 2)</b> the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 was disapplied. It was Parliament's own choice in the ECA 1972."],[2,"On what date did the UK leave the EU?",["31 January 2020","31 December 2020","1 January 2024"],"<b>Exit day</b> was 31 January 2020. The transition ended on <b>IP completion day</b>, 31 December 2020."],[3,"What is EU-derived law kept after Brexit now called?",["Assimilated law","Retained law","Converted law"],"From <b>1 January 2024</b>, the <b>REUL Act 2023</b> renamed retained EU law as <b>assimilated law</b>."],[4,"Does EU law still have supremacy over UK Acts?",["No, abolished from 2024","Yes, over later Acts only","Yes, over earlier Acts only"],"The <b>REUL Act 2023</b> abolished supremacy from 1 January 2024. The Withdrawal Agreement is the main exception."],[5,"Which rights still prevail over inconsistent domestic law under s.7A EUWA?",["Withdrawal Agreement rights","General principles of EU law","Rights under the TCA"],"<b>s.7A EUWA</b> gives effect to the <b>Withdrawal Agreement</b>, including citizens' rights and the Windsor Framework."],[6,"Can a claimant rely on general principles of EU law in a UK court now?",["No, abolished from 2024","Yes, for assimilated law","Yes, for any Act"],"The <b>REUL Act</b> (s.4) ended the general principles (such as proportionality and legal certainty) as part of domestic law."],[7,"Does a CJEU decision made after 2020 bind UK courts?",["No, but they may have regard","Yes, in all courts","Yes, in the Supreme Court only"],"<b>s.6 EUWA</b>: later CJEU decisions do not bind. References to the CJEU are possible only on limited Withdrawal Agreement matters."],[8,"Can an individual rely directly on the Trade and Cooperation Agreement in court?",["No, it has no direct effect","Yes, like an EU regulation","Yes, against public bodies only"],"The <b>TCA</b> is an ordinary treaty, with effect only via the <b>European Union (Future Relationship) Act 2020</b> (dualism)."],[9,"An Act conflicts with assimilated direct legislation. Which prevails?",["The Act prevails","The assimilated law prevails","The court picks the later one"],"Domestic law has priority, whenever made, since supremacy was abolished."],[10,"Which category was saved by s.3 EUWA 2018?",["Direct EU legislation, such as regulations","Only EU case law","Only EU treaties"],"<b>s.3</b> converted directly applicable instruments, mainly <b>EU regulations</b>, into UK law."],[11,"Which Act authorised the Prime Minister to give Article 50 notice?",["EU (Notification of Withdrawal) Act 2017","EU (Withdrawal) Act 2018","EU (Future Relationship) Act 2020"],"Following <b>Miller 1</b>, this Act was needed because the prerogative could not remove ECA 1972 rights."]],"c":[[393,"When was exit day, and what happened then?","<b>31 January 2020, 11pm</b>. The UK left the EU and the <b>ECA 1972 was repealed</b>. EU law continued during the <b>transition period</b>."],[394,"When was IP completion day, and what happened then?","<b>31 December 2020, 11pm</b>. EU law stopped applying, <b>retained EU law</b> took effect, and the <b>Trade and Cooperation Agreement</b> took effect."],[395,"What happened on 1 January 2024?","Retained EU law was <b>renamed assimilated law</b>. <b>Supremacy</b>, <b>general principles</b> and <b>s.4 EUWA rights</b> were abolished."],[396,"What happened on 23 June 2026?","The REUL Act's broad ministerial powers to <b>revoke or replace</b> (s.14) and <b>restate</b> (s.12) assimilated law <b>expired</b>."],[399,"What did s.6 EUWA provide on the CJEU?","UK courts are <b>not bound</b> by CJEU decisions made <b>after</b> IP completion day and <b>cannot refer</b> to it. They <b>may have regard</b> to later decisions. Pre-2021 decisions bind <b>lower courts</b>; the <b>Court of Appeal and Supreme Court</b> may depart."],[402,"Is EU law supreme in the UK today?","<b>No.</b> Supremacy does not apply to any enactment, whenever made, from <b>1 January 2024</b> (s.3 REUL Act). Exception: <b>Withdrawal Agreement</b> rights."],[405,"Can a claimant rely on the general principles of EU law?","<b>No.</b> From 2024 they are <b>abolished</b> (e.g. proportionality, legal certainty) and cannot be used to interpret or challenge assimilated law."]]}]},{"id":"ethics","title":"Ethics and Professional Conduct","stop":7,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M-90,-4 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 q11,-10 22,0 V4 H-90 Z\" fill=\"#4CC9F0\"/><rect x=\"-78\" y=\"-30\" width=\"7\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"-44\" y=\"-30\" width=\"7\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"-10\" y=\"-30\" width=\"7\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"24\" y=\"-30\" width=\"7\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"58\" y=\"-30\" width=\"7\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"-88\" y=\"-38\" width=\"176\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#C68B59\"/><path d=\"M-86,-58 H-14 M-86,-38 V-58 M-62,-38 V-58 M-38,-38 V-58 M-14,-38 V-58\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-80\" width=\"56\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"-72\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#E0F7FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M-10,-96 H58 V-82 Q49.5,-71 41,-82 Q32.5,-71 24,-82 Q15.5,-71 7,-82 Q-1.5,-71 -10,-82 Z\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"-10\" y=\"-94\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\" stroke=\"none\"/><rect x=\"24\" y=\"-94\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#FF8FC7\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M-10,-96 H58 V-82 Q49.5,-71 41,-82 Q32.5,-71 24,-82 Q15.5,-71 7,-82 Q-1.5,-71 -10,-82 Z\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"-58,-64 -38,-64 -48,-38\" fill=\"#E9B872\"/><path d=\"M-54,-58 l12,8 M-50,-62 l8,6\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"-54\" cy=\"-70\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#FF9EC4\"/><circle cx=\"-42\" cy=\"-70\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#95D5B2\"/><circle cx=\"-48\" cy=\"-81\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\"/><circle cx=\"-46\" cy=\"-91\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"principles-competence","title":"The SRA Principles, the Codes and the Statement of Solicitor Competence","q":[[0,"Outside work, which Principles most often catch a solicitor's conduct?",["Public trust, honesty and integrity","Independence and best interests","Equality and the rule of law"],"Private conduct matters only where it affects trust in the profession: mainly Principles <b>2, 4 and 5</b>."],[1,"What does the first stage of the Ivey test ask about?",["What the person actually knew or believed","What a reasonable solicitor would have known","Whether the client lost money"],"<b>Stage 1</b> is subjective: the person's actual knowledge or belief about the facts. <b>Stage 2</b> applies the standards of ordinary decent people."],[2,"What standard of proof does the SDT apply?",["Balance of probabilities","Beyond reasonable doubt","Reasonable suspicion"],"Since 25 November 2019 the SDT uses the <b>civil standard</b>: more likely than not."],[3,"What is the usual maximum SRA fine for an individual solicitor?",["£25,000","£10,000","£50,000"],"The SRA can fine up to <b>£25,000</b> (higher for ABS managers and unlimited for economic crime). Bigger cases go to the SDT."],[4,"Which body can strike a solicitor off the roll?",["The SDT","The Legal Ombudsman","The Law Society"],"The <b>Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal</b> can strike off, suspend and impose unlimited fines."],[5,"Can a solicitor lack integrity without being dishonest?",["Yes","No","Only if a client lost money"],"<b>Integrity</b> is wider than honesty: adherence to the ethical standards of the profession (Wingate v SRA; SRA v Malins)."],[6,"A partner tells a trainee to backdate an attendance note. What should the trainee do?",["Refuse and report it","Do it, as the partner is responsible","Do it but keep a copy"],"Following a senior's instruction is <b>no defence</b>. Backdating is dishonest, and the trainee should report it (para 7.7)."],[7,"Which case held that integrity is wider than honesty?",["Wingate v SRA; SRA v Malins","Ivey v Genting Casinos","Beckwith v SRA"],"In <b>Wingate / Malins</b> (2018) the Court of Appeal said integrity means adherence to the profession's ethical standards."],[8,"How many CPD hours must a solicitor complete each year?",["There is no set minimum","16 hours","25 hours"],"No hours target. Solicitors <b>reflect</b> on their practice, address learning needs and make an annual declaration."],[9,"Drafting a legally effective document falls under which competence area?",["Technical legal practice","Working with other people","Managing themselves and their work"],"Drafting is <b>B4</b>, within technical legal practice."],[10,"Principle 7 (client's best interests) clashes with Principle 1 (rule of law). Which prevails?",["Principle 1","Principle 7","Whichever the client prefers"],"Principles protecting the <b>wider public interest</b> take precedence over the client's interests."],[11,"When did the SRA Standards and Regulations come into force?",["25 November 2019","6 October 2011","1 October 2023"],"The principles-based <b>SRA Standards and Regulations</b> replaced the old Handbook on <b>25 November 2019</b>."]],"c":[[5,"What are the seven SRA Principles?","You act: 1 upholding the <b>rule of law</b> and <b>proper administration of justice</b>; 2 upholding <b>public trust and confidence</b>; 3 with <b>independence</b>; 4 with <b>honesty</b>; 5 with <b>integrity</b>; 6 encouraging <b>equality, diversity and inclusion</b>; 7 in the <b>best interests of each client</b>."],[6,"Do the Principles apply outside practice?","<b>Yes</b>, but only so far as conduct affects <b>public trust</b> in you and the profession. In practice this means mainly <b>Principles 2, 4 and 5</b>."],[7,"Which Principles win when they conflict?","Those safeguarding the <b>wider public interest</b> (e.g. <b>rule of law</b>, <b>public confidence</b>) <b>take precedence over</b> an individual client's interests. So <b>Principle 7</b> gives way to <b>1, 2, 4 and 5</b>."],[8,"Does acting in the client's best interests mean doing what the client says?","<b>No.</b> <b>Independence</b> (Principle 3) means giving your own professional judgement, even if unwelcome."],[10,"What is the test for dishonesty?","<b>Stage 1 (subjective)</b>: what did the person <b>actually know or believe</b> about the facts? <b>Stage 2 (objective)</b>: was the conduct dishonest by the standards of <b>ordinary decent people</b>? (Ivey v Genting)"],[13,"What is integrity, and how does it differ from honesty?","Adherence to the <b>ethical standards of the profession</b>. It is <b>wider</b> than honesty: a solicitor can <b>lack integrity without being dishonest</b>, e.g. <b>recklessly</b> making misleading statements or taking <b>unfair advantage</b>."],[15,"Is an honest mistake a breach of integrity?","<b>Usually not.</b> An honest, good-faith mistake is normally a <b>competence</b> issue (para 3.2)."],[20,"What sanctions can the SRA itself impose?","A <b>rebuke</b>. A <b>fine</b> up to <b>£25,000</b> for individuals and traditional firms (<b>unlimited</b> for <b>economic crime</b> matters). <b>Conditions</b> on a practising certificate or authorisation. Letters of advice and warnings."],[21,"What can the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal do?","<b>Strike off</b>, <b>suspend</b>, impose an <b>unlimited fine</b>, make restriction orders and order costs. Appeals go to the <b>High Court</b>."]]},{"id":"trust-fairness-undertakings","title":"Maintaining trust, acting fairly and undertakings","q":[[0,"A solicitor threatens an unrepresented neighbour with prison over a civil debt. Which para is breached?",["Para 1.2","Para 1.3","Para 1.5"],"<b>Para 1.2</b>: do not take unfair advantage of others. Imprisonment is not available for a civil debt."],[1,"A solicitor tells another solicitor by phone \"I'll send the deeds tomorrow\". Is that an undertaking?",["Yes, if the other relies on it","No, it is not in writing","No, the word \"undertake\" was not used"],"An undertaking can be oral, needs no special wording, and must be given to someone who <b>reasonably relies</b> on it."],[2,"The seller tells their solicitor not to redeem the mortgage as promised. Is the solicitor still bound?",["Yes","No, the client's instructions prevail","Only if the buyer's solicitor insists"],"The <b>client cannot release</b> an undertaking. Only the recipient can."],[3,"A solicitor gives an undertaking \"on behalf of my client\". Who is bound?",["The solicitor personally","Only the client","Nobody"],"The solicitor stays <b>personally bound</b> unless the wording clearly excludes personal liability."],[4,"An undertaking's wording is ambiguous. How is it usually read?",["In favour of the recipient","In favour of the giver","In favour of the client"],"The giver chose the words, so ambiguity is read <b>against the giver</b>."],[5,"How can a recipient enforce an undertaking given by a solicitor?",["Through the High Court's supervisory jurisdiction","By claiming compensation from the SRA","By a complaint to the Legal Ombudsman"],"Solicitors are <b>officers of the court</b>, so the court can order performance or compensation. The SRA cannot award compensation."],[6,"Can the court's supervisory jurisdiction enforce an undertaking from an unregulated person?",["No","Yes, if in writing","Yes, if it is a company"],"The jurisdiction covers <b>solicitors</b>. Be careful before relying on a non-solicitor's undertaking."],[7,"A trainee with authority gives an undertaking. Who does it bind?",["The firm","Nobody until a partner signs","The client only"],"An undertaking given by staff with authority <b>binds the firm</b> as well."],[8,"An undertaking was \"subject to receipt of funds from the bank\". The funds never arrive. Is the solicitor bound?",["No, the condition was not met","Yes, regardless","Yes, if the client agrees"],"A clear <b>condition</b> limits the promise. Always spell out what, by when and on what conditions."],[9,"Which undertakings should a solicitor be willing to give?",["Only those within their control","Any the client asks for","Only oral ones"],"Because only the recipient can release you, only undertake what <b>you can perform</b>."],[10,"A solicitor knows the client's claim has weaknesses. Must they tell the other side?",["No, provided nothing said is misleading","Yes, always","Yes, if asked informally"],"Para 1.4 bans <b>misleading</b>, not silence. There is no duty to volunteer weaknesses."],[11,"A solicitor lets their client mislead the other side. Which para is breached?",["Para 1.4","Para 1.3","Para 1.1"],"<b>Para 1.4</b>: do not mislead, including by being complicit in others' acts, such as the client's."]],"c":[[27,"What are examples of a solicitor taking unfair advantage of clients or others under para 1.2?","Exploiting an <b>unrepresented</b> person's lack of legal knowledge. Demanding sums <b>not legally due</b>. <b>Threatening criminal proceedings</b> to extract a civil payment. Oppressive <b>NDA</b> terms."],[30,"What does para 1.4 prohibit?","<b>Misleading</b> or attempting to mislead clients, the court or others, by your own acts or <b>omissions</b>, or by being <b>complicit</b> in others' (including your client's)."],[31,"Can a half-truth or silence breach para 1.4?","<b>Yes</b>: a <b>half-truth</b>, a deliberate <b>omission</b> or <b>standing by</b> while the client misleads. But there is <b>no duty to volunteer</b> weaknesses in your client's case, provided nothing said is misleading."],[33,"Writing to an unrepresented opponent: what is allowed?","You may firmly assert the client's <b>genuine legal position</b>. You must <b>not</b> exaggerate the law, threaten unavailable consequences, or exploit their ignorance. You need not advise them, but nothing may mislead."],[34,"What is an undertaking (SRA Glossary)?","A <b>statement</b>, <b>oral or written</b>, whether or not it uses the word \"undertake\", to someone who <b>reasonably relies</b> on it, that <b>you or a third party will do something</b> or <b>refrain</b> from doing something."],[38,"Is an undertaking given \"on behalf of my client\" personally binding?","<b>Yes</b>, unless personal liability is <b>clearly excluded</b> in the wording at the time."],[39,"Can the client release a solicitor from an undertaking?","<b>No.</b> It must be performed even if the client <b>withdraws instructions</b> or it becomes <b>costly</b> or difficult. Only the <b>recipient</b> can agree to vary or release it."],[41,"How is an undertaking enforced?","As <b>conduct</b>: breach is misconduct under para 1.3, for the SRA or SDT. Through the <b>court</b>: solicitors are <b>officers of the court</b>, so the High Court can order performance or compensation without proof of a contract."],[44,"Is a conditional undertaking binding if the condition fails?","<b>No.</b> E.g. \"subject to receipt of funds from X\" does not bind if the funds never arrive."],[45,"Seller's solicitor gave a mortgage redemption undertaking; the seller now wants the proceeds paid to him. What should she do?","<b>Refuse and redeem the mortgage.</b> The undertaking binds her and only the <b>buyer's solicitor</b> could release it. Paying the seller would also breach the <b>Accounts Rules</b>."]]},{"id":"duty-to-court","title":"Duty to the court and dispute resolution","q":[[0,"The client admits guilt but pleads not guilty. May the solicitor suggest someone else did it?",["No","Yes, if evidence is thin","Yes, with the court's permission"],"You can put the prosecution to proof, but must not assert innocence or a <b>false positive case</b>."],[1,"The client privately admits theft. May the solicitor submit no case to answer?",["Yes","No, they must withdraw","Only with the client's consent"],"Testing the prosecution case and a <b>no case to answer</b> submission are allowed. Asserting innocence is not."],[2,"The client insists on giving false evidence. What must the solicitor do?",["Cease to act","Call them anyway","Tell the court the client lied"],"You cannot mislead the court. If the client insists, <b>withdraw</b>."],[3,"A solicitor withdraws because the client wants to mislead the court. What is the court told?",["Professional reasons only","The client lied","Nothing, the solicitor just leaves"],"The reason is <b>confidential</b> (para 6.3), so only \"professional reasons\" is given."],[4,"A solicitor discovers the client has already misled the court. What comes first?",["Ask the client's consent to correct it","Tell the court at once","Carry on and say nothing"],"Ask for <b>consent to correct</b>. If refused, the solicitor must withdraw (para 1.4)."],[5,"Must a solicitor tell the court about the client's previous convictions?",["No, but cannot claim good character","Yes, always","Yes, if asked by the client"],"There is <b>no duty</b> to volunteer them, but you cannot assert good character or exploit a mistake."],[6,"Which payment to a witness is permitted?",["Reasonable travel expenses","A bonus if the client wins","A fee for helpful evidence"],"Reasonable <b>expenses and lost earnings</b> are fine. Benefits tied to the evidence or outcome breach para 2.3."],[7,"The other side misses a binding case against the client. Must the solicitor cite it?",["Yes, if it is material","No, it is the opponent's job","Only if the judge asks"],"<b>Para 2.7</b>: draw the court's attention to relevant law, even if adverse, where it is likely to affect the outcome."],[8,"The prosecution is unaware of a damaging fact. Must the defence solicitor reveal it?",["No","Yes, to the court","Yes, to the prosecution"],"The duty to cite adverse authority is about <b>law</b>, not facts. You must just not mislead."],[9,"Which is allowed when preparing a witness?",["Explaining court procedure","Rehearsing their answers","Discussing other witnesses' evidence"],"You may explain procedure but must not <b>coach</b> or influence the substance of evidence (para 2.2)."],[10,"A solicitor thinks an argument will probably lose but it is arguable. May it be run?",["Yes","No","Only with the client's written consent"],"<b>Para 2.4</b>: assertions must be properly arguable, not certain to win."],[11,"A witness has given a statement to the other side. May the solicitor interview them?",["Yes","No, there is property in a witness","Only with the opponent's consent"],"There is <b>no property in a witness</b>. In criminal cases, good practice is to tell the prosecution first."]],"c":[[48,"Which duty wins if the duty to the court and the duty to the client clash?","The <b>duty to the court</b>. But <b>confidentiality</b> still applies, so the solution is usually to <b>withdraw</b> without saying why."],[50,"What does para 2.3 prohibit, and what payments are allowed?","No <b>benefit to a witness</b> dependent on their <b>evidence</b> or the <b>outcome</b>. You may pay <b>reasonable expenses</b>, compensation for <b>loss of time</b>, and an expert's <b>proper fee</b>."],[52,"What does para 2.4 require?","Only make assertions or submissions that are <b>properly arguable</b>. You can run an argument likely to lose, but not assert a fact you <b>know is false</b> or allege <b>fraud</b> without a proper basis."],[55,"What does para 2.7 require?","Draw the court's attention to <b>relevant cases and statutory provisions</b>, or <b>procedural irregularities</b>, likely to have a <b>material effect</b> on the outcome. This applies even if they are <b>against your client</b> and the opponent missed them."],[56,"Must a solicitor reveal damaging facts to the court?","<b>No.</b> The para 2.7 duty is about <b>law and procedure</b>, not <b>facts</b>. But you must never <b>mislead</b>."],[58,"Can you act for a client who admits guilt but pleads not guilty?","<b>Yes.</b> Everyone may require the prosecution to <b>prove its case</b>. You can continue on a limited basis."],[60,"What can you not do for a client who has admitted guilt?","Assert or suggest they <b>did not commit</b> it. Suggest <b>someone else</b> did. Put forward an <b>alibi</b> or other false positive defence. <b>Call the client</b> to deny the offence."],[61,"What if the client who admitted guilt insists on giving evidence denying it?","You must <b>withdraw</b>."],[64,"You discover the client has already misled the court. What must you do?","Ask for the client's <b>consent to correct</b> it. If they refuse, <b>withdraw</b>, or you would be complicit (para 1.4)."],[66,"The prosecution wrongly says the client has no previous convictions. What may you do?","You need <b>not correct</b> it. But you must <b>not take advantage</b>: no assertion of <b>good character</b> and no reliance on the mistake in mitigation."],[70,"What do you tell the court when withdrawing?","Only that you are withdrawing for <b>professional reasons</b> (\"professionally embarrassed\"). <b>Never</b> the reason, which is confidential."]]},{"id":"instructions-capacity-service","title":"Taking instructions, capacity, authority and service","q":[[0,"A son wants his 88-year-old mother's house sold, with proceeds to him. What should the firm do?",["See the mother alone","Act on the son's instructions","Refuse because of her age"],"Check her instructions, capacity and freedom from <b>undue influence</b>, ideally alone, where the third party benefits."],[1,"Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, what is the starting point?",["The adult is presumed to have capacity","Older adults lack capacity","Capacity needs a medical report"],"<b>Presumption of capacity</b> (s.1). A medical opinion is needed only if there is real doubt."],[2,"A client makes an unwise decision. Does that show a lack of capacity?",["No","Yes","Only if it loses money"],"Under s.1(4) an <b>unwise decision</b> alone does not mean lack of capacity."],[3,"How is capacity assessed?",["For each decision, at the time it is made","Once, for all matters","By a doctor only"],"Capacity is <b>decision-specific and time-specific</b>. A diagnosis such as dementia does not settle it."],[4,"Which shows a person cannot make a decision under the MCA?",["They cannot retain the information","They cannot afford the fees","They cannot attend in person"],"The s.3 test: unable to <b>understand, retain, use or weigh</b>, or <b>communicate</b> the relevant information."],[5,"With which body must a lasting power of attorney be registered before use?",["Office of the Public Guardian","Land Registry","Court of Protection"],"An LPA must be registered with the <b>Office of the Public Guardian</b>."],[6,"A client loses capacity mid-retainer and has no attorney or deputy. What happens to the solicitor's authority?",["It ends","It continues unchanged","It passes to the nearest relative"],"The retainer depends on a client who can instruct. Steps such as a <b>Court of Protection</b> application may be needed."],[7,"Who appoints a deputy for someone who lacks capacity?",["The Court of Protection","The Law Society","The nearest relative"],"A <b>deputy</b> is appointed by the Court of Protection."],[8,"A client refuses to pay money on account as agreed. May the solicitor stop acting?",["Yes, on reasonable notice","No, never","Yes, immediately without notice"],"Good reason plus <b>reasonable notice</b>. Do not leave the client stranded just before a hearing or limitation date."],[9,"When may a client end the retainer?",["At any time","Only after the matter ends","Only with the firm's consent"],"The client may end it <b>at any time</b>, though may still owe fees for work done."],[10,"Unpaid bill: holding the file would stop a trial going ahead. Should the solicitor rely on the lien?",["No, it would seriously prejudice the client","Yes, the lien is absolute","Yes, if the client is told"],"A <b>lien</b> should not be used where it seriously prejudices the client or obstructs court proceedings."],[11,"A partner delegates work to a trainee. Who stays accountable for it?",["The supervising partner","Only the trainee","Nobody, once delegated"],"<b>Para 3.5</b>: supervisors remain accountable for work done through others and must supervise effectively."]],"c":[[76,"Who is the client when you act for a company?","The <b>company</b>. You take instructions from an <b>authorised</b> director or employee, but that individual is not the client (para 8.1)."],[78,"Can you accept instructions passed on by a third party?","<b>Yes</b>, but you must <b>check directly with the client</b> that they are genuine and the third party is authorised. With an elderly or vulnerable client, <b>see the client alone</b>."],[83,"What are the key capacity principles in s.1 MCA 2005?","Adults are <b>presumed to have capacity</b>. <b>All practicable steps</b> to help must be taken first. An <b>unwise decision</b> does not show incapacity. Acts for a person lacking capacity must be in their <b>best interests</b> and <b>least restrictive</b>."],[84,"What is the s.2 diagnostic test for lack of capacity?","At the material time, the person cannot make the decision because of an <b>impairment of, or disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or brain</b>."],[85,"When is a person unable to make a decision (s.3)?","If they cannot <b>understand</b> the relevant information, <b>retain</b> it, <b>use or weigh</b> it, or <b>communicate</b> the decision by any means."],[90,"Who can instruct you if the client lacks capacity?","An <b>attorney</b> under a <b>registered</b> lasting power of attorney, within its scope. A <b>deputy</b> appointed by the <b>Court of Protection</b>. Or an attorney under a registered <b>enduring power</b> made before 1 October 2007."],[94,"When must you refuse instructions or stop acting?","If acting would <b>break the law or SRA rules</b>. If you lack <b>competence or resources</b>. If there is a <b>conflict</b> or you hold an opponent's <b>confidential information</b>. If there is <b>duress or undue influence</b>. If the client lacks <b>capacity</b> and no one can instruct."],[96,"How can the solicitor end the retainer?","Only for <b>good reason</b> and on <b>reasonable notice</b>."],[97,"What are good reasons for a solicitor to end the retainer?","Failure to pay <b>money on account</b> as agreed. <b>Breakdown of trust and confidence</b>. The client insists on a <b>breach of the rules</b>. A <b>conflict</b> arises. You cannot get proper instructions."],[103,"What should you do if asked to do work you are not competent to do?","Get <b>help</b> or <b>supervision</b>, <b>refer</b> the client, or <b>decline</b>. Doing it unsupervised breaches <b>para 3.2</b>."]]},{"id":"client-money","title":"Client money and assets","q":[[0,"A client pays £5,000 on account of costs before any bill. What is it?",["Client money","Firm money","Disbursement income"],"Money for costs received <b>before a bill</b> is delivered is client money."],[1,"A client pays a bill already delivered for the firm's fees. What is it?",["Firm money","Client money","Trust money"],"Payment of a <b>delivered bill</b> for fees is the firm's own money."],[2,"A client wants their salary and household bills run through the firm's client account. What should the firm do?",["Refuse","Agree if the client signs","Agree for three months only"],"That is a <b>banking facility</b>, which the Accounts Rules prohibit. Payments must relate to regulated services."],[3,"What must the firm give before taking costs from client money?",["A bill or written notification of costs","Nothing once work is done","A verbal warning"],"Costs can be taken only after a <b>bill or other written notification</b> has been given."],[4,"Client A's account is short. May the firm use client B's money to cover it?",["No","Yes, if repaid within a week","Yes, if both are long-term clients"],"Withdrawals need <b>sufficient funds for that specific client</b>. Using another client's money creates a shortfall."],[5,"Where must client money be held?",["A client account in the firm's name","A partner's personal account","The firm's office account"],"A <b>client account</b> at a bank or building society in England and Wales, in the firm's name."],[6,"A solicitor earns commission from an insurer on a policy arranged for a client. What follows?",["Account to the client unless they agree otherwise","The solicitor keeps it","Pay it to the SRA"],"<b>Para 4.1</b>: account to clients for any financial benefit from their instructions, unless they have agreed otherwise."],[7,"Which firm can usually avoid an annual accountant's report?",["Average client money £10,000 or less, max £250,000","Any firm with fewer than ten staff","Any firm with a COFA"],"The exemption applies where average balance is up to <b>£10,000</b> and maximum up to <b>£250,000</b> (or all money is from the LAA)."],[8,"Must a firm pay interest on client money?",["A fair sum, unless agreed otherwise in writing","Never","Only if over £1 million"],"The Accounts Rules require a <b>fair sum</b> of interest under a written policy."],[9,"A payment is part client money, part firm money. What must the firm do?",["Pay it in, then move the firm's share promptly","Keep it all in office account","Hold all of it as client money indefinitely"],"Mixed payments are handled so the <b>firm's share is moved out promptly</b>, keeping accounts separate."],[10,"Money received by a solicitor as attorney under a power of attorney is what?",["Client money","Firm money","Disbursement money"],"Money held as <b>trustee or office holder</b> (attorney, deputy) falls within client money."],[11,"A sale completes and £2,000 remains with no reason to hold it. What must the firm do?",["Return it promptly","Keep it for future costs","Move it to office account"],"Client money must be <b>returned promptly</b> once there is no proper reason to hold it."]],"c":[[105,"What does para 4.1 require about commissions?","A financial benefit received because of the client's instructions (e.g. a <b>commission</b>) must be <b>accounted to the client</b>, unless the client has <b>agreed otherwise</b> with proper information."],[108,"What is client money (rule 2.1 Accounts Rules)?","Money for <b>regulated services</b> to a client. Money held for a <b>third party</b>. Money held as <b>trustee</b> or office holder (e.g. <b>attorney</b>, <b>deputy</b>). Money for <b>costs</b> and <b>unpaid disbursements</b> received <b>before a bill</b> or written notification."],[110,"Is payment of a delivered bill that includes an unpaid disbursement client money?","<b>No</b>, it is <b>firm money</b>. But the firm must pay the third party <b>promptly</b>."],[115,"When can a firm take its costs from client money?","Only after giving the client a <b>bill or other written notification</b> of costs. Finishing the work is <b>not enough</b>."],[116,"Can one client's money be used for another?","<b>No.</b> Only withdraw for a client if you hold <b>sufficient funds for that client</b>. Otherwise you create a <b>shortfall</b>."],[117,"When must client money be returned?","<b>Promptly</b> once there is no longer any <b>proper reason</b> to hold it."],[119,"What is the banking facility ban (rule 3.3)?","Do not use client account to provide <b>banking facilities</b>. Payments in and out must relate to the <b>regulated services</b> you deliver. E.g. refuse to hold sale proceeds to pay <b>household bills</b> or receive a client's <b>salary</b>."],[121,"What if client money is improperly withdrawn by an innocent mistake?","<b>Correct breaches promptly</b>. Replace the money <b>immediately</b> from the <b>firm's own money</b>, whether or not anyone was at fault."],[122,"How often must client accounts be reconciled?","At least every <b>five weeks</b> (rule 8.3). The bank statement, cash book and client ledgers must agree."],[123,"When is an accountant's report needed?","Annually, within <b>six months</b> of the end of the accounting period (rule 12.1). It is exempt if all client money comes from the <b>Legal Aid Agency</b>, or average balance is not over <b>£10,000</b> and maximum not over <b>£250,000</b>."]]},{"id":"business-referrals-publicity","title":"Business requirements: referrals, fee sharing, introducers and publicity","q":[[0,"Can client consent make a personal injury referral fee lawful?",["No","Yes, if in writing","Yes, if the fee is under £500"],"<b>LASPO 2012 s.56</b> bans PI referral fees outright. Consent cannot cure a statutory ban."],[1,"An estate agent wants £150 per conveyancing client it refers. May the firm agree?",["Yes, if disclosed and independent","No, never","Only if the client pays it"],"Allowed outside PI and criminal work if clients are told, fee sharing is in writing and <b>independence</b> is kept."],[2,"What must a client be told about a referral?",["Any financial interest in the referral","Nothing if the fee is small","Only the introducer's name"],"<b>Para 5.1</b>: tell clients of any financial or other interest you or an introducer has in the referral."],[3,"A firm pays an introducer for a client facing criminal proceedings. Is that allowed?",["No","Yes, if disclosed","Yes, if in writing"],"<b>Para 5.1(d)</b> bans payments to or from introducers for clients facing criminal proceedings."],[4,"Before referring a client to a separate unregulated business, what is needed?",["The client's informed consent","The SRA's approval","Nothing, if the business is reputable"],"<b>Para 5.3</b>: informed consent, including that the SRA's protections do not apply to that business."],[5,"A solicitor runs a separate unregulated business. What do its clients lose?",["Compensation Fund, PII and LeO","Only compulsory PII","Only the Legal Ombudsman"],"The <b>Compensation Fund</b>, compulsory <b>PII</b> and <b>LeO</b> do not cover an unregulated business, and clients must be told (para 5.3)."],[6,"Which solicitor must deliver an annual return to the SRA?",["One holding a practising certificate","Only sole practitioners","Only partners"],"<b>Para 5.5</b>: those with a practising certificate or registration must deliver an annual return."],[7,"Publicity about charges must be what?",["Accurate and not misleading","Approved by the SRA","Fixed for a year"],"<b>Para 8.8</b>: say clearly whether prices include VAT and disbursements, and avoid misleading claims."],[8,"Which marketing is permitted?",["A general online advert","Cold-calling accident victims","A letter to people picked as likely claimants"],"<b>Para 8.9</b> bans targeted unsolicited approaches to the public, but general advertising is fine."],[9,"A marketing company cold-calls accident victims for a firm. Which para is engaged?",["Para 5.1(e), via para 8.9","Para 5.5","Para 8.2"],"Para <b>5.1(e)</b>: you cannot use an introducer to do what you could not do, such as unsolicited approaches."],[10,"An estate agent introducer wants the conveyancer to downplay a bad survey. Which Principle is most engaged?",["Independence","Equality and diversity","Rule of law"],"<b>Principle 3</b>: nobody, including an introducer, may compromise your professional judgement."],[11,"A payment looks like a referral fee. Who must show it is not?",["The solicitor","The SRA","The client"],"<b>Para 5.2</b>: it is treated as a referral fee unless you show otherwise, for example a genuine marketing fee."]],"c":[[130,"What must clients be told under para 5.1(a) and (b)?","Any <b>financial or other interest</b> you have in referring them, or an <b>introducer</b> has in referring them to you. Any relevant <b>fee sharing arrangement</b>."],[132,"Are referral payments allowed in criminal matters?","<b>No.</b> No payments to or from introducers for clients subject to <b>criminal proceedings</b> (para 5.1(d))."],[135,"Are referral fees allowed in personal injury claims?","<b>No.</b> Paying and receiving are <b>banned</b> in <b>personal injury or death</b> claims by <b>LASPO 2012 s.56</b>. Client <b>consent cannot cure</b> the ban."],[136,"When are referral fees allowed?","In other matters (e.g. <b>conveyancing</b>, wills, family) if <b>disclosed</b>, <b>in writing</b> where fees are shared, and <b>independence</b> is preserved."],[138,"When can you refer a client to a separate business (para 5.3)?","Only with the client's <b>informed consent</b>, including understanding that it is <b>not regulated by the SRA</b>, so SRA protections (<b>Compensation Fund</b>, <b>indemnity insurance</b>, <b>Legal Ombudsman</b>) do not apply."],[142,"What does para 8.8 require of publicity?","<b>Accurate</b>, <b>not misleading</b> information, including about <b>charges</b> and when <b>interest</b> is payable."],[144,"What does para 8.9 prohibit?","<b>Unsolicited approaches</b> to <b>members of the public</b> to advertise legal services. Exception: <b>current or former clients</b>."],[145,"Which approaches breach para 8.9?","<b>Cold calling</b>, <b>door-stepping</b>, approaching people at an <b>accident scene</b> or in <b>hospital</b>, unsolicited <b>targeted texts</b>."],[147,"Can you use an introducer who cold-calls for you?","<b>No.</b> Para <b>5.1(e)</b> stops you getting round para 8.9 that way."]]},{"id":"conflicts-of-interest","title":"Conflict of interests: own interest and client conflicts","q":[[0,"A client wants to sell their car to the solicitor and asks them to act. What conflict is this?",["Own interest conflict","Client conflict","No conflict"],"The solicitor's interest in the price conflicts with the duty to the client, so they <b>cannot act</b> (para 6.1)."],[1,"A client gives the solicitor a box of chocolates at Christmas. Is that a problem?",["No, trivial gifts are fine","Yes, own interest conflict","Yes, it must be reported to the SRA"],"Only <b>significant</b> gifts create an own interest conflict."],[2,"A solicitor realises their mistake may give the client a claim. What is the conflict?",["Own interest conflict","Client conflict","None, if the client agrees"],"The solicitor's interest in avoiding a claim conflicts with the client's. Advise <b>independent legal advice</b>."],[3,"One firm acts for both the seller and the buyer of a business. Is there a usable exception?",["No, their interests are opposed","Yes, with written consent","Yes, if they are friends"],"Buyer and seller negotiate price and terms. Neither exception applies, so the firm <b>cannot act</b> for both."],[4,"Which pair may fit the substantially common interest exception?",["Co-founders setting up a company on agreed terms","A landlord and tenant negotiating rent","A claimant and defendant"],"A <b>common purpose</b> and strong consensus on how to achieve it, with any conflict only peripheral."],[5,"Two clients consent orally to the firm acting. Can it rely on that?",["No, consent must be given or evidenced in writing","Yes","Yes, if a partner approves"],"<b>Para 6.2(i)</b>: informed consent, given or evidenced <b>in writing</b>, plus safeguards and reasonableness."],[6,"Two clients tender for the same contract. May one firm act for both?",["Yes, with written consent, safeguards and if reasonable","No, never","Yes, with oral consent"],"They are <b>competing for the same objective</b> (a tender). All three conditions must still be met."],[7,"Buyer and lender, standard mortgage terms. Is there usually a conflict?",["No","Yes, always","Yes, unless the buyer consents"],"With the lender's standard terms and usual instructions, acting for both is usually fine. Negotiated terms change that."],[8,"Joint buyers fall out mid-matter. What should the firm usually do?",["Stop acting for both","Carry on for both","Choose the one who paid most"],"Each holds confidential information, so the firm usually <b>stops acting for both</b>."],[9,"A company's director asks the firm for advice on a personal guarantee. What is sensible?",["Advise separate advice for the director","Act for both without more","Refuse to act for the company"],"The company and director may want different terms. A conflict is likely, so the director should have <b>separate advice</b>."],[10,"A solicitor holds significant shares in the opponent. Can client consent allow them to act?",["No","Yes, if in writing","Yes, with an information barrier"],"There are <b>no exceptions</b> to para 6.1, so consent and barriers are irrelevant."],[11,"When must conflicts be considered?",["At the start and throughout the matter","Only at the start","Only if a client complains"],"A <b>significant risk</b> of conflict can arise at any stage, so keep it under review."]],"c":[[149,"What is an own interest conflict?","Where your duty to act in a client's best interests <b>conflicts, or there is a significant risk</b> it may conflict, with <b>your own interests</b> in that or a related matter. Your interests include your <b>firm's</b> and those of people <b>close to you</b>."],[150,"What is a client conflict?","Where your separate duties to <b>two or more clients</b> in the <b>same or related matters</b> conflict, or there is a <b>significant risk</b> they may."],[153,"Can a client consent to you acting despite an own interest conflict?","<b>No.</b> Para <b>6.1</b> has <b>no exceptions</b>, and consent does <b>not</b> cure it."],[154,"What situations commonly give rise to an own interest conflict?","The client buying from or selling to you, your <b>family</b> or firm. A <b>financial interest</b> in the matter. <b>Loans</b> to or from the client. A <b>mistake</b> giving the client a <b>claim against you</b>. A <b>significant gift</b>. A <b>personal relationship</b>. <b>Commission</b> depending on your advice."],[155,"A client wants to leave you a significant gift in her will. What should you do?","Advise her to take <b>independent legal advice</b>. If she <b>refuses</b>, <b>do not act</b> in preparing the will."],[158,"When can you act despite a client conflict (para 6.2)?","Only if the clients have a <b>substantially common interest</b> or are <b>competing for the same objective</b>, <b>and</b> all three conditions are met."],[159,"What are the three conditions in para 6.2?","1: All clients give <b>informed consent</b>, given or evidenced <b>in writing</b>. 2: <b>Effective safeguards</b> for confidential information where appropriate. 3: You are satisfied it is <b>reasonable</b> to act for all."],[160,"What is a substantially common interest?","A clear <b>common purpose</b> and a <b>strong consensus</b> on how to achieve it; any conflict is peripheral. E.g. co-founders setting up a company on <b>agreed terms</b>, or spouses buying a house together."],[161,"What is \"competing for the same objective\"?","Clients competing for an <b>asset, contract or business opportunity</b> which, if one gets it, is <b>unattainable</b> to the others, sought through an <b>insolvency</b> process, an <b>auction or tender</b>, or a <b>non-public bid or offer</b>. E.g. <b>two bidders</b> in a sealed-bid auction for the same business."],[162,"What factors affect whether it is reasonable to act for both clients?","Is one client <b>vulnerable</b>, much less sophisticated or in a far <b>weaker bargaining position</b>? Can you give each the advice they need? Is the matter likely to become <b>contentious</b>? If so, you cannot act for both."],[164,"What if a conflict becomes real during the matter?","You must usually <b>stop acting</b> for at least one client, and often <b>all</b>, because you hold each client's confidential information."]]},{"id":"confidentiality-disclosure","title":"Confidentiality and disclosure","q":[[0,"Does the duty of confidentiality end when the retainer ends?",["No, it continues","Yes","Only after six years"],"The duty to <b>current and former clients</b> continues after the retainer ends."],[1,"After a client dies, who can waive confidentiality?",["Their personal representatives","Their solicitor","Any beneficiary"],"The right to waive passes to the client's <b>personal representatives</b>."],[2,"Legal advice privilege protects which communications?",["Lawyer-client communications for legal advice","All communications with third parties","Any document in the file"],"It covers confidential lawyer-client communications made for the <b>dominant purpose</b> of legal advice."],[3,"Litigation privilege can cover communications with a third party if what is true?",["Litigation is in progress or reasonably in prospect","The third party is a lawyer","The client has consented"],"It needs litigation in progress or <b>reasonably in prospect</b>, and a dominant purpose of that litigation."],[4,"A client asks for advice on hiding assets from creditors fraudulently. Is it privileged?",["No, the crime/fraud exception applies","Yes","Yes, if the advice is in writing"],"There is <b>no privilege</b> for communications made to further a crime or fraud."],[5,"Which disclosure may be permitted despite confidentiality?",["To prevent a crime likely to cause serious bodily harm","To help a friend of the client","To a journalist"],"Exceptional cases only, disclosing only what is <b>necessary</b> to the appropriate authority."],[6,"The other side's solicitor emails you their privileged advice by mistake. What is good practice?",["Stop reading, tell them and return it","Read it and use it","Send it to your client"],"<b>Para 6.4(d)</b> means you need not tell your client its contents. Do not use it."],[7,"A solicitor has made a suspicious activity report. Why may they not tell the client?",["Tipping off may prejudice an investigation","Client confidentiality","The client is not entitled to advice"],"<b>Tipping off</b> under POCA is an exception to the duty of disclosure (para 6.4(a))."],[8,"A firm knows from a lender client that a seller will go bankrupt, which matters to its buyer client. What follows?",["It probably cannot act for the buyer","It must tell the buyer","It must tell the seller"],"Confidentiality to the lender wins over disclosure to the buyer, so the firm will usually <b>stop acting</b> unless the lender consents."],[9,"A firm holds no material confidential information about a former client. May it act against them?",["Yes","No, never","Only with the former client's consent"],"<b>Para 6.5</b> bites only where you hold <b>material</b> confidential information."],[10,"In Prince Jefri Bolkiah v KPMG, who had to show there was no real risk of disclosure?",["The firm","The former client","The court"],"The burden on the firm is <b>heavy</b>. Barriers work best if set up in advance, not hastily."],[11,"Is the fact that someone is a client confidential?",["Yes","No","Only for companies"],"Confidentiality covers all information about the client's affairs, <b>including that they are a client</b>."]],"c":[[172,"What does para 6.3 require?","Keep the affairs of <b>current and former clients</b> confidential unless disclosure is <b>required or permitted by law</b> or the <b>client consents</b>."],[174,"Does confidentiality end with the retainer or the client's death?","<b>No.</b> It continues after both. After death, the right to waive passes to the <b>personal representatives</b>."],[176,"How does confidentiality differ from legal professional privilege?","<b>Confidentiality</b> is a conduct and equitable duty covering <b>all</b> information, and the <b>law can override it</b>. <b>LPP</b> is a <b>legal right</b> to refuse disclosure, even to a court, covering only privileged communications. It is overridden very rarely, mainly by the <b>crime/fraud exception</b>."],[177,"What is legal advice privilege?","Confidential communications between <b>lawyer and client</b> made for the dominant purpose of giving or receiving <b>legal advice</b>, whether or not litigation is in view."],[178,"What is litigation privilege?","Confidential communications between lawyer or client and <b>third parties</b> (e.g. experts), made when litigation is <b>in progress or reasonably in prospect</b>, for the <b>dominant purpose</b> of that litigation."],[180,"When is disclosure required by law?","A <b>court order</b> or witness summons (subject to privilege). A <b>SAR</b> to the <b>National Crime Agency</b> under <b>POCA 2002</b> or the <b>Terrorism Act 2000</b>. Statutory requests (e.g. HMRC, the SRA)."],[181,"When is disclosure permitted in exceptional cases?","To prevent a <b>criminal act</b> reasonably believed likely to cause <b>serious bodily harm</b>. To protect a <b>child or vulnerable adult</b> from abuse. Disclose only what is <b>necessary</b>, to the appropriate authority."],[184,"What are the exceptions to the duty of disclosure in para 6.4?","(a) <b>Legal restrictions</b> for national security or crime prevention (e.g. <b>tipping off</b>). (b) The client's <b>written informed consent</b> to non-disclosure. (c) Risk of <b>serious physical or mental injury</b>. (d) A <b>privileged document</b> you know of only through <b>mistaken disclosure</b>."],[187,"When can you not act against a current or former client (para 6.5)?","Where you hold that client's <b>confidential information</b> that is <b>material</b> to the matter and the new client's interest is <b>adverse</b> to theirs."],[188,"What are the two ways out under para 6.5?","(a) <b>Effective measures</b> leaving <b>no real risk of disclosure</b>. Or (b) that client's <b>informed consent</b>, given or evidenced <b>in writing</b>."]]},{"id":"cooperation-reporting","title":"Cooperation, accountability and reporting duties","q":[[0,"A colleague backdated a document to hide a missed deadline. What must you do?",["Report promptly to the SRA","Wait to see if it recurs","Speak to them and take no further step"],"Dishonesty is always serious. <b>Para 7.7</b> requires prompt reporting once you reasonably believe it could be a serious breach."],[1,"A partner took client money, then repaid it. Must it still be reported?",["Yes","No, nothing was lost","Only if the client complains"],"Putting the money back <b>does not remove</b> the duty to report."],[2,"You suspect the COLP will not report a serious breach. What should you do?",["Report directly to the SRA","Wait for the COLP","Do nothing"],"Telling the COLP satisfies your duty only if you expect them to pass it on (para 7.12)."],[3,"A solicitor enters an individual voluntary arrangement. What must they do?",["Notify the SRA promptly","Wait until the annual return","Tell the Law Society only"],"<b>Para 7.6</b>: notify promptly of a relevant insolvency event, such as bankruptcy or an IVA."],[4,"A solicitor missed a limitation period. What must they do about the client?",["Tell them promptly and honestly","Wait for the client to notice","Hide it and fix it quietly"],"<b>Para 7.11</b>: be honest and open, put matters right if possible and explain fully and promptly."],[5,"A mistake may give the client a claim. What should the solicitor advise?",["Take independent legal advice","Sign a waiver","Settle quietly"],"It is an own interest conflict (<b>para 6.1</b>), so the solicitor cannot advise on the claim."],[6,"An NDA bars a person from reporting to the SRA. Which para does that breach?",["Para 7.5","Para 7.2","Para 7.9"],"<b>Para 7.5</b>: do not attempt to prevent anyone providing information to a regulator."],[7,"What should a solicitor do about the firm's insurers after a mistake?",["Notify them promptly","Wait for a claim","Notify only after settling"],"Policies require prompt notice. Do not conceal the mistake."],[8,"A one-off client account error is corrected the same day with no loss. Is a report usually needed?",["No, but record it","Yes, always","Yes, within 24 hours"],"Minor, isolated breaches put right promptly usually need no SRA report, but the firm should <b>record</b> them."],[9,"A trainee is sacked after a good-faith report the SRA drops. Is that a breach?",["Yes","No, the SRA took no action","No, if they were on probation"],"<b>Para 7.9</b>: no detrimental treatment for a reasonably held report, whatever the outcome."],[10,"How must a solicitor respond to an SRA request for information?",["Promptly, fully and accurately","Within three months","Only if it is in writing"],"<b>Para 7.4</b>: respond promptly with full and accurate explanations, information and documents."],[11,"Which factor makes a breach serious?",["Dishonesty","Being a first offence","A short delay in reporting"],"<b>Dishonesty</b> or lack of integrity is always serious, as is harm to vulnerable clients or concealment."]],"c":[[196,"What does para 7.5 prohibit?","<b>Attempting to prevent anyone</b> from providing information to the SRA or any body with regulatory, investigatory or prosecutory functions. So an <b>NDA</b> must not stop reporting."],[197,"When must you notify the SRA about yourself (para 7.6)?","<b>Promptly</b> if you are subject to a <b>criminal charge, conviction or caution</b> (subject to the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974). A <b>relevant insolvency event</b>. A <b>material change</b> to information given to the SRA. Information given being <b>false, misleading, incomplete or inaccurate</b>."],[200,"What does para 7.7 require?","<b>Report promptly</b> to the SRA, or another approved regulator, facts you <b>reasonably believe</b> are <b>capable of amounting to a serious breach</b> by <b>any person</b> they regulate, <b>including you</b>."],[202,"Must you be sure a serious breach has happened before reporting?","<b>No.</b> Report once you <b>reasonably believe</b> the facts are <b>capable of amounting</b> to one. Do not delay to finish your own <b>investigation</b>."],[203,"Whose breaches must be reported?","<b>Anyone</b> the SRA regulates: yourself, colleagues, your <b>managers</b>, solicitors at <b>other firms</b> and SRA-regulated firms. For a <b>barrister</b>, report to the <b>BSB</b>."],[206,"What does para 7.11 require when things go wrong?","Be <b>honest and open</b> with clients. If they suffer loss or harm, <b>put matters right</b> if possible and <b>explain fully and promptly</b> what happened and its likely impact."],[207,"Can you meet your reporting duty by telling the COLP or COFA?","<b>Yes</b> (para 7.12), but only if you reasonably expect them to <b>pass it on</b>. If the COLP is the wrongdoer or refuses, report <b>directly</b> to the SRA."],[208,"What factors make a breach serious?","<b>Dishonesty</b> or lack of <b>integrity</b> (always serious). <b>Harm</b> to clients, especially the <b>vulnerable</b>. A <b>pattern</b> or systemic failure. Deliberate or reckless <b>intent</b>. <b>Seniority</b>. <b>Concealment</b>. Impact on <b>public confidence</b>."],[212,"Your mistake may give the client a claim against you. What must you do?","Tell the client <b>promptly and honestly</b>. Advise <b>independent legal advice</b>. <b>Stop acting</b> if a significant risk of conflict remains: para 6.1 has no exceptions. <b>Notify your insurers</b> promptly."]]},{"id":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","title":"Client identification, complaints handling and the Legal Ombudsman","q":[[0,"Which body deals with a solicitor's misconduct rather than poor service?",["The SRA","The Legal Ombudsman","The Law Society"],"The <b>SRA</b> handles misconduct. <b>LeO</b> handles poor service."],[1,"When must a firm tell a client in writing about their right to complain?",["At engagement","When a complaint is made","At the end of the matter"],"<b>Para 8.3</b>: inform clients in writing at the time of engagement, including their rights to go to LeO."],[2,"How must complaints be handled?",["Promptly, fairly and free of charge","At the client's expense","Only by a partner"],"<b>Para 8.5</b>: promptly, fairly and free of charge. A client cannot be made to waive LeO rights."],[3,"A complaint is unresolved after 8 weeks. What must the firm do?",["Tell the client in writing about LeO","Close the complaint","Charge a handling fee"],"<b>Para 8.4</b>: inform the client in writing of the right to go to LeO, the time limit and contact details."],[4,"Who can complain to the Legal Ombudsman?",["An individual client","A large company","The opponent in a dispute"],"LeO deals with individuals, <b>micro-enterprises</b> and certain small charities and trusts, who were the lawyer's clients."],[5,"The firm's final response is dated 1 March (mentioning LeO). What is the six-month deadline?",["1 September","1 June","1 March next year"],"LeO complaints must be made within <b>six months</b> of the final response, subject to the one-year rule."],[6,"Mistake on 1 Jan 2025, known at once. Final response 1 Dec 2025. Is a complaint on 1 March 2026 in time?",["No, over one year from the act","Yes, within six months of the response","Yes, as the firm replied late"],"The <b>one-year</b> limit runs from the act or when the client should have known. LeO may extend in exceptional cases."],[7,"Can the opponent in a case complain to LeO about the other side's solicitor?",["No","Yes","Only if they lost"],"LeO is for people who received services from the solicitor, so the <b>other side</b> is not eligible."],[8,"Which can the Legal Ombudsman order?",["A reduction of fees","Strike-off","Suspension"],"LeO can order an apology, refund or fee reduction, compensation and putting things right. Discipline is for the <b>SRA/SDT</b>."],[9,"A complainant accepts LeO's final decision. Can they sue over the same matter?",["No","Yes","Only for more than £50,000"],"Once accepted, the decision is <b>binding</b> on both parties and the complainant cannot then sue."],[10,"Which service must the firm publish price information for?",["Residential conveyancing","Divorce","Commercial leases"],"Under the <b>Transparency Rules</b>, price and service information is required for services such as conveyancing, uncontested probate and employment tribunal claims."],[11,"A director instructs the firm for his company. Who is the client?",["The company","The director","The shareholders"],"<b>Para 8.1</b>: identify who you act for. A company client is the company, not its directors."]],"c":[[220,"What must clients be told in writing at the start about complaints (para 8.3)?","Their right to complain about <b>services and charges</b>. <b>How</b> and <b>to whom</b>. Any right to complain to the <b>Legal Ombudsman</b> and <b>when</b>."],[221,"What must you tell a client whose complaint is unresolved after 8 weeks (para 8.4)?","In writing: their right to complain to <b>LeO</b>, the <b>time frame</b> and contact details. If your procedure is exhausted: that you <b>cannot settle</b> it, the name and website of an approved <b>ADR</b> body, and <b>whether you agree</b> to use it."],[222,"How must complaints be dealt with (para 8.5)?","<b>Promptly, fairly and free of charge</b>. You cannot make a client <b>waive</b> LeO rights."],[223,"What does the Legal Ombudsman deal with?","Complaints about <b>poor service</b> by authorised lawyers, under <b>Part 6 LSA 2007</b>. It is <b>not</b> a disciplinary body: <b>misconduct</b> goes to the <b>SRA</b>."],[225,"Who can complain to LeO?","<b>Individuals</b>. <b>Micro-enterprises</b> (fewer than <b>10</b> employees and turnover or balance sheet up to <b>€2 million</b>). <b>Charities</b>, clubs and <b>trustees</b> under <b>£1 million</b>. <b>Personal representatives</b> and <b>beneficiaries</b> of estates."],[227,"When will LeO normally accept a complaint?","Only after the firm's <b>final response</b>, or after <b>8 weeks</b> from the complaint, unless exceptional circumstances apply."],[228,"What is the six-month LeO time limit?","The complaint must reach LeO within <b>six months</b> of the firm's <b>final response</b>, if that response mentioned LeO and the six-month limit."],[229,"What is the outer time limit for LeO complaints since 1 April 2023?","<b>One year</b> from the act or omission, <b>or one year</b> from when the complainant <b>should reasonably have known</b> of cause for complaint."],[233,"What is the cap on LeO remedies?","<b>£50,000</b> per complaint."]]},{"id":"firms-compliance-protections","title":"Code for Firms, compliance officers and client protections","q":[[0,"Which compliance officer must be a lawyer?",["The COLP","The COFA","Neither"],"The <b>COLP</b> must be a lawyer. The COFA need not be."],[1,"What is the minimum PII cover for an LLP per claim?",["£3 million","£2 million","£1 million"],"The minimum is <b>£2 million</b> per claim, or <b>£3 million</b> for LLPs and companies."],[2,"When a firm closes with no successor, how long does run-off PII cover last?",["Six years","Two years","Twelve months"],"Run-off cover lasts <b>six years</b>. After that, claims go to the Solicitors Indemnity Fund."],[3,"On whose terms must a firm's compulsory PII be written?",["The SRA's minimum terms and conditions","Any insurer's standard terms","The Law Society's model terms"],"PII must be with a participating insurer on the SRA <b>Minimum Terms and Conditions</b>."],[4,"A cashier steals client money and the firm cannot repay. Which protection applies?",["The SRA Compensation Fund","PII","The Legal Ombudsman"],"The <b>Compensation Fund</b> covers dishonesty and failure to account where loss cannot be recovered elsewhere."],[5,"A solicitor negligently misses a limitation period. Which protection fits?",["PII","The Compensation Fund","The Legal Ombudsman"],"<b>Negligence</b> claims are met by compulsory <b>PII</b>, not the Compensation Fund."],[6,"A client wants compensation for delay and poor communication. Where do they go?",["The Legal Ombudsman","The Compensation Fund","The SDT"],"<b>LeO</b> deals with poor service. The SRA cannot award compensation."],[7,"Is a Compensation Fund payment a right?",["No, it is discretionary","Yes","Yes, up to £2 million"],"It is a <b>discretionary</b> fund of last resort, with a usual maximum of £2 million per claim."],[8,"A firm cannot pay its rent or PII premium. When must it tell the SRA?",["Promptly, as an indicator of financial difficulty","Only on insolvency","At its annual return"],"<b>Code for Firms 3.6</b>: notify promptly of indicators of serious financial difficulty."],[9,"How are managers responsible for a firm's compliance with the Code?",["Jointly and severally","Only the senior partner","Only the COLP"],"Each manager can be held responsible for the whole where management is shared."],[10,"What is a licensed body (ABS)?",["A firm with non-lawyer owners or managers","A sole practice","A firm of only barristers"],"An <b>alternative business structure</b> allows non-lawyers to own or manage, licensed under the Legal Services Act 2007."],[11,"Which activity can a solicitor not carry out in an unregulated business?",["A reserved activity such as conducting litigation","Drafting a will","Giving general advice"],"<b>Reserved legal activities</b> need an authorised person. Drafting a will is not reserved."]],"c":[[242,"What is the COLP responsible for under the Code of Conduct for Firms?","Taking all reasonable steps to ensure compliance with the <b>terms and conditions of the firm's authorisation</b>, and with the <b>SRA's regulatory arrangements</b> by the firm, its managers, employees and interest holders, <b>except the Accounts Rules</b> (the COFA's job) (para <b>9.1</b>).<br>The COLP must <b>promptly report</b> to the SRA any facts reasonably believed capable of amounting to a <b>serious breach</b>."],[243,"What is the COFA responsible for?","All reasonable steps to ensure compliance with the <b>SRA Accounts Rules</b>, and <b>promptly reporting</b> serious Accounts Rules breaches."],[246,"What does professional indemnity insurance cover?","Civil claims for loss caused by the firm's <b>negligence</b> or other civil liability in its legal work. It is <b>compulsory</b> for every authorised firm."],[248,"What is the minimum PII cover?","<b>£2 million</b> for each and every claim. <b>£3 million</b> for <b>LLPs and companies</b>."],[249,"Can the insurer refuse a client's claim because the firm breached the policy?","<b>No.</b> Under the MTC it must pay and may <b>recover from the firm</b> afterwards. <b>Excesses</b> are paid by the firm, not deducted from the client's compensation."],[252,"When does the Compensation Fund pay?","For loss caused by a solicitor's or employee's <b>dishonesty</b>, or a <b>failure to account</b> for money held, that <b>cannot be recovered elsewhere</b>."],[253,"What are the Compensation Fund limits?","Usual maximum <b>£2 million</b> per claim. Claims normally within <b>12 months</b> of becoming aware of the loss."],[255,"Which protection fits which loss?","<b>Negligence</b>: <b>PII</b>. <b>Theft or dishonesty</b> with no other recovery: <b>Compensation Fund</b>. <b>Poor service</b>: <b>Legal Ombudsman</b>. Negligence claim after six-year run-off: <b>SIF</b>."],[258,"Can a solicitor in an unregulated business carry out reserved legal activities?","<b>No.</b> E.g. no <b>conducting litigation</b>, rights of audience, or reserved conveyancing and probate work there."]]},{"id":"ndas-slapps","title":"SRA warning notices: NDAs and SLAPPs","q":[[0,"What must an NDA never be used to prevent?",["Reporting to the police or a regulator","Protecting settlement terms","Protecting trade secrets"],"NDAs are lawful, but must not deter reports to regulators or police, or protected disclosures (para 7.5)."],[1,"An NDA says the employee repays the settlement if she reports to a regulator. Is that proper?",["No, it deters a permitted disclosure","Yes, it is just a clawback","Yes, if she had advice"],"Warranties, indemnities and <b>clawback</b> clauses must not deter permitted disclosures."],[2,"Is an NDA unlawful in itself?",["No","Yes","Only in employment cases"],"NDAs are lawful, but the SRA says they <b>should not be used routinely</b>."],[3,"What is the status of an SRA warning notice?",["Not a rule, but ignoring it risks action","A binding rule","Only advice with no effect"],"Failing to have <b>regard</b> to a notice is likely to be treated as evidence of a breach."],[4,"What is a SLAPP?",["A claim that misuses litigation to stifle scrutiny","A fast-track civil claim","A type of NDA"],"A <b>strategic lawsuit against public participation</b> silences journalists, campaigners and whistleblowers."],[5,"A letter to a journalist gives 24 hours and is headed \"without prejudice, not for publication\". What is wrong?",["Oppressive deadline and misused labels","Nothing, it is firm but fair","Only the heading"],"A short deadline and misleading labels are SRA examples of <b>improper</b> conduct."],[6,"Before sending a pre-action letter, what must a solicitor check?",["The claim is properly arguable","The recipient can afford to pay","The client wants it"],"<b>Para 2.4</b>: only make properly arguable assertions. Take instructions and make reasonable checks."],[7,"Who are typical targets of SLAPPs?",["Journalists and campaigners","Large banks","Local councils"],"Targets are often <b>journalists, academics, campaigners</b> and whistleblowers."],[8,"Which is an SRA red flag for a SLAPP?",["A marked imbalance of power","A short letter","A named defendant"],"Others: a publication on a matter of public importance and a client unwilling to take advice."],[9,"A solicitor sends a litigant in person a private email mocking his accent. Is that safe because it is private?",["No, it may breach Principles 2, 5 and 6","Yes, it was private","Yes, if sent after work"],"Private is <b>no excuse</b>. Offensive communications about people connected to work engage the Principles."],[10,"A trainee's AI-drafted skeleton cites cases the solicitor does not know. What must they do?",["Verify every authority first","File it, AI is reliable","Add a disclaimer"],"<b>AI does not reduce responsibility.</b> Fake citations can mislead the court (Ayinde)."],[11,"May a solicitor paste confidential client details into a public AI tool?",["No, it risks confidentiality and privilege","Yes, if the tool is free","Yes, if the client is anonymous"],"Only use AI with proper safeguards. Public tools risk breaching <b>para 6.3</b> and waiving privilege."]],"c":[[261,"What must an NDA not prevent or deter?","Reporting to the <b>SRA</b> or another <b>regulator</b> (para 7.5). Reporting an offence to the <b>police</b> or helping a <b>criminal investigation</b>. A <b>protected disclosure</b> (whistleblowing). Disclosures <b>required by law</b> or to <b>legal, medical or tax advisers</b>."],[262,"What does s.17 Victims and Prisoners Act 2024 do?","From <b>1 October 2025</b>, a confidentiality clause is <b>void</b> so far as it would stop a person who reasonably considers themselves a <b>victim of crime</b> disclosing to the <b>police</b>, a <b>lawyer</b>, a <b>regulator</b> or others listed."],[263,"Which NDA drafting tactics should solicitors avoid?","Clauses known to be <b>unenforceable</b>. <b>Warranties</b>, <b>indemnities</b> or <b>clawback</b> clauses to deter permitted disclosures. <b>Undue pressure</b> or <b>oppressive time limits</b>. Discouraging <b>independent advice</b>. Refusing a <b>copy</b> of the agreement."],[266,"A client wants an NDA stopping an employee talking to the police or a regulator. What should you do?","<b>Do not draft it in that form.</b> The NDA may protect the settlement terms, but must make clear that reports to <b>police</b>, <b>regulators</b> and <b>protected disclosures</b> are not prevented."],[267,"What is a SLAPP?","<b>Misuse</b> of the legal system, including <b>threats</b> of litigation, to <b>stifle</b> lawful public scrutiny or publication on matters of <b>public interest</b>. Targets are typically <b>journalists</b>, academics, campaigners and whistleblowers."],[273,"What counts as a meritless claim in SLAPP conduct?","Threatening claims that are <b>not properly arguable</b>. <b>Exaggerated</b> remedies, damages or costs, e.g. threatening <b>imprisonment</b> in a civil claim."],[274,"What counts as oppressive SLAPP conduct?","<b>Aggressive</b> letters. Disproportionate correspondence. Vague allegations to a lay person. Implying the recipient <b>cannot seek advice</b>. Unreasonably <b>short deadlines</b>, e.g. for a <b>right of reply</b>."],[277,"What should a solicitor check before sending a pre-action letter?","Take sufficient instructions and reasonable steps to check the claim is <b>properly arguable</b> (para <b>2.4</b>)."]]}]},{"id":"wills","title":"Wills and the Administration of Estates","stop":8,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M-64,-40 A64,64 0 0 1 64,-40 Z\" fill=\"#BDE9F7\"/><path d=\"M-40,-40 q-6,-14 0,-26 M36,-40 q6,-12 0,-24 M-10,-40 q-5,-10 0,-18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M-35,-74 L-43,-80 L-43,-68 Z\" fill=\"#FF7F11\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"-26\" cy=\"-74\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"6.5\" fill=\"#FF7F11\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-21\" cy=\"-75.5\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M27,-62 L35,-68 L35,-56 Z\" fill=\"#FFD23F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"18\" cy=\"-62\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"6.5\" fill=\"#FFD23F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"13\" cy=\"-63.5\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M-3,-88 L-11,-94 L-11,-82 Z\" fill=\"#FF6FB5\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"6\" cy=\"-88\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"6.5\" fill=\"#FF6FB5\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"11\" cy=\"-89.5\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"-6\" cy=\"-76\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"-2\" cy=\"-86\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-64,-40 A64,64 0 0 1 64,-40\" fill=\"none\"/><rect x=\"-74\" y=\"-40\" width=\"148\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#4361EE\"/><rect x=\"-12\" y=\"-30\" width=\"24\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#FFE066\"/><path d=\"M-62,-24 q8,-8 16,0 t16,0 M30,-24 q8,-8 16,0 t16,0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"validity","title":"Validity of wills: capacity, knowledge and approval, undue influence","q":[[0,"Which case sets the test for testamentary capacity?",["Banks v Goodfellow","Parker v Felgate","Barry v Butlin"],"The test comes from <b>Banks v Goodfellow</b> (1870) and still governs wills."],[1,"Does the Mental Capacity Act 2005 test decide will capacity after death?",["No, the common law test does","Yes, it replaced the old test","Yes, for wills made after 2007"],"Courts apply the common law <b>Banks v Goodfellow</b> test, not the <b>MCA 2005</b>, to wills challenged after death (Walker v Badmin)."],[2,"The testator believes he is Napoleon but leaves everything to his children. Is the will valid?",["Yes, the delusion did not affect the gift","No, he lacked capacity","No, it is unfair to others"],"A delusion only matters if it <b>affects the gift</b>. Banks v Goodfellow upheld a will despite delusions."],[3,"A testator has dementia that comes and goes. Can she make a valid will?",["Yes, during a lucid interval","No, never","Only with a court order"],"A <b>lucid interval</b> is enough if she meets the Banks v Goodfellow test at signing."],[4,"Is following the golden rule required for a will to be valid?",["No, it is good practice only","Yes, always","Yes, for over-80s"],"The <b>golden rule</b> is not a legal requirement and does not guarantee validity."],[5,"Which Wills Act section sets the minimum age at 18?",["s.7","s.9","s.15"],"<b>s.7</b> Wills Act 1837: a will made by anyone under 18 is invalid, apart from privileged wills."],[6,"Who must prove knowledge and approval of the will's contents?",["The person relying on the will","The person challenging it","The Probate Registry"],"The <b>propounder</b> must prove it, though it is presumed where capacity and due execution exist."],[7,"A beneficiary arranged and typed the will. What does this raise?",["Suspicious circumstances","A presumption of undue influence","Automatic invalidity"],"It is a <b>suspicious circumstance</b>, so the propounder must positively prove knowledge and approval."],[8,"Is undue influence presumed where the testator trusted the beneficiary?",["No, the challenger must prove it","Yes, as with lifetime gifts","Yes, if a carer is involved"],"There is <b>no presumption</b> of undue influence for wills. The challenger must prove coercion."],[9,"Which time limit applies to a rectification claim under AJA 1982 s.20?",["6 months from the grant","12 months from death","3 years from the will"],"Claims must be made within <b>6 months of the grant</b>, though the court can extend."],[10,"Rectification under s.20 is available for which kind of mistake?",["Clerical error or misunderstood instructions","Testator misunderstood legal effect","Testator changed her mind"],"Only a <b>clerical error</b> or the drafter's failure to understand instructions qualifies."],[11,"Someone lies to the testator that her son stole from her, so she cuts him out. What is this?",["Fraudulent calumny","Duress","Mistake"],"<b>Fraudulent calumny</b> is poisoning the testator's mind with lies. The affected gift fails."]],"c":[[0,"What must be true for a will to be valid?","The testator must be <b>18 or over</b>. They must have <b>testamentary capacity</b> and <b>know and approve</b> the contents. The will must be made <b>freely</b> (no undue influence, duress or fraud). It must comply with <b>Wills Act 1837 s.9</b>."],[4,"What are the four limbs of the test for testamentary capacity?","The testator must understand the <b>nature and effect</b> of making a will. They must understand the <b>extent of their property</b> in general terms. They must <b>comprehend the claims</b> of those who might expect to benefit. They must have <b>no disorder of the mind</b> that poisons their affections or perverts their judgment in making the gifts."],[7,"At what time is testamentary capacity judged?","At the time the will is <b>executed</b> (signed and witnessed). A will made during a <b>lucid interval</b> can be valid."],[8,"Testator had capacity when giving instructions but has lost it by signing. Can the will be valid?","<b>Yes</b>, if at execution they understood they were signing a will <b>prepared in accordance with their instructions</b>. They need not understand each clause. The will must <b>actually follow</b> the instructions."],[11,"When is knowledge and approval presumed?","When the testator <b>had capacity</b> and the will was <b>duly executed</b>. The testator need not have read the will if its contents were explained."],[12,"When does the presumption of knowledge and approval not apply?","Where there are <b>suspicious circumstances</b>. The <b>propounder</b> must then positively prove knowledge and approval."],[17,"When can a will be rectified, and by when?","Under <b>AJA 1982 s.20</b>, only for a <b>clerical error</b> or the drafter's <b>failure to understand instructions</b>. Apply within <b>6 months of the grant</b> (the court can extend). There is no rectification where the testator misunderstood the <b>legal effect</b> of words they chose."],[18,"What is undue influence in relation to a will?","<b>Coercion</b> that <b>overbears the testator's own wishes</b>. Persuasion is allowed, even forceful appeals to affection or pity, if the testator still makes their own decision."],[19,"Is undue influence ever presumed for wills?","<b>No.</b> Unlike lifetime gifts, there is <b>no presumption</b>, even in a relationship of trust. The <b>challenger</b> must prove it."],[24,"When is capacity presumed, and what displaces the presumption?","Presumed if the will is <b>rational on its face</b> and <b>duly executed</b>. If the challenger raises a <b>real doubt</b>, the burden returns to the <b>propounder</b> to prove capacity."]]},{"id":"formalities","title":"Formalities: Wills Act 1837 s.9, witnesses and s.15","q":[[0,"How many witnesses must be present when the testator signs or acknowledges?",["Two, at the same time","One","Two, one after the other"],"<b>s.9(c)</b>: two or more witnesses present at the same time."],[1,"Must witnesses sign in front of each other?",["No, only in the testator's presence","Yes, together","No, they can sign later alone"],"<b>s.9(d)</b>: each witness signs in the <b>testator's presence</b>, not necessarily each other's."],[2,"Which case held a will invalid when a witness left mid-signing?",["Re Colling","Re Cook","Re Gibson"],"In <b>Re Colling</b> the nurse left before the testator finished, so both were not present together."],[3,"Someone signs for the testator. What is needed?",["Presence and direction of the testator","A court order","Three witnesses"],"<b>s.9(a)</b>: signed by another in the testator's <b>presence and by their direction</b>."],[4,"A witness signs after the testator has left the room. Is s.9 met?",["No","Yes","Yes, if the testator agrees later"],"<b>s.9(d)</b> needs each witness to sign <b>in the testator's presence</b>."],[5,"Which case held a blind person cannot witness a will?",["Re Gibson","Wood v Smith","Re Colling"],"A witness must be able to <b>see</b> the signing (<b>Re Gibson</b>)."],[6,"Can a will be made only in electronic form?",["No, not valid","Yes, with an e-signature","Yes, with video witnesses"],"Electronic-only wills are <b>not valid</b> in England and Wales. Reform is only proposed."],[7,"Is an attestation clause required by s.9?",["No, but it raises a presumption","Yes, always","Yes, for typed wills"],"It is not required but gives a <b>presumption of due execution</b>."],[8,"No attestation clause is present. What may the registry ask for?",["An affidavit of due execution","A new will","A doctor's letter"],"<b>NCPR r.12</b>: an affidavit of due execution, normally from a witness."],[9,"A witness is left a gift in the will. What happens?",["The gift is void, the will stands","The whole will fails","The witness is disqualified"],"<b>s.15</b>: the <b>gift</b> fails. The will and the witness's evidence remain good."],[10,"A witness's wife is left a gift. Is it affected by s.15?",["Yes, the gift is void","No, only gifts to witnesses","No, she is not a witness"],"s.15 also covers the witness's <b>spouse or civil partner</b> at signing."],[11,"A witness's unmarried partner is left a gift. They marry after signing. Result?",["The gift is saved","The gift is void","The will is void"],"<b>s.15</b> looks at marriage <b>at the time of signing</b>. A later marriage does not void the gift."]],"c":[[28,"What does Wills Act 1837 s.9 require?","The will must be <b>in writing</b> and <b>signed by the testator</b> (or by another in their presence and by their direction). The testator must <b>intend the signature to give effect</b> to the will. The signature must be <b>made or acknowledged</b> in the presence of <b>two witnesses present at the same time</b>. <b>Each witness</b> must sign or acknowledge their signature <b>in the testator's presence</b>."],[30,"What counts as the testator's signature?","<b>Any mark intended as a signature</b>: full name, initials, a cross, a thumbprint, or a description such as \"your loving mother\"."],[34,"The testator signs in front of one witness only. Can the will still be validly executed?","<b>Yes</b>, if the testator later <b>acknowledges</b> the signature with <b>both witnesses present together</b>. Acknowledging to the second witness alone is <b>not enough</b>."],[37,"Can a will made today be witnessed by video link?","<b>No.</b> Video witnessing applied only to wills made <b>31 January 2020 to 31 January 2024</b>. It has <b>ended</b>."],[39,"Must the witnesses sign in each other's presence?","<b>No.</b> Each must sign (or acknowledge) <b>in the testator's presence</b>. A witness who signs <b>after the testator has left</b> or died does not comply."],[40,"Who can be a witness?","No minimum age, but they must <b>understand</b> and <b>see</b> the signing. A minor can witness but it is unwise. A <b>blind</b> person <b>cannot</b> witness. An <b>executor can</b> witness."],[41,"Does s.9 require an attestation clause?","<b>No.</b> But a proper clause gives a <b>presumption of due execution</b>."],[44,"What is the effect of s.15 on a gift to a witness or their spouse or civil partner?","The <b>gift is void</b>. The <b>will stays valid</b> and the witness is still a good witness."],[45,"At what time must the witness be married to the beneficiary for s.15 to apply?","<b>At the time the will is signed.</b> A later marriage or civil partnership <b>saves</b> the gift."],[47,"How does Wills Act 1968 s.1 save a gift to a witness?","If the will is <b>also signed by at least two other witnesses</b> who do not benefit, the beneficiary's signature is <b>disregarded</b>."]]},{"id":"executors","title":"Executors: appointment, eligibility, renunciation and power reserved","q":[[0,"What is an executor?",["A PR appointed by the will","A PR appointed by the court","Any beneficiary of the will"],"An <b>executor</b> is named in the will. An <b>administrator</b> is appointed by the court."],[1,"The will says 'I want Bob to collect my assets and pay my debts'. What is Bob?",["An executor according to the tenor","An administrator","A trustee only"],"An <b>implied appointment</b>: the words show he is to do an executor's job."],[2,"What is the maximum number of executors who can take one grant?",["Four","Two","Six"],"<b>SCA 1981 s.114</b>: no more than four can prove. Others can have power reserved."],[3,"A 16-year-old is the sole executor. What happens?",["Administration with will annexed to an adult","Probate to the minor","Intestacy applies"],"A minor cannot take probate. A grant goes to another for the minor's <b>use and benefit</b> until 18."],[4,"A minor and an adult are co-executors. How is the grant taken?",["Adult proves, power reserved to the minor","Both prove together","Nobody until the minor is 18"],"The adult takes probate with <b>power reserved</b> to the minor, who can prove at 18."],[5,"Which court power allows an unsuitable executor to be bypassed before a grant?",["SCA 1981 s.116","AJA 1985 s.50","AEA 1925 s.7"],"<b>s.116</b> lets the court pass over an executor in special circumstances. <b>AJA 1985 s.50</b> removes a PR after the grant."],[6,"An executor pays the deceased's debts and collects rent. Can she still renounce?",["No, she has intermeddled","Yes, any time","Yes, within 6 months"],"Acts like collecting assets or paying debts are <b>intermeddling</b>, so she has accepted office."],[7,"An executor arranges the funeral and feeds the dog. Has she intermeddled?",["No, these are humanitarian acts","Yes, she has accepted office","Yes, if she paid the bill"],"Funeral arrangements and caring for pets are not <b>intermeddling</b>."],[8,"Does renouncing as executor give up a gift left to the executor in the will?",["No, she can still inherit","Yes, all benefits are lost","Yes, unless a codicil says otherwise"],"Renunciation only gives up the <b>office</b>. The <b>gift</b> is unaffected."],[9,"Who can be cited to take probate after 6 months from death?",["An executor who has intermeddled","An executor who has renounced","Any beneficiary"],"A <b>citation to take probate</b> applies to one who has intermeddled but not applied."],[10,"What does power reserved mean?",["The executor may prove later","The executor has renounced","The executor cannot ever act"],"The executor steps back without renouncing and can take a <b>double probate</b> later."],[11,"A sole executor dies after proving, leaving a will with an executor. Who finishes the estate?",["The sole executor's own executor","The court's nominee","The residuary beneficiary"],"<b>AEA 1925 s.7</b>: the <b>chain of executorship</b> passes to the executor's executor."]],"c":[[50,"What is the difference between an executor and an administrator?","An <b>executor</b> is a PR <b>appointed by the will</b>. An <b>administrator</b> is a PR <b>appointed by the court</b> where no executor is able and willing to act."],[55,"How many executors can take a grant?","<b>No more than four</b> for the same property (<b>SCA 1981 s.114(1)</b>). Any number can be <b>appointed</b>. The others can have <b>power reserved</b>."],[56,"Can a sole executor take probate where a minority or life interest arises?","<b>Yes.</b> The rule requiring <b>two</b> PRs (or a trust corporation) applies to <b>administrators only</b>."],[57,"Can a minor executor take a grant?","<b>No</b>, not until <b>18</b>. Adult co-executors prove with <b>power reserved</b> to the minor."],[64,"Can a sole professional executor charge fees without a charging clause?","<b>No.</b> Under <b>TA 2000 s.29</b> a professional who is <b>not a sole</b> PR can charge only if <b>each other PR agrees in writing</b>. A <b>trust corporation</b> can charge reasonable fees. So a sole professional needs a <b>charging clause</b>."],[66,"When is it too late to renounce?","Once the executor has <b>intermeddled</b>, e.g. collecting debts, paying debts, selling assets or dealing with bank accounts. They are treated as having <b>accepted office</b>."],[67,"Which acts are not intermeddling?","Purely <b>humanitarian</b> acts: arranging the <b>funeral</b>, feeding <b>pets</b>, <b>securing</b> the house."],[68,"What citations can be used against an executor who neither proves nor renounces?","A <b>citation to accept or refuse</b> a grant (no intermeddling). A <b>citation to take probate</b> (has intermeddled, <b>after 6 months</b> from death)."],[73,"What is the chain of representation?","If the <b>sole or last surviving proving executor</b> dies before finishing, <b>their proving executor</b> becomes executor of the original testator too (<b>AEA 1925 s.7</b>)."],[74,"When is the chain of representation broken, and what grant is then needed?","If an executor in the chain dies <b>intestate</b>, appoints no executor, or their executor does not prove. A <b>grant de bonis non administratis</b> is needed."]]},{"id":"alterations-revocation","title":"Alterations, codicils and revocation","q":[[0,"An unattested alteration is found on a will. When is it presumed to have been made?",["After execution","Before execution","On the date of death"],"<b>s.21</b> presumes it was made after execution, so it is invalid unless executed like a will."],[1,"How can a post-execution alteration be validly made?",["Testator and two witnesses sign or initial it","Testator alone initials it","One witness initials it"],"<b>s.21</b> applies the s.9 rules to the alteration."],[2,"An invalid alteration covers the original words, but they can be read with a magnifying glass. Result?",["Original words stand","Original words are revoked","The whole will fails"],"Words apparent by <b>natural means</b> stand. Only forensic-only words are revoked."],[3,"Which case allowed original figures read by infra-red to be admitted to probate?",["Re Itter","Cheese v Lovejoy","Gill v Gill"],"<b>Re Itter</b>: <b>conditional revocation</b> applied, as she meant to revoke only if the new figures worked."],[4,"What is the effect of a codicil confirming an earlier will?",["It republishes the will","It revokes the will","It has no effect"],"Under <b>s.34</b> the will is treated as re-executed at the codicil's date."],[5,"A gift to a witness is void. A later codicil, not witnessed by her, confirms the will. Result?",["The gift is saved","The gift stays void","The will fails"],"<b>Republication</b> can save a gift void under s.15."],[6,"How can a revoked will be revived?",["Re-execution or a codicil showing intent","An oral statement","Revoking the later will"],"<b>s.22</b>: only re-execution or a codicil showing intention to revive."],[7,"Which action does NOT revoke a will?",["Writing 'cancelled' across it","Burning it intentionally","A later will with a revocation clause"],"<b>Cheese v Lovejoy</b>: an actual act of burning, tearing or destroying is needed, not just words."],[8,"A will cannot be found and was last held by the testator. What is presumed?",["She destroyed it intending to revoke","It was lost in the post","It remains valid"],"A missing will is presumed <b>revoked</b>, but the presumption is rebuttable."],[9,"Does marriage revoke an earlier will?",["Yes, unless made in contemplation of it","No, never","Yes, with no exceptions"],"<b>s.18</b> revokes automatically, unless the will shows it was made expecting marriage to a named person."],[10,"A testator's divorce is made final. What happens to the will?",["Stands, ex-spouse treated as dead","Revoked in full","Stands unchanged"],"<b>s.18A</b>: the will survives but the ex-spouse is treated as having died at the <b>final order</b>."],[11,"A spouse dies after the conditional order but before the final order. Is the spouse's gift affected?",["No, the gift stands","Yes, it fails","Yes, it is halved"],"Only a <b>final order</b> triggers s.18A. A conditional order has no effect on the will."]],"c":[[79,"When is an alteration made after execution valid?","Only if <b>executed like a will</b> (s.21): the testator <b>signs or initials</b> it before <b>two witnesses</b>, who sign or initial it."],[81,"An invalid alteration obliterates the original words, which are readable by natural means. What happens?","The <b>original words stand</b>. Natural means include looking closely, holding to the light or a magnifying glass."],[82,"An invalid alteration obliterates words readable only by forensic means. What happens?","Those words are <b>revoked</b> (treated as blank). Exception: <b>conditional revocation</b> (the testator meant to revoke them <b>only if the new words took effect</b>). Then <b>any evidence</b>, including forensic, can be used and the <b>original words stand</b>."],[90,"What are the methods of revoking a will?","A <b>later will or codicil</b> (express or implied). A <b>written declaration</b> executed like a will. <b>Destruction</b> with intention to revoke. <b>Marriage or civil partnership</b>."],[93,"What is needed for revocation by destruction?","An actual <b>act</b> of burning, tearing or destroying <b>and</b> an <b>intention to revoke</b> at the same time. Symbolic acts like crossing out or writing \"revoked\" are <b>not enough</b>."],[97,"What is presumed if a will last in the testator's possession cannot be found?","That the testator <b>destroyed it with intention to revoke</b>. The presumption is <b>rebuttable</b>. It does <b>not</b> arise if the will was held by someone else, such as a solicitor."],[99,"What is the effect of marriage or civil partnership on an existing will?","It <b>automatically revokes</b> it (s.18, s.18B), whatever the parties intended."],[100,"When does a will survive a later marriage?","If it appears <b>from the will</b> that the testator was <b>expecting to marry a particular person</b> and <b>intended the will not to be revoked</b> (s.18(3)). A general \"in contemplation of marriage\" without a named person <b>fails</b>."],[103,"What is the effect of divorce or dissolution on a will?","The will is <b>not revoked</b>. On the <b>final order</b>, the ex-spouse is treated as having <b>died on that date</b> for appointments as executor or trustee and for gifts, unless the will shows a <b>contrary intention</b>."]]},{"id":"interpretation","title":"Interpretation: types of gift and failure of gifts","q":[[0,"'My Rolex watch' is what type of gift?",["Specific","General","Demonstrative"],"A <b>specific gift</b> is a particular identified item."],[1,"'£10,000 to Ann' is what type of gift?",["Pecuniary","Specific","Residuary"],"A gift of money is a <b>pecuniary</b> legacy, a type of general gift."],[2,"The testator sold her Rolex before dying. What happens to the gift of it?",["Adeemed, the beneficiary gets nothing","The beneficiary gets the sale money","The beneficiary gets a similar watch"],"<b>Ademption</b>: a specific gift fails if the item is not in the estate at death."],[3,"Which gifts can adeem?",["Specific gifts only","General gifts only","All gifts"],"Only <b>specific</b> gifts adeem. General and pecuniary gifts are paid from the estate."],[4,"'£5,000 from my Barclays account' is closed before death. What happens?",["Paid as a general legacy","Adeemed","Passes to residue"],"A <b>demonstrative</b> gift becomes general if the fund fails."],[5,"In which order do gifts abate when the estate is short?",["Residue, general, then specific","Specific, general, then residue","General, residue, then specific"],"<b>Abatement</b>: residue first, then general gifts rateably, then specific gifts."],[6,"The will says 'my car'. Which car passes?",["The one owned at death","The one owned at the will's date","The most valuable one"],"<b>s.24</b>: a will speaks from death for property. 'My <b>present</b> car' would show contrary intention."],[7,"A beneficiary under a pecuniary legacy dies before the testator. What happens?",["The gift lapses into residue","It passes to her heirs","The court decides"],"A <b>lapsed</b> non-residuary gift falls into residue."],[8,"The testator's daughter dies first, leaving children alive at his death. What happens to her gift?",["Passes to her children per stirpes","Lapses","Passes to the testator's other children"],"<b>s.33 Wills Act</b>: a gift to a child or descendant is saved for their issue."],[9,"A gift to a predeceased sister who left children. What happens?",["It lapses","It passes to her children under s.33","It passes to her spouse"],"<b>s.33</b> covers only the testator's own children and remoter descendants."],[10,"Two people die together and the order is uncertain. Who is presumed to survive?",["The younger","The elder","They die at the same time"],"<b>LPA 1925 s.184</b>: the <b>younger</b> is presumed to survive the elder."],[11,"When can extrinsic evidence of intention be used to read a will?",["If it is meaningless or ambiguous","Always","Never"],"<b>AJA 1982 s.21</b>: only for meaningless or ambiguous wording."]],"c":[[106,"What is a specific gift?","A <b>particular, identified item</b> distinguished from the rest of the estate, e.g. \"my Rolex watch\". It can <b>adeem</b> and abates <b>last</b>."],[107,"What is a general gift?","Property of a <b>type</b>, not a particular item, e.g. \"a car\" or \"100 shares in BT plc\". The PRs provide it from the estate. It <b>cannot adeem</b>."],[109,"What is a demonstrative gift, and what happens if the fund is short or gone?","A general gift (usually money) <b>payable from a named fund</b>. It does <b>not adeem</b>. Any shortfall is paid as a <b>general</b> gift."],[113,"From what date does a will speak as to property?","From <b>death</b> (<b>Wills Act s.24</b>), unless a <b>contrary intention</b> appears. \"My car\" means the car owned at death."],[117,"When does a gift lapse, and where does it go?","When the beneficiary <b>dies before the testator</b>. A lapsed non-residuary gift falls into <b>residue</b>. A lapsed share of residue passes on <b>partial intestacy</b>."],[121,"Who is presumed to survive where the order of deaths is uncertain?","The <b>younger</b> is presumed to survive the elder (<b>LPA 1925 s.184</b>). A <b>survivorship clause</b> overrides this."],[122,"What does Wills Act s.33 do?","A gift to the testator's <b>child or remoter descendant</b> who dies first <b>leaving issue alive at the testator's death</b> does <b>not lapse</b>. It passes to that issue <b>per stirpes</b>. It applies to <b>class gifts</b> too. It does <b>not</b> apply if the will shows a <b>contrary intention</b>."],[125,"When does a specific gift adeem?","When the property is <b>not in the estate at death</b>: sold, given away, destroyed or changed in <b>substance</b>. The beneficiary gets <b>nothing</b>, not proceeds, insurance money or a replacement."],[129,"In what order do gifts abate?","<b>1. Residue.</b> <b>2. General and pecuniary</b> gifts, <b>rateably</b>. <b>3. Specific</b> (and demonstrative so far as the fund exists), <b>rateably</b>. The will can change the order."],[131,"What is the forfeiture rule?","A person who <b>unlawfully kills</b> the testator (murder or manslaughter, including aiding suicide) <b>cannot benefit</b> from the killing."]]},{"id":"intestacy","title":"Intestacy: entitlement and partial intestacy","q":[[0,"A husband dies intestate with no children. His parents survive. Who takes?",["His wife takes everything","His wife and parents share","His parents take everything"],"A spouse with <b>no issue</b> takes the whole estate. Parents take nothing."],[1,"An intestate leaves a spouse and a child. What does the spouse take first?",["Personal chattels and £322,000","Half the estate","The whole estate"],"Chattels plus the <b>statutory legacy</b> of £322,000 (deaths from 26 July 2023), then half the residue."],[2,"Intestate dies leaving a spouse and children. Who takes the other half of the residue?",["The children on statutory trusts","The parents","The spouse"],"The spouse takes half of residue. The <b>issue</b> take the other half on the statutory trusts."],[3,"How long must a spouse survive the intestate to inherit?",["28 days","7 days","6 months"],"<b>AEA s.46(2A)</b>: 28 days. This applies only to the spouse or civil partner."],[4,"The intestate's partner lived with her for ten years but they never married. What do they take?",["Nothing on intestacy","The statutory legacy","Half the estate"],"<b>Cohabitants</b> take nothing on intestacy. Their remedy is a <b>1975 Act</b> claim."],[5,"A spouse is judicially separated from the intestate. Does the spouse inherit?",["No, treated as dead","Yes, in full","Yes, but half only"],"A <b>judicially separated</b> spouse is treated as having died first. Informal separation changes nothing."],[6,"At what age does a child's share on the statutory trusts vest?",["18","21","25"],"Under <b>s.47</b> the share vests at 18, or earlier marriage. Nobody under 18 can now marry."],[7,"There is no spouse or issue. Who is next entitled?",["Parents, equally","Siblings","Grandparents"],"The order is issue, then <b>parents</b>, then whole-blood siblings, half-blood siblings, grandparents."],[8,"A parent is alive and so are the intestate's siblings. No spouse or children. Who takes?",["The parent takes everything","The siblings take everything","Parent and siblings share"],"<b>Parents</b> rank above siblings. They take equally and absolutely."],[9,"An intestate's stepchild, never adopted, claims a share. Result?",["Nothing","An equal share with children","Half a share"],"Only blood relatives and adopted children count. <b>Stepchildren</b> take nothing."],[10,"A son dies before the intestate leaving two children. What do they take?",["His share, by representation","Nothing","The whole estate"],"The grandchildren take the parent's share <b>per stirpes</b>, contingently on reaching 18."],[11,"Within what period can a spouse require the home to be appropriated?",["12 months of the grant","6 months of death","3 years of death"],"The spouse must elect in writing within <b>12 months of the grant</b> (1952 Act Sch 2)."]],"c":[[142,"Do cohabitants inherit on intestacy?","<b>No</b>, however long the relationship. Their remedy is a <b>1975 Act</b> claim."],[143,"How long must a spouse survive the intestate?","<b>28 days</b> (s.46(2A)). Otherwise the estate is distributed as if the spouse <b>died first</b>."],[144,"What does a spouse take if the intestate leaves issue?","<b>Personal chattels</b>. A <b>statutory legacy</b> of <b>£322,000</b> (deaths from 26 July 2023), with simple interest. <b>Half the residue</b> absolutely. Issue take the <b>other half</b> on the statutory trusts."],[145,"What does a spouse take if there are no issue?","<b>Everything.</b> Parents and siblings take <b>nothing</b>."],[146,"What are personal chattels?","<b>Tangible movable property</b> such as furniture, jewellery, cars and pets. Excludes <b>money and securities</b>, property used <b>mainly for business</b>, and property held <b>solely as an investment</b>."],[149,"Can the spouse take the matrimonial home owned in the intestate's sole name?","<b>Yes.</b> They can require the PRs to <b>appropriate</b> it towards their entitlement, paying any excess. They must elect <b>in writing within 12 months of the grant</b>."],[151,"With no spouse, what is the order of entitlement?","<b>1. Issue.</b> <b>2. Parents.</b> <b>3. Whole-blood siblings.</b> <b>4. Half-blood siblings.</b> <b>5. Grandparents.</b> <b>6. Whole-blood uncles and aunts.</b> <b>7. Half-blood uncles and aunts.</b> <b>8. The Crown</b> (bona vacantia)."],[152,"Which classes take absolutely rather than on the statutory trusts?","<b>Parents</b> and <b>grandparents</b>."],[154,"What are the statutory trusts?","Members take in <b>equal shares</b>. Each share is <b>contingent</b> on reaching <b>18</b> or <b>marrying or forming a civil partnership</b> earlier."],[155,"How does representation work under the statutory trusts?","Issue of a member who <b>died first</b> take their parent's share <b>equally</b> between them (<b>per stirpes</b>), also contingently."]]},{"id":"outside-estate","title":"Property passing outside the estate","q":[[0,"A joint tenant dies. How does their share of the house pass?",["To the surviving joint tenant","Under the will","Under intestacy"],"<b>Survivorship</b> applies automatically. A will cannot override it."],[1,"A tenant in common dies. How does their share pass?",["Under the will or intestacy","To the other owner","To the Crown"],"A <b>tenancy in common</b> share is part of the estate and needs a grant."],[2,"How can an owner make a joint tenancy pass under their will?",["Sever it during their lifetime","Say so in the will","Do nothing"],"A will cannot sever. <b>Severance</b> must happen in life, e.g. by written notice (LPA 1925 s.36(2))."],[3,"A life policy is written in trust for the children. Does the estate need a grant to claim it?",["No, trustees claim it","Yes, probate","Yes, administration"],"A policy <b>in trust</b> passes to the trustees outside the estate, with no grant."],[4,"Which Act creates a statutory trust for a policy for a spouse or children?",["MWPA 1882 s.11","AEA 1925 s.46","TA 2000 s.28"],"<b>MWPA 1882 s.11</b> policies create a trust, so the money passes outside the estate."],[5,"A policy on the deceased's own life, with no trust. Who receives the money?",["The personal representatives","The spouse directly","The insurer keeps it"],"Without a trust, the proceeds are paid to the <b>PRs</b> and form part of the estate."],[6,"A pension scheme pays a discretionary lump sum. Does the will govern it?",["No, the trustees decide","Yes, if the will names someone","Yes, as residue"],"Discretionary death benefits pass <b>outside the estate</b>. An expression of wishes guides but does not bind."],[7,"Is an expression of wishes form binding on pension trustees?",["No, it only guides them","Yes, always","Yes, if witnessed"],"It is <b>not binding</b>. The trustees keep their discretion."],[8,"A joint tenant dies. Is their share counted for IHT?",["Yes, it is in the estate","No, it passes by survivorship","Only for non-spouses"],"A joint tenant's <b>share</b> is included for IHT even though it passes outside the will."],[9,"A life tenant of a pre-2006 trust dies. How does the fund pass?",["Under the trust terms","Under the life tenant's will","By intestacy"],"Trust property passes under the <b>trust terms</b>, though a QIIP fund is taxed in the life tenant's estate."],[10,"What is a donatio mortis causa?",["A gift made contemplating imminent death","A gift in a will","A gift to a charity"],"The donor must contemplate death, part with dominion, and the gift takes effect on death."],[11,"What is the Small Payments Act 1965 limit for payments without a grant?",["£5,000","£500","£50,000"],"Some bodies may pay up to <b>£5,000</b> to the person apparently entitled without a grant."]],"c":[[164,"What happens to a beneficial joint tenant's interest on death?","It passes <b>automatically</b> to the surviving joint tenant(s) by <b>survivorship</b>, not under the will or intestacy. For registered land the survivor produces the <b>death certificate</b>; <b>no grant</b> is needed."],[165,"Can a will override survivorship?","<b>No.</b> A gift of \"my half of the house\" held as joint tenants <b>fails</b>."],[166,"How can a joint tenant leave their share by will?","By <b>severing during their lifetime</b>, e.g. <b>written notice</b> (LPA 1925 s.36(2)), mutual agreement, or dealing with their share. It becomes a <b>tenancy in common</b>. A <b>will cannot sever</b>."],[170,"Who receives an own-life, own-benefit policy, and is it taxed?","The <b>PRs</b>. It passes under the will or intestacy, usually needs a <b>grant</b>, and is <b>included</b> for IHT."],[171,"Who receives a life policy written in trust?","The <b>trustees</b>, on proof of death. It is <b>not</b> part of the estate, needs <b>no grant</b>, and is usually <b>outside</b> IHT."],[174,"Who decides who receives a discretionary pension lump sum death benefit?","The <b>scheme trustees or administrator</b>. An <b>expression of wishes</b> guides them but is <b>not binding</b>. It passes <b>outside the estate</b>."],[181,"When is a trust fund taxed as part of a life tenant's estate?","If the life interest is a <b>qualifying interest in possession</b>. The <b>trustees</b> pay the IHT from the fund."],[185,"What is a donatio mortis causa?","A gift made in <b>contemplation of imminent death</b>, <b>conditional on death</b>, where the donor <b>parts with dominion</b> (e.g. hands over keys). It takes effect outside the will but is <b>included for IHT</b>."]]},{"id":"grants","title":"Grants of representation: need, types, priority and application","q":[[0,"A valid will names an executor who is willing to act. Which grant?",["Probate","Letters of administration","Administration with will annexed"],"<b>Probate</b> goes only to executors who are able and willing to act."],[1,"A valid will exists but all executors have died. Which grant?",["Administration with will annexed","Probate","Simple administration"],"Without a proving executor, the will is attached to <b>letters of administration</b>."],[2,"There is no will at all. Which grant is made?",["Letters of administration","Probate","Grant de bonis non"],"<b>Total intestacy</b> leads to simple <b>letters of administration</b>."],[3,"Which rule sets priority to take a grant on intestacy?",["NCPR r.22","NCPR r.20","NCPR r.12"],"<b>r.22</b> covers intestacy. <b>r.20</b> covers a will with no proving executor."],[4,"On intestacy, who ranks first to apply for the grant?",["The surviving spouse","The eldest child","The parents"],"<b>NCPR r.22</b>: spouse first, then issue, then parents, then siblings."],[5,"A person with no beneficial interest wants to apply on intestacy. Can they?",["No, as a general rule","Yes, as a close friend","Yes, as a neighbour"],"Applicants usually need a <b>beneficial interest</b>. Creditors are a narrow exception, ranking after all relatives."],[6,"What is a grant de bonis non administratis?",["A grant for unadministered estate","A grant for a minor","A grant to the Crown"],"It finishes an estate where the <b>chain of executorship</b> has broken."],[7,"Administration is taken where a beneficiary is a minor. How many administrators are needed?",["At least two","One","Four"],"<b>SCA 1981 s.114(2)</b>: two individuals or a trust corporation where a minority or life interest arises."],[8,"Only one person is entitled to administer. Who can be added to make two?",["Someone from a lower class may join","Nobody","The Crown"],"<b>NCPR r.25</b> allows someone lower in priority to be added to make up two."],[9,"After sending IHT400, how long must the PRs wait before applying for the grant?",["20 working days","7 days","6 months"],"For a non-excepted estate they wait <b>20 working days</b> while HMRC confirms."],[10,"What does a caveat do?",["Blocks a grant for 6 months","Challenges the executor","Proves the will"],"A <b>caveat</b> stops a grant issuing for <b>6 months</b>, renewable."],[11,"Is an IHT400 needed for an excepted estate?",["No","Yes, always","Yes, if over £100,000"],"An <b>excepted estate</b> is excused from sending a full IHT account."]],"c":[[191,"Which grant applies where there is a valid will and an executor able and willing to act?","<b>Probate</b>, to the <b>executor(s)</b>."],[192,"Which grant applies where there is a will but no executor able and willing to act?","<b>Letters of administration with will annexed</b>, to the person entitled under <b>NCPR r.20</b>."],[193,"Which grant applies on total intestacy?","<b>Letters of administration</b>, to the person entitled under <b>NCPR r.22</b>."],[196,"What is the order of priority under NCPR r.20?","<b>(a)</b> Executor. <b>(b)</b> Residuary beneficiary <b>holding in trust</b>. <b>(c)</b> Other residuary beneficiary, or person entitled to undisposed residue. <b>(d)</b> PR of a residuary beneficiary. <b>(e)</b> Other legatee or devisee, or a <b>creditor</b>. <b>(f)</b> PR of another legatee or creditor."],[199,"What is the order of priority under NCPR r.22?","<b>1. Spouse or civil partner.</b> <b>2. Children</b> (and issue of a predeceased child). <b>3. Parents.</b> <b>4. Whole-blood siblings.</b> <b>5. Half-blood siblings.</b> <b>6. Grandparents.</b> <b>7. Whole-blood uncles and aunts.</b> <b>8. Half-blood uncles and aunts.</b> Then the <b>Treasury Solicitor</b>, then a <b>creditor</b>."],[200,"Spouse takes the whole intestate estate. Can a child apply for the grant?","<b>Not while the spouse is alive and able.</b> The child has <b>no beneficial interest</b>. But if the spouse <b>renounces</b>, the child can apply under <b>r.22(3)</b> as a person who would benefit on an accretion. If the spouse survived and later died, the spouse's <b>PR</b> can apply."],[202,"When must at least two administrators take the grant?","Where a <b>minority</b> or <b>life interest</b> arises (s.114(2)), unless a <b>trust corporation</b> acts. The registrar can allow one in special circumstances. A <b>sole executor</b> can always prove."],[210,"What must PRs do before applying for a grant for a non-excepted estate?","Send <b>IHT400</b>. <b>Pay IHT on non-instalment property</b>. <b>Wait 20 working days</b> before applying."],[212,"What is a low value excepted estate?","<b>Gross</b> estate (including joint property share, specified gifts and some trust assets) <b>not exceeding the NRB</b>. Specified gifts and single-trust assets each no more than <b>£250,000</b>. Foreign assets no more than <b>£100,000</b>. <b>No gift with reservation</b>."],[219,"What is loss on sale relief?","If PRs sell <b>quoted shares within 12 months</b> or <b>land within 4 years</b> for less than probate value, they can <b>substitute the sale price</b>."]]},{"id":"iht-funding-incidence","title":"Funding the IHT, liability and incidence","q":[[0,"IHT on death is due how long after the end of the month of death?",["6 months","12 months","20 working days"],"Tax is due <b>6 months after the end of the month of death</b>, and interest then runs."],[1,"Which scheme lets a bank pay IHT to HMRC from the deceased's account before the grant?",["Direct Payment Scheme","Instalment option","Loss on sale relief"],"The <b>Direct Payment Scheme</b> is the usual first choice for funding IHT."],[2,"How many yearly instalments are allowed for IHT on instalment property?",["10","5","6"],"IHT on <b>instalment property</b> can be paid in 10 equal yearly instalments."],[3,"Which of these is instalment property?",["Land and buildings","Cash in the bank","Quoted minority shares"],"Land, business interests and controlling shareholdings can be paid by instalments."],[4,"Is IHT on a bank balance payable by instalments?",["No, it is paid before the grant","Yes, over 10 years","Yes, over 5 years"],"Only <b>instalment property</b> qualifies. Cash and investments must be paid up front."],[5,"Who is liable to HMRC for IHT on the free estate?",["The PRs","Each beneficiary","The surviving spouse"],"The <b>PRs</b> pay from the estate. Beneficiaries bear tax indirectly."],[6,"Who is primarily liable for tax on a failed PET?",["The donee","The PRs","The residuary beneficiary"],"The <b>donee</b> is primarily liable. PRs only if unpaid 12 months after the end of the month of death."],[7,"By default, from which fund is IHT on UK free estate paid?",["Residue","Specific gifts","Pecuniary legacies"],"<b>IHTA s.211</b>: the tax is a testamentary expense paid from <b>residue</b>."],[8,"A specific gift of land in the will says nothing about tax. Who bears its IHT?",["Residue, so the gift is tax-free","The beneficiary","The PRs personally"],"Under <b>s.211</b> UK specific gifts are received free of tax, unless the will says otherwise."],[9,"Residue goes half to a spouse and half to a child. Who bears the tax on residue?",["The child's share only","Both equally","The spouse's share only"],"The <b>exempt</b> share bears no tax (s.41). The non-exempt share bears it all."],[10,"Which case divided residue first, then taxed only the non-exempt share?",["Re Ratcliffe","Re Benham","Re Itter"],"<b>Re Ratcliffe</b> (1999). Re Benham had grossed up so each share was equal after tax."],[11,"A surviving joint tenant takes a house. Who bears the IHT on that property?",["The surviving joint tenant","Residue","The PRs personally"],"Property outside the free estate bears its <b>own</b> tax."]],"c":[[221,"When is IHT on the death estate due?","<b>6 months after the end of the month of death</b>. Interest runs from then. E.g. death on 10 March: due <b>30 September</b>."],[222,"When must the IHT400 be delivered?","Within <b>12 months</b> of the end of the month of death. In practice it is sent much earlier, because it is needed <b>before the grant</b>."],[223,"What is the Direct Payment Scheme?","Banks, building societies and NS&amp;I transfer money <b>from the deceased's accounts direct to HMRC</b> before the grant (form <b>IHT423</b>). Usually the first choice."],[226,"What is the instalment option?","IHT on <b>instalment property</b> can be paid in <b>10 equal yearly instalments</b>, the first due 6 months after the end of the month of death. It need <b>not be paid before the grant</b>."],[227,"What is instalment property?","<b>Land and buildings</b>. A <b>business</b> or interest in one. <b>Controlling</b> shareholdings. Certain <b>unquoted non-controlling</b> shares. From 6 April 2026, <b>all property qualifying for APR or BPR</b>, even at 50%."],[229,"Which instalments are interest-free and which bear interest?","<b>Interest-free</b> (if paid on time): APR or BPR property, businesses, qualifying shareholdings. <b>Interest-bearing</b>: other <b>land</b>, e.g. the family home, with interest on the <b>whole outstanding balance</b>."],[231,"Who is liable to HMRC for IHT on the free estate?","The <b>PRs</b>, from the estate. Beneficiaries are <b>not</b> liable to HMRC."],[234,"Who is liable for IHT on failed PETs and extra tax on LCTs?","The <b>donee</b> (trustees for a trust). The <b>PRs</b> only if unpaid <b>12 months after the end of the month of death</b>, and only up to the estate they hold."],[236,"What is the default incidence rule for IHT on the UK free estate?","<b>IHTA s.211</b>: it is a <b>testamentary expense</b> paid from <b>residue</b>. So specific and pecuniary gifts are <b>tax-free</b> unless the will says \"subject to tax\"."],[238,"Residue is split between a spouse and children. Who bears the IHT on residue?","The <b>non-exempt</b> share bears <b>all</b> of it (s.41). Residue is <b>divided first</b>, then tax comes off the non-exempt share. (Hook: Re Ratcliffe.)"]]},{"id":"administration","title":"Administration: PR duties, debts, protection and distribution","q":[[0,"How soon can PRs be compelled to distribute the estate?",["After one year from death","After six months","Immediately"],"<b>AEA s.44</b>: the executor's year. PRs can distribute earlier if safe."],[1,"Pecuniary legacies unpaid after the first anniversary of death. What follows?",["They carry interest","They lapse","They abate"],"Unpaid pecuniary legacies carry <b>interest</b> from the first anniversary, unless the will says otherwise."],[2,"A mortgaged house is left to a beneficiary. Who bears the mortgage by default?",["The beneficiary of the house","Residue","The PRs"],"<b>AEA s.35</b>: a secured debt is paid primarily from the property charged."],[3,"Which creditors rank first in an insolvent estate, after secured ones?",["Funeral and administration expenses","Preferential debts","Ordinary creditors"],"Under the <b>1986 Order</b>, expenses are paid before preferential debts."],[4,"In an insolvent estate, the deceased's own unpaid income tax is which kind of debt?",["Ordinary unsecured","Preferential","Secondary preferential"],"Only tax the deceased <b>collected for HMRC</b> (VAT, PAYE) is secondary preferential."],[5,"Which step protects PRs from unknown creditors?",["s.27 Trustee Act 1925 notices","A Benjamin order","A bankruptcy search"],"<b>s.27 notices</b> give claimants at least 2 months and protect against <b>unknown</b> claims only."],[6,"PRs knew of a creditor who did not answer the s.27 notice. Are they protected?",["No, known debts must be paid","Yes, notices protect them","Yes, after 2 months"],"<b>s.27</b> gives no protection against claims the PRs <b>knew</b> about."],[7,"A beneficiary has emigrated and cannot be traced. Best protection?",["A Benjamin order","s.27 notices only","Pay the other beneficiaries early"],"A <b>Benjamin order</b> authorises distribution on a stated basis, e.g. that the person predeceased."],[8,"A residuary beneficiary may be bankrupt. What should the PRs do?",["Search, then pay the trustee in bankruptcy","Pay the beneficiary","Retain the share for good"],"Paying a bankrupt directly makes the PRs personally liable to the <b>trustee in bankruptcy</b>."],[9,"What is a devastavit?",["A PR's breach causing loss to the estate","A claim by a creditor","A type of grant"],"<b>Devastavit</b> means wasting the estate. PRs are personally liable, jointly and severally."],[10,"Which section may excuse a PR who acted honestly and reasonably?",["Trustee Act 1925 s.61","Trustee Act 1925 s.27","AEA 1925 s.44"],"<b>s.61</b> lets the court relieve a PR who ought fairly to be excused."],[11,"By what document do PRs pass land to a beneficiary?",["A written assent","A stock transfer form","A deed of variation"],"<b>AEA s.36</b>: land passes by written <b>assent</b>. Shares pass by stock transfer form."]],"c":[[240,"What are PRs' core duties under AEA 1925 s.25?","<b>Collect and get in</b> the estate. <b>Administer it according to law</b>. Produce an <b>inventory and account</b> when the court requires."],[245,"What is the executor's year?","PRs <b>cannot be compelled to distribute</b> before <b>one year from death</b> (AEA s.44). They may distribute earlier if safe."],[246,"When do pecuniary legacies carry interest?","From the <b>first anniversary of death</b> if unpaid, unless the will says otherwise."],[251,"What is the statutory order for paying unsecured debts in a solvent estate?","<b>1.</b> Undisposed property. <b>2.</b> Residue. <b>3.</b> Property given <b>for payment of debts</b>. <b>4.</b> Property <b>charged</b> with debts. <b>5.</b> Fund retained for <b>pecuniary legacies</b>. <b>6.</b> <b>Specific</b> gifts rateably. <b>7.</b> Property under a general power of appointment. Each of 1 and 2 keeps back a fund for pecuniary legacies."],[253,"What is the order of payment in an insolvent estate?","Secured creditors rely on <b>security</b>. <b>1.</b> Funeral, testamentary and administration <b>expenses</b>. <b>2.</b> <b>Preferential</b> debts. <b>3.</b> <b>Secondary preferential</b> debts. <b>4.</b> <b>Ordinary unsecured</b> debts. <b>5.</b> <b>Interest</b>. <b>6.</b> <b>Deferred</b> debts."],[260,"What is a devastavit?","A PR's breach of duty: <b>misappropriation</b>, <b>maladministration</b> (e.g. paying the wrong person or in the wrong order) or <b>negligence</b>. PRs are <b>personally liable</b>; joint PRs <b>jointly and severally</b>."],[262,"When can the court excuse a PR from liability?","Under <b>TA 1925 s.61</b>, if the PR acted <b>honestly and reasonably</b> and <b>ought fairly to be excused</b>."],[264,"How do s.27 notices protect PRs?","Advertise in <b>The London Gazette</b> and a <b>local newspaper</b> where land is situated, giving <b>at least 2 months</b>. PRs who then distribute on <b>known</b> claims are <b>not personally liable</b>. A late claimant can still <b>follow the assets</b>."],[267,"What protects PRs against a known but missing beneficiary?","A <b>Benjamin order</b> allowing distribution on a stated basis, e.g. that the person <b>died first</b>. The beneficiary can still recover from those paid, <b>not the PRs</b>. Alternatives: <b>missing beneficiary insurance</b> or payment into <b>court</b>."],[270,"When are PRs protected against 1975 Act claims?","If they distribute <b>after 6 months from the grant</b> without notice of a claim (s.20). In practice wait about <b>10 months</b> if a claim is likely."]]},{"id":"family-provision","title":"Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975","q":[[0,"What is the time limit for a 1975 Act claim?",["6 months from the grant","6 months from death","12 months from the grant"],"<b>s.4</b>: 6 months from the <b>grant</b>, though the court may extend it."],[1,"Which of these can claim under the 1975 Act?",["A child of any age","A friend of 20 years","A sibling who lived nearby"],"Categories include spouses, former spouses, cohabitants, <b>children of any age</b>, children of the family and dependants."],[2,"How long must a cohabitant have lived with the deceased before death?",["The whole of the 2 years before death","6 months","5 years"],"<b>s.1(1)(ba)</b>: the whole of the <b>2 years immediately before</b> death."],[3,"A partner lived with the deceased for 18 months and was largely maintained by her. Which category?",["Being maintained (s.1(1)(e))","Cohabitant","Spouse"],"Under 2 years fails the cohabitant test, but <b>maintained by the deceased</b> may apply."],[4,"Which standard of provision applies to a surviving spouse?",["Reasonable in all circumstances","Maintenance only","Equal division"],"The <b>spouse standard</b> is not limited to maintenance. A divorce outcome is a cross-check."],[5,"Which standard applies to an adult child?",["Maintenance","Reasonable in all circumstances","Half the estate"],"Everyone except a spouse or civil partner faces the <b>maintenance standard</b>."],[6,"Which case dealt with an estranged adult daughter and a charity beneficiary?",["Ilott v The Blue Cross","Banks v Goodfellow","Re Ratcliffe"],"In <b>Ilott</b>, the Supreme Court restored a £50,000 award and stressed testamentary freedom."],[7,"At what date does the court assess the facts for a 1975 Act claim?",["The hearing","Death","The will's date"],"<b>s.3(5)</b>: the facts are looked at as at the <b>hearing</b>."],[8,"Which of these is NOT a s.3 factor?",["Whether the will was unfair","Applicant's financial needs","Size of the net estate"],"The court weighs resources, needs, obligations, estate size, disability and conduct. Unfairness alone is not a factor."],[9,"Which order can the court make under s.2?",["A lump sum","A new will","An injunction against the will"],"Orders include <b>periodical payments</b>, a <b>lump sum</b>, a transfer or a settlement of property."],[10,"Can the court treat a joint tenant's severable share as part of the net estate?",["Yes","No","Only for spouses"],"<b>s.9</b> allows the <b>severable share</b> of joint property to be treated as net estate."],[11,"PRs distribute 7 months after the grant, unaware of any claim. Are they protected?",["Yes, under s.20","No, always liable","Only with court consent"],"<b>s.20</b>: PRs are not liable for distributing after 6 months from the grant without notice of a claim."]],"c":[[276,"Who can apply under s.1(1)?","<b>(a)</b> Spouse or civil partner. <b>(b)</b> Former spouse or civil partner <b>not remarried</b>. <b>(ba)</b> <b>Cohabitant</b> of 2 years. <b>(c)</b> <b>Child</b> of any age. <b>(d)</b> Person <b>treated as a child of the family</b>. <b>(e)</b> Person <b>being maintained</b> by the deceased."],[277,"When is a former spouse barred from claiming?","Usually where a <b>clean break</b> order made on divorce ended future claims, including under the 1975 Act. Also if they have <b>remarried</b>."],[278,"What must a cohabitant show to claim?","They lived with the deceased <b>in the same household as if married or civil partners</b> for the <b>whole of the 2 years immediately before death</b>. Short breaks (e.g. hospital) do not break the household."],[281,"What must a dependant show under s.1(1)(e)?","<b>Immediately before death</b> the deceased made a <b>substantial contribution in money or money's worth</b> to their reasonable needs, <b>not</b> under a commercial arrangement for full value."],[282,"What is the time limit for a 1975 Act claim?","<b>6 months from the grant</b> (s.4), not from death. A claim can be made <b>before</b> a grant."],[283,"What factors govern permission to claim out of time?","<b>Good reason</b> and length of delay. Whether <b>negotiations</b> were ongoing. Whether the estate is <b>distributed</b>. Whether the applicant has <b>another remedy</b> (e.g. against solicitors). <b>Strength</b> of the claim."],[285,"What is the surviving spouse standard?","Provision <b>reasonable in all the circumstances</b>, <b>whether or not required for maintenance</b>. The court cross-checks against what they would get on <b>divorce</b> (neither floor nor ceiling). It applies only to spouses and civil partners <b>not judicially separated</b>."],[286,"What is the maintenance standard, and who does it apply to?","Provision reasonable for <b>day-to-day living costs</b> at an appropriate standard, not capital or an inheritance. It applies to <b>everyone except spouses</b>: former spouses, <b>cohabitants</b>, <b>children</b>, children of the family, dependants."],[288,"What are the general s.3 factors?","<b>(a)-(c)</b> Financial resources and needs of the <b>applicant</b>, <b>other applicants</b> and <b>beneficiaries</b>. <b>(d)</b> The deceased's <b>obligations and responsibilities</b>. <b>(e)</b> <b>Size and nature</b> of the net estate. <b>(f)</b> <b>Disability</b>. <b>(g)</b> <b>Any other matter</b>, including <b>conduct</b>."],[294,"What orders can the court make?","<b>Periodical payments</b>. <b>Lump sum</b>. <b>Transfer</b> of an asset. <b>Settlement</b> on trust. <b>Acquisition</b> of property. <b>Variation</b> of a marriage settlement."]]},{"id":"iht-lifetime","title":"Inheritance tax: lifetime gifts, exemptions and anti-avoidance","q":[[0,"A gift to another individual is called what for IHT?",["A PET","An LCT","A reserved gift"],"A <b>potentially exempt transfer</b> is an outright gift to an individual, exempt if the donor survives 7 years."],[1,"A PET is made. The donor survives 7 years. What is the IHT position?",["Fully exempt","Taper relief applies","Taxed at 20%"],"After <b>7 years</b> survival the PET is ignored forever."],[2,"A gift into a discretionary trust is what type of transfer?",["An LCT","A PET","An exempt gift"],"A <b>lifetime chargeable transfer</b> is taxed at 20% above the NRB when made."],[3,"What is the lifetime IHT rate on an LCT above the nil rate band?",["20%","40%","36%"],"LCTs are charged at <b>20%</b> in lifetime, with a top-up to 40% on death within 7 years."],[4,"The donor pays the tax on an LCT. What must be done?",["Gross up the gift","Reduce the gift","Ignore the tax"],"The tax is grossed up, so tax is <b>25%</b> (20/80) of the net excess."],[5,"A donor dies 3 years and 6 months after a chargeable gift. What does taper relief do?",["Reduces the tax to 80%","Reduces the gift's value","Removes the tax"],"<b>Taper relief</b> reduces the <b>tax</b>, not the value. Years 3 to 4 means 80% payable."],[6,"A gift made 5 years before death falls within the NRB. Does taper relief help?",["No, there is no tax to reduce","Yes, it halves the gift","Yes, it removes the gift"],"Taper only reduces <b>tax</b>. The gift still uses up NRB."],[7,"What is the annual exemption?",["£3,000 per tax year","£250 per person","£5,000 per tax year"],"<b>£3,000</b> a year, with one unused year carried forward."],[8,"How many years of unused annual exemption can be carried forward?",["One","Two","Three"],"Only the previous year's unused amount is carried forward, and the current year is used first."],[9,"What is the small gifts exemption?",["£250 per recipient per tax year","£500 per recipient","£3,000 in total"],"<b>s.20</b>: £250 per recipient. A gift over £250 gets no small gifts exemption at all."],[10,"A parent gives a child on her wedding day. What is the marriage exemption?",["£5,000","£2,500","£1,000"],"<b>s.22</b>: £5,000 from a parent, £2,500 from a grandparent, £1,000 from anyone else."],[11,"A parent gives away her house but lives there rent-free. What applies?",["Gift with reservation of benefit","A fully exempt PET","Normal expenditure exemption"],"Under <b>FA 1986 s.102</b> the house is treated as still in her estate at death."]],"c":[[299,"What are the key IHT rates and thresholds for 2026/27?","<b>NRB £325,000</b> (frozen until 5 April 2031). Lifetime rate <b>20%</b>. Death rate <b>40%</b> (36% with enough to charity). Annual exemption <b>£3,000</b>. Small gifts <b>£250</b>."],[303,"What is a PET?","A non-exempt lifetime gift by an individual to <b>another individual</b>, or into a <b>bare trust</b> or <b>disabled person's trust</b> (s.3A)."],[304,"What is the tax position when a PET is made and if the donor survives 7 years?","<b>No tax</b> and nothing to report when made. If the donor survives <b>7 years</b> it is <b>completely exempt</b>."],[305,"How is a PET taxed if the donor dies within 7 years?","It becomes <b>chargeable</b>. Valued <b>at the date of the gift</b> less available exemptions. Taxed with the <b>NRB at death</b> at <b>40%</b>, after cumulation. <b>Taper relief</b> may apply. The <b>donee</b> is primarily liable."],[308,"How is an LCT taxed when made?","<b>20%</b> on the amount above the <b>available NRB</b>, after cumulating chargeable transfers in the previous <b>7 years</b>."],[309,"How does it matter whether the donor or the trustees pay the lifetime tax?","<b>Trustees</b> pay: 20% of the excess. <b>Donor</b> pays: the gift is <b>grossed up</b>, so tax is <b>25%</b> (20/80) of the net excess."],[310,"What happens to an LCT if the donor dies within 7 years?","<b>Recalculated</b> at <b>40%</b> with the <b>NRB at death</b>, with <b>taper relief</b>, and <b>credit</b> for lifetime tax. The <b>trustees</b> pay any extra. <b>No refund</b> if lifetime tax was higher."],[315,"What are the taper relief percentages?","Tax payable: <b>0-3 years 100%</b>. <b>3-4 years 80%</b>. <b>4-5 years 60%</b>. <b>5-6 years 40%</b>. <b>6-7 years 20%</b>. <b>7+ nil</b>."],[316,"What is the spouse exemption?","<b>Unlimited</b>, for lifetime and death. If the donor is <b>long-term UK resident</b> and the spouse is <b>not</b>, it is capped at <b>£325,000</b> unless the spouse <b>elects</b>. <b>Cohabitants do not qualify</b>."],[318,"How does the annual exemption work?","<b>£3,000</b> per tax year. Unused amount <b>carries forward one year only</b>. The <b>current year</b> is used <b>first</b>. <b>Lifetime only</b>."],[320,"What are the conditions for normal expenditure out of income?","<b>Habitual</b> expenditure, made <b>out of income</b> not capital, leaving the donor enough income for their <b>usual standard of living</b>. <b>Unlimited</b>. E.g. regular school fees or premiums on a trust policy."],[324,"What is a gift with reservation of benefit?","Property given away where the donor <b>keeps a benefit</b>, e.g. living in the given house rent-free. It is <b>treated as still in the donor's estate</b> at death, at its <b>value at death</b> (FA 1986 s.102)."]]},{"id":"iht-death-reliefs","title":"Inheritance tax on death: estate, reliefs, nil rate bands and calculation","q":[[0,"What is the standard IHT rate on death above the nil rate band?",["40%","20%","45%"],"The <b>death rate</b> is 40%, or 36% if enough is left to charity."],[1,"What share of the baseline amount left to charity gives the 36% rate?",["10% or more","5% or more","25% or more"],"<b>IHTA Sch 1A</b>: leaving at least <b>10%</b> of the baseline to charity lowers the rate to 36%."],[2,"Which of these is deductible for IHT on death?",["Funeral expenses","Probate fee","Solicitor's administration fees"],"<b>Funeral expenses</b> and debts are deductible. UK administration costs are not."],[3,"Which exemption applies on death?",["Spouse exemption","Annual exemption","Small gifts exemption"],"<b>Spouse</b> and <b>charity</b> exemptions apply on death. The annual exemption is lifetime only."],[4,"Does the spouse exemption apply to a cohabiting partner?",["No","Yes, after 2 years","Yes, after 5 years"],"The exemption requires <b>marriage or a civil partnership</b>."],[5,"What is the maximum transferable nil rate band?",["100% extra, so £650,000 in total","50% extra","£325,000 extra on top of RNRB"],"An unused NRB passes to the survivor as a percentage, up to a total of two NRBs."],[6,"What is the residence nil rate band?",["£175,000","£325,000","£125,000"],"The <b>RNRB</b> is £175,000, for a home closely inherited by direct descendants."],[7,"A home is left to the deceased's nephew. Is the RNRB available?",["No","Yes","Only if the estate is small"],"RNRB needs the home to pass to <b>direct descendants</b>, not nephews or siblings."],[8,"At what net estate value does the RNRB taper start?",["£2 million","£1 million","£500,000"],"RNRB reduces by <b>£1 for every £2</b> over £2 million."],[9,"What minimum ownership period applies for business property relief?",["2 years","7 years","12 months"],"BPR needs the property to be owned for <b>2 years</b> before the transfer."],[10,"Which business gets NO business property relief?",["A mainly property-investment business","A sole trade","An unquoted trading company"],"Businesses wholly or mainly <b>holding investments</b> get no BPR."],[11,"Quick succession relief applies to property received within how long?",["5 years","7 years","2 years"],"<b>s.141</b>: tax is reduced if the deceased inherited within <b>5 years</b> before death."]],"c":[[334,"What is included in the death estate?","The <b>free estate</b>. The deceased's <b>share of joint property</b>. <b>QIIP</b> trust property. <b>Gift with reservation</b> property. A <b>life policy payable to the estate</b>. From 6 April 2027, most <b>unused pension funds</b>."],[338,"What is the spouse exemption on death?","<b>Unlimited</b> for property passing to a spouse or civil partner, outright or on an <b>IPDI</b> trust. Capped at <b>£325,000</b> where the deceased was long-term UK resident and the spouse is not, unless the spouse elects. <b>Cohabitants</b> do not qualify."],[339,"When does the 36% rate apply?","Where <b>10% or more</b> of the <b>baseline amount</b> is left to charity. The rest of the estate is taxed at <b>36%</b> instead of 40%."],[341,"Which property gets 100% BPR?","A <b>business</b> or <b>interest in a business</b> (sole trade, partnership share). <b>Unquoted shares</b> in a trading company."],[342,"Which property gets 50% BPR?","<b>AIM</b> shares and other shares traded on a recognised stock exchange market but designated \"not listed\" (from 6 April 2026); private-company unquoted trading shares still get 100% within the £2.5m allowance. <b>Controlling quoted</b> shares. <b>Land, buildings or machinery</b> owned by the transferor and used by <b>their controlled company</b> or <b>partnership</b>."],[343,"What ownership period does BPR need?","<b>2 years</b> before the transfer, or replacement of other relevant business property."],[350,"What are the APR ownership conditions?","<b>Occupied</b> by the transferor for agriculture for <b>2 years</b>. Or <b>owned for 7 years</b> and occupied for agriculture by anyone throughout."],[351,"What rate of APR applies?","<b>100%</b> with <b>vacant possession</b> (or within 12 months) or a tenancy from <b>1 September 1995</b>. Otherwise <b>50%</b>."],[353,"What is the £2.5 million allowance for BPR and APR?","From <b>6 April 2026</b>, 100% relief applies only to the <b>first £2.5m</b> of <b>combined</b> 100% BPR and APR property. The excess gets <b>50%</b>."],[358,"How does the transferable NRB work?","A predeceased spouse's <b>unused percentage</b> of NRB is added to the survivor's NRB <b>as a percentage of the NRB at the second death</b>, up to <b>100%</b> extra (max <b>£650,000</b>). Claim normally within <b>2 years</b> of the end of the month of death."],[359,"What is the RNRB and what does it need?","An extra <b>£175,000</b> on <b>death only</b>. It needs a <b>qualifying residential interest</b> (a home the deceased <b>lived in at some time</b>) that is <b>closely inherited</b> by <b>direct descendants</b>."],[362,"How is the RNRB tapered?","Reduced by <b>£1 for every £2</b> the <b>net estate</b> (after debts, <b>before</b> exemptions and reliefs) exceeds <b>£2 million</b>."]]},{"id":"income-cgt-administration","title":"Income tax and CGT during administration","q":[[0,"Is death a disposal for CGT?",["No","Yes, at market value","Yes, if the estate is large"],"Death is <b>not a disposal</b>. The deceased's lifetime gains are wiped out (TCGA s.62)."],[1,"At what value do PRs acquire the deceased's assets for CGT?",["Market value at death","The deceased's original cost","Value at the date of the will"],"The base cost is the <b>probate value</b>, giving the death uplift."],[2,"PRs sell shares for more than their probate value. What is the gain?",["Sale price less probate value","Sale price less original cost","Sale price only"],"The PRs are taxed only on growth <b>since death</b>."],[3,"PRs transfer an asset to a beneficiary in specie. Is that a CGT disposal?",["No","Yes, at market value","Yes, if it rose in value"],"An <b>assent</b> or <b>appropriation</b> is not a disposal. The beneficiary takes the probate value."],[4,"For how long do PRs get an annual exempt amount?",["Tax year of death and next 2 years","Only the tax year of death","Throughout the administration"],"PRs get the annual exempt amount for the <b>year of death plus 2 more tax years</b>."],[5,"Can PRs pass their capital losses to a beneficiary?",["No","Yes, in full","Yes, within 2 years"],"PRs' losses stay with the <b>PRs</b> and cannot go to beneficiaries."],[6,"Within what period must CGT on a UK residential property sale be reported?",["60 days of completion","30 days of completion","12 months"],"UK residential property CGT must be reported and paid within <b>60 days</b>."],[7,"At what rates do PRs pay income tax on estate income?",["Basic rates only","Higher rate","Additional rate"],"PRs get <b>no allowances</b> and never pay the higher or additional rates."],[8,"What form tells a beneficiary about estate income and tax paid?",["R185","IHT400","SA100"],"The PRs give each beneficiary an <b>R185 (Estate Income)</b>."],[9,"What is the time limit for a deed of variation to be read back?",["2 years from death","6 months from death","12 months from the grant"],"<b>IHTA s.142</b> and <b>TCGA s.62(6)</b> need a written variation within <b>2 years of death</b>."],[10,"A deed of variation redirects a legacy. What else must it contain to be read back?",["A statement that s.142 applies","The PRs' consent only","A court order"],"A variation needs a <b>statement</b> to be read back. A disclaimer is read back automatically."],[11,"A parent varies a legacy to her minor child. Who pays tax on the income over £100?",["The parent","The child","The estate"],"There is <b>no income tax read-back</b>. The parent is treated as settlor."]],"c":[[368,"What are the three periods for income tax and CGT on a death?","<b>Up to death</b>: the deceased's own tax, a <b>debt</b> of the estate. <b>During administration</b>: the <b>PRs</b> pay. <b>After an asset passes</b>: the <b>beneficiary</b> pays."],[369,"Is death a disposal for CGT?","<b>No</b> (TCGA s.62). The PRs acquire assets at <b>probate value</b>, wiping out lifetime gains."],[373,"At what rates do PRs pay income tax?","The <b>basic rate</b> for the type of income. <b>No personal, savings or dividend allowances</b>. They <b>never</b> pay higher or additional rates."],[374,"What is the £500 de minimis rule for estates?","If total estate income in a tax year is <b>£500 or less</b>, <b>no income tax</b>. Above £500, tax is due on the <b>whole</b> amount."],[379,"How is income paid to residuary beneficiaries taxed?","As <b>their</b> income, with <b>credit</b> for tax the PRs paid. The PRs give form <b>R185</b> showing gross income and tax paid."],[383,"What annual exempt amount do PRs get?","The <b>same as an individual</b>, for the <b>tax year of death and the next 2 tax years</b> only."],[387,"Is a transfer of an asset to a beneficiary a disposal for CGT?","<b>No.</b> An <b>assent</b> or <b>appropriation</b> in specie is not a disposal. The beneficiary acquires at <b>probate value</b> at the <b>date of death</b> (s.62(4))."],[391,"What does read-back mean for a variation?","The variation is treated <b>as if made by the deceased</b>: <b>IHTA s.142</b> for IHT and <b>TCGA s.62(6)</b> for CGT."],[392,"What are the conditions for read-back?","In <b>writing</b> within <b>2 years of death</b>. Signed by the <b>person giving up</b> the benefit. <b>No consideration</b>. For a variation, a <b>statement</b> that s.142 and/or s.62(6) apply. The <b>PRs</b> must sign if more IHT becomes payable."]]}]},{"id":"accounts","title":"Solicitors Accounts","stop":9,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><rect x=\"-66\" y=\"-42\" width=\"10\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\"/><circle cx=\"-61\" cy=\"-66\" r=\"26\" fill=\"#52B788\"/><circle cx=\"-71\" cy=\"-72\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-53\" cy=\"-62\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-59\" cy=\"-80\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-73\" cy=\"-56\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-49\" cy=\"-76\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-36\" y=\"-10\" width=\"124\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8B5A2B\"/><path d=\"M-18,-34 q4,-8 10,-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><ellipse cx=\"-18\" cy=\"-22\" rx=\"18.75\" ry=\"13.5\" fill=\"#FF7F11\"/><path d=\"M-18,-35.5 V-10\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M10,-29.200000000000003 q4,-8 10,-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><ellipse cx=\"10\" cy=\"-19.6\" rx=\"15\" ry=\"10.8\" fill=\"#FF7F11\"/><path d=\"M10,-30.4 V-10\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M40,-14 l-6,-14 M40,-14 l0,-16 M40,-14 l6,-14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"34,-12 46,-12 40,2\" fill=\"#FF7F11\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M58,-14 l-6,-14 M58,-14 l0,-16 M58,-14 l6,-14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"52,-12 64,-12 58,2\" fill=\"#FF7F11\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M76,-14 l-6,-14 M76,-14 l0,-16 M76,-14 l6,-14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"70,-12 82,-12 76,2\" fill=\"#FF7F11\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"framework-client-money","title":"The Accounts Rules and client money","q":[[0,"A client sends £800 'towards your fees' before any bill. Which account?",["Client account","Business account","Either, at the firm's choice"],"No bill yet, so it is <b>client money</b> (r.2.1(d)) and belongs in the <b>client account</b>."],[1,"The client pays a bill the firm delivered last week. Which account?",["Business account","Client account","A designated deposit account"],"Once a bill is delivered, the money is <b>firm money</b>. It goes to the <b>business account</b>."],[2,"Which rule defines client money?",["Rule 2.1","Rule 4.1","Rule 5.1"],"<b>Rule 2.1</b> lists what counts as client money, such as money held for clients, third parties or as trustee."],[3,"Who is covered by the SRA Accounts Rules 2019?",["The firm, its managers and employees","Partners only","The firm's cashier only"],"The rules apply to <b>authorised bodies, managers and employees</b> (r.1.1), not just the cashier."],[4,"A deposit is held as stakeholder. Whose money is it?",["A third party's (client money)","The firm's","The seller's alone"],"Stakeholder money is held for both sides, so it is <b>client money</b> held for a <b>third party</b> (r.2.1(b))."],[5,"Client money must be paid into client account...",["Promptly","Within 14 days","By the next working day exactly"],"<b>Promptly</b> has no fixed number of days. It means as soon as reasonably possible."],[6,"Which payer's money for costs can go straight to business account (r.2.3)?",["The Legal Aid Agency, for costs","The Land Registry","Any local authority"],"Legal Aid Agency payments <b>for your costs</b> are an exception to paying into client account (r.2.3(b))."],[7,"A client agrees in writing that a cheque goes straight to a third party. Allowed?",["Yes, by written alternative arrangement","No, never","Only with SRA consent"],"Rule 2.3(c) permits an <b>alternative arrangement in writing</b> agreed in the individual circumstances."],[8,"Which firm need not hold a client account at all?",["One holding only advance fees and its own-liability disbursements","One that holds any SDLT money","One that acts as an executor"],"The r.2.2 exemption covers only money on account of fees and <b>disbursements the firm itself is liable for</b>, with the client told in advance."],[9,"A matter ends. The client says 'keep the money for now'. What must the firm do?",["Return it promptly","Hold it until asked","Move it to business account"],"Once there is no proper reason to hold money, it must be <b>returned promptly</b> (r.2.5)."],[10,"What is the most a firm may send to charity as a residual balance without SRA approval?",["£500 per client matter","£100 per client","£1,000 per client matter"],"The SRA's prescribed circumstances allow <b>£500 or less</b> per client matter, after reasonable steps to trace."],[11,"Can a firm deduct tracing costs from a residual balance paid to charity?",["No","Yes, if reasonable","Yes, up to half"],"The firm must <b>not deduct</b> its own costs from a residual balance paid to charity."]],"c":[[5,"What are the four categories of client money in r.2.1?","<b>(a)</b> Money for <b>regulated services</b> to a <b>client</b>. <b>(b)</b> Money held for a <b>third party</b>. <b>(c)</b> Money held as <b>trustee or office-holder</b>. <b>(d)</b> Money for <b>fees and unpaid disbursements</b> received <b>before a bill</b> is delivered."],[8,"When is money for costs client money and when is it firm money?","<b>Client money before</b> a bill or written notification of costs is delivered. <b>Firm money after</b> it."],[9,"What are the main examples of firm money?","Money paying a bill <b>already delivered</b>. Money <b>reimbursing</b> a disbursement the firm <b>already paid</b> from business account. Interest on the business account."],[10,"A bill including an unpaid counsel's fee has been delivered. Client pays. Where does the money go?","It is <b>firm money</b>, so it may go to <b>business account</b>. The firm must still pay counsel <b>promptly</b>. The old two-working-day rule <b>no longer exists</b>."],[11,"How quickly must client money be paid into client account?","<b>Promptly</b> (<b>r.2.3</b>). It is <b>not defined</b> and has <b>no fixed number of days</b>, so it means as soon as reasonably possible in the circumstances. The old 2011 'same or next working day' test <b>no longer applies</b>."],[12,"What are the exceptions to paying client money into client account?","<b>(a)</b> Trustee or office-holder money where client account would <b>conflict</b> with the office's rules. <b>(b)</b> <b>Legal Aid Agency</b> payments <b>for costs</b>. <b>(c)</b> An <b>alternative arrangement agreed in writing</b> with the client or third party in the individual circumstances."],[13,"When does a firm need no client account under r.2.2?","When <b>all</b> apply: its only client money is money <b>on account of fees and unpaid disbursements</b>. Those disbursements are ones <b>the firm is liable for</b> (e.g. counsel). It holds no client account for any other reason. It <b>told the client in advance</b> where the money is held."],[16,"When must client money be returned?","<b>Promptly</b>, as soon as there is <b>no longer any proper reason to hold it</b> (r.2.5). E.g. the matter ends, the deal falls through, or money was overpaid."],[18,"What are the conditions for withdrawing a residual balance without SRA authorisation?","<b>£500 or less</b> on any one client matter. <b>Reasonable steps</b> to return it. Paid to <b>charity</b>. <b>Records</b>, receipt and any indemnity kept <b>6 years</b>. A <b>central register</b>. <b>No deduction</b> of the firm's costs."],[19,"What if the residual balance is over £500?","The firm must apply to the SRA for <b>authorisation</b> before withdrawing it."]]},{"id":"client-account-withdrawals","title":"Client account and withdrawals","q":[[0,"What must the name of a client account include?",["The firm's name and the word 'client'","The client's name only","The word 'trust' only"],"Rule 3.2 requires the <b>authorised body's name</b> and the word <b>'client'</b>."],[1,"Where may client money be held?",["A bank or building society in England and Wales","Any bank worldwide","The firm's office safe"],"Rule 3.1 requires an account at a <b>bank or building society in England and Wales</b>."],[2,"A client wants the firm to pay her household bills via client account. Allowed?",["No, it is a banking facility","Yes, with written instructions","Yes, if she is a long-term client"],"Rule 3.3 bans <b>banking facilities</b>. Payments must relate to regulated services the firm delivers."],[3,"Which case concerned a firm paying a football club's ordinary bills through client account?",["Fuglers LLP v SRA","Salomon v Salomon","SRA v Wingate"],"In <b>Fuglers</b>, the firm improperly used client account as a <b>banking facility</b>."],[4,"How many grounds does r.5.1 give for withdrawing client money?",["Three","Two","Five"],"Purpose held, <b>client instructions</b>, or <b>SRA authority</b>: three grounds under r.5.1."],[5,"The firm holds £600 for Dan and needs to pay a £900 court fee for him. What can it do?",["Pay £600 from client, £300 from business","Pay £900 from client account","Wait for the next client deposit"],"A client's ledger cannot go overdrawn (r.5.3). Use the <b>business account</b> for the shortfall."],[6,"What does a debit balance on a client's client column show?",["The ledger is overdrawn, a breach","The client owes the firm","The firm has earned fees"],"A <b>debit on the client column</b> means other clients' money has been used, a breach of r.5.3."],[7,"A client's cheque has not cleared. The firm pays out against it and it bounces. What follows?",["A breach; replace the shortfall","No breach, it is the bank's fault","The client must replace it first"],"The ledger is overdrawn, so the firm must <b>replace</b> the money immediately from its own funds."],[8,"Client A lends money to client B using A's funds. What does the firm need?",["A's instructions","B's instructions only","Nothing, if repaid within a week"],"It is a withdrawal of A's money, so A's <b>instructions</b> are needed (r.5.1(b))."],[9,"Which entries record a £300 court fee paid from client account for Ravi?",["DR ledger client column; CR client cash","DR client cash; CR ledger client column","DR ledger business column; CR business cash"],"Payment out: <b>DR the ledger, CR cash</b>, both in the client books."],[10,"Which entries record a payment from business account for a client?",["DR ledger business column; CR business cash","DR ledger client column; CR client cash","DR business cash; CR ledger"],"The client owes the firm, so the <b>business column</b> is debited and business cash credited."],[11,"Must withdrawals from client account be authorised and supervised?",["Yes, under r.5.2","Only above £10,000","Only for the firm's own costs"],"Rule 5.2 requires <b>appropriate authorisation and supervision</b> of all withdrawals."]],"c":[[25,"Can a client account be overdrawn?","<b>Never.</b> The bank must always hold at least as much as the firm holds for all clients."],[26,"What is the banking facility rule?","A client account must <b>not provide banking facilities</b> (r.3.3). Every payment in, transfer or withdrawal must be <b>in respect of regulated services</b> the firm is delivering."],[28,"What are common banking facility red flags?","Receiving money to <b>pay on</b> with no legal transaction. Paying <b>household bills, suppliers or salaries</b>. <b>Leaving money</b> after the matter ends. Money from an <b>unexpected third party</b>, or a collapsed deal with a request to pay someone other than the payer."],[31,"On what grounds can client money be withdrawn?","<b>(a)</b> For the <b>purpose</b> for which it is held. <b>(b)</b> On the client's or third party's <b>instructions</b>. <b>(c)</b> With <b>SRA prior written authorisation</b> or in <b>prescribed circumstances</b> (r.5.1)."],[32,"What extra condition applies to withdrawing client money for the firm's costs?","A <b>bill or written notification of costs</b> must be given <b>first</b> (r.4.3)."],[34,"What is the sufficient funds rule?","Client money may be withdrawn only if <b>sufficient funds are held for that specific client</b> (r.5.3)."],[35,"What is the effect of paying more than is held for a client from client account?","Other clients' money is used, giving a <b>debit balance on the client column</b>. It is a <b>breach</b>, however short or honest."],[36,"The firm holds £1,000 for a client but must pay £1,500 for them. What can it do?","Pay <b>£1,000 from client account and £500 from business account</b>, or <b>all £1,500 from business account</b>. Never £1,500 from client account."],[37,"Is a payment against an uncleared cheque a breach?","<b>Not automatically</b>, because the cheque counts as money held. If it is <b>dishonoured</b>, the ledger is overdrawn, a breach the firm must replace <b>immediately</b> (r.6.1). Waiting for <b>clearance</b> is safest."],[38,"What is needed for an inter-client transfer?","A proper <b>r.5.1 ground</b> for withdrawing from the paying client's ledger, usually that client's <b>instructions</b> (r.5.1(b)). For a <b>private loan</b> between clients, get the lending client's instructions (best <b>in writing</b>) and watch for <b>conflict</b>. Record as DR payer's ledger, CR payee's ledger (client columns), <b>no cash entry</b>."]]},{"id":"separation-mixed-payments","title":"Keeping client and firm money separate","q":[[0,"Which rule requires client money to be kept separate from firm money?",["Rule 4.1","Rule 7.1","Rule 12.1"],"<b>Rule 4.1</b> requires separation, in both directions."],[1,"Is it a breach to leave the firm's own money in client account?",["Yes","No, it is a harmless cushion","Only if over £500"],"Firm money in client account blurs ownership and can hide a shortfall, so it <b>breaches r.4.1</b>."],[2,"A single cheque covers a delivered bill and money on account. What is it called?",["A mixed payment","A residual payment","A stakeholder payment"],"A receipt of both client and firm money is a <b>mixed payment</b> (r.4.2)."],[3,"A client pays £2,400: £1,200 for a delivered bill, £1,200 on account. Best handling?",["Pay all into client, then move £1,200 promptly","Pay all into business account","Pay all into client and leave it"],"Pay in to client, then <b>promptly transfer</b> the firm's share, or split on receipt."],[4,"Under the 2019 rules, how long has the firm to move the firm's share of a mixed payment?",["Promptly, no fixed days","14 days","5 working days"],"The old 14-day limit has gone. The rule says <b>promptly</b>."],[5,"What must the firm do before moving client money to pay its costs?",["Deliver a bill or written notification","Get SRA consent","Wait 14 days"],"<b>Rule 4.3</b> requires a bill or written notification of costs <b>before</b> the transfer."],[6,"The firm moves costs out and sends the bill the next day. Breach?",["Yes, r.4.3","No, the amount was right","No, if the client agrees later"],"The bill must come <b>first</b>. Sending it afterwards does not cure the breach."],[7,"Can an email count as written notification of costs?",["Yes, if in writing and before transfer","No, only a formal bill","Only if signed by a partner"],"Rule 4.3 allows a <b>written notification</b> of costs, which can be informal but must precede the transfer."],[8,"A client pays 'your estimate' before any bill. Client or firm money?",["Client money","Firm money","Mixed payment"],"With no bill or notification delivered, it is <b>client money</b> (r.2.1(d))."],[9,"Client money is paid into business account by mistake. What must the firm do?",["Move it to client account immediately","Leave it until the next reconciliation","Ask the client to approve"],"Correct the breach <b>promptly on discovery</b> and move it to client account (r.6.1)."],[10,"Which entries record moving £1,200 billed costs from client to business account (client books)?",["DR ledger client column; CR client cash","DR client cash; CR ledger client column","DR ledger business column; CR profit costs"],"Client-side transfer out: <b>DR client ledger (client column), CR client cash</b>. A business-side pair is also needed."],[11,"How many pairs of entries does a client-to-business transfer need?",["Two","One","Three"],"Two sets of books are touched, so <b>two pairs</b> (four entries)."]],"c":[[43,"What does the separation rule require?","<b>Keep client money separate</b> from the firm's money (r.4.1). No client money in business account, and <b>no firm money in client account</b>."],[45,"What firm money may properly be in client account?","The firm's share of a <b>mixed payment</b> until allocated (r.4.2). Money paid in to <b>replace a shortfall</b> (r.6.1), which is then treated as client money."],[47,"How must a mixed payment be dealt with?","<b>Allocate promptly</b> (r.4.2). Either pay the <b>whole into client account</b> and promptly transfer the firm's share, or <b>split on receipt</b>. <b>Never</b> pay it all into business account."],[48,"Is there a 14-day limit for transferring the firm's share of a mixed payment?","<b>No.</b> That was the 2011 rule. The 2019 rules say <b>promptly</b>, with no fixed days."],[50,"What can count as a written notification of costs?","Something less formal than a bill, e.g. a <b>letter or email</b> setting out costs incurred. It must be <b>in writing</b> and given <b>before</b> the transfer."],[52,"After a bill is delivered, what must happen to the billed sum held in client account?","It becomes <b>firm money</b> and must be transferred <b>promptly</b> to business account."],[54,"Client reimburses a disbursement the firm already paid from business account. Where does the money go?","<b>Business account</b>: it is firm money."],[55,"Client pays in advance for a disbursement not yet paid or billed. Where does it go?","<b>Client account</b>: it is client money under r.2.1(d)."],[62,"How is client money paid into business account by mistake corrected?","Transfer it to client account <b>immediately</b> (r.6.1). Business side: DR ledger business column, CR business cash. Client side: DR client cash, CR ledger client column."]]},{"id":"double-entry-basics","title":"Double-entry bookkeeping: the basics","q":[[0,"In double entry, every transaction is recorded...",["Twice, for equal amounts","Once, in the ledger","Once, in the cash book"],"One <b>DR</b> and one <b>CR</b> of the same amount keep the books balanced."],[1,"Money received into client account. Which entries?",["DR client cash; CR ledger client column","DR ledger client column; CR client cash","DR ledger business column; CR client cash"],"<b>Cash in = DR cash</b>, with the matching CR on the client's ledger."],[2,"What is the normal balance of a client ledger's client column?",["Credit","Debit","Nil"],"The firm owes the money to the client, so the normal balance is <b>CR</b>."],[3,"Can the client cash account ever have a credit balance?",["No, that means it is overdrawn","Yes, if a client is owed money","Yes, at year end"],"A <b>CR balance on client cash</b> would mean the client account is overdrawn, which is not allowed."],[4,"A bill of £1,000 plus £200 VAT is delivered. Which account is debited?",["Client ledger, business column, £1,200","Client cash, £1,200","Profit costs, £1,000"],"The client now owes £1,200: <b>DR client ledger (business column)</b>; CR profit costs £1,000 and HMRC £200. No cash moves."],[5,"Does delivering a bill need a cash entry?",["No","Yes, in business cash","Yes, in client cash"],"No money moves when a bill is delivered, so <b>no cash entry</b>."],[6,"Which entries record an inter-client transfer?",["DR giving ledger; CR receiving ledger, client columns","DR client cash; CR receiving ledger","DR business cash; CR giving ledger"],"The money stays in client account, so there is <b>no cash entry</b>."],[7,"The firm pays £2,000 rent. Which entries?",["DR rent account; CR business cash","DR business cash; CR rent account","DR client cash; CR rent account"],"Expense paid: <b>DR expense account, CR business cash</b>."],[8,"The bank pays interest on the firm's business account. Which entries?",["DR business cash; CR interest received","DR interest payable; CR business cash","DR client cash; CR interest received"],"Cash in is a <b>DR to business cash</b>, with CR to interest received."],[9,"Petty cash pays a £5 oath fee for a client. Which entries?",["DR ledger business column; CR petty cash","DR ledger client column; CR client cash","DR petty cash; CR ledger"],"Petty cash is firm money, so the <b>business column</b> is debited."],[10,"A ledger shows client column £5,000 CR, then £150 paid out. New balance?",["£4,850 CR","£5,150 CR","£4,850 DR"],"Payments out reduce the credit balance: £5,000 less £150 is <b>£4,850 CR</b>."],[11,"How many sets of books does a law firm keep?",["Two: client and business","One combined set","Three"],"The firm keeps separate <b>client</b> and <b>business</b> books."]],"c":[[67,"What do DR and CR mean on the client cash account?","<b>DR</b> = money <b>in</b>. <b>CR</b> = money <b>out</b>. Normal balance <b>DR</b>; it can <b>never</b> be CR."],[68,"Can the business cash account have a CR balance?","<b>Yes.</b> That means the business account is <b>overdrawn</b>, which is allowed because it is the firm's own money."],[69,"What do DR and CR mean on the client ledger client column?","<b>CR</b> = money <b>received</b> for the client. <b>DR</b> = money <b>paid out</b> for them. Normal balance <b>CR</b>. A <b>DR balance</b> is an <b>overdrawn ledger: a breach</b>."],[70,"What does the client ledger business column record?","<b>DR</b>: amounts the client <b>owes</b> the firm (bills, disbursements from business account). <b>CR</b>: <b>payments</b> to the firm. Normal balance <b>DR</b>."],[73,"What is the mirror rule?","<b>Money received</b>: DR cash, CR ledger. <b>Money paid out</b>: CR cash, DR ledger."],[74,"What are the entries for client money received?","<b>DR client cash</b>. <b>CR</b> client ledger <b>client</b> column."],[77,"What are the entries for delivering a bill of £1,000 plus £200 VAT?","<b>DR</b> ledger business column <b>£1,200</b>. <b>CR profit costs £1,000</b>. <b>CR HMRC £200</b>."],[78,"What are the entries for a transfer from client account to business account?","<b>Client books</b>: DR ledger client column, CR client cash. <b>Business books</b>: DR business cash, CR ledger business column."],[79,"What are the entries for a transfer from business account to client account?","<b>Business books</b>: DR ledger business column, CR business cash. <b>Client books</b>: DR client cash, CR ledger client column."]]},{"id":"bills-vat-disbursements","title":"Bills, VAT and disbursements","q":[[0,"A surveyor's invoice is addressed to the firm. Which method applies?",["Principal method","Agency method","Neither"],"An invoice addressed to the firm means the <b>principal method</b>."],[1,"Under the principal method, which account pays the invoice?",["Business account only","Client account","Either account"],"The invoice is the firm's own debt, so pay from <b>business account</b> even if client money is held."],[2,"Does the firm reclaim input VAT on an agency-method disbursement?",["No","Yes","Only if over £100"],"Under the agency method the VAT is the <b>client's</b>, so the firm does not reclaim it."],[3,"Do Land Registry fees carry VAT?",["No","Yes, at 20%","Only on large fees"],"Land Registry fees are <b>not a VAT supply</b>, so an agency disbursement with no VAT."],[4,"How does HMRC treat property search fees for VAT?",["Part of the firm's supply, with VAT","A disbursement, no VAT","Exempt"],"Per Revenue and Customs Brief 6 (2020), searches are the firm's supply: <b>principal method</b>."],[5,"Counsel bills the firm £800 plus £160 VAT (principal method). Where is the £160 debited?",["HMRC (VAT) account","The client ledger","Profit costs"],"Principal method: the firm <b>reclaims the input VAT</b>, so <b>DR HMRC £160</b>. The client ledger is debited £800 only."],[6,"A bill is abated by £200 plus £40 VAT. Which account is debited £200?",["Profit costs","Bad debts","Client cash"],"An abatement reverses part of the bill: <b>DR profit costs</b> £200 and HMRC £40, CR the client ledger business column £240."],[7,"Which account is debited when a bad debt is written off with no VAT relief?",["Bad debts account","Profit costs","Client cash"],"A bad debt is an expense: <b>DR bad debts</b>, CR ledger business column."],[8,"After how long may a firm claim VAT bad debt relief?",["6 months overdue","3 months","12 months"],"VAT bad debt relief needs the debt <b>at least 6 months overdue</b> and written off."],[9,"Does an abatement or a bad debt debit profit costs?",["Abatement only","Bad debt only","Both"],"An abatement reduces the fee (<b>DR profit costs</b>). A bad debt is an expense."],[10,"Who must receive the supply for the agency method?",["The client","The firm","The bank"],"Under the agency method the <b>client</b> receives the supply and the firm acts as agent."],[11,"Where must a central record of bills be kept?",["Readily accessible central record","On each client file only","Only with the accountant"],"<b>Rule 8.4</b> requires a central record of all bills and written notifications of costs."]],"c":[[84,"What is output tax and input tax?","<b>Output tax</b>: VAT the firm charges on its fees, owed to <b>HMRC</b>. <b>Input tax</b>: VAT the firm pays on purchases, which it can <b>reclaim</b>."],[86,"Which payments carry no VAT?","<b>SDLT</b>, <b>Land Registry fees</b>, <b>court fees</b> and <b>probate application fees</b>."],[88,"What decides agency or principal method for a disbursement?","<b>Who received the supply</b>: the <b>client</b> (agency) or the <b>firm</b> (principal)."],[89,"How do you spot an agency method disbursement?","The invoice is <b>addressed to the client</b>, or there is <b>no VAT supply</b> at all (SDLT, Land Registry, court, probate fees)."],[90,"How do you spot a principal method disbursement?","The invoice is <b>addressed to the firm</b> (e.g. counsel's or an expert's fee note). Also <b>property search fees</b>."],[92,"Which account can pay a principal method disbursement?","<b>Business account only</b>, even if client money is held for the client."],[94,"Does the firm reclaim input VAT on agency and principal disbursements?","<b>Agency</b>: <b>no</b>, it is the client's VAT. <b>Principal</b>: <b>yes</b>, the firm reclaims it."],[96,"What are the entries for a principal disbursement paid from business account?","<b>DR</b> ledger <b>business</b> column (<b>net</b>). <b>DR HMRC</b> (VAT). <b>CR business cash</b> (<b>gross</b>)."],[99,"On a bill including a principal disbursement already paid, what is credited to profit costs?","Only the <b>fees</b>. The disbursement is <b>not</b> credited to profit costs or entered again. VAT is charged on <b>fees plus the net disbursement</b>."],[101,"What is an abatement and what are its entries?","A <b>reduction</b> to a delivered bill, with a <b>VAT credit note</b>. <b>DR profit costs</b>, <b>DR HMRC</b>, <b>CR ledger business column</b>."],[102,"What is a bad debt and what are its entries?","A bill that will never be paid. <b>DR bad debts</b> account. <b>CR ledger business column</b> with the total."]]},{"id":"interest-breaches","title":"Interest and correcting breaches","q":[[0,"Which rule requires the firm to pay clients interest?",["Rule 7.1","Rule 5.1","Rule 12.1"],"<b>Rule 7.1</b>: account to clients for a <b>fair sum</b> of interest."],[1,"How much interest must a firm pay on client money?",["A fair sum","All the interest earned","A fixed 1%"],"The test is a <b>fair sum</b>, not all interest and not a fixed rate."],[2,"Can a firm agree that no interest is payable?",["Yes, by informed written agreement","Never","Yes, by a line in a website"],"Rule 7.2 allows a <b>written agreement</b> made with informed consent."],[3,"Who gets interest on a stakeholder deposit on completion?",["Normally the seller","Always the buyer","The firm"],"Interest follows the deposit, which normally goes to the <b>seller</b> on completion."],[4,"Interest on a general client account is first paid to whom?",["The firm","The client directly","The SRA"],"The bank pays the pooled interest to the <b>firm</b>, which then pays clients a fair sum."],[5,"Interest on a designated client account is credited to...",["The client's account directly","The firm's business account","The interest payable account"],"Designated account interest goes <b>directly</b> to the client's account."],[6,"Which entries record fair interest owed to a client from a general account?",["DR interest payable; CR ledger business column","DR client cash; CR interest payable","DR business cash; CR ledger client column"],"The firm owes the interest, an <b>expense</b>, so debit interest payable."],[7,"When must a breach of the Accounts Rules be corrected?",["Promptly on discovery","At the next accountant's report","Within 6 months"],"<b>Rule 6.1</b>: correct breaches promptly upon discovery."],[8,"Client money is missing after a bank error. Who replaces it?",["The firm, immediately","The bank first","The client"],"The firm replaces it <b>immediately</b> from its own money, then pursues the bank."],[9,"A shortfall is replaced at once. Is the breach cancelled?",["No, it must still be recorded","Yes, completely","Yes, if under £500"],"Replacing the money does not remove the breach, which must be <b>recorded</b> and perhaps reported."],[10,"Who must make sure serious Accounts Rules breaches are reported to the SRA?",["The COFA","The bank","The cashier alone"],"The <b>COFA</b> must ensure prompt reporting of serious breaches."],[11,"Which is always a serious breach needing a report?",["Misappropriation of client money","A £15 posting error corrected same day","A late bank statement"],"<b>Misappropriation</b> is always serious and must be reported."]],"c":[[105,"What does r.7.1 require about interest?","The firm must <b>account for a fair sum of interest</b> on client money held for clients or third parties."],[106,"How can a firm contract out of paying interest?","By <b>written agreement</b> with the client or third party, after giving <b>sufficient information</b> for <b>informed consent</b> (r.7.2)."],[107,"Is there a minimum interest rate, amount or period?","<b>No.</b> \"Fair\" depends on the circumstances. Firms should have a written <b>interest policy</b>, usually in the <b>client care letter</b>."],[112,"What are the entries to record interest owed to a client on a general client account?","<b>DR interest payable</b> account. <b>CR</b> client ledger <b>business</b> column. Then pay it from business account or transfer it to client account."],[113,"What are the entries for interest on a separate designated client account?","<b>DR client cash</b> (designated account). <b>CR</b> client ledger <b>client</b> column. No business entries."],[114,"What does r.6.1 require when a breach is discovered?","<b>Correct it promptly on discovery</b>. Money improperly withheld or withdrawn must be <b>immediately</b> paid in or replaced."],[115,"Whose money is used to replace a shortfall, and does fault matter?","The <b>firm's own money</b>, <b>whether or not anyone was at fault</b>, without waiting to recover it from the client, bank or insurer."],[116,"How is an overdrawn client ledger corrected?","Transfer the shortfall from business to client account. <b>Business</b>: DR ledger business column, CR business cash. <b>Client</b>: DR client cash, CR ledger client column."],[120,"Who must record and report breaches?","The <b>COFA</b> takes reasonable steps to ensure compliance and that <b>serious breaches</b> are <b>promptly reported</b> to the SRA (Code for Firms para 9.2). The firm keeps records showing compliance (para 2.2), in practice a <b>breach log</b>."],[124,"Does replacing the money cancel the breach?","<b>No.</b> It must still be <b>recorded</b> and, if serious, <b>reported</b>."]]},{"id":"records-reconciliation","title":"Records, reconciliation and retention","q":[[0,"How often must a client account reconciliation be done?",["At least every 5 weeks","Every 3 months","Every 6 weeks"],"<b>Rule 8.3</b> says at least every <b>five weeks</b>."],[1,"How often must bank statements be obtained?",["At least every 5 weeks","Monthly exactly","Every 6 months"],"<b>Rule 8.2</b>: at least every five weeks, for client and business accounts."],[2,"Which three figures are compared in a reconciliation?",["Bank, cash book and client ledger total","Bank, bills and VAT","Cash book, bills and profit costs"],"The <b>three-way reconciliation</b> compares bank, cash book and ledgers."],[3,"Who may sign off a reconciliation?",["The COFA or a manager","The cashier","Any trainee"],"Sign-off must be by the <b>COFA or a manager</b>."],[4,"How long must accounting records be kept?",["At least 6 years","2 years","12 months"],"<b>Rule 13.1</b>: store securely for at least six years."],[5,"Bank balance £50,000, unpresented cheque £3,000, lodgement £1,000. Adjusted bank?",["£48,000","£54,000","£52,000"],"£50,000 less £3,000 plus £1,000 gives <b>£48,000</b>."],[6,"A cheque has been issued but not yet gone through the bank. How is it treated?",["Deducted from the bank balance","Added to the bank balance","Ignored"],"<b>Unpresented cheques</b> are deducted from the statement balance."],[7,"Does a balanced reconciliation prove no breach?",["No, one ledger may still be overdrawn","Yes","Yes, if signed by a partner"],"One overdrawn ledger can hide behind <b>balanced totals</b>. It is still a breach."],[8,"What must the firm do about differences found in a reconciliation?",["Promptly investigate and resolve","Wait for the accountant","Record them next year"],"Rule 8.3 requires differences to be <b>promptly investigated and resolved</b>."],[9,"Who is a 'manager' for sign-off purposes?",["A partner, LLP member or director","An office manager","A senior cashier"],"In SRA terms, a <b>manager</b> is a partner, member or director."],[10,"Client ledgers must be kept in what order?",["Chronological, as transactions happen","Alphabetical","Grouped by client type"],"Records must be <b>contemporaneous and chronological</b> (r.8.1)."],[11,"What does the list of client ledger balances show?",["What the firm owes clients","What clients owe the firm","The firm's profit"],"It shows total <b>liabilities</b> to clients and third parties."]],"c":[[126,"What standard must accounting records meet under r.8.1?","<b>Accurate, contemporaneous</b> (made at the time) <b>and chronological</b> (in date order)."],[128,"What list must be kept under r.8.1(b)?","A <b>list of all client ledger balances</b> with a <b>running total</b>, showing the firm's total <b>liabilities</b> to clients and third parties."],[129,"What is the cash book under r.8.1(c)?","The firm's own record of the <b>client bank account</b>, with a <b>running total</b> of all client account transactions."],[131,"How often must bank statements be obtained, and for which accounts?","<b>At least every five weeks</b>, for <b>all client accounts and business accounts</b> (r.8.2)."],[132,"How often must client account be reconciled?","<b>At least every five weeks</b> (r.8.3). Monthly complies; six weeks does not."],[134,"Who must sign off the reconciliation?","The <b>COFA or a manager</b> (partner, LLP member or director). <b>Not</b> an office manager or cashier."],[136,"How is the bank statement balance adjusted?","<b>Deduct unpresented payments</b> (recorded but not yet through the bank). <b>Add outstanding lodgements</b> (received but not yet shown)."],[137,"Client ledger total exceeds the adjusted bank and cash book balance. What does this show?","A <b>shortfall</b>: the firm owes clients more than it holds. It must investigate and <b>replace missing money immediately</b> (r.6.1)."],[139,"Can an overdrawn client ledger be netted off against other balances?","<b>No.</b> It must be shown as a <b>breach</b>, even if the totals agree, and the shortfall replaced."],[141,"How long must accounting records be kept?","<b>At least six years</b>, stored <b>securely</b> (r.13.1)."]]},{"id":"joint-own-tpma","title":"Joint accounts, clients' own accounts and TPMAs","q":[[0,"Which rule covers money held jointly with another law firm?",["Rule 9","Rule 10","Rule 11"],"<b>Rule 9</b> deals with joint accounts."],[1,"Which rule covers a solicitor-attorney operating a client's own account?",["Rule 10","Rule 9","Rule 11"],"<b>Rule 10</b>: operating a client's own account as signatory."],[2,"Which rule covers third party managed accounts?",["Rule 11","Rule 9","Rule 12"],"<b>Rule 11</b> covers TPMAs."],[3,"Which rules still apply to a joint account?",["r.8.2 statements and r.8.4 bills record","All of Part 2","r.8.3 only"],"For a joint account, only <b>r.8.2</b> and <b>r.8.4</b> apply."],[4,"Which account also needs a five-weekly reconciliation?",["The client's own account (r.10)","A joint account (r.9)","A TPMA"],"Only <b>rule 10</b> adds <b>r.8.3 reconciliation</b> to statements and the bills record."],[5,"A solicitor takes money from the client's own account into the firm's client account. What is it now?",["Client money under r.2.1(c)","Still governed by r.10 only","Firm money"],"Once in client account it is <b>client money</b> and the full rules apply."],[6,"A solicitor is an LPA attorney signing on the donor's bank account. Which rule?",["Rule 10","Rule 9","Rule 11"],"The donor's own account, operated by the solicitor, falls under <b>rule 10</b>."],[7,"What is a TPMA?",["An account run by an independent payment provider","A client account with two signatories","A firm's designated deposit account"],"The <b>provider</b> holds the money, not the firm. It must be regulated by the FCA."],[8,"Using a TPMA, the firm must not...",["Receive or hold the client's money","Tell the client about fees","Obtain statements"],"Rule 11.1(a): the TPMA must <b>not result in the firm holding the money</b>."],[9,"What must a client understand before a firm uses a TPMA?",["Fee terms and right to end and dispute payments","Only the firm's hourly rate","The firm's bank sort code"],"Rule 11.1(b): fee arrangements and the client's <b>right to terminate and dispute</b> requests."],[10,"Does operating a joint account trigger an accountant's report?",["Yes","No","Only over £250,000"],"Rule 12.1 applies to firms that <b>operate a joint account</b> or client's own account."],[11,"Who must obtain TPMA statements?",["The firm, regularly","Only the client","The SRA"],"Rule 11.2: obtain <b>regular statements</b> that accurately reflect all transactions."]],"c":[[147,"Which Part 2 rules apply to a joint account?","Only <b>r.8.2</b> (statements every five weeks) and <b>r.8.4</b> (central record of bills). <b>No reconciliation</b>."],[148,"Does a joint account trigger the accountant's report duty?","<b>Yes</b> (r.12.1), and its balance counts towards the <b>exemption limits</b> (r.12.3)."],[149,"When does r.10 (client's own account) apply?","When the firm <b>operates a client's own account as signatory</b>, e.g. as <b>LPA attorney</b>, <b>Court of Protection deputy</b>, or signatory on an <b>estate account</b> in the PRs' names."],[150,"Which Part 2 rules apply to a client's own account?","<b>r.8.2</b> (statements), <b>r.8.3</b> (<b>reconciliation</b>, signed off by COFA or manager) and <b>r.8.4</b> (bills record)."],[153,"What happens if an attorney moves money from the donor's account into the firm's client account?","It becomes <b>client money under r.2.1(c)</b> and the <b>full</b> rules apply. r.10 applies only while money stays in the client's account."],[154,"What is a TPMA?","A <b>third party managed account</b> run by an <b>FCA-regulated payment service provider</b>. The <b>provider holds the money</b>, not the firm."],[156,"When may a firm use a TPMA?","Only if it <b>does not result in the firm receiving or holding client money</b>, and <b>before accepting instructions</b> the client is informed of and understands the terms (r.11.1)."],[158,"What ongoing duty applies to a TPMA?","Obtain <b>regular statements</b> from the provider and ensure they <b>accurately reflect all transactions</b> (r.11.2)."]]},{"id":"accountants-reports","title":"Accountants' reports","q":[[0,"Within what time must a firm obtain its accountant's report?",["6 months after the period ends","3 months","12 months"],"<b>Rule 12.1(a)</b>: within six months of the end of the accounting period."],[1,"Under the current rules, when must the report go to the SRA?",["If qualified with client money at risk","Always","Never"],"Rule 12.1(b): deliver only if qualified to show money <b>at risk</b>."],[2,"A report is unqualified. Must it be sent to the SRA now?",["No, keep it","Yes, within 6 months","Yes, within 28 days"],"Under the <b>current examinable rule</b>, a clean report is obtained and kept."],[3,"Which firm is exempt because all its client money is Legal Aid Agency money?",["One where all client money is LAA money","One with some LAA money","One with LAA money over £10,000"],"The legal aid exemption needs <b>all</b> client money to come from the LAA."],[4,"What are the low-balance exemption limits?",["Average £10,000 and maximum £250,000","Average £25,000 and maximum £100,000","Average £10,000 or maximum £250,000"],"Both limbs apply: average <b>£10,000</b> AND maximum <b>£250,000</b>."],[5,"Average balance £8,000, highest £300,000. Exempt?",["No","Yes","Yes, if LAA"],"The maximum exceeds £250,000, so the firm is <b>not exempt</b>."],[6,"Average balance £6,000, highest £90,000. Exempt?",["Yes","No","Only if audited"],"Both limits are met, so the firm is <b>exempt</b>."],[7,"Whose figures decide the balance test?",["Bank statement balances","The firm's cash book","The ledger total"],"Rule 12.3 uses the <b>statement or passbook balance</b>."],[8,"Which accounts count towards the exemption limits?",["Client, joint and client's own accounts","Client account only","Business account"],"Include <b>all client accounts, joint accounts and clients' own accounts</b>."],[9,"Who can sign an accountant's report?",["A chartered accountant at or for a registered auditor","Any bookkeeper","The firm's COFA"],"Rule 12.5: member of a <b>chartered body</b> who is, or works for, a registered auditor."],[10,"A firm's year ends 31 March 2026. Report due by?",["30 September 2026","30 June 2026","31 March 2027"],"Six months after 31 March is <b>30 September</b>."],[11,"A firm held client money for one week of the year. Must it obtain a report?",["Yes, unless exempt","No, it was too short","Only if over £250,000"],"Holding client money <b>at any time</b> in the period triggers the duty, unless an exemption applies."]],"c":[[162,"Who must obtain an accountant's report?","A firm that at any time in the period <b>held or received client money</b>, or <b>operated a joint account or client's own account</b> (r.12.1)."],[163,"When must the report be obtained?","Within <b>six months of the end</b> of the accounting period."],[164,"Which reports must currently be sent to the SRA?","Only a report <b>qualified</b> to show client or third-party money <b>is, has been or is likely to be at risk</b>, within <b>six months</b> of the period end."],[165,"What happens to an unqualified report or one qualified for minor breaches without risk?","It is <b>obtained and kept</b>, not sent. The firm produces it if asked."],[166,"What is the legal aid exemption from the accountant's report?","<b>All</b> client money in the period is from the <b>Legal Aid Agency</b> (r.12.2(a)). A single private client's money takes the firm outside it."],[167,"What is the low balance exemption?","Client money balances do not exceed an <b>average of £10,000 and</b> a <b>maximum of £250,000</b> in the period (r.12.2(b)). <b>Both</b> must be met."],[168,"How are balances measured for the low balance exemption?","Using <b>bank statement balances</b> (not the firm's books) of <b>all client accounts</b>, plus <b>joint accounts and clients' own accounts</b> operated (r.12.3)."],[169,"When can the SRA require a report despite an exemption?","If the firm <b>stops operating</b> as an authorised body holding a client account, or it is in the <b>public interest</b> (r.12.4)."],[170,"Who can prepare an accountant's report?","A <b>member of a chartered accountancy body</b> who <b>is, or works for, a registered auditor</b> (r.12.5)."]]},{"id":"conveyancing-accounts","title":"Accounts in conveyancing (property context)","q":[[0,"Who is the deposit held for when the seller's solicitor is stakeholder?",["Both buyer and seller","The seller alone","The buyer alone"],"A stakeholder holds neutrally for <b>both parties</b> until completion."],[1,"Can a stakeholder release the deposit to the seller before completion on the seller's say-so?",["No","Yes","Yes, if the seller is a client"],"One party cannot authorise release of money held for <b>both</b>."],[2,"Which entries move a stakeholder deposit to the seller's ledger on completion?",["DR stakeholder ledger; CR seller's ledger","DR client cash; CR seller's ledger","DR seller's ledger; CR stakeholder ledger"],"An <b>inter-client transfer</b> with no cash entry."],[3,"Usual deposit on a residential sale?",["10% of the price","5% of the price","20% of the price"],"The usual deposit is <b>10%</b>, paid on exchange."],[4,"The buyer's mortgage advance before completion is held for whom?",["The lender","The seller","The firm"],"Where the firm acts for the lender, the advance is the <b>lender's money</b> until completion."],[5,"Completion is delayed. What may the lender require?",["Return of the advance","Nothing, firm keeps it","Payment to the seller"],"The firm must follow the lender's instructions, which may include <b>returning the advance</b>."],[6,"Before completion, what should the buyer's solicitor hold?",["Cleared funds for the whole amount","The deposit only","Funds from the lender only"],"Paying against <b>uncleared funds</b> risks an overdrawn ledger (r.5.3)."],[7,"How is completion money normally sent?",["CHAPS","Second-class cheque","Cash"],"<b>CHAPS</b> gives same-day payment."],[8,"SDLT return and payment are due within what time of completion?",["14 days","30 days","6 months"],"SDLT return and payment: <b>14 days</b> after completion."],[9,"Is SDLT paid from client account an agency or principal disbursement?",["Agency, no VAT","Principal, with VAT","Principal, no VAT"],"SDLT is not a VAT supply, so <b>agency method</b> with no VAT."],[10,"Sale proceeds remain after all payments. What must the firm do?",["Pay the balance to the client promptly","Hold it until she decides","Move it to business account"],"Return money promptly (r.2.5). Holding it risks a <b>banking facility</b>."],[11,"A bridging loan is repaid from sale proceeds. Why is it done before paying the client?",["The firm gave an undertaking","The lender is the firm","It is firm money"],"The bank usually requires an <b>undertaking</b>, and breaking it is serious."]],"c":[[174,"What money received in a conveyancing transaction is client money?","<b>All of it</b>: deposit, <b>mortgage advance</b>, balance of price, <b>SDLT</b> money, <b>bridging loans</b>, sale proceeds, and money to <b>redeem</b> the seller's mortgage."],[175,"For whom does the seller's solicitor hold a deposit as stakeholder?","For <b>both buyer and seller</b> until completion. It is <b>third-party money</b> (r.2.1(b))."],[176,"For whom does the seller's solicitor hold a deposit as agent?","For the <b>seller</b>, who can use it, e.g. for a related purchase."],[177,"For whom is a mortgage advance held where the firm acts for the lender?","For the <b>lender</b> until completion. It may be used only as the <b>lender's instructions</b> allow."],[179,"How is a stakeholder deposit recorded and released on completion?","Credit a separate <b>stakeholder ledger</b>. On completion, an <b>inter-client transfer</b>: DR stakeholder ledger, CR seller's ledger (client columns), <b>no cash entry</b>."],[181,"Who gets the interest on a stakeholder deposit?","Normally the <b>seller</b> on completion. If the deal fails and the deposit returns to the buyer, the interest <b>follows the deposit</b>."],[182,"Can a stakeholder deposit be released to the seller before completion on the seller's instructions?","<b>No.</b> It is held for both parties, so one party alone cannot authorise release."],[183,"What must happen to a mortgage advance if completion is delayed?","Follow the <b>lender's instructions</b>, which may require it to be <b>returned</b>."],[186,"What must the buyer's solicitor check before sending completion money?","That it holds <b>cleared funds</b> for the <b>whole</b> amount. Paying against uncleared funds risks an <b>overdrawn</b> ledger (r.5.3)."],[187,"What is the time limit for SDLT after completion?","File the return and <b>pay</b> within <b>14 days</b> of completion."]]},{"id":"probate-accounts","title":"Accounts in probate and estates (wills context)","q":[[0,"In an estate, who is the firm's client?",["The personal representatives","The beneficiaries","The deceased's family"],"The <b>PRs</b> instruct the firm, not the beneficiaries."],[1,"A partner is executor. Money held in that capacity is...",["Client money under r.2.1(c)","Firm money","Not regulated"],"Money held as <b>executor or trustee</b> is client money (r.2.1(c))."],[2,"Which rule applies if the solicitor signs on an estate bank account in the PRs' names?",["Rule 10","Rule 9","Rule 11"],"An account in the PRs' names that the solicitor operates falls under <b>rule 10</b>."],[3,"When is IHT on most property due?",["6 months after the end of the month of death","6 months after the grant","12 months after death"],"IHT is due <b>six months after the end of the month of death</b> (IHTA 1984 s.226)."],[4,"IHT is paid by the direct payment scheme. What entries does the firm make?",["None","DR estate ledger; CR client cash","DR client cash; CR estate ledger"],"The money never reaches the firm, so <b>no entries</b>."],[5,"Can another estate's money pay this estate's IHT briefly?",["No, it breaches r.5.3","Yes, for a day","Yes, if repaid at once"],"Using another client's money is always a <b>breach</b> and must be replaced immediately."],[6,"Is the probate application fee VAT-able?",["No, agency disbursement","Yes, at 20%","Only if over £5,000"],"A court fee is <b>not a VAT supply</b>, so it is an agency disbursement."],[7,"Statutory advertisements for creditors rely on which Act?",["Trustee Act 1925 s.27","Administration of Estates Act 1925","Inheritance Act 1975"],"<b>Section 27 Trustee Act 1925</b> notices protect PRs from unknown debts."],[8,"The firm wants costs from the estate funds. What first?",["Deliver a bill or written notification","Wait until residue is paid","Get beneficiaries' consent"],"Rule 4.3 applies: a <b>bill first</b>, then transfer the specific sum."],[9,"Interim distribution to beneficiaries. What is often kept back?",["A retention for outstanding liabilities","Nothing","The firm's profit"],"A <b>retention</b> covers final tax or costs still to be met."],[10,"Estate accounts are approved by the PRs. What follows?",["Prompt distribution of the balance","Hold the balance for a year","Transfer to business account"],"Once administration is complete, distribute <b>promptly</b> (r.2.5)."],[11,"A £75 utility refund arrives after the estate is wound up. First step?",["Pay it to the residuary beneficiary","Send it to charity","Keep it as costs"],"It belongs to the estate. Charity is only for balances that cannot be returned."]],"c":[[192,"Who is the client when a firm administers an estate?","The <b>personal representatives</b> (executors or administrators). The <b>beneficiaries</b> are <b>not</b> clients."],[193,"How is estate money classified and recorded?","It is <b>client money</b> (r.2.1(a)), credited to the <b>estate ledger</b>, e.g. \"Executors of Jane Smith deceased\"."],[194,"Is money held by a partner as executor client money?","<b>Yes</b> (r.2.1(c)), and goes into client account unless the office's rules <b>conflict</b> (r.2.3(a)) or it stays in an <b>estate account</b> in the PRs' names, when <b>r.10</b> applies."],[196,"When is IHT due and why is funding it a problem?","<b>6 months after the end of the month of death</b>, generally <b>before the grant</b>. But the PRs usually cannot collect assets until they have the grant."],[198,"What are the entries for paying IHT from client account?","<b>DR</b> estate ledger <b>client</b> column. <b>CR client cash</b>. There must be <b>sufficient funds on that estate's ledger</b>."],[200,"Can another estate's money be used to pay IHT for a day?","<b>Never.</b> It breaches r.5.3 and must be <b>replaced immediately</b>."],[203,"What must happen before costs are taken from estate money?","Deliver a <b>bill or written notification</b> to the PRs (r.4.3), then transfer the <b>specific sum</b> within the estate's balance."],[206,"When must the residue be distributed?","<b>Promptly</b> once the <b>estate accounts</b> are approved (r.2.5). Holding it because a beneficiary prefers to leave it is a <b>banking facility</b>."],[207,"Must interest be paid on estate money?","<b>Yes</b>, a <b>fair sum</b> to the <b>estate</b> (r.7.1), unless the PRs agreed otherwise in writing with <b>informed consent</b>."]]}]},{"id":"trusts","title":"Trusts Law","stop":10,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M-72,-48 Q-72,-102 0,-106 Q72,-102 72,-48 Z\" fill=\"#E3F6F5\"/><rect x=\"-72\" y=\"-48\" width=\"144\" height=\"48\" fill=\"#E3F6F5\"/><path d=\"M-36,0 V-96 M0,0 V-106 M36,0 V-96 M-72,-48 H72 M-72,-24 H72\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M-18,0 C-22,-40 -14,-80 -6,-112\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"10\"/><path d=\"M-18,0 C-22,-40 -14,-80 -6,-112\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#B5835A\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M-6,-112 Q-25,-126 -44,-104 Q-25,-114 -6,-112 Z\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><path d=\"M-6,-112 Q12,-126 30,-108 Q12,-114 -6,-112 Z\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><path d=\"M-6,-112 Q-18,-140 -30,-126 Q-18,-128 -6,-112 Z\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><path d=\"M-6,-112 Q6,-144 18,-130 Q6,-132 -6,-112 Z\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><path d=\"M40,0 V-30 M40,-18 h-8 v-8 M40,-22 h8 v-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2D6A4F\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"-58\" cy=\"-8\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#FF6FB5\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-44\" cy=\"-8\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"56\" cy=\"-8\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"certainties","title":"Express trusts: the three certainties; fixed and discretionary trusts","q":[[0,"Which three certainties must a valid express trust have?",["Intention, subject matter, objects","Intention, formalities, constitution","Trustees, property, purpose"],"The three certainties are <b>intention, subject matter and objects</b> (Knight v Knight)."],[1,"Which test of certainty of objects applies to a fixed trust?",["The complete list test","The is or is not test","The one-person test"],"A <b>fixed trust</b> needs a complete list of beneficiaries (IRC v Broadway Cottages)."],[2,"Which test applies to a discretionary trust's objects?",["The is or is not test","The complete list test","The reasonable trustee test"],"<b>McPhail v Doulton</b>: it must be possible to say of any given person that they are or are not in the class."],[3,"A gift says 'I hope you will look after my sister with this money'. Trust?",["Probably not, only precatory words","Yes, a fixed trust","Yes, a discretionary trust"],"<b>Precatory words</b> (hope, wish) usually show no intention to create a trust."],[4,"Must the settlor use the word 'trust' to show intention?",["No","Yes","Yes, if land is involved"],"No particular words are needed, as long as an <b>intention</b> to create a trust is clear (Paul v Constance)."],[5,"A settlor leaves 'the bulk of my estate' on trust. Is the subject matter certain?",["No, it is too vague","Yes, it means over 50%","Yes, the trustees decide"],"<b>Palmer v Simmonds</b>: 'the bulk' is uncertain, so the trust fails for <b>subject matter</b>."],[6,"A trust of 'some' of a wine merchant's bottles, none set aside. Valid?",["No, tangible goods must be segregated","Yes, they are identical bottles","Yes, if the buyer pays"],"<b>Re London Wine</b>: for tangible goods the trust property must be identified or segregated."],[7,"Maria was to choose one house, the rest to Charlotte. Maria died first. Charlotte's gift?",["Fails: her houses are uncertain","She takes all the houses","Trustees pick her houses"],"<b>Boyce v Boyce</b>: no one could tell which houses were Charlotte's, so her gift failed for uncertain <b>subject matter</b>."],[8,"A discretionary trust for all 2.5 million inhabitants of a county is void as...",["Administratively unworkable","Conceptually uncertain","Lacking intention"],"<b>R v District Auditor ex p West Yorkshire</b>: the class was so large the trust was unworkable."],[9,"A company pays customers' prepayments into a separate trust account. Trust?",["Yes, intention is shown","No, money cannot be held on trust","No, it needs written formalities"],"<b>Re Kayford</b>: paying into a separate account showed intention to create a trust of the money."],[10,"A discretionary trust's class is clear, but some people can't prove they're in. Void?",["No, evidential uncertainty is not fatal","Yes, for uncertain objects","Yes, for uncertain intention"],"<b>Evidential uncertainty</b> does not defeat a discretionary trust; only <b>conceptual</b> uncertainty does (Re Baden No 2)."],[11,"Under a fixed trust, how are the beneficiaries' shares decided?",["The trust sets them, not the trustees","The trustees choose","The court chooses"],"In a <b>fixed trust</b> the beneficiaries' entitlements are fixed; trustees have no discretion."]],"c":[[0,"What are the three certainties for an express trust?","<b>Intention</b> to create a trust. <b>Subject matter</b> (the property and the shares). <b>Objects</b> (the beneficiaries)."],[4,"What happens if certainty of objects fails?","The trust fails. The trustee holds on <b>resulting trust</b> for the settlor or their estate."],[7,"What is the effect of precatory words like \"in full confidence\" or \"I hope\"?","They <b>usually create no trust</b>. The recipient takes an <b>outright gift</b>."],[12,"Is a trust of \"the bulk of my estate\" valid?","<b>No.</b> \"Bulk\", \"most of\" or \"the remaining part\" cannot be identified objectively. The property is <b>uncertain</b>."],[14,"Must tangible goods taken from a bulk be segregated?","<b>Yes.</b> Physical goods such as wine or gold must be <b>separated out</b> before a trust can exist. Otherwise no one can say which items are trust property."],[15,"Must shares of the same class in one company be segregated?","<b>No.</b> Intangible, interchangeable property <b>need not be segregated</b>. A trust of 50 of 950 identical shares is valid (Hunter v Moss)."],[18,"What test of objects applies to a fixed trust?","The <b>complete list test</b>. It must be possible to list <b>every beneficiary</b>."],[21,"What test of objects applies to a discretionary trust?","The <b>is or is not</b> test (given postulant test). Can it be said of <b>any given person</b> that they are or are not in the class?"],[22,"Which kind of uncertainty defeats a discretionary trust: conceptual or evidential?","<b>Conceptual</b> uncertainty (an unclear definition) defeats it. <b>Evidential</b> uncertainty (difficulty proving membership) does not."],[26,"What is administrative unworkability?","A class so <b>wide</b> that the trustees cannot sensibly exercise their duty. Example: all inhabitants of a county (about 2.5 million people). The <b>discretionary trust</b> is <b>void</b>."]]},{"id":"formalities","title":"Formalities for creating and dealing with trusts","q":[[0,"How must a declaration of trust of land be evidenced?",["Signed writing","A deed witnessed twice","Orally is enough"],"<b>LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b)</b>: signed writing, otherwise the trust is unenforceable."],[1,"Does an express trust of shares need writing?",["No","Yes, signed writing","Yes, a deed"],"A trust of <b>personalty</b> can be declared orally (Paul v Constance). Writing is needed for lifetime trusts of land."],[2,"Who must sign the writing for a trust of land?",["The person declaring the trust","The beneficiary","An independent witness"],"<b>s.53(1)(b)</b>: signed by the person who is able to declare the trust."],[3,"What does s.53(1)(c) LPA 1925 cover?",["Disposing of an existing equitable interest","Declaring a trust of land","Transferring legal land"],"<b>s.53(1)(c)</b>: a disposition of a subsisting equitable interest must be in signed writing."],[4,"A beneficiary orally tells the trustees to hold his interest for his friend. Effective?",["No, s.53(1)(c) needs writing","Yes, oral is enough","Yes, if the friend pays"],"<b>Grey v IRC</b>: that oral direction is a disposition of an equitable interest needing writing."],[5,"Which trusts are exempt from the writing rules?",["Resulting, implied and constructive","Express trusts of land","Trusts for charity"],"<b>s.53(2)</b>: resulting, implied and constructive trusts need no writing."],[6,"How many witnesses does a will creating a trust need?",["Two","One","None"],"<b>Wills Act 1837 s.9</b>: signed by the testator in presence of two witnesses."],[7,"The defendant relies on lack of writing to cheat the claimant. What can equity do?",["Allow oral evidence to prove the trust","Enforce nothing","Order the land sold"],"<b>Rochefoucauld v Boustead</b>: a statute cannot be used as an instrument of fraud."],[8,"A fully secret trust: when must the trustee be told of it?",["Before the testator's death","At the will's execution only","After the death"],"For a <b>fully secret trust</b> communication and acceptance must occur in the testator's lifetime."],[9,"A half-secret trust: the will mentions a trust. When must it be communicated?",["Before or at execution of the will","Any time before death","After death"],"<b>Blackwell v Blackwell</b>; for a <b>half-secret</b> trust, communication is before or at the will."],[10,"Does a disclaimer of an equitable interest need writing under s.53(1)(c)?",["No, it is not a disposition","Yes, signed writing","Yes, a deed"],"A <b>disclaimer</b> is not a disposition of an interest, so no writing is required (Re Paradise Motor)."],[11,"Without signed writing, a declaration of trust of land is...",["Unenforceable, not void","Void","Valid in full"],"It is <b>unenforceable</b>, so it can be enforced once evidenced in writing later."]],"c":[[40,"What does LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b) require?","A declaration of trust of <b>land</b> must be <b>manifested and proved</b> by <b>writing signed</b> by the person able to declare the trust, or by will."],[41,"What kind of writing satisfies s.53(1)(b)?","<b>Any writing</b>: a letter, a note, or an email with a typed name. <b>No deed</b> and <b>no witness</b> are needed."],[44,"What is the effect of no writing under s.53(1)(b)?","The trust of land is <b>unenforceable</b>. It is <b>not void</b>."],[47,"Can someone who took land on an oral promise to hold it on trust rely on the lack of writing?","<b>No.</b> Equity will not allow a statute to be used as an <b>instrument of fraud</b>. They hold on trust (Rochefoucauld v Boustead)."],[48,"Which trusts are exempt from s.53(1) writing rules?","<b>Resulting, implied and constructive trusts</b>: s.53(2). They arise from the facts."],[49,"What does LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c) require?","A <b>disposition</b> of an equitable interest <b>subsisting</b> at the time must be <b>in writing signed</b> by the person disposing, or their <b>authorised agent</b>."],[51,"What is the effect of no writing under s.53(1)(c)?","The disposition is <b>void</b>."],[53,"Is a beneficiary's oral direction to trustees to hold for someone else valid?","<b>No.</b> It is a <b>disposition</b> of a subsisting equitable interest and is <b>void</b> without signed writing (Grey v IRC)."],[54,"Is writing needed when a bare beneficiary tells the trustee to transfer legal title to a third party?","<b>No.</b> Where <b>legal and equitable title move together</b>, no separate writing is needed for the equitable interest (Vandervell v IRC)."],[63,"When must a fully secret trust and a half-secret trust be communicated?","<b>Fully secret</b>: any time <b>before death</b>. <b>Half-secret</b>: <b>before or at the time of</b> the will."]]},{"id":"constitution","title":"Constitution of trusts and exceptions","q":[[0,"When is an express trust constituted?",["When the property is vested in the trustees","When the document is signed","When a beneficiary is named"],"<b>Milroy v Lord</b>: the trust is constituted once the property is properly vested in the trustee."],[1,"What are the two ways to constitute a trust?",["Transfer to trustees, or self-declaration","Writing or deed","Oral or written"],"<b>Milroy v Lord</b>: settlor transfers the property, or declares himself trustee."],[2,"The settlor promised to settle shares but never transferred them. Can a volunteer enforce?",["No, equity will not assist a volunteer","Yes, equity treats as done","Yes, within 6 years"],"<b>Equity will not assist a volunteer</b> or perfect an imperfect gift."],[3,"Which case lets a gift stand if the donor did everything in their power?",["Re Rose","Strong v Bird","Re Diplock"],"<b>Re Rose</b>: where the donor has done everything needed, the gift is effective in equity."],[4,"Which of these shows the donor did all in his power?",["Signed share transfer and certificate given","A mere promise to give","A will not yet signed"],"<b>Re Rose</b>: handing over the signed transfer and share certificate leaves nothing more for the donor to do."],[5,"The intended donee is later appointed executor of the donor's estate. Which rule helps?",["Strong v Bird","Re Rose","Saunders v Vautier"],"<b>Strong v Bird</b>: the continuing intention to give plus the donee becoming executor perfects the gift."],[6,"Which three conditions apply to a donatio mortis causa?",["Contemplation of death, conditional on death, delivery of dominion","Written, witnessed, delivered","Notice, writing, signature"],"<b>Re Craven's Estate</b>: made in contemplation of death, conditional on dying, with transfer of dominion."],[7,"Can land be the subject of a donatio mortis causa?",["Yes","No","Only registered land"],"<b>Sen v Headley</b>: land can be given by donatio mortis causa."],[8,"A settlor holds property and declares 'I hold X on trust for B'. Is a transfer needed?",["No, the declaration constitutes it","Yes, to the trustee","Yes, to B"],"A settlor-trustee constitutes the trust by <b>declaration</b> and the property is already with him."],[9,"The court is asked to treat a failed transfer as a declaration of trust. Will it?",["No, it will not","Yes, always","Yes, if the donee is a relative"],"<b>Richards v Delbridge</b>: equity will not construe a failed gift as a declaration of trust."],[10,"A settlor declares a trust of money he hopes to inherit. Valid?",["No, a mere expectancy cannot be held on trust","Yes","Yes, if in writing"],"A <b>mere expectancy</b> is not property, so cannot be the subject of a present trust."],[11,"A donor declares a gift to a trust he is a trustee of, then dies before transferring. Which case helps?",["Choithram v Pagarani","Re Ellenborough","Milroy v Lord"],"<b>Choithram</b>: the gift is complete once the property is with the donor as <b>one of the trustees</b>; his estate must transfer it."]],"c":[[64,"When is a trust constituted?","When the trust property is <b>vested in the trustee</b>, so the trustee holds the <b>legal title</b>."],[69,"If a gift or transfer to trustees fails, will the court treat it as a self-declaration of trust?","<b>No.</b> The court will not switch methods (Milroy v Lord). The donor intended a gift, not to be a trustee."],[70,"How does legal title to registered land pass?","By <b>deed</b> (usually form TR1) <b>and registration</b> at HM Land Registry. Title passes only <b>on registration</b>."],[71,"How does legal title to certificated shares pass?","A signed <b>stock transfer form</b> with the share certificate, then <b>registration</b> in the company's register of members."],[77,"What is the Re Rose rule?","A gift is effective <b>in equity</b> once the donor has done <b>everything in their power</b>. Only a <b>third party</b> (company or Land Registry) still has to act."],[78,"Who holds legal title between a Re Rose gift and registration?","The <b>donor</b>, <b>on trust</b> for the donee."],[82,"When is an imperfect gift perfected because the donee becomes the donor's executor?","Under the rule in Strong v Bird: <b>1.</b> Intention to make an <b>immediate</b> lifetime gift. <b>2.</b> Intention <b>continues unchanged until death</b>. <b>3.</b> The donee becomes the donor's <b>executor</b>."],[85,"What happens where a donor gives property to a trust of which they are one of the trustees?","Where a donor gives to a trust of which <b>they are one of the trustees</b>, the gift is complete once the property is vested in the donor as <b>one trustee</b>. They cannot in conscience deny the trust (Choithram v Pagarani)."],[87,"What are the requirements of a donatio mortis causa?","<b>1. Contemplation of impending death</b> from an identified cause. <b>2.</b> Gift <b>conditional on death</b>. <b>3. Delivery of dominion</b> over the property."]]},{"id":"beneficial-entitlement","title":"Beneficial entitlement and Saunders v Vautier","q":[[0,"What does Saunders v Vautier allow?",["Beneficiaries to end the trust","Trustees to end the trust","The court to end the trust only"],"If all beneficiaries are adult and together absolutely entitled, they can <b>end the trust</b> and call for the property."],[1,"Who must agree under Saunders v Vautier?",["All the beneficiaries","A majority","The trustees only"],"<b>All</b> beneficiaries must agree, and be sui juris."],[2,"What does sui juris mean here?",["Adult and of full capacity","Born in the UK","Named in the trust"],"<b>Sui juris</b> means 18 or over and mentally capable."],[3,"One beneficiary is a minor. Can the others use Saunders v Vautier?",["No, all must be sui juris","Yes, if they hold a majority","Yes, with the trustees' consent"],"A <b>minor</b> cannot consent, so the rule cannot be used."],[4,"Which Act lets the court approve a variation on behalf of minors?",["Variation of Trusts Act 1958","Trustee Act 2000","Trusts of Land Act 1996"],"The <b>Variation of Trusts Act 1958</b> lets the court consent for minors and unborn beneficiaries."],[5,"What must the court see before approving a variation for a minor?",["A benefit for that person","A guarantee from the trustees","A written will"],"The variation must <b>benefit</b> the person for whom the court consents."],[6,"Can the 1958 Act approve a variation for an adult beneficiary who refuses?",["No","Yes, if it benefits","Yes, after 6 months"],"The 1958 Act cannot override a <b>competent adult</b> who refuses to consent."],[7,"What right does a discretionary beneficiary have?",["To be considered, not to any set share","To a fixed share","To demand payment"],"<b>Gartside v IRC</b>: no entitlement to any asset, only to be considered and to have the trust properly run."],[8,"Can beneficiaries use Saunders v Vautier to direct the trustees to transfer to a third party?",["Yes","No","Only with court approval"],"Beneficiaries who are together absolutely entitled may direct trustees to <b>transfer</b> the property as they wish."],[9,"'To C if she reaches 25.' C's interest is...",["Contingent","Vested in possession","Vested in interest"],"A condition (\"<b>if</b> she reaches 25\") that may never be met makes the interest <b>contingent</b>."],[10,"'To A for life, remainder to B.' While A lives, B's interest is...",["Vested in interest","Contingent","Discretionary"],"No condition attaches to B, who just waits for A to die, so B's interest is <b>vested in interest</b>."]],"c":[[100,"What does vested in possession mean?","The beneficiary is entitled to enjoy the property <b>now</b>. Example: a <b>life tenant</b> receiving income."],[101,"What does vested in interest mean?","A fixed right to enjoy the property <b>later</b>, once an earlier interest ends. Example: the <b>remainderman</b> after a life interest."],[102,"What is a contingent interest?","One that depends on a <b>condition</b> that may never be met, or on a beneficiary not yet identified. Example: \"to C <b>if</b> she reaches 25\"."],[103,"What happens if a contingency fails?","The beneficiary's interest <b>ends</b>. The property goes to the <b>gift over</b>, or if none, to the settlor or their estate on <b>resulting trust</b>."],[105,"If a remainderman dies before the life tenant, what happens to the remainder?","It <b>passes to the remainderman's estate</b>, because the interest was <b>vested in interest</b>."],[110,"When can beneficiaries end a trust and call for the property?","If <b>all</b> beneficiaries are <b>adults</b> with <b>capacity</b> and <b>together absolutely entitled</b> to the whole interest, they can <b>end the trust</b> and call for the property (Saunders v Vautier)."],[113,"Can a sole adult beneficiary with a vested interest call for the fund before the age the trust names?","<b>Yes.</b> Where the gift is <b>vested</b> and only <b>payment is postponed</b>, they need not wait."],[117,"Can a beneficiary with a contingent interest end the trust alone?","<b>No.</b> Those who take if the contingency fails must <b>join in</b> too."],[120,"What can be done if some beneficiaries are minors or unborn?","The adults can ask the <b>court</b> to approve a <b>variation</b> under the <b>Variation of Trusts Act 1958 s.1</b>."]]},{"id":"purpose-charitable","title":"Non-charitable purpose trusts and charitable trusts","q":[[0,"What is the beneficiary principle?",["A trust needs a person able to enforce it","Trustees must be beneficiaries","A trust needs a written purpose"],"<b>Morice v Bishop of Durham</b>: a trust needs someone who can enforce it; a pure purpose has none."],[1,"Are non-charitable purpose trusts usually valid?",["No, with limited exceptions","Yes","Yes, if in writing"],"They generally fail on the <b>beneficiary principle</b>, apart from anomalous exceptions."],[2,"Which case refused to add new anomalous purpose trusts?",["Re Endacott","Re Denley","McGovern v AG"],"<b>Re Endacott</b>: the anomalous exceptions (specific animals, tombs, private masses) are <b>closed categories</b>."],[3,"A trust to look after a named pet dog is valid as an...",["Anomalous exception","Ordinary trust","Charitable trust"],"Specific animals are an <b>anomalous exception</b>, as in Re Dean."],[4,"A trust for a sports ground for the company's employees. Which case saves it?",["Re Denley","Re Endacott","Re Rymer"],"<b>Re Denley</b>: a purpose which directly benefits identifiable people may be valid."],[5,"A charitable trust needs a s.3 purpose, exclusivity and what else?",["Public benefit","A written deed","Named beneficiaries"],"<b>Charities Act 2011 s.4</b>: the purpose must also be for the <b>public benefit</b>."],[6,"Is public benefit presumed for charities after the 2011 Act?",["No","Yes, for all","Yes, for religion"],"<b>Charities Act 2011 s.4</b>: no presumption; public benefit must be shown."],[7,"Is a trust for 'charitable or benevolent' purposes valid?",["No, 'benevolent' is wider than charitable","Yes","Yes, if public"],"<b>Chichester Diocesan Fund v Simpson</b>: 'or' makes it wider than charity, so it fails."],[8,"Is a trust to change the law of the country charitable?",["No, political","Yes, public benefit","Yes, education"],"<b>McGovern v AG</b>: trusts for political purposes are not charitable."],[9,"What is cy-près?",["Applying funds to a similar charitable purpose","Splitting the trust fund","Reclaiming the gift"],"<b>Charities Act 2011 s.62</b>: when the original purpose fails, funds go to a similar charitable purpose."],[10,"A gift to a named charity that ceased to exist before death, with no general charitable intent. Result?",["The gift fails","Cy-près applies","It goes to the Crown"],"<b>Re Rymer</b>: without general charitable intent there is no cy-près, so the gift fails."],[11,"What is the contract-holding theory?",["Members hold a gift to a club under their contract","The club holds it as trustee","The gift goes to the state"],"<b>Re Recher</b>: a gift to an unincorporated association is held by members under their contract."],[12,"What perpetuity period limits a non-charitable purpose trust?",["A life in being plus 21 years","125 years","No limit"],"The common law <b>perpetuity period</b> applies; the 125-year period in the 2009 Act does not cover purpose trusts."]],"c":[[125,"Are non-charitable purpose trusts valid?","<b>Generally void</b>, because no human beneficiary can enforce them. There are a few narrow exceptions."],[130,"When is a purpose trust treated as a trust for people (Re Denley)?","When it <b>directly and tangibly benefits ascertainable individuals</b> who can enforce it. Example: a sports ground for a company's employees."],[132,"What are the anomalous exceptions to the beneficiary principle?","<b>Maintenance of specific animals.</b> <b>Graves, tombs and monuments.</b> <b>Saying private masses.</b>"],[142,"What are the three requirements for a charitable trust?","<b>1.</b> A purpose in <b>Charities Act 2011 s.3(1)</b>. <b>2.</b> <b>Public benefit</b> (s.4). <b>3.</b> <b>Exclusively</b> charitable."],[145,"What are the two aspects of public benefit?","<b>Benefit aspect</b>: the purpose must be <b>beneficial</b>, with any harm weighed against it. <b>Public aspect</b>: it must benefit the public or a <b>sufficient section</b> of it."],[146,"What is the personal nexus test?","A class defined by a <b>personal link</b> to a named person or company (relatives, employees) is <b>not</b> a section of the public, however large."],[148,"Which charitable purpose is excepted from the personal nexus test?","<b>Relief of poverty.</b> A trust for <b>poor employees</b> or <b>poor relatives</b> can be charitable."],[151,"Are political purposes charitable?","<b>No.</b> Trusts to <b>change the law</b> or government policy, here or abroad, or to support a party, are not charitable."],[153,"What does \"exclusively charitable\" require?","<b>All</b> the purposes must be charitable. If money could go to a non-charitable purpose, the <b>whole trust fails</b>."],[157,"When does cy-près apply on initial failure?","Only if the donor had a <b>general charitable intention</b>. Otherwise the gift <b>lapses</b> to the estate."],[158,"When does cy-près apply on subsequent failure?","<b>Automatically</b>, without general charitable intention. The property was already dedicated to charity."]]},{"id":"resulting-trusts","title":"Resulting trusts: automatic and presumed","q":[[0,"When does an automatic resulting trust arise?",["When the beneficial interest is not fully disposed of","When a gift is made to a child","When a will is invalid"],"<b>Vandervell v IRC</b>: if the settlor does not dispose of all the beneficial interest, it results back to him."],[1,"Which transfers give rise to a presumed resulting trust?",["Voluntary transfers or purchase contributions","Gifts to children","Gifts by husband to wife"],"<b>Dyer v Dyer</b>: a voluntary transfer or contribution to the purchase price presumes a resulting trust."],[2,"A friend pays £40,000 of the price of a house put in another's name. Nothing said. Result?",["Resulting trust in proportion","Constructive trust of half","Gift to the owner"],"The payer takes a <b>resulting trust</b> share in proportion to the contribution."],[3,"Which relationship raises the presumption of advancement?",["Husband to wife","Wife to husband","Friend to friend"],"The <b>presumption of advancement</b> covers husband to wife and father to child; it is weak and easily rebutted."],[4,"A father pays for a house in his daughter's name. Presumption?",["Advancement, a gift","Resulting trust","Constructive trust"],"The <b>presumption of advancement</b> says a father's transfer to a child is a gift."],[5,"How can the presumptions be rebutted?",["By evidence of contrary intention","They cannot","By the trustee's consent"],"Presumptions are <b>rebuttable</b> by evidence of the transferor's real intention."],[6,"Which case says a resulting trust is not based on unjust enrichment?",["Westdeutsche v Islington","Tinsley v Milligan","Re Vinogradoff"],"<b>Westdeutsche</b>: resulting trusts depend on the presumed intention of the transferor."],[7,"What is a Quistclose trust?",["Money lent for a specific purpose","A trust of land","A trust of shares"],"<b>Barclays v Quistclose</b>: money advanced for a particular purpose is held on trust for the lender if the purpose fails."],[8,"Does the claimant's illegality bar a resulting trust claim?",["Not automatically (Patel v Mirza)","Yes, always","Yes, if over 6 years"],"<b>Patel v Mirza</b>: the court weighs the policy factors rather than applying a strict rule."],[9,"Does a resulting trust of land need writing?",["No","Yes, signed writing","Yes, a deed"],"<b>s.53(2) LPA 1925</b>: resulting trusts are exempt from formalities."],[10,"An express trust fails to dispose of the whole interest. Who gets the remainder?",["The settlor, by resulting trust","The trustees","The Crown"],"The settlor takes the undisposed interest on <b>automatic resulting trust</b>."],[11,"A wife pays for property in her husband's name. Which presumption applies?",["Resulting trust","Advancement","None"],"There is no presumption of advancement from wife to husband, so a <b>resulting trust</b> is presumed."]],"c":[[162,"What are the two kinds of resulting trust?","<b>Automatic</b>: an express trust fails or leaves a surplus. <b>Presumed</b>: a voluntary transfer, or paying for property in another's name."],[164,"When does an automatic resulting trust arise on failure of an express trust?","When the trust fails, for example for uncertain objects, a void purpose trust, or a failed <b>contingent</b> interest with no gift over. The trustee holds for the <b>settlor</b>."],[167,"When is there no resulting trust of surplus funds?","Where the donors made an <b>out-and-out gift</b>, such as anonymous collections or raffles. The money may go to the Crown as <b>bona vacantia</b>."],[168,"What is a Quistclose trust?","Money <b>lent for a specific purpose</b>, not at the borrower's free disposal. The borrower holds it <b>on trust</b>. If the purpose <b>fails</b>, it is held on <b>resulting trust for the lender</b>."],[172,"What is a purchase money resulting trust?","A pays all or part of the price for property put in <b>B's name</b>. B holds on trust for A <b>in proportion to A's contribution</b>."],[176,"How is a presumed resulting trust rebutted?","By evidence of an intended <b>gift</b> or <b>loan</b>. Or by the <b>presumption of advancement</b>."],[179,"Which relationships attract the presumption of advancement?","<b>Husband to wife</b> (and fiancée). <b>Father to child.</b> Someone <b>in loco parentis</b> to a child."],[182,"Has the presumption of advancement been abolished?","<b>No.</b> Equality Act 2010 <b>s.199</b> would abolish it but has <b>never been brought into force</b>."],[183,"Can someone who transferred property for an illegal purpose rely on a resulting trust?","<b>Usually yes.</b> The test is now <b>policy-based</b>: would denying the claim be <b>contrary to the public interest</b>? (Patel v Mirza)"]]},{"id":"family-home","title":"The family home: constructive trusts and proprietary estoppel","q":[[0,"Which case is the starting point for a home in joint names?",["Stack v Dowden","Lloyds Bank v Rosset","Eves v Eves"],"<b>Stack v Dowden</b>: joint names presume joint beneficial ownership."],[1,"In a home in joint names, who must prove unequal shares?",["The person claiming unequal shares","The joint owner","The court"],"The claimant of a <b>different split</b> must show a contrary common intention."],[2,"Which case covers a home in one name and a common intention constructive trust?",["Lloyds Bank v Rosset","Gillett v Holt","Re Rose"],"<b>Lloyds Bank v Rosset</b>: express agreement, or direct contributions, can show common intention."],[3,"What two things does the claimant show in an express common intention trust?",["Agreement and detrimental reliance","Writing and deed","Marriage and children"],"<b>Eves v Eves / Grant v Edwards</b>: an agreement or understanding, and acting to their detriment."],[4,"Does paying the household bills usually create a share?",["Not usually","Yes","Yes, for 10 years"],"After Rosset, paying <b>household expenses</b> alone is generally insufficient."],[5,"Do direct contributions to the deposit or mortgage count?",["Yes, they can show common intention","No","Only the deposit"],"Direct payments to the price or mortgage can show a <b>common intention</b> to share (Lloyds Bank v Rosset)."],[6,"Which case says shares can be imputed to what is fair?",["Jones v Kernott","Stack v Dowden","Eves v Eves"],"<b>Jones v Kernott</b>: the court can impute an intention giving a fair share on the whole course of dealing."],[7,"What three elements make proprietary estoppel?",["Assurance, reliance, detriment","Contract, writing, payment","Intention, notice, delay"],"<b>Thorner v Major</b>: assurance, reliance and detriment, making it <b>unconscionable</b> to go back on the promise."],[8,"Which case says the remedy must be proportionate to the detriment?",["Jennings v Rice","Eves v Eves","Rosset"],"<b>Jennings v Rice</b>: the remedy should be <b>proportionate</b> to the detriment, and may be money."],[9,"Which statute lets a person apply to the court for the sale of the family home?",["TLATA 1996 s.14","Trustee Act 2000","LPA 1925 s.53"],"<b>TLATA 1996 s.14</b>: any person with an interest may apply to the court, which weighs the <b>s.15</b> factors."],[10,"How is a buyer protected from beneficial interests in the home?",["Paying two trustees, overreaching","Buying from one owner","Registering at Companies House"],"<b>City of London BS v Flegg</b>: payment to two trustees overreaches the beneficiaries' interests."]],"c":[[190,"What is the starting point for a home in joint names?","<b>Equity follows the law</b>: beneficial <b>joint tenants</b> in <b>equal shares</b>."],[191,"What is the starting point for a home in one person's sole name?","<b>Equity follows the law</b>: the legal owner owns <b>100%</b> beneficially."],[192,"What is the effect of an express declaration of beneficial shares, such as in the TR1?","It is <b>conclusive</b>. The court will not look at who paid what."],[195,"Who bears the burden in a joint names case with no declaration?","The party claiming <b>unequal shares</b>. It is <b>not lightly</b> done."],[197,"Which factors are relevant to the whole course of dealing?","Discussions at purchase. The <b>purpose</b> of the purchase. The relationship. <b>Children</b>. How the purchase was <b>financed</b>. How <b>outgoings</b> were paid. Whether <b>finances were pooled</b> or separate."],[203,"What are the two stages for a sole name claim?","<b>Stage 1</b>: a <b>common intention</b> that the claimant has a share, plus <b>detrimental reliance</b>. <b>Stage 2</b>: <b>quantify</b> the share."],[204,"What are the two ways to show common intention in a sole name case?","<b>1. Express agreement</b> from discussions, however imprecise. <b>2. Inferred from conduct</b>, traditionally only by <b>direct contributions</b> to the price (Lloyds Bank v Rosset)."],[210,"Can the court impute a common intention at stage 1 in a sole name case?","<b>No.</b> At stage 1 it must be <b>actual</b> (express or inferred). <b>Imputation</b> is only for stage 2 (Capehorn v Harris)."],[213,"How is the share quantified in a sole name case?","By the <b>whole course of dealing</b>. The court can impute a <b>fair</b> share if no actual intention is found (Oxley v Hiscock: 40%)."],[215,"What are the elements of proprietary estoppel?","<b>1. Assurance.</b> <b>2. Reliance.</b> <b>3. Detriment.</b> <b>4. Unconscionability.</b>"],[223,"How does a constructive trust differ from proprietary estoppel?","<b>Constructive trust</b>: a <b>mutual</b> common intention gives a <b>share</b>. <b>Estoppel</b>: a <b>one-sided</b> assurance gives a <b>flexible</b> remedy."]]},{"id":"fiduciary-duties","title":"Fiduciary duties: no profit, no conflict, self-dealing","q":[[0,"What are the core fiduciary duties?",["No profit and no conflict","No loss and no delay","No sale and no gift"],"A fiduciary must not <b>make an unauthorised profit</b> or have a conflict of interest (Bray v Ford)."],[1,"A trustee renews the trust's lease in his own name. Result?",["He holds it on constructive trust","He keeps it","The lease ends"],"<b>Keech v Sandford</b>: the lease is held on constructive trust for the beneficiaries."],[2,"A fiduciary profits using trust information, though the trust lost nothing. Liable?",["Yes, he must account for the profit","No, no loss","No, if honest"],"<b>Boardman v Phipps</b>: liability to account even where honest and the trust gained."],[3,"Is an honest director who profits from a company opportunity excused?",["No","Yes","Yes, if the company is rich"],"<b>Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver</b>: liability does not depend on bad faith or the company's inability to act."],[4,"Can a fiduciary escape by proving he'd have made the profit anyway?",["No, there is no 'but for' defence","Yes, if he proves it","Yes, if he acted honestly"],"<b>Rukhadze v Recovery Partners</b> (2025): no <b>but for</b> causation defence; Regal and Boardman were reaffirmed."],[5,"How can a fiduciary escape liability for a profit?",["Informed consent of the beneficiaries","Acting honestly","The trust suffering no loss"],"With <b>fully informed consent</b>, trust instrument authority, or court approval, the profit may be kept."],[6,"Under the self-dealing rule, a trustee buys trust property. The sale is...",["Voidable by the beneficiaries","Valid if fair","Void absolutely"],"<b>Tito v Waddell (No 2)</b>: voidable at the beneficiary's option however fair the price."],[7,"What test applies when a trustee buys a beneficiary's interest?",["Fair-dealing rule: fair and no advantage taken","Automatically void","Always valid"],"Under the <b>fair-dealing rule</b> the sale stands if fair and the trustee took no advantage."],[8,"Is a lay trustee entitled to be paid for acting?",["No, unless authorised","Yes, always","Yes, at a market rate"],"The default rule is <b>no remuneration</b> unless the instrument, court, consent or statute allows."],[9,"Which Act allows reasonable payment to a professional trustee?",["Trustee Act 2000 s.29","Trustee Act 1925 s.61","LPA 1925 s.53"],"<b>TA 2000 s.29</b>: reasonable pay for a professional trustee (not a sole trustee) if each other trustee agrees in writing."],[10,"What remedy gives the beneficiary the fiduciary's profit?",["Account of profits","Damages for loss","Specific performance"],"An <b>account of profits</b> requires no proof of loss by the beneficiary."],[11,"Which case holds a bribe is held on constructive trust for the principal?",["FHR European Ventures v Cedar","Keech v Sandford","Re Diplock"],"<b>FHR v Cedar</b>: a bribe or secret commission is held on constructive trust."]],"c":[[225,"What are the two core fiduciary rules?","<b>No profit</b> from the position. <b>No conflict</b> between personal interest and duty. Both apply unless there is <b>fully informed consent</b>."],[226,"Is honesty or benefit to the trust a defence to breach of fiduciary duty?","<b>No.</b> The rules are <b>strict</b>. Honesty, good faith and benefit to the trust are no defence."],[231,"What does the no profit rule forbid?","Making an <b>unauthorised profit</b> from the position, or from property, information or opportunities that came <b>because of</b> it."],[234,"What happens to a fiduciary who uses information obtained through the trust to make a personal profit?","They must <b>account</b> for the profit unless all beneficiaries gave <b>fully informed consent</b>. An honest, skilled fiduciary may get an <b>equitable allowance</b> (Boardman v Phipps)."],[235,"Is there a \"but for\" causation defence to the no profit rule?","<b>No.</b> The Supreme Court refused to change the law in 2025 (Rukhadze). Profits are held on <b>constructive trust</b> from when made."],[237,"What can authorise a fiduciary to keep a profit?","<b>1.</b> The <b>trust instrument</b>. <b>2.</b> <b>Fully informed consent</b> of all beneficiaries (adults with capacity). <b>3.</b> The <b>court</b>."],[244,"How is a bribe or secret commission received by a fiduciary held?","On <b>constructive trust</b> for the principal <b>from the moment it is received</b> (FHR v Cedar)."],[247,"How likely must a conflict be?","A <b>real sensible possibility</b> of conflict is enough. The fiduciary need not have acted on it."],[250,"What is the self-dealing rule?","If a trustee <b>buys trust property</b> (or sells their own to the trust), the deal is <b>voidable</b> by <b>any beneficiary</b>, <b>however fair</b> the price."],[254,"What is the fair-dealing rule?","A trustee buying a <b>beneficiary's interest</b> directly from them. The deal <b>stands</b> if the trustee proves <b>no advantage</b> taken, <b>full disclosure</b>, and a <b>fair and honest</b> deal at a fair price."]]},{"id":"trustees-appointment","title":"Trustees: who can act, appointment, retirement and removal","q":[[0,"What is the minimum number of trustees needed to overreach on land?",["Two (or a trust corporation)","One","Three"],"<b>LPA 1925</b>: capital money is paid to two trustees or a trust corporation to overreach."],[1,"What is the maximum number of trustees of land?",["Four","Two","Six"],"<b>Trustee Act 1925 s.34</b>: no more than four trustees of land."],[2,"Can a minor hold the legal estate in land as trustee?",["No","Yes","Yes, if over 16"],"<b>LPA 1925 s.20</b>: a person under 18 cannot hold a legal estate in land."],[3,"Who can appoint new trustees if the instrument names no one?",["The surviving trustees","The court only","The beneficiaries only"],"<b>TA 1925 s.36</b>: surviving or continuing trustees, or personal representatives, may appoint."],[4,"Under s.36, when can new trustees be appointed?",["On death, refusal or unfitness of a trustee","Only on death","Only on bankruptcy"],"<b>s.36</b> covers death, absence abroad over 12 months, refusal, unfitness or incapacity."],[5,"Which Act lets beneficiaries together absolutely entitled direct a trustee to retire?",["TLATA 1996 s.19","Trustee Act 2000","Variation of Trusts Act 1958"],"<b>TLATA s.19</b>: adult, capable beneficiaries together absolutely entitled can direct retirement in writing."],[6,"Can a trustee retire without being replaced?",["Yes, if two trustees or a trust corporation remain","No, never","Only with court order"],"<b>TA 1925 s.39</b>: retirement by deed with co-trustees' consent, if two persons or a trust corporation remain."],[7,"Which authority governs the court's removal of a trustee?",["The welfare of the beneficiaries","The settlor's wishes only","The trustees' agreement"],"<b>Letterstedt v Broers</b>: the court's main guide is the welfare of the beneficiaries."],[8,"Must trustees of a private trust act unanimously?",["Yes","No, majority","Only for land"],"Private trustees must be <b>unanimous</b>; charitable trustees may act by majority."],[9,"The trustee dies. Does the trust fail?",["No, equity won't let a trust fail for want of a trustee","Yes","Yes, if sole"],"A new trustee is appointed: <b>equity never wants a trustee</b>."],[10,"Does a retiring trustee escape liability for breaches earlier?",["No","Yes","Yes, once replaced"],"A retired trustee remains liable for <b>breaches</b> during his time in office."],[11,"Appointed by deed, how does most trust property reach new trustees?",["It vests automatically (TA 1925 s.40)","Only by separate transfers","Only by court order"],"<b>TA 1925 s.40</b>: a deed of appointment vests most property automatically; shares still need registering."]],"c":[[258,"What is the effect of appointing a minor as trustee?","The appointment is <b>void</b> (LPA 1925 <b>s.20</b>)."],[262,"What is the maximum number of trustees of land?","<b>Four</b> (TA 1925 <b>s.34</b>). Only the first four willing and able take the legal title. Personalty has <b>no maximum</b>."],[263,"How many trustees are needed to give a valid receipt for capital money on a sale of land?","<b>Two trustees</b> or a <b>trust corporation</b>."],[265,"How must trustees of a private trust make decisions?","<b>Unanimously</b>, unless the trust instrument says otherwise."],[271,"When can a new trustee be appointed under TA 1925 s.36(1)?","In place of a trustee who is <b>dead</b>, <b>outside the UK over 12 months</b>, <b>wishes to retire</b>, <b>refuses</b>, is <b>unfit</b>, <b>incapable</b>, a <b>minor</b>, or a <b>dissolved</b> corporation. Or one <b>removed</b> under an express power."],[272,"Who appoints new trustees under s.36, in order?","<b>1.</b> The person <b>nominated</b> in the trust instrument. <b>2.</b> The <b>surviving or continuing trustees</b>. <b>3.</b> The <b>PRs of the last surviving trustee</b>."],[275,"When can additional trustees be appointed under s.36(6)?","Where there are <b>not more than three</b> trustees. The appointment may <b>not take the total above four</b> (any trust)."],[277,"When can beneficiaries direct trustees to retire or appoint under TLATA 1996 s.19?","<b>No nominated person</b> in the trust. Beneficiaries are <b>all adults with capacity</b> and <b>together absolutely entitled</b>."],[282,"When can a trustee retire without a replacement under TA 1925 s.39?","By <b>deed</b> declaring the wish to retire, if <b>two persons or a trust corporation</b> remain to act, and the <b>co-trustees</b> (and any <b>person with power to appoint</b> trustees) <b>consent by deed</b>."]]},{"id":"trustee-duties-powers","title":"Trustees' duty of care, investment, maintenance and advancement","q":[[0,"What is the statutory duty of care for trustees?",["Reasonable care and skill","Utmost skill","Slight care"],"<b>TA 2000 s.1</b>: such care and skill as is reasonable, with a higher standard for professionals."],[1,"Does the statutory duty of care apply to a professional trustee more strictly?",["Yes","No","Only for land"],"<b>TA 2000 s.1</b> takes account of any special knowledge or professional status."],[2,"What general power of investment do trustees have?",["Any investment as if absolutely entitled","Only government bonds","Only shares"],"<b>TA 2000 s.3</b>: any kind of investment as if absolutely entitled (land is covered by <b>s.8</b> instead)."],[3,"What are the standard investment criteria?",["Suitability and diversification","Profit and speed","Safety and secrecy"],"<b>TA 2000 s.4</b>: have regard to the suitability of investments and the need for diversification."],[4,"Must trustees take advice before investing?",["Yes, unless reasonably unnecessary","No","Only over £50,000"],"<b>TA 2000 s.5</b>: obtain and consider proper advice unless it is unnecessary."],[5,"Which case says trustees should judge investments across the whole portfolio?",["Nestle v NatWest","Learoyd v Whiteley","Cowan v Scargill"],"<b>Nestle</b>: modern portfolio theory; performance is assessed across the fund."],[6,"Can trustees refuse a profitable investment on moral grounds?",["Generally no","Yes","Yes, if they all agree"],"<b>Cowan v Scargill</b>: trustees must act in the beneficiaries' best financial interests."],[7,"Under TA 1925 s.31, what can trustees do for a minor beneficiary?",["Apply income for maintenance","Pay out capital","Sell the trust"],"<b>s.31</b>: trustees may pay income for maintenance, education or benefit."],[8,"A beneficiary aged 18 has a contingent interest until 25. Income?",["Entitled to income from 18","Nothing until 25","Capital at 18"],"<b>s.31(1)(ii)</b>: from 18 the beneficiary gets the <b>income</b> until the interest vests or fails."],[9,"Under s.32, how much capital can be advanced on trusts created on or after 1 October 2014?",["Up to the whole presumptive share","Up to half","Up to a quarter"],"<b>Inheritance and Trustees' Powers Act 2014</b> raised the limit to the whole share."],[10,"Whose written consent is needed for an advancement under s.32?",["Any prior interest holder","The settlor","The court"],"A person with a <b>prior interest</b> (such as a life tenant) must consent in writing."],[11,"Under TA 2000 s.23, when is a trustee liable for an agent's default?",["Only if he breached the duty of care in choosing or supervising","Always","Never"],"<b>s.23</b>: liability only if the trustee failed the duty of care when appointing or reviewing the agent."],[12,"Must trustees give reasons for a discretionary decision?",["Not generally (Re Londonderry)","Always","Only to the court"],"<b>Re Londonderry's Settlement</b>: trustees need not generally disclose reasons for their discretion."]],"c":[[295,"What is the statutory duty of care (TA 2000 s.1)?","<b>Such care and skill as is reasonable in the circumstances</b>, having regard to the trustee's <b>special knowledge or experience</b>, and what is expected of a <b>professional</b> in that line of work."],[300,"What is the general power of investment (TA 2000 s.3)?","Trustees may make <b>any kind of investment</b> they could make if <b>absolutely entitled</b>, subject to restrictions in the trust instrument. <b>Land</b> is excluded (except loans secured on land): buying land in the UK comes under <b>s.8</b>, which allows it as an investment or <b>for a beneficiary to live in</b>."],[302,"What does TA 2000 s.8 allow?","Acquiring <b>freehold or leasehold land in the UK</b> as an investment, for <b>occupation by a beneficiary</b>, or for any other reason."],[304,"What are the standard investment criteria (TA 2000 s.4(3))?","<b>Suitability</b> of that kind of investment and that particular investment. The need for <b>diversification</b>, so far as appropriate."],[306,"When must trustees take proper advice (TA 2000 s.5)?","Before exercising any investment power, and when <b>reviewing</b>."],[316,"What is maintenance under TA 1925 s.31?","Using <b>income</b> for a <b>minor's</b> maintenance, education or benefit."],[318,"What limit applies to maintenance in a trust created before 1 October 2014?","The payment must be <b>reasonable</b>, considering the minor's age, needs and any <b>other income</b> available."],[320,"What happens at 18 to a beneficiary with a contingent interest, such as \"at 25\"?","Trustees <b>must</b> pay them the <b>income</b> of their share until it vests or fails. They do <b>not</b> get the capital."],[322,"What is advancement under TA 1925 s.32?","Paying or applying <b>capital</b> for a beneficiary <b>before</b> they become entitled to it."],[325,"How much can be advanced in a trust created on or after 1 October 2014?","Up to the <b>whole</b> of the <b>presumptive share</b>."],[326,"How much can be advanced in a trust created before 1 October 2014?","Up to <b>one-half</b> of the presumptive share, unless the trust extends it."]]},{"id":"breach-of-trust","title":"Liability for breach of trust and defences","q":[[0,"What is the main remedy against a trustee for loss caused by breach?",["Equitable compensation","Specific performance","Rescission"],"The trustee must restore the loss; <b>causation</b> is required (Target Holdings v Redferns)."],[1,"Which case requires a causal link between breach and loss?",["Target Holdings v Redferns","Keech v Sandford","Re Rose"],"<b>Target Holdings</b>, followed by AIB v Redler: compensation reflects loss caused by the breach."],[2,"Trustees A and B breach the trust together. Liability?",["Joint and several","Several only","Equal only"],"Co-trustees are <b>jointly and severally</b> liable; contribution under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978."],[3,"Which section lets the court excuse a trustee?",["TA 1925 s.61","TA 1925 s.31","TA 2000 s.1"],"<b>s.61</b>: the trustee acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused."],[4,"A trustee leaves everything to a co-trustee, who makes a bad unauthorised investment. Liable?",["Yes","No","Only if paid"],"<b>Bahin v Hughes</b>: a passive trustee is liable too; standing by is itself a breach."],[5,"An adult beneficiary freely consented to the breach, knowing the facts. Can they sue?",["No","Yes","Yes, within 6 years"],"An <b>adult beneficiary</b> who consented or acquiesced cannot later sue for it."],[6,"Which section lets the court impound a beneficiary's interest?",["TA 1925 s.62","TA 1925 s.61","TA 2000 s.4"],"<b>s.62</b>: where the beneficiary instigated, requested or consented in writing to the breach."],[7,"What is the normal limitation period for breach of trust claims?",["6 years","12 years","3 years"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.21(3)</b>: 6 years."],[8,"When does time start for a remainderman's breach of trust claim?",["When the interest falls into possession","On the date of the breach","On the settlor's death"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.21(3)</b>: for a <b>future interest</b>, time runs only once it falls into possession."],[9,"Is there a limitation period for fraud by the trustee?",["No","Yes, 6 years","Yes, 12 years"],"<b>s.21(1)</b>: no limit for fraud, or for recovering trust property from the trustee."],[10,"Can a trust deed exclude liability for negligence?",["Yes, but not for dishonesty","No","Yes, including fraud"],"<b>Armitage v Nurse</b>: exemption clauses are valid except for dishonesty or fraud."],[11,"When may one trustee get a full indemnity from a co-trustee?",["The co-trustee alone took the money","Both were equally involved","The co-trustee was unpaid"],"A <b>full indemnity</b> may be ordered where the co-trustee alone took or benefited from the money, or was a solicitor whose advice controlled the breach."]],"c":[[337,"What is the basic measure of liability for breach of trust?","<b>Equitable compensation</b> to <b>restore the fund</b> to where it would have been <b>but for</b> the breach."],[340,"Is a trustee liable for a loss that would have happened anyway?","<b>No.</b> Example: a loss from a fraudulent valuation that would have occurred regardless (Target Holdings)."],[346,"What is joint and several liability?","The beneficiary can sue <b>any one</b> trustee for the <b>whole</b> loss, or several together."],[347,"How is contribution between trustees decided?","Under the <b>Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978</b>: what is <b>just and equitable</b> given each trustee's <b>responsibility</b>, from nothing to a <b>full indemnity</b>."],[352,"When can a beneficiary not sue for a breach?","If they were an <b>adult with capacity</b> who <b>consented</b>, <b>instigated</b> or <b>acquiesced</b> with <b>full knowledge</b> of the facts, and freely."],[355,"What is impounding under TA 1925 s.62?","The court may take a beneficiary's interest to <b>indemnify</b> the trustee where the beneficiary <b>instigated, requested or consented in writing</b> to the breach."],[358,"What can an exemption clause never exclude?","Liability for <b>actual fraud</b> or <b>dishonesty</b>. The <b>irreducible core</b> is honesty and good faith (Armitage v Nurse)."],[363,"What are the conditions for court relief under TA 1925 s.61?","The trustee acted <b>honestly</b>, <b>reasonably</b>, and <b>ought fairly to be excused</b>. All three are needed, and the burden is on the trustee."],[366,"What is the general limitation period for breach of trust?","<b>6 years</b> from when the cause of action accrued, usually the <b>breach</b> (Limitation Act 1980 s.21(3))."],[367,"When is there no limitation period for breach of trust?","<b>Fraud</b> to which the trustee was a party or privy. Or recovering trust property <b>still held</b> by the trustee or <b>converted to their own use</b> (s.21(1))."]]},{"id":"strangers","title":"Liability of strangers: knowing receipt and dishonest assistance","q":[[0,"What two claims exist against strangers to a trust?",["Knowing receipt and dishonest assistance","Fraud and negligence","Tracing and subrogation"],"The two personal claims are <b>knowing receipt</b> and <b>dishonest assistance</b>."],[1,"Which element is essential for knowing receipt but not dishonest assistance?",["Receipt of trust property","Dishonesty","A breach of trust"],"<b>Receipt</b> for the stranger's own benefit is required for knowing receipt."],[2,"What state of mind does knowing receipt need?",["Knowledge making retention unconscionable","Dishonesty","Negligence"],"<b>BCCI v Akindele</b>: knowledge such that it is unconscionable to retain the benefit."],[3,"Which case gives the test for dishonest assistance?",["Royal Brunei v Tan","BCCI v Akindele","Re Diplock"],"<b>Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan</b>: the assistant must have acted dishonestly."],[4,"Is mere negligence enough for dishonest assistance?",["No","Yes","Yes, for professionals"],"<b>Royal Brunei</b>: an honest person who is merely careless is not liable."],[5,"Which case sets the modern test of dishonesty?",["Ivey v Genting","Re Kayford","Re Rose"],"<b>Ivey v Genting</b>: the person's actual knowledge, then the standards of ordinary decent people."],[6,"Must the trustee's breach be dishonest for assistance liability?",["No, only the assistant","Yes","Yes, it must be fraudulent"],"<b>Royal Brunei</b>: the trustee need not be dishonest, but the assistant must be."],[7,"Must the assistant receive any of the property?",["No","Yes","Yes, a fee"],"Dishonest assistance needs <b>no receipt</b> by the assistant."],[8,"An agent receives trust funds for a principal, then passes them on. Liable for knowing receipt?",["Generally no, not for own benefit","Yes","Yes, if paid"],"An agent who receives only for the principal is a <b>ministerial</b> recipient, not a beneficial one, so not liable in receipt."],[9,"A solicitor knowingly helps a client misapply trust money. Which claim?",["Dishonest assistance","Knowing receipt","Resulting trust"],"Helping a breach dishonestly without receiving is <b>dishonest assistance</b>."],[10,"A knowing recipient has spent the trust money. Is the claim defeated?",["No, the claim is personal","Yes, nothing is left","Yes, if spent on living costs"],"<b>Knowing receipt</b> is a <b>personal</b> claim, so it survives even if the property has gone."],[11,"Are strangers' liabilities personal or proprietary?",["Personal","Proprietary","Both"],"Both claims are <b>personal</b>, against the stranger's own assets."]],"c":[[374,"What are the two main ways a stranger to a trust can be personally liable?","<b>Knowing receipt</b> and <b>dishonest assistance</b>."],[375,"Are knowing receipt and dishonest assistance personal or proprietary claims?","<b>Personal.</b> The stranger pays from their own assets, even if they <b>no longer have</b> the property."],[377,"What must a claimant show for knowing receipt?","<b>1.</b> A <b>disposal</b> of their assets in <b>breach</b> of trust or fiduciary duty. <b>2.</b> <b>Beneficial receipt</b> of traceable assets. <b>3.</b> <b>Knowledge</b> that they are traceable to a breach."],[382,"What is the knowledge test for knowing receipt?","Was the recipient's knowledge such as to make it <b>unconscionable</b> for them to <b>retain the benefit</b>? (BCCI v Akindele)"],[383,"Is dishonesty required for knowing receipt?","<b>No.</b>"],[392,"What must a claimant prove for dishonest assistance?","<b>1.</b> A <b>breach</b> of trust or fiduciary duty. <b>2.</b> The defendant <b>assisted</b> or <b>procured</b> it. <b>3.</b> The defendant acted <b>dishonestly</b>."],[393,"Must the trustee be dishonest for a stranger to be liable for dishonest assistance?","<b>No.</b> Only the <b>assistant's</b> dishonesty matters (Royal Brunei v Tan)."],[395,"What is the test for dishonesty?","<b>Stage 1</b>: the defendant's <b>actual knowledge or belief</b> as to the facts. <b>Stage 2</b>: was the conduct dishonest by the standards of <b>ordinary decent people</b>? (Ivey v Genting)"],[401,"What is a trustee de son tort?","Someone <b>never appointed</b> who takes it upon themselves to <b>act as trustee</b> by intermeddling with trust property."],[402,"How is a trustee de son tort treated?","As a <b>real trustee</b>, with the same duties and liabilities, including the trustees' limitation rules."]]},{"id":"remedies-tracing","title":"Equitable remedies and tracing","q":[[0,"What is tracing?",["A process to identify the property","A remedy awarding damages","A defence"],"<b>Tracing</b> identifies assets; the remedy is a proprietary claim, such as a trust or charge."],[1,"What does equitable tracing require?",["A fiduciary relationship","A contract with the trustee","The trustee's written consent"],"<b>Re Diplock</b>: tracing in equity needs an initial fiduciary relationship."],[2,"A trustee mixes his own money with trust money. Which rule applies?",["Re Hallett: own money spent first","Clayton's Case: first in, first out","Pari passu sharing"],"<b>Re Hallett</b>: the trustee is presumed to spend his own money first."],[3,"A trustee buys shares from a mixed account, then spends the rest. Claim the shares?",["Yes, under Re Oatway","No, Hallett says own money first","No, only a personal claim"],"<b>Re Oatway</b>: beneficiaries have a <b>first charge</b> on the mixed fund and anything bought with it."],[4,"Two innocent trusts' money is mixed in a current account. Usual modern approach?",["Pari passu sharing","Re Hallett","Re Oatway"],"<b>Barlow Clowes v Vaughan</b>: courts usually share <b>pari passu</b>; Clayton's Case (first in, first out) is often displaced."],[5,"What limits the claim to money left in a bank account?",["The lowest intermediate balance","The highest balance","The final balance"],"<b>Roscoe v Winder</b>: the claim cannot exceed the lowest balance."],[6,"Can equity trace into an overdrawn account?",["Generally no, the money is gone","Yes, always","Yes, if mixed"],"<b>Bishopsgate v Homan</b>: there is no asset to trace into."],[7,"Which case says the beneficiary may take proportionate share or lien?",["Foskett v McKeown","Re Hallett","Tito v Waddell"],"<b>Foskett v McKeown</b>: a proportionate share, or a lien, over the mixed asset."],[8,"Which defences stop a proprietary claim?",["Bona fide purchase, change of position","Delay only","Being a trustee"],"<b>Bona fide purchaser</b> and <b>change of position</b> can defeat a tracing claim."],[9,"Why prefer a proprietary remedy over a personal claim?",["Priority in the defendant's insolvency","It is quicker","It is cheaper"],"A <b>proprietary</b> claim ranks ahead of unsecured creditors."],[10,"Equitable remedies are...",["Discretionary","Available as of right","Automatic"],"They are <b>discretionary</b>; barred by delay, unclean hands or adequate damages."],[11,"What is a freezing injunction?",["An order stopping assets being dissipated","An order to sell assets","An order for delivery"],"A <b>freezing injunction</b> (Mareva) prevents the defendant moving assets away."]],"c":[[404,"What is the difference between a personal and a proprietary claim?","<b>Personal</b>: the defendant must <b>pay</b> money. <b>Proprietary</b>: a claim to <b>specific property</b>, or a <b>charge</b> over it."],[405,"Why is a proprietary claim better on insolvency?","The asset is <b>not</b> part of the defendant's estate, so the beneficiary takes it <b>ahead of creditors</b>."],[409,"What is the difference between following and tracing?","<b>Following</b>: tracking the <b>same asset</b> from hand to hand. <b>Tracing</b>: identifying a <b>new asset</b> as the substitute."],[413,"When is tracing lost?","<b>Dissipation</b>. Reaching a <b>bona fide purchaser for value without notice</b>. Where tracing would be <b>inequitable</b>."],[418,"What is the rule in Re Hallett?","A wrongdoing trustee is <b>presumed to spend their own money first</b>. What remains in the account is <b>trust money</b>."],[419,"What is the rule in Re Oatway?","Beneficiaries have a <b>first charge</b> on the mixed fund <b>and anything bought with it</b>. The trustee cannot say the purchase used their own money if the rest has gone."],[421,"What is the lowest intermediate balance rule?","The beneficiary cannot claim more from an account than the <b>lowest balance</b> it reached after the trust money went in."],[424,"What can beneficiaries claim where trust and trustee money together bought an asset?","Either a <b>proportionate share</b> of the asset, including growth, or a <b>lien</b> for the trust money (Foskett v McKeown)."],[426,"When is the rule in Clayton's Case (first in, first out) displaced?","<b>Clayton's Case</b>: in a current account, money <b>first paid in is first paid out</b>; traditionally used between <b>innocent</b> beneficiaries whose money is mixed. It is displaced where it would be <b>impracticable</b>, <b>unjust</b>, or contrary to the parties' <b>intentions</b>. Courts now usually prefer <b>pari passu</b> (proportionate) sharing."],[430,"What is the change of position defence?","An <b>innocent</b> recipient who changed position in <b>good faith</b> because of the receipt, so that full repayment is <b>inequitable</b>, has a defence <b>to the extent</b> of the change."],[437,"When is specific performance available?","Where <b>damages are inadequate</b>, such as land, unique goods or private company shares. Not for personal services or constant supervision."]]}]},{"id":"land","title":"Land Law","stop":11,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M-90,0 q20,-12 40,0 q20,-10 40,0 q20,-12 40,0 q20,-10 40,0 q20,-12 20,0 Z\" fill=\"#95D5B2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M-56.4,-48.400000000000006 L-55.2,-37.599999999999994 M-39.6,-48.400000000000006 L-40.8,-37.599999999999994\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"-56.64\" y=\"-37.599999999999994\" width=\"17.28\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#B5835A\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M-48,-47.2 C-79.2,-68.8 -74.4,-100 -48,-100 C-21.599999999999998,-100 -16.799999999999997,-68.8 -48,-47.2 Z\" fill=\"#E63946\"/>\n  <path d=\"M-48,-47.2 C-58.8,-68.8 -57.6,-100 -48,-100 C-38.4,-100 -37.2,-68.8 -48,-47.2 Z\" fill=\"#FFE066\"/><path d=\"M3.3500000000000005,-82.15 L4.3,-73.6 M16.65,-82.15 L15.7,-73.6\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"3.16\" y=\"-73.6\" width=\"13.68\" height=\"9.5\" fill=\"#B5835A\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10,-81.2 C-14.7,-98.3 -10.900000000000002,-123 10,-123 C30.900000000000002,-123 34.7,-98.3 10,-81.2 Z\" fill=\"#2EC4B6\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10,-81.2 C1.4499999999999993,-98.3 2.3999999999999995,-123 10,-123 C17.6,-123 18.55,-98.3 10,-81.2 Z\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M48.3,-30.700000000000003 L49.4,-20.799999999999997 M63.7,-30.700000000000003 L62.6,-20.799999999999997\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"48.08\" y=\"-20.799999999999997\" width=\"15.84\" height=\"11\" fill=\"#B5835A\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M56,-29.6 C27.4,-49.4 31.799999999999997,-78 56,-78 C80.2,-78 84.6,-49.4 56,-29.6 Z\" fill=\"#9B5DE5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M56,-29.6 C46.1,-49.4 47.2,-78 56,-78 C64.8,-78 65.9,-49.4 56,-29.6 Z\" fill=\"#FF9EC4\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"nature-of-land","title":"The nature of land: estates and interests","q":[[0,"Which two estates can exist as legal estates in land?",["Freehold and leasehold","Life estate and freehold","Fee tail and leasehold"],"Only these two exist at law under <b>s.1(1) LPA 1925</b>: freehold and leasehold."],[1,"A statue rests by its own weight but was designed into the garden layout. What is it?",["A fixture","A chattel","A licence"],"<b>Purpose of annexation</b> can override the degree test: items forming part of the architectural design can be fixtures."],[2,"Paintings are tacked to a wall only to show them off. Fixtures or chattels?",["Chattels","Fixtures","Fixtures once a year has passed"],"Fixed only to display and enjoy them, so the <b>purpose</b> is enjoyment of an object, not improving the land."],[3,"Which of these can exist only in equity?",["A restrictive covenant","A legal charge by deed","A rentcharge in fee simple"],"Restrictive covenants are not listed in <b>s.1(2)</b>, so by <b>s.1(3)</b> they can only be equitable."],[4,"Can an easement granted for the life of the neighbour be a legal easement?",["No, only equitable","Yes, if made by deed","Yes, if registered"],"A legal easement must last as long as a <b>fee simple or term of years</b>. A life easement is always equitable."],[5,"A 10-year easement is granted by deed over registered land but never registered. It is...",["Equitable","Legal","Void"],"An express easement over registered land is a <b>registrable disposition</b> and is legal only once registered (s.27 LRA 2002)."],[6,"How many trustees must receive the purchase money to overreach beneficial interests?",["At least two","Just one","At least three"],"Payment to <b>two trustees</b> (or a trust corporation) moves the beneficial interests from the land to the money."],[7,"The buyer receives the land as a gift. Is the buyer bound by unprotected interests?",["Yes","No","Only if the buyer knew of them"],"A <b>volunteer</b> gives no value, so is bound by all existing interests, protected or not."],[8,"A freeholder lets the house to a tenant. Is the freehold still 'in possession'?",["Yes, receiving rent counts","No, the tenant has possession","Only once the lease ends"],"<b>s.205(1)(xix) LPA 1925</b>: 'possession' includes receipt of rents and profits, so the freehold stays a legal estate."],[9,"What problem does commonhold solve?",["Positive covenants not binding successors","Leases being too short","Lack of a Land Registry"],"Under <b>commonhold</b>, the community statement makes repair and service obligations bind every unit-holder."],[10,"From when is an estoppel equity a proprietary interest in registered land?",["From the moment it arises","From completion of the sale","From registration of a notice"],"<b>s.116 LRA 2002</b> says an estoppel equity is an interest capable of binding a buyer from the time it arises."],[11,"A houseboat can be moved without damaging it. Is it part of the land?",["No, it is a chattel","Yes, it is a fixture","Yes, once it is moored"],"Easily removed without damage points to a <b>chattel</b>, not a fixture."]],"c":[[3,"Does a licence bind a buyer of the land?","<b>No.</b> A licence is a <b>personal</b> permission and binds only the person who granted it."],[10,"What does the degree of annexation test presume?","Attached other than by its own weight: <b>presumed fixture</b>. Resting by its own weight: <b>presumed chattel</b>."],[11,"Which test is more important: degree or purpose of annexation?","<b>Purpose.</b> It can override the degree test <b>either way</b>."],[12,"What does the purpose of annexation test ask?","Was it attached to <b>improve the land permanently</b> (fixture), or to <b>enjoy it as an object</b> (chattel)?"],[22,"Which two estates can exist at law?","The <b>fee simple absolute in possession</b> (freehold) and the <b>term of years absolute</b> (leasehold): LPA 1925 <b>s.1(1)</b>."],[26,"Which interests are capable of being legal under LPA 1925 s.1(2)?","<b>Easements</b> for a fee simple or term of years. <b>Rentcharges</b>. <b>Charges by way of legal mortgage</b>. <b>Rights of entry</b> over a legal lease or rentcharge."],[31,"What are the main equitable interests in land?","<b>Beneficial interests under a trust.</b> <b>Restrictive covenants.</b> <b>Estate contracts</b>, including options and pre-emption rights. <b>Equitable leases, easements and mortgages.</b> <b>Estoppel equities.</b>"],[34,"What are the three conditions for a right to be legal?","<b>1. Capable</b> of being legal under s.1. <b>2.</b> Created by <b>deed</b> (or an exception). <b>3. Registered</b>, if a registrable disposition."],[35,"If a right capable of being legal fails a formality, can it still exist?","<b>Yes</b>, in <b>equity</b>, if there is a valid contract. <b>Equity treats as done that which ought to be done</b> (Walsh v Lonsdale)."]]},{"id":"formalities","title":"Formalities: deeds, contracts and s.53","q":[[0,"What is needed to create or transfer a legal estate in land?",["A deed","Signed writing only","An oral agreement"],"<b>s.52 LPA 1925</b>: a legal estate must be created or transferred by deed (plus registration where required)."],[1,"What does a contract for the sale of land need under s.2 LP(MP)A 1989?",["Signed writing with all agreed terms","A deed","Registration at the Land Registry"],"The contract must be in <b>writing</b>, contain all expressly agreed terms and be signed by or for each party."],[2,"Does an individual need a seal to execute a deed?",["No","Yes","Only for land"],"A seal is not required. The individual signs in the presence of an <b>attesting witness</b>."],[3,"An oral 2-year lease starts today at market rent, no premium. Is it a legal lease?",["Yes","No, it needs a deed","No, it needs registration"],"<b>s.54(2) LPA 1925</b>: leases of 3 years or less, in possession, at best rent, without a premium, need no deed."],[4,"An oral 2-year lease at market rent starts in 6 months' time. Is it legal?",["No, it is not in possession","Yes, it is under 3 years","Yes, if rent is paid"],"It does not <b>take effect in possession</b>, so it falls outside s.54(2) and needs a deed."],[5,"A contract for sale of land fails to meet s.2. What is its effect?",["It is void: no contract","It is valid but unenforceable","It is voidable"],"Since 1989, non-compliance with <b>s.2</b> means there is no contract at all."],[6,"Must the exercise of an option to buy land comply with s.2?",["No","Yes, in a deed","Yes, in signed writing"],"<b>Exercise</b> is a one-sided act under an existing contract. Only the grant of the option needs s.2."],[7,"What does s.53(1)(c) require to dispose of an existing equitable interest?",["Signed writing","A deed","Registration"],"Without signed writing, a disposition of an existing <b>equitable interest</b> is void."],[8,"A trust of land is declared orally. What is the effect under s.53(1)(b)?",["Unenforceable until signed writing exists","Void","Valid, but only for 6 months"],"A declaration of trust of land must be <b>proved by signed writing</b>. Until then it is unenforceable, not void."],[9,"Which case held that hints over many years can be an assurance for estoppel?",["Thorner v Major","Cobbe v Yeoman's Row","Walsh v Lonsdale"],"<b>Thorner v Major</b>: an assurance need not be express if clear enough in context."],[10,"A couple buy a home in joint names with no declaration of trust. What is the starting point?",["Equal shares","Shares matching contributions","Shares set by the court at sale"],"For a family home in joint names, <b>equity follows the law</b>: equal beneficial shares unless a different intention is shown."],[11,"Which can validly execute a deed for a company?",["One director signing before a witness","One director signing alone","The company secretary alone"],"A company may use the common seal, <b>two authorised signatories</b>, or one director with an attesting witness."]],"c":[[44,"What formality is needed to create or transfer a legal estate or interest?","A <b>deed</b> (LPA 1925 <b>s.52</b>), plus <b>registration</b> if it is a registrable disposition."],[45,"What formality does a contract for an interest in land need?","<b>Writing</b>, containing <b>all the expressly agreed terms</b>, <b>signed by or for each party</b> (LP(MP)A 1989 <b>s.2</b>)."],[50,"What are the three requirements for a valid deed (LP(MP)A 1989 s.1)?","<b>1. Clear on its face</b> that it is a deed. <b>2. Validly executed.</b> <b>3. Delivered</b> as a deed."],[51,"How does an individual validly execute a deed?","<b>Signs in the presence of a witness</b> who <b>attests</b> the signature, or someone signs <b>at their direction and in their presence</b> with <b>two attesting witnesses</b>. It must also be <b>delivered</b> as a deed (LP(MP)A 1989 s.1(3))."],[57,"When can a lease be legal without a deed (LPA 1925 s.54(2))?","<b>3 years or less</b>, taking effect <b>in possession</b>, at the <b>best rent reasonably obtainable</b>, <b>without a premium</b>."],[69,"What is the effect of failing to comply with s.2?","There is <b>no contract at all</b>: it is <b>void</b>. Part performance does not save it."],[78,"What does LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c) require?","A <b>disposition of an existing equitable interest</b> must be in <b>signed writing</b>. Otherwise it is <b>void</b>."],[79,"Which trusts are exempt from s.53?","<b>Resulting, implied and constructive trusts</b> (s.53(2))."],[84,"What must a non-owner prove for a home in the other partner's sole name?","A <b>common intention</b> to share, plus <b>detrimental reliance</b>."],[89,"What are the elements of proprietary estoppel?","<b>Assurance</b>, <b>reliance</b>, <b>detriment</b>, and <b>unconscionability</b>."]]},{"id":"registered-land","title":"Registered title (LRA 2002)","q":[[0,"Which principle of registration keeps trusts off the register?",["The curtain principle","The mirror principle","The insurance principle"],"<b>Curtain</b>: trusts are kept off the register. <b>Mirror</b>: it reflects the title. <b>Insurance</b>: the State compensates for mistakes."],[1,"Which lease must be registered with its own title when granted?",["A 10-year lease","A 7-year lease","A 3-year lease"],"Compulsory registration applies to leases of <b>more than 7 years</b>. Exactly 7 does not qualify."],[2,"How long after a triggering event must first registration be applied for?",["2 months","6 months","12 months"],"<b>s.6 LRA 2002</b>: 2 months, though Land Registry can extend the time."],[3,"First registration was not applied for in time. What happens to the legal estate?",["It reverts to the transferor","It stays with the transferee","The transfer is valid but unregistered"],"<b>s.7</b>: the transfer is void as to the legal estate, which reverts to the transferor on bare trust for the transferee."],[4,"Where on the register is a restriction entered?",["Proprietorship register","Property register","Charges register"],"The <b>proprietorship register</b> records the class of title, the owners and any restrictions."],[5,"Which interest cannot be protected by notice?",["A beneficial interest under a trust of land","A restrictive covenant","An estate contract"],"<b>s.33 LRA 2002</b>: trust interests are protected by restriction (or actual occupation), never by notice."],[6,"What does a Form A restriction ensure?",["Money is paid to two trustees","The land cannot be sold","The mortgage ranks first"],"It prevents registration of a disposition by a sole proprietor unless capital money is paid to <b>two trustees</b>, so interests are overreached."],[7,"On which date is actual occupation judged for Sch 3 para 2?",["The date of the disposition","The date of exchange","The date of the survey"],"Occupation is tested at <b>completion</b> (the disposition), not exchange."],[8,"Do a spouse's home rights override a buyer without being noted?",["No, never","Yes, if in occupation","Yes, for 12 months"],"<b>FLA 1996 s.31(10)</b>: home rights must be protected by notice. They never override."],[9,"How long does an official search (OS1) give priority?",["30 working days","15 working days","3 months"],"The <b>OS1</b> priority period is 30 working days. A land charges search gives 15."],[10,"A legal lease granted by deed for 5 years is not on the register. Does it bind a buyer?",["Yes, it overrides","No, it must be noted","Only if the tenant is in occupation"],"<b>Sch 3 para 1</b>: legal leases of 7 years or less override."],[11,"After how long can a squatter first apply to be registered in registered land?",["10 years","12 years","20 years"],"The owner can serve a <b>counter-notice</b>. 12 years applies to unregistered land."]],"c":[[97,"Which events trigger compulsory first registration (LRA 2002 s.4)?","A <b>transfer</b> of a freehold or lease over 7 years (for value, <b>gift</b>, court order or <b>assent</b>). Grant of a <b>lease over 7 years</b>. A lease starting <b>more than 3 months</b> after grant. A <b>first legal mortgage</b>."],[98,"What is the time limit for first registration?","<b>2 months</b> from the triggering event (s.6). Land Registry can extend it."],[99,"What happens if first registration is not applied for in time?","The transfer is <b>void</b> as to the legal estate. It <b>reverts to the transferor</b>, who holds on <b>bare trust</b> for the transferee (s.7)."],[110,"What are the main registrable dispositions of a registered estate?","<b>Transfer</b> of the estate. Grant of a <b>lease over 7 years</b>. <b>Express legal easement</b>. <b>Legal charge</b>."],[115,"Which interests are protected by notice?","<b>Restrictive covenants</b>, <b>estate contracts</b> and <b>options</b>, <b>equitable easements</b>, <b>leases over 3 years</b> (up to 7), <b>home rights</b>, <b>estoppel equities</b>."],[116,"Which interests cannot be protected by notice (s.33)?","<b>Beneficial interests under a trust of land.</b> <b>Leases of 3 years or less.</b> Landlord and tenant <b>restrictive covenants</b> about the leased property."],[121,"Which leases override under Sch 3 para 1?","<b>Legal</b> leases of <b>7 years or less</b>. Not reversionary leases starting more than 3 months after grant, and not <b>equitable</b> leases."],[122,"What two things are needed to override under Sch 3 para 2?","A <b>proprietary interest</b> in the land, and <b>actual occupation</b> at the date of the disposition."],[131,"When does an occupier's interest not override under para 2?","If they <b>failed to disclose</b> it on <b>reasonable inquiry</b>. Or if occupation was <b>not obvious</b> on careful inspection <b>and</b> the buyer did not <b>actually know</b>. Or a reversionary lease not yet in effect."],[137,"What is the special priority rule (s.29)?","A <b>registered</b> disposition for <b>valuable consideration</b> takes priority over earlier interests <b>unless</b> protected by <b>notice</b>, a registered charge, <b>overriding</b>, or appearing from the register."],[147,"When can a squatter apply to be registered for registered land?","After <b>10 years'</b> adverse possession."]]},{"id":"unregistered-land","title":"Unregistered title","q":[[0,"How old must the root of title be in unregistered land?",["At least 15 years","At least 12 years","At least 30 years"],"<b>s.44 LPA 1925</b>: title must start with a good root at least 15 years old."],[1,"How long does a land charges search certificate give priority?",["15 working days","30 working days","6 months"],"The land charges priority period is <b>15 working days</b>."],[2,"An option to buy unregistered land should be registered as which land charge?",["Class C(iv)","Class D(ii)","Class F"],"<b>C(iv)</b> covers estate contracts: contracts to buy, options, pre-emption rights and equitable leases."],[3,"A post-1925 restrictive covenant in unregistered land is a land charge of which class?",["Class D(ii)","Class C(iv)","Class D(iii)"],"<b>D(ii)</b> is restrictive covenants (not landlord and tenant). <b>D(iii)</b> is equitable easements."],[4,"Which land charge protects a spouse's home rights?",["Class F","Class C(i)","Class D(iii)"],"<b>Class F</b> is for home rights under the Family Law Act 1996."],[5,"A C(iv) charge is not registered. Against whom is it void?",["Buyer of a legal estate for money","Only a buyer without notice","Anyone who inherits the land"],"<b>LCA 1972 s.4(6)</b>: void against a buyer of a legal estate for money or money's worth. A donee or heir is still bound."],[6,"The buyer knew of an unregistered C(iv) land charge. Is the buyer bound?",["No, knowledge is irrelevant","Yes, because the buyer knew","Only if the buyer was dishonest"],"Registration, not knowledge, decides. An unregistered charge is <b>void</b> against a protected buyer even if known."],[7,"Capital money is paid to two trustees. What happens to a beneficiary's interest?",["It is overreached","It binds the buyer","It becomes a land charge"],"<b>Overreaching</b> moves the beneficiary's interest from the land to the money."],[8,"Who takes free under the doctrine of notice?",["Bona fide legal buyer without notice","Any buyer for value","Anyone who registers first"],"The buyer must act in good faith, give value, take a <b>legal estate</b> and have no notice."],[9,"What is constructive notice?",["What reasonable inquiries would have revealed","What the buyer was told directly","What the seller's agent was told"],"<b>Actual</b>: knew. <b>Constructive</b>: should have found out. <b>Imputed</b>: known to the buyer's agent."],[10,"A second mortgage without the title deeds in unregistered land is registered as...",["Class C(i)","Class C(iv)","Class D(iii)"],"A <b>puisne mortgage</b> is a legal mortgage not protected by deposit of deeds: Class C(i)."],[11,"Which case shows a buyer can take free of an unregistered option even when defeating it deliberately?",["Midland Bank v Green","Kingsnorth Finance v Tizard","Hunt v Luck"],"<b>Midland Bank v Green</b>: the land charges system does not ask about good faith."]],"c":[[153,"What makes a good root of title?","It deals with the <b>whole legal and equitable interest</b>, <b>describes the land</b>, <b>casts no doubt</b> on title, and is at least <b>15 years old</b>."],[158,"What priority period does a land charges search give?","<b>15 working days</b> from the certificate."],[165,"What is a Class C(iv) land charge?","An <b>estate contract</b>: contracts to buy or grant a lease, <b>options</b>, <b>pre-emption</b> rights, and <b>equitable leases</b>."],[166,"What is a Class D(ii) land charge?","A <b>restrictive covenant</b> created <b>after 1925</b>, other than one between landlord and tenant."],[171,"Against whom are unregistered C(iv) and D land charges void?","A <b>purchaser of a legal estate for money or money's worth</b> (LCA 1972 s.4(6))."],[172,"Against whom are unregistered C(i)–C(iii) and F land charges void?","A <b>purchaser for value of any interest</b>, legal or equitable (s.4(5))."],[174,"Does actual knowledge matter if a land charge is unregistered?","<b>No.</b> It is void against a protected purchaser even if they <b>knew</b> of it."],[181,"Who takes free under the doctrine of notice?","A <b>bona fide purchaser of a legal estate for value without notice</b>."],[186,"What is the order of questions in unregistered land?","<b>1. Legal</b> right: binds. <b>2. Land charge</b>: binds only if registered. <b>3. Trust</b>: overreached if two trustees paid. <b>4.</b> Anything else: <b>doctrine of notice</b>."]]},{"id":"co-ownership","title":"Co-ownership and trusts of land","q":[[0,"How many people can hold the legal title to co-owned land?",["Up to four","Up to two","Any number"],"<b>Trustee Act 1925 s.34</b>: legal title is held by a maximum of four joint tenants."],[1,"Can a legal joint tenancy be severed?",["Never","Yes, by written notice","Yes, by a will"],"<b>s.36(2) LPA 1925</b>: only the beneficial interest can be severed. Legal title passes by survivorship."],[2,"Which unity alone is needed for a tenancy in common?",["Possession","Time","Title"],"A tenancy in common needs only <b>unity of possession</b>. A joint tenancy needs possession, interest, title and time."],[3,"Land is transferred 'to A and B in equal shares'. How do they hold the beneficial interest?",["As tenants in common","As joint tenants","As trustees only"],"<b>Words of severance</b> such as 'equally' or 'in equal shares' create a tenancy in common."],[4,"A divorce petition asks for 'such order as may be just'. Does it sever?",["No","Yes","Yes, if served on the spouse"],"<b>Harris v Goddard</b>: it refers only to a possible future order, so shows no immediate intention to sever."],[5,"A severance notice is posted, and the recipient never reads it. Is severance effective?",["Yes, on delivery","No, it must be read","Only if signed for"],"<b>Kinch v Bullard</b>: a notice served properly takes effect on delivery, even if it is never read."],[6,"Does a will leaving a joint tenant's share to a friend sever the joint tenancy?",["No","Yes, on death","Yes, when signed"],"<b>Survivorship</b> operates at death, before any will takes effect."],[7,"A joint tenant secretly mortgages her beneficial share. What is the effect?",["It severs her share","Nothing changes","It is void without consent"],"Dealing with your own share, such as a sale or charge, <b>severs</b> it even if the others do not know."],[8,"Which event automatically severs a beneficial joint tenancy?",["Bankruptcy of a joint tenant","A joint tenant moving out","An oral wish kept private"],"The bankrupt's interest vests in the <b>trustee in bankruptcy</b>, so the unity of title ends."],[9,"How long after bankruptcy do creditors' interests presumptively outweigh all others on a sale application?",["One year","Six months","Three years"],"<b>Insolvency Act 1986 s.335A</b>: after one year, creditors win unless the circumstances are exceptional."],[10,"Money is paid to two trustees while a beneficiary lives in the house. Is her interest overreached?",["Yes","No, she is in actual occupation","Only if she consents"],"<b>City of London BS v Flegg</b>: overreaching defeats the interest even if the beneficiary is in occupation."],[11,"The surviving co-owner holds as trustee for tenants in common. What must be done before a sale?",["Appoint a second trustee","Obtain a court order","Register a notice"],"Two trustees must receive the money so that the beneficial interests are <b>overreached</b>."]],"c":[[187,"How is the legal title held when land is co-owned?","Always as <b>joint tenants</b>, by a maximum of <b>four</b> people, who are <b>trustees</b>."],[196,"What should you look for first to decide the beneficial interests?","An <b>express declaration of trust</b>, for example in <b>panel 10 of the TR1</b>."],[198,"What are words of severance?","Words showing distinct shares, creating a <b>tenancy in common</b>: \"<b>equally</b>\", \"<b>in equal shares</b>\", \"<b>share and share alike</b>\", \"<b>to be divided between</b>\"."],[200,"When does equity presume a tenancy in common?","<b>Business partners</b>, <b>joint mortgagees</b>, and <b>unequal</b> contributors buying as a <b>business investment</b>."],[205,"How is severance by written notice made (LPA 1925 s.36(2))?","<b>Notice in writing</b> to <b>all</b> the other joint tenants. <b>No consent</b> or reason is needed."],[211,"Which acts operating on one's own share sever a joint tenancy?","<b>Selling</b>, <b>mortgaging</b> or <b>charging</b> one's interest, even secretly. A <b>contract</b> to sell one's share."],[214,"Does severance by mutual agreement need a binding contract?","<b>No.</b> An oral agreement can sever, even if never carried out (Burgess v Rawnsley)."],[227,"What factors does the court consider under TLATA s.15?","<b>Intentions</b> of the creator. <b>Purposes</b> of the trust. <b>Welfare of minors</b> in occupation. <b>Secured creditors'</b> interests. <b>Wishes</b> of adult beneficiaries in possession."],[230,"What is presumed one year after bankruptcy under s.335A?","The <b>creditors' interests outweigh all other considerations</b>, unless the circumstances are <b>exceptional</b>."],[233,"Does overreaching work even if beneficiaries are in actual occupation?","<b>Yes</b> (City of London BS v Flegg)."]]},{"id":"easements","title":"Easements","q":[[0,"Can an easement exist in gross, without any benefited land?",["No","Yes, if in writing","Yes, for roads"],"An easement needs a <b>dominant tenement</b> that it benefits. A profit, by contrast, can exist in gross."],[1,"Which of these cannot be an easement?",["A right to a view","A right of way","A right of drainage"],"A view is too vague and indefinite to be an <b>easement</b>."],[2,"Which principle stopped an all-day parking right being an easement in Batchelor v Marlow?",["Owner left no reasonable use of land","The right was not in writing","There was no dominant land"],"The <b>ouster principle</b>: an easement must not take away the servient owner's possession or reasonable use."],[3,"An express easement over registered land is legal when it is...",["Registered","Granted by deed","Used for 12 months"],"It is a <b>registrable disposition</b>: legal only once completed by registration (s.27(2)(d))."],[4,"An equitable easement over registered land is protected by...",["A notice on the register","A Form A restriction","Actual occupation of the servient land"],"Equitable easements do not override. They need a <b>notice</b> in the charges register."],[5,"Which easement arises when land is landlocked and has no other access?",["Easement of necessity","Prescriptive easement","Easement by estoppel"],"<b>Necessity</b> requires that the land cannot be used at all without the right."],[6,"On a sale of part, who can claim an easement under Wheeldon v Burrows?",["The buyer","The seller","Both"],"<b>Wheeldon v Burrows</b> (and s.62) benefit the buyer only. Only necessity and common intention help the seller."],[7,"Section 62 LPA 1925 can upgrade which right into an easement?",["A permission being enjoyed","An oral promise never used","A right to a view"],"On a <b>conveyance</b>, s.62 can turn an existing licence or permission into a legal easement."],[8,"What is the usual period for a prescriptive easement?",["20 years","12 years","10 years"],"Prescription needs 20 years' use as of right: <b>without force, secrecy or permission</b>."],[9,"The owner has tolerated a neighbour's path use for 25 years. Is prescription defeated?",["No, toleration is not permission","Yes, toleration is permission","Yes, if the owner knew of it"],"Prescription is defeated by <b>permission</b>, not by mere toleration. The owner's knowledge is in fact needed for prescription."],[10,"When does an implied legal easement override a buyer of registered land (Sch 3 para 3)?",["If used within the last year","Only if registered","Only if the buyer was told"],"It also overrides if the buyer knew of it or it was obvious on <b>careful inspection</b>."],[11,"Is long non-use of an easement enough to show it was abandoned?",["No, intention is needed","Yes, after 12 years","Yes, after 20 years"],"<b>Benn v Hardinge</b>: even very long non-use was not enough without a clear intention to give up the right permanently."]],"c":[[242,"What are the four requirements for an easement?","<b>1.</b> A <b>dominant and servient</b> tenement. <b>2.</b> It <b>accommodates</b> the dominant tenement. <b>3.</b> <b>Different</b> owners. <b>4.</b> <b>Capable of forming the subject matter of a grant</b>."],[244,"What does \"accommodate the dominant tenement\" mean?","It must <b>benefit the land itself</b>, not just the owner personally. The plots must be <b>sufficiently close</b>, though not necessarily adjoining."],[252,"What is the ouster principle?","An easement must <b>not deprive the servient owner</b> of possession or reasonable use of its land."],[261,"When is an easement of necessity implied?","Where the land <b>cannot be used at all</b> without it, such as <b>landlocked</b> land. Mere inconvenience is not enough."],[263,"When is an easement implied by common intention?","Where the parties shared an intention about a <b>particular use</b>, and the easement is <b>necessary</b> to carry it out (Wong v Beaumont)."],[265,"On a sale of part, when does a quasi-easement become an easement for the buyer?","When it was <b>continuous and apparent</b>, <b>necessary for reasonable enjoyment</b>, and <b>in use</b> at the transfer (Wheeldon v Burrows)."],[267,"Which implied routes can benefit the seller?","Only <b>necessity</b> and <b>common intention</b>. <b>Wheeldon v Burrows</b> and <b>s.62</b> are <b>grant only</b>."],[268,"What does LPA 1925 s.62 do?","Implies general words into every <b>conveyance</b>. It can <b>upgrade a permission</b> being enjoyed into a full <b>easement</b>."],[272,"What are the requirements for prescription?","Use <b>as of right</b> (without <b>force</b>, <b>secrecy</b> or <b>permission</b>), <b>continuous</b>, for <b>20 years</b>, <b>by and against a freeholder</b>."],[280,"When does an implied or prescriptive legal easement bind a buyer of registered land?","It <b>overrides</b> under <b>Sch 3 para 3</b> if the buyer <b>knew</b> of it, it was <b>obvious</b> on inspection, or it was <b>used within 1 year</b>."],[281,"How must an equitable easement be protected?","<b>Registered land</b>: by <b>notice</b>. <b>Unregistered</b>: post-1925 as a <b>D(iii)</b> land charge."]]},{"id":"covenants","title":"Freehold covenants","q":[[0,"Does the burden of a positive covenant run with freehold land?",["No","Yes, in equity","Yes, at law"],"<b>Rhone v Stephens</b>: positive burdens never run with freehold land, in law or equity."],[1,"Which of these covenants is restrictive?",["Not to build on the garden","To maintain the drive","To pay towards the shared wall"],"A covenant is restrictive if it can be complied with by <b>doing nothing</b>."],[2,"Does the burden of a covenant run at common law?",["Never","Yes, if restrictive","Yes, if in a deed"],"<b>Austerberry v Oldham</b>: the burden only passes in equity under Tulk v Moxhay."],[3,"The seller covenanted, then sold the land. Is the seller still liable on the covenant?",["Yes, in contract","No, the buyer is now liable","Only for 12 months"],"The original covenantor stays liable unless the covenant limits liability to the period of ownership."],[4,"A covenantee owns no land benefited when the covenant is made. Can the burden run?",["No","Yes, if restrictive","Yes, if registered"],"<b>LCC v Allen</b>: the covenantee must retain land that benefits from the covenant."],[5,"How is a restrictive covenant protected in registered land?",["Notice in the charges register","Restriction in the proprietorship register","Actual occupation"],"Without a <b>notice</b>, a buyer for value takes free of the covenant."],[6,"How is a post-1925 restrictive covenant protected in unregistered land?",["Class D(ii) land charge","Class C(iv) land charge","Doctrine of notice only"],"Register it as a <b>D(ii)</b> land charge against the burdened owner's name."],[7,"Which is not a way for the benefit to pass in equity?",["Prescription","Annexation","Express assignment"],"The three routes are <b>annexation</b>, <b>express assignment</b> and a <b>building scheme</b>."],[8,"What did Federated Homes decide about s.78 LPA 1925?",["It annexes the benefit automatically","It makes the burden run","It abolishes the touch and concern test"],"<b>s.78</b> annexes the benefit to each part of the benefited land, if it is identifiable and the covenant touches and concerns it."],[9,"A buyer uses a road shared with the estate and also faces a duty to contribute to its upkeep. Which principle applies?",["Benefit and burden","Tulk v Moxhay","Wheeldon v Burrows"],"<b>Halsall v Brizell</b>: someone taking a benefit must accept a linked burden."],[10,"Which body can discharge or modify a restrictive covenant?",["Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)","The county court","Land Registry"],"<b>LPA 1925 s.84</b>: grounds include obsolescence, impeding reasonable use, agreement and no injury."],[11,"Damages in place of an injunction for breach are often based on what?",["A fair fee for a release","The claimant's legal costs","The defendant's total profit"],"<b>Wrotham Park</b> damages: a reasonable sum to release the covenant, often a share of profit."]],"c":[[290,"How do you tell whether a covenant is restrictive or positive?","By <b>substance, not wording</b>. If compliance needs <b>money or action</b>, it is <b>positive</b>."],[294,"Does the original covenantor remain liable after selling?","<b>Yes</b>, by <b>LPA 1925 s.79</b>, unless the covenant limits liability to the period of ownership."],[297,"Does the burden of a positive covenant run with freehold land?","<b>Never</b>, not even in equity (Rhone v Stephens)."],[298,"What are the requirements for the burden of a covenant to run in equity?","<b>1. Restrictive.</b> <b>2.</b> Benefits the <b>covenantee's land</b>. <b>3.</b> Intended to run (<b>s.79</b>). <b>4.</b> <b>Protected</b> / notice (Tulk v Moxhay)."],[301,"How is a restrictive covenant protected in registered land?","By a <b>notice</b> in the charges register. Otherwise a buyer <b>for value</b> takes free."],[304,"How does a chain of indemnity covenants work?","The original covenantor stays liable, so on each sale the buyer covenants to <b>comply</b> and <b>indemnify</b> the seller. The chain <b>breaks</b> if any owner fails to take a new covenant or is insolvent."],[305,"What is the mutual benefit and burden principle?","A successor who <b>chooses to take a benefit</b> under the deed must accept a <b>linked burden</b>, such as paying towards a road they use (Halsall v Brizell)."],[313,"What are the three ways the benefit passes in equity?","<b>Annexation</b>, <b>express assignment</b>, or a <b>building scheme</b>. The covenant must also <b>touch and concern</b> the land."],[316,"What does LPA 1925 s.78 do for annexation?","<b>Automatically annexes</b> the benefit of a post-1925 covenant that touches and concerns the land, to <b>each and every part</b> of it (Federated Homes)."],[321,"What are the requirements for a building scheme?","A <b>common seller</b>. Estate laid out in <b>lots</b>. Restrictions meant to benefit <b>all</b> lots. Buyers bought on that basis. The <b>area</b> must be clearly defined."],[328,"What are the grounds for discharge or modification under s.84?","<b>(a) Obsolete.</b> <b>(aa) Impedes reasonable use</b> (no substantial benefit or contrary to public interest, and money is adequate). <b>(b) Agreement.</b> <b>(c) No injury.</b>"]]},{"id":"mortgages","title":"Mortgages","q":[[0,"How is a legal mortgage of registered land created?",["A charge by deed, then registered","Signed writing only","Oral agreement and deposit of deeds"],"It must be a <b>charge by deed</b> expressed to be by way of legal mortgage, and registered."],[1,"The mortgage gives the lender an option to buy the property. Is the option valid?",["No, it is a clog","Yes, if the price is fair","Yes, if the borrower signs separately"],"A <b>clog on the equity of redemption</b> is void. A genuinely separate later option may be valid."],[2,"When is a lender put on inquiry about undue influence?",["Surety and debtor are in a non-commercial relationship","The loan exceeds £100,000","The debtor is a company"],"<b>Etridge</b>: a non-commercial relationship the lender knows about, such as spouses or parent and child."],[3,"Which Etridge step protects the lender?",["Written confirmation from the surety's solicitor","A valuation of the home","A letter from the debtor"],"The lender should get <b>written confirmation</b> that the surety has received independent advice."],[4,"When does a legal mortgagee's right to possession arise?",["As soon as the mortgage is made","On the first missed payment","After 3 months' arrears"],"The lender has the rights of a long lessee (<b>s.87</b>), so possession is available even without default, unless the mortgage says otherwise."],[5,"What can the court do under AJA 1970 s.36 for a borrower's home?",["Suspend or postpone possession","Cancel the debt","Order a sale to a connected buyer"],"If the borrower can pay arrears within a <b>reasonable period</b>, the court may adjourn, suspend or postpone."],[6,"When does the power of sale arise under s.101 LPA 1925?",["When the mortgage money is due","When the borrower is 3 months in arrears","When the court allows it"],"It <b>arises</b> once the mortgage is by deed and the money is due. Exercisability needs a further trigger."],[7,"Which is a s.103 trigger making the power of sale exercisable?",["Notice to repay unpaid for 3 months","One missed payment","The borrower's 60th birthday"],"Also <b>interest 2 months in arrears</b> or breach of another term."],[8,"A lender sells before the power was exercisable. What does the buyer get?",["Good title","Nothing","A voidable title"],"<b>s.104</b>: the buyer need check only that the power has arisen. The borrower's remedy is damages against the lender."],[9,"What duty does a selling lender owe on the sale price?",["Reasonable care to get the true market value","To get the highest price ever offered","To wait for the market to improve"],"The lender must act in good faith and take reasonable care at the date of sale (<b>Cuckmere Brick</b>)."],[10,"After all lenders and costs are paid, who gets any balance?",["The borrower","The lender","The Land Registry"],"<b>s.105</b>: any surplus goes to later lenders first, then to the borrower."],[11,"How do registered charges rank against each other?",["Order of registration","Order of creation","Size of the loan"],"<b>LRA 2002 s.48</b>: priority follows the order entered on the register."]],"c":[[333,"How is a legal mortgage of registered land created?","By a <b>charge by deed expressed to be by way of legal mortgage</b>, which must be <b>registered</b>."],[342,"Is an option for the lender to buy the property, granted in the mortgage, valid?","<b>No.</b> It is <b>void</b>, because the borrower might never get the property back."],[350,"When is a lender put on inquiry?","Whenever the surety and debtor have a <b>non-commercial</b> relationship (spouses, cohabitants, parent and child, friends) and the lender knows it."],[351,"What steps must a lender take once put on inquiry (Etridge)?","<b>Communicate directly</b> with the surety. Give the solicitor the <b>financial information</b>. Get <b>written confirmation</b> from the surety's solicitor that they have been advised."],[359,"What can the court do under AJA 1970 s.36?","<b>Adjourn</b>, or <b>suspend or postpone</b> a possession order of a <b>dwelling-house</b>, if the borrower is <b>likely to pay sums due within a reasonable period</b>."],[360,"What are \"sums due\" under AJA 1973 s.8?","Only the <b>arrears</b> plus current instalments, even if an <b>acceleration clause</b> makes the whole debt due."],[368,"When does the power of sale arise (LPA 1925 s.101)?","When the mortgage is by <b>deed</b> and the <b>mortgage money is due</b>: the <b>legal redemption date has passed</b> or an instalment is overdue."],[369,"When does the power of sale become exercisable (s.103)?","<b>Notice</b> requiring repayment unpaid for <b>3 months</b>. <b>Interest 2 months</b> in arrears. Or <b>breach</b> of another term."],[372,"What are the lender's duties on sale?","Act in <b>good faith</b>, and take <b>reasonable care to obtain the true market value</b> on the date it sells."],[376,"In what order are sale proceeds applied (s.105)?","<b>1. Prior</b> mortgages. <b>2. Costs</b> of sale. <b>3.</b> The lender's <b>debt</b>. <b>4. Later</b> lenders. <b>5. Balance</b> to the borrower."]]},{"id":"leases-nature","title":"Leases: essential characteristics and creation","q":[[0,"What is the test for a lease under Street v Mountford?",["Exclusive possession for a certain term","A written agreement","Rent paid monthly"],"The label is irrelevant. The right to <b>exclude everyone</b>, including the landlord, is the key."],[1,"Is payment of rent essential to a lease?",["No","Yes","Yes, if over 3 years"],"A term of years can exist 'whether or not at a rent'."],[2,"Why is a lodger a licensee, not a tenant?",["The landlord provides services needing access","The lodger pays too little","The lodger lacks a written agreement"],"Attendance or services requiring the landlord's unrestricted access mean no <b>exclusive possession</b>."],[3,"A clause lets the landlord move strangers into a couple's flat. What does the court do?",["Ignores it as a pretence","Enforces it","Treats the couple as licensees"],"A <b>pretence clause</b> not intended to be used is disregarded (Antoniades v Villiers)."],[4,"A lease is granted 'until the war ends'. Is it valid as a fixed term?",["No, the term is uncertain","Yes, it lasts for the war","Yes, but only for 7 years"],"<b>Lace v Chantler</b>: the maximum duration must be known when the lease begins."],[5,"A 4-year lease is in signed writing but not a deed. What is it at best?",["An equitable lease","A legal lease","A tenancy at will"],"A valid s.2 contract can give an <b>equitable lease</b> (Walsh v Lonsdale) if specific performance is available."],[6,"An equitable lease in registered land is protected by...",["Notice or actual occupation","Sch 3 para 1","Nothing, it always binds"],"Only legal leases of 7 years or less override under <b>Sch 3 para 1</b>."],[7,"A lease for 12 years is granted by deed. When is it legal?",["On registration with its own title","On signing","On payment of the first rent"],"Leases of <b>more than 7 years</b> must be registered to take effect at law."],[8,"What is the maximum gap before a reversionary lease must take effect?",["21 years","7 years","3 months"],"<b>s.149(3) LPA 1925</b>: a lease taking effect more than 21 years after grant is void."],[9,"What describes a tenancy at will?",["Either party can end it at any time","It lasts for exactly one year","It needs a deed"],"It often arises while terms are being negotiated."],[10,"After the Renters' Rights Act 2025 reforms, can a private landlord use a section 21 notice?",["No, it was abolished","Yes, with 2 months' notice","Yes, after 6 months"],"Private residential lettings became <b>assured periodic tenancies</b>. The landlord needs a section 8 ground."],[11,"Under the Standard Conditions, the buyer moves in before completion. What is the buyer's status?",["A licensee","A tenant for a year","A tenant at will"],"The Standard Conditions say the buyer occupies as a <b>licensee</b>, not a tenant, pending completion."]],"c":[[387,"What is the test for a lease?","<b>Exclusive possession</b> for a <b>certain term</b>. The parties' <b>label</b> cannot change the legal effect (Street v Mountford)."],[392,"When is an occupier a lodger rather than a tenant?","Where the landlord provides <b>attendance or services</b> (cleaning, linen, meals) needing <b>unrestricted access</b>. A lodger is a <b>licensee</b>."],[395,"When can multiple occupiers be joint tenants?","Only if they have exclusive possession <b>together</b> and the <b>four unities</b> are present."],[398,"Is a family arrangement with exclusive possession a lease?","<b>No</b>, if there is <b>no intention to create legal relations</b>, such as family, friendship or charity."],[403,"What does certainty of term require?","The <b>maximum duration</b> must be <b>known at the start</b>. The lease may end earlier, such as under a break clause."],[409,"What formality does a lease of 3 years or less need?","<b>None</b>, if in possession, at the best rent, without a premium (s.54(2)). It <b>overrides</b> in registered land."],[410,"What formality does a lease of more than 3 and up to 7 years need?","A <b>deed</b>. If legal, it <b>overrides</b>, and it can also be protected by <b>notice</b>."],[411,"What formality does a lease of more than 7 years need?","A <b>deed</b> plus <b>registration</b> with its own title. Until registered it is <b>equitable</b>."],[416,"How is an equitable lease protected in registered land?","By <b>notice</b> or <b>actual occupation</b>. Never under Sch 3 para 1."]]},{"id":"leases-covenants","title":"Leasehold covenants: liability and alienation","q":[[0,"Which date decides whether the old or new leasehold covenant rules apply?",["The date the lease was granted","The date of assignment","The date of breach"],"Leases granted <b>before 1 January 1996</b> are old. From that date, the LTCA 1995 applies."],[1,"The original tenant of an old lease assigns it. Is the original tenant still liable?",["Yes, for the whole term","No, released at once","Only for 6 years"],"<b>Privity of contract</b> lasts for the whole term in old leases."],[2,"When is the original tenant of a new lease released?",["On a lawful assignment","On any assignment","After 6 years"],"<b>LTCA 1995 s.5</b>: release from future liability on lawful assignment. Not for an excluded assignment."],[3,"What does an authorised guarantee agreement (AGA) cover?",["The immediate assignee only","All later assignees","The landlord's liabilities"],"The outgoing tenant guarantees its <b>immediate assignee</b>, and the AGA ends when that assignee assigns lawfully."],[4,"Which assignment gives the outgoing tenant no release?",["One in breach of covenant","One made by deed","One to a company"],"An <b>excluded assignment</b> (in breach of covenant or by operation of law) gives no release until the next lawful one."],[5,"How does a landlord of a new lease seek release when selling the reversion?",["Serve notice within 4 weeks","Wait 12 months","Get the tenant's deed"],"If the tenant does not object within 4 weeks, or the court declares release reasonable, the landlord is released."],[6,"A landlord wants arrears from a former tenant. Within what time must a s.17 notice be served?",["6 months of the sum falling due","12 months","28 days"],"Without the notice, the former tenant is not liable for that <b>fixed charge</b>."],[7,"Can a head landlord sue a subtenant for rent?",["No","Yes","Only for service charge"],"There is no <b>privity</b> of contract or estate between them."],[8,"What does a qualified covenant against assignment imply under LTA 1927 s.19(1)(a)?",["Consent cannot be unreasonably withheld","Consent can be refused for any reason","No consent is needed"],"An <b>absolute</b> covenant needs no reasonableness test. A qualified one does."],[9,"What must a landlord do on a written application for consent under LTA 1988?",["Reply in writing within a reasonable time","Reply within 7 days","Reply only if consenting"],"The landlord must give consent unless reasonable not to, and bears the burden of proof. Breach gives <b>damages</b>."],[10,"Under LTA 1985 s.11, for which lettings must the landlord repair structure and exterior?",["Dwellings let for under 7 years","All residential leases","All commercial leases"],"The duty cannot be contracted out of and arises once the landlord has notice."],[11,"A landlord refuses consent to assign to get a surrender of the lease. Is that reasonable?",["No, it is a collateral purpose","Yes, it protects the landlord","Yes, if the rent is paid"],"Refusing for a purpose unconnected with the <b>lease relationship</b> is unreasonable."]],"c":[[427,"What decides which rules apply to leasehold covenants?","The <b>date of grant</b>. <b>Old</b> lease: before <b>1 January 1996</b> (common law). <b>New</b> lease: on or after it (<b>LTCA 1995</b>)."],[434,"What does LTA 1985 s.11 require?","For a <b>dwelling let for under 7 years</b>, the landlord must repair the <b>structure and exterior</b> and keep <b>installations</b> (water, gas, electricity, sanitation, heating) in repair and working order."],[438,"Is the original tenant of an old lease liable after assigning?","<b>Yes</b>, for the <b>whole term</b>, under privity of contract."],[439,"Which covenants bind an assignee of an old lease?","Covenants that <b>touch and concern</b> the land, such as rent, repair, user and alienation. Not personal promises."],[443,"Which covenants pass on assignment of a new lease (LTCA 1995 s.3)?","<b>All</b> landlord and tenant covenants, unless <b>expressed to be personal</b>."],[445,"What happens to the original tenant of a new lease on a lawful assignment?","It is <b>automatically released</b> from future liability (s.5). It stays liable for earlier breaches."],[449,"When is an AGA valid?","The lease needs <b>landlord's consent</b> to assign, consent is given on a <b>lawful condition</b> of an AGA, and it covers <b>only the immediate assignee</b>."],[455,"What notice must a landlord serve to recover arrears from a former tenant (s.17)?","A notice of the <b>fixed charge</b> within <b>6 months</b> of it falling due. Otherwise the former tenant is <b>not liable</b> for that sum."],[463,"What does LTA 1927 s.19(1)(a) imply into a qualified covenant?","Consent is <b>not to be unreasonably withheld</b>."],[465,"What duties does LTA 1988 s.1 impose on a landlord after a written application?","Within a <b>reasonable time</b>: <b>give consent</b> unless reasonable not to, and serve <b>written notice</b> of the decision, with any <b>conditions</b> or <b>reasons</b>."]]},{"id":"leases-remedies","title":"Remedies for breach and ending leases","q":[[0,"Which landlord remedy ends the lease?",["Forfeiture","CRAR","Suing for arrears"],"The other remedies keep the lease alive."],[1,"What is the limitation period for suing for rent arrears?",["6 years","12 years","3 years"],"Each instalment is time-barred 6 years after it falls due."],[2,"Where is CRAR available?",["Commercial premises only","Any lease","Residential lettings"],"<b>CRAR</b> needs a written lease and at least 7 days' net rent unpaid, plus 7 clear days' notice."],[3,"The landlord knows of a breach, then demands rent due afterwards. What has happened?",["Waiver of the right to forfeit","Forfeiture","Surrender"],"<b>Waiver</b> is an unequivocal act, with knowledge, treating the lease as continuing."],[4,"What does forfeiture need in the lease?",["An express right of re-entry","A break clause","A guarantor"],"Without a <b>forfeiture clause</b>, the right exists only if the lease is granted on condition."],[5,"The landlord changes the locks of a flat where the tenant lives. What is this?",["A criminal offence","Lawful peaceable re-entry","Lawful if rent is unpaid"],"<b>PEA 1977</b>: re-entry to a dwelling while anyone lawfully lives there needs a court order (s.2). Unlawful eviction is an offence (s.1)."],[6,"Is a s.146 notice needed to forfeit for non-payment of rent?",["No","Yes","Yes, for long leases"],"A <b>s.146 notice</b> is for other breaches. Rent needs only a formal demand, usually excluded by the lease."],[7,"How can a tenant obtain relief from forfeiture for rent arrears?",["Pay all arrears and costs","Pay half the arrears","Show the breach was minor"],"Relief is granted almost as of right, normally within <b>6 months</b> of the landlord recovering possession."],[8,"Under the 1938 Act, how long does a tenant have to serve a counter-notice?",["28 days","14 days","3 months"],"It applies to leases of 7 years or more with at least 3 years left. The landlord then needs the court's <b>leave</b>."],[9,"Which breach is typically irremediable?",["Use for illegal or immoral purposes","Late painting","Unpaid service charge"],"Breaches carrying <b>lasting stigma</b> cannot be put right by remedying."],[10,"What caps a landlord's damages for breach of a repairing covenant?",["The fall in value of the reversion","The cost of the repairs","Two years' rent"],"<b>LTA 1927 s.18(1)</b>: damages cannot exceed the reduction in the reversion's value."],[11,"How much notice at common law ends a yearly periodic tenancy?",["Half a year","One month","One year"],"Weekly, monthly or quarterly tenancies need at least <b>one full period</b>."]],"c":[[483,"When is CRAR available?","<b>Commercial premises only</b>, under a <b>written</b> lease, with at least <b>7 days' rent</b> unpaid."],[484,"What can CRAR recover?","Only <b>pure rent</b>, plus <b>VAT</b> and <b>interest</b>. Not service charges, insurance or rates, even if reserved as rent."],[489,"What is waiver of forfeiture?","An <b>unequivocal act</b>, with <b>knowledge</b> of the breach, recognising the lease as <b>continuing</b>, such as demanding or accepting rent due after the breach."],[492,"What is the effect of waiver on a once-and-for-all breach?","The right to forfeit for that breach is <b>lost permanently</b>. Example: assigning without consent."],[493,"What is the effect of waiver on a continuing breach?","It covers only the <b>past</b>. The landlord can forfeit later for the <b>continuing</b> breach, such as disrepair."],[498,"How does a tenant get relief from forfeiture for non-payment of rent?","By <b>paying all arrears and costs</b>. Relief is granted almost <b>as of right</b>."],[501,"What must a s.146 notice contain?","<b>Specify the breach.</b> Require it to be <b>remedied</b>, if capable of remedy. Require <b>compensation</b>, if wanted."],[506,"Which breaches are irremediable?","Those carrying lasting <b>stigma</b>, such as use for <b>illegal or immoral purposes</b>, unless the tenant did not know and stopped promptly. Unlawful <b>subletting</b> has been held irremediable."],[507,"When does the Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938 apply?","To forfeiture or damages for breach of a <b>repairing</b> covenant in a lease of <b>7 years or more</b> with <b>3 years or more</b> left."],[516,"What caps a landlord's damages for breach of a repairing covenant?","The fall in value of the <b>reversion</b> (LTA 1927 s.18(1)). Nothing if the landlord intends to <b>demolish</b>."]]}]},{"id":"property","title":"Property Law and Practice","stop":12,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M28,0 V-100 Q56,-112 86,-100 V0 Z\" fill=\"#8D99AE\"/><rect x=\"44\" y=\"-102\" width=\"22\" height=\"96\" fill=\"#4CC9F0\"/><path d=\"M50,-90 V-60 M58,-80 V-40 M52,-40 V-16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><ellipse cx=\"55\" cy=\"-4\" rx=\"32\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#4CC9F0\"/><rect x=\"-64\" y=\"-76\" width=\"12\" height=\"76\" fill=\"#6B4226\"/><circle cx=\"-74\" cy=\"-86\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><circle cx=\"-42\" cy=\"-84\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><circle cx=\"-58\" cy=\"-102\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><rect x=\"-16\" y=\"-100\" width=\"12\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#6B4226\"/><circle cx=\"-26\" cy=\"-110\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><circle cx=\"6\" cy=\"-108\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><circle cx=\"-10\" cy=\"-126\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#40916C\"/><path d=\"M-30,-104 q4,20 -2,40 M8,-108 q-4,24 2,44\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1B4332\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M-48,-56 H-24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6B4226\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M-30,-58 l6,20 l4,-16 Z\" fill=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"-34\" cy=\"-66\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-36\" cy=\"-77\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M-42,-78 l-5,3 l5,2 Z\" fill=\"#FFC300\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"-37\" cy=\"-78\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"none\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"transaction-overview","title":"The freehold transaction, the Protocol and ethics","q":[[0,"Which form lists the fittings and contents the seller is leaving?",["TA10","TA7","TA13"],"<b>TA10</b> is the Fittings and Contents Form. TA7 is for leasehold; TA13 is completion information."],[1,"A flat is being sold. Which extra form does the seller complete?",["TA7","TA13","LLC1"],"<b>TA7</b> is the Leasehold Information Form, completed alongside the TA6."],[2,"Which standard conditions are normally used for a house sale?",["Standard Conditions of Sale","Standard Commercial Property Conditions","Code for Completion by Post"],"The <b>SCS</b> (5th edn) are used for residential sales. The SCPC is for commercial property."],[3,"Who usually acts for the lender on a residential purchase?",["The buyer's solicitor","The seller's solicitor","A separate firm chosen by the buyer"],"In residential deals the <b>buyer's solicitor</b> usually acts for both buyer and lender."],[4,"In a commercial purchase, who usually acts for the lender?",["Its own separate solicitor","The buyer's solicitor","The seller's solicitor"],"Commercial lenders usually instruct <b>their own solicitor</b>, unlike residential lenders."],[5,"Which regulations require identity checks on a new conveyancing client?",["Money Laundering Regulations 2017","Land Registration Rules 2003","Consumer Rights Act 2015"],"Conveyancing is in the regulated sector, so <b>MLR 2017</b> customer due diligence applies."],[6,"What does 'source of funds' mean?",["Where this deal's money comes from","How the client built up wealth","Who owns the property now"],"<b>Source of funds</b> is the origin of the money for this transaction. Source of wealth is the wider picture."],[7,"A solicitor wants to buy the house her client is selling. What kind of conflict is this?",["An own-interest conflict","A client conflict","No conflict if the price is fair"],"Code para <b>6.1</b>: a conflict between the solicitor's own interest and the client's."],[8,"A solicitor gives an undertaking over the phone. Is it binding?",["Yes, oral undertakings bind","No, only written ones","Only if the other side confirms it"],"Undertakings can be <b>oral or written</b>. Breaking one is a conduct matter."],[9,"Who normally drafts the transfer deed in a sale?",["The buyer's solicitor","The seller's solicitor","HM Land Registry"],"The <b>buyer's solicitor</b> drafts the transfer and sends it to the seller's solicitor for approval."],[10,"Under Dreamvar, how is the buyer's money held by the seller's solicitors before genuine completion?",["On trust for the buyer","As the seller's property","As agent for the lender"],"Money is held <b>on trust</b> until there is a genuine completion, so a fraud can make the firm liable."],[11,"Contracts are exchanged. What has just happened?",["The sale has become legally binding","The buyer is the registered owner","The seller has handed over the keys"],"<b>Exchange</b> makes the contract binding. Ownership and the keys follow on completion."]],"c":[[1,"Is either party bound before exchange of contracts?","<b>No.</b> Before exchange the deal is <b>subject to contract</b>. Either party can withdraw <b>without liability</b>."],[9,"When can a solicitor act for two clients whose interests conflict (Code para 6.2)?","Only if an exception applies: a <b>substantially common interest</b>, or <b>competing for the same objective</b>. Plus all clients give <b>informed written consent</b>, <b>effective safeguards</b> are in place, and acting is <b>reasonable</b>."],[10,"Can a solicitor act for both the seller and the buyer if both consent in writing?","<b>Almost never.</b> Their interests conflict on price, contract and title. <b>Neither exception</b> applies, so consent cannot cure it. Different offices of the same firm make no difference: the rules apply to the <b>firm</b>."],[11,"Can a solicitor act for the buyer and the lender?","<b>Residential, standard mortgage: usually yes</b>, as interests are aligned. <b>Commercial or individually negotiated loan: usually no</b>; the lender instructs separate solicitors. Stop if a conflict arises."],[14,"The buyer refuses to let you tell the lender about a cashback. What must you do?","<b>Stop acting for the lender</b>, without telling it the confidential details. Consider whether you can act for the buyer. If the buyer wants to deceive the lender, this is likely <b>mortgage fraud</b>: stop acting for both and consider <b>money laundering</b> reporting."],[16,"What is an undertaking and when must it be performed?","A statement by a solicitor that they <b>will or will not do something</b>, which the recipient reasonably relies on. It need not be in writing. It must be performed <b>within the agreed time</b>, or a <b>reasonable time</b> (Code para 1.3)."],[17,"Who is bound by an undertaking, and what if the money turns out to be short?","The <b>solicitor personally</b>. If an unconditional undertaking to redeem a mortgage is given and funds are short, <b>the solicitor must make good</b> the shortfall. Any condition must be written in <b>when it is given</b>."],[20,"Which POCA offences matter to a conveyancer?","<b>Arranging</b> (s.328) unless an <b>authorised disclosure</b> is made and NCA consent obtained. <b>Failure to disclose</b> (s.330) suspicion to the <b>MLRO</b>. <b>Tipping off</b> (s.333A)."],[22,"Can a buyer's solicitor release completion money if the seller turns out to be a fraudster?","No. Completion money is held <b>on trust</b> and may be released only for a <b>genuine completion</b>. Paying it to an impostor is a <b>breach of trust</b> even without negligence (Dreamvar)."]]},{"id":"title","title":"Investigating and reporting on title","q":[[0,"Where would a right of way benefiting the property appear on the register?",["Property register","Proprietorship register","Charges register"],"The <b>property register</b> describes the land and the rights that benefit it."],[1,"Where are restrictive covenants burdening the land shown?",["Charges register","Property register","Proprietorship register"],"The <b>charges register</b> lists burdens: covenants, mortgages and notices."],[2,"Which is the best class of title?",["Absolute","Possessory","Qualified"],"<b>Absolute</b> title is the best: the register is guaranteed apart from limited exceptions."],[3,"A squatter applies for first registration of unregistered land with no deeds. Which class of title?",["Possessory","Absolute","Good leasehold"],"<b>Possessory</b> title is given where the applicant has no documentary title, such as a squatter on unregistered land."],[4,"What does a buyer's solicitor obtain to investigate registered title?",["Official copies of the register and plan","The seller's original deeds","A local search certificate"],"Registered title is proved by <b>official copies</b> of the register and title plan."],[5,"Which document is a good root of title for unregistered land?",["A conveyance on sale 20 years ago","A contract made 20 years ago","A conveyance on sale 10 years ago"],"A good root is a <b>conveyance on sale</b> at least 15 years old, dealing with the whole legal and equitable interest."],[6,"What is an epitome of title?",["A list of documents proving title","A summary of the searches","A copy of the register"],"In <b>unregistered land</b> the seller supplies an epitome of the documents that prove title."],[7,"A sole registered owner sells. Her partner has a share and lives there. Does paying her overreach it?",["No, two trustees are needed","Yes","Only if the partner consents later"],"<b>Overreaching</b> needs capital money paid to two trustees; otherwise the occupier's interest may bind."],[8,"Which schedule of the LRA 2002 lists overriding interests on a registered disposition?",["Schedule 3","Schedule 1","Schedule 12"],"<b>Schedule 3</b> covers overriding interests on a registered disposition; Schedule 1 is first registration."],[9,"A legal lease of 5 years was granted but not registered. Does it bind a buyer?",["Yes, it is an overriding interest","No, it is void","Only if the tenant lives there"],"Legal leases of <b>7 years or less</b> are overriding interests under Sch 3 para 1."],[10,"A freehold owner covenanted to repair a boundary wall. Does this bind a buyer of the freehold?",["No, positive burdens do not run","Yes, as it touches the land","Only if registered"],"The burden of a <b>positive covenant</b> does not run with freehold land (Austerberry; Rhone v Stephens)."],[11,"In unregistered land, which land charge protects a post-1925 restrictive covenant?",["Class D(ii)","Class C(iv)","Class F"],"A restrictive covenant is a <b>D(ii)</b> land charge. C(iv) is an estate contract and F is home rights."]],"c":[[27,"Why must all title issues be dealt with before exchange?","Under the standard conditions the buyer buys with <b>full knowledge</b> of the title shown before exchange. It <b>cannot raise requisitions</b> on that title afterwards."],[35,"What is good leasehold title and how should the buyer deal with it?","The <b>lease</b> is guaranteed, but the <b>landlord's right to grant</b> it was not checked. Ask for the <b>freehold title</b> to be deduced and upgraded, or <b>title indemnity insurance</b>. Check the <b>lender</b> will accept it."],[38,"Form A restriction and one co-owner has died: can the survivor sell alone?","<b>No.</b> The survivor must <b>appoint a second trustee</b> so the price is paid to <b>two trustees</b>, which <b>overreaches</b> the beneficial interests. Alternatively sell with the deceased's <b>PRs</b>."],[42,"How is a spouse's home right protected and dealt with on a sale?","By a <b>notice</b> (registered land) or <b>Class F land charge</b> (unregistered). It is <b>never an overriding interest</b>. The spouse must sign a <b>release</b> and cancellation by completion."],[43,"Which overriding interests matter most to a buyer of registered land?","<b>Legal leases of 7 years or less</b>. Interests of persons in <b>actual occupation</b> if obvious on inspection or known. <b>Legal easements</b> arising by implication or prescription."],[44,"How should a buyer deal with non-owner occupiers?","Every occupier aged <b>17 or over</b> signs a <b>release</b> and agrees to leave, or joins in the contract for <b>vacant possession</b>. Paying <b>two trustees</b> also overreaches trust interests."],[45,"What is a good root of title in unregistered land?","A document at least <b>15 years old</b> dealing with the <b>whole legal and equitable interest</b>, describing the land adequately and casting <b>no doubt</b> on title. Usually a <b>conveyance on sale</b> or <b>legal mortgage</b>."],[53,"How can a restrictive covenant that blocks the client's plans be dealt with?","Obtain a <b>release or consent</b> by deed. <b>Indemnity insurance</b>. An application under <b>LPA s.84</b> to the <b>Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)</b>. Otherwise advise against proceeding."],[55,"How is a positive covenant dealt with on a sale?","It does not run at law, so the seller stays liable. The buyer gives an <b>indemnity covenant</b> in the transfer, creating a <b>chain of indemnity</b>."],[58,"What must be checked on a sale by personal representatives?","The <b>grant of probate</b> or <b>letters of administration</b>. <b>All proving PRs</b> must join in the transfer of land (AEA 1925 s.2(2)). In unregistered land, check for a <b>memorandum of a previous assent</b> on the grant."]]},{"id":"searches","title":"Pre-contract searches and enquiries","q":[[0,"Which two forms make up the standard local authority search?",["LLC1 and CON29","TA6 and TA10","OS1 and OS2"],"The <b>LLC1</b> (land charges) and <b>CON29</b> (enquiries) make up the local search."],[1,"Which search shows whether the road outside is maintained at public expense?",["CON29","CON29DW","LLC1"],"The <b>CON29</b> covers highways, planning history and other council matters."],[2,"Who holds the records on drainage and water supply to the property?",["The water company","The local council","HM Land Registry"],"The <b>water company</b> answers the CON29DW drainage and water search."],[3,"Which search checks contaminated land risk?",["Environmental search","Drainage search","Bankruptcy search"],"An <b>environmental search</b> checks contamination and similar risks."],[4,"The house is in a former mining area. Which search is useful?",["Coal Authority mining search","Chancel check","K15 search"],"A <b>mining search</b> shows past and present coal workings near the property."],[5,"The seller says nothing about a leaking roof. Is the seller liable under caveat emptor?",["No, but must not answer falsely","Yes, always","Yes, if the repair costs over £10,000"],"<b>Caveat emptor</b>: no duty to volunteer physical defects, but false replies give a claim."],[6,"The seller wrongly says there are no boundary disputes. What is the buyer's main remedy?",["Misrepresentation","None, caveat emptor applies","Specific performance only"],"A false reply to an enquiry can be a <b>misrepresentation</b>, giving damages or rescission."],[7,"A seller's solicitor knows a reply to an enquiry is false. May they pass it on?",["No","Yes, they are only the agent","Yes, if the client insists"],"A solicitor must not <b>mislead</b> the other side, even on the client's instructions."],[8,"Why should the buyer inspect the property before exchange?",["To find occupiers whose rights may bind","To check the SDLT","To register the transfer"],"Occupiers can have <b>overriding interests</b>, so inspection can reveal them."],[9,"The seller is a company. Which extra search is made?",["Company search at Companies House","Land charges search","Local land charges search"],"A <b>company search</b> shows charges and whether it is in insolvency or struck off."],[10,"How does a buyer confirm the seller is the registered owner?",["Official copies of the register","A CON29 search","An LLC1 search"],"<b>Official copies</b> show the registered proprietor and the entries on the title."],[11,"Which risk might a chancel check or insurance address on a rural house?",["Chancel repair liability","Rent review","Stamp duty"],"<b>Chancel repair liability</b> can still bind some land, so a check or indemnity insurance is common."]],"c":[[62,"What does caveat emptor mean for a buyer of land?","The seller need not disclose <b>physical defects</b> or problems with the area. The buyer must find out for itself through <b>searches, enquiries and a survey</b>, before exchange."],[63,"What must the seller disclose despite caveat emptor?","<b>Latent defects in title</b>, such as an unusual covenant. The seller must also <b>not mislead</b>: an untrue answer can found a <b>misrepresentation</b> claim."],[66,"What does the LLC1 search reveal?","<b>Local land charges</b>: planning permissions with conditions, <b>s.106 agreements</b>, <b>TPOs</b>, <b>listed building</b> and <b>conservation area</b> status, smoke control orders and council financial charges."],[67,"Does an unregistered local land charge bind the buyer?","<b>Yes.</b> But a buyer who searched and suffers loss can claim <b>compensation</b> (LLCA 1975 s.10)."],[68,"What does CON29 Part I cover?","Planning and <b>building regulation</b> decisions. Whether roads are <b>adopted</b>. Proposed road or rail schemes. <b>Enforcement</b> and stop notices. <b>Contaminated land</b>. <b>Compulsory purchase</b>. <b>Radon</b>."],[74,"Why is an environmental search important?","Under <b>EPA 1990 Part 2A</b>, if the polluter cannot be found the <b>current owner or occupier</b> can be made to pay for <b>remediation</b>. Flood risk affects insurance and value."],[75,"Which additional search do these clues point to: mining village; unregistered land; next to a village green; near the parish church?","Mining village: <b>CON29M</b> coal search. Unregistered land: <b>SIM</b>. Village green: <b>CON29O commons</b> enquiry. Parish church: <b>chancel repair</b> search."],[76,"Is chancel repair liability an overriding interest?","<b>No</b>, not since <b>13 October 2013</b>. It binds a buyer of registered land only if <b>protected by a notice</b>, or if there has been no sale for value since. Practical answer: <b>chancel search</b> or <b>indemnity insurance</b>."],[79,"What is the legal effect of the seller's replies to enquiries?","They are <b>representations</b>. An untrue reply that induced the contract allows <b>rescission and/or damages</b> for misrepresentation. A solicitor who knowingly or recklessly gives a false reply breaches <b>Code para 1.4</b>."]]},{"id":"finance-lender","title":"Finance and acting for a lender","q":[[0,"A buyer's solicitor acts for buyer and lender. Whose interests must be protected?",["Both clients","The buyer only","The lender only"],"The solicitor owes duties to <b>both</b>, and must stop if a conflict arises."],[1,"Which document does the buyer's solicitor send the lender to release the loan?",["Certificate of title","TA6","Completion statement"],"The <b>certificate of title</b> tells the lender the security is good and the Handbook is followed."],[2,"The buyer's mortgage bans letting, but he plans to let. What must the solicitor do?",["Tell the lender, or stop acting for it","Keep it confidential and proceed","Tell the seller"],"The solicitor must <b>disclose</b> to the lender, or cease acting if the client refuses."],[3,"A legal charge over registered land takes effect as legal when what happens?",["It is registered at HM Land Registry","It is signed","The loan is paid out"],"A legal charge over registered land must be <b>registered</b> (LRA 2002 s.27)."],[4,"Which Land Registry form is a standard legal charge?",["CH1","TR1","DS1"],"<b>CH1</b> is the form for a legal charge. TR1 transfers; DS1 discharges."],[5,"Which form discharges a registered charge?",["DS1","AP1","CH1"],"<b>DS1</b> (or an electronic discharge) cancels the lender's charge on the register."],[6,"How does the seller's solicitor reassure the buyer about the seller's mortgage?",["Undertaking to redeem on completion","TA6 replies","Official copies"],"The seller's solicitor <b>undertakes</b> to redeem the mortgage and send evidence of discharge."],[7,"A mortgage offer includes a retention. What is that?",["Part of the loan held back","An extra arrangement fee","A deposit paid to the seller"],"A <b>retention</b> is held by the lender until the stated condition (e.g. repairs) is met."],[8,"A company buys with a loan. Besides HM Land Registry, where must the charge be registered?",["Companies House","Land Charges Department","Local council"],"A company's charge must also be registered at <b>Companies House</b> (CA 2006 s.859A)."],[9,"Under Abbey National v Cann, who has priority: the lender or an occupier whose interest arose on completion?",["The lender","The occupier","Whoever registered first"],"The purchase-money mortgage takes priority, as the buyer's interest and the charge arise <b>together</b>."],[10,"Whose protection is the lender's mortgage valuation mainly for?",["The lender's","The buyer's","The seller's"],"The <b>valuation</b> checks the security for the lender; the buyer should get their own survey."],[11,"May one firm act for buyer and lender on a standard residential mortgage?",["Yes, usually","Never","Only with the seller's consent"],"Acting for <b>both</b> is common where the lender uses standard terms, unless a conflict arises."]],"c":[[85,"When must the buyer have all purchase funds in place?","<b>Before exchange</b>, because exchange commits the buyer to pay."],[86,"What does a lender require where part of the deposit is a gift?","A <b>gifted deposit letter</b> confirming it is a gift, the giver has <b>no interest</b> in the property, and will sign an <b>occupier's consent</b> if living there. Check the giver's <b>source of funds</b>."],[91,"Can a solicitor advise a client which mortgage to take?","<b>No</b>, unless <b>FCA-authorised</b>. Explaining an offer the client already has is fine. Recommending a product is not: refer to an <b>independent mortgage adviser</b>."],[93,"Can the buyer exchange before receiving a satisfactory mortgage offer?","<b>Never.</b> If the loan falls through the buyer is still bound and could lose the <b>10% deposit</b> and be sued for further loss."],[97,"Which matters must the buyer's solicitor report to the lender under the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook?","A <b>price different</b> from the one in the mortgage instructions. <b>Incentives or cashback</b>. The seller has owned the property for <b>under 6 months</b>. Any part of the price paid <b>direct to the seller</b>."],[98,"Who must sign an occupier's consent for the lender?","Anyone aged <b>17 or over</b> who will live there but is <b>not a borrower</b>."],[99,"What pre-completion searches does the lender need?","An <b>OS1/OS2</b> priority search in the <b>lender's name</b>. A <b>bankruptcy search (K16)</b> against each borrower."],[101,"What does the certificate of title do?","Certifies to the lender that title is <b>good and marketable</b> and its requirements are met (or lists exceptions), and <b>requests the advance</b>. The solicitor is <b>liable</b> if it is wrong."]]},{"id":"contract","title":"Drafting the contract: standard conditions","q":[[0,"Under the SCS, how much deposit is paid on exchange?",["10% of the price","5%","20%"],"<b>SC 2.2</b>: the deposit is 10% of the price, paid by electronic transfer."],[1,"A buyer paid a 5% deposit. A notice to complete is served. What must the buyer do?",["Pay the balance up to 10%","Nothing more","Pay a further 20%"],"<b>SC 6.8.3</b>: on a notice to complete the buyer must pay up to the full 10% deposit."],[2,"Under the SCS, money arrives at 3pm on completion day. When is it treated as received?",["Next working day","The same day","The day before"],"Money received after <b>2pm</b> counts as received on the next working day (SC 6.1.2)."],[3,"Under the SCS, by when must the buyer send the draft transfer?",["12 working days before completion","5 working days before","20 working days before"],"<b>SC 4.3.2</b>: draft at least 12 working days before completion; engrossment 5."],[4,"Under the SCS, how long does the seller have to approve the draft transfer?",["4 working days","10 working days","2 working days"],"<b>SC 4.3.2</b>: the seller approves or revises within 4 working days."],[5,"What must a contract for the sale of land be?",["In writing and signed by both parties","Made by deed","Registered at HM Land Registry"],"<b>LP(MP)A 1989 s.2</b>: in writing, with all terms, signed by or for each party."],[6,"Which seller would usually give limited title guarantee?",["Personal representatives","A homeowner selling her house","A developer selling a new house"],"<b>Limited</b> guarantee is for sellers with little knowledge of the title, such as PRs or trustees."],[7,"What is a 'specified incumbrance' in a sale contract?",["A burden the buyer agrees to take subject to","A mortgage to be paid off","A defect in the seller's title"],"Specified burdens, such as covenants, are listed. <b>Mortgages</b> are not: they are redeemed."],[8,"Which Act implies the covenants for title in 'full title guarantee'?",["LP(MP)A 1994","LPA 1925","LRA 2002"],"<b>LP(MP)A 1994</b> implies the covenants for title when the guarantee words are used."],[9,"Under SC 2.2.5, how may a residential seller use the deposit?",["Towards the deposit on its own purchase","To repay its mortgage","It may not use it at all"],"In a residential chain the deposit may be passed up on <b>similar terms</b>."],[10,"Which capacity for holding the deposit protects the buyer best?",["Stakeholder","Agent for the seller","Agent for the buyer"],"A <b>stakeholder</b> holds it for both parties, so an insolvent seller cannot spend it."],[11,"A contract is made 'subject to finance' with no further detail. What is the risk?",["It may be void for uncertainty","It is always valid","Only the seller can withdraw"],"Vague conditions may be <b>void for uncertainty</b>. Good ones are precise and have a long-stop date."]],"c":[[110,"What are specified incumbrances, and is the seller's mortgage one?","Burdens the buyer agrees to take subject to, such as covenants in the charges register. <b>Mortgages are not specified</b>: they will be <b>paid off</b> on completion."],[113,"What deposit do the SCS require and how is it held?","<b>10%</b>, paid by the date of the contract by electronic transfer from a conveyancer's account. Held as <b>stakeholder</b> (SC 2.2)."],[114,"When can a residential seller use the buyer's deposit under SC 2.2.5?","Towards the deposit on a related <b>residential purchase in England and Wales</b>, held on similar terms up the chain."],[115,"What incumbrances does the property remain subject to under SC 3.1?","Those <b>specified</b> in the contract. Those <b>discoverable by inspection</b> before the contract. Those the seller <b>could not reasonably know</b> about. <b>Public requirements</b>."],[118,"Who bears the risk between exchange and completion under the current SCS and SCPC?","The <b>buyer</b>, from the date of the contract (SC 5.1.1; SCPC 8.1). It must complete at the <b>full price</b> even if the property is damaged."],[119,"Must the seller insure between exchange and completion?","<b>No</b>, unless the contract requires it or a <b>lease</b> obliges the seller to insure (SC 5.1.2). So the <b>buyer should insure from exchange</b>."],[122,"What is the completion date if none is agreed, and what if money arrives late in the day?","<b>20 working days</b> after the contract. Money received after <b>2pm</b> is treated as received the <b>next working day</b> (SC 6.1)."],[125,"What does a notice to complete do (SC 6.8)?","A party <b>ready, able and willing</b> serves it after the completion date. Completion must take place within <b>10 working days</b>, and <b>time becomes of the essence</b>."],[126,"When can a buyer rescind for misdescription under SC 7.1?","Only for <b>fraud or recklessness</b>, or where the property <b>differs substantially</b> from what the contract suggests. Otherwise damages only."],[133,"What must a buyer who paid a reduced deposit do on receiving a notice to complete?","Under <b>SC 6.8.3</b> the buyer must <b>forthwith pay a further deposit</b> equal to the <b>balance of 10%</b>, so the full <b>10%</b> is at stake if it fails to complete."],[134,"What covenants does full title guarantee imply?","The seller has the <b>right to dispose</b> and will do all it reasonably can to give the title claimed. The property is <b>free from all incumbrances</b> except those the seller does not and could not reasonably know about."],[136,"Under the SCPC, is the price inclusive or exclusive of VAT?","<b>Exclusive</b>. If the sale is a taxable supply the buyer pays VAT <b>on top</b> on completion."]]},{"id":"exchange","title":"Exchange of contracts","q":[[0,"Each solicitor holds its own client's signed part. Which formula applies?",["Formula B","Formula A","Formula C"],"<b>Formula B</b>: each solicitor holds their own client's part and agrees to exchange by phone."],[1,"The buyer posts his signed part but the seller never sends his. Is there a contract?",["No, not until exchange","Yes, once posted","Yes, once terms are agreed"],"<b>Eccles v Bryant</b>: where exchange is intended, there is no contract until it happens."],[2,"Is exchange of contracts by telephone binding?",["Yes","No","Only if confirmed in writing that day"],"<b>Domb v Isoz</b>: a solicitor with authority can exchange by any recognised method."],[3,"Which exchange method is normal in a chain?",["Telephone using a formula","By post","In person"],"<b>Telephone</b> exchange with a Law Society formula gives certainty on timing."],[4,"What must each solicitor record after a telephone exchange?",["An attendance note","A fresh contract","A TA13 form"],"The <b>attendance note</b> records time, formula, completion date and deposit details."],[5,"After exchange, who owns the property in equity?",["The buyer","The seller","Both equally"],"<b>Lysaght v Edwards</b>: the buyer becomes the equitable owner; the seller holds the legal title."],[6,"The seller dies after exchange. What is the effect on the contract?",["It still binds the personal representatives","It is void","The buyer may rescind"],"Death does <b>not</b> end the contract: the PRs must complete."],[7,"A seller is made bankrupt after exchange. What may the trustee do?",["Complete it, or disclaim it","Only complete it","Nothing, the contract is void"],"The trustee may <b>complete</b> or <b>disclaim</b> the contract as onerous property."],[8,"What must the buyer's solicitor hold before exchange?",["The deposit as cleared funds","The seller's keys","A completion statement"],"The solicitor undertakes to send the deposit, so it must be in <b>client account</b> first."],[9,"What must a solicitor acting in a chain make sure of?",["Never bound to buy without selling","Exchange the purchase first","Use different completion dates"],"<b>Synchronisation</b>: exchange on both together, with matching completion dates."],[10,"What does Part I of Formula C commit a solicitor to?",["Exchange if called by an agreed deadline","Send the deposit","Complete that day"],"Part I is an <b>undertaking</b> to exchange if the other calls within the deadline."],[11,"There are two joint buyers. Whose authority is needed to exchange?",["Each of them","Either one","The lender's only"],"With joint clients the solicitor needs <b>authority from all</b> of them."]],"c":[[144,"What must the buyer's solicitor check before exchange?","Title resolved. Searches, enquiries and <b>survey</b> done. <b>Mortgage offer</b> received. <b>Deposit in client account as cleared funds</b>. Report given and contract <b>signed</b>. <b>Authority</b> to exchange. <b>Insurance</b> from exchange. Related transaction ready."],[145,"Why must the deposit be in client account as cleared funds before exchange?","The solicitor <b>undertakes</b> to send it on exchange, and client money <b>cannot be paid out before it is received</b> (SRA Accounts Rules)."],[146,"In a chain, which exchange should the client's solicitor make first?","Exchange on the <b>sale first</b>, then the <b>purchase immediately after</b>, with matching completion dates. Or use <b>Formula C</b>. The client must never be bound to buy without being bound to sell."],[147,"When is a contract made on exchange by post or DX under the SCS?","When the <b>last copy is posted</b> or deposited at the document exchange."],[149,"When is Formula A used?","When <b>one solicitor</b> (usually the seller's) already <b>holds both</b> signed parts. It undertakes to send the seller's part to the buyer's solicitor that day."],[150,"When is Formula B used?","When <b>each solicitor holds its own</b> client's signed part. Each undertakes to send its part that day, and the buyer's solicitor undertakes to <b>send the deposit</b>."],[151,"How does Formula C work?","For <b>chains</b>. <b>Part I</b>: each solicitor undertakes to exchange if the other calls to <b>release</b> by an agreed <b>deadline</b> that day. <b>Part II</b>: the call back, exchange, and sending of parts and deposit, which may go up the chain."],[153,"What is the consequence of failing to send the contract or deposit as undertaken on exchange?","Breach of a <b>professional undertaking</b> (Code para 1.3), whatever the client later instructs."],[156,"What interest does the buyer acquire on exchange?","An <b>equitable interest</b>. The seller holds the legal title as a <b>qualified trustee</b> until completion and must take reasonable care of the property."]]},{"id":"pre-completion","title":"Pre-completion steps","q":[[0,"Which form transfers part of a registered title?",["TP1","TR1","AP1"],"<b>TP1</b> transfers part, with a plan; TR1 transfers the whole."],[1,"Which official search is made for part of a registered title?",["OS2","OS1","K16"],"<b>OS2</b> is for part; <b>OS1</b> is for the whole title."],[2,"What does an OS1 give the buyer?",["Priority over later entries","Proof SDLT is paid","Discharge of the mortgage"],"The <b>priority period</b> protects the buyer's registration against entries made meanwhile."],[3,"How is a deed validly signed by an individual?",["Signed and witnessed by someone present","Signed in front of a video witness","Signed alone, with no witness"],"The witness must be <b>physically present</b> and attest: video witnessing is not accepted for registration."],[4,"Which signing is enough for a company's deed?",["One director, with a witness","The company secretary alone","A solicitor alone"],"CA 2006: two authorised signatories, or <b>one director</b> signing in front of a witness."],[5,"The seller is a company. Which extra pre-completion search is made?",["Company search","K16 search","CON29DW"],"A <b>company search</b> checks it is not wound up or in administration."],[6,"Which form is used for completion information and undertakings?",["TA13","TA6","TR1"],"<b>TA13</b> covers completion arrangements and the seller's undertakings."],[7,"Two buyers want 70:30 shares. What should the TR1 declare?",["They are tenants in common","They are joint tenants","The seller's shares"],"A <b>tenancy in common</b> declaration leads to a Form A restriction on the register."],[8,"The seller prepaid a service charge covering days after completion. What happens?",["Buyer pays the seller extra","Buyer gets a credit","No adjustment"],"<b>Apportionment</b>: the buyer reimburses the seller for the period after completion."],[9,"Rent was paid to the seller in advance for days after completion. What happens?",["Buyer gets a credit","Buyer pays extra","No adjustment"],"Income for the buyer's period is <b>credited</b> to the buyer."],[10,"Who prepares the completion statement?",["The seller's solicitor","The buyer's solicitor","The lender"],"The seller's solicitor shows the <b>exact sum</b> the buyer must pay."],[11,"What does the seller's solicitor obtain before undertaking to redeem the mortgage?",["A redemption statement","The buyer's consent","An OS1 result"],"The <b>redemption statement</b> gives the exact payoff figure, to check the proceeds cover it."]],"c":[[162,"Which transfer form is used for the whole of a registered title, and which for part?","<b>TR1</b> for the whole (also to assign a registered lease). <b>TP1</b> for <b>part</b>, with a plan, rights granted and reserved, and usually new covenants."],[164,"What is the timetable for the transfer under SC 4.3.2 and SCPC 7.3.2?","Draft at least <b>12 working days</b> before completion. Seller approves within <b>4 working days</b>. Engrossment at least <b>5 working days</b> before completion."],[166,"Joint buyers want shares reflecting unequal contributions. What should the TR1 say?","That they hold as <b>tenants in common</b> in the stated shares. HM Land Registry will enter a <b>Form A restriction</b>."],[168,"How does an individual validly execute a deed?","Signs <b>in the presence of a witness</b> who <b>attests</b>, and the deed is <b>delivered</b>. The witness must be <b>physically present</b> and not a party. Video witnessing is not accepted."],[170,"What must be checked where an attorney signs a transfer?","The power is valid, covers the sale and is <b>not revoked</b>. If over <b>12 months</b> old at the transfer, HM Land Registry needs evidence of <b>non-revocation</b>."],[173,"What does an OS1 search do and what protection does it give?","Shows <b>new entries</b> since the official copies' search-from date. Gives a <b>priority period of 30 working days</b> for the application to register."],[174,"What protection does a K15 search give in unregistered land?","If the buyer completes within <b>15 working days</b> of the search, it takes free of entries made after it (LCA 1972 s.11(5))."],[175,"Against whom is a K16 bankruptcy search made, and why?","Against the <b>buyer/borrower</b>. It is required by the <b>lender</b>. It is not a priority search."],[183,"Who prepares the completion statement and what does it show?","The <b>seller's solicitor</b>. Price <b>less deposit</b>, <b>plus contents</b> price, plus or minus <b>apportionments</b> (and any late completion compensation)."]]},{"id":"completion","title":"Completion and post-completion","q":[[0,"Under the Code for Completion by Post, how does the money travel?",["By CHAPS to the seller's solicitor","By cheque","By hand"],"The buyer's solicitor sends the money by <b>same-day transfer</b>, and the seller's solicitor completes."],[1,"When does legal title pass to the buyer of registered land?",["On registration","On exchange","On completion"],"<b>LRA 2002 s.27</b>: the transfer takes effect at law only when registered."],[2,"Between completion and registration, how does the seller hold the legal title?",["On bare trust for the buyer","On trust for the lender","As the owner"],"The seller is a <b>bare trustee</b> and the buyer owns in equity."],[3,"Which form registers a transfer of registered land?",["AP1","FR1","DS1"],"<b>AP1</b> applies to change the register; FR1 is for first registration."],[4,"Which form applies for first registration?",["FR1","AP1","TR1"],"<b>FR1</b> is used for first registration, within 2 months of completion."],[5,"Where is the SDLT return delivered?",["HMRC","HM Land Registry","Companies House"],"<b>SDLT</b> goes to HMRC. Land Registry wants evidence it has been done."],[6,"Besides HM Land Registry, who must be told of a company buyer's mortgage?",["Companies House","The Land Charges Department","The local council"],"A company's charge must be <b>registered at Companies House</b> or it is void against a liquidator."],[7,"Which form (or electronic version) shows a registered charge is discharged?",["DS1","AP1","CH1"],"<b>DS1</b> or the electronic <b>ED</b> discharge cancels the seller's lender's charge."],[8,"How is an unregistered mortgage shown to be discharged?",["Receipt endorsed on the mortgage deed","DS1","AP1"],"For unregistered land the lender endorses a <b>receipt</b> on the mortgage deed."],[9,"In unregistered land, when does the legal estate pass to the buyer?",["On completion","On first registration","On exchange"],"It passes on completion, but the buyer must then apply for <b>first registration</b>."],[10,"The buyer later finds a title defect the seller knew about. Which claim is open?",["Covenants for title","Caveat emptor","Completion date clause"],"The <b>covenants for title</b> implied by the guarantee give a post-completion claim."],[11,"Normally, when is the effective date for SDLT?",["Completion, or earlier substantial performance","Exchange","Registration"],"The <b>effective date</b> is usually completion, unless the buyer takes substantial performance earlier."]],"c":[[186,"How is completion normally carried out today?","<b>Remotely</b>, under the <b>Law Society Code for Completion by Post</b>. Money is sent by <b>CHAPS</b> and documents follow."],[192,"When does legal title pass on a sale of registered land?","Only on <b>registration</b> (LRA 2002 s.27). In the <b>registration gap</b> the seller holds on <b>bare trust</b> for the buyer, protected by the OS1 priority period."],[193,"When does legal title pass in unregistered land, and what if first registration is not made?","On <b>completion</b>. If the buyer does not apply within <b>2 months</b>, the transfer is <b>void</b> as to the legal estate, which reverts to the seller on <b>bare trust</b> (s.7)."],[194,"Does the contract merge with the transfer on completion?","At common law <b>yes</b>. But SC 7.3 and SCPC 10.4 keep <b>outstanding obligations</b> alive, such as late completion compensation."],[196,"When is the undertaking to redeem the seller's mortgage discharged?","Only when <b>evidence of discharge</b> is provided."],[197,"What is the deadline for the SDLT return and payment?","<b>14 days</b> after the <b>effective date</b> (normally completion, or earlier <b>substantial performance</b>)."],[198,"What is the deadline for the LTT return and payment in Wales?","<b>30 days</b> after the effective date, to the <b>Welsh Revenue Authority</b>."],[199,"Which form registers a transfer of registered land, and when must it reach HM Land Registry?","<b>AP1</b>, within the OS1 <b>priority period of 30 working days</b>."],[202,"What happens if a company buyer's charge is not registered at Companies House within 21 days?","It is <b>void</b> against a <b>liquidator, administrator and creditors</b>, and the money becomes immediately repayable. Late registration needs a <b>court order</b>."]]},{"id":"delayed-completion","title":"Delayed completion and remedies","q":[[0,"Is time of the essence for the completion date under the SCS?",["No, unless a notice to complete is served","Yes","Only on commercial sales"],"Time is <b>not of the essence</b> until a notice to complete makes it so."],[1,"The seller completes late because its own purchase was delayed. Is it in breach?",["Yes","No, it was excused","Only if the buyer warned it"],"<b>Raineri v Miles</b>: late completion is a breach even when caused by a linked transaction."],[2,"SCS. Price £200,000, deposit £20,000. The buyer is late. What is the compensation base?",["£180,000","£200,000","£20,000"],"Where the <b>buyer</b> pays, the deposit is deducted from the price."],[3,"SCS. The seller is late. On what is compensation calculated?",["The full price","Price less deposit","The deposit"],"Where the <b>seller</b> pays, there is no deduction for the deposit."],[4,"Under the SCS, both parties are late. Who pays compensation?",["The party in default longer, for the excess","Each pays the other","The buyer only"],"<b>Relative fault</b>: the longer default pays for the difference."],[5,"A notice to complete is served. When must the parties complete?",["Within 10 working days, excluding service day","Within 10 calendar days","The next working day"],"The period is <b>10 working days</b>, and time is then of the essence."],[6,"A seller who cannot give vacant possession serves a notice to complete. Is it valid?",["No","Yes","Only if the buyer agrees"],"The server must itself be <b>ready, able and willing</b> to complete."],[7,"The buyer ignores a notice to complete. Which option does the seller have?",["Rescind, keep the deposit and resell","Only claim interest","Rescind and return the deposit"],"The seller may <b>forfeit the deposit</b>, resell, and claim any further loss."],[8,"The seller ignores a notice to complete. What may the buyer do?",["Rescind and recover the deposit with interest","Wait only","Claim a 10% penalty"],"The buyer may <b>rescind</b>, recover the deposit with interest, and claim damages."],[9,"Which court order compels a party to complete a land sale?",["Specific performance","Rectification","Declaration"],"<b>Specific performance</b> is available because each piece of land is unique."],[10,"Under SC 7.1, when may the buyer rescind for an error in the contract?",["If fraud, recklessness or a substantial difference","For any error","Whenever the value differs"],"Damages are the usual remedy; <b>rescission</b> is for serious cases only."],[11,"After exchange the seller sells to someone else. What is this?",["A repudiatory breach","Frustration","A misrepresentation"],"The buyer may accept the <b>repudiation</b>, terminate and claim damages."]],"c":[[205,"Is late completion a breach if time is not of the essence?","<b>Yes.</b> The innocent party can recover <b>damages</b> for the delay, though it cannot yet terminate (Raineri v Miles)."],[206,"Who pays contractual compensation for late completion under the SCS?","The party whose <b>total period of default is greater</b>, for the period by which it <b>exceeds</b> the other's (SC 7.2.1)."],[207,"How is SCS compensation calculated?","Interest at the <b>contract rate</b> on the <b>purchase price</b>, or the price <b>less the deposit</b> where the <b>buyer</b> pays (SC 7.2.2)."],[210,"Who pays contractual compensation under the SCPC?","<b>Only the buyer</b>, on the price <b>less deposit</b> (SCPC 10.3). A late seller pays only <b>common law damages</b>."],[211,"Buyer 6 days in default, seller 2 days, under the SCS. Who pays and for how long?","The <b>buyer</b> pays compensation for <b>4 days</b>."],[215,"What is the effect of a notice to complete?","Completion must take place within <b>10 working days</b>, <b>excluding the day of service</b>, and <b>time is of the essence</b>."],[217,"What can the seller do if the buyer fails to comply with a notice to complete?","<b>Rescind</b>, <b>forfeit and keep the deposit</b> with interest, <b>resell</b> and claim <b>damages</b>, giving credit for the deposit. Or affirm and seek <b>specific performance</b>."],[218,"What can the buyer do if the seller fails to comply with a notice to complete?","<b>Rescind</b>, recover the <b>deposit with interest</b>, and claim <b>damages</b>. Or seek <b>specific performance</b>."],[219,"Can the court order return of a forfeited deposit?","<b>Yes</b>, under <b>LPA 1925 s.49(2)</b> if it thinks fit. Used <b>sparingly</b>, where keeping it would be unconscionable."]]},{"id":"lease-contents","title":"Commercial leases: key clauses and the Code","q":[[0,"Which clause lets a landlord re-enter and end the lease for breach?",["Forfeiture clause","Rent review clause","Parcels clause"],"A <b>forfeiture</b> (re-entry) clause is needed: without it the landlord cannot forfeit."],[1,"Which clause describes exactly what is let?",["Parcels clause","Reddendum","Provisos"],"The <b>parcels clause</b> and plan define the premises."],[2,"'Not to alter without consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld.' What is this?",["A fully qualified covenant","An absolute covenant","A qualified covenant"],"<b>Fully qualified</b>: the reasonableness wording is written into the covenant itself."],[3,"An absolute covenant bars alterations. May the landlord refuse for any reason?",["Yes","No, only unreasonable ones","Only if damage results"],"An <b>absolute</b> covenant leaves the landlord free to refuse or to waive."],[4,"What does 'upwards-only' mean in a rent review?",["Rent can rise but never fall","Rent can fall but not rise","Rent stays fixed"],"<b>Upwards-only</b> reviews favour the landlord: the rent cannot go down."],[5,"How are the tenant's own improvements treated on an open market review?",["Disregarded","Added to the rent","Valued at half"],"The tenant is not charged rent on <b>its own improvements</b>."],[6,"Which case says rent review time limits are presumed not of the essence?",["United Scientific v Burnley","Mannai v Eagle Star","Marks and Spencer v BNP"],"<b>United Scientific</b>: a landlord can still review despite missing the stated timetable."],[7,"A break notice has a minor error. Can it still be valid?",["Yes, if a reasonable recipient would understand it","No, never","Yes, always"],"<b>Mannai</b>: an error may not matter if a reasonable recipient would not be misled."],[8,"How are break conditions such as vacant possession applied?",["Strictly","Leniently","They are ignored"],"Break clauses and their conditions are applied <b>strictly</b>."],[9,"A tenant covenants 'to keep in repair' premises already in disrepair. What does that cover?",["Putting them into repair first","Only future damage","Nothing"],"The covenant includes <b>putting in repair</b> if the premises start in disrepair."],[10,"Who is bound by the RICS Code for Leasing Business Premises?",["RICS members","All solicitors","Landlords only"],"The Code binds <b>RICS members</b> acting on lease negotiations; lawyers are not bound."],[11,"Will a court usually order specific performance of a keep-open covenant?",["No, damages are the remedy","Yes","Only for 5 years"],"<b>Co-op Insurance v Argyll Stores</b>: the court will not usually order a business to trade."]],"c":[[225,"What does the parcels clause usually let in a multi-let building?","Only the <b>internal skin</b>: floors, ceilings, internal walls and non-structural parts. A lease of a whole building includes the <b>structure</b>."],[232,"Who usually repairs in a multi-let building and in a lease of a whole building?","Multi-let: tenant repairs the <b>interior</b>; landlord repairs <b>structure, roof and common parts</b> and recovers via a <b>service charge</b>. Whole building: an <b>FRI lease</b> puts all costs on the tenant."],[235,"A tenant takes an old building in disrepair on a covenant to keep in repair. What is the risk?","The covenant includes <b>putting</b> the premises into repair first. The tenant can face a large bill."],[239,"What insurance protections should a tenant negotiate?","Cover against <b>insured risks</b> for full <b>reinstatement</b>. A covenant to <b>reinstate</b>. <b>Rent suspension</b> while unfit. A right to <b>terminate</b> if not reinstated. Exclusion from repair. Provision for <b>uninsured risks</b>."],[241,"What does LTA 1927 s.19(2) imply into a qualified covenant against alterations?","For <b>improvements</b>, consent is <b>not to be unreasonably withheld</b>. The landlord may require reasonable <b>reinstatement</b> and compensation for loss in value."],[244,"What does LTA 1927 s.19(3) say about a qualified user covenant?","The landlord may not charge a <b>fine</b> for consent. There is <b>no implied</b> reasonableness requirement."],[248,"What are the usual assumptions in an open market rent review?","A <b>willing landlord and tenant</b>. Let with <b>vacant possession</b>. <b>Fit for immediate use</b>. The tenant has <b>complied with its covenants</b>. Same terms as the actual lease other than rent."],[249,"What is disregarded in an open market rent review?","The tenant's <b>occupation</b>, its <b>goodwill</b> and its own <b>improvements</b>."],[250,"Is a late rent review notice valid?","<b>Usually yes.</b> Time is <b>presumed not of the essence</b>, unless the lease <b>says so expressly</b> or the review is <b>linked to a break clause</b>."],[255,"How strictly are break conditions applied, and what should a tenant accept?","<b>Strictly.</b> The tenant should limit them to paying the <b>principal rent</b> and giving <b>vacant possession</b>."]]},{"id":"lease-dealings","title":"Grant, assignment and underletting of leases","q":[[0,"A lease for more than 3 years must be made how?",["By deed","In writing only","Orally"],"<b>LPA 1925 s.52</b>: leases over 3 years need a deed."],[1,"A lease of 8 years is granted but not registered. What is the effect?",["It takes effect only in equity","It is void","It is a full legal lease"],"A lease over 7 years must be <b>registered</b> to take effect at law."],[2,"An AGA obliges the outgoing tenant to guarantee whom?",["Its immediate assignee","All later assignees","The landlord's lender"],"An <b>AGA</b> (LTCA 1995 s.16) covers only the immediate assignee."],[3,"A new lease is lawfully assigned. What happens to the original tenant?",["Released, unless an AGA is given","Liable for the whole term","Liable for 6 months"],"Under the <b>LTCA 1995</b> the tenant is released on a lawful assignment."],[4,"An old lease is assigned. What is the original tenant's position?",["Still liable for the whole term","Released","Liable for 6 years"],"Old leases keep <b>privity of contract</b>: the original tenant remains liable."],[5,"Which Act implies that consent to assign must not be unreasonably withheld?",["LTA 1927 s.19(1)(a)","LTA 1954 s.30","LTA 1988 s.4"],"<b>s.19(1)(a) LTA 1927</b> implies it into a qualified covenant."],[6,"A landlord sits on a consent request for months. What is the result?",["Breach of s.1 LTA 1988","No remedy","Consent is deemed given"],"<b>Go West v Spigarolo</b>: unreasonable delay is a breach, and damages may follow."],[7,"A tenant assigns without the required consent. What is the effect?",["Valid transfer, but a breach of covenant","The assignment is void","Valid, with no breach"],"<b>Old Grovebury</b>: the assignment works, but the landlord may be able to forfeit."],[8,"Why does a landlord of an old lease take a direct covenant from the assignee?",["To bind it for the whole term","To release the original tenant","To reduce the rent"],"It creates <b>privity of contract</b> with the new tenant."],[9,"An underlease must be shorter than the head lease by at least how much?",["One day","One year","Six months"],"If it is not shorter it takes effect as an <b>assignment</b>."],[10,"The head lease is forfeited. What happens to the underlease?",["It ends too, unless relief is given","It continues unchanged","It passes to the landlord on the same terms"],"Forfeiture of the head lease also ends the underlease; the undertenant can ask for <b>relief</b>."],[11,"Under SC 8.3, when may either party rescind for lack of landlord's consent?",["If not given 3 working days before completion","10 working days after exchange","After 6 months"],"<b>SC 8.3</b> (SCS): no consent 3 working days before completion. The SCPC differs."]],"c":[[260,"Which date decides who is liable on lease covenants after assignment?","Whether the lease was granted <b>before</b> or <b>on or after 1 January 1996</b>."],[262,"When must LTA 1954 contracting-out formalities be done where there is an agreement for lease?","<b>Before</b> the tenant signs the <b>agreement for lease</b>, because that is when the tenant becomes bound."],[264,"Why check the landlord's mortgage on the grant of a lease?","The lender's <b>consent</b> is usually needed. Without it the lease may <b>not bind the lender</b> if it repossesses."],[269,"Which leases must be registered and which are overriding interests?","Leases <b>over 7 years</b> must be <b>registered</b> with their own title. Leases of <b>7 years or less</b> are <b>overriding interests</b>. Leases over 3 years can be protected by <b>notice</b>."],[272,"What must a landlord do under LTA 1988 s.1 after a written application for consent?","Within a <b>reasonable time</b>: <b>give consent</b> unless reasonable not to, and serve <b>written notice</b> of the decision with any <b>conditions</b> or <b>reasons</b> for refusal."],[276,"What happens under SCS 8.3 if landlord's consent is not obtained?","Either party may <b>rescind</b> if consent is not given <b>3 working days before completion</b>, or is given on a condition reasonably objected to."],[277,"What happens under SCPC 11.3 if landlord's consent is not obtained?","Completion is <b>postponed</b> until <b>5 working days</b> after consent is notified. Either party may <b>rescind</b> after <b>6 months</b> from the original completion date."],[278,"Is an assignment without the landlord's required consent effective?","<b>Yes</b>, it transfers the lease. But it is a <b>breach</b>, risking <b>forfeiture</b>. For a new lease it is an <b>excluded assignment</b>, so the outgoing tenant is <b>not released</b>."],[283,"What is an authorised guarantee agreement (AGA)?","In a <b>new lease</b>, the outgoing tenant guarantees its <b>immediate assignee only</b>. It ends when that assignee <b>lawfully assigns</b> on."],[289,"Is the original tenant of a new lease released on assignment?","<b>Yes</b>, on a <b>lawful assignment</b> (LTCA 1995 s.5), unless it is an <b>excluded assignment</b>."]]},{"id":"lease-remedies","title":"Remedies for breach and ending a commercial lease","q":[[0,"How far back can a landlord recover rent arrears by action in debt?",["6 years","12 years","3 years"],"<b>Limitation Act 1980 s.19</b>: rent arrears recoverable for 6 years."],[1,"Which sums can CRAR recover?",["Rent, plus interest and VAT","Service charge as well","Any sum due under the lease"],"<b>CRAR</b> is for pure rent only, even if the lease calls other sums rent."],[2,"When is CRAR unavailable?",["If part of the premises is a dwelling","If the lease is for 10 years","If the tenant is a company"],"CRAR applies only to <b>wholly commercial</b> premises (TCEA 2007 s.75)."],[3,"A landlord uses CRAR for arrears. What happens to its right to forfeit for those arrears?",["It is waived","Unaffected","It is strengthened"],"CRAR treats the lease as continuing, so it <b>waives</b> forfeiture for those arrears."],[4,"A landlord learns of a breach, then demands next quarter's rent. What is the risk?",["Waiver of the right to forfeit","None","Loss of the rent"],"Demanding rent after knowing of a breach <b>waives</b> the right to forfeit for it."],[5,"When is peaceable re-entry safe?",["Premises are unoccupied","Occupied by the tenant","Occupied by an undertenant"],"Re-entry must be <b>peaceable</b>: never where anyone is living or present."],[6,"The 1938 Act applies to a repair breach. What must the s.146 notice tell the tenant?",["Its right to serve a counter-notice","To pay within 14 days","The landlord's costs"],"The <b>Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938</b> gives a right to serve a counter-notice within 28 days."],[7,"How is the landlord's recovery of repair costs under a Jervis v Harris clause classed?",["A debt","Capped damages","A penalty"],"As a <b>debt</b>, it avoids the s.18 cap and the 1938 Act."],[8,"Which remedy restrains a tenant's unauthorised change of use?",["Injunction","CRAR","s.17 notice"],"An <b>injunction</b> stops a continuing or threatened breach."],[9,"Against whom can a s.17 notice recover arrears?",["A former tenant or its guarantor","The current tenant only","The landlord's lender"],"<b>LTCA 1995 s.17</b> applies to former tenants and guarantors."],[10,"A former tenant pays in full after a s.17 notice. What can it call for?",["An overriding lease","A refund of the premium","The rent deposit"],"<b>s.19</b>: the former tenant may call for an overriding lease."],[11,"Arrears are paid with costs after forfeiture for rent. Is relief usually granted?",["Yes","No","Only after 6 months"],"The court usually grants <b>relief</b> if the arrears and costs are paid."]],"c":[[297,"What are the conditions for using CRAR?","<b>Commercial premises</b> only (no part a <b>dwelling</b>). Lease <b>in writing</b>. <b>Pure rent</b> only, plus interest and VAT. Arrears of at least <b>7 days' rent</b>. <b>7 clear days' notice</b> of enforcement. A <b>certificated enforcement agent</b>."],[300,"What is the effect of using CRAR on forfeiture?","It <b>affirms</b> the lease and <b>waives</b> the right to forfeit for those arrears."],[303,"What must a landlord do to claim rent from a former tenant or its guarantor?","Serve a <b>s.17 notice</b> within <b>6 months</b> of the sum falling due."],[304,"What are the conditions for forfeiture for non-payment of rent?","A <b>forfeiture clause</b>. A <b>formal demand</b>, unless the lease says 'whether formally demanded or not'. <b>No s.146 notice</b> is needed."],[306,"When will relief from forfeiture for non-payment of rent be granted?","Usually if the tenant <b>pays all arrears and costs</b>. After peaceable re-entry the tenant should apply <b>promptly</b> (usually within about 6 months)."],[307,"What waives the right to forfeit?","Knowing of the breach, doing something that <b>recognises the lease as continuing</b>, especially <b>demanding or accepting rent</b> due after it. 'Without prejudice' does <b>not</b> prevent waiver."],[308,"How does waiver differ for continuing and once-and-for-all breaches?","<b>Continuing</b> breach (disrepair, wrongful use): waiver covers only the past and the right <b>revives</b>. <b>Once-and-for-all</b> breach (assignment without consent): <b>waived permanently</b>."],[309,"What must a s.146 notice contain before forfeiture for breaches other than rent?","It must <b>specify the breach</b>, require it to be <b>remedied</b> if capable of remedy, and require <b>compensation</b> if wanted. Then allow a <b>reasonable time</b>."],[310,"When does the Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938 apply and what does it do?","Lease of <b>7 years or more</b> with <b>3 years or more</b> left. The tenant may serve a <b>counter-notice within 28 days</b>. The landlord then needs the <b>court's permission</b> and a statutory ground."],[311,"What caps damages for disrepair?","The <b>diminution in value of the reversion</b> (LTA 1927 s.18(1)). If the landlord will demolish, damages may be <b>nil</b>."],[312,"What is a landlord's self-help repair clause (Jervis v Harris clause) and why is it useful?","It lets the landlord <b>enter, do the repairs</b> and <b>recover the cost</b>. The claim is a <b>debt</b>, so the <b>s.18 cap</b> and the <b>1938 Act</b> do not apply."]]},{"id":"lta1954","title":"Security of tenure: LTA 1954 Part II","q":[[0,"A tenant sublets the whole and leaves. Does the 1954 Act protect it?",["No, it is not in occupation","Yes","For one year only"],"The tenant must <b>occupy</b> the premises for business (s.23)."],[1,"What must the landlord serve first to contract out of the 1954 Act?",["A warning notice","A declaration","A court order"],"The landlord serves a <b>warning notice</b>, then the tenant makes a declaration."],[2,"The warning notice is served 3 days before the tenant signs. Which declaration is needed?",["Statutory declaration","Simple declaration","None"],"Less than 14 days: <b>statutory</b> declaration, sworn before an independent solicitor."],[3,"The contractual term of a protected tenancy ends. What happens?",["It continues on the same terms","It ends","It becomes periodic at market rent"],"<b>s.24</b>: the tenancy continues until ended in a way the Act allows."],[4,"A tenant has a 2-year fixed term and wants a new lease. Which route is open?",["A s.26 request","A s.25 notice","A s.27 notice"],"A <b>s.26 request</b> needs a fixed term of more than 1 year."],[5,"A landlord serves a s.25 notice 13 months before its termination date. Is it valid?",["No","Yes","Only if hostile"],"A s.25 notice must be <b>6 to 12 months</b> before the termination date."],[6,"The tenant misses the court application deadline. What follows?",["It loses its right to renew","The deadline extends","The landlord must grant a lease"],"Missing the <b>statutory period</b> ends the tenancy and the renewal right."],[7,"Ground (f) requires the landlord to intend what?",["To demolish or reconstruct","To sell the premises","To raise the rent"],"<b>Ground (f)</b>: demolition or reconstruction, with a firm, unconditional intention."],[8,"Which ground covers persistent late payment of rent?",["Ground (b)","Ground (d)","Ground (g)"],"<b>Ground (b)</b> is persistent delay in paying rent; it is discretionary."],[9,"Which ground is mandatory and based on the landlord's own use?",["Ground (g)","Ground (a)","Ground (c)"],"<b>Ground (g)</b>: own occupation, subject to the 5-year rule."],[10,"What rent does the court set for a new lease under s.34?",["Open market rent, ignoring the tenant's goodwill","The existing rent","Whatever the landlord proposes"],"<b>s.34</b> disregards the tenant's occupation, goodwill and certain improvements."],[11,"The tenant is refused on ground (g) after 14 years' occupation. What compensation?",["Double the rateable value","The rateable value","Twice the annual rent"],"<b>Double</b> compensation applies after 14 years of business occupation."]],"c":[[324,"When does Part II apply (s.23)?","To a <b>tenancy</b> where the property is <b>occupied by the tenant</b> for the purposes of a <b>business</b> carried on by it."],[327,"When is a fixed-term tenancy of 6 months or less protected?","Only if it provides for <b>renewal or extension</b> beyond 6 months, or the tenant (and predecessors in the business) has occupied for <b>more than 12 months</b>."],[329,"What is the contracting-out procedure under s.38A?","The landlord serves a <b>warning notice</b>. The tenant makes a <b>declaration</b> accepting it. The lease <b>refers to</b> the notice, declaration and agreement. All <b>before</b> the tenant is bound."],[330,"When is a simple declaration enough, and when is a statutory declaration needed?","Notice served <b>at least 14 days</b> before the tenant enters the lease or becomes contractually bound: <b>simple declaration</b>. <b>Less than 14 days</b>: <b>statutory declaration</b> before an independent solicitor."],[334,"What are the timing rules for a landlord's s.25 notice?","Prescribed form. Given <b>not less than 6 nor more than 12 months</b> before the termination date. The date cannot be <b>earlier than contractual expiry</b>."],[336,"Which tenants can serve a s.26 request?","Only tenants with a <b>fixed term of more than 1 year</b>. Not if a s.25 notice has already been served."],[337,"How must a landlord respond to a s.26 request to oppose renewal?","By <b>counter-notice within 2 months</b> stating its grounds. Otherwise it <b>cannot later oppose</b>."],[339,"When must the court application be made?","By the <b>termination date</b> in the <b>s.25 notice</b>, or the day <b>before</b> the start date in the <b>s.26 request</b> (s.29A)."],[343,"Which grounds of opposition are discretionary and which mandatory?","<b>Discretionary</b>: (a) disrepair, (b) persistent late rent, (c) other substantial breaches, (e) sub-letting of part. <b>Mandatory if proved</b>: (d) alternative accommodation, (f) redevelopment, (g) own occupation."],[344,"What must the landlord show for ground (f)?","A firm intention to <b>demolish or reconstruct</b> (or substantial construction) that it <b>could not reasonably do without possession</b>. Judged at the <b>hearing</b>, and the intention must <b>not be conditional</b> on the tenant's claim for a new tenancy."],[348,"How does the court fix the terms of a new lease?","<b>Holding</b> (s.32). <b>Duration</b> reasonable, up to <b>15 years</b> (s.33). <b>Open market rent</b> disregarding occupation, goodwill and improvements (s.34). <b>Other terms</b> starting from the <b>current lease</b>; a change must be justified (s.35)."],[350,"How is compensation calculated?","<b>Rateable value × 1</b>. <b>Double</b> if the tenant (or predecessors in the same business) occupied for the whole <b>14 years</b> before termination."]]},{"id":"planning","title":"Planning and building regulations","q":[[0,"Is internal work that does not affect external appearance development?",["No, under s.55(2)","Yes","Only in a flat"],"<b>s.55(2)</b> excludes internal works (but listed buildings need separate consent)."],[1,"A house is converted into two flats. Is that development?",["Yes, under s.55(3)","No","Only if the roof changes"],"<b>s.55(3)</b>: using a house as two or more dwellings is development."],[2,"Which use class covers a dwellinghouse?",["C3","C4","E"],"<b>C3</b> covers a family or up to six people living as a household."],[3,"Which use class covers a small HMO of 3 to 6 unrelated people?",["C4","C3","Sui generis"],"<b>C4</b> covers small houses in multiple occupation."],[4,"Which use class covers shops, cafes and offices?",["E","B8","F1"],"<b>Class E</b> covers commercial, business and service uses."],[5,"A shop moves to another use within the same class. Is that development?",["No","Yes","Only in a conservation area"],"A change <b>within the same class</b> is not development."],[6,"What does outline planning permission give?",["Approval in principle, details later","Full approval of all details","Permission to demolish only"],"<b>Outline</b> permission leaves reserved matters for later approval."],[7,"Does a planning permission bind later owners of the land?",["Yes, it runs with the land","No, it is personal","It lapses on sale"],"Permission and its conditions <b>run with the land</b>."],[8,"Is there a right of appeal against a breach of condition notice?",["No","Yes, to the Secretary of State","Yes, to the High Court"],"<b>s.187A</b>: no appeal, and non-compliance is an offence."],[9,"Which notice takes effect at least 28 days after service and can be appealed?",["Enforcement notice","Stop notice","Planning contravention notice"],"An <b>enforcement notice</b> (s.172) has an appeal to the Secretary of State."],[10,"How long can a temporary stop notice last in England?",["56 days","28 days","6 months"],"Since 25 April 2024 it lasts up to <b>56 days</b> in England."],[11,"Building work was done without approval. What may the owner apply for?",["A regularisation certificate","A lawful development certificate","A completion notice"],"A <b>regularisation certificate</b> covers building regulation approval after the event."]],"c":[[354,"When is planning permission required (TCPA 1990 ss.55 and 57)?","For any <b>development</b>: <b>operational development</b> (building, engineering, mining or other operations, including demolition) or a <b>material change of use</b>."],[356,"Which works and uses are not development under s.55(2)?","Works affecting only the <b>interior</b> or not materially affecting <b>external appearance</b>. Uses <b>incidental to the enjoyment</b> of a dwellinghouse within its curtilage. <b>Agriculture and forestry</b>. A change <b>within the same use class</b>."],[359,"What is in Use Class E?","<b>Commercial, business and service</b>: shops, cafés and restaurants, financial and professional services, <b>offices</b>, R&amp;D, <b>light industry</b>, indoor sport, medical services, nurseries."],[361,"What is a sui generis use and what are examples?","A use in <b>no class</b>, so any change <b>to or from</b> it needs permission. Examples: <b>pubs</b>, <b>hot food takeaways</b>, betting shops, cinemas, petrol stations, <b>large HMOs</b> (more than 6)."],[362,"What does the GPDO do?","Grants <b>deemed planning permission</b> for listed classes: householder extensions within limits, certain <b>changes of use</b> (such as Class E to C3 with <b>prior approval</b>), temporary uses, minor works."],[364,"Within what time must development begin under a full planning permission?","Within <b>3 years</b> of the grant, unless the permission says otherwise (s.91)."],[365,"What are the time limits under an outline planning permission?","Apply for <b>reserved matters</b> within <b>3 years</b> of the outline permission. Begin development within <b>2 years</b> of final approval of the last reserved matter (s.92)."],[367,"What is a s.106 agreement?","A <b>planning obligation</b> with the LPA (such as affordable housing or contributions), registered as a <b>local land charge</b> and binding successors."],[369,"What consent do works to a listed building need, and is there a time limit for enforcement?","<b>Listed building consent</b> for demolition or works affecting character, inside or out. Unauthorised works are a <b>criminal offence</b> with <b>no time limit</b> for enforcement."],[372,"How does an enforcement notice work?","It requires steps to remedy the breach. It takes effect at least <b>28 days</b> after service. An <b>appeal</b> before then <b>suspends</b> it. Non-compliance is a <b>criminal offence</b>."],[375,"What is the enforcement time limit in England for breaches on or after 25 April 2024?","<b>10 years</b> for all breaches: operational development, change to a single dwelling, and other breaches."]]},{"id":"tax","title":"Property taxes: SDLT, LTT, VAT and CGT","q":[[0,"Who pays SDLT on a land purchase in England?",["The buyer","The seller","The lender"],"<b>SDLT</b> is paid by the buyer on the chargeable consideration."],[1,"What does the slice system mean for SDLT?",["Each rate applies only to its band","The top rate applies to the whole price","The average rate applies to all"],"Each rate is charged only on the part of the price <b>within its band</b>."],[2,"A genuine price is set for carpets and curtains in the contract. What is the SDLT effect?",["Excluded from SDLT","Included in the SDLT","Taxed at the higher rate"],"<b>Chattels</b> are not land, so a genuine price for them is not taxed."],[3,"Which body collects Land Transaction Tax?",["Welsh Revenue Authority","HMRC","HM Land Registry"],"<b>LTT</b> applies in Wales and is collected by the WRA."],[4,"Does Wales have first-time buyer relief for LTT?",["No, it has a higher nil-rate band","Yes","Only up to £300,000"],"Wales has no FTB relief; the <b>nil-rate band</b> is higher for everyone."],[5,"On the grant of a lease, SDLT on the rent is based on what?",["Net present value of the rent","Total rent unadjusted","First year's rent"],"The <b>NPV</b> of the rent over the term is charged above the threshold."],[6,"Which SDLT rates apply to a mixed residential and commercial property?",["Non-residential rates","Residential rates","A blend of both"],"<b>Mixed-use</b> property takes the non-residential rates."],[7,"How is the sale of a normal residential home treated for VAT?",["Exempt, so no VAT","Standard-rated","Standard-rated if the seller opts"],"Sales of existing homes are usually <b>exempt</b> from VAT."],[8,"Which supply is zero-rated?",["First sale of a new house by its builder","Sale of a 10-year-old house","Sale of a new shop"],"The first grant of a major interest in a <b>new dwelling</b> by the builder is zero-rated."],[9,"A seller has opted to tax a commercial building. What follows for a buyer who cannot recover VAT?",["20% VAT is an extra cost","No effect","A VAT refund"],"An <b>option to tax</b> makes the sale standard-rated: a real cost for banks, charities and insurers."],[10,"A business is sold as a going concern with its premises. What is the VAT treatment?",["Outside the scope of VAT as a TOGC","Standard-rated","Exempt"],"A <b>TOGC</b> is outside the scope of VAT, if the conditions are met."],[11,"A seller lived in the home throughout and sells it. What is the CGT position?",["No CGT through Private Residence Relief","Full CGT","Half CGT"],"<b>PRR</b> exempts the gain on a main home occupied throughout."]],"c":[[389,"What is the SDLT effective date?","Normally <b>completion</b>, or earlier <b>substantial performance</b> such as taking possession."],[390,"What is SDLT first-time buyers' relief?","<b>0% up to £300,000</b> and <b>5%</b> from £300,001 to <b>£500,000</b>. <b>No relief</b> if the price is over £500,000. <b>All</b> joint buyers must be first-time buyers."],[391,"When does the SDLT higher rate for additional dwellings apply?","A <b>5% surcharge</b> where the buyer will own <b>more than one dwelling</b> at the end of the day and is <b>not replacing</b> its main residence. Companies always pay it."],[394,"How is SDLT charged on the grant of a lease?","On any <b>premium</b> at normal rates, and on the <b>net present value</b> of the rent at <b>1%</b> above the threshold."],[395,"How does LTT differ from SDLT?","<b>No first-time buyers' relief</b>, but a higher nil-rate band of <b>£225,000</b>. Separate <b>higher residential</b> bands. Return and payment within <b>30 days</b>. <b>No non-resident surcharge</b>."],[397,"Which property supplies are exempt from VAT?","The <b>default</b> for land: <b>residential</b> sales and leases (other than new builds), the freehold of a <b>commercial building over 3 years old</b>, most commercial <b>leases</b>."],[398,"Which property supply is zero-rated?","The <b>first grant of a major interest</b> (freehold, or lease over <b>21 years</b>) in a <b>new dwelling</b> by the <b>person constructing it</b>."],[399,"Which property supplies are compulsorily standard-rated?","The freehold of a <b>new commercial building</b> (under <b>3 years</b> from completion) or civil engineering work. Car parking, hotel accommodation, sporting rights."],[400,"What is the option to tax and why would an owner opt?","An election to make supplies of a <b>commercial</b> property <b>standard-rated</b>. The reason is to <b>recover input VAT</b> on the purchase, works and fees."],[404,"What is a TOGC and what are its conditions?","Sale of a <b>let</b> property as a going concern is <b>outside the scope of VAT</b>. The buyer continues the <b>same letting business</b>, is <b>VAT registered</b>, and if the seller opted, the buyer <b>opts and notifies HMRC</b> before the tax point."],[407,"When is the disposal for CGT, and when must UK residential property gains be reported?","Disposal is at <b>exchange</b> of an unconditional contract. Report and pay within <b>60 days of completion</b>."],[409,"Which periods always count for PRR?","Actual <b>occupation</b>, and the <b>final 9 months</b> of ownership (<b>36 months</b> if disabled or moving into care)."]]}]},{"id":"crimlaw","title":"Criminal Liability","stop":13,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><rect x=\"-69\" y=\"-36\" width=\"6\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#6B4226\"/><polygon points=\"-84,-30 -66,-90 -48,-30\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><path d=\"M-75,-60 L-66,-90 L-57,-60 q-5,5 -9,0 q-4,5 -9,0 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"-43\" y=\"-36\" width=\"6\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#6B4226\"/><polygon points=\"-58,-30 -40,-106 -22,-30\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><path d=\"M-49,-68 L-40,-106 L-31,-68 q-5,5 -9,0 q-4,5 -9,0 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"41\" y=\"-36\" width=\"6\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#6B4226\"/><polygon points=\"26,-30 44,-100 62,-30\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><path d=\"M35,-65 L44,-100 L53,-65 q-5,5 -9,0 q-4,5 -9,0 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"67\" y=\"-36\" width=\"6\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#6B4226\"/><polygon points=\"52,-30 70,-86 88,-30\" fill=\"#2D6A4F\"/><path d=\"M61,-58 L70,-86 L79,-58 q-5,5 -9,0 q-4,5 -9,0 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"-16\" rx=\"86\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"#E0F7FF\"/><path d=\"M-50,-18 q20,-6 40,0 M14,-12 q20,-6 36,2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7BDFF2\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M-20,-24 L-28,-10 M-12,-24 L-8,-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M-32,-10 h8 M-12,-8 h8\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"-27,-24 -5,-24 -14,-56\" fill=\"#E63946\"/><circle cx=\"-14\" cy=\"-62\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#F4C7A1\"/><path d=\"M-21,-64 a7,7 0 0 1 14,0 Z\" fill=\"#E63946\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"-14\" cy=\"-72\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M18,-24 L10,-10 M26,-24 L30,-8\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M6,-10 h8 M26,-8 h8\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"11,-24 33,-24 20,-56\" fill=\"#4361EE\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"-62\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#F4C7A1\"/><path d=\"M13,-64 a7,7 0 0 1 14,0 Z\" fill=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"-72\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M-40,-96 l0,8 M-44,-92 h8 M30,-110 l0,8 M26,-106 h8 M-6,-118 l0,8 M-10,-114 h8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4361EE\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"actus-reus","title":"Actus reus: acts, omissions and causation","q":[[0,"Which is a conduct crime, needing no result?",["Fraud by false representation","Murder","Manslaughter"],"Fraud is complete on the <b>conduct</b> alone. Murder and manslaughter are <b>result crimes</b> needing death."],[1,"A mother lets her baby starve to death. What is the source of her duty to act?",["Special relationship","Contract","Public office"],"A <b>parent</b> owes a duty to a young child by reason of the <b>special relationship</b>."],[2,"A friend agrees to look after an ill lodger, then ignores her as she declines. Source of duty?",["Voluntary assumption of care","A statutory duty","Public office"],"Taking on the care of a vulnerable person creates a duty (<b>voluntary assumption of care</b>, Stone and Dobinson)."],[3,"The defendant wakes to find his cigarette has set the mattress alight and goes back to sleep. Why is he liable?",["He created a dangerous situation","He owed a contractual duty","He owed a family duty"],"Creating a danger gives a <b>duty to take reasonable steps</b> to put it right (Miller)."],[4,"A stranger watches a child drown in a shallow pool and does nothing. Criminal liability?",["None: no duty to act","Manslaughter","Murder"],"There is <b>no general duty to act</b> in English criminal law. A moral duty is not enough."],[5,"The defendant poisons his mother's drink, but she dies of an unrelated heart attack first. Did he cause her death?",["No, she would have died anyway","Yes, he intended her death","Yes, the poison was in the glass"],"Under the <b>but for</b> test the death would have happened anyway (White). Only attempted murder is possible."],[6,"Negligent medical treatment breaks the chain of causation only if it is...",["So potent the defendant's act is insignificant","Any mistake by a doctor","Contrary to hospital guidance"],"Only <b>extraordinary</b> treatment breaks the chain (Cheshire). Ordinary negligence does not."],[7,"A light push causes a victim with an unusually thin skull to die. Is the defendant liable for the death?",["Yes, he takes the victim as he finds her","No, the death was unforeseeable","No, the push was too slight"],"The <b>thin skull rule</b> applies: the defendant takes the victim as found."],[8,"A victim jumps from a moving car to escape an attack and is hurt. Does this break the chain?",["No, unless the reaction was daft or unexpected","Yes, it was a voluntary act","Yes, the victim chose to jump"],"A victim's escape attempt keeps the chain intact unless it was <b>unreasonable</b> (Roberts)."],[9,"A free, deliberate and informed act of a third party after the defendant's act will usually...",["Break the chain of causation","Keep the chain intact","Make the defendant a secondary party"],"A <b>free, voluntary and informed</b> act of another breaks the chain (Kennedy (No 2))."],[10,"A swarm of bees makes a driver lose control and hit someone. Why is he not liable?",["His act was involuntary","He owed no duty of care","The bees broke the chain"],"An act caused by an <b>external factor</b> with no control by the mind is <b>automatism</b>: not voluntary, so no actus reus."],[11,"A police officer watches a man being attacked and walks away. What is the source of his duty?",["Public office","Contract","Special relationship"],"A <b>public office</b> holder such as a police officer can owe a duty to act (Dytham)."]],"c":[[0,"What are the two elements the prosecution must prove for every crime, and to what standard?","The <b>actus reus</b> (guilty act) and the <b>mens rea</b> (guilty mind), <b>beyond reasonable doubt</b>."],[7,"When can the defendant be liable for an omission?","The offence <b>can be committed by omission</b>, the defendant was under a <b>legal duty to act</b> and <b>breached</b> it, and (for a result crime) the breach <b>caused</b> the result."],[8,"What are the six sources of a duty to act?","<b>Statute</b>. <b>Contract</b>. <b>Special relationship</b> (e.g. parent and child). <b>Voluntary assumption of care</b>. <b>Creating a dangerous situation</b>. <b>Public office</b>."],[11,"When does creating a dangerous situation give rise to a duty?","Once the defendant <b>becomes aware</b> of a danger they created, even innocently, they must take <b>reasonable steps</b> to deal with it. Mens rea is judged from that moment (Miller)."],[15,"What is the test for factual causation?","<b>But for</b> the defendant's conduct, would the result have happened <b>when and as it did</b>? If it would have happened anyway, the defendant is not the cause."],[16,"What is the test for legal causation?","The defendant's conduct must be an <b>operating and substantial</b> cause: more than minimal, not necessarily the only or main cause. And no <b>new intervening act</b> must break the chain."],[18,"What is the thin skull rule?","The defendant must <b>take the victim as they find them</b>, including hidden <b>physical</b> weaknesses and the victim's <b>beliefs</b>. The defendant is liable for the full result."],[20,"When does medical treatment break the chain of causation?","<b>Rarely</b>: only if so <b>independent</b> and <b>potent</b> that the defendant's acts are insignificant, for example 'palpably wrong' treatment once the wound has largely healed. Negligent treatment usually does not."],[22,"When does the victim's own escape or reaction break the chain?","Only if it is so <b>'daft'</b> or unexpected that no reasonable person could foresee it. Panicked flight is within the range of expected responses."],[23,"Does the victim's free, deliberate and informed decision to self-inject drugs break the chain?","<b>Yes</b>, normally. The supplier does not cause death. If <b>the defendant injects</b> the victim, the defendant does cause death."]]},{"id":"mens-rea","title":"Mens rea: intention, recklessness, negligence and strict liability","q":[[0,"Which describes direct intention?",["The defendant's aim or purpose","The result was likely","The defendant did not care"],"<b>Direct intent</b> means the consequence was the defendant's aim or purpose."],[1,"Under Woollin, oblique intent may be found if death or GBH was...",["A virtual certainty, and he realised it","Probable","Possible"],"Intent may be found where the result was a <b>virtual certainty</b> and the defendant appreciated this (Woollin)."],[2,"Cunningham recklessness means the defendant...",["Foresaw the risk and took it unreasonably","Failed to notice an obvious risk","Acted carelessly"],"<b>Subjective</b> recklessness: the defendant is aware of the risk and unreasonably goes on."],[3,"Which approach to recklessness did R v G restore?",["Subjective","Objective","Mixed"],"<b>R v G</b> overruled Caldwell. The defendant must actually foresee the risk."],[4,"The defendant swings a belt at one person but hits another. Is his mens rea for the second person established?",["Yes, it transfers","No, he did not intend that person","Only if she was nearby"],"<b>Transferred malice</b>: intent to commit the same offence against one victim carries to another (Latimer)."],[5,"The defendant throws a stone at a person but breaks a window. Can his intent transfer to criminal damage?",["No, it is a different offence","Yes, it is the same fault","Yes, if he was reckless"],"Malice transfers only between the <b>same offence</b> (Pembliton)."],[6,"The defendant hits the victim, thinks him dead, and dumps the body. The victim dies of exposure. Murder?",["Yes, it is one series of acts","No, the mens rea came too late","No, only manslaughter"],"Mens rea and actus reus coincide over a <b>series of acts</b> (Thabo Meli)."],[7,"In a strict liability offence, mens rea need not be proved for...",["At least one element of the actus reus","Any element","The defendant's identity"],"<b>Strict liability</b> means no fault need be shown as to at least one element."],[8,"Which case says mens rea is presumed unless Parliament clearly excludes it?",["Sweet v Parsley","Alphacell v Woodward","Prince"],"<b>Sweet v Parsley</b> confirms the presumption of mens rea. It is strongest for truly criminal offences."],[9,"Which level of mens rea is judged against the reasonable person?",["Negligence","Recklessness","Intention"],"<b>Negligence</b> is objective: falling below the reasonable person's standard. Recklessness and intention look at the defendant's own mind."],[10,"A careful shopkeeper reasonably mistakes a buyer's age under a strict liability offence. Defence?",["No, mistake is irrelevant","Yes, the mistake was reasonable","Yes, if he asked for ID"],"For a <b>strict liability</b> element, even an honest, reasonable <b>mistake</b> is no defence."],[11,"The defendant steals to give the money to charity. Does his motive affect liability?",["No, motive is not mens rea","Yes, it is a defence","Yes, it reduces the offence"],"<b>Motive</b> is distinct from intention. A good motive is not a defence, though relevant to sentence."]],"c":[[29,"What is direct intention?","The result is the defendant's <b>aim or purpose</b>, however unlikely success is."],[30,"What is the current test for oblique intention?","The jury <b>may find</b> intention where the result (in murder, death or serious harm) was a <b>virtual certainty</b> barring unforeseen intervention, <b>and</b> the defendant <b>appreciated</b> that (Woollin)."],[32,"Is foresight that death was highly probable enough for intention?","<b>No.</b> 'Highly probable', 'likely' or a 'serious risk' is <b>recklessness</b>, not intention."],[33,"What is the test for recklessness?","The defendant was <b>aware of a risk</b> that a circumstance existed or a result would occur, <b>and</b> it was <b>unreasonable</b> to take it in the circumstances known to the defendant (R v G)."],[34,"Is the defendant reckless if a reasonable person would have seen an obvious risk the defendant did not see?","<b>No.</b> The old objective test is <b>overruled</b>. Recklessness is <b>subjective</b>."],[35,"What does 'maliciously' mean in the OAPA 1861?","<b>Intention or subjective recklessness</b>: the defendant must have <b>foreseen</b> the risk of some harm."],[38,"What is a strict liability offence?","One needing <b>no mens rea</b> for at least one element. The defendant can be guilty despite an honest, <b>reasonable mistake</b>. Mostly <b>regulatory</b> offences."],[42,"What is the coincidence rule?","The actus reus and mens rea must <b>coincide in time</b>."],[44,"What is transferred malice?","If the defendant intends harm to X but the <b>same type</b> of harm happens to Y, the defendant's mens rea <b>transfers</b> to Y."],[45,"Does malice transfer from a person to property or vice versa?","<b>No.</b> Look instead for <b>recklessness</b> as to the actual harm."]]},{"id":"non-fatal-offences","title":"Non-fatal offences against the person","q":[[0,"Can words alone amount to an assault?",["Yes","No","Only if shouted"],"<b>Words alone</b> can cause apprehension of immediate force (Ireland; Constanza)."],[1,"The defendant says he will hit the victim next week. Is this an assault?",["No, the force is not immediate","Yes, it is a threat","Yes, if the victim is scared"],"Assault needs apprehension of <b>immediate</b> unlawful force."],[2,"A driver stops his car on an officer's foot and refuses to move. Why is this battery?",["It is a continuing act","It was an omission","The officer consented"],"The act continued after mens rea arose, so there was a <b>continuing act</b> (Fagan)."],[3,"What mens rea is needed for assault occasioning ABH (s.47)?",["Intent or recklessness as to assault or battery","Intent to cause ABH","Foresight of ABH"],"The defendant need not foresee <b>actual bodily harm</b> (Savage; Parmenter)."],[4,"Which of these can be actual bodily harm?",["A recognised psychiatric illness","Fear","Panic"],"ABH includes psychiatric illness but <b>not mere emotions</b> (Chan-Fook; Ireland)."],[5,"What mens rea is needed for a s.20 offence?",["Intent or recklessness as to some harm","Intent to kill","Foresight of serious harm"],"<b>Section 20</b> needs foresight of some harm, not serious harm (Mowatt)."],[6,"What mens rea is needed for wounding with intent (s.18)?",["Intent to cause GBH","Recklessness as to GBH","Recklessness as to some harm"],"<b>Section 18</b> needs intent; recklessness is not enough."],[7,"A punch causes only internal bleeding. Is this a wound?",["No, the skin must be broken","Yes, blood was shed","Yes, it was serious"],"A <b>wound</b> requires a break in the continuity of the whole skin (JCC v Eisenhower)."],[8,"What does grievous bodily harm mean?",["Really serious harm","Harm needing hospital treatment","Any visible injury"],"GBH is <b>really serious</b> harm (DPP v Smith). It can include psychiatric injury (Burstow)."],[9,"A defendant knowingly infects a partner with HIV, who did not know. Can he commit a s.20 offence?",["Yes, by recklessly inflicting GBH","No, it is not an infliction","No, sex is consensual"],"Reckless transmission can be <b>inflicting GBH</b> (Dica). Consent to sex is not consent to infection."],[10,"Can a victim consent to ABH in a private sadomasochistic act?",["No","Yes","Yes, if no hospital treatment"],"<b>Brown</b>: consent is no defence to ABH or worse, save for recognised exceptions."],[11,"What is the maximum sentence for wounding with intent (s.18)?",["Life imprisonment","10 years","5 years"],"<b>Section 18</b> carries a maximum of life. Sections 20 and 47 carry 5 years."]],"c":[[49,"What are the mode of trial and maximum for s.47, s.20 and s.18?","<b>s.47</b> and <b>s.20</b>: either way, maximum <b>5 years</b>. <b>s.18</b>: indictable only, maximum <b>life</b>."],[50,"What is the actus reus of assault?","Causing the victim to <b>apprehend</b> the application of <b>immediate unlawful force</b>. No touching is needed."],[56,"What is the mens rea of assault and battery?","<b>Intention or subjective recklessness</b> as to causing apprehension (assault) or applying force (battery)."],[57,"What is the actus reus of battery?","<b>Applying unlawful force</b> to the victim. The <b>slightest touch</b> is enough; no injury or pain is needed."],[62,"What is actual bodily harm?","Any hurt or injury <b>calculated to interfere with health or comfort</b>. More than <b>transient or trifling</b>, but need not be serious or permanent. Includes bruises, brief unconsciousness and cutting hair."],[64,"What is the mens rea of s.47?","Only the mens rea for the <b>assault or battery</b>. The defendant need <b>not intend or foresee any harm</b>."],[65,"What is a wound?","A break in <b>both layers of the skin</b>. Inside the mouth or lip counts. A broken bone or internal bleeding without a skin break is <b>not a wound</b>."],[66,"What is grievous bodily harm?","<b>Really serious harm</b> ('serious harm' is acceptable). Judged on all the circumstances including the victim's <b>age and health</b>. Injuries can be added together. Includes serious <b>psychiatric illness</b> and knowingly infecting with serious disease."],[68,"What is the mens rea of s.20?","The defendant <b>intended or foresaw</b> that <b>some harm</b> might result. The defendant need not foresee a wound or GBH."],[69,"What is the mens rea of s.18?","<b>Intent to cause GBH</b>, or <b>intent to resist or prevent lawful arrest</b> together with malice as to the harm. Recklessness is never enough for the intent to cause GBH."],[72,"When is consent a defence to non-fatal offences?","Always to <b>assault and battery</b>. For <b>ABH or worse</b>, only within a <b>recognised exception</b>."],[74,"What are the recognised exceptions where consent can cover ABH or worse?","<b>Properly conducted sports</b>. <b>Reasonable surgery</b>. <b>Tattooing and piercing</b> (not extreme body modification). <b>Rough horseplay</b> without intent to injure. Informed consent to the <b>risk of infection</b> in sex."]]},{"id":"theft","title":"Theft (Theft Act 1968 ss.1-6)","q":[[0,"Which wording is the definition of theft?",["Dishonest appropriation with intent to deprive permanently","Taking property without the owner's consent","Dishonestly taking property by deception"],"<b>Section 1 Theft Act 1968</b> needs dishonesty, appropriation, property, belonging to another and intent to permanently deprive."],[1,"The owner freely gives the defendant an item. Can taking it still be an appropriation?",["Yes, consent is irrelevant","No, the owner consented","Only if there was deception"],"<b>Appropriation</b> can occur even with the owner's consent (Gomez; Hinks)."],[2,"A walker picks wild blackberries to eat. Is this theft?",["No, unless picked for sale or reward","Yes, they belong to the landowner","Yes, if he lacks permission"],"Picking wild plants or fruit is not theft unless done <b>for reward or sale</b> (s.4(3))."],[3,"The defendant dishonestly uses a neighbour's electricity. Which offence applies?",["Abstracting electricity","Theft","Robbery"],"<b>Electricity</b> is not property for theft. It is covered by s.13 abstraction."],[4,"The owner secretly retakes his car from a garage holding it for an unpaid bill. Theft?",["Yes, the garage had possession","No, he owns it","No, he was not dishonest"],"The garage had <b>possession or control</b>, so the car belonged to another (Turner (No 2))."],[5,"Money is given to the defendant to buy a gift, and he spends it on himself. Which rule makes it another's property?",["Section 5(3)","Section 5(4)","Section 3(2)"],"<b>Section 5(3)</b>: property received subject to an obligation to deal with it in a particular way."],[6,"A bank wrongly credits the defendant's account, and he knowingly spends it. Which rule applies?",["Section 5(4)","Section 5(3)","Section 6"],"<b>Section 5(4)</b>: a mistaken overpayment, which the defendant must restore, belongs to another."],[7,"The defendant borrows a friend's bike and returns it next day. Intent to permanently deprive?",["No, it was only borrowed","Yes, he used it","Yes, without consent"],"<b>Borrowing</b> is not enough unless the item is returned with all its goodness gone (Lloyd; s.6)."],[8,"Under Ivey and Barton, dishonesty is judged by...",["The standards of ordinary decent people","The defendant's own standards","The victim's view"],"Find the defendant's <b>belief as to the facts</b>, then apply the standard of <b>ordinary decent people</b>."],[9,"Which belief means the defendant is not dishonest under s.2(1)?",["A belief in a legal right to the property","A belief the owner is wealthy","A belief he would repay later"],"<b>Section 2(1)</b> covers belief in a legal right, belief of consent, or that the owner cannot be found."],[10,"A buyer pays a fair price in good faith, then learns the goods were stolen and keeps them. Theft?",["No, s.3(2) protects him","Yes, he now knows","Yes, he must return them"],"<b>Section 3(2)</b>: a later assumption of rights by a good faith purchaser for value is not theft."],[11,"What is the maximum sentence for theft?",["7 years","10 years","5 years"],"<b>Theft</b> carries a maximum of 7 years' imprisonment."]],"c":[[77,"What is the definition of theft (s.1 TA 1968)?","<b>Dishonestly appropriating property belonging to another</b> with the <b>intention of permanently depriving</b> the other of it."],[80,"What is an appropriation (s.3)?","<b>Any assumption of the rights of an owner</b>. Assuming <b>any one</b> right is enough, such as switching price labels."],[81,"Can there be an appropriation with the owner's consent?","<b>Yes</b>, even a consent obtained by deception or a <b>valid gift</b>. Liability then turns on <b>dishonesty</b>."],[84,"What counts as property (s.4)?","<b>Money</b> and all other property, <b>real or personal</b>, including <b>things in action</b> (such as a bank credit balance) and other <b>intangible property</b>."],[89,"When does property belong to another (s.5(1))?","When any person has <b>possession or control</b> of it, or <b>any proprietary right or interest</b>. The defendant can even steal <b>his own property</b> from someone in possession."],[90,"What is the effect of s.5(3)?","Where the defendant receives property from or for another and is under a <b>legal obligation</b> to deal with <b>that property or its proceeds</b> in a particular way, it is treated as <b>belonging to the other</b>."],[91,"What is the effect of s.5(4) on mistaken payments?","Property got by <b>another's mistake</b>, which the defendant is under a <b>legal obligation</b> to restore, belongs to the other. An intention not to restore it is an intention to permanently deprive."],[93,"What three beliefs mean the defendant is not dishonest under s.2(1)?","The defendant believes he has a <b>legal right</b> to deprive. Or the owner would <b>consent</b> if he knew. Or the owner <b>cannot be discovered</b> by reasonable steps (not for trustees or PRs)."],[96,"What is the test for dishonesty where s.2(1) does not apply?","<b>(1)</b> What was the defendant's <b>actual knowledge or belief as to the facts</b>? <b>(2)</b> Given that, was the conduct <b>dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people</b>? (Ivey; Barton and Booth)"],[98,"What does s.6(1) add to intention to permanently deprive?","The defendant intends to <b>treat the thing as his own to dispose of</b> regardless of the other's rights, such as offering to <b>sell it back</b> to the owner."],[99,"When does borrowing amount to an intention to permanently deprive?","Only if it is <b>equivalent to an outright taking</b>: the property is returned with its <b>goodness or virtue</b> gone, such as an expired season ticket."]]},{"id":"robbery-burglary","title":"Robbery, burglary and aggravated burglary","q":[[0,"What turns theft into robbery?",["Force or threat of force to steal","Taking from a person","Taking from a shop"],"<b>Robbery</b> is theft plus force or threat of force used immediately before or at the time of stealing, in order to steal."],[1,"The defendant uses force on a bystander so he can steal from a shop. Robbery?",["Yes, force on any person counts","No, force must be on the owner","No, the bystander was not robbed"],"<b>Section 8</b> requires force on any person, not just the owner."],[2,"The defendant wrenches a bag from the victim's hand. Is this force?",["Yes, force on a person","No, only the bag was touched","Only if the victim is hurt"],"Wrenching a bag from someone's grasp is <b>force on the person</b> (Clouden)."],[3,"The defendant uses force to retake his own property, honestly believing he has a right. Robbery?",["No, there is no theft","Yes, force was used","Yes, if the force was serious"],"<b>No theft</b> means no robbery. A belief in a legal right is not dishonest."],[4,"What is the maximum sentence for robbery?",["Life imprisonment","14 years","10 years"],"<b>Robbery</b> carries a maximum of life."],[5,"Which intent on entry makes a trespasser a burglar under s.9(1)(a)?",["Intent to steal","Intent to commit a battery","Intent to trespass"],"Intent to <b>steal</b>, inflict GBH or do unlawful damage is needed."],[6,"A son with general permission to enter his father's house enters to steal a TV. Trespasser?",["Yes, he exceeded his permission","No, he had permission","No, it is his family home"],"Entering beyond the permission given makes a <b>trespasser</b> (Jones and Smith)."],[7,"The defendant reaches through a window with his arm to steal. Has he entered?",["Yes, part of the body can be enough","No, he must be fully inside","Only if he takes something"],"<b>Entry</b> need only be effective, and part of the body can suffice (Brown)."],[8,"Is an inhabited houseboat a building for burglary?",["Yes","No","Only if moored"],"<b>Section 9(4)</b> includes an inhabited vehicle or vessel."],[9,"What mens rea is needed for the trespass element of burglary?",["Knowing or reckless that he is trespassing","Intent to trespass","None, it is strict"],"The defendant must <b>know or be reckless</b> as to trespass (Collins)."],[10,"What is the maximum sentence for burglary of a dwelling?",["14 years","10 years","Life"],"<b>Dwelling burglary</b>: 14 years. Non-dwelling: 10 years."],[11,"What turns burglary into aggravated burglary?",["Having a firearm or weapon with him","Entering at night","Entering a dwelling"],"<b>Section 10</b>: a firearm, imitation firearm, weapon of offence or explosive carried at the relevant time."]],"c":[[103,"What is robbery (s.8 TA 1968)?","<b>Stealing</b> and, <b>immediately before or at the time</b> and <b>in order to</b> steal, using <b>force on any person</b> or putting or seeking to put any person <b>in fear of force then and there</b>."],[105,"Is there a robbery if the defendant uses force but genuinely believes he is entitled to the property?","<b>No.</b> There is no dishonesty, so <b>no theft</b> and <b>no robbery</b>. Consider an <b>assault</b> offence."],[107,"How much force is needed for robbery?","<b>Very little</b>: nudging, jostling or <b>wrenching a bag</b> from the victim's hand is enough. Snatching an item without touching the victim is <b>not</b> force on a person."],[110,"How is the timing requirement in robbery applied?","Force must be used <b>immediately before or at the time</b> of stealing. Appropriation can be a <b>continuing act</b>, so force used while still 'in the act' counts."],[113,"What is burglary under s.9(1)(a)?","<b>Entering</b> a building or part of a building <b>as a trespasser</b> with <b>intent</b> to <b>steal</b>, <b>inflict GBH</b> or do <b>unlawful damage</b>. Complete at <b>entry</b>."],[114,"What is burglary under s.9(1)(b)?","Having <b>entered as a trespasser</b>, the defendant <b>steals or attempts to steal</b>, or <b>inflicts or attempts to inflict GBH</b>. Unlawful damage is <b>not</b> included."],[117,"When is burglary indictable only?","Where it involves an intent to commit, or commission of, an <b>indictable-only</b> offence (such as s.18). Or burglary of a <b>dwelling</b> where someone inside was subjected to <b>violence or the threat of violence</b>."],[122,"When does the defendant enter as a trespasser?","When entering <b>without permission</b> or <b>beyond the permission</b> given, such as entering a parent's house to steal."],[124,"The defendant enters within his permission and only then decides to steal. Is it burglary?","<b>No.</b> He was not a trespasser at entry. It is <b>theft</b> only."],[126,"What is aggravated burglary (s.10)?","Any burglary where the defendant <b>at the time</b> has with him a <b>weapon of offence, imitation firearm, firearm or explosive</b> (WIFE). <b>Indictable only</b>, maximum <b>life</b>."],[128,"When must the defendant have the weapon for aggravated burglary?","For <b>s.9(1)(a)</b>, at the <b>time of entry</b>. For <b>s.9(1)(b)</b>, at the <b>time of stealing or inflicting GBH</b>. It can be picked up inside."]]},{"id":"criminal-damage","title":"Criminal damage and arson (Criminal Damage Act 1971)","q":[[0,"Which wording matches simple criminal damage?",["Damaging another's property, intending or reckless","Damaging any property","Damaging property worth over £5,000"],"<b>Section 1(1)</b> Criminal Damage Act 1971 needs damage, property belonging to another, mens rea and no lawful excuse."],[1,"Mud smeared on a cell wall needs effort to clean. Is this damage?",["Yes, it impaired its usefulness","No, it is not permanent","No, it is only dirt"],"<b>Damage</b> is a matter of fact and degree, including cost and effort of repair (Roe v Kingerlee)."],[2,"Is property under the Act limited to tangible property?",["Yes","No","Only for land"],"<b>Section 10(1)</b>: property means <b>tangible</b> property, including land. Wild mushrooms and wild flowers or fruit are excluded."],[3,"The defendant smashes his own car, which another person has hired for the week. Property belonging to another?",["Yes, the hirer has a proprietary interest","No, he owns it","No, it was his choice"],"<b>Section 10(2)</b> covers anyone with custody, control or a proprietary right."],[4,"A drunk defendant wrongly but honestly believes a house is a friend's who would consent and breaks in. Lawful excuse?",["Yes, the belief need not be reasonable","No, he was drunk","No, the belief was wrong"],"<b>Section 5</b>: an honest belief in consent counts, even if drunken (Jaggard v Dickinson)."],[5,"Protestors cut a base fence, believing it protects their homes from attack. Lawful excuse?",["No, it did not protect the property","Yes, it was an honest belief","Yes, it was a protest"],"Whether the act was done <b>in order to protect</b> property is judged objectively. Cutting the fence was <b>too remote</b> (Hill and Hall)."],[6,"The defendant fires a gun at a window, and the bullet endangers life. Aggravated damage?",["No, the danger came from the act","Yes, life was endangered","Yes, he used a gun"],"Danger must come from the <b>damage itself</b>, not the means (Steer)."],[7,"What is the maximum sentence for arson?",["Life imprisonment","14 years","10 years"],"<b>Arson</b> is damage by fire (s.1(3)) and carries life."],[8,"What recklessness applies to criminal damage?",["Foresight of a risk, unreasonably taken","An obvious risk he did not notice","Carelessness"],"After R v G, recklessness is <b>subjective</b>."],[9,"The defendant does £3,000 of damage to a car (not by fire). Where is it tried?",["Magistrates' court only","Either court, at his choice","Crown Court only"],"Damage of <b>£5,000 or less</b> is tried as if <b>summary only</b>: no right to elect, maximum 3 months."],[10,"A tenant removes fixtures, honestly believing they are his. Guilty of criminal damage?",["No, he lacks the mens rea","Yes, they were another's","Yes, if he acted unreasonably"],"An honest belief that the property is his own means no mens rea (Smith [1974])."],[11,"Can aggravated criminal damage (s.1(2)) be committed against the defendant's own property?",["Yes","No","Only if rented"],"<b>Section 1(2)</b> applies to any property, whether the defendant's or another's."]],"c":[[131,"What is simple criminal damage?","Without lawful excuse, <b>destroying or damaging property belonging to another</b>, <b>intending</b> or <b>being reckless</b> as to its destruction or damage."],[132,"How is simple criminal damage tried where the damage is £5,000 or less?","<b>As if summary only</b>: no right to elect Crown Court trial, maximum <b>3 months</b>. Otherwise either way, maximum <b>10 years</b>."],[134,"What counts as damage?","<b>Permanent or temporary</b> physical harm, or <b>impairment of value or usefulness</b> that costs time, effort or money to put right. It is a question of <b>fact and degree</b>."],[140,"Does an honest but mistaken belief that property is the defendant's own negate mens rea?","<b>Yes</b>, even if mistaken in law and unreasonable. The defendant lacks mens rea as to damaging <b>another's</b> property."],[141,"What is aggravated criminal damage (s.1(2))?","Damaging <b>any property</b>, including the defendant's own, intending or reckless as to damage, <b>and</b> intending or reckless that the <b>damage</b> will <b>endanger the life of another</b>. Indictable only, maximum <b>life</b>."],[143,"Where must the danger to life come from for s.1(2)?","From the <b>damage</b> itself, not the act causing it. Danger from a <b>bullet</b> is not enough; danger from falling debris or a burning building is."],[145,"To which offences does the s.5 lawful excuse defence apply?","Only <b>simple</b> damage and simple arson (s.1(1)). <b>Never</b> aggravated damage (s.1(2))."],[146,"What is the s.5(2)(a) lawful excuse?","The defendant believed the person entitled to consent <b>had consented</b> or <b>would have consented</b> had they known of the damage and circumstances."],[148,"What is the s.5(2)(b) lawful excuse?","The defendant damaged property <b>in order to protect property</b> and believed it was in <b>immediate need of protection</b> and the <b>means were reasonable</b>."]]},{"id":"murder","title":"Murder","q":[[0,"Which wording matches murder?",["Unlawful killing with intent to kill or cause GBH","Killing with intent to cause harm","Killing with recklessness as to death"],"Murder needs an <b>unlawful killing</b> with <b>malice aforethought</b>: intent to kill or cause GBH."],[1,"The defendant intends only GBH, and the victim dies. Is it murder?",["Yes","No, only manslaughter","No, intent to kill is needed"],"Intent to cause <b>GBH</b> is enough for murder."],[2,"What is the sentence for murder?",["Mandatory life","Discretionary life","30 years"],"Murder carries <b>mandatory life</b>, with a minimum term set by the court."],[3,"Is a foetus in the womb a reasonable creature in being?",["No","Yes","Only after 24 weeks"],"The victim must be <b>in being</b>, meaning born and living independently."],[4,"A victim dies four years after the attack. What is needed for a murder prosecution?",["The Attorney General's consent","Nothing further","A new charge"],"After the <b>year and a day rule</b> was abolished, consent is needed if death occurs after over three years."],[5,"Doctors switch off the life support machine after brain stem death. Does it break the chain?",["No","Yes, it is a new act","Only if the family agreed"],"The defendant's injury remains an operating cause (Malcherek and Steel)."],[6,"Is voluntary intoxication relevant to murder?",["Yes, it may negate intent","No, never","Only if involuntary"],"Murder is a <b>specific intent</b> crime, so it may show no intent. Manslaughter remains."],[7,"Is mercy killing at the victim's request a defence to murder?",["No","Yes","Only for the terminally ill"],"There is <b>no defence of mercy killing</b>, whatever the motive (Inglis)."],[8,"Is duress a defence to murder as a principal?",["No","Yes","Only if there was a threat to family"],"<b>Duress</b> is not available for murder or attempted murder (Howe; Gotts)."],[9,"Which partial defences reduce murder to manslaughter?",["Loss of control and diminished responsibility","Duress and necessity","Self-defence and consent"],"Both are <b>partial defences</b>, leading to a manslaughter conviction."],[10,"A burglar causes a death with no intent to kill or cause GBH. Murder?",["No, there is no malice aforethought","Yes, he was committing a felony","Yes, death was foreseeable"],"<b>Constructive malice</b> was abolished. Manslaughter may apply."],[11,"The defendant sets a house on fire, knowing death is virtually certain, with no wish to kill. Intent?",["The jury may find intent","Intent is established","No intent is possible"],"Woollin: a jury <b>may find</b> intent where death was virtually certain and he realised it."]],"c":[[152,"What is murder?","A <b>common law</b> offence: <b>unlawfully causing the death</b> of a <b>human being</b> under the King's peace, <b>intending to kill or to cause GBH</b>."],[153,"What sentence follows a murder conviction?","<b>Mandatory life</b> imprisonment, with a <b>minimum term</b> set using <b>Schedule 21 Sentencing Act 2020</b>. A <b>whole life order</b> is possible in the worst cases."],[156,"When does a child become a 'creature in being'?","When <b>fully expelled</b> from the mother's body with an <b>existence independent</b> of her. The cord need not be cut."],[162,"What are express and implied malice?","<b>Express malice</b>: intention to <b>kill</b>. <b>Implied malice</b>: intention to cause <b>GBH</b> (really serious harm)."],[163,"Can the defendant be guilty of murder without intending or foreseeing death?","<b>Yes.</b> An intention to cause <b>really serious harm</b> is enough."],[164,"Is recklessness ever enough for murder?","<b>No.</b> However great the risk, foresight of a risk of death gives <b>manslaughter</b> at most."],[165,"When may a jury find oblique intention for murder?","Where death or serious harm was a <b>virtual certainty</b> and the defendant <b>appreciated</b> that. The jury <b>may</b>, not must, find intention."],[166,"What is the mens rea for attempted murder, compared with murder?","<b>Attempted murder</b> requires an <b>intention to kill</b> only. Intending GBH is enough for murder if the victim dies, but if the victim survives that is <b>s.18</b>."],[169,"Which partial defences reduce murder to voluntary manslaughter?","<b>Loss of control</b>, <b>diminished responsibility</b> and <b>suicide pact</b>. They apply to <b>murder only</b>."]]},{"id":"voluntary-manslaughter","title":"Voluntary manslaughter: loss of control and diminished responsibility","q":[[0,"Which is NOT needed for loss of control?",["Suddenness","Loss of self-control","A qualifying trigger"],"Since 2009, loss of control <b>need not be sudden</b> (s.54(2))."],[1,"Which can be a qualifying trigger for loss of control?",["Fear of serious violence","Sexual infidelity","Simple insults"],"<b>Section 55</b>: fear of serious violence, or a grave wrong causing a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged."],[2,"Can sexual infidelity alone be a qualifying trigger?",["No","Yes","Only if discovered"],"<b>Section 55(6)(c)</b> excludes sexual infidelity as a trigger."],[3,"The defendant provokes the victim into an attack so he can kill. Qualifying trigger?",["No, he incited it","Yes, there was a real attack","Yes, if he feared violence"],"A trigger the defendant <b>incited</b> to provide an excuse is excluded (s.55(6))."],[4,"Loss of control is unavailable if the defendant acted in...",["A considered desire for revenge","Panic","A rage"],"<b>Section 54(4)</b>: a considered desire for revenge excludes the defence."],[5,"Once loss of control is raised, who must disprove it?",["The prosecution","The defendant","The judge"],"The prosecution disproves it <b>beyond reasonable doubt</b>."],[6,"Loss of control and diminished responsibility reduce murder to...",["Voluntary manslaughter","Involuntary manslaughter","Assault"],"Both are <b>partial defences</b> to murder only."],[7,"In the loss of control comparison, is the defendant's voluntary intoxication considered?",["No","Yes","Only if severe"],"The comparator is a person of <b>normal tolerance and self-restraint</b>, sober (Asmelash)."],[8,"Diminished responsibility requires an abnormality of mental functioning arising from...",["A recognised medical condition","Any strong emotion","Intoxication"],"<b>Section 2 Homicide Act 1957</b> (as amended) needs a recognised medical condition."],[9,"'Substantial' impairment for diminished responsibility means...",["Important or weighty","Anything more than trivial","Total"],"<b>Substantial</b> has its ordinary meaning of <b>important or weighty</b>; merely more than trivial is not enough (Golds)."],[10,"Who bears the burden for diminished responsibility?",["The defendant, on the balance of probabilities","The prosecution","The judge"],"The defendant must prove it on the <b>balance of probabilities</b>."],[11,"Is acute voluntary intoxication alone a recognised medical condition for diminished responsibility?",["No","Yes","Only if extreme"],"Voluntary acute intoxication is <b>not</b> a medical condition (Dowds). Alcohol dependency may be."]],"c":[[179,"What are the three elements of loss of control (s.54(1))?","<b>(a)</b> the defendant's killing resulted from a <b>loss of self-control</b>. <b>(b)</b> It had a <b>qualifying trigger</b>. <b>(c)</b> A person of the defendant's <b>sex and age</b> with <b>normal tolerance and self-restraint</b>, in the defendant's circumstances, might have reacted the <b>same or similarly</b>."],[181,"When is loss of control excluded by the defendant's motive?","When the defendant acted in a <b>considered desire for revenge</b> (s.54(4))."],[182,"What is the fear trigger?","The defendant's loss of control was attributable to <b>fear of serious violence from the victim</b> against <b>the defendant or another identified person</b> (s.55(3))."],[183,"What is the anger trigger?","Things done or said that were of an <b>extremely grave character</b> and caused the defendant a <b>justifiable sense of being seriously wronged</b> (s.55(4)). Both are judged <b>objectively</b>."],[185,"How is sexual infidelity treated under loss of control?","It must be <b>disregarded</b> as a trigger on its own, but can be considered as <b>context</b> for other triggers (Clinton)."],[186,"What does 'circumstances of D' include in the objective test?","All the defendant's circumstances <b>except</b> those whose <b>only</b> relevance is to the defendant's <b>general capacity for tolerance or self-restraint</b>. A history of abuse counts; a short temper does not."],[188,"Who bears the burden of proof for loss of control?","The defendant must raise <b>sufficient evidence</b> for a jury to reasonably conclude it might apply. The <b>prosecution</b> must then <b>disprove</b> it beyond reasonable doubt."],[189,"What are the elements of diminished responsibility (s.2(1))?","An <b>abnormality of mental functioning</b> from a <b>recognised medical condition</b>, which <b>substantially impaired</b> the defendant's ability to understand conduct, form rational judgment or exercise self-control, and <b>provides an explanation</b> for the killing."],[191,"Who bears the burden of proof for diminished responsibility?","<b>The defendant</b>, on the <b>balance of probabilities</b>."],[193,"What does 'substantially impaired' mean?","<b>Important or weighty</b>. Merely more than trivial is <b>not</b> enough."]]},{"id":"involuntary-manslaughter","title":"Involuntary manslaughter: unlawful act and gross negligence","q":[[0,"Which wording matches unlawful act manslaughter?",["A dangerous criminal act causing death","Any unlawful act causing death","A negligent act causing death"],"The act must be <b>unlawful</b>, <b>dangerous</b>, and cause death (Church)."],[1,"Can an omission found unlawful act manslaughter?",["No","Yes","Only for a parent"],"Unlawful act manslaughter needs an <b>act</b>, not an omission (Lowe)."],[2,"Does a civil wrong, such as a tort, count as the unlawful act?",["No, it must be a crime","Yes","Only if serious"],"The act must be a <b>criminal offence</b> (Franklin)."],[3,"What must the defendant foresee for unlawful act manslaughter?",["Nothing about death or harm","Death","Serious harm"],"Only the <b>mens rea of the base offence</b> is needed (Newbury and Jones)."],[4,"A dangerous act is one a sober reasonable person would see as risking...",["Some harm, not serious harm","Death","Serious injury"],"<b>Some harm</b> to another is enough (Church)."],[5,"Burglars see the occupant is 87 and frail, carry on, and he dies of shock. Is the act dangerous?",["Yes, his frailty was obvious","No, burglary is not violent","No, they never touched him"],"The reasonable person has the <b>knowledge gained during the crime</b>, so obvious frailty makes it dangerous (Watson)."],[6,"Need the unlawful act be aimed at the victim?",["No","Yes","Only for assault"],"A dangerous act need not be directed at the victim (Goodfellow)."],[7,"Gross negligence manslaughter requires a serious risk of...",["Death","Serious injury","Any harm"],"The risk must be of <b>death</b> (Misra; Rose)."],[8,"How is the duty of care in gross negligence manslaughter found?",["By ordinary negligence law","By a statute","By contract only"],"A duty of care is established under the <b>ordinary law of negligence</b> (Adomako)."],[9,"Who decides whether negligence is gross enough to be criminal?",["The jury","The judge","An expert"],"The jury decides whether the conduct was so bad as to be <b>criminal</b> (Adomako)."],[10,"Under the Corporate Manslaughter Act 2007, an organisation is liable where death results from...",["A gross breach involving senior management","Any employee's negligence","Any breach of health rules"],"Liability needs a <b>gross breach of a duty of care</b>, with <b>senior management</b> a substantial element in it."],[11,"What is the maximum sentence for involuntary manslaughter?",["Life imprisonment","14 years","10 years"],"Manslaughter carries a <b>discretionary</b> maximum of life."]],"c":[[199,"What four things must be proved for unlawful act manslaughter?","<b>(1)</b> An <b>unlawful act</b>: a criminal offence, done by an <b>act</b>, with its mens rea. <b>(2)</b> The act was <b>dangerous</b> (objective test). <b>(3)</b> It <b>caused</b> death. <b>(4)</b> No intent to kill or cause GBH."],[201,"Can unlawful act manslaughter be based on an omission?","<b>No.</b> It needs an <b>act</b>. An omission with a duty goes to <b>gross negligence manslaughter</b>."],[204,"When does supplying drugs lead to unlawful act manslaughter?","Not where the victim <b>freely self-injects</b>. But if <b>the defendant injects</b> the victim, the defendant commits <b>administering a noxious thing</b> (s.23 OAPA), which can found it."],[205,"What is the dangerousness test for unlawful act manslaughter?","<b>All sober and reasonable people</b> would inevitably recognise the act risked <b>some harm</b>, albeit not serious harm."],[207,"Is a risk of fright or shock enough for dangerousness?","<b>No.</b> It must be <b>physical</b> harm. Fright counts only if a reasonable person would see a risk of physical harm, for example because the victim's <b>frailty was obvious</b>."],[208,"Whose knowledge and characteristics does the reasonable person have in the dangerousness test?","<b>The defendant's knowledge</b>, but not the defendant's <b>age</b> or personal characteristics: the sober and reasonable <b>adult</b>."],[210,"What are the five elements of gross negligence manslaughter?","<b>(1)</b> A <b>duty of care</b>. <b>(2)</b> <b>Breach</b>. <b>(3)</b> A reasonably foreseeable <b>serious and obvious risk of death</b> at the time. <b>(4)</b> <b>Causation</b>. <b>(5)</b> The breach was so bad it was <b>criminal</b> (gross)."],[213,"What risk is required for gross negligence manslaughter?","A <b>serious and obvious risk of death</b>, not merely of serious injury."],[216,"When is negligence 'gross'?","When the jury finds it <b>truly exceptionally bad</b>, so far below the standard that it deserves <b>criminal punishment</b>. Serious civil negligence is not enough."],[217,"What risk does each type of involuntary manslaughter require?","<b>Unlawful act</b>: a risk of <b>some harm</b>. <b>Gross negligence</b>: a risk of <b>death</b>."]]},{"id":"fraud","title":"Fraud (Fraud Act 2006)","q":[[0,"A trustee dishonestly diverts funds he was meant to protect. Which Fraud Act offence?",["Abuse of position (s.4)","False representation (s.2)","Failing to disclose (s.3)"],"<b>Section 4</b> applies where the defendant occupies a position of trust to safeguard another's financial interests."],[1,"Must the victim actually lose money for fraud by false representation?",["No","Yes","Only for large sums"],"Fraud is a <b>conduct crime</b> and is complete when the representation is made."],[2,"The fraudulent intent under the Act is to make a gain or cause...",["Loss to another or risk of loss","Harm to anyone","Distress"],"The defendant must intend <b>gain or loss</b> (or risk of loss) in money or property."],[3,"Can a false representation be made to a machine?",["Yes","No","Only to a bank machine"],"<b>Section 2(5)</b>: a representation can be submitted to any system or device."],[4,"Fraud by failing to disclose (s.3) requires the defendant to be under...",["A legal duty to disclose","A moral duty to disclose","A contractual promise only"],"<b>Section 3</b> needs a <b>legal duty</b> to disclose information."],[5,"What is the maximum sentence for fraud?",["10 years","7 years","Life"],"<b>Fraud</b> carries a maximum of 10 years on indictment."],[6,"After Ivey, must the defendant realise ordinary people would see his conduct as dishonest?",["No","Yes","Only if experienced"],"The test is <b>objective</b>: ordinary decent people's standards."],[7,"The defendant holds a list of stolen card numbers to use later. Which offence?",["Possessing articles for fraud (s.6)","False representation (s.2)","Theft"],"<b>Section 6</b> covers possessing articles for use in fraud."],[8,"The defendant dishonestly uses a gym with no intention to pay. Which offence?",["Obtaining services dishonestly (s.11)","Theft","Abuse of position"],"<b>Section 11</b> covers obtaining services dishonestly, knowing payment is required."],[9,"Does 'gain' under the Act include keeping what he already has?",["Yes","No","Only for money"],"<b>Section 5</b>: gain and loss include keeping what one has, or not getting what one might."],[10,"For s.2, the defendant must know the representation is untrue or misleading, or...",["Be aware it might be","Ought to know","Be indifferent"],"<b>Section 2</b> requires knowledge or awareness that it <b>might be</b> untrue or misleading."],[11,"The defendant pays with a card he knows the bank has cancelled. What does he represent?",["That he has authority to use it","That his account has funds","Nothing, as he said nothing"],"Using a card <b>impliedly represents authority</b> to use it (Lambie). That is fraud under <b>s.2</b>."]],"c":[[220,"What are the three ways of committing fraud under the Fraud Act 2006?","<b>False representation</b> (s.2). <b>Failing to disclose information</b> (s.3). <b>Abuse of position</b> (s.4)."],[221,"What mental elements do all three forms of fraud share?","<b>Dishonesty</b> and an <b>intention to make a gain</b> or <b>cause a loss</b> or <b>expose another to a risk of loss</b>."],[222,"Is fraud a conduct crime or a result crime?","A <b>conduct crime</b>. <b>Nobody need be deceived</b> and <b>nothing need be gained or lost</b>."],[224,"What do 'gain' and 'loss' cover (s.5)?","Only <b>money or other property</b>, <b>temporary or permanent</b>. Gain includes <b>keeping what one has</b>. Loss includes <b>not getting what one might get</b>."],[228,"Can a representation be implied by conduct?","<b>Yes</b>, for example wearing a college gown, ordering a meal (implying intent to pay), or using a card (implying <b>authority</b> to use it)."],[230,"When is a representation false for s.2?","When it is <b>untrue or misleading</b> and the defendant <b>knows it is, or might be</b>, untrue or misleading. Half-truths can be false."],[232,"What is fraud by failing to disclose (s.3)?","Dishonestly failing to disclose information the defendant is under a <b>legal duty</b> to disclose, intending gain or loss."],[235,"What is fraud by abuse of position (s.4)?","The defendant occupies a position in which the defendant is expected to <b>safeguard, or not act against, another's financial interests</b>, and <b>dishonestly abuses</b> it, intending gain or loss."],[237,"Which positions typically fall within s.4?","<b>Trustee</b>, <b>director</b>, <b>professional and client</b>, <b>agent</b>, <b>employee</b>, <b>partner</b>, a carer with access to money, an <b>attorney</b>. It need not be fiduciary."]]},{"id":"general-defences","title":"General defences: intoxication and self-defence","q":[[0,"Which of these is a specific intent offence?",["Wounding with intent (s.18)","Unlawful wounding (s.20)","Assault occasioning ABH"],"<b>Section 18</b> requires intent to cause GBH. Sections 20 and 47 are basic intent."],[1,"Voluntary intoxication is generally no defence to...",["Basic intent offences","Specific intent offences","Strict liability only"],"<b>Majewski</b>: self-induced intoxication supplies the recklessness needed for basic intent crimes."],[2,"The defendant's drink is spiked. He forms the intent and attacks someone. Defence?",["No, he had mens rea","Yes, it was involuntary","Yes, partial"],"Involuntary intoxication helps only if he <b>lacked</b> mens rea (Kingston)."],[3,"The defendant drinks to get Dutch courage and then kills. Intoxication defence?",["No","Yes","Only for manslaughter"],"<b>Dutch courage</b> is no defence, as the intent was formed first (Gallagher)."],[4,"A drunk defendant mistakes a harmless person for an attacker and hits him. Self-defence?",["No, the mistake was drink-induced","Yes, he honestly believed it","Yes, force was reasonable"],"A mistake caused by <b>voluntary intoxication</b> cannot support self-defence (s.76(5); O'Grady)."],[5,"In self-defence, the force used must be...",["Reasonable in the circumstances","Equal to the attack","The minimum possible"],"Force must be <b>reasonable</b> on the facts as the defendant believed them (s.76)."],[6,"The defendant honestly but unreasonably believes he is being attacked. Self-defence?",["It can still be raised","No, the belief must be reasonable","No, the belief must be correct"],"An <b>honest belief</b> is judged as held, unless drink-induced (s.76(4))."],[7,"Must the defendant wait to be struck before using force?",["No","Yes","Only in the home"],"A <b>pre-emptive strike</b> is allowed (Beckford)."],[8,"In a householder case, force is not reasonable if it is...",["Grossly disproportionate","Disproportionate","More than the attacker's"],"<b>Section 76(5A)</b>: householders get more latitude, but not for grossly disproportionate force."],[9,"An honest, instinctive reaction in the heat of the moment is...",["Good evidence of reasonable force","Irrelevant","Proof of unreasonable force"],"<b>Section 76(7)</b>: the defendant cannot be expected to weigh up force exactly."],[10,"Who must disprove self-defence once it is raised?",["The prosecution","The defendant","The judge"],"The prosecution disproves it <b>beyond reasonable doubt</b>."],[11,"Which provision allows reasonable force to prevent crime or arrest offenders?",["Section 3 Criminal Law Act 1967","Section 5 Criminal Damage Act 1971","Section 2 Homicide Act 1957"],"<b>Section 3 Criminal Law Act 1967</b> and common law cover reasonable force to prevent crime."]],"c":[[244,"What is the effect of voluntary intoxication on a basic intent offence?","<b>No defence</b> (Majewski). The defendant is judged on whether the defendant <b>would have foreseen</b> the risk if sober."],[245,"What is the effect of voluntary intoxication on a specific intent offence?","It <b>can</b> show the defendant <b>did not form</b> the intent, giving an acquittal of that offence, usually with a <b>basic intent</b> fall-back."],[246,"What distinguishes a specific intent offence from a basic intent offence?","<b>Specific intent</b>: requires <b>intention</b> (or knowledge or dishonesty); recklessness is not enough. <b>Basic intent</b>: <b>recklessness</b> or negligence is enough."],[247,"Which common offences are specific intent?","<b>Murder</b>, <b>s.18</b>, <b>theft</b>, <b>robbery</b>, <b>burglary with intent to steal</b>, <b>attempts</b>, <b>fraud</b>, and s.1(2) criminal damage charged as <b>intending</b> to endanger life."],[248,"Which common offences are basic intent?","<b>Manslaughter</b>, <b>s.20</b>, <b>s.47</b>, <b>assault</b>, <b>battery</b>, criminal damage charged as reckless, s.1(2) charged as <b>reckless</b>, and the touching in <b>sexual assault</b>."],[253,"When is involuntary intoxication a defence?","To <b>any</b> offence, specific or basic intent, <b>if the defendant did not form the mens rea</b>. If the defendant still formed it, <b>no defence</b> (a drugged intent is still intent)."],[255,"Can the defendant rely on a mistaken belief in the need for self-defence caused by voluntary intoxication?","<b>No</b>, for <b>any</b> offence including murder (s.76(5) CJIA 2008). This includes a later drug-induced psychosis 'attributable to' intoxication."],[259,"What is the first stage of the self-defence test?","<b>Subjective</b>: did the defendant <b>honestly believe</b> force was necessary? The defendant is judged on the facts as the defendant believed them, even if mistaken or <b>unreasonable</b>."],[261,"What is the second stage of the self-defence test?","<b>Objective</b>: was the degree of force <b>reasonable</b> in the circumstances as the defendant believed them? <b>Disproportionate</b> force is not reasonable (s.76(6))."],[264,"Is there a duty to retreat?","<b>No</b>, but the possibility of retreat is a <b>factor</b> in reasonableness."],[268,"How is force judged in a householder case?","<b>Grossly disproportionate</b> force is <b>never</b> reasonable. Disproportionate but not grossly so <b>may</b> be reasonable; the jury decides."]]},{"id":"parties","title":"Parties to crime: principals and accessories","q":[[0,"How is an accessory punished under the Accessories and Abettors Act 1861?",["As a principal offender","More lightly","More harshly"],"<b>Section 8</b>: an accessory is tried and punished as a principal."],[1,"Who is a principal offender?",["The one whose act causes the actus reus","Anyone who helps","The planner"],"The <b>principal</b> commits the actus reus with the required mens rea."],[2,"The defendant has a seven-year-old child steal for him. How is he liable?",["As a principal, using an innocent agent","As an accessory","He is not liable"],"An <b>innocent agent</b> commits no crime, so the instigator is the principal."],[3,"Which of aiding, abetting, counselling and procuring needs a causal link?",["Procuring","Aiding","Abetting"],"<b>Procuring</b> means causing the offence to happen (Att-Gen's Ref (No 1 of 1975))."],[4,"A bystander watches a fight without helping or encouraging. Is he an accessory?",["No, mere presence is not enough","Yes, he was there","Yes, he could have stopped it"],"Presence is not enough without <b>encouragement</b> (Coney)."],[5,"An accessory must know which matters of the offence?",["The essential matters","Every detail","Only the victim's name"],"He must know the <b>essential matters</b> constituting the offence (Johnson v Youden)."],[6,"The defendant lends tools knowing a break-in is planned but not which house. Is he liable?",["Yes, he knew the type of offence","No, he did not know which","No, he only lent tools"],"He need know only the <b>type of crime</b> to be committed (Bainbridge)."],[7,"After Jogee, does foresight that a principal might commit an offence make a person liable?",["No, only evidence of intent","Yes","Only for murder"],"Intent to <b>assist or encourage</b> is required. Foresight is just evidence (Jogee)."],[8,"To withdraw before the offence, an accessory must...",["Communicate withdrawal and take reasonable steps","Simply leave","Tell no one"],"Withdrawal needs <b>timely communication</b> and reasonable steps to undo help (Becerra)."],[9,"A 14-year-old girl encourages unlawful sex with her. Is she an accessory?",["No, she is protected","Yes, she encouraged it","Yes, if willing"],"A member of a <b>protected class</b> cannot be an accessory (Tyrrell)."],[10,"Can an accessory be convicted if the principal is acquitted?",["Yes, if the offence was committed","No","Only for murder"],"The accessory may be convicted if the <b>actus reus</b> was committed by someone."],[11,"An accessory intends to help an assault, but the principal kills with intent to kill. Accessory's liability?",["Manslaughter","Murder","None"],"He lacks murder mens rea but intended assault: <b>manslaughter</b> (Jogee)."]],"c":[[272,"What are the four ways of being an accessory?","<b>Aiding</b> (help). <b>Abetting</b> (encouragement at the scene). <b>Counselling</b> (advice or encouragement before). <b>Procuring</b> (setting out to bring it about)."],[274,"Is mere presence at a crime enough for accessory liability?","<b>No</b>, unless the presence <b>actually encourages</b> the principal and the defendant <b>intends</b> it to. Voluntary presence can be <b>evidence</b> of encouragement."],[276,"Must a principal offence be committed for accessory liability?","<b>Yes</b>, at least its <b>actus reus</b>. Liability is <b>derivative</b>: no offence, nothing to be an accessory to."],[279,"What is the accessory's mens rea after Jogee?","<b>(1)</b> An <b>intention to assist or encourage</b> the principal to commit the crime (which may be <b>conditional</b>). <b>(2)</b> <b>Knowledge of the essential facts</b> making the conduct criminal."],[280,"Is foresight that the principal might commit the crime enough?","<b>No.</b> Foresight is <b>evidence</b> from which intention <b>may</b> be inferred. Parasitic accessorial liability based on foresight is <b>abolished</b>."],[283,"When is a joint attacker guilty of murder after Jogee?","When the defendant <b>intended to assist or encourage</b> the principal to <b>kill or cause GBH</b>, including <b>conditionally</b>."],[284,"The defendant intended to encourage only some harm, but the principal killed with intent. What is the defendant's liability?","Not murder, but <b>manslaughter</b> if a reasonable person would recognise a risk of <b>some harm</b> from the joint attack."],[286,"How can an accessory withdraw from a planned crime?","By <b>timely and unequivocal communication</b> to the principal. Where the defendant gave material help, the defendant may need <b>reasonable steps to prevent</b> the crime. Silently not turning up is not enough."]]},{"id":"attempts","title":"Attempts (Criminal Attempts Act 1981)","q":[[0,"Which Act governs criminal attempts?",["Criminal Attempts Act 1981","Criminal Law Act 1977","Theft Act 1968"],"<b>Section 1(1)</b>: an act more than merely preparatory, with intent to commit an indictable offence."],[1,"What mens rea is needed for attempted murder?",["Intent to kill","Intent to cause GBH","Recklessness"],"Intent to kill is needed. <b>GBH</b> intent is not enough (Whybrow)."],[2,"A burglar walks to a house carrying tools. Is this an attempt?",["No, it is only preparation","Yes, he is on his way","Yes, he has the tools"],"Being equipped and nearby is only preparation. He has not yet <b>embarked on the crime proper</b> (Gullefer; Geddes)."],[3,"A robber points a gun at a victim, about to demand money. Attempt?",["Yes, more than merely preparatory","No, still preparation","No, he has not asked"],"Pointing the gun was an attempt (Jones)."],[4,"The defendant believes a powder is heroin, but it is harmless. Attempt to import drugs?",["Yes, impossibility is no defence","No, it is not a drug","No, no harm done"],"<b>Section 1(2)</b>: the attempt is made even if the offence is impossible (Shivpuri)."],[5,"Can a summary-only offence be attempted?",["No","Yes","Only if serious"],"<b>Section 1(4)</b>: only <b>indictable</b> offences can be attempted."],[6,"What is the maximum penalty for attempting an offence?",["The same as the full offence","Half the full offence","Life"],"An attempt carries the <b>same maximum</b> as the full offence."],[7,"Can someone be convicted of attempted involuntary manslaughter?",["No, intent is required","Yes","Only gross negligence"],"An attempt needs <b>intent</b> to commit the full offence, which involuntary manslaughter lacks."],[8,"For attempted rape, what mens rea is required as to consent?",["Recklessness","Intention","Knowledge"],"Intent to have intercourse, plus recklessness as to <b>consent</b> (Khan)."],[9,"The defendant changes his mind after doing an act that was more than merely preparatory. Liable?",["Yes, abandonment is no defence","No, he withdrew","No, no harm done"],"Once the <b>attempt</b> has begun, later abandonment does not undo it."],[10,"Who decides whether an act is more than merely preparatory?",["The jury, if the judge finds it capable","The judge alone","An expert"],"The judge decides whether the evidence is <b>capable</b> of showing an attempt; the jury decides if it is."],[11,"Intending to kill, the defendant shoots at the victim and misses. Which offence?",["Attempted murder","No offence","Attempted manslaughter"],"He did more than prepare, with <b>intent to kill</b>, so <b>attempted murder</b> is made out."]],"c":[[289,"What is the offence of attempt (s.1(1) CAA 1981)?","With <b>intent to commit</b> an offence, doing an <b>act</b> that is <b>more than merely preparatory</b> to its commission."],[290,"Which offences can be attempted?","Offences <b>triable on indictment</b> (indictable only and either way). <b>Not summary-only</b> offences, so there is no attempted common assault or battery."],[294,"When does an act become more than merely preparatory?","When the defendant <b>embarks on the crime proper</b>. The defendant need not reach the <b>last act</b> or a point of no return, but must go beyond getting ready."],[295,"What question helps decide if the defendant has gone beyond preparation?","Has the defendant <b>actually tried</b> to commit the offence, or only got ready, put himself in position or <b>equipped</b> himself?"],[299,"What mens rea is required for an attempt as to the result?","<b>Intention</b> (direct or oblique). <b>Recklessness is not enough</b>, even where it is enough for the full offence."],[304,"Is impossibility a defence to an attempt?","<b>No.</b> The defendant is judged on the facts <b>as the defendant believed them to be</b> (s.1(2)-(3)). Physical and factual impossibility are no defence."],[305,"When is impossibility a defence?","Only where the defendant's intended act would <b>not be a crime even if the defendant's beliefs were true</b> (an <b>imaginary crime</b>)."],[306,"Can a defendant who abandons the plan after crossing the line escape liability for the attempt?","<b>No.</b> There is <b>no defence of withdrawal</b>. It is relevant only to <b>sentence</b>."]]}]},{"id":"crimprac","title":"Criminal Law and Practice","stop":14,"pic":"<svg viewBox=\"-100 -150 200 156\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g stroke=\"#111\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><polygon points=\"-11,-132 -82,-150 -82,-116\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><polygon points=\"11,-132 82,-150 82,-116\" fill=\"#FFF3B0\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M-90,-4 q11,-10 22,0 t22,0 t22,0 t22,0 t22,0 t22,0 t22,0 t22,0 V4 H-90 Z\" fill=\"#4CC9F0\"/><path d=\"M-52,-2 C-48,-24 -20,-28 0,-24 C20,-30 48,-22 52,-2 Z\" fill=\"#8D99AE\"/><polygon points=\"-22,-16 22,-16 13,-112 -13,-112\" fill=\"#fff\"/><polygon points=\"-19.75,-40 19.75,-40 18.25,-56 -18.25,-56\" fill=\"#E63946\"/><polygon points=\"-16.5625,-74 16.5625,-74 15.0625,-90 -15.0625,-90\" fill=\"#E63946\"/><rect x=\"-6\" y=\"-34\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#10294A\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"-20\" y=\"-118\" width=\"40\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#10294A\"/><rect x=\"-11\" y=\"-140\" width=\"22\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#FFE066\"/><polygon points=\"-16,-140 0,-158 16,-140\" fill=\"#E63946\"/></g></svg>","topics":[{"id":"police-station-detention","title":"Police station: rights and detention limits","q":[[0,"Who decides at the station whether to authorise detention?",["The custody officer","The arresting officer","The duty solicitor"],"The <b>custody officer</b> (at least a sergeant) is independent of the investigation and authorises detention."],[1,"What is the basic maximum detention without charge for a summary-only offence?",["24 hours","36 hours","48 hours"],"The basic limit under PACE s.41 is <b>24 hours</b> from the relevant time. A summary-only offence cannot be extended."],[2,"Who can extend detention without charge from 24 to 36 hours?",["A superintendent","An inspector","The custody officer"],"A <b>superintendent</b> (or above) can extend, for an indictable offence, before the 24 hours expire."],[3,"Who can delay a suspect's right to legal advice?",["A superintendent or above","An inspector or above","The custody officer"],"Delay of <b>legal advice</b> needs a <b>superintendent</b>. Delaying having someone told needs only an inspector."],[4,"Who can delay a suspect's right to have someone told of the arrest?",["An inspector or above","A custody sergeant","The arresting officer"],"Under s.56 an <b>inspector</b> or above can delay informing someone, for an indictable offence only."],[5,"A suspect held for common assault asks for a solicitor. Can advice be delayed?",["No, never","Yes, for up to 36 hours","Yes, if a sergeant agrees"],"Delay is possible only for <b>indictable</b> offences. Common assault is <b>summary-only</b>, so advice cannot be delayed."],[6,"What is the longest legal advice can be delayed?",["36 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"Delay of rights is capped at <b>36 hours</b> from the relevant time, and only while the grounds still apply."],[7,"What is the absolute maximum detention without charge for a non-terrorism suspect?",["96 hours","72 hours","7 days"],"Magistrates' warrants of further detention can take the total up to <b>96 hours</b> from the relevant time."],[8,"When must the first review of detention take place?",["Within 6 hours of authorisation","Within 9 hours of arrival","Within 12 hours of arrest"],"The first review is due within <b>6 hours</b> of detention first being authorised, then at intervals of no more than 9."],[9,"How often must later reviews of detention take place?",["At least every 9 hours","At least every 6 hours","At least every 12 hours"],"After the first review, later reviews are at intervals of no more than <b>9 hours</b>."],[10,"Detention limits (24, 36, 96 hours) normally run from which time?",["The relevant time, usually arrival at the station","The time of arrest","The time detention was authorised"],"The detention clock runs from the <b>relevant time</b>. Review times run from authorisation of detention."],[11,"Can legal advice be delayed because the solicitor might advise silence?",["No","Yes, with a superintendent's approval","Yes, for up to 36 hours"],"Annex B forbids delay on that ground. In <b>Samuel</b> a wrongful refusal led to the confession being excluded under s.78."]],"c":[[3,"When can the custody officer authorise detention without charge (s.37(2))?","Where there are reasonable grounds for believing detention is <b>necessary</b> to <b>secure or preserve evidence</b>, or to <b>obtain evidence by questioning</b>."],[5,"What are the three continuing rights of a detained suspect?","To have <b>someone informed</b> of the arrest (s.56). To consult a <b>solicitor privately and free of charge</b> at any time (s.58). To consult the <b>PACE Codes</b>."],[8,"What conditions must be met to delay the right to have someone informed or to legal advice?","An <b>indictable</b> offence. <b>Not yet charged</b>. Authorised by the right rank. Reasonable grounds to believe the right <b>will</b> cause a specified harm."],[9,"Who can authorise delay of each right?","<b>Inspector</b> to delay having <b>someone informed</b>. <b>Superintendent</b> to delay <b>legal advice</b>."],[11,"What is the maximum delay of a suspect's rights?","<b>36 hours</b> from the <b>relevant time</b>."],[15,"What is the relevant time for detention limits?","Arrival at the <b>first police station</b> after arrest, or <b>24 hours after arrest</b>, whichever is <b>earlier</b>."],[16,"What is the basic limit on detention without charge?","<b>24 hours</b> from the relevant time."],[17,"When can a superintendent extend detention to 36 hours?","The offence is <b>indictable</b>, the investigation is conducted <b>diligently and expeditiously</b>, and detention is still <b>necessary</b>. Authorised <b>before 24 hours expire</b> and <b>not before the second review</b>."],[18,"How can detention be extended beyond 36 hours?","By a <b>warrant of further detention</b> from the <b>magistrates' court</b>, up to an absolute maximum of <b>96 hours</b> from the relevant time. The suspect may be present and <b>legally represented</b>."],[19,"When must reviews of detention take place?","The <b>first</b> no later than <b>6 hours</b> after detention was first <b>authorised</b>, then at intervals of no more than <b>9 hours</b>."]]},{"id":"police-interview","title":"Police interviews: advising the client, silence and vulnerable suspects","q":[[0,"What is the solicitor's role in the police station?",["Protect and advance the client's legal rights","Help the police find the truth","Persuade the client to confess"],"Under Code C Note 6D the solicitor's role is to <b>protect and advance</b> the client's legal rights."],[1,"Which CJPOA section covers failing to mention a fact later relied on?",["Section 34","Section 35","Section 38"],"<b>Section 34</b>: failing, when questioned under caution, to mention a fact later relied on in defence."],[2,"Which section covers failure to account for a mark on clothing?",["Section 36","Section 34","Section 35"],"<b>Section 36</b> covers objects, substances or marks. Section 37 covers presence at a place."],[3,"Which inferences need a special warning first?",["Sections 36 and 37","Sections 34 and 35","Section 34 only"],"<b>Sections 36 and 37</b> need a special warning. Section 34 does not."],[4,"Police have given only minimal disclosure. What is usually the safest advice?",["Say nothing (no comment)","Answer every question","Give a full written confession"],"With <b>minimal disclosure</b> the solicitor cannot advise properly, so silence or a prepared statement is usual."],[5,"A prepared statement avoids a section 34 inference only if it contains what?",["Every fact the client later relies on","The client's previous convictions","The police's own evidence"],"It protects only against facts <b>mentioned</b> in it. Any new fact at trial risks a s.34 inference."],[6,"Who cannot act as a child's appropriate adult?",["A witness to the offence","A social worker","A responsible adult over 18"],"Witnesses, victims, suspects, police and anyone told of admissions cannot be the <b>appropriate adult</b>."],[7,"A suspect appears to be 17. Is an appropriate adult needed?",["Yes, anyone under 18 needs one","No, only under 16","No, only if in care"],"Anyone who appears to be <b>under 18</b> is a juvenile and needs an appropriate adult."],[8,"When is the restricted caution used?",["Suspect was denied a solicitor before questioning","The suspect is under 18","The suspect is a foreign national"],"The <b>restricted caution</b> is used where no s.34 inference can be drawn, e.g. no chance to consult a solicitor."],[9,"How much continuous rest must a suspect get in any 24 hours?",["8 hours","6 hours","10 hours"],"Code C requires a continuous <b>8 hours'</b> rest, normally at night, free from questioning."],[10,"A client admits guilt privately but wants to give a false alibi. What must the solicitor do?",["Withdraw if the client insists","Give the alibi to the police","Tell the police the truth"],"The solicitor must <b>not mislead</b> anyone. If the client insists on a false account, the solicitor must withdraw."],[11,"Client stays silent on a solicitor's advice. Does that stop a s.34 inference?",["No, not automatically","Yes, always","Yes, if the solicitor attended"],"Under <b>Condron</b>, advice is relevant but jurors ask whether the client genuinely and reasonably relied on it."]],"c":[[35,"When might a prepared statement be the best advice?","The client has a defence but is <b>vulnerable or nervous</b>, or disclosure is limited. It protects against s.34 only if it contains <b>every fact</b> later relied on."],[37,"When may an inference be drawn under CJPOA s.34?","The suspect, questioned <b>under caution</b> or on charge, <b>fails to mention a fact</b> later <b>relied on</b> in their defence, which they could <b>reasonably have been expected to mention</b>."],[38,"What are ss.36 and 37 CJPOA?","<b>s.36</b>: failure to account for an <b>object, substance or mark</b>. <b>s.37</b>: failure to account for <b>presence at a place</b>. Both need a <b>special warning</b>."],[40,"When can no inference be drawn from silence at the police station (s.34(2A))?","Where the suspect had <b>not been allowed an opportunity to consult a solicitor</b> before questioning."],[41,"Can a defendant be convicted solely on an inference from silence?","<b>No</b> (s.38(3)). There must be other evidence giving a case to answer."],[43,"Does silence on legal advice prevent an inference?","<b>Not automatically.</b> The jury asks whether the defendant <b>genuinely and reasonably relied</b> on the advice, or used it as a <b>shield</b> because the defendant had no answer."],[44,"When does a defendant waive privilege about advice to remain silent?","Saying 'I was advised to stay silent' does <b>not</b> waive it. Giving the <b>reasons</b> for the advice does, exposing the defendant (and possibly the solicitor) to cross-examination."],[51,"What is the solicitor's role in the police station (Note 6D)?","Only to <b>protect and advance the legal rights</b> of the client, which may mean advising the client not to give evidence that strengthens the prosecution."],[52,"When can a solicitor intervene in an interview?","To seek <b>clarification</b>. To <b>challenge</b> an improper question or its manner. To <b>advise the client not to reply</b>. To ask for a <b>break</b> for further advice."],[55,"Who needs an appropriate adult?","Anyone who appears to be <b>under 18</b>, and a <b>vulnerable</b> adult who may have difficulty understanding or communicating, or be prone to <b>suggestion or compliance</b>. Any reason to suspect vulnerability is enough."]]},{"id":"identification","title":"Identification procedures (PACE Code D)","q":[[0,"What is the normal first-choice identification procedure?",["Video identification","Identification parade","Confrontation"],"<b>Video identification</b> is the first choice unless it is not practicable."],[1,"How many other people must appear in a video identification or parade?",["At least eight","At least four","At least twelve"],"The suspect is shown with at least <b>eight</b> others who resemble them."],[2,"Who runs an identification procedure?",["An inspector not involved in the case","The investigating officer","The custody sergeant"],"The <b>identification officer</b> is an inspector or above with no involvement in the investigation."],[3,"Which procedure is the last resort?",["Confrontation","Group identification","Video identification"],"<b>Confrontation</b> (witness sees the suspect alone) is used only if nothing else is practicable."],[4,"When may police show a witness photographs?",["Only if the suspect is not yet known","Whenever the witness asks","Once a suspect is arrested"],"Photographs are allowed only where the suspect is <b>unknown</b>. Once known, a formal procedure is needed."],[5,"The suspect admits being at the scene and no witness contradicts them. Is a procedure needed?",["No, it serves no useful purpose","Yes, always","Yes, a parade only"],"Where identity is not really disputed a procedure would serve <b>no useful purpose</b>."],[6,"A procedure must be held when the suspect is known and what else?",["A witness can identify and the suspect disputes it","The offence is indictable-only","The suspect asks for one"],"Para 3.12: a witness able to identify, and the suspect <b>disputes</b> being the person seen."],[7,"When must the first description be recorded?",["Before the witness takes part in a procedure","After the witness picks someone out","Only if the witness asks"],"The <b>first description</b> must be recorded beforehand so the defence can compare it with the suspect."],[8,"What is the usual remedy for a Code D breach?",["Application to exclude under s.78 PACE","Automatic exclusion","Automatic acquittal"],"A breach does not automatically exclude evidence. The defence applies under <b>s.78</b>."],[9,"Police show a witness one photo of the known suspect before a procedure. This is...",["A Code D breach","Good practice","Required by Code D"],"Showing photos after a suspect is known <b>breaches</b> Code D and may support a s.78 application."],[10,"A suspect refuses to take part in a procedure. What may police do?",["Proceed covertly","Drop the case","Force the suspect to attend"],"The identification officer may arrange a <b>covert</b> procedure, and the refusal may be given in evidence."],[11,"A witness has known the suspect for years and that is not disputed. Is a procedure needed?",["Usually not, it is recognition","Yes, a video identification","Yes, a confrontation"],"In a <b>recognition</b> case a procedure usually serves no useful purpose."]],"c":[[61,"When is a suspect a 'known suspect' for Code D?","When there is enough information to justify the <b>arrest</b> of a particular person for the offence."],[62,"Why must the witness's first description be recorded?","It must be recorded <b>before</b> any identification procedure, so the defence can compare it with the suspect's actual appearance."],[66,"When must an identification procedure be held (para 3.12)?","A witness has identified, or may be able to identify, the suspect. The <b>suspect disputes</b> being the person seen. And the witness has not already identified the suspect in a formal procedure."],[67,"When need no identification procedure be held?","Where it is <b>not practicable</b> or would <b>serve no useful purpose</b>: the suspect <b>admits being at the scene</b> and the eyewitness saw nothing contradicting their account, or it is <b>not disputed</b> that the suspect is <b>well known</b> to the witness."],[69,"Which identification procedure is the normal first choice?","<b>Video identification</b>, unless not practicable or a parade is practicable and <b>more suitable</b>."],[70,"What does a video identification involve?","Moving images of the suspect with at least <b>eight</b> others who resemble them in <b>age, height, general appearance and position in life</b>."],[75,"How must witnesses view a video identification?","Each views the set <b>at least twice</b>, <b>separately</b>, told the person <b>may or may not</b> be present. They must not confer or be told if they picked the suspect."],[78,"What can the police do if a suspect refuses a procedure?","Arrange a <b>covert</b> procedure, or as a last resort a <b>confrontation</b>. The refusal can be mentioned at trial."],[79,"Is identification evidence obtained in breach of Code D automatically inadmissible?","<b>No.</b> The defence can apply to <b>exclude</b> it under <b>s.78 PACE</b> (adverse effect on fairness)."]]},{"id":"bail","title":"Bail (Bail Act 1976)","q":[[0,"What is an unconvicted defendant's starting point on bail?",["A general right to bail","A presumption of custody","A matter of pure discretion"],"BA 1976 <b>s.4</b> gives a right to bail unless a Schedule 1 exception applies."],[1,"Which is a main ground for refusing bail on an imprisonable offence?",["Risk of interfering with witnesses","Previous convictions alone","Seriousness of the offence alone"],"The main grounds are failing to surrender, <b>offending on bail</b> and <b>interfering with witnesses</b>."],[2,"How strong must the court's belief be to rely on a main ground?",["Substantial grounds for believing","Mere suspicion","Proof beyond reasonable doubt"],"The test is <b>substantial grounds for believing</b> the risk would arise."],[3,"Who alone can grant bail to a defendant charged with murder?",["A Crown Court judge","Magistrates","The custody officer"],"In murder cases only a <b>Crown Court judge</b> can grant bail."],[4,"How must the prosecution start an appeal against a grant of bail?",["Oral notice before the defendant is released","Written notice within 14 days","Application to the magistrates later"],"Oral notice at the end of the hearing, then written notice within <b>2 hours</b>. The defendant stays in custody."],[5,"What does the defence need to appeal a refusal of bail to the Crown Court?",["A certificate of full argument","The prosecution's consent","A change of circumstances"],"Magistrates must issue a <b>certificate of full argument</b> after a full application."],[6,"Failing to surrender to bail without reasonable cause is...",["A criminal offence under s.6","Only a breach of condition","Contempt of court only"],"<b>Section 6</b> Bail Act 1976 makes it an offence to fail to surrender without reasonable cause."],[7,"A defendant breaks a curfew condition. What follows?",["Possible arrest under s.7(3)","A new criminal offence","Automatic prison"],"Breach of a condition is <b>not an offence</b>, but police can arrest and the court reconsiders bail."],[8,"Which condition best meets a risk of absconding abroad?",["Surrender of passport","Non-contact with witnesses","Exclusion zone"],"Handing in the <b>passport</b> targets the specific risk of fleeing the country."],[9,"Which condition best meets a risk of witness interference?",["Non-contact with named witnesses","Reporting to a police station","Surety"],"A <b>non-contact</b> condition matches the risk. Reporting and surety address absconding."],[10,"What is a surety?",["Someone promising to pay if the defendant absconds","Money the defendant deposits","A promise by the defendant to attend"],"A <b>surety</b> is a third party who pledges a sum (recognisance) if the defendant fails to surrender."],[11,"Bail is refused at the first hearing. What can the defendant do at the next hearing?",["Make a full fresh application","Argue only new points","Wait for trial"],"The defendant gets <b>two full applications</b>. After that, only new arguments or changed circumstances."]],"c":[[84,"Who has a right to bail under s.4 Bail Act 1976?","An accused person at any stage <b>before conviction</b>, and a convicted person whose case is <b>adjourned for reports</b> before sentence."],[88,"What are the three main grounds for refusing bail for an indictable imprisonable offence?","Substantial grounds for believing the defendant would <b>fail to surrender</b>, <b>commit an offence</b> on bail, or <b>interfere with witnesses</b> or obstruct justice."],[91,"What is the 'no real prospect of custody' rule after 22 March 2026?","For the defendant aged <b>18+</b>, the main grounds, offence-on-bail and s.7 arrest exceptions <b>do not apply</b> if there is <b>no real prospect of custody other than a suspended sentence</b>. It now applies <b>after conviction</b> too."],[92,"What factors does the court consider on bail (para 9)?","<b>Nature and seriousness</b> and likely sentence. <b>Character, antecedents, associations, community ties</b>. Record on <b>previous bail</b>. <b>Strength of evidence</b>. <b>Risk of harm</b>. Since 2026: <b>pregnancy</b>, being a <b>primary carer</b>, being a <b>domestic abuse victim</b>."],[96,"When can the court impose bail conditions (s.3(6))?","Where <b>necessary</b> to secure surrender, prevent offending or interference, for the defendant's protection, or to make the defendant available for <b>reports</b> or to see their <b>lawyer</b>. They must be <b>proportionate</b>."],[102,"How many full bail applications can the defendant make?","Effectively <b>two</b>: at the first hearing and the next. After that, arguments <b>heard before</b> need not be heard; the defendant needs a <b>change in circumstances</b> or new argument."],[104,"Where does the defence appeal a magistrates' refusal of bail?","To the <b>Crown Court</b> (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.81), with the <b>certificate of full argument</b>. Certain conditions can also be appealed there (CJA 2003 s.16)."],[105,"What is the procedure for a prosecution appeal against a grant of bail?","For an <b>imprisonable</b> offence: <b>oral notice</b> at the end of the hearing <b>before the defendant is released</b>, <b>written notice within 2 hours</b>, the defendant stays in custody, and the appeal is heard within <b>48 hours</b>."],[106,"What are the consequences of failing to surrender without reasonable cause?","A <b>s.6 offence</b>: up to <b>3 months</b> in the magistrates' court or <b>12 months</b> in the Crown Court. A <b>warrant</b> will usually issue."],[107,"Is breaching a bail condition an offence?","<b>No.</b> Police may <b>arrest under s.7(3)</b>. The defendant must be brought before a magistrate <b>within 24 hours</b>, who may remand in custody or re-grant bail."]]},{"id":"first-hearings","title":"First hearings, funding and classification of offences","q":[[0,"In which court does every criminal case start?",["Magistrates' court","Crown Court","Youth court"],"Every case begins in the <b>magistrates' court</b>, however serious the offence."],[1,"An adult is charged with an indictable-only offence. What happens at the first hearing?",["Sent to the Crown Court, no plea taken","Plea before venue","Allocation decision"],"Under CDA 1998 <b>s.51</b> the case is sent forthwith. There is no plea before venue or allocation."],[2,"Which offence is indictable-only?",["Robbery","Theft","Common assault"],"<b>Robbery</b> is indictable-only. Theft is either way and common assault is summary-only."],[3,"Which offence is triable either way?",["Assault occasioning actual bodily harm","Murder","Common assault"],"<b>ABH</b> (OAPA s.47) is either way. Murder is indictable-only and common assault summary-only."],[4,"In PACE, 'indictable offence' includes which offences?",["Either-way and indictable-only","Indictable-only only","Summary-only offences"],"<b>Indictable</b> covers both either-way and indictable-only offences. This matters for delaying rights."],[5,"A taxi driver is charged with theft. Which legal aid factor fits best?",["Loss of livelihood","Substantial question of law","Unable to understand proceedings"],"A conviction could cost the driver a licence and job, so <b>livelihood</b> applies."],[6,"What two tests must be passed for a representation order?",["Interests of justice and means","Merits and urgency","Liberty and reputation"],"Legal aid needs the <b>interests of justice</b> test and the <b>means</b> test."],[7,"How is the interests of justice test met in the Crown Court?",["Automatically","By showing a risk of custody","By proving a point of law"],"In the <b>Crown Court</b> the interests of justice test is automatically met."],[8,"When must the prosecution serve IDPC?",["At or before the first hearing","14 days after the first hearing","At the PTPH"],"<b>Initial details of the prosecution case</b> are served at or before the first hearing."],[9,"What is the maximum guilty plea reduction at the first stage?",["One third","One quarter","One half"],"A plea indicated at the <b>first stage</b> earns up to <b>one third</b> off."],[10,"Is the court duty solicitor means tested?",["No, it is free","Yes, always","Only in the Crown Court"],"The <b>duty solicitor</b> at court is free and not means tested."],[11,"The client admits guilt but wants a not guilty plea. The solicitor may...",["Put the prosecution to proof","Call a false alibi witness","Refuse to act"],"The solicitor can test the evidence but cannot advance a positive defence known to be <b>false</b>."]],"c":[[109,"What are the three classifications of offence?","<b>Summary only</b> (magistrates only). <b>Either way</b> (magistrates or Crown Court). <b>Indictable only</b> (Crown Court only)."],[115,"What two tests must the defendant pass for a representation order?","The <b>interests of justice</b> test and the <b>means</b> test."],[116,"What are the interests of justice factors (LASPO s.17(2))?","Likely loss of <b>liberty</b>. Loss of <b>livelihood</b>. Serious damage to <b>reputation</b>. A substantial <b>question of law</b>. The defendant <b>unable to understand</b> the proceedings. <b>Tracing, interviewing or expert cross-examination</b> of witnesses. The <b>interests of another person</b>."],[118,"When is the interests of justice test automatically met?","In the <b>Crown Court</b>."],[121,"What are initial details of the prosecution case (IDPC) and when must they be served?","Served <b>at or before the first hearing</b>: a <b>summary</b> of the offence, the defendant's <b>interview</b> account, material <b>statements and exhibits</b>, the defendant's <b>previous convictions</b> and any <b>victim personal statement</b>."],[123,"What happens at the first hearing for an either-way offence?","<b>Plea before venue</b>. A guilty indication leads to sentence or <b>committal for sentence</b>. Otherwise <b>allocation</b>, and the defendant may still <b>elect</b> Crown Court trial."],[124,"What happens at the first hearing for an indictable-only offence?","<b>No plea is taken</b>. The court <b>sends</b> the defendant to the Crown Court at once (s.51 CDA 1998) and decides <b>bail</b>. The next hearing is the <b>PTPH</b>."],[127,"Must the defence solicitor volunteer the client's previous convictions?","<b>No</b>, but must <b>not mislead</b> the court, for example by saying the defendant has none."]]},{"id":"allocation-sending","title":"Plea before venue, allocation and sending","q":[[0,"For an adult either-way offence, what comes first?",["Plea before venue","Allocation","Election"],"The defendant <b>indicates a plea</b> first (MCA s.17A), then allocation if not guilty."],[1,"The defendant indicates guilty at plea before venue. What happens?",["Treated as a guilty plea, then sentence","Case goes to the Crown Court for trial","Allocation follows"],"A guilty indication is a <b>guilty plea</b>. The court sentences or commits to the Crown Court for sentence."],[2,"Who decides if an either-way case is suitable for summary trial?",["The magistrates","The defendant","The CPS"],"The <b>court</b> decides suitability. The defendant chooses only if it says suitable."],[3,"The magistrates decide the case is unsuitable for summary trial. The defendant...",["Is sent to the Crown Court","Can still choose magistrates","Can ask for a re-hearing"],"If the court declines jurisdiction the defendant has <b>no choice</b> and is sent."],[4,"The magistrates accept jurisdiction. What may the defendant do?",["Consent to summary trial or elect Crown Court","Only consent to summary trial","Only be sent to the Crown Court"],"If suitable, the defendant can <b>consent</b> to summary trial or <b>elect</b> jury trial."],[5,"What is the magistrates' maximum custody for one summary offence?",["6 months","12 months","3 months"],"Summary offences carry a maximum of <b>6 months</b> (or the offence's own lower limit)."],[6,"Criminal damage of £5,000 or less: can the defendant elect Crown Court trial?",["No, tried as if summary only","Yes, always","Yes, if on bail"],"Low-value damage under MCA <b>s.22</b> is tried as if summary only, with no election."],[7,"After an indication of sentence, the defendant pleads guilty. If non-custodial was indicated...",["Custody cannot then be imposed","Custody can still be imposed","The plea is withdrawn"],"The court cannot impose <b>custody</b> if it indicated a non-custodial sentence."],[8,"Why might a client challenging a confession prefer the Crown Court?",["The jury never hears excluded evidence","Sentences are lower","It is quicker"],"The <b>judge</b> rules on admissibility in the jury's absence. Magistrates hear it before ruling."],[9,"An adult is charged with robbery and a related theft. What happens to the theft?",["Sent too, no plea before venue","Plea before venue first","Dealt with by magistrates"],"A related either-way offence is <b>sent</b> with the indictable-only offence (s.50A)."],[10,"Magistrates feel their powers are too low after a guilty indication. They...",["Commit to the Crown Court for sentence","Send for jury trial","Impose the maximum anyway"],"They can <b>commit for sentence</b> under Sentencing Code s.14. The defendant still gets full plea credit."],[11,"Which reason makes a case unsuitable for summary trial?",["Sentence clearly beyond magistrates' powers","The defendant prefers a jury","The trial will be long"],"Under the Allocation guideline, summary trial is usual unless the likely sentence <b>clearly exceeds</b> their powers or the case is unusually complex."]],"c":[[130,"What is the order of events for an adult charged with an either-way offence?","Charge read. <b>Plea before venue</b>. If not guilty or no indication, <b>allocation</b>. If suitable, the defendant may seek an <b>indication of sentence</b>, then <b>consents</b> to summary trial or <b>elects</b> Crown Court trial."],[131,"What happens if the defendant indicates guilty at plea before venue?","It is treated as a <b>guilty plea</b>. The magistrates sentence, or <b>commit to the Crown Court for sentence</b> (s.14 Sentencing Code) if their powers are inadequate. The defendant still gets <b>one third</b> credit."],[133,"When does the Allocation guideline say an either-way case should go to the Crown Court?","When the likely sentence would <b>clearly exceed</b> the magistrates' powers (after mitigation and guilty plea credit), or the case has unusual <b>legal, procedural or factual complexity</b>. Otherwise try it summarily."],[134,"What happens if the magistrates decide an either-way case is unsuitable for summary trial?","The defendant is <b>sent</b> to the Crown Court. The defendant has <b>no choice</b>."],[136,"What is the effect of an indication of sentence (s.20)?","The court <b>may</b> say whether custody or a non-custodial sentence is more likely on a guilty plea. If it indicates non-custodial and the defendant pleads guilty, it <b>cannot impose custody</b> or commit for sentence on that basis. Otherwise it is <b>not binding</b>."],[138,"How is criminal damage of £5,000 or less tried?","<b>As if summary only</b>: the defendant <b>cannot elect</b>. Maximum <b>3 months</b> and a level 4 fine. Not arson or aggravated damage."],[142,"Which related offences can be sent with an indictable-only offence?","Related <b>either-way</b> offences. Related <b>summary</b> offences only if <b>imprisonable</b> or carrying <b>disqualification</b>. An adult co-defendant's related either-way offence."],[144,"What are the magistrates' maximum custodial powers?","<b>6 months</b> for a summary offence. <b>12 months</b> for a single either-way offence (since 18 November 2024). <b>3 months</b> for low-value criminal damage."]]},{"id":"case-management-disclosure","title":"Case management and disclosure","q":[[0,"Is a defence statement compulsory in the Crown Court?",["Yes","No, it is voluntary","Only for alibi defences"],"Under CPIA s.5 a <b>defence statement</b> is compulsory in the Crown Court and voluntary in the magistrates' court."],[1,"How long does the defence have to serve a Crown Court defence statement?",["28 days from initial disclosure","14 days from initial disclosure","7 days from the PTPH"],"The Crown Court limit is <b>28 days</b> from the prosecution's initial disclosure."],[2,"What is the defence statement time limit in the magistrates' court?",["14 days","28 days","7 days"],"A voluntary magistrates' court defence statement is due within <b>14 days</b> of initial disclosure."],[3,"What is unused material?",["Material the prosecution does not rely on","Evidence the defence will not call","Evidence ruled inadmissible"],"<b>Unused material</b> is gathered in the investigation but not relied on by the prosecution."],[4,"What is the test for prosecution initial disclosure?",["Might undermine the prosecution or assist the defence","Anything relevant to the case","Anything the defence requests"],"CPIA s.3: material <b>reasonably capable</b> of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence."],[5,"At trial the defendant runs a defence not in the defence statement. What follows?",["Comment and possible adverse inferences","The defence is excluded","Automatic conviction"],"Under <b>s.11</b> the court or jury may comment and draw inferences. The defence is not excluded."],[6,"When can the defence make a section 8 disclosure application?",["Only after serving a defence statement","Any time before trial","Only in the magistrates' court"],"A <b>s.8</b> application for specific disclosure requires a defence statement first."],[7,"Which unused material might be withheld on public interest immunity grounds?",["An informant's identity","The defendant's interview","A prosecution witness statement"],"<b>PII</b> protects sensitive material, such as an informant's identity, where disclosure risks serious prejudice."],[8,"What happens at the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing (PTPH)?",["The defendant is arraigned and a timetable set","The trial takes place","Sentence is passed"],"At the PTPH the indictment is put to the defendant and the court fixes <b>trial dates</b> and stage dates."],[9,"What must a defence statement give for an alibi witness?",["Name, address and date of birth","Name only","A signed witness statement"],"An <b>alibi</b> needs the witness's name, address and date of birth, or as much as is known."],[10,"Which form records issues and witnesses at a magistrates' not guilty first hearing?",["Preparation for Effective Trial form","Plea and Trial Preparation form","Initial Disclosure form"],"The <b>PET form</b> is used in the magistrates' court. The PTPH form is Crown Court."],[11,"What does the MG6D schedule list?",["Sensitive unused material","Non-sensitive unused material","Used material"],"The <b>MG6D</b> lists sensitive unused material and is not given to the defence. MG6C lists non-sensitive."]],"c":[[149,"What is the overriding objective of the Criminal Procedure Rules?","Dealing with cases <b>justly</b>: acquitting the innocent and convicting the guilty, fairness, <b>Article 6</b> rights, respecting witnesses and victims, <b>efficiency</b>, information on bail and sentence, and proportionality to gravity and complexity."],[154,"What is the first Crown Court hearing after sending and when is it held?","The <b>Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing (PTPH)</b>, normally within <b>28 days</b> of sending."],[160,"What is the prosecution's initial disclosure test (CPIA s.3)?","Material <b>not previously disclosed</b> that <b>might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence</b>."],[161,"When is initial disclosure made in each court?","<b>Magistrates</b>: once the defendant pleads <b>not guilty</b>. <b>Crown Court</b>: after sending, usually with the prosecution case."],[162,"When can sensitive material meeting the test be withheld?","Only by <b>court order</b> on <b>public interest immunity</b> grounds. If fairness requires disclosure, the prosecution must disclose or <b>drop</b> the case."],[163,"Is a defence statement compulsory?","<b>Compulsory</b> in the <b>Crown Court</b> (s.5), within <b>28 days</b> of initial disclosure. <b>Voluntary</b> in the <b>magistrates' court</b> (s.6), within <b>14 days</b>."],[165,"What must a defence statement contain (s.6A)?","The <b>nature of the defence</b> and particular defences. Facts <b>in issue</b> and why. <b>Facts relied on</b>. <b>Points of law</b>, including admissibility and abuse of process. <b>Alibi</b> witness details."],[169,"When can the defendant make a s.8 application for specific disclosure?","Only <b>after serving a defence statement</b>, where the defendant has <b>reasonable cause to believe</b> material should have been disclosed."],[170,"What are faults in defence disclosure (s.11)?","No or <b>late</b> statement where compulsory. <b>Inconsistent</b> defences. Running a <b>defence, fact or alibi not mentioned</b>. No witness notice, or calling a witness not on it."],[171,"What are the consequences of a fault in defence disclosure?","<b>Comment</b> and <b>adverse inferences</b>. The defence evidence is <b>not excluded</b>, and the defendant cannot be convicted <b>solely</b> on the inference."]]},{"id":"evidence-identification-silence","title":"Evidence: identification (Turnbull) and adverse inferences","q":[[0,"Who bears the legal burden of proof in a criminal trial?",["The prosecution","The defendant","The judge"],"<b>Woolmington</b>: the prosecution must prove guilt, subject to rare statutory exceptions."],[1,"What standard of proof must the prosecution meet?",["Beyond reasonable doubt","Balance of probabilities","Clear and convincing evidence"],"The jury must be <b>sure</b> of guilt, i.e. beyond reasonable doubt."],[2,"What must the defendant prove for the defence of insanity?",["On the balance of probabilities","Beyond reasonable doubt","Nothing, only raise it"],"Insanity is a rare <b>reverse legal burden</b>: the defendant proves it on the balance of probabilities."],[3,"The defendant raises self-defence with some evidence. What now?",["Prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt","Defendant must prove it","The defence fails"],"The defendant meets the <b>evidential burden</b>. The prosecution then disproves it to the criminal standard."],[4,"When do the Turnbull guidelines apply?",["Case rests on disputed identification","Defendant admits being present","Witness is said to be lying"],"<b>Turnbull</b> applies where the case depends wholly or substantially on identification alleged to be mistaken."],[5,"In ADVOKATE, what does K ask?",["Did the witness know or see the person before","Was the witness knocked down","How reliable is the witness's knowledge"],"<b>K</b> is <b>Known or seen before</b>. Recognition is stronger but can still be wrong."],[6,"Poor identification, no supporting evidence. What should the judge do?",["Withdraw the case and direct acquittal","Give a Turnbull warning only","Leave it to the jury"],"Poor and <b>unsupported</b> identification should be withdrawn from the jury."],[7,"Why does Turnbull not apply if the defendant admits being there?",["Identity is not in dispute","The witness is more reliable","Recognition is always safe"],"Turnbull targets <b>mistaken identity</b>. If presence is admitted, the dispute is about what happened."],[8,"Section 35 allows an inference if the defendant...",["Does not give evidence at trial","Stays silent in interview","Refuses to be fingerprinted"],"<b>Section 35</b> covers failure to testify, after the defendant has been warned of the consequences."],[9,"Which sections require a special warning?",["Sections 36 and 37","Sections 34 and 35","Section 35 only"],"The <b>special warning</b> is needed for failing to account for objects, marks or presence."],[10,"Can a conviction rest solely on an adverse inference?",["No","Yes, if the jury is sure","Yes, under s.34"],"<b>Section 38(3)</b> bars a conviction based solely on an inference from silence."],[11,"No comment in interview, then at trial the defendant just puts the prosecution to proof. Section 34 inference?",["No, no fact is relied on","Yes","Yes, if advised to stay silent"],"<b>Section 34</b> needs a fact later relied on. Putting the prosecution to proof relies on none."]],"c":[[173,"Who bears the legal burden in a criminal trial, and to what standard?","The <b>prosecution</b>, on every element and to disprove defences, <b>beyond reasonable doubt</b>: the jury must be <b>sure</b>."],[174,"What burden does the defendant bear for defences such as self-defence or duress?","Usually only an <b>evidential</b> burden. The prosecution must then <b>disprove</b> the defence beyond reasonable doubt."],[175,"When does the defendant bear a legal burden, and to what standard?","In <b>insanity</b>, <b>diminished responsibility</b> and some <b>statutory</b> defences (e.g. good reason for carrying a blade), on the <b>balance of probabilities</b>."],[177,"When does the Turnbull warning apply?","Where the case depends <b>wholly or substantially</b> on the correctness of an <b>identification</b> the defence says is <b>mistaken</b>. It applies to <b>recognition</b> too."],[178,"When does Turnbull not apply?","Where the defendant <b>admits being present</b> and disputes what happened, or the defence is that the witness is <b>lying</b>, not mistaken."],[179,"What must a Turnbull warning contain?","The <b>special need for caution</b>. <b>Why</b>: a mistaken witness can be convincing and several can all be wrong. Examine the <b>circumstances</b>. Remind of <b>weaknesses</b>. Identify <b>supporting</b> evidence."],[183,"What should the judge do with a poor, unsupported identification?","<b>Withdraw</b> the case and <b>direct an acquittal</b>, normally on a <b>submission of no case to answer</b>."],[185,"When may an inference be drawn under s.35 CJPOA?","When the defendant <b>does not give evidence</b> or refuses without good cause to answer questions, having been told of the right and the consequences, where guilt is in issue and the defendant's <b>condition</b> does not make giving evidence undesirable."],[188,"Can an inference be drawn where the defendant gave a prepared statement then said 'no comment'?","<b>Not</b> if the statement contained <b>all the facts</b> relied on at trial. An inference is possible on facts <b>not</b> in it."],[190,"What must the jury find before drawing a s.35 inference?","That the prosecution has a <b>case to answer</b>, and that silence can only sensibly be attributed to the defendant having <b>no answer</b> that would stand up to cross-examination."]]},{"id":"hearsay-confessions","title":"Hearsay, confessions and exclusion of evidence","q":[[0,"What is hearsay under the CJA 2003?",["An out-of-court statement used to prove what it says","Any statement by a police officer","Evidence from a witness's memory"],"A statement not made in oral evidence, relied on as evidence of the matter stated."],[1,"A witness repeats what Ella said, to prove the defendant stabbed the victim. Is this hearsay?",["Yes","No, Ella is identified","No, it is relevant"],"It is an out-of-court statement used for its <b>truth</b>, so it is hearsay."],[2,"A threat is repeated in court to show it was made. Is it hearsay?",["No, it is not used for its truth","Yes, always","Yes, unless agreed"],"If used to show the words <b>were said</b>, not that they were true, it is not hearsay."],[3,"Witness too frightened to attend. Hearsay is admissible if...",["The court gives leave","The witness agrees","The police agree"],"<b>Section 116(2)(e)</b> needs the court's leave, given if in the interests of justice."],[4,"A witness has died. Can their first-hand statement be admitted?",["Yes, under s.116","No, never","Only if the defence agrees"],"Death is one of the five <b>s.116</b> reasons for unavailability."],[5,"Which section covers business documents as hearsay?",["Section 117","Section 116","Section 119"],"<b>Section 117</b> covers documents created in the course of business with a supplier who had personal knowledge."],[6,"Res gestae statements are admissible when the speaker was...",["Emotionally overpowered by the event","Speaking to the police","Under oath"],"The excitement must exclude any real chance of <b>concoction or distortion</b>."],[7,"What are the two grounds under s.76(2) for excluding a confession?",["Oppression or unreliability","Delay or unfairness","Oppression or inducement only"],"<b>Section 76(2)</b>: obtained by oppression, or by something likely to make any confession unreliable."],[8,"Under s.76, who must prove the confession was properly obtained?",["The prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt","The defence, on the balance","The judge decides alone"],"Once the defence raises s.76, the <b>prosecution</b> must disprove it to the criminal standard."],[9,"How does s.78 PACE differ from s.76?",["It is a discretion covering any prosecution evidence","It is mandatory","It covers confessions only"],"<b>Section 78</b> is discretionary and applies to all prosecution evidence, not just confessions."],[10,"A suspect confessed after police promised bail. Best argument?",["Section 76(2)(b), unreliability","Section 76(2)(a), oppression","Section 116"],"A promise of this kind is something said or done likely to make a confession <b>unreliable</b>."],[11,"A confession is excluded. Can facts found as a result be used?",["Yes, under s.76(4)","No, never","Only if the defendant agrees"],"Facts discovered because of an excluded confession remain <b>admissible</b> (s.76(4))."]],"c":[[194,"What is hearsay (CJA 2003 ss.114-115)?","A <b>statement not made in oral evidence</b> in the proceedings, relied on as evidence of <b>any matter stated</b>, where a purpose of the maker was to cause someone to <b>believe</b> or <b>act</b> on it."],[197,"What are the four routes to admitting hearsay (s.114(1))?","<b>(a)</b> A <b>statutory provision</b>. <b>(b)</b> A <b>preserved common law</b> rule. <b>(c)</b> All parties <b>agree</b>. <b>(d)</b> The <b>interests of justice</b>."],[200,"What are the conditions for admitting hearsay under s.116?","A <b>first-hand</b> statement, the maker's <b>oral evidence would have been admissible</b>, the maker is <b>identified</b>, and one of <b>five reasons</b> for unavailability applies."],[201,"What are the five s.116 reasons for unavailability?","<b>Dead</b>. <b>Unfit</b> through bodily or mental condition. <b>Outside the UK</b> and attendance not reasonably practicable. <b>Cannot be found</b> despite reasonable steps. Does not testify through <b>fear</b>."],[202,"What extra requirement applies to the fear ground?","The <b>court's leave</b>, given only in the <b>interests of justice</b>, considering the statement, unfairness and whether <b>special measures</b> would help. 'Fear' is widely construed."],[212,"What is a confession (s.82(1) PACE)?","Any statement <b>wholly or partly adverse</b> to its maker, in <b>words or otherwise</b>. A <b>mixed statement</b> counts, and the whole goes before the jury."],[214,"What are the grounds for mandatory exclusion of a confession under s.76(2)?","<b>(a) Oppression</b>. <b>(b)</b> Anything <b>said or done</b> likely to make <b>any</b> confession <b>unreliable</b> in the circumstances."],[215,"Who must prove what once s.76 is raised?","The <b>prosecution</b> must prove <b>beyond reasonable doubt</b> that the confession was <b>not</b> so obtained. Its <b>truth</b> is irrelevant."],[216,"What is oppression?","Includes <b>torture</b>, <b>inhuman or degrading treatment</b> and the <b>use or threat of violence</b>. Also exercise of power in a <b>burdensome, harsh or wrongful</b> manner. Upsetting but true information is not enough."],[217,"What typically makes a confession unreliable under s.76(2)(b)?","A <b>promise</b> such as bail. Interviewing a <b>vulnerable</b> suspect without an <b>appropriate adult</b> or solicitor. Interviewing someone <b>unfit</b>. Wrongful denial of <b>legal advice</b>."],[221,"What does s.78 PACE allow?","The court <b>may refuse</b> to admit <b>prosecution</b> evidence if, in all the circumstances including how it was obtained, admission would have such an <b>adverse effect on fairness</b> that it ought not to be admitted."]]},{"id":"bad-character","title":"Bad character (CJA 2003)","q":[[0,"How many gateways are there for the defendant's bad character?",["Seven","Five","Four"],"Section 101(1) lists gateways <b>(a) to (g)</b>."],[1,"Which gateway covers propensity to commit offences of the kind charged?",["(d)","(c)","(f)"],"<b>Gateway (d)</b>: an important matter in issue between the defendant and the prosecution."],[2,"Which gateway can only be used by a co-defendant?",["(e)","(d)","(g)"],"<b>Gateway (e)</b> is for evidence of substantial probative value as against a co-defendant."],[3,"Who alone can use gateway (g)?",["The prosecution","A co-defendant","The defendant"],"<b>Gateway (g)</b> (attack on another person's character) is prosecution only."],[4,"The defendant says the police planted the drugs. Which gateway opens?",["(g), attack on another's character","(f), false impression","(e), co-defendant"],"Alleging police fabrication is an <b>attack on character</b>, opening gateway (g)."],[5,"The defendant falsely says 'I've never been in trouble'. Which gateway?",["(f), false impression","(g), attack on character","(d), propensity"],"A false or misleading impression about the defendant opens <b>gateway (f)</b>."],[6,"Under Hanson, when does a single conviction show propensity?",["If it shows unusual behaviour","Never","Always"],"One conviction rarely shows propensity unless it reveals <b>unusual behaviour</b> or a distinctive method."],[7,"Evidence of a threat made on the night of the alleged assault is...",["Not bad character, it relates to the facts","Bad character under gateway (c)","Bad character under gateway (d)"],"Evidence that has to do with the <b>alleged facts</b> falls outside the bad character rules."],[8,"On the defendant's application, s.101(3) can exclude which gateways?",["(d) and (g)","(a) and (b)","(e) and (f)"],"<b>Section 101(3)</b> applies only to gateways <b>(d) and (g)</b>, where admission would be unfair."],[9,"What is needed to adduce a prosecution witness's bad character?",["Leave of the court","Notice only","The witness's consent"],"Under <b>s.100</b> non-defendant bad character needs leave unless all parties agree."],[10,"What does 'misconduct' cover in the bad character rules?",["An offence or other reprehensible behaviour","Convictions only","Convictions and cautions only"],"<b>Misconduct</b> (s.112) means an offence or other reprehensible behaviour."],[11,"Kim, charged with shoplifting, has five shoplifting convictions. Which gateway?",["(d), propensity","(c), explanatory evidence","(f), false impression"],"Repeated like offences show <b>propensity</b> to commit offences of the kind charged."]],"c":[[226,"What is bad character evidence (s.98 CJA 2003)?","Evidence of, or of a <b>disposition towards</b>, <b>misconduct</b> on the person's part."],[230,"What evidence is excluded from the bad character definition?","Evidence that <b>has to do with the alleged facts</b> of the offence. Misconduct <b>in connection with the investigation or prosecution</b> of that offence. It is admissible in the ordinary way if relevant."],[235,"What is gateway (c), important explanatory evidence (s.102)?","Without it the jury would find it <b>impossible or difficult properly to understand</b> other evidence, <b>and</b> its value for understanding the case as a whole is <b>substantial</b>."],[236,"What is gateway (d) and who can use it?","Relevant to an <b>important matter in issue</b> between the defendant and the <b>prosecution</b>, including <b>propensity</b>. <b>Prosecution only</b>."],[237,"What is gateway (e) and who can use it?","<b>Substantial probative value</b> on an <b>important matter in issue</b> between the defendant and a <b>co-defendant</b>. <b>Co-defendant only</b>."],[238,"What is gateway (f) and who can use it?","To <b>correct a false impression</b> given by or for the defendant. <b>Prosecution only</b>."],[239,"What is gateway (g) and who can use it?","The defendant has made an <b>attack on another person's character</b>. <b>Prosecution only</b>."],[240,"What two propensities can gateway (d) show?","<b>Propensity to commit offences of the kind charged</b> and <b>propensity to be untruthful</b>."],[241,"How can propensity to commit offences of the kind charged be shown?","Previous convictions for an offence of the <b>same description</b> or the <b>same category</b>."],[246,"What three questions must the judge ask about propensity (Hanson)?","Does the <b>history</b> establish a <b>propensity</b>? Does it make it <b>more likely</b> the defendant committed this offence? Is it <b>just</b> to rely on the convictions, and would the proceedings be <b>unfair</b>?"],[253,"When does the defendant attack another person's character (s.106)?","By <b>adducing evidence</b> or <b>asking questions</b> intended or likely to show the person <b>committed an offence</b> or <b>behaved reprehensibly</b>, or making such an <b>imputation</b> in interview or on charge."],[258,"When must the court exclude bad character under s.101(3)?","For gateways <b>(d) and (g)</b> only, <b>on the defendant's application</b>, if admission would have <b>such an adverse effect on fairness</b> that it ought not to be admitted."]]},{"id":"trial-procedure","title":"Trial procedure, witnesses and the Welsh language","q":[[0,"In a Crown Court trial, who decides the facts?",["The jury","The judge","The prosecutor"],"The <b>jury</b> decides facts and the judge decides law and sentence."],[1,"Who makes the closing speech last?",["The defence","The prosecution","The judge"],"The prosecution closes first, so the <b>defence</b> has the last word."],[2,"What is the Galbraith test about?",["Submission of no case to answer","Admissibility of confessions","Jury majority verdicts"],"<b>Galbraith</b>: the judge stops the case if the evidence is non-existent or too tenuous for a jury to convict."],[3,"Which questions are not allowed in examination-in-chief?",["Leading questions","Open questions","Questions about the facts"],"A <b>leading question</b> suggests the answer. Leading is for cross-examination."],[4,"What may a re-examination cover?",["Only matters raised in cross-examination","Any new topic","Only the witness's character"],"<b>Re-examination</b> clarifies points arising from cross-examination. No new topics."],[5,"What is the least jury majority that can convict (12 jurors)?",["10 to 2","9 to 3","8 to 4"],"After enough deliberation a majority of <b>10-2</b> or better can convict."],[6,"When does the defendant give evidence in the defence case?",["First, before other defence witnesses","Last","Whenever the defence chooses"],"Under PACE <b>s.79</b> the defendant testifies before other defence witnesses."],[7,"A husband is charged with assaulting his wife. Is she compellable for the prosecution?",["Yes","No","Only if she agrees"],"A spouse is compellable for the prosecution for assault or injury to <b>the spouse</b>, a child or a sexual offence against a child."],[8,"Can the defendant be called as a prosecution witness?",["No","Yes, if compelled","Yes, on a co-defendant's application"],"The defendant is <b>not competent</b> for the prosecution and cannot be compelled to give evidence."],[9,"Which special measure stops a witness seeing the defendant?",["Screens","Live link","Intermediary"],"<b>Screens</b> block the witness's view of the defendant but not the judge's or jury's."],[10,"What are the two limbs of a good character direction?",["Credibility and propensity","Motive and opportunity","Credibility and mitigation"],"Under <b>Vye</b>, good character goes to credibility and to lack of propensity."],[11,"In a Welsh court, a defence witness will give evidence in Welsh. What should the defence do?",["Tell the court at the earliest opportunity","Wait until the trial day","Ask for an English witness instead"],"Anyone may speak <b>Welsh</b> in court in Wales (Welsh Language Act 1993). Early notice lets the court arrange <b>interpretation</b>."]],"c":[[271,"What is the order of a criminal trial?","<b>Prosecution opening</b>. <b>Prosecution evidence</b>. Possible <b>no case to answer</b> submission. <b>Defence case</b>. <b>Closing speeches</b>. <b>Summing up</b> (Crown Court). <b>Verdict</b>. Sentence if convicted."],[279,"When can a majority verdict be accepted?","After at least <b>2 hours and 10 minutes</b> of deliberation, having first tried for <b>unanimity</b>."],[280,"What majorities are allowed?","<b>11-1 or 10-2</b> with 12 jurors. <b>10-1</b> with 11. <b>9-1</b> with 10. A jury of <b>9</b> must be <b>unanimous</b>."],[282,"When must the judge stop the case on a submission of no case to answer?","<b>(1)</b> There is <b>no evidence</b> that the defendant committed the crime. <b>(2)</b> The evidence is <b>tenuous</b> (weak, vague or inconsistent) so that, taken at its <b>highest</b>, a jury could <b>not properly convict</b> (Galbraith)."],[293,"What is the competence test (YJCEA s.53)?","Everyone is competent unless they cannot <b>understand questions</b> as a witness and give <b>answers that can be understood</b>. Age alone does not matter."],[295,"When does a witness give sworn evidence (s.55)?","Aged <b>14 or over</b> and understands the <b>solemnity</b> of the occasion and the responsibility to tell the truth. Otherwise evidence is <b>unsworn</b>."],[296,"Is the defendant competent and compellable?","<b>Not competent for the prosecution</b>. <b>Competent for the defence</b>. <b>Not compellable</b>."],[298,"Is the defendant's spouse or civil partner competent and compellable (PACE s.80)?","<b>Competent</b> for all. <b>Compellable for the defendant</b> (unless jointly charged). Compellable for the <b>prosecution</b> or a co-defendant <b>only for specified offences</b>."],[304,"Who is a vulnerable witness (s.16)?","<b>Under 18</b> at the hearing, or evidence likely diminished by <b>mental disorder</b>, impaired <b>intelligence and social functioning</b>, or <b>physical disability</b>."],[310,"What are the two limbs of a good character direction?","<b>Credibility</b>: the defendant more likely truthful. <b>Propensity</b>: the defendant less likely to have offended."],[325,"The court wrongly states the defendant has no previous convictions. What must the solicitor do?","<b>Not confirm</b> or rely on it. Seek the client's <b>permission to correct</b> it. If refused, <b>withdraw</b> without explaining why beyond professional embarrassment."]]},{"id":"sentencing","title":"Sentencing","q":[[0,"Must courts follow Sentencing Council guidelines?",["Yes, unless contrary to the interests of justice","No, they are advisory","Only in the Crown Court"],"Sentencing Code <b>s.59</b> requires courts to follow relevant guidelines unless it would be contrary to the interests of justice."],[1,"Maximum guilty plea reduction at the first stage?",["One third","One quarter","One fifth"],"A plea indicated at the first stage earns up to <b>one third</b>."],[2,"Maximum reduction for a plea entered after the first stage?",["One quarter","One third","One fifth"],"After the first stage the maximum is <b>one quarter</b>."],[3,"Maximum reduction for a plea on the first day of trial?",["One tenth","One quarter","One twentieth"],"A plea on the first day of trial earns up to <b>one tenth</b>."],[4,"What is the maximum guilty plea reduction for murder?",["One sixth","One third","One quarter"],"For <b>murder</b> the guideline caps the reduction at one sixth."],[5,"When is a Newton hearing held?",["Guilty plea on a disputed factual basis","Not guilty plea and sentence disputed","Fitness to plead is in doubt"],"It resolves a factual dispute that would <b>materially affect</b> sentence after a guilty plea."],[6,"At a Newton hearing, what standard must the prosecution meet?",["The criminal standard","The balance of probabilities","Reasonable suspicion"],"The judge must be <b>sure</b> of the prosecution's version, or sentence follows the defendant's."],[7,"What is the custody threshold?",["Offence so serious that no fine or community sentence will do","Any imprisonable offence","A previous conviction"],"Under the Sentencing Code custody is for offences so serious that <b>neither a fine alone nor a community sentence</b> can be justified."],[8,"What is the maximum length of a community order?",["3 years","2 years","5 years"],"A community order can last up to <b>3 years</b>."],[9,"A suspended sentence is breached. What must the court do?",["Activate it unless unjust","Always activate in full","Always extend the order"],"The court <b>must activate</b> the term, in full or part, unless that would be unjust."],[10,"Which is a statutory aggravating factor?",["Offence committed on bail","Careful planning","Use of a weapon"],"Offending <b>on bail</b> is a statutory aggravating factor. Planning and weapons are other common factors."],[11,"Offences arising from one incident are usually sentenced...",["Concurrently","Consecutively","Separately by different judges"],"Same-incident offences usually run <b>concurrently</b>, subject to totality."]],"c":[[331,"When must a court follow a Sentencing Council guideline (s.59)?","<b>Always</b>, unless satisfied it would be <b>contrary to the interests of justice</b>."],[341,"What are the statutory aggravating factors?","Relevant recent <b>previous convictions</b>. Offending <b>on bail</b>. <b>Hostility</b> based on race, religion, disability, sexual orientation or transgender identity. Assault on an <b>emergency worker</b> or public service provider."],[345,"What is the custody threshold (s.230)?","The offence is <b>so serious that neither a fine alone nor a community sentence can be justified</b>."],[361,"What custodial terms can be suspended?","At least <b>14 days</b>, up to <b>3 years</b> if convicted <b>on or after 22 March 2026</b>. Before then the maximum was <b>2 years</b>."],[363,"What must the court do on breach of a suspended sentence or a new offence?","<b>Activate</b> the custodial term in <b>full or in part</b>, <b>unless unjust</b>."],[365,"What is the presumption of suspension?","For the defendant aged <b>18+</b> convicted <b>on or after 22 March 2026</b>, custody of <b>12 months or less</b> <b>must be suspended</b> unless there are <b>exceptional circumstances</b>."],[366,"When does the presumption of suspension not apply?","The defendant <b>already in custody</b>. Offence in <b>breach of a court order</b> or under <b>supervision</b>. Suspension would put a <b>particular individual</b> at <b>significant risk of harm</b>. Sentences on the same occasion together <b>exceed 12 months</b>."],[372,"What is the guilty plea reduction at the first stage?","<b>One third</b>, if indicated at the <b>first hearing</b> (including at plea before venue or for an indictable-only offence)."],[373,"What is the reduction after the first stage?","Maximum <b>one quarter</b>, such as at the <b>PTPH</b>."],[374,"What is the reduction on the first day of trial?","<b>One tenth</b>, decreasing during trial potentially to <b>zero</b>."],[381,"When is a Newton hearing needed?","The defendant pleads <b>guilty</b> on a <b>different factual basis</b> from the prosecution and the difference would <b>materially affect sentence</b>."]]},{"id":"appeals","title":"Appeals","q":[[0,"Time limit to appeal from magistrates to the Crown Court?",["15 business days","28 days","21 days"],"The notice must be served within <b>15 business days</b> of sentence."],[1,"What kind of hearing is a magistrates' appeal in the Crown Court?",["A full rehearing","A review of legal errors only","A paper review"],"The Crown Court rehears the case with witnesses. Appeals can be against conviction and/or sentence."],[2,"On a magistrates' appeal, can the Crown Court increase the sentence?",["Yes, up to the magistrates' powers","No, never","Only if the prosecution asks"],"The Crown Court can vary the decision, including <b>increasing</b> sentence within the magistrates' powers."],[3,"A defendant pleaded guilty before magistrates. What can they appeal?",["Sentence only","Conviction and sentence","Neither"],"A guilty plea limits the appeal to <b>sentence</b>, unless the plea was equivocal."],[4,"Case stated to the High Court is on what ground?",["Wrong in law or in excess of jurisdiction","Unsafe conviction","Manifestly excessive sentence"],"<b>Case stated</b> (MCA s.111) is for errors of law or excess of jurisdiction."],[5,"How can the prosecution challenge a magistrates' acquittal?",["Case stated or judicial review","Appeal to the Crown Court","Appeal to the Court of Appeal"],"The prosecution has <b>no</b> Crown Court appeal. It uses case stated or judicial review."],[6,"Time limit for applying to the Court of Appeal against conviction?",["28 days","14 days","3 months"],"Notice of appeal and grounds are due within <b>28 days</b>, and leave is required."],[7,"What is the ground for allowing a conviction appeal in the Court of Appeal?",["The conviction is unsafe","The sentence was harsh","The jury was too large"],"Under CAA 1968 s.2 the court allows an appeal only if the conviction is <b>unsafe</b>."],[8,"On the defendant's sentence appeal, can the Court of Appeal increase the sentence?",["No, not more severe overall","Yes, up to the maximum","Yes, with the judge's consent"],"The defendant must not be dealt with <b>more severely</b> overall (s.11(3))."],[9,"Who can refer an unduly lenient sentence to the Court of Appeal?",["The Attorney General","The CPS","The victim"],"The <b>Attorney General</b> can refer within 28 days of sentence."],[10,"What is required for an appeal to the Supreme Court?",["A point of law of general public importance","Any error of fact","A new witness"],"The Court of Appeal must certify a <b>point of law of general public importance</b>."],[11,"What does the CCRC do?",["Investigates possible miscarriages of justice","Hears appeals from magistrates","Grants legal aid"],"The <b>Criminal Cases Review Commission</b> can refer cases to the appeal courts."]],"c":[[388,"Who can appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court (s.108)?","The <b>defendant only</b>. The prosecution <b>cannot</b>."],[390,"What can the defendant appeal to the Crown Court after pleading guilty?","<b>Sentence only</b>, unless the plea was <b>equivocal</b> or entered under <b>duress</b>. Then the Crown Court can <b>remit</b> for a not guilty plea."],[391,"What is the time limit for appealing to the Crown Court?","Notice served within <b>15 business days</b> after <b>sentence</b>. The Crown Court can <b>extend</b> time."],[394,"What form does a Crown Court appeal take?","A <b>complete rehearing</b>: the prosecution proves its case again and <b>new evidence</b> can be called."],[397,"Can the Crown Court increase a sentence on the defendant's appeal from the magistrates?","<b>Yes</b>, up to the magistrates' maximum. The solicitor must <b>warn</b> the client."],[399,"Who can appeal by case stated (s.111)?","<b>Either party</b>. It is the main way the <b>prosecution</b> challenges a magistrates' <b>acquittal</b>."],[401,"What is the time limit for case stated?","Apply to the magistrates within <b>21 days</b> of the decision (from <b>sentence</b> if adjourned)."],[414,"What is the time limit for appealing to the Court of Appeal?","Notice and grounds within <b>28 days</b> of <b>conviction</b> or <b>sentence</b>. Time can be <b>extended</b>."],[417,"What is the ground for a conviction appeal (s.2)?","The conviction is <b>unsafe</b>. Otherwise the appeal is <b>dismissed</b>."],[422,"Can the Court of Appeal increase a sentence on the defendant's appeal?","<b>No.</b> The defendant must not be dealt with <b>more severely on the whole</b> (s.11(3))."]]},{"id":"youth-court","title":"Youth court and sentencing children","q":[[0,"What is the age of criminal responsibility?",["10","12","14"],"A child under <b>10</b> cannot be convicted of an offence."],[1,"Where is a 15-year-old charged with burglary normally tried?",["Youth court","Crown Court","Adult magistrates' court"],"Children are normally tried in the <b>youth court</b>, even for serious offences."],[2,"Which offence must be sent to the Crown Court for a child?",["Homicide","Robbery","ABH"],"Homicide offences must be sent under CDA s.51A. Robbery may be if long detention is likely."],[3,"A child is jointly charged with an adult sent to the Crown Court. The child is sent...",["Only if necessary in the interests of justice","Automatically","Never"],"Under CDA <b>s.51(7)</b> the child is sent only where necessary in the interests of justice."],[4,"Can a child elect Crown Court trial?",["No","Yes, for either-way offences","Yes, if over 15"],"A child has <b>no right to elect</b>. The youth court decides venue."],[5,"What does CYPA 1933 s.49 provide?",["Automatic reporting restrictions","Open court hearings","Compulsory referral orders"],"<b>Section 49</b> bars media identification of a child in the youth court."],[6,"Must parents attend the youth court for a 15-year-old?",["Yes, normally","No, never","Only if the child asks"],"Parents of defendants <b>under 16</b> must attend, unless unreasonable."],[7,"What is the longest detention and training order?",["24 months","12 months","36 months"],"A <b>DTO</b> is for fixed terms up to <b>24 months</b>."],[8,"Can an 11-year-old receive a DTO?",["No","Yes, if a persistent offender","Yes, for serious offences"],"DTOs are available from age <b>12</b>, and 12 to 14 only for persistent offenders."],[9,"What is the length range of a referral order?",["3 to 12 months","1 to 6 months","6 to 24 months"],"A <b>referral order</b> lasts 3 to 12 months."],[10,"When is a referral order compulsory?",["First-time, guilty plea, imprisonable offence","Any guilty plea","Any first offence"],"It is compulsory if the offence is imprisonable, the child pleads guilty and has no previous convictions."],[11,"What is the sentence for murder by a child?",["Detention at His Majesty's pleasure","Life imprisonment","Detention and training order"],"<b>Detention at His Majesty's pleasure</b> (Sentencing Code s.259) is mandatory for murder by under-18s."]],"c":[[431,"What is the age of criminal responsibility?","<b>10.</b> A child under 10 cannot be convicted. Youths are aged <b>10 to 17</b>."],[435,"Can a child elect Crown Court trial?","<b>Never.</b>"],[445,"Which offences must be sent to the Crown Court (CDA s.51A)?","<b>Homicide</b>. <b>Firearms</b> offences with a mandatory minimum where the defendant was <b>16 or 17</b>. <b>Dangerous offender</b> cases where an <b>extended sentence</b> is likely."],[446,"What is a grave crime?","An offence punishable with <b>14 years or more</b> for an adult (not murder), such as <b>robbery</b> or <b>domestic burglary</b>, or certain <b>sexual</b> or <b>firearms</b> offences."],[448,"When should the youth court send a grave crime to the Crown Court?","Only if there is a <b>real prospect</b> the sentence needed is <b>beyond the 2-year</b> DTO maximum, judged on the <b>prosecution's</b> facts."],[449,"When is a child jointly charged with an adult sent to the Crown Court (CDA s.51(7))?","<b>Only if necessary in the interests of justice</b>. The <b>presumption</b> is a <b>separate</b> youth court trial."],[466,"What is a referral order?","Referral to a <b>youth offender panel</b> to agree a <b>youth offender contract</b> (e.g. <b>reparation</b>, school), lasting <b>3 to 12 months</b>."],[468,"When is a referral order compulsory (s.85(2))?","Where available, the offence is <b>imprisonable</b>, the child <b>pleaded guilty</b>, and has <b>never been convicted</b> before."],[476,"Who can receive a detention and training order?","Children aged <b>12 to 17</b>. For <b>12-14</b> only if a <b>persistent offender</b>. Not 10 or 11."],[478,"What terms can a DTO be?","Fixed: <b>4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18 or 24 months</b>, not exceeding the <b>adult maximum</b>."]]}]}];
